PSI-10,metadata,PSI-10_metadata_PSI-10-ISA.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-10/metadata/PSI-10_metadata_PSI-10-ISA.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,1,"Consider a broad range of reactant dilutions, flow rates and pressures for normal and inverse convection directions. Observe the flames with a broad suite of cameras, radiometers, photomultiplier tubes, and thermocouples. Comment[DOI] 10.60555/w4zy-bt45 Comment[Data Source Accession] Comment[Data Source Link] Comment[Experiment Hardware] Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME), Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR), Reconfiguration of Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) Comment[Experiment Hardware Link] https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/combustion-science/acme/implementation/, https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/, https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Videos/2017/11/Refurbishing_the_ISS_combustion_rack Comment[Flight Platform] International Space Station (ISS) Comment[Funding] NCC-3697, NCC-1063, NNC06AA32A, NNX09AO53A, NNX15AC75A, 80NSSC20M0072 Comment[Hypothesis] ""1. Soot formation requires a region where local C/O ratio and T exceed their critical values, and where strain rate is sufficiently low. 2. Microgravity spherical diffusion flames extinguish when the peak temperature first decreases below 1130 K. 3.",,False
PSI-10,metadata,PSI-10_metadata_PSI-10-ISA.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-10/metadata/PSI-10_metadata_PSI-10-ISA.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,2,"Steady-state spherical diffusion flames are possible."" Comment[Identifiers] Comment[Investigation Acronym] ACME/Flame Design Comment[Investigation End Date] 04-30-2025 Comment[Investigation Start Date] 01-14-1999 Comment[Managing NASA Center] Glenn Research Center (GRC) Comment[Mission End] Comment[Mission Link] Comment[Mission Name] Comment[Mission Start] Comment[Objective] ""The primary objectives of this flight experiment are: A. Obtain soot-inception limits of quasi-steady normal and inverse spherical diffusion flames as functions of flow rate, Zst, amount of inert (N2 or CO2), and pressure for C2H4 and CH4 flames. Identify the corresponding temperatures in hot regions. Obtain similar limits and temperatures for inverse and normal coflow flames. B. Obtain detailed measurements in quasi-steady normal and inverse spherical diffusion flames for C2H4 and CH4. Determine the effects of flow rate, Zst, amount of N2, and pressure on flame temperature, size, color, and soot volume fraction. Evaluate the possible existence of steady flames. C. Obtain extinction limits as functions of Zst, amount of N2, and pressure for normal and inverse spherical C2H4 and CH4 flames. Identify the corresponding temperatures in hot regions. Identify the presence of radiative or kinetic extinction.",,False
PSI-10,metadata,PSI-10_metadata_PSI-10-ISA.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-10/metadata/PSI-10_metadata_PSI-10-ISA.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,3,"Evaluate whether pseudo-flammability limits can be obtained for these flames."" Comment[Project Identifier] Comment[Project Link] Comment[Project Title] Comment[Project Type] Microgravity Investigation Comment[Proposal Title] Flame Design: A Novel Approach to Clean Efficient Diffusion Flames Comment[Research Area] Combustion Science Comment[Research Impacts] This project has the potential for improving efficiency and reducing pollutant emissions in terrestrial combustors. For example, it is contributing to novel designs of future coal power plants.",,False
