PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,p92,0,ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) 6 April 2023 92 Appendix L. ACME Operations per Astronauts on Twitter This tweet is from an ISS crew member who conducted operations in support of the Flame Design experiment. There may be others which are not included here. Christina H. Koch (@Astro_Christina) on Twitter https://twitter.com/astro_christina/ 2019 April 23: GMT 113,,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,p93,0,"ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) 6 April 2023 93 Appendix M. Summary of the ISS data collection The Flame Design data is all digital and includes both image and non-image data, where that is generally true for the ACME experiments. To be clear, there was no collection of soot or other physical samples that were subsequently transported to the ground. The Flame Design image data includes images from the following cameras: 1. ACME DataCam, used with a clear fused-silica ‘filter’ and various zoom and focus settings (but those settings were consistent within each individual test) 2. CFI intensified camera, used at least generally with a CH* filter 3. High-Bitdepth Multispectral (HiBMs) camera, equipped with a red filter corresponding to the opposing CIR illumination package The raw DataCam images are in a Halcon Iconic Object (hobj) format as well as a converted Tagged Image File (tif) format. NASA’s post-processing of the raw images was carried out without each of the following: a. color balancing (to avoid possible saturation) b. dark frame subtraction c. removal of hot spots from dead pixels (e.g., resulting from radiation damage) Furthermore, the conversion employed a standard bilinear demosaicing process which is detrimental when determining temperature profiles via Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) because it will average pixels both on and off the glowing fibers.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,p93,1,"The images from the CFI and HiBMs cameras are provided in a tif format. Meanwhile, the analog operations (ops) video was recorded on the ground in a Transport Stream (ts) format. The key non-imaging data from a test is a comma-delimited file that is described in detail in the Software Interface Design Description (ACME-DOC-010 Rev. D). The CIR’s Gas Chromatograph Instrumentation Package (GCIP) was used during a limited number of Flame Design test days and the resulting raw data files are in a text (i.e., ASCII) format. Consult the forthcoming ACME/Flame Design Data Interpretation document to learn more. The raw Flame Design data is currently archived at NASA and work is underway to make it available publicly through NASA’s Physical Science Informatics System (PSI).",,False
