PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_SRD_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_SRD_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,p37,0,"37 Urban, D.L., Yuan, Z.-G, Sunderland, P.B., Linter is, G.T., Voss, J.E., Lin, K.-C., Dai, Z., Sun, K. and Faeth, G.M., (1998) “Structure and Soot Properties of Nonbuoyant Ethylene/Air Laminar Jet Diffusion Flames,” AIAA J., 36(8):1346. Varagani, R., Chatel-Pelage, F., Pranda, P., L u., Y., Chen, S., Rostam-Abadi, M., Farzan, H., Vecci, S.J. and Bose, A.C., (2004) Thir d Annual Conference on Carbon Sequestration, May 3-6, 2004, Alexandria, VA. Villimpoc, V. and Goss, L.P. (1988) Proc. Combust. Inst. 22, 1907-1914. Williams, F.A. (1981) Fire Safety J. 3:163-175. Williams, S.J., Cuervo, L.A., and Chapman, M.A. (1989) High-Temperature Industrial Process Heating: Oxygen-Gas Combustion and Plasma Heating Systems, Gas Research Institute report GRI-89/0256, Chicago. Yih, C.-S., Fluid Mechanics. McGraw Hill, NY, 1969, p. 107. 5 APPENDIX A Estimate of Required Gravity Environment The acceleration induced on a fluid packet by natural convection is",,False
PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_SRD_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_SRD_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,p37,1,"a = 2 ρ − ρ∞ 2ρ + ρ∞ gs = 2 (ρ/ ρ∞ ) −1 2(ρ/ ρ∞ ) + 1 gs (A1) where ρ is the density of the fluid packet, ρ∞ the density of the fluid in the ambient, a the acceleration of the fluid packet, and gs the gravitational acceleration (Yi et al., 1969, Batchelor, 1983). Considering the flow to be isobaric and applying the ideal gas law, p = ρR T where p is the pressure and R the ideal gas constant, we have ρ/ ρ∞ = T∞ / T . In a laboratory in the space station or a space shuttle, the gravity is caused by vibration so that it is taken to be periodic with amplitude A and frequency ω, given by gs = A sin ( 2π ω t ), where t is the time. Adopting these assumptions, Eq. (B1) is modified to a= A (T∞ / T ) −1 (T∞ / T )+ 0.5 sin ( 2πωt ) . (A2) Integrating Eq. (B2) yields the velocity of the fluid packet induced by gravity, given by u =− A 2πω (T∞ / T ) −1 (T∞ / T ) + 0. 5 cos( 2πωt )+ c . (A3) If the acceleration starts at t = 0 so that u = 0 at t = 0, Eq. (B3) is changed to u = A 2 πω (T∞ / T )−1 (T∞ / T ) +0.5 [1 −cos (2 πωt )] . (A4) This gravity induced flow velocity introdu ces a modification of the mass flux of the reactant supplied from the burner, and conseque ntly, a distortion of the flame shape from spherical symmetric. At the unperturbed flame front located at r = rf , the normal component of the additional mass flux is",,False
PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_SRD_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_SRD_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,p37,2,"(ρf uf )n = ρf A 2 πω (T∞ / T f ) −1 (T∞ / Tf ) + 0. 5 [1−cos(2πωt )]( cos θ ) (A5) where the subscript “ f” denotes values at rf , the subscript “ n” denotes the normal component, and θ the angle with θ = 0 along the direction of gravity, Since only the magnitude is of concern, the direction to define θ = 0 or π is not important. The tangential component of the gravity induced flow velocity will not alter the reactant supply rate (consumption rate) at the flame front",,False
PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_SRD_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_SRD_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,p38,0,"38 and will be discarded. For the mass flow rate of m, the mass flux at the flame front is F = m /(4π rf 2 ). Because the actual flame standoff distance depends linear ly on the mass flow rate, if the maximum allowable distortion of the flame geom etry from symmetrical symmetric is ∆ rf / rf = ε , we require (ρf uf )n ≤ ε F , or ρf A 2πω (T∞ / Tf ) −1 (T∞ / T f ) +0.5[1− cos (2 πωt )](cosθ ) ≤ ε m 4 π rf 2 . (A6) Since the maximum distortion occurs at cos (2 πω t ) = −1 and cos θ = 1, as well as T∞ < Tf , the maximum allowable gravity, expressed by A, can be reduced from Eq. (B6) to A ≤ ε ω m 4ρf rf 2 (T∞ / T f ) +0.5 1−(T∞ / T f ) . (A7) Considering the fluid be sufficiently close to air, we approximate the fluid density by that of air such that the ideal gas equation of state gives ρf = p /[(R / Ma )Tf ] where R is the universal gas constant and Ma the average molecular weight of the components (or air). Applying this approximation, Eq. (B7) is re-expressed in its final form by A g ≤ ε ω mR T f 4 gpMa rf 2 (T∞ / T f ) +0.5 1 −(T∞ / T f ) = εωm R 4 gpMa rf 2 T∞ +0.5Tf 1−(T∞ / T f ) , (A8) where g is the normal gravitational acceleration on earth. Taking R = 8.314 J/mole⋅K, Ma = 28.97 gm/mole (for air) and g = 9.8 m/s2, Eq. (B8) is reduced to",,False
PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_SRD_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_SRD_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,p38,1,"A g ≤ 0.07225 mm 2 ⋅sec 2⋅atm gm ⋅K ⎛ ⎝ ⎜ ⎞ ⎠ ⎟ εωm prf 2 T∞ + 0.5Tf 1−(T∞ / T f ) . (A9) In the space laboratory, the typical freque ncy of vibration is between 1 to 22 Hz (DeLombard, 1996). If the maximum allowable flame distortion is ε = 0.1, p = 1 atm, and adopting the most restrictive frequency of ω = 1 Hz = 1 s-1, Eq. (B9) is further reduced to A g ≤ 0.007225 mm 2 ⋅ sec gm ⋅ K ⎛ ⎝ ⎜ ⎜ ⎞ ⎠ ⎟ ⎟ m rf 2 T∞ + 0.5Tf 1− (T∞ / Tf ) . (A10) Equation (B10) will then be applied to estimat e the allowable gravity induced by vibration in the experiments, namely flames (a) – (d). In the following, T∞ will be taken as 300 K while rf and Tf are results of the numerical calculations. (1) Flame (a): m = 0.00151 gm/sec , Tf = 1477 K , rf = 17.72 mm ⇒ A/g = 4.53 × 10–5 or A = 4.53 × 10–5 g = 45.3 µg (2) Flame (b): m = 0.01855 gm/sec , Tf = 2021 K , rf = 9.40 mm ⇒ A/g = 2.37 × 10–3 or A = 2.37 × 10–3 g (3) Flame (c): m = 0.02222 gm/sec , Tf = 2266 K , rf = 6.22 mm ⇒ A/g = 6.85 × 10–3 or A = 6.85 × 10–3 g (4) Flame (d): m = 0.00518 gm/sec , Tf = 1572 K , rf = 16.23 mm ⇒ A/g = 1.91 × 10–4 or A = 1.91 × 10–4 g = 191 µg",,False
PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_SRD_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_SRD_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf,p39,0,"39 Among the 4 limiting cases, the most restrictive case is Flame (a). It is then concluded that for the flame distortion to be within an allowable 10% limit, the gravity level in the space laboratory should be lower than 45 µg. For other conditions, the allowable gravity level can be calculated using Eqs. (B8 or B9).",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p1,0,"Fiscal Year: FY 2024 Task Last Updated: FY 02/06/2024 PI Name: Axelbaum, Richard Ph.D. Project Title: Flame Design - A Novel Approach to Clean Efficient Diffusion Flames (80NSSC20M0072 ; NNX15AC75A) Division Name: Physical Sciences Program/Discipline: Program/Discipline-- Element/Subdiscipline: COMBUSTION SCIENCE--Combustion science Joint Agency Name: TechPort: No Human Research Program Elements: None Human Research Program Risks: None Space Biology Element: None Space Biology Cross-Element Discipline: None Space Biology Special Category: None PI Email: rla@wustl.edu Fax: FY PI Organization Type: UNIVERSITY Phone: 314 935-7560 Organization Name: Washington University PI Address 1: Department of Energy, Environmental and Chemical Engineering PI Address 2: [address removed] PI Web Page: City: [address removed] Congressional District: 1 Comments: Project Type: FLIGHT Solicitation / Funding Source: 97-HEDS-01 Start Date: 12/12/2014 End Date: 04/30/2024 No. of Post Docs: 0 No. of PhD Degrees: 1 No. of PhD Candidates: 2 No. of Master' Degrees: 0 No. of Master's Candidates: 0 No. of Bachelor's Degrees: 0 No. of Bachelor's Candidates: 0 Monitoring Center: NASA GRC Contact Monitor: Stocker, Dennis P Contact Phone: 216-433-2166 Contact Email: dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov Flight Program: ISS Flight Assignment: NOTE: End date changed to 04/30/2024 per NSSC information (Ed., 11/6/23) NOTE: End date changed to 04/30/2023 per D. Stockton/GRC (Ed., 6/6/22) NOTE:",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p1,1,"End date changed to 06/11/2022 per NSSC information (Ed., 5/17/22) NOTE: Extended to 4/30/2022 with new grant number 80NSSC20M0072, per NSSC information (Ed., 8/12/20) NOTE: Extended to 06/11/2020 per NSSC information (Ed., 1/29/2020) NOTE: Extended to 12/11/2019 per NSSC information (Ed., 2/3/17) Key Personnel Changes/Previous PI: COI Name (Institution): Sunderland, Peter Ph.D. ( University of Maryland ) Urban, David Ph.D. ( NASA Glenn Research Center ) Grant/Contract No.: 80NSSC20M0072 ; NNX15AC75A Performance Goal No.: Performance Goal Text: NOTE Update August 2020: New grant issued for Flame Design - A Novel Approach to Clean Efficient Diffusion Page 1 of 3 Task Book Report Generated on: 02/28/2024",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p2,0,"Task Description: NOTE Update August 2020: New grant issued for Flame Design - A Novel Approach to Clean Efficient Diffusion Flames, with grant number 80NSSC20M0072, which continues to 4/30/2022. NOTE (September 2015): Continuation of Flame Design - A Novel Approach to Clean Efficient Diffusion Flames (NNX09AO53A). The overwhelming majority of practical combustion processes involve non-premixed combustion of hydrocarbons. These processes must be efficient and the resulting emissions low. For example, soot production, which can adversely affect efficiency, emissions, and equipment lifetime, must be controlled, and flame extinction must be avoided. Many methods have been considered to reduce soot, such as increasing strain rates and inert concentrations and reducing pressures, but these techniques generally weaken the flame, leading to extinction and resulting in substantial performance penalties. The proposed study considers a novel method of approaching combustion to address these concerns. The approach is termed “Flame Design” as it attempts to take our fundamental understanding of diffusion flames and, within the inherent constraints imposed on the problem, design flames that best optimize efficiency and minimize pollutants. This effort is a collaborative effort between the PI (Principal Investigator), the Co-Is (Co-Investigators), and staff at NASA Glenn Research Center.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p2,1,"The division of labor has Richard Axelbaum directing normal gravity experiments and computations at Washington University, David Urban providing guidance for the flight experiments, and Peter Sunderland directing experiments and numerical simulations at University of Maryland. Rationale for HRP Directed Research: Research Impact/Earth Benefits: This project has the potential for improving efficiency and reducing pollutant emissions in terrestrial combustors. For example, it is contributing to novel designs of future coal power plants. Task Progress: We have completed Round 1 (normal flames) and Round 2 (inverse flames) of Flame Design testing and are processing and analyzing the data. We are running Sphdif simulations and publishing the findings. Three journal papers were published this year and one other was submitted. One conference paper was published and presented. Kendyl Waddell defended and published his Ph.D. dissertation. Our work on the numerical model has continued. We have run multiple cases with the University of California San Diego (UCSD) mechanism. We continue to investigate the boundary conditions for radiation and conduction. We simulated ethylene flames to perform parametric studies in an attempt to find conditions that are favorable to steady state flames. February 2024 Update per PI. See Bibliography for additional information.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p2,2,"(Ed., 2/15/24) Despite a range of conditions, all flames extinguish at a critical temperature of 1130 K, a fuel-based mass flux of 0.2 g/m2-s for normal flames, and an oxygen-based mass flux of 0.68 g/m2-s for inverse flames. With this information, we have developed a simple equation to estimate the flame size (i.e., location of peak temperature) at extinction for any atmospheric-pressure ethylene spherical diffusion flame given only the reactant mass flow rate. Flame growth, which ultimately leads to radiative extinction if the critical extinction point is reached, is attributed to the natural development of the diffusion limited system as it approaches steady state and the reduction in the transport properties as the flame temperature drops due to increasing flame radiation with time (radiation-induced growth). Reference: Irace PH, Waddell KA, Constales D, Sunderland PB, Axelbaum RL. ""Critical temperature and reactant mass flux for radiative extinction of ethylene microgravity spherical diffusion flames at 1 bar."" Proc. Combust. Inst. 2023 Jan 1;39(2):1905-13. <a target=""_blank"" href=""https://doi.org/10.1016/j.proci.2022.07.043"">https://</a> Despite a narrow range of attainable conditions, flames that exhibit steady-state behavior are observed aboard the International Space Station (ISS) for up to 870 seconds, even with the constraint of a finite boundary. Steady-state flames are simulated using the numerical model for over 100,000 seconds. Reference:",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p2,3,"Irace PH, Waddell KA, Constales D, Kim M, Yablonsky G, Sunderland PB, Axelbaum RL. ""On the existence of steady-state gaseous microgravity spherical diffusion flames in the presence of radiation heat loss."" Proc. Combust. Inst. 2023 Jan 1;39(2):1721-9. <a target=""_blank"" href=""https://doi.org/10.1016/j.proci.2022.07.049"">https://</a> Bibliography Type: Description: (Last Updated: 02/16/2024) Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Frolov SM, Ivanov VS, Frolov FS, Vlasov PA, Axelbaum R, Irace PH, Yablonsky G, Waddell K. ""Soot formation in spherical diffusion flames."" Mathematics. 2023 Jan 4;11(2):261. https://doi.org/10.3390/math11020261 , Jan-2023 Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Irace PH, Waddell KA, Constales D, Sunderland PB, Axelbaum RL. ""Critical temperature and reactant mass flux for radiative extinction of ethylene microgravity spherical diffusion flames at 1 bar."" Proc. Combust. Inst. 2023 Jan 1;39(2):1905-13. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.proci.2022.07.043 [NOTE: previously reported in May 2022 as ""in revision.""] , Jan-2023 Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Irace PH, Waddell KA, Constales D, Kim M, Yablonsky G, Sunderland PB, Axelbaum RL. ""On the existence of steady-state gaseous microgravity spherical diffusion flames in the presence of radiation heat loss."" Proc. Combust. Inst. 2023 Jan 1;39(2):1721-9. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.proci.2022.07.049 [NOTE:",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p2,4,"previously reported in May 2022 as ""in revision.""] , Jan-2023 Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Irace PH, Gopan A, Axelbaum RL. ""An investigation of thermal radiation from laminar diffusion flames in a tri-coflow burner with central oxygen."" Combustion and Flame. 2022 Aug 1;242:112158. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2022.112158 , Aug-2022 Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Krishnan S, Kumfer BM, Wu W, Li J, Nehorai A, Axelbaum RL. ""An approach to thermocouple measurements that reduces uncertainties in high-temperature environments."" Energy & Fuels. 2015 Mar 30;29(5):3446-55. https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.energyfuels.5b00071 , Mar-2015 Waddell K. ""Near-limit spherical diffusion flames and cool diffusion flames."" Dissertation, University of Maryland, Page 2 of 3 Task Book Report Generated on: 02/28/2024",,False
