PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p26,0,"Task Description: The overwhelming majority of practical combustion processes involve non-premixed combustion of hydrocarbons. These processes must be efficient and the resulting emissions low. For example, soot production, which can adversely affect efficiency, emissions, and equipment lifetime, must be controlled, and flame extinction must be avoided. Many methods have been considered to reduce soot, such as increasing strain rates and inert concentrations and reducing pressures, but these techniques generally weaken the flame, leading to extinction and resulting in substantial performance penalties. The proposed study considers a novel method of approaching combustion to address these concerns. The approach is termed “Flame Design” as it attempts to take our fundamental understanding of diffusion flames and, within the inherent constraints imposed on the problem, design flames that best optimize efficiency and minimize pollutants. This effort is a collaborative effort between the PI, the three Co-Is, and staff at NASA Glenn Research Center. The division of labor has PBS and DLU performing microgravity experiments at the NASA Glenn Research Center, PBS directing numerical simulations at University of Maryland, BHC performing analytical work and computations at University of Hawaii, and RLA performing normal gravity experiments and computations at Washington University.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p26,1,"Rationale for HRP Directed Research: Research Impact/Earth Benefits: This project has the potential for improving efficiency and reducing pollutant emissions in terrestrial combustors. For example, it is contributing to novel designs of future coal power plants. Task Progress: The research team published three peer-reviewed journal articles during this period. Work at Washington University continued to pursue normal gravity experiments of soot formation in diffusion flames, numerical modeling, and chemical kinetics. Work at Univ. of Maryland continued to pursue numerical models of spherical diffusion flames, including soot formation and extinction. Work at Univ. of Hawaii continued to pursue analytical and computational studies of soot formation, radiation, and extinction in diffusion flames. The flight experiment was supported in various ways. For example, the research team responded to issues raised by NASA engineers in refining the test matrix and ACME requirements document. Bibliography Type: Description: (Last Updated: 02/16/2024) Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Lecoustre VR, Sunderland PB, Chao BH, Axelbaum RL. ""Modeled Quenching Limits of Spherical Hydrogen Diffusion Flames."" Proceedings of the Combustion Institute. (in press), as of August 2012. , Aug-2012 Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Lecoustre VR, Sunderland PB, Chao BH, Axelbaum RL. ""Numerical investigation of spherical diffusion flames at their sooting limits."" Combustion and Flame 2012 Jan;159(1):194-9.",,False
