PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p33,0,"Fiscal Year: FY 2009 Task Last Updated: FY 12/31/2009 PI Name: Axelbaum, Richard Ph.D. Project Title: Flame Design - A Novel Approach to Clean Efficient Diffusion Flames (NNC06AA32A) Division Name: Physical Sciences Program/Discipline: COMBUSTION SCIENCE Program/Discipline-- Element/Subdiscipline: COMBUSTION SCIENCE--Gaseous diffusion flames Joint Agency Name: TechPort: No Human Research Program Elements: None Human Research Program Risks: None Space Biology Element: None Space Biology Cross-Element Discipline: None Space Biology Special Category: None PI Email: rla@wustl.edu Fax: FY PI Organization Type: UNIVERSITY Phone: 314 935-7560 Organization Name: Washington University PI Address 1: Department of Energy, Environmental and Chemical Engineering PI Address 2: [address removed] PI Web Page: City: [address removed] Congressional District: 1 Comments: Project Type: FLIGHT Solicitation / Funding Source: 97-HEDS-01 Start Date: 10/01/2006 End Date: 09/30/2009 No. of Post Docs: 1 No. of PhD Degrees: 3 No. of PhD Candidates: 2 No. of Master' Degrees: 2 No. of Master's Candidates: 2 No. of Bachelor's Degrees: 1 No. of Bachelor's Candidates: 1 Monitoring Center: NASA GRC Contact Monitor: Contact Phone: Contact Email: Flight Program: ISS Flight Assignment: Key Personnel Changes/Previous PI: COI Name (Institution):",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p33,1,"Chao, Beei-Huan ( University of Hawaii at Manoa ) Sunderland, Peter ( University of Maryland ) Urban, David ( NASA Glenn Research Center ) Grant/Contract No.: NNC06AA32A Performance Goal No.: Performance Goal Text: Task Description: This effort is a collaborative effort between the PI, the three Co-I's, and staff at NASA Glenn Research Center. The division of labor is roughly broken up with DLU and PBS performing microgravity experiments at the NASA Glenn Research Center, PBS directing numerical simulations at University of Maryland, BHC performing analytical work at University of Hawaii, and RLA performing normal gravity experiments at Washington University. The overwhelming majority of practical combustion processes involve non-premixed combustion of hydrocarbons. These processes must be efficient and the resulting emissions low. For example, soot production, which can adversely affect efficiency, emissions, and equipment lifetime, must be controlled, and flame extinction must be avoided. Many methods have been considered to reduce soot, such as increasing strain rates and inert concentrations and reducing pressures, but these techniques generally weaken the flame, leading to extinction and resulting in substantial performance penalties. The Page 1 of 3 Task Book Report Generated on: 02/28/2024",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p34,0,"proposed study considers a novel method of approaching combustion to address these concerns. The approach is termed “Flame Design” as it attempts to take our fundamental understanding of diffusion flames and, within the inherent constraints imposed on the problem, design flames that best optimize efficiency and minimize pollutants. Rationale for HRP Directed Research: Research Impact/Earth Benefits: This project has the potential for improving efficiency and reducing pollutant emissions in terrestrial combustors. For example, it is contributing to novel designs of future coal power plants. Task Progress: The following is a summary of the research activities during the period Oct. 2006 - Sept. 2009. 1) Numerical and Experimental Observations of Spherical Diffusion Flames. Spherical diffusion flames supported on a porous sphere were studied numerically and experimentally. Experiments were performed in 2.2 s and 5.2 s microgravity facilities. Numerical results were obtained from a Chemkin-based program. The program simulates flow from a porous sphere into a quiescent environment, yields both steady state and transient results and accounts for optically thick gas-phase radiation. The low flow velocities and long residence times in these diffusion flames lead to enhanced radiative and diffusive effects. Despite similar adiabatic flame temperatures, the measured and predicted temperatures varied by as much as 700 K.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p34,1,"The temperature reduction correlates with flame size but characteristic flow times and Lewis number also influence temperature. The numerical results show that the ambient gas Lewis number would have a strong effect on flame temperature if the flames were steady and nonradiating. For example, a 10% decrease in Lewis number would increase the steady state flame temperature by 200 K. However, for these transient, radiating flames the effect of Lewis number is small. It was also observed that when hydrocarbon fuel is supplied from the ambient the large diffusion distances associated with these flames can lead to unusual steady state compositions near the outer boundary because decomposition products can diffuse to the outer boundary. This results in a loss of chemical enthalpy from the system but the effect on flame temperature is small. Transient predictions of flame sizes are larger than those observed in microgravity experiments. Close agreement could not be obtained without either increasing the model’s thermal and mass diffusion properties by 30% or reducing mass flowrate by 25%. 2) Radiative Extinction of Gaseous Spherical Diffusion Flames in Microgravity. Radiative extinction of spherical diffusion flames was investigated experimentally and numerically. The experiments involved microgravity spherical diffusion flames burning ethylene and propane at 0.98 bar.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p34,2,"Both normal (fuel flowing into oxidizer) and inverse (oxidizer flowing into fuel) flames were studied, with nitrogen supplied to either the fuel or the oxygen. Flame conditions were chosen to ensure that the flames extinguished within the 2.2 s of available test time; thus extinction occurred during unsteady flame conditions. Diagnostics included color video and thin-filament pyrometry. The computations, which simulated flow from a porous sphere into a quiescent environment, included detailed chemistry, transport, and radiation and yielded transient results. Radiative extinction was observed experimentally and simulated numerically. Extinction time, peak temperature, and radiative loss fraction were found to be independent of flow rate except at very low flow rates. Radiative heat loss was dominated by the combustion products downstream of the flame and was found to scale with flame surface area, not volume. For large transient flames the heat release rate also scaled with surface area and thus the radiative loss fraction was largely independent of flow rate. Peak temperatures at extinction onset were about 1100 K, which is significantly lower than for kinetic extinction.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p34,3,"An important observation of this work is that while radiative heat losses can drive transient extinction, this is not only because radiative losses are increasing with time but also because the heat release rate is falling off as the flame expands away from the burner and the reactant supply to the flame decreases. 3) Soot Inception Limits in Laminar Diffusion Flames with Application to Oxy–Fuel Combustion. For diffusion flames, the combination of oxygen enrichment and fuel dilution results in an increase in the stoichiometric mixture fraction, Zst, and alters the flame structure, i.e., the relationship between the local temperature and the local gas composition. Increasing Zst has been shown to result in the reduction or even elimination of soot. In the present work, the effects of variable Zst on soot inception are investigated in normal and inverse coflow flames, using ethylene as the fuel. Use of the inverse coflow flame underscores the validity of these concepts, since the convective field in the inverse flame directs particles into the fuel-rich region. Sooting limits based on particle luminosity are measured as a function of Zst. The sooting limit is obtained by varying the amount of inert gas until soot appears above a predefined height. For each limit flame, the adiabatic flame temperature is calculated and the flame temperature at the half-height is measured. The flame temperature at the sooting limit is found to increase with Zst for both normal and inverse flames.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p34,4,"The effects of residence time are also investigated, and the sooting limit inception temperature is found to be dependent on fuel stream velocity for both the normal and inverse configurations. A simple model applicable to oxy–fuel combustion is presented which describes how increasing Zst results in the reduction and ultimately elimination of soot. This model assumes that soot inception can only occur in a region where critical values for species, temperature, and residence time are met. The soot inception region is shown to be bounded by two isotherms: a low-temperature boundary that is a function of residence time, and a high-temperature boundary that corresponds to the location of a critical local carbon-to-oxygen ratio. The effect of increasing Zst is to move the boundaries of the soot inception zone towards each other, until the zone is infinitely thin and thus the sooting limit is reached. By comparing the model to experimental data, a critical local C/O ratio of 0.53 and a sooting limit inception temperature of 1640 K (for a characteristic residence time of 22 ms) were determined for ethylene. 4) Characteristics of Non-Premixed Oxygen-Enhanced Combustion: I. The Presence of Appreciable Oxygen at the Location of Maximum Temperature.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p34,5,"The presence of appreciable molecular oxygen at the location of maximum temperature has been observed in non-premixed oxygen-enhanced combustion (OEC) processes, specifically in flames having a high stoichiometric mixture fraction (Zst) produced with diluted fuel and oxygen-enrichment. For conventional fuel–air flames, key features of the flame are consistent with the flame sheet approximation (FSA). In particular, the depletion of O2 at the location of maximum temperature predicted by the FSA correlates well with the near-zero O2 concentration measured at this location for conventional fuel–air flames. In contradistinction, computational analysis with detailed kinetics demonstrates that for OEC flames at high Zst: (1) there is an appreciable concentration of O2 at the location of maximum temperature and (2) the maximum temperature is not coincident with the location of global stoichiometry, O2 depletion, or maximum heat release. We investigate these phenomena computationally in three non-premixed ethylene flames at low, moderate, and high Zst, but with equivalent adiabatic flame temperatures. Results demonstrate that the location of O2 depletion occurs in the vicinity of global stoichiometry for flames of any Zst and that the presence of appreciable O2 at the location of maximum temperature for high Zst flames is caused by a shift in the location of maximum temperature relative to the location of O2 depletion. This shifting is attributed to:",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p34,6,(1) finite-rate multi-step chemistry resulting in exothermic heat release that is displaced from the location of O2 depletion and (2) the Page 2 of 3 Task Book Report Generated on: 02/28/2024,,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p35,0,"multi-step chemistry resulting in exothermic heat release that is displaced from the location of O2 depletion and (2) the relative location of the heat release region with respect to the fuel and oxidizer boundaries in mixture fraction space. A method of superposition involving a variation of the flame sheet approximation with two heat sources is shown to be sufficient in explaining this phenomenon. Bibliography Type: Description: (Last Updated: 02/16/2024) Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Santa KJ, Sun Z, Chao BH, Sunderland PB, Axelbaum RL, Urban DL, Stocker DP. ""Numerical and experimental observations of spherical diffusion flames."" Combustion Theory and Modeling 2007 Aug;11(4):639-52. http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/13647830601161567 , Aug-2007 Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Santa KJ, Chao BH, Sunderland PB, Urban DL, Stocker DP, Axelbaum RL. ""Radiative extinction of gaseous spherical diffusion flames in microgravity."" Combustion and Flame 2007 Dec;151(4):665-75. http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2007.08.009 , Dec-2007 Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Kumfer BM, Skeen SA, Axelbaum RL. ""Soot inception limits in laminar diffusion flames with application to oxy–fuel combustion"" Combustion and Flame 2008 Aug;154(3):546–56. http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2008.03.008 , Aug-2008 Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Skeen SA, Yablonsky G, Axelbaum RL. ""Characteristics of non-premixed oxygen-enhanced combustion: I.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p35,1,"The presence of appreciable oxygen at the location of maximum temperature."" Combustion and Flame 2009 Nov;15(11);2145–52. http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2009.07.009 , Nov-2009 Dissertations and Theses Lecoustre VR. ""Numerical Investigations of Gaseous Spherical Diffusion Flames."" Ph.D. Dissertation, Department of Mechanical Engineering, 2009. , May-2009 Dissertations and Theses Skeen SA. ""Ph.D. Dissertation."" Ph.D. Dissertation, Department of Energy, Environmental and Chemical Engineering, Washington University, 2009. , May-2009 Dissertations and Theses Kumfer BM. ""Ph.D. Dissertation."" Ph.D. Dissertation, Department of Energy, Environmental and Chemical Engineering, Washington University, 2008. , May-2008 Page 3 of 3 Task Book Report Generated on: 02/28/2024",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p36,0,"Fiscal Year: FY 2007 Task Last Updated: FY 09/28/2007 PI Name: Axelbaum, Richard Ph.D. Project Title: Flame Design - A Novel Approach to Clean Efficient Diffusion Flames (NNC06AA32A) Division Name: Physical Sciences Program/Discipline: COMBUSTION SCIENCE Program/Discipline-- Element/Subdiscipline: COMBUSTION SCIENCE--Gaseous diffusion flames Joint Agency Name: TechPort: No Human Research Program Elements: None Human Research Program Risks: None Space Biology Element: None Space Biology Cross-Element Discipline: None Space Biology Special Category: None PI Email: rla@wustl.edu Fax: FY PI Organization Type: UNIVERSITY Phone: 314 935-7560 Organization Name: Washington University PI Address 1: Department of Energy, Environmental and Chemical Engineering PI Address 2: [address removed] PI Web Page: City: [address removed] Congressional District: 1 Comments: Project Type: FLIGHT Solicitation / Funding Source: 97-HEDS-01 Start Date: 10/01/2006 End Date: 09/30/2009 No. of Post Docs: No. of PhD Degrees: No. of PhD Candidates: 2 No. of Master' Degrees: No. of Master's Candidates: 1 No. of Bachelor's Degrees: No. of Bachelor's Candidates: Monitoring Center: NASA GRC Contact Monitor: Contact Phone: Contact Email: Flight Program: ISS Flight Assignment: Key Personnel Changes/Previous PI: COI Name (Institution): Chao, Beei-Huan ( University of Hawaii at Manoa ) Sunderland, Peter ( University of Maryland ) Urban, David ( NASA Glenn Research Center ) Grant/Contract No.:",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p36,1,"NNC06AA32A Performance Goal No.: Performance Goal Text: Task Description: The effort is a collaborative effort between the three Co-PI's and staff at NASA Glenn (Dr. David Urban and Mr. Dennis Stocker). The division of labor is roughly broken up with PBS performing microgravity experiments at the NASA Glen Research Center and numerical simulations at University of Maryland, BHC performing analytical work at University of Hawaii and RLA performing normal gravity experiments at Washington University. The overwhelming majority of practical combustion processes involve non-premixed combustion of hydrocarbons. These processes must be efficient and the resulting emissions low. For example, soot production, which can adversely affect efficiency, emissions, and equipment lifetime, must be controlled, and flame extinction must be avoided. Many methods have been considered to reduce soot, such as increasing strain rates and inert concentrations and reducing pressures, but these techniques generally weaken the flame, leading to extinction and resulting in substantial Page 1 of 3 Task Book Report Generated on: 02/28/2024",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p37,0,"performance penalties. The proposed study considers a novel method of approaching combustion to address these concerns. The approach is termed 'Flame Design' as it attempts to take our fundamental understanding of diffusion flames and, within the inherent constraints imposed on the problem, design flames that best optimize efficiency and minimize pollutants. Rationale for HRP Directed Research: Research Impact/Earth Benefits: Task Progress: The following is a summary of the research activities during this period. 1) Work has continued on radiative extinction of spherical diffusion flames, which are being investigated experimentally and numerically. The experiments involved microgravity spherical diffusion flames burning ethylene and propane at 0.98 bar. Both normal (fuel flowing into oxidizer) and inverse (oxidizer flowing into fuel) flames are studied, with nitrogen supplied to either the fuel or the oxygen. Flame conditions were chosen to ensure that the flames extinguished within the 2.2 s of available test time; thus extinction occurred during unsteady flame conditions. Diagnostics included color video and thin-filament pyrometry. The computations, which simulated flow from a porous sphere into a quiescent environment, include detailed transport properties, detailed chemistry and an optically thick radiative heat loss model to study the transient radiative extinction of spherical diffusion flames supported by a porous spherical burner.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p37,1,"A flow of a reactant (fuel or oxidizer) issued from a porous sphere into a quiescent environment of the other reactant was considered in the simulation. Radiative extinction was observed experimentally and simulated numerically. Extinction time, peak temperature, and radiative loss fraction (ratio of the radiative heat loss rate to the heat generation rate) were found to be independent of flow rate except at very low flow rates. Radiative heat loss was dominated by the combustion products downstream of the flame and was found to scale with flame surface area, not volume. For large transient flames the heat release rate also scaled with surface area and thus the radiative loss fraction was largely independent of flow rate. Peak temperatures at extinction onset were about 1100 K, which is significantly lower than for kinetic extinction. One observation is that while radiative heat losses can drive transient extinction, this is not because radiative losses are increasing with time (flame size) but rather because the heat release rate is falling off as the temperature drops. 2) A new numerical study of limiting conditions for soot particle inception in spherical diffusion flames has been initiated. The flames were modeled using a one-dimensional, time accurate diffusion flame code with detailed chemistry and transport and an optically thick radiation model.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p37,2,"Seventeen normal and inverse flames were considered, covering a wide range of stoichiometric mixture fraction, adiabatic flame temperature, and residence time. These flames were previously observed to reach their sooting limits after 2 s of microgravity. Sooting-limit diffusion flames with residence times longer than 200 ms were found to have temperatures near 1190 K where C/O = 0.6, whereas flames with shorter residence times required increased temperatures. Acetylene was found to be a reasonable surrogate for soot precursor species in these flames, having peak mole fractions of about 0.01. 3) Our previous studies have shown a linear relationship between the limit flame temperature and stoichiometric mixture fraction for coflow flames at their sooting limit. The sooting limit in coflow flames is obtained by varying the amount of inert gas until soot appears above a predefined height. In this work sooting limits based on particle luminosity were measured as a function of Zst. For each limit flame, the flame temperature was measured and defined as the limit flame temperature. The limit flame temperature increases linearly with Zst, for both normal and inverse flames. The effects of residence time have also been investigated, and the soot inception temperature was found to be dependent on fuel stream velocity for both the normal and inverse configuration.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p37,3,"Earlier experimental results and modeling indicated that a critical carbon to oxygen (C/O) ratio exists in the fuel rich region of the flame where soot inception is no longer favorable due to the presence of oxygen containing species. We extended this model to include the effects of finite rate chemistry and thus residence time on the sooting limit. This involved considering two boundaries for the soot inception zone, one dictated by the C/O ratio and the other by the finite rate chemistry of fuel pyrolysis and soot inception. By combining these two boundaries, a simple model was developed that describes how a sooting limit is attained. By utilizing measured limit temperature data with the model we found the limit inception temperature for soot formation to be 1640 K and the critical carbon to oxygen ratio to be 0.53. 4) Performed analytical analysis of the kinetic and radiative extinction limits of a steady spherical diffusion flame supported by a porous burner. A single step, overall and irreversible reaction that follows high activation energy Arrhenius kinetics was adopted to describe the reaction rate. An optically thin radiation model was used to describe the radiative heat loss. The kinetic and radiative extinction limits were observed from the analysis. Results show that the flame is easier to extinguish when the radiation intensity is higher, as expected, and there exists a maximum radiation intensity above which steady burning is not possible.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p37,4,"Bibliography Type: Description: (Last Updated: 02/16/2024) Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Santa KJ, Sun Z, Chao BH, Sunderland PB, Axelbaum RL, Urban DL, Stocker DP. ""Numerical and experimental observations of spherical diffusion flames."" Combustion Theory and Modelling 2007 Aug;11(4):639-52. http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/13647830601161567 , Aug-2007 Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Kumfer BM, Skeen SA, Chen R, Axelbaum RL. ""Measurement and analysis of soot inception limits of oxygen-enriched coflow flames."" Combustion and Flame 2006 Nov;147(3):233-42. http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2006.08.004 , Nov-2006 Awards Sunderland PB, Urban DL, Stocker DP, Chao BH, Axelbaum RL. ""Third Prize, Art Competition Poster for, “Spherical Ethylene Diffusion Flame in Microgravity,“ 5th U.S. Combustion Meeting, San Diego, March, 2007."" Mar-2007 Papers from Meeting Proceedings Santa KJ, Sun Z, Chao BH, Sunderland PB, Axelbaum RL, Urban DL, Stocker DP. ""Numerical and experimental observations of spherical diffusion flames."" 45th Aerospace Sciences Meeting, Reno, Nevada, January 8–11, 2007. 45th Aerospace Sciences Meeting, Paper No. AIAA-2007-736, 2007. , Jan-2007 Santa KJ, Chao BH, Sunderland PB, Urban DL, Stocker DP, Axelbaum RL. ""Radiative extinction of gaseous spherical Page 2 of 3 Task Book Report Generated on: 02/28/2024",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p38,0,"Papers from Meeting Proceedings Santa KJ, Chao BH, Sunderland PB, Urban DL, Stocker DP, Axelbaum RL. ""Radiative extinction of gaseous spherical diffusion flames in microgravity."" 5th U.S. Combustion Meeting, San Diego, California, March 25–28, 2007. 5th U.S. Combustion Meeting, Paper No. A06, 2007. , Mar-2007 Papers from Meeting Proceedings Wang Q, Chao BH. ""Kinetic and radiative extinctions of sperical diffusion flames."" 5th U.S. Combustion Meeting, San Diego, California, March 25–28, 2007. 5th U.S. Combustion Meeting, Paper No. A08, 2007. , Mar-2007 Papers from Meeting Proceedings Lecoustre VR, Skeen SA, Chao BH, Sunderland PB, Urban DL, Stocker DP, Axelbaum RL. ""A computational and analytical investigation of sooting limitsof sperical diffusion flames."" 5th U.S. Combustion Meeting, San Diego, California, March 25–28, 2007. 5th U.S. Combustion Meeting, Paper No. F23, 2007. , Mar-2007 Page 3 of 3 Task Book Report Generated on: 02/28/2024",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p39,0,"Fiscal Year: FY 2006 Task Last Updated: FY 04/13/2006 PI Name: Axelbaum, Richard Ph.D. Project Title: Flame Design - A Novel Approach to Clean Efficient Diffusion Flames Division Name: Physical Sciences Program/Discipline: COMBUSTION SCIENCE Program/Discipline-- Element/Subdiscipline: COMBUSTION SCIENCE--Gaseous diffusion flames Joint Agency Name: TechPort: No Human Research Program Elements: None Human Research Program Risks: None Space Biology Element: None Space Biology Cross-Element Discipline: None Space Biology Special Category: None PI Email: rla@wustl.edu Fax: FY PI Organization Type: UNIVERSITY Phone: 314 935-7560 Organization Name: Washington University PI Address 1: Department of Energy, Environmental and Chemical Engineering PI Address 2: [address removed] PI Web Page: City: [address removed] Congressional District: 1 Comments: Project Type: FLIGHT Solicitation / Funding Source: 97-HEDS-01 Start Date: 01/01/2003 End Date: 09/30/2006 No. of Post Docs: 0 No. of PhD Degrees: 2 No. of PhD Candidates: 1 No. of Master' Degrees: 2 No. of Master's Candidates: 3 No. of Bachelor's Degrees: 8 No. of Bachelor's Candidates: 3 Monitoring Center: NASA GRC Contact Monitor: Urban, David Contact Phone: 216-433-2835 Contact Email: david.l.urban@nasa.gov Flight Program: Flight Assignment: NOTE: End date changed from 12/31/2006 to 9/30/2006 (1/2009) NOTE: grant to be reinstated with a new grant number. 8/2006, per D. Urban",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p39,1,"Key Personnel Changes/Previous PI: 2 COI Name (Institution): Chao, Beei-Huan ( University of Hawaii ) Sunderland, Peter ( University of Maryland ) Urban, David ( NASA Glenn Research Center ) Grant/Contract No.: NCC3-1063 Performance Goal No.: Performance Goal Text: The overwhelming majority of practical combustion processes involve non-premixed combustion of hydrocarbons. Page 1 of 3 Task Book Report Generated on: 02/28/2024",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p40,0,"Task Description: The overwhelming majority of practical combustion processes involve non-premixed combustion of hydrocarbons. These processes must be efficient and the resulting emissions low. For example, soot production, which can adversely affect efficiency, emissions, and equipment lifetime, must be controlled, and flame extinction must be avoided. Many methods have been considered to reduce soot, such as increasing strain rates and inert concentrations and reducing pressures, but these techniques generally weaken the flame, leading to extinction and resulting in substantial performance penalties. The proposed study considers a novel method of approaching combustion to address these concerns. The approach is termed 'Flame Design' as it attempts to take our fundamental understanding of diffusion flames and, within the inherent constraints imposed on the problem, design flames that best optimize efficiency and minimize pollutants. Recognizing the unique benefits of spherical diffusion flames, employ such flames to: 1. Obtain soot-inception limits as functions of stoichiometric mixture fraction, flow direction (direction across the flame front, i.e., towards the fuel or towards the oxidizer), amount of inert (nitrogen) and pressure for ethylene and methane flames. Identify the corresponding soot-inception temperature; 2.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p40,1,"Determine effects of stoichiometric mixture fraction and flow direction on the phenomenon of permanently blue flames and, to this end, determine the effects of stoichiometric mixture fraction and flow direction on flame temperature, size, color and soot volume fraction; 3. Obtain extinction limits as functions of stoichiometric mixture fraction, flow direction, amount of inert and pressure for ethylene and methane flames. Identify the corresponding extinction temperature; 4. Evaluate whether this flame system can be used to obtain a pseudo-flammability limit for non-premixed flames in terms of stoichiometric mixture fraction and amount of inert. If so, determine these limits for ethylene and methane flames. Soot formation and flame extinction are problems of great concern in practical combustors. Recent normal-gravity experiments using counterflow diffusion flames have shown that permanently-blue flames (which are devoid of soot regardless of strain rate) and extinction-resistant flames can be produced for many hydrocarbon fuels by nitrogen exchange (increased stoichiometric mixture fraction). Furthermore, there is a unique stoichiometric mixture fraction wherein the flame temperature at extinction can be up to 200 K lower and the strain rate up to four times greater than that for the standard hydrocarbon/air flame with the same flame temperature.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p40,2,"The present experiments employ microgravity conditions to allow for a unique set of experiments to clarify the underlying mechanisms responsible for flame response to stoichiometric mixture fraction. Rationale for HRP Directed Research: Research Impact/Earth Benefits: As the goal of this program is to develop an understanding of combustion with the aim of reducing pollutants and increasing combustion efficiency, the results are directly applicable to terrestial power generation. In particular, with the present interest in carbon sequestration via oxygen-enriched combustion the present work is of particular importance as it identifies methods of controlling a number of pollutants through oxygen-enriched combustion. Task Progress: Below is a summary of the progress on this project for the year ending January 1, 2006: Flame Design benefited from several analytical and computational advances in 2005. These have yielded important insight into many experimental observations by this project. This has laid the groundwork for two journal publications, one which was submitted and one which is nearing submission. Work on the general behavior of spherical diffusion flames has demonstrated that a detailed transient numerical model (with detailed chemistry, transport, and radiation) can predict flame size and flame temperature for microgravity spherical diffusion flames.",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p40,3,A simplified model indicates that Lewis number can have a large effect on flame temperature for microgravity diffusion flames. This work has been submitted to the Combustion Symposium. Work on radiative extinction of spherical diffusion flames has demonstrated that the detailed transient model can predict extinction times for microgravity spherical diffusion flames. The work identified several scaling relationship for extinguishing diffusion flames. This work is being finalized for an upcoming journal submission. A Nikon D100 color digital still camera has been configured for use in the 2.2 second drop tower. This provides four images per drop. The images have 6 megapixels and 12 x 3 bit depth and are among the highest-fidelity images ever recorded in the drop tower. This camera has been used to record images of 15 micron SiC fibers in flames in the 2.2 second drop tower. This has yielded qualitative thin-filament pyrometry results for extinguishing spherical diffusion flames. Eighteen successful drop tests were performed in the 2.2 second drop tower.,,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p40,4,"Bibliography Type: Description: (Last Updated: 02/16/2024) Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Sunderland PB, Urban DL, Stocker DP, Chao BH, Axelbaum RL. ""Sooting limits of microgravity spherical diffusion flames in oxygen-enriched air and diluted fuel."" Combustion Science and Technology. 2004 Dec;176(12):2143-2164. http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/00102200490514994 , Dec-2004 Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Santa KJ, Sun Z, Chao BH, Sunderland PB, Axelbaum RL, Urban DL, Stocker DP. ""Numerical and experimental observations of spherical diffusion flames."" Combustion Theory and Modelling. 2007 Aug;11(4):639-52. http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/13647830601161567 , Aug-2007 Articles in Peer-reviewed Journals Liu S, Chao BH, Axelbaum RL. ""A theoretical study on soot inception in spherical burner-stabilized diffusion flames."" Combustion and Flame 2005 Jan;140(1-2):1-23. http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2004.08.016 , Jan-2005 Papers from Meeting Proceedings Santa KJ, Chao BH, Sunderland P, Taylor JL, Urban DL, Stocker DP, Axelbaum RL. ""Radiative Extinction of Gaseous Spherical Diffusion Flames in Microgravity."" 44th AIAA Aerospace Sciences Meeting and Exhibit, Reno, Nevada, Jan. 9-12, 2006 . AIAA Paper AIAA-2006-747, 9 p. January 2006. , Jan-2006 Sunderland PB, Taylor JL, Santa KJ, Chao B-H, Urban DL, Stocker DP, Axelbaum RL. ""Extinction of Normal and Page 2 of 3 Task Book Report Generated on: 02/28/2024",,False
PSI-10,Reports,PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Reports/PSI-10_Reports_TaskbookRpts.pdf,p41,0,"Papers from Meeting Proceedings Sunderland PB, Taylor JL, Santa KJ, Chao B-H, Urban DL, Stocker DP, Axelbaum RL. ""Extinction of Normal and Inverse Spherical Diffusion Flames in Microgravity."" Presented at 4th Joint Meeting of the U.S. Sections of the Combustion Institute, Philadelphia, PA, March 2005. 4th Joint Meeting of the U.S. Sections of the Combustion Institute, Philadelphia, PA, March 2005. 6 p. Abstract: http://ci.confex.com/ci/2005/techprogram/P1370.HTM , Mar-2005 Papers from Meeting Proceedings Sun Z, Axelbaum RK, Chao B-H, Santa KJ, Sunderland PB, Urban DL, Stocker DP. ""Numerical and Experimental Observations of Spherical Diffusion Flames."" Presented at 4th Joint Meeting of the U.S. Sections of the Combustion Institute, Philadelphia, PA, March 2005. 4th Joint Meeting of the U.S. Sections of the Combustion Institute, Philadelphia, PA, March 2005. 6 p. Abstract: http://ci.confex.com/ci/2005/techprogram/P1900.HTM , Mar-2005 Page 3 of 3 Task Book Report Generated on: 02/28/2024",,False
