PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p42,0,ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 42 Figure 44. Velocity profiles 0.5 mm downstream of the coflow burner’s inner tube with mismatched inner and annular velocities respectively intended to be 50 and 10 cm/s. Figure 45. Velocity profiles 0.5 mm downstream of the coflow burner’s inner tube with mismatched inner and annular velocities respectively intended to be 50 and 30 cm/s.,,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p43,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 43 As previously mentioned, additional measurements and information about the pre- launch characterization of the coflow burner can be found in the Dec. 2016 document titled ACME CLD burner hot wire anemometry measurements. 3. Spherical Burners The spherical burners have thus far been used for the CFI-G, Flame Design, and s-Flame experiments. In contrast to initial plans, spaceflight burners were only fabricated with a 6.35-mm porous sphere. It was initially expected that flames which were more spherical might be generated with larger diameter porous spheres, but 2.2-s drop testing indicated that 6.35-mm diameter spheres were as good or better than the larger sizes in creating spherical flames. Multiple copies of the spherical burner were launched to the ISS because of concerns that they might be damaged and/or might prove to yield flames which aren’t sufficiently spherically symmetric. The porous spheres were fabricated from sintering 316 stainless steel with 10-micron media grade, for which the porosity is approximately 41% and the density is 4.6 g/cm3. More specifically, each sphere was formed by sintering together sintered disks, where the supply tube should extend to the sphere’s center. Neglecting the interior of the supply tube, there is presumably no cavity within the porous sphere.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p43,1,"Each sphere was furthermore sinter-bonded to its stainless-steel supply tube. As a side effect, the tubing lost its temper and became very pliable because of the high temperature required for the bonding. Therefore, a close-fitting support tube, with an outer diameter of 1.8 mm, was added to the design. It doesn’t quite extend to the porous sphere as can be seen in Figure 46. The gap between the support tube and the burner is often obvious in flame imaging because the supply tube is more reflective than the support tube. Each burner was equipped with an extremely fine, sheathed, type-K thermocouple which was embedded as much as ~1 mm into the porous sphere. The thermocouple hole was 0.76 to 1.0 mm deep (and 0.25-0.30 mm in diameter) where the thermocouple was meant to be inserted fully, i.e., until reaching the hole’s bottom, but that may not be its exact position. The junction is of course not at the extreme end of the sheathed thermocouple, but it is very fine and presumably close. If the porous sphere is imagined as a globe with the supply tube as the south pole, the thermocouple is inserted in the lower hemisphere, between the equator and pole as seen in Figure 46.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p44,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 44 Figure 46. A spherical burner, prior to launch, showing the positioning of the slightly embedded thermocouple. The gap between the support tube and porous sintered sphere can also be seen. The white cable with the black connector on the left is for grounding, while the yellow connector is for the type-K thermocouple. The spherical burners were first used for the Flame Design experiment, where the first flame tests were conducted on 25 April 2019. However, after several days of testing it was concluded by the investigators that the flames were insufficiently symmetric at high burner flow rates to meet the science objectives. At the high flow rates, the flame ceased to be spherically symmetric and its center didn’t coincide with the burner’s center. The results indicate that the sintered attachment of the sphere to the supply tube was more porous than the sintered sphere itself, where the flame was shifted toward the supply tube. Therefore, the spherical burner was replaced on 14 June 2019 where the shift was from serial number 1002 to 1003. The subsequent burner (s/n 1003) showed similar behavior, but to a significantly reduced degree. As a result, some of the early Flame Design testing was repeated after the burner swap. This burner (s/n 1003) has thus far been used for all subsequent spherical flame testing including the tests for the s-Flame and CFI- G experiments.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p45,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 45 4. BRE Burners In terms of both size and instrumentation, the two BRE burners are significantly different than the other existing ACME burners. The outlet diameters of the two burners are 25 and 50 mm, where the latter burner is shown in Figure 47. The overall burner diameters are even larger, for example because each burner is encompassed with 6 mm of ceramic insulation. Figure 47. The 50-mm BRE burner prior to launch. The white cable with the black connector on the right is for grounding, while the yellow connector on the left was used to secure the cable for the burner thermocouple, where the measurements from the BRE burner’s thermocouples and heat flux transducers were transmitted through the black cables. The two heat flux transducers can be seen in the perforated outlet plate as black ‘circles.’ The two light dots in the same ‘row’ as the two transducers are the tips of embedded thermocouples.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p46,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 46 Figure 48. Assembly schematic for the 25-mm BRE burner, a detail from drawing S1101MFA1245. The dimensions for the ceramic insulation are in inches. While the 25-mm BRE burner, depicted in Figure 48, has an outlet diameter corresponding to the coflow burner, it is nonetheless larger and more complex. The perforated copper outlet plate of each BRE burner is 5.3 mm thick and has two sheathed type-K thermocouples (0.25 mm in diameter) embedded so their tips are at the blackened outer surface. This design allows the outlet plate to be used for calorimetric measurements of the burner heating by the flame. In this regard, note that the BRE burners - including their instrumentation - are not water-cooled, where that was not",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p47,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 47 feasible within ACME’s constraints. For flow control, each BRE burner has a ceramic honeycomb, 25 mm thick, upstream of the outlet plate. Each ceramic element is sandwiched between two layers of perforated brass which are 0.4 mm thick and have staggered holes which are 0.4 mm in diameter yielding a 22% open area. The perforated brass is visible in Figure 49. Figure 49. The 50-mm BRE burner with a coil-type igniter tip inserted above it during set- up by astronaut Chris Cassidy on 27 Aug. 2020. The center and offset heat flux transducers, which include thermocouples, are within openings in the perforated outlet plate and pointed out by green arrows. The tips of two additional thermocouples are visible as faint dots between holes in the black outlet below the transducers, as pointed out by the red arrows. [Cropped detail from ISS063e078811] As seen in Figure 49, the BRE burners are each equipped with two Medtherm heat flux transducers, of the Schmidt-Boelter type, which each include a type-T thermocouple. The Medtherm calibrations for the four transducers are included in Figures 50-53. The sensors pass through the holes in the outlet plate, where one is in burner’s center and the other is offset, where their radial positions are indicated in Table 4. The 25- and 50-mm burners respectively have sensors with outer diameters of 1/16 and 1/8 inch, where the model and serial numbers are also provided in the table.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p48,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 48 Figure 50. The Medtherm calibration for the center Schmidt-Boelter heat flux transducer (model 4-10SB- 2.688-0.43-36-22066KSA, serial number 190508) in the spaceflight copy of the 25-mm BRE burner.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p49,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 49 Figure 51. The Medtherm calibration for the offset Schmidt-Boelter heat flux transducer (model 4-10SB- 2.688-0.43-36-22066KSA, serial number 190507) in the spaceflight copy of the 25-mm BRE burner.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p50,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 50 Figure 52. The Medtherm calibration for the center Schmidt-Boelter heat flux transducer (model 8-2.688- 10SB-0-0-36-21427KSA, serial number 190503) in the spaceflight copy of the 50-mm BRE burner.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p51,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 51 Figure 53. The Medtherm calibration for the offset Schmidt-Boelter heat flux transducer (model 8-2.688- 10SB-0-0-36-21427KSA, serial number 190504) in the spaceflight copy of the 50-mm BRE burner.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p52,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 52 Table 4. Radial positions of BRE burner instrumentation BRE burner Thermocouples, type-K (qty 2) Medtherm heat flux transducers, including a type-T thermocouple (qty 2) 25-mm 4.72 mm, 10.4 mm 0 mm (s/n 190508), 9.46 mm (s/n 190507) (model 4-10SB-2.688-0.43-36-22066KSA) 50-mm 5.72 mm, 13.3 mm 0 mm (s/n 190503), 15.2 mm (s/n 190504) (model 8-2.688-10SB-0-0-36-21427KSA) BRE was a late addition to the ACME project and as a result it had to adapt to existing engineering design. As an important example, the BRE burner thermocouples were connected as if the burner was an alternate to the thermocouple rakes discussed in section 3.G. This is important because each of the rakes have 2 type-K thermocouples and 2 type-B thermocouples. Furthermore, the BRE wiring wasn’t optimized to minimize mixed connections. The thermocouples and wiring are described in Table 5, where accurate measurements from the thermocouples will require accounting for the mismatched connections. Table 5. Mismatched BRE thermocouple wiring (presented in the top-to-bottom order corresponding to Figures 49 and 54) BRE burner position Thermocouple type Wiring type Offset heat flux transducer T K (mismatched) Center heat flux transducer T B (mismatched) South thermocouple K K (matched) Far south thermocouple K B (mismatched)",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p52,1,"The flow from the two spaceflight BRE burners was characterized at the University of Maryland prior to the burners’ launch. Flow profiles were measured 10 mm downstream from the burner outlet along four lines across the burner surface, namely N-S, NE-SW, E- W, and SE-NW, where the offset heat flux transducer is to the north (N) as shown in the subsequent figure. The flow profiles were made for the 25- and 50-mm burners with 3 and 12 slpm of nitrogen, respectively. At these flow rates, it can be seen in Figures 55 and 56, respectively, that flow from both burners was nominally axisymmetric. While it was nominally flat across the center of the 25-mm burner, the characterized flow was significantly depressed in the center of the 50-mm burner. However, the 50-mm burner was only used on 2 of the 17 days in which BRE flames were ignited. Furthermore, the 50-mm burner flow (i.e., of both fuel and nitrogen, if any) in the spaceflight testing was always less than 0.5 slpm total (on a nitrogen basis), in great contrast to the 12 slpm used in the characterization. As such, it is likely that the flow profile was more uniform than that seen in Figure 56.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p53,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 53 Figure 54. Drawing detail showing the arrangement of the four lines, N-S, NE-SW, E-W, and SE-NW, along which flow profiles were measured prior to launch. In this arrangement, the offset heat flux transducer is to the north (N), while the two separate thermocouples are to the south (S). Figure 55. Flow profiles measured for the spaceflight copy of the 25-mm burner.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p54,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 54 Figure 56. Flow profiles measured for the spaceflight copy of the 50-mm burner. D. Igniter Tips Two types of crew-exchangeable igniter tips have been used during ACME testing. Most tests were conducted with a tip featuring a simple loop of Kanthal A-1 wire (0.25 mm in diameter) which was resistively heated with specification of the voltage and duration. A pre-heat voltage and duration, e.g., prior to burner flow, can also be specified. The second type features a small coil of Kanthal A-1 wire (0.32 mm in diameter), where it was used in the final six s-Flame test days (in June-July 2020) and throughout the second round of BRE testing (in Sept. 2020 to Jan. 2021). The coil igniter tip is also being used in the CFI-G investigation. Figure 57. A standard ACME igniter tip prior to launch. The igniter tip’s white ceramic insulator is 4.75 mm in diameter and the pin-to-pin spacing (as measured from their centers) is 2 mm.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p55,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 55 Figure 58. Coil type igniter from details in drawing S1101MFA1339, where the dimensions are in inches. E. Optical Layout This section graphically presents the layout of the cameras and non-imaging optical sensors. Note that the Low Light-Level Ultraviolet (LLL-UV) camera was only used for the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment. Figure 59: Full layout of the cameras and optical instrumentation, showing the location of the Fuel and Oxidizer Management Assembly (FOMA) control unit, avionics package, Low Light Level Ultraviolet (LLL-UV) camera, High Bit-depth Multispectral (HiBMs) camera and the opposing illumination package, the color ACME Data Camera (DataCam), the Image Processing and Storage Unit for Gigabit ethernet (IPSU-G) cameras (IPSU-G), the two standard IPSUs, the Cool Flame Investigation (CFI) intensified cameras, the internal window covers (as installed when not using the LLL-UV camera), array of three Photomultiplier Tubes (PMTs), analog operations camera, and the array of radiometers (simply marked ‘R’).",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p56,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 56 Figure 60. Optical arrangement for all ACME investigations except the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment. Figure 61. Optical arrangement for ACME’s Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment, with the important exception that the CFI intensified camera was used for s-Flame and was not blocked with an internal window cover.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p57,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 57 Figure 62. Solid model representation of the chamber insert as seen looking from the Low Light Level Ultraviolet (LLL-UV) camera, which has only been used for the s-Flame experiment. Similarly, the thermocouple rake, of which there are two versions, was only used for spherical flame experiments. To be clear, this figure is not meant to represent the camera’s Field of View (FOV).",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p58,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 58 Figure 63. Solid model representation of the chamber insert as seen looking from the direction of the High Bit-depth Multispectral (HiBMs) camera toward the opposing illumination package. Close inspection will reveal that the illumination was significantly blocked in the E-FIELD Flames experiment by the bracket for the electrode mesh. The radiometer array is to the left of the burner and igniter arm (when inserted). To be clear, this figure is not meant to represent the camera’s Field of View (FOV).",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p59,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 59 Figure 64. Solid model representation of the chamber insert as seen looking from the ACME Data Camera (DataCam). The thermocouple rake, of which there are two versions, was only used for the spherical flame experiments. To be clear, this figure is not meant to represent the camera’s Field of View (FOV).",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p60,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 60 Figure 65. Solid model representation of the chamber insert as seen looking from the Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) intensified camera. The thermocouple rake, of which there are two versions, was only used for the spherical flame experiments. To be clear, this figure is not meant to represent the camera’s Field of View (FOV).",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p61,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 61 F. Radiometer Fields of View Detailed information about the ACME radiometers can be found in the following documents. ACME-RPT-011 Radiometer Characterization Report ACME-RPT-012 BRE Radiometer Characterization Report CIR-RPT-4600 Cool Flames Radiometer Characterization Report where the BRE radiometer array was only used in the first round of that experiment. However, the Fields of View (FOVs) are not clearly presented in those reports, so they are shown graphically in this section for the standard, offset, and BRE radiometers, but not the CFI-G radiometer array. Note that the ACME burners vary in length, where the subsequent figures show a spherical burner, for which the porous sphere should be nominally centered at the intersection of the CIR window axes, the Photomultiplier Tube (PMT) FOVs, and standard radiometer array’s central radiometer. The other ACME burners were shorter in length so that the burner-stabilized flames would be approximately centered relative to most of the optical instrumentation. More specifically, the outlets of the existing BRE, coflow, and gas-jet spaceflight burners are respectively about 10, 12, and 10 mm ‘below’ (i.e., upstream) of plane defined by the axes of CIR chamber windows.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p61,1,"Figure 66. Representation of the FOVs of the five radiometers in the standard linear array. The thermopile detectors are of the same make and model, but the FOV is restricted by an additional aperture (not a lens) in front of each of the four outer radiometers.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p62,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 62 Figure 67. Representation of the offset radiometer’s FOV, where that detector is always present. It was primarily intended to support the experiments with spherical flames and features a thermopile detector with a faster response than the other radiometers. Figure 68. Representation of the FOVs for the Burning Rate Emulator (BRE) radiometer array, which was used in place of the standard radiometer array during BRE’s first round of testing.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p63,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 63 G. Thermocouples The thermocouple rakes, of which there are two versions with different probe lengths (referred to as far and near or alternatively short and long), were developed for ACME’s spherical flame experiments. The four thermocouple tips on each rake are nominally at the distances below from the center of the burner’s porous sphere Far/short 45, 60, 100, 100 mm Near/long 13, 25, 50, 100 mm In the far/short case, the two thermocouples at 100 mm distance are in orthogonal directions from the spherical burner. Figure 69. Thermocouple rake dimensions. The size of the exposed thermocouple beads was not measured, but it should be at least twice the 0.2-mm wire diameter. The two innermost thermocouples (i.e., closest to the spherical burner) on each rake are type-B, while the outer thermocouples are type-K. In contrast, only the two thermocouples at 13 and 25 mm were intended to be type B, where this discrepancy is documented in deviation ACME-DEV- 026. There are also two separate thermocouples which are each at nominally 200 mm distance from the center of the spherical burner where these have thus far been always installed. However, they are located far upstream of the burner outlets and as such will be of limited value for experiments with the other burner types.",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p63,1,"Thermocouple 2 (at 200 mm, on the relay side) was reported to be bent by about 10 degrees on 3 April 2018, about 10-15 degrees on 22 May 2018, and about 30 degrees on 19 June 2018. The next three reports indicated the same bend, and the procedures were changed to cover the tip",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p64,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 64 during crew operations. Photographs, where an example is shown in Figure 70, and crew inspection indicate that has remained bent about 30 degrees since that time. Given the 5 mm length of the bent exposed thermocouple tip, the effect on the 200 mm distance to the spherical burner center is small. Figure 70. Bent relay-side thermocouple, meant to be 200 mm from the center of the spherical burner’s porous sphere, as seen on 19 Sept. 2018 when the chamber insert was reconfigured for the second round of E-FIELD Flames testing. The bent length of exposed wire is 5 mm long. [ISS056e177654] 4. Engineering Documents This section identifies a variety of engineering documents, some of which are relevant to interpreting ACME experiment results. Section A is broad in scope while B is limited to deviations and waivers. A. General Documents A list of general engineering documents including analyses, inspections, plans, procedures, requirements, tests, verifications, etc. is included in this section, where only a portion of these documents is available through the Physical Sciences Informatics Systems (PSI) website, http://psi.nasa.gov/. To avoid unnecessary repetition, only the latest revision for each document has been listed. The great majority of documents subsequently listed are not required for interpreting the experimental results. But some can be critically important, where the following are some",,False
PSI-10,Engineering Documents,PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Engineering Documents/PSI-10_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p65,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 65 examples: ACME-RPT-010 A Data Camera Characterization Report ACME-RPT-011 - Radiometer Characterization Report ACME-RPT-012 - BRE Radiometer Characterization Report ACME-RPT-013 - PMT Characterization Report Understanding the design requirements may also provide to be useful, especially in comparison with the verification documents listed in this section and the deviation and waiver documents discussed in the subsequent section. Example documents that may be helpful in this regard include: ACME-DOC-002 D REQUIREMENTS COMPLIANCE MATRIX ACME-DOC-009 - BRE Requirements Compliance Matrix ACME-REQ-001 K Engineering Requirements Document For interpretation of the experimental results, the most important verification reports are the two listed below. For example, key burner dimensions are provided in ACME-VER-001. Meanwhile, an informal document ACME Science Requirement Verifications (where the file is named ACME_science_reqt_verifications_...) indicates, at least in part, how science requirements specified in the ACME Integrated Science Requirements (ISRD) were verified and in what documents they are addressed. ACME-VER-001 A ACME Inspection Verification Report ACME-VER-002 B ACME ERD Verification Report",,False
