PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,p105,0,"ACME-SRD-001.B Integrated Science Requirements Document (ISRD) 2013-SEPT-30 105 Burner outlet plate This section describes some of the methodology in designing the perforated, copper outlet plate. Copper plate properties 3 62 8.95 0.383 111 10 g/cm , plate density J/g‐K, plate heat capacity , plate thickness m/s , hole opening area proportion c l        Let dh=1/8”=3.175 mm for 50 mm burner dh=1/16”=1.59 mm for 25 mm burner Number of holes on the plate (d: diameter of the plate, dh: diameter of the hole) 2(/ )hNdd  l (mm) N_25 N_50 0.2 5.6 50 50 0.3 6.4 75 75 0.4 7.5 100 100 0.5 9 125 125 3(1 ) (1 0.4) 8950 0.383 7.5 10 15.43cl  Time for heat penetration 232 6 (7.5 10 ) 0.5111 10 penetrate lt k c     sec Reynolds number 25 g/m ‐sm  5 mm/savgvm /15 mm/shole avgvv For 50 mm burner: 2 (1.5 )(0.3175 )Re 2.98(0.16 ) cm/s cm cm /s 50 mm top plate example dh=1/8”=3.175 mm ds=5 mm N=69 =0.28",,False
PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,p106,0,"ACME-SRD-001.B Integrated Science Requirements Document (ISRD) 2013-SEPT-30 106 Heat Flux Gages The recommended Medtherm sensors are from their 64 Series: Heat Flux Transducers and Infrared Radiometers for the Direct Measurement of Heat Transfer Rates. The PI team uses the Schmidt-Boelter gage with a multi-junction thermop ile. This is important because of the goal that the sensor surface have a uniform temperatur e (in contrast to the alternative Gardon gage). A thermocouple also measures the temperature of the sensor. There is no transmitting window or aperture. The Medtherm thermopile gage has a nominal absorptance of 0.95 over a spectral range of 0.6 to 15.0 µm. Currently, the PI team uses 10 mV output at a full range of 100 kW/m 2. Recent drop tests indicate an in itial heat flux of about 50 kW/m 2 at ignition and then generally dropping to values of about 5 kW/m 2. As the Medtherm sensors can easily over range by 150%, within their linear calibration, the 10 mV lim it can be exceeded. The sensors have reported maximum non-linearity of 2 % of full range and a repeatability of 0.5 %. They are calibrated with an uncertainty of 3 %. The sensor has a respons e time of less than 250 ms. For ground- based operation water-cooling is important, but circulating water is not needed during spaceflight. Tests by the PI team have shown that water condensation and sensor overheating are unlikely for the proposed flight test matrix.",,False
PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,p106,1,"The sensor thermocouple specification is sufficient for operation without water, provid ed the sensor does not exceed 600 ºF (316 ºC), which is the maximum (body) temperature for the Space Shuttle Flight Qualified sensors.",,False
PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,p107,0,"ACME-SRD-001.B Integrated Science Requirements Document (ISRD) 2013-SEPT-30 107 Figure 15. 1/8” heat flux gage as seen in Medtherm schematic. The models used in the ground experiment are from Medtherm. The 1/8” model for 50 mm burner is 8-1.8-10SB-4-0-36- 20425AT. It has 1/8” diameter, 1.8” long stem. The cylindrical body has ½-20 NF-2A thread to integrate with the burner and an O-ring to prevent the gas leakage from the interface. It is equipped with a T type thermocouple to measure the surface temperature at the sensor, although other thermocouple types are acceptable.",,False
PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,p108,0,"ACME-SRD-001.B Integrated Science Requirements Document (ISRD) 2013-SEPT-30 108 Figure 16. 1/8” heat flux gage as seen in Medt herm schematic. The 1/16” model for 25 mm burner is 4-10SB-1.4-0.43-4-36-21919T. It has 1/16” diameter, 1.4” long stem. The cylindrical body has ¼ -28 NF-2A thread to integrate with the burner. It is also equipped with a T type thermocouple to measure the surface temperat ure at the sensor, although other thermocouple types are acceptable. The O-ring is not included in this model but this option is recommended to seal the burner inlet plenum (i.e., upstream of the ceramic honeycomb). Surface Temperature Measurement The temperature of the outlet plate should be me asured as the burner surface temperature. To represent its distribution throughout the area, the thermocouples should be placed at two positions, close to or corresponding to where the heat flux sensors are located. In the early ground experiments, two K-type 0.01”diameter bare wire thermocouples were used, sewed into the screen. Thermocouples thermally bonded to th e outlet plate are needed for the spaceflight experiment.",,False
PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,p109,0,"ACME-SRD-001.B Integrated Science Requirements Document (ISRD) 2013-SEPT-30 109 Appendix E: BRE Radiometer Design Reference The radiometer array specified in the 3.6.8 is me ant to measure the radiation from the largest anticipated BRE flame, but exclude the burner radia tion in the case of the primary measurement at position Z0. DR BD BD 2BD 1Z 2Z 0Z Z presumed flame centroid    detector distance from burner axis burner diameter Three radiometers mounted on miniature mirror mounts with their FOVs covering the entire flame volume which presumably has its height and diameter equal to the burner diameter. FLAME BURNER Figure 17. BRE radiometer concept in which the primary sensor, at position Z0, is equipped with a straight “knife” edge (not shown) to blo ck its view of the entire burner, neglecting the penumbra. The primary sensor and secondary se nsors, where the latter are at positions Z 1 and Z2, are individually tilted so that all three see the flame, but where the secondary sensors also see the burner including the outlet surface. The angle of the conical FOV for each sensor is identical and is selected so as to minimize the FOV while still completely including the cylindrical zone representing the flame, where DB is the diameter of the larger, 50-mm burner.",,False
PSI-10,Science Documents,PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-10/Science Documents/PSI-10_Science Documents_ACME_ISRD.pdf,p110,0,"ACME-SRD-001.B Integrated Science Requirements Document (ISRD) 2013-SEPT-30 110 Figure 18. BRE radiometer concept, where a straight knife edge is used to block the burner from view in the otherwise conical FOV of the primary measurement. The clipped FOV’s cross-section is shown by the heavy dashed line in this image. Figure 19. BRE radiometer concept where the thick dashed lines represent the two planes – orthogonally in and out of the page - for which the radiometer FOVs are to be characterized. The horizontal plane is common with the chamber window cen ters, while the vertical plane passes through the chamber and burner axes and is perpendicular to the radiometers. For both planes, the characterization is to be conducted along parallel lines which are 10 mm apart as discussed in requirement 3.6.9.",,False
PSI-20,Experimental table,PSI-20_Experimental table_ACME BRE.csv,data/raw/files/PSI-20/Experimental table/PSI-20_Experimental table_ACME BRE.csv,all,0,sheet PSI-20_Experimental table_ACME BRE.csv: 19 non-empty rows header: Year | Month | Date | Burner Size (mm) | Fuel Bottle | Oxygen Conc (percent) | Flames Ignited | Notes row 1: 2019 | Feb | 8 | 25 | 100% C2H4 | 21; 26.5; 40 | 10 | Oxygen Conc. (as diluted with nitrogen on a volume basis) was varied between tests where approximate concentrations for tests that ignited are listed. The chamber pressures were also varied but are not identified in this summary table. row 2: 2019 | Feb | 20 | 25 | 100% C2H4 | 26.5; 34 | 8 | Flames Ignited is the number ignited on that day (neglecting the day change on ISS). row 3: 2019 | Feb | 25 | 25 | 50% C2H4 | 21; 26.5; 34; 40 | 8 | row 4: 2019 | March | 4 | 25 | 50% C2H4 | 26.5; 40 | 6 | row 5: 2019 | March | 7 | 25 | 50% C2H4 | 3400% | 6 |,,False
PSI-20,metadata,PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-20/metadata/PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,0,"ONTOLOGY SOURCE REFERENCE Term Source Name PSI EFO Term Source File https://psi.nasa.gov/ http://data.bioontology.org/ontologies/EFO Term Source Version 1.0 132 Term Source Description User defined terms Experimental Factor Ontology INVESTIGATION Investigation Identifier Investigation Title Investigation Description Investigation Submission Date Investigation Public Release Date INVESTIGATION PUBLICATIONS Investigation PubMed ID Investigation Publication DOI Investigation Publication Author List Investigation Publication Title Investigation Publication Status Investigation Publication Status Term Accession Number Investigation Publication Status Term Source REF INVESTIGATION CONTACTS Investigation Person Last Name Investigation Person First Name Investigation Person Mid Initials Investigation Person Email Investigation Person Phone Investigation Person Fax Investigation Person Address Investigation Person Affiliation Investigation Person Roles Investigation Person Roles Term Accession Number Investigation Person Roles Term Source REF STUDY Study Identifier PSI-20 Study Title Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments - Burning Rate Emulator Study Description Study Submission Date 15-Sep-2022 Study Public Release Date 15-Sep-2022 Study File Name s_PSI-20.txt Comment[Acknowledgments] Comment[Approach] ""To accomplish the above objectives, flight experiments are proposed.",,False
PSI-20,metadata,PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-20/metadata/PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,1,"Additionally, preliminary flight tests will be performed to verify the experiment operations and for comparison with ground-based tests. For each test, conditions will be established for the chamber contents, namely choice of diluent, oxygen concentration, and pressure. Fuel flow will commence followed by ignition. The flames will initially be observed without varying the fuel flow rate. Because burner surface temperature and heat flux will vary, this will allow a broad range of conditions to be observed during a single test. As the surface temperature of the burner increases, and the flame remains steady, a continuous set of data is produced for the flammability map. Eventually either the flame will extinguish, the surface temperature of the burner will equilibrate, or the temperature limit of the heat flux sensor will be reached. At this time the fuel flow will be terminated. So a test should terminate for a given flow rate at extinction, and for continued burning when the burner surface temperature has steadied or the temperature limit of the heat flux sensor is neared.",,False
PSI-20,metadata,PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-20/metadata/PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,2,"The extinction limit could be found once the burner temperature has steadied, and the heat flux sensor has not exceeded its limit. At that state, the flow rate can be reduced in small increments, until extinction is observed and the flow rate shut off. Again, in this process, new data is being produced for the flammability map. Ignition limit tests are not proposed for microgravity, but we will explore these under normal gravity. We believe there is better control on Earth-based ignition testing and that these results should carry over to microgravity conditions. In addition to the flight experiments, in normal gravity testing will continue. These tests will involve gaseous, liquid, and solid fuels. These experiments will further establish the validation of the BRE for 1-g applications and show its usefulness to establish 1-g ignition and extinction limits. As available, drop facility testing will be used to refine procedures, test instrumentation, and help to establish viable flow conditions.",,False
PSI-20,metadata,PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-20/metadata/PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,3,"We will also pursue a CFD model for the quiescent microgravity burning. This will also guide us in the flight experiments, and potentially provide a predictive methodology."" Comment[DOI] 10.60555/vb93-3y72 Comment[Data Source Accession] Comment[Data Source Link] Comment[Experiment Hardware] Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR), ACME Comment[Experiment Hardware Link] https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/, https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/combustion-science/acme/ Comment[Flight Platform] International Space Station (ISS) Comment[Funding] NNX10AD98G, NNX15AD06A, 80NSSC20M0119 Comment[Hypothesis] ""The BRE investigation hypothesizes that there can exist a regime of quasi-steady burning in microgravity as a function of materials properties. Steady burning may never be achieved, and only slowly changing unsteady flames are possible. The proposed tests aboard the ISS aims to query this hypothesis by establishing a range of quasi-steady burning conditions for condensed fuels in the microgravity environment. It is hypothesized that BRE can determine a range of burning conditions in microgravity as a function of heat of gasification, reradiation heat flux, applied radiant heat flux, heat of combustion and smoke point. This could enable the Cone Calorimeter (NASA Test 2) test as a basis for establishing flammability maps for real materials.",,False
PSI-20,metadata,PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-20/metadata/PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,4,"The key hypothesis is that the heat of combustion, smoke point, and heat of gasification of the fuel mixture will represent a real solid fuel (or liquid fuel absorbed in an inert matrix). Thus achieved steady burning would represent the potential for real solid fuels to achieve steady burning in microgravity. Other than a candle flame, this has not been shown before."" Comment[Identifiers] Comment[Investigation Acronym] ACME BRE Comment[Investigation End Date] 02-08-2025 Comment[Investigation Start Date] 01-01-2010 Comment[Managing NASA Center] Glenn Research Center (GRC) Comment[Mission End] Comment[Mission Link] Comment[Mission Name] Comment[Mission Start] Comment[Objective] ""The BRE flight program seeks to establish the burning conditions in a quiescent microgravity ambient in terms of material properties. The specific flight objectives are as follows. 1. Observe burning behavior in long-duration microgravity for CH4/N2 and C2H4/N2mixtures on porous circular burners of 25 and 50 mm diameter. Consider O2/N2 ambients with various pressures and ambient O2 concentrations and pressures. NASA - considered atmosphere for human space missions will be examined: 14.7 psia, 21 % oxygen: 10.2 psia, 26.5 %; and 8.2 psia, 34 % oxygen. Emphasize fuel conditions that can be related to a diverse range of condensed fuels. 2. Consider the conditions of Objective 1 for ambients of O2/CO2 (desired). 3.",,False
PSI-20,metadata,PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-20/metadata/PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,5,"Consider the conditions of Objectives 1 – 2 for extinction limits."" Comment[Project Identifier] Comment[Project Link] Comment[Project Title] Comment[Project Type] Microgravity Investigation Comment[Proposal Title] Experimental Investigation of Emulated Burning Rate at Various Gravity Levels Comment[Research Area] Combustion Science Comment[Research Impacts] BRE yielded important data relevant to spacecraft fire safety. Quasi-steady burning corresponding to various condensed-phase materials (such as PMMA and Nylon) was observed in microgravity despite the absence of air flow, where the cessation of ventilation is an established step in putting out spacecraft fires. The ongoing combustion without air flow was notably seen in elevated-oxygen atmospheres which are being considered for use in future spacecraft, whereas such flames in a normal atmosphere of 21% oxygen self-extinguished in a short time. While BRE’s results are still preliminary, they suggest that NASA may want to increase use of its alternative flammability Test 2, with the cone calorimeter, where that test can measure material properties corresponding to the microgravity flames observed with the BRE burner.",,False
PSI-20,metadata,PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-20/metadata/PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,6,"Comment[Space Program] National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) Comment[Study Grant Number] Comment[SubResearch Area] Flames of Gaseous Fuels STUDY DESIGN DESCRIPTORS Study Design Type Buoyancy ACME Diffusion Flames Extinction Limit Fire Safety Flammability Heat Flux Modeling Radiative Extinction Triple Flame Polymer Study Design Type Term Accession Number https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ Study Design Type Term Source REF PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI STUDY PUBLICATIONS Study PubMed ID Study Publication DOI 10.1177/0734904113495650 10.3850/978-981-07-5936-0_03-01 10.1016/j.combustflame.2021.01.035 10.1016/j.proci.2020.06.110 10.1002/fam.2896 10.1134/S0010508220040036 10.1016/j.combustflame.2019.09.030 10.1016/j.proci.2018.05.006 10.1016/j.firesaf.2019.02.003 10.1016/j.combustflame.2018.01.044 10.1016/j.firesaf.2016.11.003 10.1016/j.combustflame.2015.05.005 10.1016/j.combustflame.2023.112809 10.1016/j.proci.2022.08.107 10.1016/j.combustflame.2021.111572 Study Publication Author List Zhang Yi, Bustamante Michael J, Gollner Michael J, Sunderland Peter B, Quintiere James G Zhang Y., Bustamante M. J., Dotson K. T., Sunderland P. B., Quintiere J. G.",,False
PSI-20,metadata,PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-20/metadata/PSI-20_metadata_PSI-96_ACME BRE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,7,"MJ Bustamante P Dehghani, PB Sunderland, JG Quintiere, JL deRis Snegirev Alexander, Kuznetsov Egor, Markus Ekaterina, Dehghani Parham, Sunderland Peter Auth Eric, Quintiere James G., Sunderland Peter B. Kuznetsov E. A., Snegirev A. Yu., Markus E. S. Markan Akshit, Baum Howard R., Sunderland Peter B., Quintiere James G., de Ris John L. Markan Akshit, Sunderland Peter B., Quintiere James G., de Ris John L., Baum Howard R. Vermina Plathner Frida, Quintiere James G., van Hees Patrick Markan Akshit, Sunderland Peter B., Quintiere James G., de Ris John L., Stocker Dennis P., Baum Howard R. Lundström Frida Vermina, Sunderland Peter B., Quintiere James G., van Hees Patrick, de Ris John L. Zhang Yi, Kim Matt, Guo Haiqing, Sunderland Peter B., Quintiere James G., deRis John, Stocker Dennis P. Dehghani Parham, Quintiere James G. Dehghani Parham, de Ris John L., Quintiere James G. Dehghani Parham, Quintiere James G. Study Publication Title Burning on flat wicks at various orientations A Gas Burner to Emulate Condensed-Phase Burning Experimental Investigation of Liquid and Gas Fueled Flames Towards the Development of a Burning Rate Emulator (BRE) for Microgravity Applications Burning in microgravity:",,False
