PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,p7,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration 5 ACME Experiments 8 Universities (+NASA Glenn) and 13 Investigators • BRE – Burning Rate Emulator – PIs: James G Quintiere Peter B Sunderland (both U Maryland) PIs: James G. Quintiere, Peter B. Sunderland (both U. Maryland) • CLD Flame – Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame – PIs: Marshall Long, Mitchell Smooke (both Yale U.) g, ( ) • E-FIELD Flames – Electric-Field Effects on Laminar Diffusion Flames – PIs: Derek Dunn-Rankin (UC Irvine), Felix Weinberg (Imperial Cll ) Z G Y (NCSER t NASA Gl ) College) , Zeng-Guang Yuan (NCSER at NASA Glenn) • Flame Design - a novel approach to clean, efficient diffusion flames – PIs: Richard L. Axelbaum (Washington U. in St. Louis), Beei-Huan Chao (U. Hawaii), Peter B. Sunderland (U. Maryland), David L. (), (y), Urban (NASA Glenn) • s-Flame – Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames www.nasa.gov – PIs: C.K. Law (Princeton U.), Stephen D. Tse (Rutgers U.), Kurt R. Sacksteder (NASA Glenn) 7",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,p8,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration BRE • Burning Rate Emulator – Jim Quintiere & Peter Sunderland (both U. Maryland) • Concept – Flat-plate burner with closed-loop regulation of gaseous fuel flow based on heat flux to the burner i l ti th b ti f d d f l simulating the combustion of condensed fuels – Fuels to be simulated include paper, plastic, alcohols • Goals Qifhiii diibhif – Quantify the ignition and extinction behavior of condensed fuels in microgravity. – Qunatify the effects of burning at enhanced oxygen conditions in microgravity conditions in microgravity. • Why? – Spacecraft fire prevention through improved assessment for materials selection www.nasa.gov 8 assessment for materials selection",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,p9,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration CLD Flame Cae • Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame – Marshall Long & Mitchell Smooke (both Yale U.) • Concept – Lifted coflow (2D) flame, so little/no heat loss to burner • enabling improved modeling of the flame • Goals – Generation of modified kinetic mechanisms for hydrocarbon fuels that are able to model effectively diff i fl d l diffusion flame structure under a larger parameter range than existing mechanisms. – Development of submodels for soot formation that are capable of predicting both high and low soot loading capable of predicting both high and low soot loading levels in hydrocarbon flames of various fuels. • Why? – Improved design capability through validation of www.nasa.gov 9 Improved design capability through validation of combustion models over wider parameter range",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,p10,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration E-FIELD Flames • Electric-Field Effects on Laminar Diffusion Flames – Derek Dunn-Rankin (UC Irvine), Felix Weinberg (Imperial College London) Zeng Guang Yuan (NCSER (Imperial College, London), Zeng-Guang Yuan (NCSER at NASA Glenn) • Concept Gas jet or coflow (2D) flame with high voltage mesh (up 1g, 0 kV, gas-jet – Gas-jet or coflow (2D) flame with high-voltage mesh (up to ±10 kV) electrode downstream of (above) burner • Goal – Understand chemi-ionization behavior and the resulting – Understand chemi-ionization behavior and the resulting ion driven winds sufficiently well so that electrical properties of flames can be used to characterize (by monitoring ion current) and control them (via direct 0g, -0.6 kV, gas-jet chemical or local convective influences). • Why? – Advanced control capability enabling improved www.nasa.gov 10 combustion performance 0g, 0kv, 24 cm/s coflow",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,p11,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration Flame Design aeesg • A novel approach to clean, efficient diffusion flames – Rich Axelbaum (Washington U., St. Louis), Beei- Huan Chao (U Hawaii) Peter Sunderland (U 18% C2H4 into 27% O2 Huan Chao (U. Hawaii), Peter Sunderland (U. Maryland), David L. Urban (NASA Glenn) • Concept – Spherical (1D) flame with nitrogen exchange 18% C2H4 Intop() g g • Goals – Evaluate the effects of flame structure on soot inception and flame extinction Obtain a correlation of sooting limits with Into 28% O2 – Obtain a correlation of sooting limits with stoichiometric mixture fraction, Zst, and adiabatic temperature, Tad – Determine the importance of gas-phase oxidation on soot inception for high Zst flames 100% O2 into 12% C2H4 soot inception for high Zst flames – Demonstrate the existence of steady spherical flames – Identify pseudo-flammability limits for diffusion flames in terms of Zst and Tad 100% O2 into www.nasa.gov 11 • Why? – To reduce soot and NOx through nitrogen exchange into 13% C2H4",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,p12,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration s-Flame • Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames – C.K. Law (Princeton U.), Stephen D. Tse (Rutgers U.), Kurt R. Sacksteder (NASA Glenn) Sacksteder (NASA Glenn) • Concept – Spherical (1D) flame stabilized on a porous burner G • Goals – Use spherical diffusion flames in long-duration ISS environment to: bt i i t l d t i i l ll d fi d fl fi ld •obtain experimental data in simple, well-defined flow fields • understand various key elemental diffusion flame processes – Compare data with those obtained through independent computational simulation with detailed chemistry and transport computational simulation with detailed chemistry and transport to improve the accuracy and comprehensiveness of these descriptions • Why? flame lti www.nasa.gov 12 – Improvements in the design of practical, high-efficiency, low- emission combustion systems on Earth evolution with time",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,p13,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration ACME Approach pp • In general, ACME is not a technology demonstration, but is seeking to improve ,gp life on Earth via the path below. Improved understanding of Improved understanding of combustion fundamentals Improved numerical models ACME Improved numerical models of combustion processes Improved design tools Improved practical from ACME www.nasa.gov 13 Improved practical combustion devices ACME",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_AIAA presentation - ACME on the ISS Jan2012.pdf,p14,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration ACME Commonality y • All experiments are microgravity studies of flames that are: – Gas-fueled – Laminar • flow is smooth and not turbulent (i.e., it does not have swirling vortices) +1.75 kV not have swirling vortices) – Non-premixed (aka diffusion) • where the fuel and oxidizer (e.g., air) are on opposite sides of the reaction sheet where +2.00 kV pp they come together, react, and emit products of combustion (including heat and light) • Most are 1D (spherical) or 2D (jet) +2.25 kV www.nasa.gov 14",,False
