PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p20,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 20 Cmd ID Name Description Mnemonic 0xC510 ExtinctionDetectionSetActionSing leStep Set extinction action to a single step ACME_EXTINCTION_DETECTION _SET_ACTION_SINGLE_STEP 0xC511 ExtinctionDetectionSetActionSing leRamp Set extinction action to a single ramp ACME_EXTINCTION_DETECTION _SET_ACTION_SINGLE_RAMP 0xC512 ExtinctionDetectionSetActionStep ping Set extinction action to stepping ACME_EXTINCTION_DETECTION _SET_ACTION_STEPPING 0xC513 ExtinctionDetectionSetActionRam ping Set extinction action to ramping ACME_EXTINCTION_DETECTION _SET_ACTION_RAMPING 0xC514 DataRecordingSetSampleRate Change the period between data samples ACME_DATA_RECORDING_SAMPLE_RATE E-Field Group 0xC601 EFieldEnable Enable or disable E-Field ACME_EFIELD_ENABLE 0xC602 EFieldSetVoltage Set E-Field voltage ACME_EFIELD_SET_VOLTAGE 0xC603 EFieldSteppedSignal E-Field stepped signal ACME_EFIELD_STEPPED_SIGNAL 0xC604 EFieldRampedSignal E-Field ramped signal ACME_EFIELD_RAMPED_SIGNAL 0xC605 EFieldRectangularWave E-Field rectangular wave ACME_EFIELD_RECTANGULAR_WAVE 0xC606 EFieldRampedWave E-Field ramped wave ACME_EFIELD_RAMPED_WAVE,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p21,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 21 3.1.2 ACME Common Group Command Details These commands are called ‘common’ because they are implemented using code shared between several payloads. The detailed descriptions below are derived from the original documentation for these commands in Appendix K of FCF-ICD-0076, Rev C or later. As stated in that appendix the high order bytes of the command ID are replaced by the command group number assigned to ACME common commands, which is 0xC0. In that appendix the command IDs range from 0x0001 to 0x0038. For ACME they range from 0xC001 to 0xC038. 3.1.2.1 NullCommand The NullCommand does nothing. It can be used to test communication paths. Command ID: 0xC001 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_NULL 3.1.2.2 ExternalCommand The ExternalCommand command allows an avionics Main Processor (MP) CSCI to have no knowledge of this command and make use of the External Module feature. The command is passed to an External Module without intervention. This command enforces the requirement that an External Module requires a “module identifier” as the first parameter. The command group portion of the command ID, XX, depends on the module. Command ID: 0xXX02 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_EXTERNAL_PASS_THRU Parameter # Bytes Type Description moduleIdentifier 2 Uint16 Identifier (e.g. camera number 1,2) Command parameters 80 Area for module command-dependent parameters.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p21,1,3.1.2.3 GoToIdle The GoToIdle command requests an avionics MP CSCI to be set to the IDLE state. Command ID: 0xC003 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_STATE_IDLE 3.1.2.4 GoToExp The GoToExp command requests an avionics MP CSCI to be set to the EXPERIMENT state. Command ID: 0xC004 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_STATE_EXPERIMENT,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p22,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 22 3.1.2.5 GoToMaint The GoToMaint command requests an avionics MP CSCI to be set to the MAINTENANCE state. Command ID: 0xC005 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_STATE_MAINTENANCE 3.1.2.6 GoToUpDownLink The GoToUpDownLink command requests an avionics MP CSCI to be set to the UPLINK/DOWNLINK state. Command ID: 0xC006 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_STATE_UPLINK_DOWNLINK 3.1.2.7 GoToSafed The GoToSafed command requests an avionics MP CSCI to be set to the SAFED state. Command ID: 0xC007 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_STATE_SAFED 3.1.2.8 SetRegistryItem The SetRegistryItem command selects an item in the registry file, /payload/app/config/AcmeConfig.ini, for modification by the SetRegistryValue command. The full path including group is required. In Table 32, AcmeConfig.ini Contents, the full path is /Group/Key. Command ID: 0xC008 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON _REGISTRY_SET _ITEM Parameter # Bytes Type Description registryName 82 String /Group/Key in AcmeConfig.ini (e.g. /EField/OutputFactorA) 3.1.2.9 SetRegistryValue The SetRegistryValue command selects the key set via the SetRegistryItem command and modifies it with the supplied value. All software sub-systems are notified of the change and apply it used in the sub-system. This is just a temporary change. It must be saved with the SaveRegistry command to become permanent in the AcmeConfig.ini file. See Table 32. Command ID: 0xC009 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_REGISTRY_SET_VALUE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p23,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 23 Parameter # Bytes Type Description registryValue 82 String String value (e.g. [ip removed]) 3.1.2.10 SaveRegistry The SaveRegistry command dumps the “in memory” registry to /payload/app/config/AcmeConfig.ini. Command ID: 0xC00A Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_REGISTRY_SAVE 3.1.2.11 Reboot The Reboot command initiates an avionics MP CSCI reboot. The system must placed in the Safed state using the GoToSafed command prior to issuing this command. Command ID: 0xC00B Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_SYSTEM_REBOOT 3.1.2.12 Shutdown The Shutdown command initiates an avionics MP CSCI shutdown. The system must placed in the Safed state using the GoToSafed command prior to issuing this command. Command ID: 0xC00C Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_SYSTEM_SHUTDOWN 3.1.2.13 RunCli The RunCli command executes the given linux command in the command shell. CLI stands for command line interface. Command ID: 0xC00D Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_CLI_RUN Parameter # Bytes Type Description commandLine 82 String Linux shell command (e.g. ls /payload/app/bin),,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p24,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 24 3.1.2.14 AbortCli The AbortCli command terminates the command running in the shell started by the RunCli command. It terminates any child processes the command may have started and the shell itself. It restarts the shell (SH) with the /payload working directory as the current directory. Command ID: 0xC00E Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_CLI_ABORT 3.1.2.15 FtpLocalFile The FtpLocalFile command stores the path for later use with the FtpSendFile command. It may be an absolute file path or a relative file path. If a relative path is given it is combined with the absolute directory set via the FtpLocalDirectory command to form the complete path. Command ID: 0xC00F Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FTP_LOCAL_FILE Parameter # Bytes Type Description file 82 String Local path (e.g. /payload/app/config/myFile or app/config/myFile or myFile) 3.1.2.16 FtpLocalDirectory The FtpLocalDirectory command stores the path for later use with the FtpSendFile, FtpGetFile, FtpSendDirectory, and FtpGetDirectory commands. It must be an absolute directory path. If a relative path is provided in those commands the relative path is combined with this directory to form the complete path. Command ID: 0xC010 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FTP_LOCAL_DIR Parameter # Bytes Type Description directory 82 String Local directory (e.g. /payload/app/config)",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p24,1,3.1.2.17 FtpRemoteFile The FtpRemoteFile command stores the path for later use with the FtpGetFile command. It may be an absolute file path or a relative file path. If a relative path is given it is combined with the absolute directory set via the FtpRemoteDirectory command to form the complete path. Command ID: 0xC011 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FTP_REMOTE_FILE,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p25,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 25 Parameter # Bytes Type Description file 82 String Remote path (e.g. /payload/app/config/myFile or app/config/myFile or myFile) 3.1.2.18 FtpRemoteDirectory The FtpRemoteDirectory command stores the path for later use with the FtpSendFile, FtpGetFile, FtpSendDirectory, and FtpGetDirectory commands. It must be an absolute directory path. If a relative path is given in those commands the relative path is combined with this directory to form the complete path. Command ID: 0xC012 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FTP_REMOTE_DIR Parameter # Bytes Type Description directory 82 String Remote directory (e.g. /payload/app/config) 3.1.2.19 FtpLoginParameters The FtpLoginParameters command stores the information required to FTP files to/from a remote machine for later use with the FtpSendFile, FtpGetFile, FtpSendDirectory, and FtpGetDirectory commands. Command ID: 0xC013 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FTP_LOGIN_PARAMETERS Parameter # Bytes Type Description hostname 25 String Remote machine IP or hostname user 25 String Username password 25 String Password 3.1.2.20 FtpAbort The FtpAbort command aborts the current FTP transfer. Command ID: 0xC014 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FTP_ABORT",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p26,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 26 3.1.2.21 FtpSendFile The FtpSendFile command uses the information set via the FtpLocalFile, FtpLocalDirectory, and FtpRemoteDirectory commands. If an absolute path is provided in the FtpLocalFile command the result of the FtpLocalDirectory command is ignored, otherwise the two are combined to form an absolute file path for the source. The destination is provided as an absolute path from the FtpRemoteDirectory command. The file is sent from the local machine to the remote machine. Command ID: 0xC015 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FTP_SEND_FILE 3.1.2.22 FtpSendDirectory The FtpSendDirectory command uses the absolute paths set via the FtpLocalDirectory command and the FtpRemoteDirectory command to copy a directory recursively from the local machine to the remote machine. Command ID: 0xC016 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FTP_SEND_DIRECTORY 3.1.2.23 FtpGetFile The FtpGetFile command uses the information set via the FtpRemoteFile and FtpRemoteDirectory commands and the FtpLocalDirectory command. If an absolute path is provided in the FtpRemoteFile command the result of the FtpRemoteDirectory command is ignored, otherwise the two are combined to form an absolute file path for the source. The destination is provided as an absolute path from the FtpLocalDirectory command. The file is sent from the remote machine to the local machine. Command ID: 0xC017 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FTP_GET_FILE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p26,1,3.1.2.24 FtpGetDirectory The FtpGetDirectory command uses the absolute paths set via the FtpRemoteDirectory command and FtpLocalDirectory commands to copy a directory recursively from the remote machine to the local machine. Command ID: 0xC018 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FTP_GET_DIRECTORY 3.1.2.25 ChangeDirectory The ChangeDirectory (linux ‘cd’) command moves the notion of the current directory for the common filesystem commands to the given path. The path may be an absolute path or a relative,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p27,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 27 path. Relative path ChangeDirectory commands can be chained to traverse a deep directory structure. The path must exist for the command to successfully complete. Command ID: 0xC019 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_CHANGE_DIR Parameter # Bytes Type Description path 82 String Directory path 3.1.2.26 MakeDirectory The MakeDirectory (linux ‘mkdir -P’) command creates a new directory(s). The path may be an absolute path or a relative path. All parent directories necessary to create the full path are made. If a relative path is given it is combined with the current working directory from a single or series of ChangeDirectory commands to form the complete path. Command ID: 0xC01A Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_MAKE_DIR Parameter # Bytes Type Description path 82 String Directory path (e.g. /tmp/a/b/c or a/b/c) 3.1.2.27 RemoveSingleEmptyDirectory The RemoveSingleEmptyDirectory (linux ‘rmdir’) command removes a directory. The given directory path must be empty for the command to succeed. The path may be an absolute path or a relative path. If a relative path is given it is combined with the current working directory from a single or series of ChangeDirectory commands to form the complete path. Command ID: 0xC01B Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_REMOVE_SINGLE_EMPTY_DIR Parameter # Bytes Type Description path 82 String Directory path,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p27,1,"3.1.2.28 RemoveMultipleEmptyDirectories The RemoveMultipleEmptyDirectories (linux ‘rmdir -p’) command removes all parent directories in the path, provided they are empty. The path may be an absolute path or a relative path. If a relative path is given it is combined with the current working directory from a single or series of ChangeDirectory commands to form a complete path. This command is the opposite of the",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p28,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 28 MakeDirectory command. This command performs actions to the best of its ability and will stop if/when it finds a non-empty directory. Actions completed to that point are not rolled back. Command ID: 0xC01C Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_REMOVE_MULTIPLE_EMPTY_DIRS Parameter # Bytes Type Description path 82 String Directory path 3.1.2.29 MoveFiles The MoveFiles (linux ‘mv SRC DIR’) command moves SRC(s) to DIR. Source directory is set via the SetSource command and the destination directory is set via the SetDestination command. Wildcards * (any number of unknown characters) and ? (one unknown character) may be used in the filter. A blank filter or a filter consisting of a single * is interpreted as all files. If a single file move is desired, give filenames in the source and destination. A better solution is to use the RenameFile command. If moving an entire directory tree is desired, use the RenameDirectory command. Command ID: 0xC01D Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_MOVE_FILES Parameter # Bytes Type Description filter 82 String Name filter (e.g. *.jpeg or image?003.jpeg) 3.1.2.30 RenameFile The RenameFile command renames a source file set via the SetSource command to a destination file set via the SetDestination command. Command ID: 0xC01E Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_RENAME_FILE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p28,1,3.1.2.31 RenameDirectory The RenameDirectory command renames a source directory set via the SetSource command to a destination directory set via the SetDestination command. Command ID: 0xC01F Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_RENAME_DIR,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p29,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 29 3.1.2.32 RemoveFiles The RemoveFiles (linux ‘rm’) command removes files from a directory. Source directory is set via the SetSource command. Wildcards * (any number of unknown characters) and ? (one unknown character) may be used in the filter. A blank filter or a filter consisting of a single * is interpreted as all files. If a single file remove is desired, give a filename in the source. Command ID: 0xC020 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_REMOVE_FILES Parameter # Bytes Type Description filter 82 String Name filter (e.g. *.jpeg or image?003.jpeg) 3.1.2.33 RecursiveRemoveFiles The RecursiveRemoveFiles (linux ‘rm –r’) command removes directories and their contents recursively. The source directory is set via the SetSource command. Command ID: 0xC021 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS _REMOVE_FILES_RECURSIVE 3.1.2.34 CopyFiles The CopyFiles (linux ‘cp SRC DIR’) command copies the SRC(s) to DIR. Source is set via the SetSource command and the destination is set via the SetDestination command. Wildcards * (any number of unknown characters) and ? (one unknown character) may be used in the filter. A blank filter or a filter consisting of a single * is interpreted as all files. If a single file copy is desired, give filenames in the source and destination. File timestamps may be preserved by setting the preserve flag. Command ID: 0xC022 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_COPY_FILES",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p29,1,"Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description preserve 1 Uint8 0 = No 1 = Yes Preserve original time stamp filter 81 String Name filter (e.g. *.jpeg or image?003.jpeg) 3.1.2.35 RecursiveCopyFiles The RecursiveCopyFiles (linux ‘cp –r SRC DIR’) command copies the directory set via the SetSource command to the destination directory set via the SetDestination command. The destination directory, which is created, will contain the target directory. The destination directory",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p30,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 30 must not exist for this command to succeed. File timestamps may be preserved by setting the preserve flag. Command ID: 0xC023 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS _COPY_FILES_RECURSIVE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description preserve 1 Uint8 0 = No 1 = Yes Preserve original time stamp 3.1.2.36 ListFileInfo The ListFileInfo (linux ‘ls –a –l –r –t –R’) command lists file information for the source directory set via the SetSource command. Wildcards * (any number of unknown characters) and ? (one unknown character) may be used in the filter. A blank filter or a filter consisting of a single * is interpreted as all files. Command ID: 0xC024 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_LIST_FILES Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description all 1 Uint8 0 = No 1 = Yes Include hidden files. (Those beginning with .) (ls –a option) crc 1 Uint8 Display SHA256 of file. longformat 1 Uint8 0 = No 1 = Yes Long format (which includes file permissions) (ls –l option) reverse 1 Uint8 0 = No 1 = Yes Sort by reverse time. (ls –r option) sortByTime 1 Uint8 0 = No 1 = Yes Sort by time. (ls –t option) Recursive 1 Uint8 0 = No 1 = Yes Recursive. (ls –R option) afterDate 4 time_t 0=don’t use List only file after this date, inclusive. beforeDate 4 time_t 0=don’t use List only file before this date, inclusive. filter 68 String Name filter (e.g. *.jpeg or image?003.jpeg)",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p30,1,"3.1.2.37 SetSource The SetSource command provides the source for filesystem commands. The path may be an absolute path or a relative path. If a relative path is given it is combined with the current working directory from a single or series of ChangeDirectory commands. In some cases (COPY/MOVE/REMOVE) for a single file action, a filename may be included.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p31,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 31 Command ID: 0xC025 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_SET_SOURCE Parameter # Bytes Type Description path 82 String Source path (e.g. /payload/app/config/IpsugConfig.ini or app/config or /payload/app/config) 3.1.2.38 SetDestination The SetDestination command provides the destination for filesystem commands. The path may be an absolute path or a relative path. If a relative path is given it is combined with the current working directory from a single or series of ChangeDirectory commands to form a complete path. In some cases (COPY/MOVE/REMOVE) for a single file action, a filename may be included. Command ID: 0xC026 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_SET_DESTINATION Parameter # Bytes Type Description path 82 String Destination path (e.g. /payload/app/config/IpsugConfig.ini or app/config or /payload/app/config) 3.1.2.39 CompareCrcTwoFiles The CompareCrcTwoFiles command performs a SHA256 (Secure Hash Algorithm) on two files and compares the values. Source directory is set via the SetSource command and the destination directory is set via the SetDestination command. If a relative path is given for either source or destination it is combined with the current working directory from a single or series of ChangeDirectory commands to form the complete path. The file1 specified in the command is combined with the calculated source path and file2 is combined with the calculated destination path. Command ID: 0xC027 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_COMPARE_CRC_TWO_FILES",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p31,1,Parameter # Bytes Type Description file1 41 String File 1 filename file2 41 String File 2 filename,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p32,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 32 3.1.2.40 AbortFilesystemCmd The AbortFilesystemCmd Command command aborts the currently running filesystem command. Command ID: 0xC028 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FS_ABORT_FS_COMMAND 3.1.2.41 RunBashScript The RunBashScript command initiates execution of a BASH script. The path may be an absolute path or a relative path. If a relative path is given it is combined with the absolute directory set via the SetBashScriptDirectory command to form the complete path All bash scripts reside in /payloald/app/scripts/shell. Command ID: 0xC029 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_BASH_SCRIPT_RUN Parameter # Bytes Type Description script 82 String Bash Script path (e.g. /payload/app/scripts/shell/myScript.sh or myScript.sh) 3.1.2.42 SetBashScriptDirectory The SetBashScriptDirectory command specifies a source directory of a BASH script. An absolute path must be provided. This path is used to form the complete path if a relative path is provided in the RunBashScript command. All bash scripts reside in /payload/app/scripts/shell. Command ID: 0xC02A Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_BASH_SCRIPT_DIRECTORY Parameter # Bytes Type Description directory 82 String Bash Script directory path 3.1.2.43 AbortBashScript The AbortBashScript command terminates the command running in the SH shell started by the RunBashScript command. It terminates any child processes the command may have started and the shell itself. Command ID: 0xC02B Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_BASH_SCRIPT_ABORT,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p33,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 33 3.1.2.44 SetScriptDirectory The SetScriptDirectory command specifies a source directory for a Qt script. An absolute path must be provided. This path is used to form the complete path if a relative path is provided in the LoadScript command. All Qt Script scripts reside in /payload/app/scripts. Command ID: 0xC02C Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_SCRIPT_DIRECTORY Parameter # Bytes Type Description directory 82 String Script file directory 3.1.2.45 SetScriptParameterFileDirectory The SetScriptParameterFileDirectory command specifies a source directory for a Qt script parameter file. The script parameter file feature is still in work and this command has no effect. Command ID: 0xC02D Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_SCRIPT_PARAMETER_FILE_DIRECTORY Parameter # Bytes Type Description directory 82 String Script file directory 3.1.2.46 LoadScript Use the SystemLoadScript command instead for the reasons listed under that command description. The LoadScript command loads a script file into the Qt scripting engine. The path may be an absolute path or a relative path. If a relative path is given it is combined with the absolute path set via the SetScriptDirectory command to form the complete path. Command ID: 0xC02E Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_SCRIPT_LOAD Parameter # Bytes Type Description script 82 String Script source path (e.g. /payload/app/script/myScript.js or myScript.js),,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p33,1,3.1.2.47 RunScript Use the SystemRunScript command instead. The RunScript command initiates execution of a script previously loaded into the Qt scripting engine via the LoadScript command. Command ID: 0xC02F Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_SCRIPT_RUN,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p34,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 34 3.1.2.48 AbortScript Use the SystemAbortScript command instead. The AbortScript command aborts the execution of the currently running Qt script. Command ID: 0xC030 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_SCRIPT_ABORT 3.1.2.49 PauseScript Use the SystemPauseScript command instead. The PauseScript command pauses the execution of the currently running Qt script. Command ID: 0xC031 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_SCRIPT_PAUSE 3.1.2.50 ResumeScript Use the SystemResumeScript command instead. The ResumeScript command resumes the execution of the currently paused Qt script. Command ID: 0xC032 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_SCRIPT_RESUME 3.1.2.51 ChangeScriptParameter The ChangeScriptParameter command updates a single parameter value for a loaded script. Command ID: 0xC033 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_SCRIPT_PARAMETER_CHANGE Parameter # Bytes Type Description parameterInfo 82 String Parameter to update ( like JSON e.g. “parameterName”:value 3.1.2.52 SetFileUplinkDirectory The SetFileUplinkDirectory command specifies a source directory of a file to uplink. An absolute path must be provided. This path is used to form the complete path if a relative path is provided in the FileUplinkStart command. Command ID: 0xC034 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FILE_UPLINK_DIRECTORY,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p35,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 35 Parameter # Bytes Type Description directory 82 String File uplink directory path 3.1.2.53 FileUplinkStart The FileUplinkStart command initiates a file upload via the commanding stream. The path may be an absolute path or a relative path. If a relative path is given it is combined with the path set via the SetFileUplinkDirectory command to form the complete path. The path is created if it does not exist. Command ID: 0xC035 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FILE_UPLINK_START Parameter # Bytes Type Description fileSizeInBytes 4 Uint32 File size in bytes path 78 String File uplink destination path 3.1.2.54 FileUplinkBlock The FileUplinkBlock command transfers a block of file data identified with a sequential id. All block data sent will be aggregated upon receiving the FileUplinkDone command. Command ID: 0xC036 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FILE_UPLINK_BLOCK Parameter # Bytes Type Description blockIndex 4 Uint32 Index of Block size 2 Uint16 Size in Bytes data 76 Byte array File block data 3.1.2.55 FileUplinkDone The FileUplinkDone command verifies the block data received by comparing the SHA of the received block data with the SHA provided in the command. If the given SHA matches the block data SHA the file is written in entirety to the destination set via the FileUplinkStart command. Command ID: 0xC037 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FILE_UPLINK_DONE,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p36,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 36 Parameter # Bytes Type Description SHA256 32 Byte array SHA256 for compare 3.1.2.56 FileUplinkAbort The FileUplinkAbort command aborts the current file uplink transfer. Command ID: 0xC038 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_FILE_UPLINK_ABORT 3.1.2.57 MountDevice The MountDevice command mounts a remote file system to the local file system. When done unmount with the UnmountDevice command. Command ID: 0xC039 Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_MOUNT_DEVICE Parameter # Bytes Type Description ipAddr 15 String IP address localPath 15 String Local path remotePath 52 String Remote path 3.1.2.58 UnmountDevice The UnmountDevice command unmounts a device mounted with the MountDevice command. Command ID: 0xC03A Mnemonic: ACME_COMMON_UNMOUNT_DEVICE Parameter # Bytes Type Description localPath 82 String Local path 3.1.2.59 DstatControl The DstatControl command stops or starts processor usage statistics from being placed in the telemetry slush pile. The linux command that is run is: ‘dstat –cdnm –noheader –nocolor’. The ‘zt.dstat’ logging category must be enabled using the SetAppLogging command. Ground software can direct output to a separate window by looking for this category.,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p37,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 37 Command ID: 0xC03B Mnemonic: ACME _COMMON_DSTAT Parameter # Bytes Type Description Onoff 1 Uint8 0=off/stop, 1=on/start 3.1.2.60 SetAppLogging The SetAppLogging command turns on or off the logging of messages based on their category. See the comments in /payload/app/config/AcmeLogging.ini for available categories and how to use filters. Command ID: 0xC03C Mnemonic: ACME _COMMON_SET_APP_LOGGING Parameter # Bytes Type Description type 1 Uint8 0=Debug, 1=Warning, 2=Critical/System, 3=Fatal enable 1 Uint8 0=Disable, 1=Enable category 80 String Category name or filter 3.1.2.61 SetFtpLogging The SetFtpLogging command sets the verbosity level for FTP operations. Command ID: 0xC03D Mnemonic: ACME _COMMON_SET_FTP_LOGGING Parameter # Bytes Type Description ftploglevel 1 Uint8 0=Not Verbose, 1=Verbose, 2=Very Verbose 3.1.3 ACME System Group Command Details 3.1.3.1 SystemDefineSetup The SystemDefineSetup command specifies the experimental configuration. All information communicated by this command is recorded in the header of each recorded data file. The burner type is used to determine igniter limits. See the IgniterMove command. The useIpsu parameters are used to determine which IPSUs to record images on. See the StartIpsus command. This",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p38,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 38 command must be issued before the software is commanded to the Experiment state. See the GoToExp command. The parameters contained in this command are also made available to scripts. For example: if ( SystemDefineSetup.radiomConfig == 1 ) { // Do some BRE thing } Command ID: 0xC101 Mnemonic: ACME_SYSTEM_DEFINE_SETUP Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description experimentType 1 Uint8 0 = Flame Design 1 = Coflow 2 = Spherical 3 = E-Field 4 = BRE mfc1GasType 1 Uint8 0 Oxygen/Nitrogen (21/79) 1 Oxygen/Nitrogen (30/70) 2 Oxygen/Nitrogen (40/60) 3 Oxygen/Nitrogen (85/15) 4 Oxygen/Helium (30/70) mfc2GasType 1 Uint8 0 Nitrogen 1 Helium mfc3GasType 1 Uint8 0 Methane 1 Methane/Nitrogen (30/70) 2 Methane/Nitrogen (40/60) 3 Methane/Nitrogen (45/55) 4 Ethylene 5 Ethylene/Nitrogen (30/70) 6 Ethylene/Nitrogen (50/50) 7 Hydrogen 8 Hydrogen/Methane (44.4/55.6) 9 Hydrogen/Methane (55.6/44.4) 10 Hydrogen/Methane (66.7/33.3) 11 Hydrogen/Methane/Helium (20/25/55) 12 Hydrogen/Methane/Helium (25/20/55) 13 Hydrogen/Methane/Helium (30/15/55) 14 Hydrogen/Helium (45/55) 15 Methane/Helium (45/55) 16 Ethylene/Helium (20/80) 17 Ethylene/Helium (25/75) 18 Ethylene/Helium (30/70),,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p39,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 39 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description efieldConfig 1 Uint8 0 = Mesh Removed 1 = Mesh in 35 mm position 2 = Mesh in 40 mm position Distance from the chamber window centerline. efieldPolarity 0 = Supply Not Installed 1 = Negative 2 = Positive igniterArm 0 = 0 mm Position 1 = 10 mm Position 2 = 30 mm Position burnerType 1 Uint8 0 = Spherical (1/4) 1 = Spherical(3/8) 2 = Spherical(1/2) 3 = Coflow 4 = Gas Jet(1.3mm), 10 mm position 5 = Gas Jet(2.1mm), 10 mm position 6 = BRE (25mm) 7 = BRE (50mm) thermoConfig 1 Uint8 0 = Near 1 = Far 2 = Removed radiomConfig 1 Uint8 0 = Standard 1 = BRE useIpsu1 1 Uint8 0 = No 1 = Yes useIpsu2 1 Uint8 0 = No 1 = Yes useIpsu3 1 Uint8 0 = No 1 = Yes useIpsu4 1 Uint8 0 = No 1 = Yes useIpsug5 1 Uint8 0 Do not archive images 1 Archive images during test point (ACME camera and/or Intensified Camera) useIpsug6 1 Uint8 0 = No 1 = Yes fiberConfig 1 Uint8 0 Not installed 1 Installed",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p39,1,"3.1.3.2 SystemOpenTestPoint The SystemOpenTestPoint command prepares the software for recording data. A directory is created at /payload/sci/testpointName to accept files, where testpointName is the supplied parameter. If that directory already exists then ‘_x’ is appended to the name, where x is 1 or 2 or 3 or more until a non-existent directory is found. In this directory a file is created named testpointName_x_data.csv, where testpointName_x matches the directory name. Two lines of header are written to the file and it is left open ready to accept data once an DataRecordingStart",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p40,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 40 command is received. See the details under the DataRecordingStart command for more information about the headers. Command ID: 0xC102 Mnemonic: ACME_SYSTEM_TEST_POINT_OPEN Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description testpointName 30 String Any whitespace characters are trimmed from the beginning and end of the string. 3.1.3.3 SystemCloseTestPoint The SystemCloseTestPoint command marks the end of the test point started with the SystemOpenTestPoint command. Data recording is stopped, if it has not been stopped already using the DataRecordingStop command. The recorded data file, current command log file, and current message log file are compressed into the file /payload/sci/testpointName_x/testpointName_x.zip, where testpointName_x is as described under the SystemOpenTestPoint command. Command ID: 0xC103 Mnemonic: ACME_SYSTEM_TEST_POINT_CLOSE 3.1.3.4 SystemResetMotionController The SystemResetMotionController command resets the motion control board in the CIA that controls the igniter arm and TFP motors. Any current motion and waveforms are stopped, the board is reset, and the board version is reported in the log. This is a ‘soft reset’ as referenced in the Prodigy PC-104 Users Guide. Hard resets of the individual motor stepper drivers are achieved using the IgniterMotorSetState and TfpMotorSetState commands. Command ID: 0xC104 Mnemonic: ACME_SYSTEM_RESET_MOTION_CONTROLLER",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p40,1,3.1.3.5 SystemCirFlowState The SystemCirFlowState command is designed to be sent from the IOP to inform the ACME software about the state of the CIR manifolds. Scripts can access this information as shown: If ( SystemCirFlowState.diluentManifoldState == 1) { // Diluent manifold is opened } Command ID: 0xC107 Mnemonic: ACME_SYSTEM_CIR_FLOW_STATE,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p41,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 41 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description fuelManifoldState 1 Uint8 0 = closed 1 = open State of CIR fuel manifold. diluentManifoldState 1 Uint8 0 = closed 1 = open State of CIR diluent manifold. oxygenManifoldState 1 Uint8 0 = closed 1 = open State of CIR oxygen manifold. nitrogenManifoldState 1 Uint8 0 = closed 1 = open 2 = bypass State of CIR nitrogen manifold. 3.1.3.6 SystemLoadScript The SystemLoadScript command loads a script file into the Qt scripting engine. The path may be an absolute path or a relative path. If a relative path is given it is combined with the absolute path set via the SetScriptDirectory command to form the complete path. Use this, and the other system script commands below, instead of the common LoadScript command in order to get accurate script state and line numbers in telemetry and better messaging during aborts. Command ID: 0xC108 Mnemonic: ACME_SYSTEM_SCRIPT_LOAD Parameter # Bytes Type Description script 82 String Script source path (e.g. /payload/app/script/myScript.js or myScript.js) 3.1.3.7 SystemRunScript The SystemRunScript command initiates execution of a script previously loaded into the Qt scripting engine via the LoadScript command. Use instead of common RunScript command. Command ID: 0xC109 Mnemonic: ACME_ SYSTEM _SCRIPT_RUN",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p41,1,3.1.3.8 SystemAbortScript The SystemAbortScript command aborts the execution of the currently running Qt script. Use instead of common AbortScript command. Command ID: 0xC10A Mnemonic: ACME_ SYSTEM _SCRIPT_ABORT,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p42,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 42 3.1.3.9 SystemPauseScript The SystemPauseScript command pauses the execution of the currently running Qt script. Use instead of common PauseScript command. Command ID: 0xC10B Mnemonic: ACME_ SYSTEM _SCRIPT_PAUSE 3.1.3.10 SystemResumeScript The SystemResumeScript command resumes the execution of the currently paused Qt script. Use instead of common ResumeScript command. Command ID: 0xC10C Mnemonic: ACME_ SYSTEM _SCRIPT_RESUME 3.1.4 ACME Imaging Group Command Details 3.1.4.1 DataCameraSetBinning The DataCameraSetBinning command sends a command to the IPSU-G to set the horizontal (X) and vertical (Y) binning. Binning is summing of charge of adjacent pixels. Command ID: 0xC201 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SET_BINNING Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description binningX 4 Uint32 1-8 binningY 4 Uint32 1-14 3.1.4.2 DataCameraSetPixelFormat The DataCameraSetPixelFormat command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC202 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SET_PIXEL_FORMAT,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p43,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 43 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description pixelFormat 4 Uint32 Only settable when not capturing images 0 = Mono8 1 = BayerRG8 2 = BayerRG12 (desired format) 3 = BayerGR12Packed 4 = RGB8Packed 5 = BGR8Packed 6 = YUV411Packed 7 = YUV422Packed 8 = YUV444Packed 3.1.4.3 DataCameraSetRegionOfInterest The DataCameraSetRegionOfInterest command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC203 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SET_ROI Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description xCoordinate 4 Uint32 0-1360 OffsetX yCoordinate 4 Uint32 0-1024 OffsetY height 4 Uint32 1-1024 width 4 Uint32 1-1360 3.1.4.4 DataCameraSetAutomaticExposure The DataCameraSetAutomaticExposure command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC204 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SET_AUTO_EXPOSURE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description algorithm 4 Uint32 0x0 = Mean 0x1 = FitRange tolerance 4 Uint32 0-50 % maxTime 4 Uint32 Microseconds,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p44,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 44 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description minTime 4 Uint32 Microseconds outliers 4 Uint32 .01% units rate 4 Uint32 % target 4 Uint32 % (0 = black, 100 = white) 3.1.4.5 DataCameraSetManualExposure The DataCameraSetManualExposure command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC205 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SET_EXPOSURE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description integrationTimeUs 4 Uint32 10 - 60,000,000 Microseconds 3.1.4.6 DataCameraSetFrameRate The DataCameraSetFrameRate command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC206 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SET_FRAME_RATE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description frameRate 4 Float 0.0127329 – 30.0111 3.1.4.7 DataCameraSetAutoGain The DataCameraSetAutoGain command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC207 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SET_AUTO_GAIN Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description algorithm 4 Uint32 0=Mean, 1=FitRange",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p45,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 45 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description tolerancePercent 4 Uint32 0-50 % maxDb 4 Uint32 dB minDb 4 Uint32 dB outliers 4 Uint32 .01% units ratePercent 4 Uint32 % targetPercent 4 Uint32 % 3.1.4.8 DataCameraSetManualGain The DataCameraSetManualGain command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC208 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SET_MANUAL_GAIN Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description gainValueDb 4 Uint32 0 - 33 3.1.4.9 DataCameraSetAutoWhiteBalance The DataCameraSetAutoWhiteBalance command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC209 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SET_AUTO_WHITE_BAL Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description tolerance 4 Uint32 rate 4 Uint32 3.1.4.10 DataCameraSetManualWhiteBalance The DataCameraSetManualWhiteBalance command sends a command to the IPSU-G. Sets the gain of the red and blue channels. The green channel gain is always 1.00.,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p46,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 46 Command ID: 0xC20A Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SET_MAN_WHITE_BAL Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description redPercent 4 Uint32 0.8 – 3.0 Red gain as % of camera default bluePercent 4 Uint32 0.8 – 3.0 Blue gain as % of camera default 3.1.4.11 DataCameraLoadConfig The DataCameraLoadConfig command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC20B Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_LOAD_CONFIG Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description index 4 Uint32 0 - 5 3.1.4.12 DataCameraSaveConfig The DataCameraSaveConfig command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC20C Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SAVE_CONFIG Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description index 4 Uint32 0 - 5 3.1.4.13 DataCameraSetPowerUpConfig The DataCameraSetPowerUpConfig command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC20D Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SET_PWR_UP_CONFIG Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description index 4 Uint32 0 - 5,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p47,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 47 3.1.4.14 DataCameraStartCapture The DataCameraStartCapture command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC20E Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_START_CAPTURE 3.1.4.15 DataCameraStopCapture The DataCameraStopCapture command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC20F Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_STOP_CAPTURE 3.1.4.16 DataCameraStartArchive The DataCameraStartArchive command sends a command to the specified IPSU-G to start recording images using the specified cameras. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC210 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_START_ARCHIVE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description fileName 81 bytes String IPSU-G Number 1 Uint8 5 or 6 Camera 1 1 Uint8 1-4 Camera 2 1 Uint8 1-4, or 0 for no camera 2-4 Camera 3 1 Uint8 1-4, or 0 for no camera 3-4 Camera 4 1 Uint8 1-4, or 0 for no camera 4 3.1.4.17 DataCameraStopArchive The DataCameraStopArchive command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC211 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_STOP_ARCHIVE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p48,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 48 3.1.4.18 DataCameraGrabOneImage The DataCameraGrabOneImage command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC212 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_GRAB_ONE_IMAGE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description fileName 81 bytes String 3.1.4.19 DataCameraMoveZoomAxis The DataCameraMoveZoomAxis command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC213 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_MOVE_ZOOM_AXIS Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description position 4 Uint32 3.1.4.20 DataCameraMoveIrisAxis The DataCameraMoveIrisAxis command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC214 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_MOVE_IRIS_AXIS Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description position 4 Uint32 3.1.4.21 DataCameraMoveFocusAxis The DataCameraMoveFocusAxis command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC215 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_MOVE_FOCUS_AXIS,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p49,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 49 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description position 4 Uint32 3.1.4.22 DataCameraMoveFilterAxis The DataCameraMoveFilterAxis command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC216 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_MOVE_FILTER_AXIS Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description position 4 Uint32 3.1.4.23 DataCameraMoveWithParameters The DataCameraMoveWithParameters command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC217 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_MOVE_WITH_PARAMS Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description axisNumber 1 Uint8 position 4 Uint32 velocity 4 Uint32 acceleration 4 Uint32 deceleration 4 Uint32 jerk 4 Uint32 3.1.4.24 DataCameraSetupMoveWithParameters The DataCameraSetupMoveWithParameters command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC218 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SETUP_MOVE_WITH_PARAMS,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p50,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 50 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description axisNumber 1 Uint8 position 4 Uint32 velocity 4 Uint32 acceleration 4 Uint32 deceleration 4 Uint32 jerk 4 Uint32 3.1.4.25 DataCameraExecuteSetupMove The DataCameraExecuteSetupMove command sends a command to the IPSU-G. More details TBD. Command ID: 0xC219 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_EXECUTE_SETUP_MOVE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description axisNumber 1 Uint8 1-4 3.1.4.26 DataCameraSelectFilter The DataCameraSelectFilter command sends a command to the IPSU-G to move the filter barrel to the position of the specified filter. Positions are defined in AcmeConfig.ini using the keys AvtCameraMotion/FilterXPosition, where X is 1 through 4. Command ID: 0xC21A Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_SELECT_FILTER Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description filter 1 Uint8 1-4 Filter barrel filter number.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p51,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 51 3.1.4.27 AnalogCameraSetAgc The AnalogCameraSetAgc command disables automatic gain control or enables it and sets the maximum gain on the operations camera located in the ACME CIA. For more details about the camera see the Hitachi KP-D20B web page: http://www.hitachikokusai.com/Products/IndustrialVisionSystems/InterlaceScanCameras/POC_0 07075.html Command ID: 0xC21B Mnemonic: ACME_ANALOG_CAMERA_SET_AGC Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description gain 1 Uint8 0 = disabled 1 = 6 dB 2 = 12 dB 3 = 21 dB 4 = 31 dB 3.1.4.28 AnalogCameraSetShutter The AnalogCameraSetShutter command sets the shutter speed on the operations camera located in the ACME CIA. See the AnalogCameraEnableAgc command for more details about the camera. Command ID: 0xC21C Mnemonic: ACME_ANALOG_CAMERA_SET_SHUTTER Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description shutter 1 Uint8 0 = 1/60 sec 1 = 1/100 sec 2 = 1/250 sec 3 = 1/500 sec 4 = 1/1000 sec 5 = 1/2000 sec 6 = 1/4000 sec 7 = 1/10000 sec 8 = 1/20000 sec 9 = 1/30000 sec 3.1.4.29 IlluminationConfigure The IlluminationConfigure command turns on or off the illumination LEDs in the CIA on the analog camera assembly. Command ID: 0xC21D Mnemonic: ACME_ILLUMINATION_CONFIG,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p52,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 52 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description sideLEDBankState 1 Uint8 0 = Off 1 = On This is the bank of 3 LEDs to the side of the camera axis and turning mirror, oriented in a row parallel to the camera axis. endLEDBankState 1 Uint8 0 = Off 1 = On This is the bank of 3 LEDs inline with the axis of the analog camera, just past the turning mirror, at the end of the analog camera assembly. It is oriented in a row perpendicular to the camera axis. 3.1.4.30 IlluminationCycle The IlluminationCycle command cycles one or both banks of illumination LEDs in the CIA on and off for specified durations and number of cycles. The final state is always both banks off. Hint: An ongoing cycle of illumination can be cancelled and the lights turned off by sending this command with bank states of 0, a cycle count of 1, and minimum on and off durations. Command ID: 0xC21E Mnemonic: ACME_ILLUMINATION_CYCLE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description sideLEDBankStat e 1 Uint8 0 = Off 1 = Cycle On/Off Include the side bank in the on and off cycling or not. endLEDBankStat e 1 Uint8 0 = Off 1 = Cycle On/Off Include the end bank in the on and off cycling or not. onDuration 4 Float 0.03 - 999.99 Duration in seconds that the LED(s) are on. offDuration 4 Float 0.03 - 999.99 Duration in seconds that the LED(s) are off. cycleCount 4 Uint32 1-9999 Number of times to cycle LED(s) on and off.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p52,1,3.1.4.31 StartIpsus The StartIpsus command initiates a sequence of commands to the CIR IPSUs and IPSU-Gs to start recording images under the specifed test point name and for the IPSU-Gs using the specified cameras. Command sequences are only sent to those IPSUs and IPSU-Gs that were specified as active in the SystemDefineSetup command. If sequence is running it must be stopped using the StopIpsus command before a new start sequence can be started. The command sequence is a follows where each row in the table corresponds to a commanding phase. IPSUs IPSU-Gs,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p53,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 53 Test Point Open, ID 0x4020 Test Point Open(testpointName), ID 0x4001 Enable Archiving, ID 0x4025 (Waiting for IPSU response to any Enable Archiving commands) Disable Live Video, ID 0x4036 (Waiting for IPSU response to any Disable Live Video commands) Start Test Point(testpointName), ID 0x4022 Camera Start Test Point Archive, ID 0x420D When all active IPSUs and IPSU-Gs respond to their phase command, then the next command phase begins. If a response is not received within a timeout period then a message is logged identifying the offending IPSU(s), the system is sent to the Idle state, and no further commands are sent to any IPSUs or IPSU-Gs. Any test points that were successfully started must be stopped by hand. The response timeout period is the registry value, Ipsu/IpsuCommandResponseTimoutMsec. Command ID: 0xC21F Mnemonic: ACME_IPSUS_START Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description testpointName 9 String Ipsu 5 Number Cameras 1 Uint8 0-4 Ipsu 5 Camera 1 1 Uint8 1-4 Ipsu 5 Camera 2 1 Uint8 1-4 Ipsu 5 Camera 3 1 Uint8 1-4 Ipsu 5 Camera 4 1 Uint8 1-4 Ipsu 6 Number Cameras 1 Uint8 0-4 Ipsu 6 Camera 1 1 Uint8 1-4 Ipsu 6 Camera 2 1 Uint8 1-4 Ipsu 6 Camera 3 1 Uint8 1-4 Ipsu 6 Camera 4 1 Uint8 1-4 3.1.4.32 StopIpsus The StopIpsus command initiates a sequence of commands to the CIR IPSUs and IPSU-Gs to stop recording images and close a currently running test point. Command sequences are only",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p54,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 54 sent to those IPSUs and IPSU-Gs that were specified as active in the SystemDefineSetup command. The command sequence is a follows where each row in the table corresponds to a commanding phase. IPSUs IPSU-Gs Test Point Stop, ID 0x4023 Test Point Stop, ID 0x420E Test Point Close, ID 0x4021 Test Point Close, ID 0x4002 When all active IPSUs and IPSU-Gs respond to their phase command, then the next command phase begins. If a response is not received within a timeout period then a message is logged identifying the offending IPSU(s), the system is sent to the Idle state, and no further commands are sent to any IPSUs or IPSU-Gs. The response timeout period is the registry value, Ipsu/IpsuCommandResponseTimoutMsec. Command ID: 0xC220 Mnemonic: ACME_IPSUS_STOP 3.1.4.33 DataCameraGetReport The DataCameraGetReport command, when the ACME avionics package is controlling the ACME camera instead of the IPSU-G, causes a report of camera parameters to be placed in the telemetry log. Command ID: 0xC221 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_GET_REPORT 3.1.4.34 IntensifiedCameraGate The IntensifiedCameraGate command sends associated commands to the IPSUG to set the intensifier gate type and width. If the gateType parameter is continuous the pulsedGateWidthCounts parameter is not used. Raw gate width of 0-49999 corresponds to 20 to 1,000,000 nS. Command ID: 0xC222 Mnemonic: ACME_INTENSIFIED_CAMERA_GATE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p54,1,Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description gateType 1 Uint8 0 = Pulsed (safe) 1= Continuous pulsedGateWidthCounts 4 Uint32 0-49999 The raw gate width (not nanoseconds),,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p55,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 55 3.1.4.35 IntensifiedCameraGainAmplitude The IntensifiedCameraGainAmplitude command sends an associated command to IPSUG to change the intensifier gain. Command ID: 0xC223 Mnemonic: ACME_INTENSIFIED_CAMERA_GAIN_AMPLITUDE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description gainAmplitudeCounts 4 Uint32 2048 (safe gain) to 4095 Intensifier gain 3.1.4.36 DataCameraExposureBracketing The DataCameraExposureBracketing command issues an associated command to the IPSUG to perform (WHEN IMPLEMETED on the IPSUG) exposure bracketing on the ACME camera. Several images are taken at an exposure before increasing the exposure and taking several more, repeating for a specified number of exposures, then going back to the initial exposure. For example, with imagesPerExposure=2, exposures=3, exposureStartUsec=50000 usec, and exposureIncrementUsec=5000 the following image frames and exposures will be acquired: 1 50000 2 50000 3 55000 4 55000 5 60000 6 60000 7 50000 8 50000 9 55000 … Command ID: 0xC224 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_CAMERA_EXPOSURE_BRACKETING Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description enable 1 Uint8 0=disable, 1=enable Turn bracketing on or off imagesPerExposure 2 Uint16 1-65535 Number of images at an exposure at given frame rate before moving to next exposure exposures 2 Uint16 2-65535 Number of different exposures exposureStartUsec 4 Int32 First exposure exposureIncrementUsec 4 Int32 Amount to increment exposure time each time.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p56,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 56 3.1.4.37 IntensifiedCameraMoveFilter The IntensifiedCameraMoveFilter command moves the filter on the intensified camera to the specified position. This is implemted by sending the associated command to IPSUG. Command ID: 0xC225 Mnemonic: ACME_INTENSIFIED_CAMERA_MOVE_FILTER Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description position 4 Uint32 Filter position 3.1.4.38 IntensifiedCameraSafe The IntensifiedCameraSafe command places the intensified camera in a state that is safe for turning on lights without harming the camera by sending commands to the IPSUG. The filter is commanded to home position and a fixed delay occurs to allow this to finish (without checking that it is finished). Then the filter is sent to a safe position with another fixed delay. The gain is set to 2048 (reporting back 2500). Gate type is set to pulsed and gate width is set to zero (reporting back 20). The delays and safe parameters are defined in the Intensifier group in AcmeConfig.ini. Command ID: 0xC226 Mnemonic: ACME_INTENSFIED_CAMERA_SAFE 3.1.5 ACME Igniter Group Command Details 3.1.5.1 IgniterDisable The IgniterDisable command shuts down power to the igniter. Specifically, it pulls low the primary control (PC) pin on the DC-DC converter that supplies the igniter. For more information see the data sheet for the Vicor V24C12C100BL2 DC-DC converter. Command ID: 0xC301 Mnemonic: ACME_IGNITER_DISABLE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p56,1,"3.1.5.2 IgniterPulse The IgniterPulse command turns on the igniter for a specified duration and power and then shuts it off. Specifically, it sets the secondary control (SC) pin on the DC-DC converter that supplies the igniter to the specfied power voltage and then sets to primary control (PC) pin to 5V which enables output to the igniter. After the specified pulse duration the PC pin is set to 0V, disabling output to the igniter. The power parameter is converted to a control voltage using the equation,",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p57,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 57 Vcontrol = B * power + A, where A and B are ConversionFactorA and ConversionFactorB in the Igniter group of AcmeConfig.ini. See Table 32. The power parameter is limited by MinPowerVolts and MaxPowerVolts in the Igniter group in AcmeConfig.ini. These are control voltages not voltages at the igniter. Command ID: 0xC302 Mnemonic: ACME_IGNITER_PULSE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description power 4 Float 0.46V to 12V Igniter voltage corresponding to - 0.6 to 0.8V control voltage. durationMs 4 Uint32 Pulse duration in milliseconds. 3.1.5.3 IgniterMove The IgniterMove command moves the igniter arm to the specified absolute postion. The velocity, acceleration, and deceleration values used for the move are specified in AcmeConfig.ini. See Table 32, Group: Igniter, Keys: VelocityCountsPerSec, AccelerationCountsPerSecSquared, and DecelerationCountsPerSecSquared. To go to the home (retracted) position specify zero as the position. In this case the keys used are HomeVelocityCountsPerSec, HomeAccelerationCountsPerSecSquared, and DecelerationCountsPerSecSquared. The igniter arm is moved using a trapezoidal velocity versus time profile. If the acceleration parameter is greater than zero then movement begins with a ramp up until the specified velocity is achieved. If the deceleration parameter is greater than zero then there is a ramp down to zero velocity. The duration of the movement is calculated to reach the desired position.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p57,1,This command takes a few seconds to execute. To shorten the duration between command and movement use the IgniterSetupMove or IgniterSetupMoveWithParameters command followed by the IgniterExecuteSetupMove command when ready to move. The motor is a Haydon Kerk 25544-05-004ENG captive linear actuator. There is no encoder. Command ID: 0xC303 Mnemonic: ACME_IGNITER_MOVE,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p58,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 58 Paramete r # Bytes Type Values Description position 4 Uint32 0 = home (retracted) 1 to maximum position. Move igniter arm motor to this absolute position in counts. Maximum position is specified in AcmeConfig.ini and depends on the installed burner. See Table 32, Group: Igniter, Keys: BurnerLimits and MaxPosition. 3.1.5.4 IgniterSetupMove An IgniterSetupMove command followed by an IgniterSetupMove command is the same as the IgniterMove command. By separating the setup, which takes a few seconds, and the execute, which occurs quickly, the beginning of movement can be commanded or scripted closer to an event or other command. See the IgniterMove command for information about what velocity, acceleration, and deceleration are used. Command ID: 0xC304 Mnemonic: ACME_IGNITER_SETUP_MOVE Paramete r # Bytes Type Values Description position 4 Uint32 0 = home (retracted) 1 to maximum position. Prepare to move igniter arm motor to this absolute position in counts. Maximum position is specified in AcmeConfig.ini and depends on the installed burner. See Table 32, Group: Igniter, Keys: BurnerLimits and MaxPosition. 3.1.5.5 IgniterSetupMoveWithParameters The IgniterSetupMoveWithParameters command is the same as the IgniterSetupMove command except that velocity, acceleration, and deceleration are specified in the command not in AcmeConfig.ini. Follow with an IgniterExecuteSetupMove command when ready to start motion.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p58,1,Command ID: 0xC305 Mnemonic: ACME_IGNITER_SETUP_MOVE_WITH_PARAMS,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p59,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 59 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description position 4 Uint32 0 = home (retracted) 1 to maximum position. Prepare to move igniter arm motor to this absolute position in counts. Maximum position is specified in AcmeConfig.ini and depends on the installed burner. See Table 32, Group: Igniter, Keys: BurnerLimits and MaxPosition. velocity 4 Uint32 Software limit: 0 to 128,000,000 Hardware limit TBD Prepare to move igniter arm using this velocity in counts/sec. The range of values at the PMDSetVelocity function is 0 to 2,147,483,647 following software limits. acceleration 4 Uint32 Software limit: 0 to 4,294,967,295, Hardware limit TBD Prepare to move igniter arm using this acceleration in counts/sec2. The range of values at the PMDSetAcceleration function is 0 to 18,446,744 following software limits. deceleration 4 Uint32 Software limit: 0 to 4,294,967,295, Hardware limit TBD Prepare to move igniter arm using this deceleration in counts/sec2. The range of values at the PMDSetDeceleration function is 0 to 18,446,744 following software limits. 3.1.5.6 IgniterExecuteSetupMove The IgniterExecuteSetupMove command executes a move previously set up with the IgniterSetupMove or IgniterSetupMoveWithParameters commands. Command ID: 0xC306 Mnemonic: ACME_IGNITER_EXECUTE_SETUP_MOVE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p59,1,"3.1.5.7 IgniterMotorSetState The IgniterMotorSetState command disables or enables the igniter motor by setting the RST line on the stepper driver to 0 V or 5 V respectively. In normal operations this command should not be necessary because the motor is automatically enabled as soon as the cube is active (the signal originates in the cube), and is disabled when the software shuts down. For reference, the stepper driver module is an Intelligent Motion System (IMS) XIM481H installed on an IMS INT-481 board with MS0, MS2, and MS3 in the on position. To reset the motion controller board instead use the SystemResetMotionController command. Command ID: 0xC307",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p60,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 60 Mnemonic: ACME_IGNITER_MOTOR_SET_STATE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description state 1 Uint8 0 = Reset 1 = On Sets IGN_MOTOR_RST (Table 18) to 0 V (Reset) or 5 V (On).,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p61,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 61 3.1.6 ACME Flow Group Command Details For flow group commands refer to the latest revision of drawing S1101MFS004, “ACME/CIR Integrated Pneumatic Schematic” to see how flow devices are connected to each other. A screen shot of the ACME CIA portion of this drawing (Rev A) is shown below. Gas from the CIR comes in at the bottom of the diagram; fuel through QD11, nitrogen bypass through QD12, and static mixer through QD13. Figure 2 ACME CIA Flow Schematic 3.1.6.1 FlowEnableMfcs The FlowEnableMfcs command turns the mass flow controllers on or off. Initially they are all turned on as soon as the cube boots. After this first power-on the MFCs are queried for their configuration, including full scale flow which appears in telemetry. Subsequent commanded power-ons do not cause a query of configuration.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p62,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 62 The current state of a MFC is shown in telemetry by the Statix Mixer MFC State, Bypass MFC State, and Fuel MFC State items. The mass flow controllers are Teledyne Hastings HFC-D-302 with different options for flow range depending on position, MFCA1, MFCA2, or MFCA3, and on experiment hardware configuration. Command ID: 0xC401 Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_ENABLE_MFCS Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description MFCA1State 1 Uint8 0 = Off 1 = On Sets MFCA1_PWR (Table 18) to 0 V (Off) or 5 V (On). MFCA2State 1 Uint8 0 = Off 1 = On Sets MFCA2_PWR (Table 18) to 0 V (Off) or 5 V (On). MFCA3State 1 Uint8 0 = Off 1 = On Sets MFCA3_PWR (Table 18) to 0 V (Off) or 5 V (On). 3.1.6.2 FlowSetMfcRate The FlowSetMfcRate command sets the flow rate setpoint for the selected mass flow controller. The flow rate specified in the command is in standard liters per minute which is converted to 0 to 5V that is applied to the analog input control on the mass flow controller according to the equation: Analog input control (V) = flowSlm / fullScaleValue * 5.0 ,where fullScaleValue depends on the installed mass flow controller and is retrieved from the unit during the configuration query after power-on and is reported in telemetry for each MFC. Command ID: 0xC402 Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_SET_MFC_RATE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p62,1,"Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description massFlowController 1 Uint8 1 = MFCA1, Static Mixer 2 = MFCA2, Bypass 3 = MFCA3, Fuel flowSlm 4 Float 0 to maximum flow. Desired flow setpoint in standard liters per minute. Maximum flow is the Full Scale value shown in telemetry for the selected MFC.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p63,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 63 3.1.6.3 FlowSetAllMfcRates The FlowSetAllMfcRates command sets the flow rate setpoint for all three mass flow controllers. See the FlowSetMfcRate command for more information about flow setpoints. Command ID: 0xC403 Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_SET_ALL_MFC_RATES Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description MFCA1FlowSlm 4 Float 0 to maximum flow. Desired flow setpoint in standard liters per minute. Maximum flow is the Full Scale value shown in telemetry for this MFC. MFCA2FlowSlm 4 Float 0 to maximum flow. Desired flow setpoint in standard liters per minute. Maximum flow is the Full Scale value shown in telemetry for this MFC. MFCA3FlowSlm 4 Float 0 to maximum flow. Desired flow setpoint in standard liters per minute. Maximum flow is the Full Scale value shown in telemetry for this MFC.,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p63,1,3.1.6.4 FlowZeroMfcs The FlowZeroMfcs command issues the ZRO serial command to the selected mass flow controllers. From the Teledyne Hastings Digital 300 Software Guide the description of this command is as follows: This command adjusts the offset value of the flow meter such that the indicated flow reads zero flow at the present flow rate. Updates user offset value. This command should be executed periodically on flow meters when the flow rate is known to be zero. If this command is sent when the flow rate is non-zero this can cause significant flow reading errors. Flow controllers typically have the “autozero” function enabled and will not need to be re-zeroed. ACME mass flow controllers have the autozero function TBD. Command ID: 0xC404 Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_ZERO_MFCS Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description zeroMFCA1 1 Uint8 0 = Do not zero 1 = Zero this MFC Whether or not to zero this MFC zeroMFCA2 1 Uint8 0 = Do not zero 1 = Zero this MFC Whether or not to zero this MFC zeroMFCA3 1 Uint8 0 = Do not zero 1 = Zero this MFC Whether or not to zero this MFC,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p64,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 64 3.1.6.5 FlowMfcSerialCommand The FlowMfcSerialCommand command sends the specified raw serial command to the daisy- chained mass flow controllers. The start of the string is the address that determines the destination controller: ‘*00’ = MFCA1 Static Mixer, ‘*01’ = MFCA2 Bypass, ‘*02’ = MFCA3 Fuel. The command follows the address, for example ‘*01ZRO’ to zero MFCA2, ‘*02MS’ to get the state of MFCA3, ‘*00sfl’ to get a status of all configuration items (the command sent after power-on) from MFCA1. See the Teledyne Hastings Digital 300 Software Guide for available commands. Replies to commands are placed in the telemetry text area. Serial commands are queued. A command will not be issued until a response is received from any prior command or until the prior command response times out. The response time out is specified by the registry value: Flow/MfcSerialCommandTimeoutMsec. See Table 32. Command ID: 0xC405 Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_MFC_SERIAL_COMMAND Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description serialCommand 81 String 3.1.6.6 FlowSetSolenoidValveState The FlowSetSolenoidValveState command opens or closes the specified ACME solenoid valve. A valve is opened (allowing flow) by applying 5 V. A valve is closed (stopping flow) by applying 0 V. The valves are the normally-closed type, that is they do not allow flow when un- powered. Command ID: 0xC406 Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_SET_SOLENOID_VALVE_STATE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p64,1,"Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description solenoidValve 1 Uint8 1 to 9 Valve SVAx, where x=1 to 9. state 1 Uint8 0 = closed (stop flow) 1 = open (allow flow) Close or open the valve by applying 0V or 5V. 3.1.6.7 FlowSetAllSolenoidValveStates The FlowSetAllSolenoidValveStates command sets the state, open or closed, of all nine ACME solenoid valves at once. Command ID: 0xC407 Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_SET_ALL_SOLENOID_VALVE_STATES",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p65,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 65 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description SVA1State 1 Uint8 0 = closed (stop flow) 1 = open (allow flow) SVA2State 1 Uint8 0 = closed (stop flow) 1 = open (allow flow) SVA3State 1 Uint8 0 = closed (stop flow) 1 = open (allow flow) SVA4State 1 Uint8 0 = closed (stop flow) 1 = open (allow flow) SVA5State 1 Uint8 0 = closed (stop flow) 1 = open (allow flow) SVA6State 1 Uint8 0 = closed (stop flow) 1 = open (allow flow) SVA7State 1 Uint8 0 = closed (stop flow) 1 = open (allow flow) SVA8State 1 Uint8 0 = closed (stop flow) 1 = open (allow flow) SVA9State 1 Uint8 0 = closed (stop flow) 1 = open (allow flow) 3.1.6.8 FlowOpenAll The FlowOpenAll command sets MFCA1, MFCA2, and MFCA3 to full scale flow and opens solenoid valves SVA1 through SVA9. Command ID: 0xC408 Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_OPEN_ALL 3.1.6.9 FlowCloseAll The FlowCloseAll command sets MFCA1, MFCA2, and MFCA3 to zero flow and closes solenoid valves SVA1 through SVA9. Any flow waveforms started with the FlowStepped, FlowRamped, FlowRectangularWave, or FlowRampedWave commands are stopped. Command ID: 0xC409 Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_CLOSE_ALL 3.1.6.10 FlowConfigForFill The FlowConfigForFill command configures ACME mass flow controllers and solenoid valves for filling the chamber. MFCA1, MFCA2, and MFCA3 are set to full scale flow and SVA1, SVA2, and SVA3 solenoid valves are opened. SVA4 through SVA9 are closed. Command ID: 0xC40A Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_CONFIG_FOR_FILL",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p66,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 66 3.1.6.11 FlowStepped The FlowStepped command steps the flow of each mass flow controller up or down, or not at all, by a specified step size, waits a specified duration, and steps again, repeating until each specified maximum flow (or minimum flow if stepping down) is reached, and then leaving the mass flow controller at the last flow. For positive step sizes the setpoints start at the minimum flow and step up until the maximum is reached, and does not go above. For negative step sizes the setpoints start at the maximum and step down until the minimum is reached, and does not go below. Stepping can be stopped with the FlowStopWaveform command, which leaves the flow setpoints where they were last, or with the FlowCloseAll command, which sets all flows to zero and closes all valves, with the FlowSetMfcRate or FlowSetAllMfcRates commands which leave the flow at the setpoint specified by the command. Stepping is stopped for a MFC by any other flow waveform command if the parameters say to control that MFC, otherwise ongoing stepping continues until complete. Command ID: 0xC40B Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_STEPPED",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p66,1,Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description stepDurationSecs 4 Float 0.1 to 999.9 Seconds to wait between steps MFCA1StepSizeSlm 4 Float -1*full scale to full scale 0 = do not step this MFC Static mixer MFC step size. Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA1MinimumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA1MaximumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA2StepSizeSlm 4 Float -1*full scale to full scale 0 = do not step this MFC Bypass MFC step size. Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA2MinimumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA2MaximumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA3StepSizeSlm 4 Float -1*full scale to full scale 0 = do not step this MFC Fuel MFC step size. Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA3MinimumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA3MaximumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry.,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p67,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 67 3.1.6.12 FlowRamped The FlowRamped command ramps the flow of each mass flow controller up or down, or not at all, at a specified ramp rate for a specified duration, waits a specified hold duration, and ramps again, repeating until each specified maximum flow (or minimum flow if ramping down) is reached, and then leaving the mass flow controller at the last flow. For positive ramp rates the setpoints start at the minimum flow and ramp up until the maximum is reached, and does not go above. For negative ramp rates the setpoints start at the maximum and ramp down until the minimum is reached, and does not go below. During the ramp the control voltage of the mass flow controller is updated every millisecond. Ramping can be stopped with the FlowStopWaveform command, which leaves the flow setpoints where they were last, with the FlowCloseAll command, which sets all flows to zero and closes all valves, or with the FlowSetMfcRate or FlowSetAllMfcRates commands which leave the flow at the setpoint specified by the command. Ramping is stopped for a MFC by any other flow waveform command if the parameters say to control that MFC, otherwise ongoing ramping continues until complete. Command ID: 0xC40C Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_RAMPED",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p67,1,Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description rampDurationSec 4 Float 0.1 – 999.9 Seconds to ramp each MFC. holdDurationSec 4 Float 0.1 – 999.9 Seconds to hold between ramps. MFCA1RampRateSlmPerSec 4 Float 0 = do not ramp this MFC Static mixer MFC ramp rate. MFCA1MinimumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA1MaximumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA2RampRateSlmPerSec 4 Float 0 = do not ramp this MFC Bypass MFC ramp rate. MFCA2MinimumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA2MaximumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA3RampRateSlmPerSec 4 Float 0 = do not ramp this MFC Fuel MFC ramp rate. MFCA3MinimumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA3MaximumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry.,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p68,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 68 3.1.6.13 FlowRectangularWave The FlowRectangularWave command steps the flow of each mass flow controller a value, A, up, down, or not at all, waits a specified A duration, steps another value, B, up, down, or not at all, and waits a specified B duration. This sequence is repeated until the maximum or minimum flow is reached or until stopped by a command. Rectangular waves can be stopped with the FlowStopWaveform command, which leaves the flow setpoints where they were last, with the FlowCloseAll command, which sets all flows to zero and closes all valves, or with the FlowSetMfcRate or FlowSetAllMfcRates commands which leave the flow at the setpoint specified by the command. Rectangular waves are stopped for a MFC by any other flow waveform command if the parameters say to control that MFC. Command ID: 0xC40D Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_RECTANGULAR_WAVE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p68,1,Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description stepADurationSec 4 Float 0.1 – 999.9 Seconds to wait after A steps. stepBDurationSec 4 Float 0.1 – 999.9 Seconds to wait after B steps. MFCA1StepASlm 4 Float -1*full scale to full scale 0 = do not step A this MFC Static mixer MFC step A size. Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA1StepBSlm 4 Float -1*full scale to full scale 0 = do not step B this MFC Static mixer MFC step B size. Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA1MinimumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA1MaximumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA2StepASlm 4 Float -1*full scale to full scale 0 = do not step A this MFC Bypass MFC step A size. Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA2StepBSlm 4 Float -1*full scale to full scale 0 = do not step B this MFC Bypass MFC step B size. Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA2MinimumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA2MaximumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA3StepASlm 4 Float -1*full scale to full scale 0 = do not step A this MFC Fuel MFC step A size. Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA3StepBSlm 4 Float -1*full scale to full scale 0 = do not step B this MFC Fuel MFC step B size. Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry.,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p69,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 69 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description MFCA3MinimumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA3MaximumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. 3.1.6.14 FlowRampedWave The FlowRampedWave command ramps the flow of each mass flow controller up or down, or not at all, at a specified ramp rate A for a specified duration A, waits a specified hold duration A, then ramps up or down or not at all at a specifried ramp rate B for a specified duration B, waits for a hold duration B, and repeats the sequence until specified maximum or minimum flow is reached or until stopped by command. Ramped waves can be stopped with the FlowStopWaveform command, which leaves the flow setpoints where they were last, with the FlowCloseAll command, which sets all flows to zero and closes all valves, or with the FlowSetMfcRate or FlowSetAllMfcRates commands which leave the flow at the setpoint specified by the command. Ramped waves are stopped for a MFC by any other flow waveform command if the parameters say to control that MFC. Command ID: 0xC40E Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_RAMPED_WAVE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p69,1,Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description rampADurationSec 4 Float 0.1 – 999.9 holdADurationSec 4 Float 0.1 – 999.9 rampBDurationSec 4 Float 0.1 – 999.9 holdBDurationSec 4 Float 0.1 – 999.9 MFCA1RampARateSlmPerSec 4 Float 0 = do not ramp A this MFC MFCA1RampBRateSlmPerSec 4 Float 0 = do not ramp B this MFC MFCA1MinimumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA1MaximumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA2RampARateSlmPerSec 4 Float 0 = do not ramp A this MFC MFCA2RampBRateSlmPerSec 4 Float 0 = do not ramp B this MFC MFCA2MinimumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry.,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p70,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 70 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description MFCA2MaximumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA3RampARateSlmPerSec 4 Float 0 = do not ramp A this MFC MFCA3RampBRateSlmPerSec 4 Float 0 = do not ramp B this MFC MFCA3MinimumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. MFCA3MaximumFlowSlm 4 Float 0 to full scale flow Full scale depends on the MFC and is in telemetry. 3.1.6.15 FlowStopWaveform The FlowStopWaveform command. Command ID: 0xC40F Mnemonic: ACME_FLOW_STOP_WAVEFORM Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description stopMFCA1Waveform 1 Uint8 0 = Do not stop 1 = Stop waveform Whether or not to stop the waveform for this MFC stopMFCA2Waveform 1 Uint8 0 = Do not stop 1 = Stop waveform Whether or not to stop the waveform for this MFC stopMFCA2Waveform 1 Uint8 0 = Do not stop 1 = Stop waveform Whether or not to stop the waveform for this MFC,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p70,1,"3.1.7 ACME Diagnostic Group Command Details 3.1.7.1 DataRecordingStart The DataRecordingStart command starts recording analog input and output data to a file at a rate defined by the Diagnostics/SampleTimeMsec registry entry, see Table 32, which defaults to 8 msec for 125 Hz sampling. Prior to this command the SystemOpenTestPoint command must be issued to define the file name, otherwise the command fails. The testpointName parameter in the SystemOpenTestPoint command results in the data being save to a path and file named /payload/sci/testpointName/testpointName_data.csv. See section 8.1 for the file contents. Command ID: 0xC501 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_RECORDING_START 3.1.7.2 DataRecordingStop The DataRecordingStop command stops acquistion of data started with the DataRecordingStart command. The resultant data file is left open until the SystemCloseTestPoint command is received. Issuing another DataRecordingStart command instead will append data to the existing",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p71,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 71 file. However, there is no indication in the file between the two acquisition periods except for differences in the data, which, for example, could include a tell-tale signature in the E-Field control voltage. Command ID: 0xC502 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_RECORDING_STOP 3.1.7.3 FiberMove The FiberMove command translates the thin filament pyrometry (Fiber) fiber array to the specified position at the specified velocity or translation rate. Movement acceleration and deceleration are registry values specified in AcmeConfig.ini, see Table 32, items Fiber/AccelerationCountsPerSecSquared and Fiber/DecelerationCountsPerSecSquared. The response time to start movement is on the order of seconds. To start movement closer to an event use the FiberSetupMove command followed by the FiberExecuteSetupMove command when ready to move. If the specified position exceeds the limit the command is not performed. The position limit is the Fiber/MaxPosition registry value minus the Fiber/FiberToBurnerOffsets registry value (a comma-separated string of offsets in order of burner type number) for the burner type specified in the SystemDefineSetup command. See the descriptions of these registry values in Table 32 for more information. Motion can be stopped prematurely using the FiberHaltMove command. The motor is an Ultramotion HT17 stepper motor linear actuator with 6 inch stroke and encoder. Command ID: 0xC503 Mnemonic: ACME_FIBER_MOVE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p71,1,Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description position 4 Uint32 0 to maximum position. Counts. Position 0 is retracted away from burner. See paragraphs above for max position calculation. velocity 4 Uint32 Counts per second. 3.1.7.4 FiberSetupMove The FiberSetupMove command sets up a move of the Fiber fiber array. Movement is not started until a FiberExecuteSetupMove command is received. This command pair can be used to place the start of movement closer to an event since setup takes on the order of seconds to complete. The rest of the details of this command are the same as for the FiberMove command except that acceleration and deceleration are command parameters. Command ID: 0xC504,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p72,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 72 Mnemonic: ACME_FIBER_SETUP_MOVE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description position 4 Uint32 0 to maximum position. Counts. Position 0 is retracted away from burner. See paragraphs under FiberMove command for max position calculation. velocity 4 Uint32 Counts per second. acceleration 4 Uint32 Counts per second squared. deceleration 4 Uint32 Counts per second squared. 3.1.7.5 FiberExecuteSetupMove The FiberExecuteSetupMove command starts movement after a FiberSetupMove command. Motion can be stopped prematurely using the FiberHaltMove command. Command ID: 0xC505 Mnemonic: ACME_FIBER_EXECUTE_SETUP_MOVE 3.1.7.6 FiberRampedMove The FiberRampedMove command moves the Fiber fiber array from its current position to a position the specified segment distance away, which can be positive or negative, waits the specified hold duration, and repeats this sequence the specified number of cycles. The command fails and is not performed at all if the calculated end position after all cycles exceeds the limit. See the FiberMove command description for limit calculation. The starting position is retrieved from the encoder. Motion can be stopped prematurely using the FiberHaltMove command. Command ID: 0xC506 Mnemonic: ACME_FIBER_RAMPED_MOVE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description segmentDistance 4 Int32 -1 * max position to max position, except zero. Counts. Negative moves away from burner. Positive moves toward burner. velocity 4 Uint32 acceleration 4 Uint32",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p73,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 73 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description deceleration 4 Uint32 holdDuration 4 Float 0.00 – 999.99 Seconds. cycles 4 Uint32 1 - 999 1 cycle is 1 translation and 1 hold. 3.1.7.7 FiberRampedWaveMove The FiberRampedWaveMove command moves the Fiber fiber array from its current position to a position the specified segment distance A away, which can be positive or negative, waits the specified hold duration A, moves a relative segment distance B, waits the specified hold duration B, and repeats this sequence the specified number of cycles. A triangle wave, essentially moving back and forth, is achieved by making the A and B segment distances equal but opposite in sign, and both hold durations zero. The command fails and is not performed at all if the calculated end position after all cycles exceeds the limit. See the FiberMove command description for limit calculation. The starting position is retrieved from the encoder. Motion can be stopped prematurely using the FiberHaltMove command. Command ID: 0xC507 Mnemonic: ACME_FIBER_RAMPED_WAVE_MOVE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p73,1,"Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description segmentDistanceA 4 Uint32 -1 * max position to max position, except zero. Counts. Negative moves away from burner. Positive moves toward burner. velocityA 4 Uint32 accelerationA 4 Uint32 decelerationA 4 Uint32 holdDurationA 4 Float 0.00 – 999.99 segmentDistanceB 4 Uint32 -1 * max position to max position, except zero. Counts. Negative moves away from burner. Positive moves toward burner. velocityB 4 Uint32",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p74,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 74 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description accelerationB 4 Uint32 decelerationB 4 Uint32 holdDurationB 4 Float 0.00 – 999.99 cycles 4 Uint32 1 - 999 3.1.7.8 FiberHaltMove The FiberHaltMove command stops any Fiber fiber array motion and aborts any ramps or ramped waves in progress. Command ID: 0xC508 Mnemonic: ACME_FIBER_HALT_MOVE 3.1.7.9 FiberMotorSetState The FiberMotorSetState command disables or enables the Fiber motor by setting the RST line on the stepper driver to 0 V or 5 V respectively. In normal operations this command should not be necessary because the motor is automatically enabled as soon as the cube is active (the signal originates in the cube), and is disabled when the software shuts down. For reference, the stepper driver module is an Intelligent Motion System (IMS) XIM481H installed on an IMS INT-481 board with MS0, MS2, and MS3 in the on position. To reset the motion controller board instead use the SystemResetMotionController command. Command ID: 0xC509 Mnemonic: ACME_FIBER_MOTOR_SET_STATE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description state 1 Uint8 0 = Reset 1 = On Sets Fiber_MOTOR_RST (Table 18) to 0 V (Reset) or 5 V (On). 3.1.7.10 PmtPower The PmtPower command turns all three photo multiplier tubes (PMTs) on or off. The PMTs are Hamamatsu part number H10722-10-MOD2. PMT1 has a 310nm bandpass interference filter. PMT2 has a UV fused silica broadband filter. PMT3 has a 430nm bandpass interference filter.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p75,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 75 Command ID: 0xC50A Mnemonic: ACME_PMT_POWER Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description state 1 Uint8 0 = Off 1 = On 3.1.7.11 PmtSetGain The PmtSetGain command sets the gain of the specified photo multipler tube. The gain parameter in this command specifies the volts out of the cube DNx-AO-333 board channels AO-25, AO-26, and AO-27, see Table 18. These voltages are fed through a voltage divider of 10 Kohms and 1.21 Kohms before entering the control input on the PMT. According to the PMT data sheet the recommended control voltage is 0.5 V to 1.1 V, corresponding to 4.63 V to 10.19 V for the gain command parameter, which is limited to a maximum of 10 V. The equation to convert PMT control input, x, to PMT gain is: PMTGain = 2,000,000 * x ^ 8.643856 ,which gives a range of PMTGain from 5000 to 3,500,000 for 0.5 V to 1.1V control input, or 4.63 V to 10.19 V command gain parameter. The equation to convert command gain parameter, gain, to PMT gain is: PMTGain = 2,000,000 * (gain/9.2645) ^ 8.643856 The equation to convert PMT gain to command gain parameter, gain, is: gain = 10 ^ (0.115689 * log(PMTGain) + 0.23786213) Command ID: 0xC50B Mnemonic: ACME_PMT_SET_GAIN Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description pmtNumber 1 Uint8 1 = PMT1 2 = PMT2 3 = PMT3 gain 4 Float 0 - 10 Volts 3.1.7.12 PmtSetAllGains The PmtSetAllGains command sets the gain of each photo multipler tube. See the PmtSetGain command for determination of gain parameters.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p75,1,Command ID: 0xC50C Mnemonic: ACME_PMT_SET_ALL_GAINS,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p76,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 76 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description PMT1Gain 4 Float 0 - 10 Volts PMT2Gain 4 Float 0 - 10 Volts PMT3Gain 4 Float 0 - 10 Volts 3.1.7.13 ExtinctionDetectionConfigure The ExtinctionDetectionConfigure command defines the limits, averaging duration, and method of extinction detection. Detection of extinction using this configuration does not begin until the ExtinctionDetectionSetState command enables detection. It ends when extinction is detected or when the ExtinctionDetectionSetState command is used to force detection or disable detection. During detection the instruments that have limits in this command have their values continuously averaged over the specified duration. These averages are compared to the specified limts. The numberOfInstruments parameter is used to indicate how many instrument averages must go below their limits before extinction is detected. Instruments can be assigned no influence on the detection state by specifying negative one limit. Command ID: 0xC50D Mnemonic: ACME_EXTINCTION_CONFIGURE",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p76,1,Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description averagingDurationSecs 4 Float 0.0 – 99.99 Seconds numberOfInstruments 1 Uint8 1-8 1 = any 8 = all plus60RadiometerLimit 4 Float -1 = Don’t use. 0 – 5V plus30RadiometerLimit 4 Float -1 = Don’t use. 0 – 5V centerRadiometerLimit 4 Float -1 = Don’t use. 0 – 5V minus30RadiometerLimit 4 Float -1 = Don’t use. 0 – 5V minus60RadiometerLimit 4 Float -1 = Don’t use. 0 – 5V PMT1Limit 4 Float -1 = Don’t use. 0 – 4V PMT2Limit 4 Float -1 = Don’t use. 0 – 4V PMT3Limit 4 Float -1 = Don’t use. 0 – 4V,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p77,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 77 3.1.7.14 ExtinctionDetectionSetState The ExtinctionDetectionSetState command enables or disables extinction detection or forces the state to Detected. The Detected state can be used by the operator to inform the system when extinction has occurred or to test the resultant actions which are set up by the ExtinctionDetectionSetAction… commands. Command ID: 0xC50E Mnemonic: ACME_EXTINCTION_DETECTION_SET_STATE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description state 1 Uint8 0 = Disabled 1 = Enabled 2 = Detected 3.1.7.15 ExtinctionDetectionSetActionZero The ExtinctionDetectionSetActionZero command sets the action when extinction is detected to zero all mass flow controller flow setpoints after the specified wait duration. Command ID: 0xC50F Mnemonic: ACME_EXTINCTION_DETECTION_SET_ACTION_ZERO Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description waitDuration 4 Float 0.0 – 99.99 Seconds 3.1.7.16 ExtinctionDetectionSetActionSingleStep The ExtinctionDetectionSetActionSingleStep command sets the action when extinction is detected to step each mass flow controller flow setpoint up or down or not at all by the specified amount. Command ID: 0xC510 Mnemonic: ACME_EXTINCTION_DETECTION_SET_ACTION_SINGLE_STEP Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description MFCA1StepSlm 4 Float -MFCA1FullScale to +MFCA1FullScale SLPM MFCA2StepSlm 4 Float -MFCA2FullScale to +MFCA2FullScale SLPM MFCA3StepSlm 4 Float -MFCA3FullScale to +MFCA3FullScale SLPM,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p78,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 78 3.1.7.17 ExtinctionDetectionSetActionSingleRamp The ExtinctionDetectionSetActionSingleRamp command sets the action when extinction is detected to ramp each mass flow controller flow setpoint up or down or not at all by the specified step size within the specified duration. Command ID: 0xC511 Mnemonic: ACME_EXTINCTION_DETECTION_SET_ACTION_SINGLE_RAMP Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description rampDuration 4 Float 0.0 – 99.99 Seconds MFCA1RampRateSl mPerSec 4 Float SLPM per second MFCA2RampRateSl mPerSec 4 Float SLPM per second MFCA3RampRateSl mPerSec 4 Float SLPM per second 3.1.7.18 ExtinctionDetectionSetActionStepping The ExtinctionDetectionSetActionStepping command sets the action when extinction is detected to stepping each mass flow controller flow setpoint up or down or not all by the specified amount, holding those setpoints for the specified duration, then setting all setpoints to zero. Command ID: 0xC512 Mnemonic: ACME_EXTINCTION_DETECTION_SET_ACTION_STEPPING Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description stepDuration 4 Float 0.0 – 99.99 Seconds MFCA1StepSlm 4 Float SLPM MFCA1MaxSlm 4 Float SLPM MFCA2StepSlm 4 Float SLPM MFCA2MaxSlm 4 Float SLPM MFCA3StepSlm 4 Float SLPM MFCA3MaxSlm 4 Float SLPM zeroWhenDone 1 Quint8 0 = Don’t zero when done 1 = Zero when done",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p79,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 79 3.1.7.19 ExtinctionDetectionSetActionRamping The ExtinctionDetectionSetActionRamping command sets the action when extinction is detected to ramping each mass flow controller flow setpoint up or down or not at all by the specified step within the specified duration, holding the final setpoint for the specified duration, then zeroing all setpoints. Command ID: 0xC513 Mnemonic: ACME_EXTINCTION_DETECTION_SET_ACTION_RAMPING Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description rampDuration 4 Float 0.0 – 99.99 Seconds holdDuration 4 Float 0.0 – 99.99 Seconds MFCA1RampRateSlmPerSec 4 Float SLPM per second MFCA1MaxSlm 4 Float MFCA2RampRateSlmPerSec 4 Float SLPM per second MFCA2MaxSlm 4 Float MFCA3RampRateSlmPerSec 4 Float SLPM per second MFCA3MaxSlm 4 Float zeroWhenDone 1 Quint8 0 = Don’t zero when done 1 = Zero when done 3.1.7.20 DataRecordingSetSampleRate The DataRecordingSetSampleRate command sets the period/frequency of data acquisition when the DataRecordingStart command is issued. On startup the sample time is the SampleTimeMsec value in the Diagnostics group in AcmeConfig.ini. The current value is in telemetry. Command ID: 0xC514 Mnemonic: ACME_DATA_RECORDING_SAMPLE_RATE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description sampleTimeMs 2 Uint16 0-65535 Duration in milliseconds between data samples.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p80,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 80 3.1.8 ACME E-Field Group Command Details 3.1.8.1 EFieldEnable The EFieldEnable command enables or disables the high voltage supply that powers the electric field mesh. This command also effectively performs a EFieldSetVoltage command with voltage parameter equal to zero prior to an enable and after a disable. Any waveforms are stopped by a disable. This command controls the cube DNx-AO-333 board AO-6 (HV2_EN) signal. See Table 18. The high voltage supply is an Ultravolt part number 20A12-N4-Y6-C. Command ID: 0xC601 Mnemonic: ACME_EFIELD_ENABLE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description state 1 Uint8 0 = Disable 1 = Enable Disable sets HV_EN to 0V. Enable sets HV_EN to 5V. 3.1.8.2 EFieldSetVoltage The EFieldSetVoltage command controls the voltage present on the electric field mesh. The voltage parameter, which is desired voltage on the mesh, is converted to control voltage output to the cube DNx-AO-333 board AO-4 (HV_CTRL) signal. See Table 18. The conversion is according to the equation: 𝐶𝑜𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑜𝑙 𝑉𝑜𝑙𝑡𝑎𝑔𝑒 = −𝐵 + √𝐵2 − 4𝐶(𝐴 − 𝑣𝑜𝑙𝑡𝑎𝑔𝑒) 2𝐶",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p80,1,",where A, B, and C are items in AcmeConfig.ini, See Table 32, and depend on whether the negative or positive supply is being used. For the negative supply the names are: EField/NegativeOutputFactorA EField/NegativeOutputFactorB EField/NegativeOutputFactorC For the positive supply the names are: EField/PositiveOutputFactorA EField/PositiveOutputFactorB EField/PositiveOutputFactorC The SystemDefineSetup command tells the software which polarity of E-Field supply is installed. The reverse equation is: 𝑣𝑜𝑙𝑡𝑎𝑔𝑒 = 𝐶 ∗ 𝑐𝑣2 + 𝐵 ∗ 𝑐𝑣 + 𝐴 , where cv is control voltage and A,B,andC are as above. The EfieldEnable command must be issued first to enable the supply.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p81,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 81 Command ID: 0x602 Mnemonic: ACME_EFIELD_SET_VOLTAGE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description voltage 4 Float -10000 to 0 if negative polarity supply is installed. 0 to +10000 for positive polarity supply. 3.1.8.3 EFieldSteppedSignal The EFieldSteppedSignal command steps the electric field voltage between a specfied minimum and maximum voltage with steps of a specified size and duration and back down (or up) for a specified number of cycles. One cycle is a series of steps in one direction, therefore set cycles to a factor of 2 to perform complete up and down waveforms. The maximum and minimum voltages are not violated even if the step voltage does not divide evenly between them. The EfieldEnable command must be issued first to enable the supply. Special cases: - stepVoltage < 0 starts at maxVoltage and steps to minVoltage (and back up if cycles > 1). - Cycles = 1 produces a single staircase. - stepVoltage = (maxVoltage – minVoltage) produces a square waveform. Command ID: 0xC603 Mnemonic: ACME_EFIELD_STEPPED_SIGNAL Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description minVoltage 4 Float -10000 to 0 if negative polarity supply is installed. 0 to +10000 for positive polarity supply. maxVoltage 4 Float -10000 to 0 if negative polarity supply is installed. 0 to +10000 for positive polarity supply. stepVoltage 4 Float -10000 to 10000 stepDurationSecs 4 Float 0.005 – 999.999 cycles 4 Uint32 1-9999",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p82,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 82 3.1.8.4 EFieldRampedSignal The EFieldRampedSignal command is essentially an EFieldSteppedSignal command with steps replaced by ramps of specified rate and duration. The EfieldEnable command must be issued first to enable the supply. Special cases: - rampRatePerSec < 0 starts a maxVoltage and ramp/holds to minVoltage. - Cycles = 1 produces a single ramp/hold sequence betweem min and max. - holdDurationSec = 0 produces a triangle waveform. Command ID: 0xC604 Mnemonic: ACME_EFIELD_RAMPED_SIGNAL Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description minVoltage 4 Float -10000 to 0 if negative polarity supply is installed. 0 to +10000 for positive polarity supply. maxVoltage 4 Float -10000 to 0 if negative polarity supply is installed. 0 to +10000 for positive polarity supply. rampRatePerSec 4 Float rampDurationSecs 4 Float 0.005 – 999.999 holdDurationSecs 4 Float 0.005 – 999.999 cycles 4 Uint32 1 - 9999 3.1.8.5 EFieldRectangularWave The EFieldRectangularWave command is an EFieldSteppedSignal command but there is a second step with its own size and duration. The two step sizes can be the same or opposite polarity. The EfieldEnable command must be issued first to enable the supply. Special cases: - If (stepAVoltage + stepBVoltage) < 0 the sequence starts at maxVoltage. - Cycles = 1 produces a single step sequence between min and max voltage. - stepAVoltage = -1 * stepBVoltage produces a square wave between minVoltage and minVoltage + stepAVoltage.,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p82,1,Command ID: 0xC605 Mnemonic: ACME_EFIELD_RECTANGULAR_WAVE,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p83,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 83 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description minVoltage 4 Float -10000 to 0 if negative polarity supply is installed. 0 to +10000 for positive polarity supply. maxVoltage 4 Float -10000 to 0 if negative polarity supply is installed. 0 to +10000 for positive polarity supply. stepAVoltage 4 Float -10000 to 10000 stepADurationSecs 4 Float 0.005 – 999.999 stepBVoltage 4 Float -10000 to 10000 stepBDurationSecs 4 Float 0.005 – 999.999 Cycles 4 Uint32 1 - 9999 3.1.8.6 EFieldRampedWave The EFieldRampedWave command is essentially an EFieldRectangularWave command with ramps replacing the steps. The EfieldEnable command must be issued first to enable the supply. Special cases: - rampARatePerSec OR rampBRatePerSec = 0 produces a sawtooth or reverse- sawtooth waveform. Command ID: 0xC606 Mnemonic: ACME_EFIELD_RAMPED_WAVE Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description minVoltage 4 Float -10000 to 0 if negative polarity supply is installed. 0 to +10000 for positive polarity supply. maxVoltage 4 Float -10000 to 0 if negative polarity supply is installed. 0 to +10000 for positive polarity supply. rampARatePerSec 4 Float rampADurationSecs 4 Float 0.0 – 999.999 holdADurationSecs 4 Float 0.0 – 999.999 rampBRatePerSec 4 Float,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p84,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 84 Parameter # Bytes Type Values Description rampBDurationSecs 4 Float 0.0 – 999.999 holdBDurationSecs 4 Float 0.0 – 999.999 cycles 4 Uint32 1 - 9999,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p85,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 85 3.1.9 ACME Telemetry The IOP requests telemetry at 1 Hz from all packages in the system, including the PI Location (PIL). See Section A.2, FCF Telemetry Protocol in FCF-ICD-0076A for a description of the telemetry protocol, including all required header fields. The user data of the ACME 1 Hz telemetry message that follows has a maximum size of 1460 - 16 (FCF header size) = 1444. In the FCF header, the BCD time is filled in by the FCFCommunications applications (the base classes). In the FCF header, the state field (byte 4) is filled in by ACME according to Table 21. In the tables below in the units column, the word ‘raw’ is used to indicate that the value is the same as the internal interface source and not converted prior to placement in the telemetry stream. Table 3 ACME Telemetry ACME Telemetry Description Reference Offset (bytes) Size (bytes) Comments System Telemetry Table 4 0 54 System-level data. System Setup Telemetry Table 14 54 18 Hardware configuration data Igniter Telemetry Table 5 72 20 Igniter data. IPSU Telemetry",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p85,1,Table 8 92 15 Status of communication with IPSUs. Flow Telemetry Table 9 107 51 Gas flow data. E-field Telemetry Table 10 158 14 E-field high voltage data. Diagnostic Telemetry Table 11 172 139 Sensor data. Fiber Telmetry Table 12 311 13 TFP motion data Extinction Detection Telemetry Table 13 324 39 Extinction detection state and parameters Imaging Telemetry Table 6 363 22 Commanded digital camera motion data. Chamber camera data. Imaging Motion Telemetry Table 7 385 25 Digital camera motion data from GetMotionTelemetry return value command. Log Messages 410 1034 A buffer of text for log messages.,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p86,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 86 Table 4 ACME System Telemetry System Telemetry Name Offset (bytes) Size (bytes) Data Type Description, Units, Range Mnemonic Heartbeat 0 1 Uint8 0-255 then repeat HEARTBEAT Cube Connected 1 1 Uint8 0 = Not connected, 1 = Connected CUBE_CONNECT CPU Temperature 2 4 Float °C, CPU on Lippert Cool XpressRunner-GS45, using LEMT. CPU_TEMP CPU Board Temperature 6 4 Float °C, Lippert Cool XpressRunner-GS45 board, using LEMT. CPU_BOARD_TEMP Cube Temperature 1 10 4 Float °C, TEMP1 on UEI Cube Power Layer CUBE_TEMP_1 Cube Temperature 2 14 4 Float °C, TEMP2 on UEI Cube Power Layer CUBE_TEMP_2 Hard Drive Temperature 18 1 Uint8 °C, /dev/sda1 Airflow Temperature from smartctl output. HARD_DRIVE_TEMP Test Point Name 19 30 String Current test point name Script State 49 1 Uint8 Script Line Number 50 2 Uint16 Script Duration Remaining 52 2 Uint16 Table 5 ACME Igniter Telemetry Igniter Telemetry Name Offset (bytes) Size (bytes) Data Type Units or Enumeration Source Mnemonic Igniter Arm Position 0 4 Int32 counts IGN_MOTOR_GET_POS Igniter Arm Commanded Position 4 4 Int32 counts Igniter Arm Max Position 8 4 Int32 counts Last Igniter Pulse Power 12 4 Float Volts Last Igniter Pulse Duration 16 4 Uint32 Milliseconds",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p87,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 87 Table 6 ACME Imaging Telemetry Imaging Telemetry Name Offset (bytes) Size (bytes) Data Type Description, Units, Range Source Mnemonic End LED Bank State 0 1 Uint8 State of the end (horizontal) LED bank on the ops camera. 0 = Off. 1 = On. Side LED Bank State 1 1 Uint8 State of the side (vertical) LED bank on the ops camera. 0 = Off. 1 = On. Illumination Cycing State 2 1 Uint8 State of LED bank cycling. 0 = Not cycling. 1 = Cycling. Analog Camera AGC 3 1 Uint8 Automatic gain control setting of the analog camera. 0=Off. 1=6 dB, 2 = 12 dB, 3 = 21 dB, 4 = 31 dB. Analog Camera Shutter Speed 4 1 Uint8 Shutter speed setting of the analog camera. 0=1/60s, 1=1/100s, 2=1/250s, 3=1/500s, 4=1/1000s, 5=1/2000s, 6=1/4000s, 7=1/10000s, 8= 1/20000s, 9=1/30000s Commanded Zoom Position 5 4 Uint32 Last commanded position for zoom. Actual is in Imaging Motion Telemetry and in IPSU-G telemetry if ACME is not controlling the camera. Commanded Iris Position 9 4 Uint32 Last commanded position for iris. Actual is in Imaging Motion Telemetry and in IPSU-G telemetry if ACME is not controlling the camera. Commanded Focus Position 13 4 Uint32 Last commanded position for focus. Actual is in Imaging Motion Telemetry and in IPSU-G telemetry if ACME is not controlling the camera. Commanded Filter Position 17 4 Uint32 Last commanded position for filter. Actual is in Imaging Motion Telemetry and in IPSU-G telemetry if ACME is not controlling the camera.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p87,1,"Filter Number 21 1 Uint8 0 = In between filters, 1-4 Table 7 ACME Imaging Motion Telemetry Imaging Motion Telemetry Name Offset (bytes) Size (bytes) Data Type Description, Units, Range Source Mnemonic Unused 0 1 Uint8 There are unused fields in this telemetry group because when ACME is controlling the camera motor directly it uses the same software and data structure as IPSU-G does.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D.pdf,p88,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 88 Imaging Motion Telemetry Name Offset (bytes) Size (bytes) Data Type Description, Units, Range Source Mnemonic Zoom Position 1 4 UInt32 Axis 1 of the PMD motion controller retrieved from the IPSU-G. Zoom lens position 0-85000 counts. 0 is zoomed out. 85000 is zoomed in. ZOOM_ MOTOR _GET_POS Iris Position 5 4 UInt32 Axis 2 of the PMD motion controller retrieved from the IPSU-G. Iris position 0-76000 counts. 0 is open. 76000 is closed. IRIS_ MOTOR _GET_POS Focus Position 9 4 UInt32 Axis 3 of the PMD motion controller retrieved from the IPSU-G. Focus position 0-101000 counts. 0 is near focus. 101000 is far focus. FOCUS_ MOTOR _GET_POS Filter Position 13 4 UInt32 Axis 4 of the PMD motion controller retrieved from the IPSU-G. Filter position 0-451000 counts. Filters at 0, 120000, 240000, and 360000 counts. FILTER_ MOTOR _GET_POS Unused 17 2 Uint16 Unused 19 2 Uint16 Unused 21 2 Uint16 Unused 23 2 Uint16 Table 8 ACME IPSU Telemetry IPSU Telemetry Name Offset (bytes) Size (bytes) Data Type Units or Enumeration Mnemonic IPSU Phase 0 1 Uint8 Table 22 IPSU_PHASE IPSU 1 Status 1 1 Uint8 Table 23 IPSU1_STATUS IPSU 2 Status 2 1 Uint8 Table 23 IPSU2_STATUS IPSU 3 Status 3 1 Uint8 Table 23 IPSU3_STATUS IPSU 4 Status 4 1 Uint8 Table 23 IPSU4_STATUS IPSU 5 Status 5 1 Uint8 Table 23 IPSU5_STATUS IPSU 6 Status 6 1 Uint8 Table 23 IPSU6_STATUS",,False
