PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,p11,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 11 1.0 SCOPE 1.1 Identification The purpose of this document is to identify the design of the communication interfaces used by the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME). 1.2 System Overview The Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) is mini-facility designed to support Principal Investigators (PI) interested in studying various aspects of advanced combustion in a microgravity environment. ACME will be integrated into the Fluids and Combustion Facility’s (FCF) Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) onboard the International Space Station in the US Lab Module. ACME will perform microgravity experiments to study fire suppression/flame extinguishment via 5 experiments: s-flame, Flame design, CLD Flame, E-Field Flames, and Burning Rate Emulator Flames as described in the ACME Baseline Description Document, ACME-DOC-004. ACME will allow investigation using pure as well as mixtures of gaseous fuels at various chamber atmosphere pressures, compositions, and oxygen concentrations. The microgravity environment allows for observation and measurement of flame formation and extinguishment processes that cannot be made in sustained gravity. The modular design of ACME allows for flexibility in configuration for specific experiments and easy maintainability of the hardware. The core elements of gaseous combustion are included in the ACME design as a basis for current and future gaseous combustion studies.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,p11,1,"These elements are establishment of gas flow through various exchangeable mass flow controllers, ignition of gas at the burner and the use of appropriate diagnostic measurement devices. The five identified experiments, and any future experiments, use these core elements to build a unique set of requirements for the study of various phenomena distinctive to gaseous combustion. The goal of ACME is to improve the fundamental understanding of combustion processes in order to enable increased efficiency and reduce pollutant emission in practical combustion. 1.3 Document Overview This document is divided into interfaces used to communicate with the ISS (i.e., external communication interfaces) and interfaces used to communicate with devices within ACME that require a protocol (i.e., internal communication interfaces).",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,p12,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 12 2.0 REFERENCED DOCUMENTS This section shall list the number and title of all documents referenced in this description Table 1 Referenced Documents Document Number Title NPR 7150.2A NASA Software Engineering Requirements GRC-SW-TPLT-IDD Interface Design Description Template ACME-REQ-001 Engineering Requirements Document FCF-ICD-0076A FCF Software Interface Control Document FCF-ICD-CIR-ACME FCF CIR/ACME Interface Control Document,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,p13,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 13 3.0 EXTERNAL COMMUNICATION INTERFACES ACME commands originate from the ACME Ground Software (AGS) CSCI on a ground workstation at the Telescience Center (TSC) at Glenn Research Center (GRC). These commands are then forwarded through a variety of systems until they eventually end up at the CIR on the ISS. Figure 1 ACME System Diagram 3.1 CIR Input/Output Processor (IOP) Interface Commands sent through the CIR IOP (Input Output Processor) are in the format required by FCF-ICD-0076A. See section A.1, FCF Command Protocol for a listing of the required command formats, including all required headers. All commands from the ground go to the IOP with the FCF Command Header and User Data filled in. Commands from ACME to the IPSUs and IPSU-Gs go through the FCFCommunications application (Base Classes) which fills in the Command Sequence Count and Time Fields in the FCF Command Header before submitting the packet to the IOP for routing. AGS CSCI AMP CSCI TReK POIC to ISS, etc IOP IPSU IPSU IPSU Color Camera HDD CIA PowerDNA Cube Flow Sys PMT s Temp Sys TFP E-Field Igniter",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,p14,0,"ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 14 3.1.1 ACME Commands Because it is a CIR PI package, ACME Package Command IDs in the FCF Command Header are assigned the range 0xC000-0xCFFF. The table below lists the commands, their IDs, and mnemonics. Detailed descriptions and parameter lists for each command follow the table. In the parameter list for each command the parameter name is the variable name in the code, which is useful to know when Qt scripts are being written. The name of the command, for example ‘NullCommand’, is also the name in code, in Qt scripts, and in log messages announcing the reception and completion of commands. Regarding parameters of type String, the entire available length can be used for characters without a terminating zero character. If a string is less than the available length then a terminating zero character needs to be used. The ground command software will make this transparent to the normal user. The Common Group is so-named because it is implemented in code shared between payloads.",,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,p14,1,Table 2 ACME Command ID Assignments Cmd ID Name Description Mnemonic Common Group 0xC001 NullCommand Do nothing ACME_COMMON_NULL 0xC002 ExternalCommand Pass through to external module ACME_COMMON_EXTERNAL_PASS_THRU 0xC003 GoToIdle Set the system state to Idle ACME_COMMON_STATE_IDLE 0xC004 GoToExp Set the system state to Experiment ACME_COMMON_STATE_EXPERIMENT 0xC005 GoToMaint Set the system state to Maintenance ACME_COMMON_STATE_MAINTENANCE 0xC006 GoToUpDownLink Set the system state to Uplink/downlink ACME_COMMON_STATE_UPLINK_DOWNLINK 0xC007 GoToSafed Set the system state to Safed. ACME_COMMON_STATE_SAFED 0xC008 SetRegistryItem Select registry item to change ACME_COMMON_REGISTRY_SET_ITEM 0xC009 SetRegistryValue Change registry item value ACME_COMMON_REGISTRY_SET_VALUE 0xC00A SaveRegistry Save registry changes ACME_COMMON_REGISTRY_SAVE 0xC00B Reboot Reboot operating system ACME_COMMON_SYSTEM_REBOOT,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,p15,0,ACME-DOC-010 Rev D 15 Cmd ID Name Description Mnemonic 0xC00C Shutdown Shutdown processor ACME_COMMON_SYSTEM_SHUTDOWN 0xC00D RunCli Run a command line interface command ACME_COMMON_CLI_RUN 0xC00E AbortCli Abort a command line interface command ACME_COMMON_CLI_ABORT 0xC00F FtpLocalFile Set FTP local file ACME_COMMON_FTP_LOCAL_FILE 0xC010 FtpLocalDirectory Set FTP local directory ACME_COMMON_FTP_LOCAL_DIR 0xC011 FtpRemoteFile Set FTP remote file ACME_COMMON_FTP_REMOTE_FILE 0xC012 FtpRemoteDirectory Set FTP remote directory ACME_COMMON_FTP_REMOTE_DIR 0xC013 FtpLoginParameters Set FTP login parameters ACME_COMMON_FTP_LOGIN_PARAMETERS 0xC014 FtpAbort Abort current FTP command ACME_COMMON_FTP_ABORT 0xC015 FtpSendFile Send file via FTP ACME_COMMON_FTP_SEND_FILE 0xC016 FtpSendDirectory Send directory via FTP ACME_COMMON_FTP_SEND_DIRECTORY 0xC017 FtpGetFile Get file via FTP ACME_COMMON_FTP_GET_FILE 0xC018 FtpGetDirectory Get directory via FTP ACME_COMMON_FTP_GET_DIRECTORY 0xC019 ChangeDirectory Linux ‘cd’ command ACME_COMMON_FS_CHANGE_DIR 0xC01A MakeDirectory Linux ‘mkdir -P’ command ACME_COMMON_FS_MAKE_DIR 0xC01B RemoveSingleEmptyDirectory Linux ‘rmdir command ACME_COMMON_FS_REMOVE_SINGLE_EMPTY_DIR 0xC01C RemoveMultipleEmptyDirectories Linux ‘rmdir –p’ command ACME_COMMON_FS_REMOVE_MULTIPLE_EMPTY_DIRS 0xC01D MoveFiles Linux ‘mv’ command ACME_COMMON_MOVE_FILES 0xC01E RenameFile Rename file ACME_COMMON_FS_RENAME_FILE 0xC01F RenameDirectory Rename directory ACME_COMMON_FS_RENAME_DIR,,False
PSI-23,Reference Data,PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reference Data/PSI-23_Reference Data_ACME-DOC-010_D Software Interface Design Description.pdf,p15,1,0xC020 RemoveFiles Linux ‘rm’ command ACME_COMMON_FS_REMOVE_FILES 0xC021 RecursiveRemoveFiles Linux ‘rm -r’ command ACME_COMMON_FS_REMOVE_FILES_RECURSIVE 0xC022 CopyFiles Linux ‘cp’ command ACME_COMMON_FS_COPY_FILES 0xC023 RecursiveCopyFiles Linux ‘cp -r command ACME_COMMON_FS_COPY_FILES_RECURSIVE 0xC024 ListFileInfo Linux ‘ls command ACME_COMMON_FS_LIST_FILES 0xC025 SetSource Set source for filesystem commands ACME_COMMON_FS_SET_SOURCE 0xC026 SetDestination Set destination for filesystem commands ACME_COMMON_FS_SET_DESTINATION 0xC027 CompareCrcTwoFiles Perform comparative CRC check ACME_COMMON_FS_COMPARE_CRC_TWO_FILES,,False
