PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,p8,0,National Aeronautics and Space Administration Science Requirements Disallowedq • ACME must be done within resource constraints • Difficult/costly or desired requirements were not allowedDifficult/costly or desired requirements were not allowed – Measurements • CH* imaging • infrared imaging • infrared imaging • Schlieren and shadowgraph imaging • PAH limit soot inception limit by laser scattering • soot inception limit by laser scattering • soot sampling • species concentrations (other than via CIR’s GC) b rner gas temperat res • burner gas temperatures – Testing • oxygen concentrations over 40% in the chamber f www.nasa.gov 8 • dynamic venting for constant pressure testing,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,p9,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration Nominal Operational Sequencepq • Flow > Ignite > Measure > Extinguish – Initiate burner flows at ignition condition g – Ignite flame • Wait for flame to stabilize – If needed adjust flows to initial test condition– If needed, adjust flows to initial test condition • Wait for flame to stabilize – If needed: ramp burner flow(s) • ramp burner flow(s) • activate and adjust electric field • translate TFP array St b fl – Stop burner flows • per extinction detection or test duration www.nasa.gov 9 Nicole Stott with CIR",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,p10,0,National Aeronautics and Space Administration ACME Hardware Requirementsq • Experiment Configuration – General (1.1) () – Burners (1.2-1.4) – Electric Field (1.5-1.7) Gas Delivery (1 8 19) – Gas Delivery (1.8-1.9) – Ignition (1.10) – Ambient Environment (1.11) – Data Acquisition (1.12) • Monitoring Measurements • Science Diagnostics • Science Diagnostics www.nasa.gov 10 Frank DeWinne with CIR,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,p11,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration General Requirements (1.1)q() • Burners all burners accommodated, but only one at a time exchangeable during spaceflight one duplicate of each provided to Project Scientist • Compatibility of burners and gas delivery system – fuels H2, CH4, C2H6, C3H8, C2H4, C3H6, and mixes – inerts N2, CO2, He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe, and mixes – fuel/inert mixtures of any fuel(s) and inert(s) above – oxygen mix ≤85% oxygen with any fuel(s) and/or inerts above • Chamber contents compatible with ≤40% oxygen • Simultaneous all configuration requirements apply simultaneously unless otherwise specified www.nasa.gov 11",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,p12,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration Burners (1.2-1.4)( ) • 3 types of exchangeable burners – porous spherical burners (3 sizes) pp () – circular gas-jet burners (6 sizes) – axisymmetric, coflow burner (1 size) • capable of inverse flames • capable of inverse flames www.nasa.gov 12",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,p13,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration Spherical Burners (1.2)p() • Porous sphere ODs 6.4, 9.5, 12.7 mm known to ±0.1 mm • Burner tube – inner diameter so pressure drop is ≤20% of that across burner – outer diameter ≤25% of sphere diameter – length ≥100 mm – material stainless steel • Sphericity 2.2s drop verification of flame with rmin/rmax>0.8 • Positions (1) centered in chamber & windows or (2) 30 mm ‘below’ window center • Volume (internal) ≤10 cc from last solenoid valves • Resistance withstand 0-450 oC www.nasa.gov 13",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,p14,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration Gas-Jet Burners (1.3)() • Shape straight tube with circular cross section • Length ≥40 inner diameters • Tube sizes 0.4±0.1 mm ID, 0.8±0.1 mm OD 0.8±0.1 mm ID, 1.2±0.1 mm OD 1.3±0.1 mm ID, 1.6±0.1 mm OD 2.1±0.1 mm ID, 2.4±0.1 mm OD 2.7±0.1 mm ID, 3.1±0.1 mm wall 3.2±0.1 mm ID, 3.6±0.1 mm wall ID and OD known to ±0.05 mm • Burner tip smooth, free from burs • Position on chamber axis either: (1) 10 mm ‘below’ window center (2) 30 mm ‘below’ window center • Material stainless steel www.nasa.gov 14 • Resistance withstand 0-1000 oC Soot volume fraction measurement in LSP experiment on STS-83",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_ACME Integrated Science Requirements Definition Review 20100505.pdf,p15,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration Coflow Burner (1.4)() • Concentricity within ±0.3 mm • Outlets orthogonal and flat within ±0.1 mm • Dimensions Honeycomb • Dimensions – inner tube 2.1±0.1 mm ID, 2.4±0.1 mm OD, length of ≥40 inner diameters ≤10 cc volume from last solenoid valves≤10 cc volume from last solenoid valves 0.5 to 1.0 mm downstream of annular outlet stainless steel – outer tube 25±0.5 mm ID 0.0 to 0.5 downstream of annular outlet • Annular flow axisymmetric, plug flow for velocities of ≤50 cm/s no swirl or turbulence ≤0.75 mm boundary layer at inner tube well characterized with cold flow for 10-50 cm/s • Axisymmetry flame axisymmetry demonstrated in 1g and 0g Position centered 12 mm ‘below’ window center www.nasa.gov 15 • Position centered, 12 mm below window center • Resistance withstand 0-1000 oC range",,False
