PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p8,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 1 of 117 1.0 INTRODUCTION 1.1 Purpose This document will define the engineering requirements necessary to successfully complete science and safety requirements. This document will be used by the ACME design engineers to develop the systems necessary for ACME. Interface and carrier requirements levied on ACME will be documented in the ACME ICD, FCF-ICD-CIR-ACME. ACME will use the existing Multi-User Droplet Combustion Apparatus (MDCA) hardware and software platform design already developed as a baseline for meeting the requirements found in this document where applicable. Due to the reuse of the MDCA platform design, ACME operational safety concerns will be addressed in existing MDCA documents where applicable. Requirements in this document levied on the ACME carrier, the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR), will be communicated to the ACME-CIR Interface / Sustaining Engineering Team 1.2 Scope This Engineering Requirements Document assesses the system requirements and lessons learned of the existing Multi-user Droplet Combustion Apparatus (MDCA) and Flame Extinguishment Experiment requirements needed to successfully build the hardware and develop avionics software and how these requirements are implemented. The Engineering Requirements Document (ERD) will provide information regarding the ACME experiment and ensure that requirements are quantifiable, verifiable and specific.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p8,1,"The ACME ERD consists of six sets of requirements: Science Requirements, Operational Requirements, FCF/CIR Integration Requirements, Derived Requirements, Software Requirements and Safety Requirements.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p9,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 2 of 117",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p9,1,"2.0 APPLICABLE DOCUMENTS This section lists the NASA/Government and non-NASA/Government specifications, standards, guidelines, handbooks, or other special publications applicable to this document. 2.1 Order of Precedence for Documents In the event of a conflict between this document and other documents specified herein, the requirements of this document shall apply. In the event of a conflict between this document and higher level documents, the higher level documents shall take precedence over this document. Nothing in this document supersedes applicable laws and regulations unless a specific exemption has been obtained. 2.2 Applicable Documents Applicable documents are those documents that form a part of this document. These documents carry the same weight as if they were stated within the body of this document. Table 2-1—Applicable Documents Document Number Document Title FCF-IA-MV-ACME ACME Integration Agreement FCF-ICD-0076 FCF Software Interface Control Document FCF-ICD-CIR-ACME ACME Interface Control Document for the FCF FCF-IDD-CIR_Rev.C CIR Payload Interface Definition Document CIR-DOC-4064 FCF CIR Payloads Accommodations Handbook CIR-DOC-3850A CIR Phase III Flight Safety Data Package ACME-SRD-001 ACME Science Requirements Document ACME-PLN-002 ACME Systems Engineering Management Plan (SEMP) ACME-PLN-003 ACME Science and Engineering Verification Plan (SEVP) P40020 SpaceDOC II Safety and Mission Assurance Plan P04005 Requirements Management P40030 Software Safety Plan",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p10,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 3 of 117 2.3 Reference Documents Reference documents are those documents that, though not a part of this document, serve to clarify the intent and contents of this document. Table 2-2—Reference Documents Document Number Document Title KHB 1700.7 Space Shuttle Payload Ground Safety Handbook MSFC-HDBK-527/ JSC 09604 Materials Selection List for Space Hardware Systems MSFC-SPEC-522 Design Criteria for Stress Corrosion Cracking NSTS 13830 Implementation Procedure for NSTS Payloads System Safety Requirements NSTS 14046 Payload Verification Requirements NSTS 1700.7 Safety Policy and Requirements for Payloads Using the Space Transportation System NSTS 1700.7 ISS Addendum Safety Policy and Requirements for Payloads Using the International Space Station NSTS 18798A MA2-95-048 Interpretation of NSTS Payload Safety Requirements SSP 50005 International Space Station Flight Crew Integration Standard SSP 52005 International Space Station Payload Flight Equipment Requirements and Guidelines for Safety-Critical Structures",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p11,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 4 of 117 3.0 REQUIREMENTS MANAGEMENT 3.1 Preparation for Requirements The preparation for developing proper project requirements is an important first step. Preparing in this sense means establishing procedures for identifying, approving, allocating and managing changes to requirements. This document along with the ZIN Requirements Management Procedure (P04005) is meant to facilitate this process. Other project documentation supports this effort and their completion is also required. A project shall use the following documents and procedures during the requirements development process:  ZIN Requirements Management Procedure P04005  SpaceDOC II Systems Engineering Management Plan (SEMP) or a Project SEMP as necessary T04001  ZIN Requirements Database T04003  Project Concept of Operations (ConOps) T04002  Project Engineering Requirements Document (ERD) 3.1.1 Stakeholder Analysis An understanding of a customer’s needs and goals for a project is paramount to the requirements development process. Ultimately, it is the customer who will be using the hardware built to the specifications laid out by the requirements a project develops. A customer’s satisfaction with the performance of the developed hardware and the benefits the hardware’s operation provides to stakeholders is the ultimate measurement of a projects success.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p11,1,"Therefore, a project shall perform a stakeholder analysis in the early phases (Pre-phase A or Phase A) of the project life cycle. 3.1.1.1 Identify Stakeholders Project requirements are elicited from Project Stakeholders. A stakeholder is any entity with a legitimate interest in the project. They are any person or organization that is actively involved in a project, or whose interests may be positively or negatively affected by execution or completion of the project. Some stakes an entity may have in your project are: Interest, rights, ownership, knowledge, impact or influence, contribution, etc. Examples of stakeholders can include:  ZIN Technologies (further broken into Project Manager, QA Manager, Resource Manager, etc.)  Principal Investigators and Project Scientists  Customer  NASA (further broken into GRC, JSC, ISS, etc.)  Product users  Product integrator Stakeholders shall be identified and documented in a Project SEMP using the Project SEMP template T04001.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p12,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 5 of 117 3.1.1.2 Identify Level 0 Stakeholder Requirements To initiate the requirements elicitation process a project should discuss and develop a short list of the main goals and products of the project. Ideally there would be less than 10 and around 4 or 5 main Level 0 requirements. This step should immediately follow the identification of the Project Stakeholders. 3.2 Obtaining Requirements Once the preparations for developing requirements have been made early in the life cycle of a project, the engineering team can begin to develop a set of requirements that will guide the design and development of the systems necessary to fulfill the Project Stakeholder’s needs. These “build-to” or engineering requirements begin to take shape typically in Phase A of a project life cycle and continue to be developed throughout the design phase of the project. 3.2.1 Identifying All Stakeholder Requirements After the initial, Level 0, stakeholder requirements have formed a foundation for discussion, a project can initiate the collection of all stakeholder requirements. Inputs for identifying more detailed stakeholder requirements will include: Source documents, stakeholder needs and project constraints such as cost, schedule and hardware/software solution restrictions.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p12,1,"Stakeholder requirements can also be found in interface documents, concept documents developed by the project, ZIN organizational and industry standards, test results and design maturation discussions, stakeholder meetings, etc. At this point in the project trade studies are ongoing and specific hardware and software solutions are beginning to be formulated. Requirements shall be properly documented with traceability and captured in a Requirements Development Matrix Database using the ZIN template T04003. Traceability shall include (as appropriate):  The source document identity  The paragraph title  The sentence number The requirements will then further be documented using this template as the project’s Engineering Requirements Document, which is generated from the database. Figure 1 shows the various sources of project requirements in a block flow diagram. This section should also make apparent what requirements are housed and maintained by the project engineering team and which are external requirements that are not included in this document but must also be considered during the design process. Note the color coded differentiation between internal project documents and external documents.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p13,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 6 of 117 Figure 3-1—Example Project Requirements Source Diagram 3.3 Analyzing Requirements Once all stakeholder requirements have been collected and organized it is essential that these requirements be analyzed. Ensuring that the requirement statements meet the necessary standard of quality for communication and implementation is part of this process. Projects shall review their requirement statements for quality and validity.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p14,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 7 of 117 3.3.1 Requirement Quality Requirement statements shall be clear and concise and decomposed to such a level that there is only one requirement in each requirement statement. Requirements shall use the word “shall” to clearly identify the demand upon the project team. Some tips to identify poor requirement statements are:  Use of superlatives such as best, most  Subjective language such as “user friendly”, “easy to use”  Vague pronouns  Ambiguous adverbs and adjectives such as “almost always”, “significant”, “real-time”, “various”, “few”, “many”, “appropriately”  Open-ended, non-verifiable terms such as “provide support”, “as a minimum”, “but not limited to”  Comparative phrases like “better than” or “higher quality”  Loopholes such as “if possible”, “as appropriate”  Other indefinites such as “etc.”, “to be determined (TBD)” At the time a requirement is identified, a verification method shall also be identified and documented in the Requirements Database. Verification methods include test, inspection, demonstration and analysis. This will encourage the writers of the requirement to evaluate whether they have written a verifiable requirement and provide an instant check of the requirement’s value. This step shall be done early in the project, typically in Phase B of the project life cycle.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p14,1,"3.3.2 Requirement Validation As mentioned above, understanding stakeholders’ needs and goals is an important step in forming requirements. Ensuring that your requirements are written in a way that fulfills these expectations is the next step. Along with understanding the manner in which the requirement will be verified it is also important to analyze whether or not the requirements and the solutions they offer align themselves with stakeholders initial operational and programmatic goals. Requirement validation requires forethought as to whether or not the hardware that the engineering requirements will produce can perform in a manner that fulfills the stakeholders goals. Simplified, validation becomes the question: Are you building the right hardware? For example, have your requirements properly captured the precision and accuracy needs of a particular measurement device? All project requirements shall be validated against stakeholder’s requirements. This includes any transition or derived requirements. 3.4 Approving Requirements The approval of the ERD serves as an official method of communication between the engineering team and the NASA Project Manager. Communication between the engineering team and NASA stakeholders will have invariably been occurring throughout the requirements formulation process. However, the ERD approval is where these requirements are formally agreed upon and baselined.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p15,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 8 of 117 3.5 Maintaining Requirements Maintenance of requirements is one of the most essential steps to a project’s requirements development effort. Even after the ERD has been baselined there could be many instances when it would need to be updated. A new stakeholder could be identified, maturation of the product design could bring about new derived requirements from within the engineering team, and of course frequent review of the requirements could result in finding flaws in the requirement statements that can be fixed. Following the logic of this process it is easy to see how many of the project’s fundamental documents are affected by the requirements. Therefore, when a requirement is modified or added, each document discussed in this process is also subject to change. This step shall happen regularly in the Project Life-Cycle to constantly ensure the integrity of the end product. Requirements documents, the Requirements Development Matrix and associated project documents shall be updated prior to major project gateway reviews at a minimum.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p16,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 9 of 117 4.0 ACME REQUIREMENT SOURCES 4.1 Evaluation of Requirement Sources Figure 1 represents the requirements flow for all of ACME requirements. Science requirements originate from the ACME Science Requirements Document (SRD). Operational requirements originate from the Science Operations Requirements Document (SORD). The carrier requirements are found in the CIR – ACME Interface Control Document (FCF-ICD-CIR- ACME). Derived Requirements develop from engineering requirements and are created by the ACME Project Team. Software Requirements are derived by the ACME engineering team with the purpose of command and control of hardware as well as telemetry reporting. Safety requirements are created from the ACME safety review hazard reports. These requirements are all incorporated into this the ACME Engineering Requirements Document (ACME-REQ-0001). FIGURE 1. ACME Requirements Flow",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p17,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 10 of 117 5.0 ACME HARDWARE PRODUCT DESCRIPTION The ACME investigation will be using the MDCA hardware and software concepts and design when appropriate. The ACME hardware consists of three hardware system domains, the Chamber Insert Assembly (CIA) System, the Avionics Package (AVP) System and the ACME Imaging Packages (IMAG) System. The CIA provides the mechanism for the combustion event. The ACME CIA Flow subsystem, which interfaces with the CIR Fuel/Oxidizer Management Assembly (FOMA), provides gas mixing and flow control. The AVP is the center of control and power distribution for ACME. And, the IMAG provides imaging of the combustion event including that provided by the carrier (CIR). The ACME PBS numbering convention for the systems in Figure 2 corresponds to the ACME Work Breakdown Structure (WBS) numbering for traceability across the ACME Project Lifecycle. The number convention utilizes a SSC (System/Subsystem Classification) followed by an underscore and the uniquely allocated four digit requirement identification number.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p17,1,FIGURE 2. ACME Product Breakdown Structure (PBS) 500_xxxx CIA ACME System S1101 600_xxxx Avionics Package (AVP) **2_xxxx Ground S/W (AGS) 620_xxxx Color Camera Data Store (CDS)520_xxxx Flow System (FLOW) 530_xxxx Burner System (BURNER) 540_xxxx Ignition System (IGN) 300_xxxx Imaging (IMAG) 510_xxxx Experiment Mounting Structure (EMS) 560_xxxx Diagnostics System (DIAG) 570_xxxx Measurement System (MEAS) 580_xxxx E-FIELD System (E-FIELD) 550_xxxx Engineering Analog Camera (OpsCam) 310_xxxx Data Color Camera Package 610_xxxx Main Computer System (MCS) 630_xxxx Power Distribution System (PDS) ACME Subsystems 320_xxxx HiBMS-1 Camera Package 320_xxxx HiBMS-2 Camera Package Cooling System Illumination System Flow Rate Measure Chamber Presure Chamber O2 Acceleration Presure Differential Burner Temp Far Field Temp Post Gas Comp. Burner as Electrode Electrode Mesh Electric Potential Ion Current 320_xxxx UV Imaging (UVI) Soot Vol Frac (SVF) Hot Soot Region (HSR) Hot Soot Free (HSF) Flight S/W (AMP) Data Sync & Record TFP Array Hot Soot Free (HSF) Radiometers Radiant Emissions PMT Package Chemilluminescence Ground Commanding Ops Procedures ACME CIR SSC_xxxx SSC = System/Subsystem Classification xxxx = REQ ID,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p18,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 11 of 117 5.1 ACME Chamber Insert Assembly (CIA) System The ACME CIA System provides all the necessary hardware and interfaces to perform science on one platform. The available interfaces are the CIR Water Thermal Cooling System (WTCS), and the CIR Fuel/Oxidizer Management Assembly (FOMA). Most experimental functions occur within the CIA structure and many associated subsystems are found on the CIA. The CIA consists of the Experiment Mounting Structure (EMS) which is the functional hardware and upon which the various subsystems are structurally based. Other subsystems found on and around the CIA include the Gas Flow System (FLOW), Exchangeable Gas Burner System (Burner), Ignition System (IGN), Engineering Analog Camera System (OpsCam), Diagnostics System (DIAG), Measurement System (MEAS), E-Field System (E-FIELD). FIGURE 3. ACME Chamber Insert Assembly (CIA) Design 5.2 Experiment Mounting Structure (EMS) System The EMS provides the main structure for the CIA and acts as the primary mechanical interface with the CIR combustion chamber. It consists of two endplates, support structure, and extraction handles.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p19,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 12 of 117 5.3 Cooling Plate System ACME will use two cold plates mounted to the Experiment Mounting Structure interfacing with the CIR Water Thermal Control System (WTCS). One cooling plate can be attached to each end plate. The hardware to accommodate this is identical to that used on MDCA System. The cooling plates are plumbed together using rigid tubing, and attached to the IRR WTCS interfaces with flexible hoses and quick disconnects. 5.4 Gas Flow System (FLOW) There is a reconfigurable flow system on the CIA that is auxiliary to the CIR FOMA. Gases are received from FOMA via quick disconnects on the Interface Resource Ring (IRR). The gases are directed through interchangeable Mass Flow Controllers, which are replaced as experiments require. Gases are then directed through a series of switching and isolation valves to the burner or directly vented into the chamber for chamber fills. 5.5 Gas Burner System (Burner) The burner system consists of three different types of exchangeable burners made from varying inner and outer diameter stainless steel tubing supported by an electrically insulating ceramic base. There are 4 types of burners: Spherical (3), Gas Jet (7), Co-flow (1) and Burning Rate Emulator (2). Each burner tip is smooth and without burs or sharp edges to aid in the gas deployment.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p19,1,"The burners are mounted with a coupling that allows exchangeability of the burners and maintains the precise alignment of the system. Spherical and Gas-Jet burners also include burner temperature thermocouples. 5.6 Ignition System (IGN) The Ignition System will reuse the design of the MDCA System igniter, with a different deployment scheme. The ignition system consists of a hot wire igniter subassembly mounted to a deployment arm. A Haydon-Kerk linear actuator motor and driver board controls the position of the deployment arm. The igniter tip is mounted to an insulating ceramic structure attached to the end of the arm. The entire top assembly is removed from the arm and replaced on an experiment basis to maintain the precise geometry necessary for igniter functionality and to reposition the igniter tip based on the burner tip’s position. 5.7 Analog Camera System (OpsCam) The Analog Camera System will be provided, internal to the combustion chamber, primarily as a near real-time visual aid to engineering for hardware operation monitoring and troubleshooting. In addition, this view will provide a near real-time “look” into the chamber for science throughout the flame combustion event. 5.8 Illumination System (OpsCam) The Illumination System consists of an array of high intensity, white light LEDs that receive power from the relay board hardware and are controlled by the Data Acquisition Unit. The",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p20,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 13 of 117 illumination system intensity will be variable to avoid saturation of diagnostics. This illumination system is provided to support the Analog Camera System. 5.9 Diagnostic System (DIAG) Two of the diagnostic systems the Radiometers, the Photomultiplier Tube Modules (PMTs), and the Thin Fiber Pyrometry (TFP) Array are located on the Insert. This hardware supports Diagnostic Analysis of the Flames. 5.10 Radiometer System The Radiometers will provide relative measure of flame radiation and emissions, over the wavelength range of 0.2 m – 11 m, throughout the combustion event. The Radiometer System will be mounted on the CIA providing a direct view of the combustion event. Data from the Radiometers will be handled by the Data Acquisition Unit mounted inside the Chamber. Power via the Relay Board to the Radiometer System is provided by ACME AVP System (section 3.4.2). 5.11 Photomultiplier Tube (PMT) System The Photomultiplier Tube Package will provide high-sensitivity relative measurements of flame radiation and chemiluminescent emissions of electromagnetic energy over a wide range of frequencies and intensity levels throughout the combustion event as well as flame detection and extinction determination. The PMT System consists of three Hamamatsu PMT modules, mounted on the CIA providing a direct view of the combustion event.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p20,1,"Additional filters will be required for the 2 PMT modules used for narrowband emissions. Data from the PMT modules will be handled by the Data Acquisition Unit mounted inside the Chamber. Power via the Relay Board to the PMT System is provided by ACME AVP System (section 3.4.2). 5.12 Thin Fiber Pyrometry (TFP) Array System A Thin Fiber Pyrometry (TFP) Array arm mounted to a translating motor to move the array into and out of the flame. The translating motor will be an Ultramotion brand linear stepper motor and encoder, similar to that used by MDCA System for its igniter. The Array will be stowed out of the combustion area during E-Field testing, or when the Array is not required, but will not be removed from the chamber. The array can be aligned with either the HiBMs-2 or ACME data color camera. 5.13 Measurement System (MEAS) The ACME measurement system encompasses the measurement subsystems involved in detection and measurement of the experiments with the exception of diagnostic systems mentioned above in section 3.4.1.6 and CIR provided diagnostics. The following sensors are a part of the Measurement System: Far Field Thermocouple Array, Pressure Transducers for differential pressure measurement, and heat flux sensors for the BRE burners.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p20,2,"5.14 E-Field System (E-FIELD) The E-Field system consists of two High Voltage (HV) sources (one negative and one positive polarity), a passive network, an insulated mesh electrode and an insulated burner electrically connected to the returns of the HV supplies.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p21,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 14 of 117 5.15 ACME Avionics Package System (AVP) The Avionics Package System (AVP) provides power conditioning and power distribution, system control, data acquisition and CIR and ground interface for the ACME insert. The AVP will house the main electronics for the ACME hardware and will be mounted on the CIR Optics Bench. FIGURE 4. ACME Avionic Package (AVP) Design 5.16 Avionics Package (AVP) The Avionics Package will house the main electronics and data storage for the ACME hardware and will be mounted at the CIR PIL (PI location), using the standard Optics Bench interfaces. 5.16.1 Main Computer System (MCS) – Control and Data Management A main computer processor is responsible for controlling CIA hardware, imaging hardware to execute test points as well as acquiring, storing, and downlinking experiment data. Experiment control and sequencing will be uplinked from the ACME Ground System (AGS) software, at the TSC, to the ACME Main Processor (AMP) System software that is resident on the main processor in a PC/104 stack configuration. The main processor communicates with the CIR",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p22,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 15 of 117 Input/Output Processor (IOP). The interface to the CIR IOP is necessary for transferring commands, downlinked data and images, through the International Space Station (ISS) communications system. 5.16.2 Color Camera Data Store (CDS) System The ACME Color Camera data is stored on a hard drive in the AVP. The hard drive is removable and replaceable on orbit. 5.16.3 Power Distribution System (PDS) The AVP Power & Distribution System provides power to the equipment in within the AVP and the CIA via four 28VDC, 8 amp circuits from the CIR’s Electrical Power Control Unit (EPCU). In the AVP, the power is converted to the necessary voltages, regulated and filtered. Power distribution and control within the Chamber is handled by a data acquisition unit and a pair of custom designed relay boards. These devices handle all of the communications with the AVP as well as the activation of various components of the CIA. 5.17 ACME Imaging Package System (IMAG) The ACME Imaging (IMAG) System provides all optical imaging and non-optical imaging information for acquisition and storage concerning each of the ACME experiments. 5.18 Data Color Camera Imaging System (DataCam) The ACME Data Color Camera Package (see Figure 4) will provide a view of the combustion event. The current Color Camera Package concept consists of an Allied Vision (formerly Prosilica) GC1380CH (30fps) camera.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p22,1,"The camera is external to the chamber mounted at a UML Location on the Optics Bench. Power to the Data Color Camera Package is supplied via the UML location. 5.19 CIR Imaging Subsystems ACME will utilize the CIR LLL-UV, HiBMS-1, and HiBMS-2 Packages.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p23,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 16 of 117 FIGURE 5. ACME Data Color Camera Package (DataCam) Design 6.0 INTERFACES This section outlines the interfaces between the project’s hardware as a system to other external systems, any launch configuration interfaces, and on-orbit external interfaces that are needed for successful operation of the ACME System. Internal interfaces are also addressed. Data Color Camera Fixed Mirror Interface to CIR Optics Bench Motorized Zoom Lens Optics Housing Electronics Enclosure",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p24,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 17 of 117 6.1 Internal Interfaces The internal interfaces between ACME and CIR are listed below and the requirements for these interfaces will be defined in ACME Interface Control Document for the Fluids and Combustion Facility, FCF-ICD-CIR-ACME.  Structural/Mechanical and Stowage  Electrical Power  Command and Data Handling  Payload and Diagnostics  Thermal Control  Exhaust Vent  FOMA Gas Delivery  Environmental 6.2 External Interfaces 6.2.1 Launch Configuration Interfaces ACME provided hardware is non-operational in whatever launch vehicle is chosen to get it on- orbit. ACME hardware will be stowed in standard containers provided by ACME and will only have physical and structural interfaces with the containers. 6.2.2 On-orbit External Interfaces ACME has no direct external interfaces on-orbit. All interfaces with the FCF, US LAB, ISS and the ground are provided indirectly through CIR.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p25,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 18 of 117 7.0 ACME ENGINEERING REQUIREMENTS Engineering requirements are ACME’s build-to requirement response to science requirements levied by NASA. This section is drawn from the information filled into the ZIN Requirements Development Matrix (T04003) using the Requirements Management Procedure (P04005.) ACME’s requirements are organized according to the project’s PBS. This helps to keep the reader and reviewer thinking in terms of the whole system. The SSC_ID column is for useful organization and sorting of the system requirements in the Requirements Tracking Matrix (RTM) into categories by system, subsystem, or other traits for measurements and metrics that may be of interest. The SSC (System/Subsystem Classification) is a three digit number which relates to the Product Breakdown Structure (PBS) which in turn is a subset of the Work Breakdown Structure (WBS). The first digit of the SSC relates the requirement to the system assignment. The second digit of the SSC relates the requirement to the subsystem assignment. The third digit of the SSC is used to relate the requirement to the product classification or type: flight hardware product (0), flight software product (1), or ground software product (2), in which they affect. The SSC is followed by an underscore as a delineator and then a unique four digit identification number.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p25,1,"The underscore, versus the dash, is used to keep the SSC and ID married as one object in the database. Requirements with like qualities of interest may be grouped together by ID number for ease of sorting. Table 7-1—Engineering Requirements System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description SPACE110 1.1 General Experiment Configuration BURNER 530_0010 1.1.1 Burners – Installation ACME shall install a single burner one at a time within the CIR chamber. BURNER 530_0020 1.1.2 Burners – Exchangeable ACME shall provide that all burners are exchangeable during spaceflight. BURNER 530_0030 1.1.2 Burners – ORU ACME shall provide all burners as ORUs. FLOW 520_0010 1.1.4.1 Flow System H/W Compatibility - Hydrogen ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the gaseous fuel hydrogen. BURNER 530_0050 1.1.4.1 Burner H/W Compatibility - Hydrogen ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the gaseous fuel hydrogen. FLOW 520_0020 1.1.4.1 Flow H/W Compatibility - Methane ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the gaseous fuel methane. BURNER 530_0060 1.1.4.1 Burner H/W Compatibility - Methane ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the gaseous fuel methane. FLOW 520_0030 1.1.4.1 Flow H/W Compatibility - Ethane ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the gaseous fuel ethane.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p25,2,BURNER 530_0070 1.1.4.1 Burner H/W Compatibility - Ethane ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the gaseous fuel ethane.,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p26,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 19 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description FLOW 520_0040 1.1.4.1 Flow H/W Compatibility - Propane ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the gaseous fuel propane. BURNER 530_0080 1.1.4.1 Burner H/W Compatibility - Propane ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the gaseous fuel Propane. FLOW 520_0050 1.1.4.1 Flow H/W Compatibility - Ethylene ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the gaseous fuel ethylene. BURNER 530_0090 1.1.4.1 Burner H/W Compatibility - Ethylene ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the gaseous fuel Ethylene. FLOW 520_0060 1.1.4.1 Flow H/W Compatibility - Propylene ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the gaseous fuel propylene. BURNER 530_0100 1.1.4.1 Burner H/W Compatibility - Propylene ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the gaseous fuel Propylene. FLOW 520_0070 1.1.4.1 Flow H/W Compatibility - Fuel Mixtures ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with mixtures of any of the following gaseous fuels: 1) hydrogen 2) methane 3) ethane 4) propane 5) ethylene 6) propylene BURNER 530_0110 1.1.4.1 Burner H/W Compatibility - Fuel Mixtures ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with gaseous fuel mixtures of any of the following gaseous fuels:",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p26,1,1) hydrogen 2) methane 3) ethane 4) propane 5) ethylene 6) propylene FLOW 520_0080 1.1.4.2 Flow H/W Compatibility - Nitrogen ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the inert gas nitrogen. BURNER 530_0120 1.1.4.2 Burner H/W Compatibility - Nitrogen ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the inert gas nitrogen. FLOW 520_0090 1.1.4.2 Flow H/W Compatibility - Carbon Dioxide ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the inert gas carbon dioxide. BURNER 530_0130 1.1.4.2 Burner H/W Compatibility - Carbon Dioxide ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the inert gas carbon dioxide. FLOW 520_0100 1.1.4.2 Flow H/W Compatibility - Helium ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the inert gas helium.,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p27,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 20 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description BURNER 530_0140 1.1.4.2 Burner H/W Compatibility - Helium ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the inert gas helium. FLOW 520_0110 1.1.4.2 Flow H/W Compatibility - Neon ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the inert gas neon. BURNER 530_0150 1.1.4.2 Burner H/W Compatibility - Neon ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the inert gas neon. FLOW 520_0120 1.1.4.2 Flow H/W Compatibility - Argon ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the inert gas argon. BURNER 530_0160 1.1.4.2 Burner H/W Compatibility - Argon ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the inert gas argon. FLOW 520_0130 1.1.4.2 Flow H/W Compatibility - Krypton ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the inert gas krypton. BURNER 530_0170 1.1.4.2 Burner H/W Compatibility - Krypton ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the inert gas krypton. FLOW 520_0140 1.1.4.2 Flow H/W Compatibility - Xenon ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with the inert gas xenon. BURNER 530_0180 1.1.4.2 Burner H/W Compatibility - Xenon ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with the inert gas xenon.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p27,1,FLOW 520_0150 1.1.4.2 Flow H/W Compatibility - Inert Mixtures ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with mixtures of any of the following inert gases: 1) nitrogen 2) carbon dioxide 3) helium 4) neon 5) argon 6) krypton 7) xenon BURNER 530_0190 1.1.4.2 Burner H/W Compatibility - Inert Mixtures ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with mixtures of any of the following inerts gases: 1) nitrogen 2) carbon dioxide 3) helium 4) neon 5) argon 6) krypton 7) xenon,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p28,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 21 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description FLOW 520_0160 1.1.4.1 1.1.4.2 1.1.4.3 Flow H/W Compatibility - Fuel-Inert Mixtures ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with mixtures of any of the following gaseous fuels and inert gases: 1) hydrogen 2) methane 3) ethane 4) propane 5) ethylene 6) propylene 7) nitrogen 8) carbon dioxide 9) helium 10) neon 11) argon 12) krypton 13) xenon BURNER 530_0200 1.1.4.1 1.1.4.2 1.1.4.3 Burner H/W Compatibility - Fuel-Inert Mixtures ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with mixtures of any of the following gaseous fuels and inert gases: 1) hydrogen 2) methane 3) ethane 4) propane 5) ethylene 6) propylene 7) nitrogen 8) carbon dioxide 9) helium 10) neon 11) argon 12) krypton 13) xenon FLOW 520_0170 1.1.4.4 Flow H/W Compatibility - O2 - Fuel Mixtures ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with mixtures of oxygen and one or more gaseous fuels (hydrogen, methane, ethane, propane, ethylene, and propylene) for oxygen concentrations up to 85% on a volume (molar) basis. BURNER 530_0210 1.1.4.4 Burner H/W Compatibility - O2 - Fuel Mixtures ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with mixtures of oxygen and one or more gaseous fuels (hydrogen, methane, ethane, propane, ethylene, and propylene) for oxygen concentrations up to 85% on a volume (molar) basis.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p28,1,"FLOW 520_0180 1.1.4.4 Flow H/W Compatibility - O2 - Inert Mixtures ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with mixtures of oxygen and one or more inert gases (nitrogen, carbon dioxide, helium, neon, argon, krypton, and xenon) for oxygen concentrations up to 85% on a volume (molar) basis.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p29,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 22 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description BURNER 530_0220 1.1.4.4 Burner H/W Compatibility - O2 - Inert Mixtures ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with mixtures of oxygen and one or more inert gases (nitrogen, carbon dioxide, helium, neon, argon, krypton, and xenon) for oxygen concentrations up to 85% on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0190 1.1.4.4 Flow H/W Compatibility - O2 - Fuel & Inert Mixtures ACME shall provide a gas delivery system that is compatible with mixtures of oxygen and one or more gaseous fuels (hydrogen, methane, ethane, propane, ethylene, and propylene) and one or more inert gases (nitrogen, carbon dioxide, helium, neon, argon, krypton, and xenon) for oxygen concentrations up to 85% on a volume (molar) basis. BURNER 530_0230 1.1.4.4 Burner H/W Compatibility - O2 - Fuel & Inert Mixtures ACME shall provide all burners to be compatible with mixtures of oxygen and one or more gaseous fuels (hydrogen, methane, ethane, propane, ethylene, and propylene) and one or more inert gases (nitrogen, carbon dioxide, helium, neon, argon, krypton, and xenon) for oxygen concentrations up to 85% on a volume (molar) basis. EMS 510_0010 1.1.5 Hardware Compatibility – CIR Chamber The CIA hardware shall be compatible with concentrations of oxygen up to 40% on a volume (molar) basis.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p29,1,1.8 Source Gas Supply FLOW 520_0200 1.8.1 1.8.1.1 Experimental Fuels – H2 ACME shall provide 100% Hydrogen on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0210 1.8.1 1.8.1.1 Experimental Fuels – CH4 ACME shall provide 100% Methane on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0220 1.8.1 1.8.1.1 Experimental Fuels – C2H4 ACME shall provide 100% Ethylene on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0230 1.8.1 1.8.1.1 Experimental Fuels – Hydrocarbon Mixture 1 ACME shall provide a fuel mixture of 44.4/55.6 H2/CH4 on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0240 1.8.1 1.8.1.1 Experimental Fuels – Hydrocarbon Mixture 2 ACME shall provide a fuel mixture of 55.6/44.4 H2/CH4 on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0250 1.8.1 1.8.1.1 Experimental Fuels – Hydrocarbon Mixture 3 ACME shall provide a fuel mixture of 66.7/33.3 H2/CH4 on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0260 1.8.1 1.8.1.2 Oxidizer Mixture 1 ACME shall provide an oxidizer mixture of 85/15 O2/N2 on a volume (molar) basis.,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p30,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 23 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description FLOW 520_0270 1.8.1 1.8.1.2 Oxidizer Mixture 2 ACME shall provide an oxidizer mixture of 50/50 O2/N2 on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0280 1.8.1 1.8.1.2 Oxidizer Mixture 3 ACME shall provide an oxidizer mixture of 40/60 O2/N2 on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0290 1.8.1 1.8.1.2 Oxidizer Mixture 4 ACME HWCI shall provide an oxidizer mixture of 21/79 O2/N2 on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0300 1.8.1 1.8.1.2 Oxidizer Mixture 5 ACME shall provide an oxidizer mixture of 40/60 O2/He on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0310 1.8.1 1.8.1.3 Inert – N2 ACME shall provide 100% Nitrogen on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0320 1.8.1 1.8.1.3 Inert – He ACME shall provide 100% Helium on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0330 1.8.2 1.8.2.2 Oxidizer Mixture 6 ACME shall provide an oxidizer mixture of 85/15 O2/CO2 on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0340 1.8.2 1.8.2.2 Oxidizer Mixture 7 ACME shall provide an oxidizer mixture of 40/60 O2/CO2 on a volume (molar) basis. FLOW 520_0350 1.8.2 1.8.2.3 Inert – CO2 ACME shall provide 100% Carbon Dioxide on a volume (molar) basis. 1.1 Chamber Atmosphere CIA 500_0081 1.11.4 Chamber Atmosphere - Initial Pressure Precision The ACME flow system shall provide the capability to vary the initial pretest chamber with a precision of ±3%.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p30,1,"CIA 500_0110 1.11.6 Chamber Atmosphere – Free Volume ACME shall provide a chamber free volume of at least 80 liters. CIA 500_0120 1.11.7 Chamber Atmosphere – Free ACME should distribute hardware components uniformly in the chamber free volume relative to the chamber axis and the intersection of the CIR chamber window axes. CIA 500_0130 1.11.8 Chamber Atmosphere – ZOE Spherical Burner ACME shall provide a free area of at least 200mm in diameter centered on the spherical burner and be kept clear of solid objects except the burner, tube, igniter, probes, TFP fibers and diagnostics for the spherical burner tests.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p31,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 24 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description CIA 500_0140 1.11.9 Chamber Atmosphere – ZOE 1 Gas-jet, Coflow, & BRE ACME shall provide a cylindrical free area for the gas-jet burner, coflow, and BRE burner tests that is at least 200mm in diameter, extends at least 100mm downstream and at least 50mm upstream from the burner outlet. CIA 500_0150 1.11.9 Chamber Atmosphere – ZOE 2 Gas-jet, Coflow, & BRE ACME shall provide a cylindrical free area for the gas-jet burner and coflow burner tests that is at least 150mm in diameter, extends at least 200mm downstream from the burner outlet. CIA 500_0180 1.11.12 Chamber Atmosphere – Flame Only FOV ACME shall provide chamber hardware with a non-reflective background such that there is nothing other than the flame that emits light within each optical detector’s field of view and spectral range of sensitivity. 1.9 Burner Gas Delivery FLOW 520_0380 1.9.1 Gas Supply - BRE, Spherical & Gas-Jet Burner The ACME flow system shall simultaneously supply the BRE gas-jet or spherical burner with any combination of fuel, inert, or oxidizer source gas. FLOW 520_0410 1.9.2 Gas Supply - Normal Coflow Burner The ACME flow system shall simultaneously deliver fuel, oxidizer and inert to either the inner tube or the outer tube of the coflow burner.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p31,1,"FLOW 520_0440 1.9.3 Gas Supply Mixture – Control The ACME flow system shall provide the capability for the composition of any mixture to be controlled to within ± 0.01 mole fraction. FLOW 520_0450 1.9.3 Gas Supply Mixture – MFC Response Time ACME flow system shall know the gas mixture concentrations ejected from the burner as a function of time on a 1 second basis. FLOW 520_0500 1.9.4 Gas Flow Rate – Shutoff ACME shall provide a flow system which has a gas flow shutoff capability for all gases. FLOW 520_0530 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.1 1.9.5.1.1 CLD Flame – Coflow Fuel Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source fuel flow range of 0 to 0.2 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ±5% of the set point for the coflow burner. FLOW 520_0540 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.1 1.9.5.1.2 CLD Flame – Coflow Oxidizer Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source oxidizer flow range of 0 to 15 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the coflow burner.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p32,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 25 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description FLOW 520_0550 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.1 1.9.5.1.3 CLD Flame – Coflow Inert Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source inert flow range of 0 to 0.1 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the coflow burner. FLOW 520_0560 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.2 1.9.5.2.1 E-FIELD Flame – Gas-Jet Fuel Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source fuel flow range of 0 to 0.1 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the gas-jet burner. FLOW 520_0570 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.2 1.9.5.2.3 E-FIELD Flame – Gas-Jet Inert Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source inert flow range of 0 to 0.05 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the gas-jet burner. FLOW 520_0580 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.3 1.9.5.3.1 E-FIELD Flame – Coflow Fuel Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source fuel flow range of 0 to 0.1 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the coflow burner. FLOW 520_0590 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.3 1.9.5.3.2 E-FIELD Flame – Coflow Oxidizer Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source oxidizer flow range of 0 to 2.0 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the coflow burner.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p32,1,"FLOW 520_0600 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.3 1.9.5.3.3 E-FIELD Flame – Coflow Inert Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source inert flow range of 0 to 0.05 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the coflow burner. FLOW 520_0610 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.4 1.9.5.4.1 Flame Design – Spherical Fuel Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source fuel flow range of 0 to 0.3 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the spherical burner. FLOW 520_0620 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.4 1.9.5.4.2 Flame Design – Spherical Oxidizer Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source oxidizer flow range of 0 to 3.0 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the spherical burner. FLOW 520_0630 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.4 1.9.5.4.3 Flame Design – Spherical Inert Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source inert flow range of 0 to 10 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the spherical burner. FLOW 520_0700 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.7 1.9.5.7.1 s-Flame – Spherical Fuel Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source fuel flow range of 0 to 0.5 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the spherical burner.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p33,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 26 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description FLOW 520_0710 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.7 1.9.5.7.3 s-Flame – Spherical Inert Flow ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing a source inert flow range of 0 to 1.0 slpm, on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the spherical burner. FLOW 520_0711 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.8 1.9.5.8.1 BRE- BRE Fuel Flow ACME shall provide a flow system capable of providing a source fuel flow range of 0 to 2.0 slpm on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the BRE Burner. FLOW 520_0712 1.9.4 1.9.5 1.9.5.8 1.9.5.8.3 BRE- BRE Inert Flow ACME shall provide a flow system capable of providing a source inert flow range of 1.0 slpm on a N2 basis, within ± 5% of the set point for the BRE Burner. FLOW 520_0720 1.9.6 Gas Flow – Capacity ACME shall provide a flow system which is capable of providing gases at the maximum flow rates (20 slpm for oxidizer and inert and 2.0 slpm for fuel) for at least 60 seconds. FLOW 520_0730 1.9.7 Burner Gas – Temperature ACME shall design a flow system that causes no more than a 300±10K variance in burner gas temperature. FLOW 520_0740 1.9.8 Burner Gas – Dry Gases ACME shall provide dry burner gas. FLOW 520_0750 1.9.8 Burner Gas – Trace Contaminants ACME shall provide the burner gas such that the net amount of trace contaminants is less than 0.001 mole fraction.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p33,1,1.2 Spherical Burner BURNER 530_0240 1.2.1 Spherical Burner Tip – Porous Sphere ACME shall provide each spherical burner with a porous sphere at the outlet tip. BURNER 530_0250 1.2.1 Spherical Burner Tip – 6.4 OD ACME shall provide a spherical burner with a fixed outer diameter of 6.4mm for the spherical tip with a dimensional tolerance within ± 0.3mm and a measurement precision of ± 0.1mm. BURNER 530_0260 1.2.1 Spherical Burner Tip – 9.5 OD ACME shall provide a spherical burner with a fixed outer diameter of 9.5mm for the spherical tip with a measured tolerance within ± 0.3mm and an accuracy of ± 0.1mm. BURNER 530_0270 1.2.1 Spherical Burner Tip – 12.7 OD ACME shall provide a spherical burner with a fixed outer diameter of 12.7mm for the spherical tip with a measured tolerance within ± 0.3mm and an accuracy of ± 0.1mm. BURNER 530_0280 1.2.2 1.2.2.3 Spherical Burner – 6.4 OD Single 100 Supply ACME shall provide a single supply tube of at least 100mm length from the sphere’s center for the spherical burner with a 6.4 OD tip.,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p34,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 27 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description BURNER 530_0290 1.2.2 1.2.2.3 Spherical Burner – 9.5 OD Single 100 Supply ACME shall provide a single supply tube of at least 100mm length from the sphere’s center for the spherical burner with a 9.5 OD tip. BURNER 530_0300 1.2.2 1.2.2.3 Spherical Burner – 12.7 OD Single 100 Supply ACME shall provide a single supply tube of at least 100mm length from the sphere’s center for the spherical burner with a 12.7 OD tip. BURNER 530_0310 1.2.2 1.2.2.1 Spherical Burner – 6.4 OD Differential Pressure ACME shall provide a pressure drop, in the spherical burner supply tube, which is at most 20% of the pressure drop across the 6.4 OD burner sphere with a cold flow rate of 5 slpm. BURNER 530_0320 1.2.2 1.2.2.1 Spherical Burner – 9.5 OD Differential Pressure ACME shall provide a pressure drop, in the spherical burner supply tube, which is at most 20% of the pressure drop across the 9.5 OD burner sphere with a cold flow rate of 5 slpm. BURNER 530_0330 1.2.2 1.2.2.1 Spherical Burner – 12.7 OD Differential Pressure ACME shall provide a pressure drop, in the spherical burner supply tube, which is at most 20% of the pressure drop across the 12.7 OD burner sphere with a cold flow rate of 5 slpm.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p34,1,"BURNER 530_0340 1.2.2 1.2.2.2 Spherical Burner – 6.4 OD Tip versus Supply OD ACME shall provide an outer diameter, for each spherical burner supply tube, of no more than 25% of the 6.4 OD porous sphere. BURNER 530_0350 1.2.2 1.2.2.2 Spherical Burner – 9.5 OD Tip versus Supply OD ACME shall provide an outer diameter, for each spherical burner supply tube, of no more than 25% of the 9.5 OD porous sphere. BURNER 530_0360 1.2.2 1.2.2.2 Spherical Burner – 12.7 OD Tip versus Supply OD ACME shall provide an outer diameter, for each spherical burner supply tube, of no more than 25% of the 12.7 OD porous sphere. BURNER 530-0370 1.2.3 Spherical Burner – Flame Spherical Test ACME shall produce a flame in microgravity conditions with rmin/rmax exceeding 0.8. BURNER 530_0380 1.2.4, 1.2.4.1 Spherical Burner – Supply Tube Axis Coincident ACME shall position the spherical burner such that supply tube axis is coincident with the CIR chamber axis within ± 2mm along its length. BURNER 530_0390 1.2.4, 1.2.4.1 Spherical Burner – Supply Tube Axis Orthogonal ACME shall position the spherical burner such that supply tube axis is orthogonal to the CIR chamber window axes.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p35,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 28 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description BURNER 530_0400 1.2.4, 1.2.4.2 Spherical Burner – Position 0 ACME shall position the spherical burner such that the sphere’s center is optionally positioned within ± 2mm at the intersection of the CIR chamber window axes. BURNER 530_0410 1.2.4, 1.2.4.2 Spherical Burner – Position 30 ACME shall position the spherical burner such that the sphere’s center is optionally positioned within ± 2mm, 30mm upstream from the intersection of the CIR window axes. BURNER 530_0420 1.2.5 Spherical Burner – Pressure Transducer ACME shall position the differential pressure transducer downstream of the last solenoid valve. BURNER 530_0430 1.2.5 Spherical Burner – Volume ACME shall provide an internal fluid volume of no more than 10 cc from the solenoid valve to the porous sphere, including the differential pressure transducer, for the spherical burners. BURNER 530_0440 1.2.6 Spherical Burner – Material ACME shall provide spherical burners fabricated using stainless steel tubing. BURNER 530_0450 1.2.7 Spherical Burner – Temperature All materials located within 75mm of the spherical burner tip center shall be capable of withstanding temperatures of at least 0 to 450 degrees Celsius.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p35,1,"1.3 Gas-Jet Burner BURNER 530_0460 1.3.1, 1.3.2 Gas-Jet Burner – Dimensions ACME shall provide each gas-jet burner consisting of a straight tube of a constant internal cross-section over a length of at least 40 times greater than the inner diameter starting from the tip. BURNER 530_0470 1.3.3 Gas-Jet Burner – Nozzle Plane ACME shall provide Gas-Jet burners such that the plane defined by the nozzle tip is orthogonal to the burner tube axis ± 5 degrees. BURNER 530_0480 1.3.4.1, 1.3.5, 1.3.6 Gas-Jet Burner – Size A ACME shall provide the gas-jet burner with a 0.4 I.D., 0.8 O.D circular cross-sectional size, in mm, to a tolerance of ± 0.1mm with an accuracy of ± 0.05mm, and with the O.D. maintained up to 10mm from the tip. BURNER 530_0490 1.3.4.2 1.3.5, 1.3.6 Gas-Jet Burner – Size B ACME shall provide the gas-jet burner with a 0.8 I.D., 1.2 O.D circular cross-sectional size, in mm, to a tolerance of ± 0.1mm with an accuracy of ± 0.05mm, and with the O.D. maintained up to 10mm from the tip.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p36,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 29 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description BURNER 530_0500 1.3.4.3 1.3.5, 1.3.6 Gas-Jet Burner – Size C ACME shall provide the gas-jet burner with a 1.3 I.D., 1.6 O.D circular cross-sectional size, in mm, to a tolerance of ± 0.1mm with an accuracy of ± 0.05mm, and with the O.D. maintained up to 10mm from the tip. BURNER 530_0510 1.3.4.4 1.3.5, 1.3.6 Gas-Jet Burner – Size D ACME shall provide the gas-jet burner with a 1.6 I.D., 2.0 O.D circular cross-sectional size, in mm, to a tolerance of ± 0.1mm with an accuracy of ± 0.05mm, and with the O.D. maintained up to 10mm from the tip. . BURNER 530_0520 1.3.4.5 1.3.5, 1.3.6 Gas-Jet Burner – Size E ACME shall provide the gas-jet burner with a 2.1 I.D., 2.4 O.D circular cross-sectional size, in mm, to a tolerance of ± 0.1mm with an accuracy of ± 0.05mm, and with the O.D. maintained up to 10mm from the tip. . BURNER 530_0530 1.3.4.6 1.3.5, 1.3.6 Gas-Jet Burner – Size F ACME shall provide the gas-jet burner with a 2.7 I.D., 3.1 O.D circular cross-sectional size, in mm, to a tolerance of ± 0.1mm with an accuracy of ± 0.05mm, and with the O.D. maintained up to 10mm from the tip. . BURNER 530_0540 1.3.4.7 1.3.5, 1.3.6 Gas-Jet Burner – Size G ACME shall provide the gas-jet burner with a 3.2 I.D., 3.6 O.D circular cross-sectional size, in mm, to a tolerance of ± 0.1mm with an accuracy of ± 0.05mm, and with the O.D.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p36,1,"maintained up to 10mm from the tip. . BURNER 530_0550 1.3.7 Gas-Jet Burner – Nozzle Quality ACME shall provide an internal and external surface finish average roughness of no more than 100 μm for each Gas-Jet burner nozzle. BURNER 530_0560 1.3.7 Gas-Jet Burner – Tip Quality ACME shall provide the gas-jet tip free of burrs, sharp edges, and smoothed to an average roughness of no more than 20 μm. BURNER 530_0570 1.3.8, 1.3.8.1 Gas-Jet Burner – Supply tube axis Coincident ACME shall position the gas-jet burner such that supply tube axis is coincident with the CIR chamber axis within ± 2mm, along its length. BURNER 530_0571 1.3.8, 1.3.8.1 Gas -Jet Burner – Supply Tube Axis Orthogonal ACME shall position the gas-jet burner such that supply tube axis is orthogonal to the CIR chamber window axes.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p37,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 30 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description BURNER 530_0580 1.3.8, 1.3.8.2 Gas-Jet Burner – Position Tip 1 ACME shall position each gas-jet burner such that the tip is within ± 2mm, 10mm upstream from the intersection of the CIR window axes. BURNER 530_0590 1.3.8, 1.3.8.2 Gas-Jet Burner – Position Tip 2 ACME shall position each gas-jet burner such that the tip is optionally positioned within ± 2mm, 30mm upstream from the intersection of the CIR window axes. BURNER 530_0600 1.3.9 Gas-Jet Burner – Material ACME shall provide each gas-jet burner fabricated from stainless steel tubing. BURNER 530_0610 1.3.10 Gas-Jet Burner – Temperature All materials located within 50mm of the Gas - Jet burner tip center shall be capable of withstanding temperatures of at least 0 to 1000 degrees Celsius. 1.4 Coflow Burner BURNER 530_0620 1.4.1 Coflow Burner – Axisymmetric ACME shall provide an axisymmetric coflow burner consisting of a small inner tube within a larger outer tube. BURNER 530_0630 1.4.2 Coflow Burner – Concentricity ACME shall provide the coflow burner such that the inner and outer tubes are concentric within ± 0.3mm along their common length. BURNER 530_0650 1.4.3 Coflow Burner Outlet – Mesh ACME shall provide a coflow burner annulus with a gas permeable outlet surface across the annulus and 3± 0.1mm upstream of the inner tube's outlet.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p37,1,BURNER 530_0660 1.4.3 Coflow Burner Outlet – Outer Tube Mesh Orthogonal ACME shall provide coflow burners such that the annular outlet surface across the inner tube (outlet) is orthogonal to the burner axis within ± 5 degrees. BURNER 530_0670 1.4.3 Coflow Burner Outlet – Outer Tube Mesh Flat ACME shall provide coflow burners such that the annular outlet surface across the inner tube (outlet) is flat to within ±0.1mm. BURNER 530_0680 1.4.4 Coflow Burner Outlet – Outer Tube ACME shall provide coflow burners such that the outlet of the outer tube is 0.0 to 0.5mm downstream of the burner’s outlet surface. BURNER 530_0690 1.4.5 Coflow Burner Inner Tube – ID & OD Dimensions ACME shall provide coflow burners such that the circular inner tube has an inner diameter (ID) and an outer diameter (OD) within ± 0.1mm of 2.1 and 2.4mm respectively with an accuracy of ± 0.05mm.,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p38,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 31 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description BURNER 530_0700 1.4.5 Coflow Burner Inner Tube – ID versus Length ACME shall provide coflow burners such that the inner tube's inner diameter (ID) is constant for a length of 40 times the inner diameter. BURNER 530_0710 1.4.6 Coflow Burner – Inner Tube Surface Quality ACME shall provide coflow burners such that each inner tube has an internal and external surface finish with an average roughness of no more than 100 μm. BURNER 530_0720 1.4.6 Coflow Burner – Tip Quality ACME shall provide coflow burners such that the tip has a surface finish with an average roughness of no more than 20 μm. BURNER 530_0730 1.4.7 Coflow Burner – Inner Tube Volume ACME shall provide coflow burner's inner tube such that the internal volume of the inner tube from the last solenoid valve to the tip, including the pressure transducer, is less than 10 cc. BURNER 530_0740 1.4.8 Coflow Burner – Outer Tube ID ACME shall provide a flow profile of the coflow burners such that at the burner outlet, the outer tube has an inner diameter of 25 ± 0.5mm with an accuracy of ± 0.1mm. BURNER 530_0750 1.4.9.a.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p38,1,"1.4.11 1.4.11.1 Coflow Flow Profile – Axisymmetric – 20 cm/s ACME shall provide a flow profile of the coflow burner that have a uniform, axisymmetric flow profile at 1g using a cold flow, with no flame, such that the variation in axial velocity with angular position is less than 10% of the mean velocity for select radial positions from 0 to 10mm across one 30mm diameter orthogonal to the burner axis that is within 0.5mm downstream of the inner tube outlet; at increments of no greater than 1mm within 5mm of the burner axis and at increments no greater than 5mm thereafter; for a matched velocity in the inner and outer tubes of 20 cm/s. BURNER 530_0760 1.4.9.a. 1.4.11 1.4.11.1 Coflow Flow Profile – Axisymmetric – 30 cm/s ACME shall provide a flow profile of the coflow burner that have a uniform, axisymmetric flow profile at 1g using a cold flow, with no flame, such that the variation in axial velocity with angular position is less than 10% of the mean velocity for select radial positions from 0 to 10mm across one 30mm diameter orthogonal to the burner axis that is within 0.5mm downstream of the inner tube outlet; at increments of no greater than 1mm within 5mm of the burner axis and at increments no greater than 5mm thereafter; for a matched velocity in the inner and outer tubes of 30 cm/s.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p39,0,"ACME Engineering Requirements Document ACME-REQ-001 rev. K April, 2014 Page 32 of 117 System SSC_ID ISRD Trace ERD REQ Title/Description BURNER 530_0770 1.4.9.a. 1.4.11 1.4.11.1 Coflow Flow Profile – Axisymmetric – 40 cm/s ACME shall provide a flow profile of the coflow burner that have a uniform, axisymmetric flow profile at 1g using a cold flow, with no flame, such that the variation in axial velocity with angular position is less than 10% of the mean velocity for select radial positions from 0 to 10mm across one 30mm diameter orthogonal to the burner axis that is within 0.5mm downstream of the inner tube outlet; at increments of no greater than 1mm within 5mm of the burner axis and at increments no greater than 5mm thereafter; for a matched velocity in the inner and outer tubes of 40 cm/s. BURNER 530_0780 1.4.9.a. 1.4.11 1.4.11.1 Coflow Flow Profile – Axisymmetric – 50 cm/s ACME shall provide a flow profile of the coflow burner, that have a uniform, axisymmetric flow profile at 1g using a cold flow, with no flame, such that the variation in axial velocity with angular position is less than 10% of the mean velocity for select radial positions from 0 to 10mm across one 30mm diameter orthogonal to the burner axis that is within 0.5mm downstream of the inner tube outlet; at increments of no greater than 1mm within 5mm of the burner axis and at increments no greater than 5mm thereafter; for a matched velocity in the inner and outer tubes of 50 cm/s. BURNER 530_0790 1.4.9.a.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME - Engineering Requirements Doc Rev K.pdf,p39,1,"1.4.11 1.4.11.2 Coflow Flow Profile – Axisymmetric 10 cm/s – 0 Degrees ACME shall provide a flow profile of the coflow burner that have a uniform, axisymmetric flow profile at 1g using a cold flow, with no flame, such that the variation in axial velocity with a angular position is less than 10% of the mean velocity for select radial positions from 0 to 10mm across one 30mm diameter at the zero-degree position orthogonal to the burner axis that is within 0.5mm downstream of the burner outlet; at increments of no greater than 1mmr within 5mm of the burner axis and at no greater than 5mm thereafter; for a matched velocity in the inner and outer tubes of 10 cm/s.",,False
