PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_s-Flame_RDR_201005.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_s-Flame_RDR_201005.pdf,p20,0,"Achieving Objective B s-Flame gj • Objective B – Quasi-Steady Extinction Limits, Low Da F l/i i ill b i d f h h i l b d– Fuel/inert mixtures will be issued from the spherical burner and ignited at initial flow rates expected (or known/observed) to form flames that evolve toward idealized steady-state conditions. – Following ignition and initial flame front movement, the fuel/inert flow rate will be abruptly reduced to a second flow rate below the expected extinction limitrate below the expected extinction limit. – Quasi-steady extinction is expected to occur as the flame adjusts to the reduced flow rate. – To refine the determination of the extinction flow rate for each fuel/diluent mixture, repeated tests are to be conducted at successively lower initial flow rates and higher secondary flow 1919 successively lower initial flow rates and higher secondary flow rates.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_s-Flame_RDR_201005.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_s-Flame_RDR_201005.pdf,p21,0,"Achieving Objective B (cont.) s-Flame gj () • Objective B – Quasi-Steady Extinction Limits, Low Da –At the low Da extinction limit, chemical kinetic rates are retarded with respect to flow rates by inert gas in the fuel stream that reduces the flame temperaturestream that reduces the flame temperature. –Based on theoretical predictions, fuel/inert mixtures are to be chosen for which the extinction flame radius is large g enough to minimize the effects of heat loss to the burner. –The surface temperature of the burner will be directly measured to quantify this loss. 2020",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_s-Flame_RDR_201005.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_s-Flame_RDR_201005.pdf,p22,0,"Achieving Objective B (cont.) s-Flame gj () • Objective B – Quasi-Steady Extinction Limits, High Da –Fuel/inert mixtures will be issued from the spherical burner and ignited at flow rates above and beyond those expected (or known/observed) to form steady flames when radiative(or known/observed) to form steady flames when radiative loss is considered. –The quasi-steady extinction is expected to occur as the q yp flame moves outward before reaching the adiabatic steady- state radius. –Rates of radiative emissions from the flame and radiative reabsorption elsewhere are to be accentuated in some cases by using a radiatively active diluent such as CO2 2121 by using a radiatively active diluent such as CO2.",,False
