PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_s-Flame_RDR_201005.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_s-Flame_RDR_201005.pdf,p26,0,"Achieving Objective C s-Flame gj • Objective C – Flame Front Instabilities –Pulsating flame front instabilities are predicted to occur near the extinction conditions at both low and high Da. –The approach taken to observe the instabilities is similar toThe approach taken to observe the instabilities is similar to the approach for observing extinction except fuel/diluent mixtures with large system Lewis numbers (Le) are used. • Low Da: moderate initial flow rate, abrupt change to flow rateLow Da: moderate initial flow rate, abrupt change to flow rate below predicted extinction rate. • High Da: initial flow rate above predicted extinction flow rate with radiation. • Instabilities are expected to appear before extinction. – Radiating diluents are used to further encourage the instabilities 2525 instabilities.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_s-Flame_RDR_201005.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_s-Flame_RDR_201005.pdf,p27,0,"Instabilities SDEP C1, C2, C3, C4 s-Flame • C1) Total chemiluminescence as a function of time. • C2) Total radiation loss (based on radiation flux) as a function of radius for different flow rates and fuel compositions ,,, radius, for different flow rates and fuel compositions. • C3) Oscillation mode, frequency, and amplitude near extinction as a function of flow rate for flames of different Le and mass flow rates. • C4) Comparison of extinction times and extinction radii as a function of flow rate, for different fuel compositions. • Requirements • PMT (chemiluminescence) • Radiometer data • Color imagesg • UV images • Temperature profiles • Mass flow rate 2626",,False
