PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p2,0,1 1 s-Flame Outline • Goals • Scientific significance and justification for mg experimentation – Merits of spherical diffusion flames – Elemental diffusion flame processes – Development of fundamental physical & chemical database • Experimental plan,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p3,0,"2 2 s-Flame Goals • Use spherical diffusion flames in long-duration mg environment to: – obtain experimental data of exceptional quality in simple, well-defined flow fields. – understand various key elemental diffusion flame processes. • Compare these data with those obtained through independent computational simulation with detailed chemistry and transport in order to: – scrutinize and improve the accuracy and comprehensiveness of these descriptions.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p4,0,3 3 s-Flame,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p5,0,"4 4 s-Flame Merits of One-Dimensional Flames for Fundamental Studies • Flame structure fundamentally affected by aerodynamics. • Simple flow and flame configuration facilitate experimentation, computation, analysis, and comparison, with enhanced accuracy and reliability. • Reduced effort in describing flows allows more detailed study of other aspects of flames such as chemistry. • A one-dimensional flame is the simplest possible configuration.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p6,0,5 5 s-Flame • Only spherical (vs. planar and cylindrical) configuration admits steady-state 1D behavior in semi-infinite domain. • Spherically symmetric droplet combustion complicated by transient effects due to droplet heating and surface regression. Kinetics of large HC fuels also less well established. • Burner generation assures steady-state surface boundary conditions. • Planar flame in counterflow is not 1D in terms of diffusion-convection as well as radiation misalignment. Merits of Burner-Generated Spherical Diffusion Flames Spherical Burner Flame F O Burner F O Surface regressing Droplet Droplet Flame Counterflow Flame F O Diffusion Stagnation surface Flame •,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p7,0,"6 6 s-Flame Program Components Development of fundamental chemical & physical database, especially comprehensive reaction mechanisms Experimentation on and understanding of elemental (diffusion) flame processes FOCUS BFOCUS A",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p8,0,7 7 s-Flame Focus A: Experimentation on Elemental (Diffusion) Flame Processes I. Spreading of diffusion flame sheets II. Dual extinction states III. Flamefront instabilities,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p9,0,"8 8 s-Flame I. Spreading of Diffusion Flame Sheets: Phenomenology • Steady-state assumption ignores initial conditions. • An important process is fuel accumulation, i.e. scalar profile buildup, leading to spreading of the flame sheet from the fuel source. • Relevant for all non-planar processes! Fuel Ejected Steady Fuel Burned Transient Time Low YO, Flame Size High YO, Droplet Flame Behavior • Previous observation of droplet flame complicated by far field diffusion and surface regression. • Insensitive to chemistry, allows focus on diffusion. • Coupling to density and hence temperature fields allows assessment of radiation effects. Fuel vapor accumulation Flame spreading Droplet",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p10,0,"9 9 s-Flame I. Spreading of Diffusion Flame Sheets: Experimental • Fuel/diluent mixtures will be issued from the spherical burner and ignited at flow rates expected to form flames that evolve toward idealized steady-state conditions • As the flame spreads outward, transient measurements of flame-front velocity and flame structure will be obtained using imaging and radiometric systems • The flame characteristics will be compared with the predictions of the transient numerical simulation of spherical diffusion flames for evaluations of detailed chemical kinetics and radiation models.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p11,0,"10 10 s-Flame II. Flame Extinction: Phenomenology (1/3) • Unlike premixed flames, reactant leakage is the root cause of diffusion flame extinction. • For thin reaction zones, effects of other loss processes (radiation, diffusional stratification, unsteadiness, multi-dimensional) can all be folded into reactant leakage through flame temperature reduction. Oxidizer leakage F O Reaction zone Fuel leakage",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p12,0,"11 11 s-Flame • Consequently practically all diffusion flame extinction phenomena have been found to be described by Linán’s canonical extinction criterion: where d is (Linán’s) flame Damköhler number and dE ~ e(1–|g |), g =g (freestream energy parameters). d < dE II. Flame Extinction: Phenomenology (2/3) dE Damköhler Number, d Flame Response ~",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p13,0,"12 12 s-Flame • Flame can extinguish at both small & large Damköhler numbers • Kinetically limited extinction occurs for small Da. • Increasing Da implies longer residence time or large system dimension, leading to increased radiation loss, reduced flame temperature, and eventually extinction through excessive leakage • Linán’s extinction criterion applies at both limits • Dual extinction observed in mg droplet burning (?) • Present experiment further facilitates interpretation (e.g. no droplet heating, simpler fuels) II. Flame Extinction: Phenomenology (3/3) System Damköhler Number Max Temperature Purely kinetic extinction Radiation-affected extinction ~",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p14,0,"13 13 s-Flame II. Flame Extinction: Experimental, Low Da • Fuel/inert mixtures will be issued from the spherical burner and ignited at initial flow rates expected (or known/observed) to form flames that evolve toward idealized steady-state conditions • Following ignition and initial flame front movement, the fuel/inert flow rate will be abruptly reduced to a second flow rate below the expected extinction limit • Quasi-steady extinction is expected to occur as the flame adjusts to the reduced flow rate • To refine the determination of the extinction flow rate for each fuel/diluent mixture, repeated tests are to be conducted at successively lower initial flow rates and higher secondary flow rates.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p15,0,"14 14 s-Flame II. Flame Extinction: Experimental, High Da • Fuel/inert mixtures will be issued from the spherical burner and ignited at flow rates slightly above and beyond that of the radiation extinction limit. • Upon ignition the flame will grow and subsequently extinguish at the radiation extinction limit. • Rates of radiative emissions from the flame and radiative reabsorption elsewhere are to be accentuated in some cases by using a radiatively active diluent such as CO2.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p16,0,"15 15 s-Flame III. Flamefront Instabilities: Phenomenology • Stoichiometry constraint and lack of chemistry render diffusion flame sheets absolutely stable. • However, chemistry becomes important for near-limit burning. Will flame become unstable? • Recent theories & experiments show instability characteristics similar to those of premixed flames for near-limit burning • Since (positive) stretch suppresses cellular instability while promotes pulsating instability, the (stretchless) spherical flame is well suited for such studies.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p17,0,"16 16 s-Flame III. Flamefront Instabilities: Experimental • Manipulate fuel and oxidizer Le through inert substitution • Le<1: Cellularly unstable • Le>1: Pulsatingly unstable • Radiating diluents are used to further promote instability • Pulsating instabilities are predicted to occur near the extinction conditions at both low and high Da, thereby modifying the extinction mechanism and limit; also allows for extinction detection and flame control 0 1 2 3 4 5 100 400 700 1000 1300 Da * SF G=1.6E-42.0E-42.3E-4 Radiation zone O() Reaction zone O() x = x f F uel O xidizer stream x = 0 Oxidant stream x=xf Fuel",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p18,0,17 17 s-Flame Focus B: Development of Fundamental Physical & Chemical Database • Detailed fuel oxidation mechanisms • Reduced fuel oxidation mechanisms • Diffusion coefficients • Radiation models,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p19,0,18 18 s-Flame Fundamental Needs in Computational Aerothermochemistry • Satisfactory quantitative description of combustion phenomena depends on: – Accurate input of physics & chemistry – Computationally accessible • Components of input: – Turbulence (DNS & modeled) – Chemistry (detailed & reduced) – Diffusion (mixture & second order) – Radiation (various models),,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p20,0,"19 19 s-Flame Complexities in Description of Chemistry • Fuel oxidation is highly nonlinear and nonmonotonic in terms of temperature, pressure, and species concentrations • Oxidation mechanisms consist of large number of species and reactions • Characteristic reaction rates span over many orders of magnitude, hence computationally stiff • Multicomponent diffusive transport can also be computationally expensive",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p21,0,20 20 s-Flame Importance of Data Fidelity: Methane-Air Flame Speeds =1 data by 1970s; Stretch contaminated Year of Publication (A.D.) Burning Velocity (cm. sec) at 298°K 0 5 10 15 20 25 30 35 40 45 0.5 0.6 0.7 0.8 0.9 1.0 1.1 1.2 1.3 1.4 1.5 Present Work Hassan et al. (1998) Gu et al. (2000) Vagelopoulos et al. (1994) Van Maaren et al. (1994) Vagelopoulos & Egolfopoulos (1998) GRI-Mech 3.0 GRI-Mech 1.2 Present Laminar Flame Speed (cm/s)  1 atm data after 1980s; Stretch effect eliminated,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p22,0,21 21 s-Flame (Inadequate) State of Detailed Mechanisms • Hydrogen: Disagreement between calculation & experiment in ignition delay at low temperatures & high pressures • Methane: Disagreement in the nature of ignition for calculations with different mechanisms (GRI-Mech 2.11 vs 3.0),,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p23,0,"22 22 s-Flame Targets for Comprehensiveness in Experimental Data • Broad coverage of thermodynamic states: temperature, pressure, concentration • Ignition (homogeneous and diffusive): low-temperature chemistry • Steady burning & extinction: high-temperature chemistry • Premixed & diffusion flames: mixedness & time scale • Global responses (flame speeds and extinction strain rates) • Detailed flame structure (thermal and major/minor species) • Pollutant chemistry",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p24,0,"23 23 s-Flame The Role of mg Spherical Diffusion Flame in Comprehensive Mechanism Development • Substantial research on homogeneous systems (shock tubes, flow reactors, etc.) and premixed flames • Diffusion flame experiments mostly involve counter- and stagnation flows, and droplet flames • Burner-supported spherical flame is 1D and steady state • Experiments of interest to chemistry are those involving ignition and extinction. • Experiments for situations away from extinction would yield information on the diffusion and radiation parameters.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p25,0,"24 24 s-Flame Development of Reduced Mechanisms • Methodologies mostly in place for fast, systematic & accurate mechanism reduction – Elimination of unimportant species & reactions – Time-scale analysis (steady-state and partial equilibrium assumptions) – Diffusion coefficient lumping • Example: Detailed n-heptane mechanism with 561 species and 2539 elementary reactions reduced to 55 species and 51 lumped reactions",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p26,0,"25 25 s-Flame Validation of Reduced Heptane Mechanism Ignition Delay PSR Extinction 1500 1600 1700 1800 1900 2000 1E-6 1E-4 1E-2 1E+0 1E+2 Residence Time, sec Detailed 68-species 55-species 1200 1300 1400 1500 1400 1800 2200 Temperature, K n-heptane T1 = 300K  = 0.5  = 1.0  = 1.5 p = 50 atm 1 5 50 5 1 50 5 1 1E-7 1E-5 1E-3 1E-1 1E+1 Detailed 68-species 55-species 1E-7 1E-5 1E-3 1E-1 1E+1Ignition Delay, (sec) 1E-7 1E-5 1E-3 1E-1 1E+1 0.5 1 1.5 1000/T, (1/K)  = 0.5  = 1.0  = 1.5 n-heptane p = 1 atm 5 50 5 50 50 5 1 1",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p27,0,"26 26 s-Flame • mg experimentation facilitates understanding of three important elemental flame processes: • Flame spread and fuel accumulation • Dual extinction limits: kinetic vs. radiation affected • Flame-front instabilities and extinction monitoring • Acquisition of diffusion flame data from the clean and well- controlled mg experiments allow scrutiny and development of physical and chemical database, especially comprehensive detailed reaction mechanisms. Summarizing Comments",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p28,0,"27 27 s-Flame SCR at NASA Glenn Principal Investigator: Chung K. Law Princeton University Co-Investigator: Stephen D. Tse Rutgers University Co-Investigator: Kurt R. Sacksteder NASA-Glenn Research Center February 20, 2008",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p29,0,28 28 s-Flame Experimental Objectives,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p30,0,"29 29 s-Flame • A series of experiments is proposed, in both soot- free and sooty flames, to compare with detailed computational simulation to assess transport properties and detailed chemical kinetics (including chemiluminescence). Experimental Objectives",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p31,0,"30 30 s-Flame Experimental Objectives Objective A. Measure and characterize the transient structure (such as temperature and species distribution) of diffusion flames established at initially non-steady, spherical flame structures for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty), for different flow rates.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p32,0,31 31 s-Flame Flame Structure Spread 500 1000 1500 2000 0 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 Temperature (K) CH* Mass Fraction ( x 10-12) T (no radiation) T (SNB reabsorption) CH* (SNB reabsorption) CH* (no radiation) -1 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Standoff Distance (cm) Heat Release Rate (MW/m3) no radiation SNB reabsorption 1.0 1.1 1.2 1.3 1.4 1.5 1.6 0 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 3.0 Mass Consumption Rate of CH4 and H2 (mg/s) Time (s) SNB reabsorption no radiation,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p33,0,32 32 s-Flame Experimental Objectives Objective B. Determine the quasi-steady convective/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (low system Damkohler numbers) and radiative/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (high system Damkohler numbers) for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty). Max Temperature System Damkohler NumberSystem Number Purely Kinetic Extinction Radiative Extinction,,False
