PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p35,0,"34 34 s-Flame Analysis of Dual Extinction Turning Points • • For given extinction Daf , Daf,e, there are two roots: me,1 : Pure kinetic limit me,2 : Radiation-affected limit Daf Daf,e me,1 me,2 m Daf Daf,e me,1 me,2 m Daf Daf,e me,1 me,2 m Adiabatic Radiative Kinetic Extinction Radiation Extinction Mass Flow Rate Maximum Temperature Adiabatic Radiative Kinetic Extinction Radiation Extinction Mass Flow Rate Maximum Temperature Maximum temperature in steady-state spherical methane diffusion flames versus mass flow rate, both adiabatic and with radiative heat loss. )exp(~ 22 AmmDa f −",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p36,0,"35 35 s-Flame Experimental Objectives Objective C. Determine the existence (for different Zeldovich number, Ze), onset (as a function Da), and nature (i.e. mode, frequency, amplitude) of pulsating instabilities theoretically predicted to occur in transient spherical diffusion flames using fuel/diluent mixtures that are above a critical Lewis number.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p37,0,"36 36 s-Flame Oscillatory Response Prior to Extinction • 50%CH4 / 50%He vs. air (LeF = 2.3, Leo = 1.9) • Oscillation observed at both kinetic and radiation extinction limits Computation Kinetic Extinction Radiation Extinction",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p38,0,37 37 s-Flame Proposed Experimental Approach,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p39,0,"38 38 s-Flame Experimental Approach (1/6) • A series of experiments is proposed in which mixtures of various gaseous fuels, diluents and oxygen will be issued into an oxidizing environment through a spherical porous burner and ignited to nominally obtain a spherical, one-dimensional stabilized flame. Fuel (Oxidizer) Diffusion Flame Oxidizing (Fuel) Environment Porous Burner T r-r0 Fuel (Oxidizer) Diffusion Flame Oxidizing (Fuel) Environment Porous Burner T r-r0",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p40,0,"39 39 s-Flame Experimental Approach (2/6) • Objective A – Flame Structure Evolution –Fuel/diluent mixtures will be issued from the spherical burner and ignited at flow rates expected to form flames that evolve toward idealized steady-state conditions. –As the flame spreads outward, transient measurements of flame front velocity and flame structure will be obtained using imaging and radiometric systems. –The flame characteristics will be compared with the predictions of the transient numerical simulation of spherical diffusion flames for evaluations of detailed chemical kinetic and transport models.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p41,0,"40 40 s-Flame Experimental Approach (3/6) • Objective B – Quasi-Steady Extinction Limits, Low Da – Fuel/inert mixtures will be issued from the spherical burner and ignited at initial flow rates expected (or known/observed) to form flames that evolve toward idealized steady-state conditions. – Following ignition and initial flame front movement, the fuel/inert flow rate will be abruptly reduced to a second flow rate below the expected extinction limit. – Quasi-steady extinction is expected to occur as the flame adjusts to the reduced flow rate. – To refine the determination of the extinction flow rate for each fuel/diluent mixture, repeated tests are to be conducted at successively lower initial flow rates and higher secondary flow rates.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p42,0,"41 41 s-Flame Experimental Approach (4/6) • Objective B – Quasi-Steady Extinction Limits, Low Da –At the low Da extinction limit, chemical kinetic rates are retarded with respect to flow rates by inert gas in the fuel stream that decreases the flame temperature. –Based on theoretical predictions, fuel/inert mixtures are to be chosen for which the extinction flame radius is large enough to minimize the effects of heat loss to the burner. –The surface temperature of the burner will be directly measured to quantify this loss.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p43,0,"42 42 s-Flame Experimental Approach (5/6) • Objective B – Quasi-Steady Extinction Limits, High Da –Fuel/inert mixtures will be issued from the spherical burner and ignited at flow rates above and beyond those expected (or known/observed) to form steady flames when radiative loss is considered. –The quasi-steady extinction is expected to occur as the flame moves outward before reaching the adiabatic steady-state radius. –Rates of radiative emissions from the flame and radiative reabsorption elsewhere are to be accentuated in some cases by using a radiatively active diluent such as CO2.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p44,0,"43 43 s-Flame Experimental Approach (6/6) • Objective C – Flame Front Instabilities –Pulsating flame front instabilities are predicted to occur near the extinction conditions at both low and high Da. –The approach taken to observe the instabilities is similar to the approach for observing extinction except fuel/diluent mixtures with large system Lewis numbers (Le) are used. • Low Da: moderate initial flow rate, abrupt change to flow rate below predicted extinction rate. • High Da: initial flow rate above predicted extinction flow rate with radiation. • Instabilities are expected to appear before extinction. –Radiating diluents are used to further encourage the instabilities.",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p45,0,44 44 s-Flame Justification for Extended Duration Microgravity,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p46,0,45 45 s-Flame Limitations of Testing in n-g • Need truly one-dimensional flames • Inverse-flame configuration is limited and constrained Hydrogen Methane,,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p47,0,"46 46 s-Flame Projection image (2.81um) • 9%O2/91%N2, m = 0.078 g/s, into 100%H2 at 0.079 atm • AGEMA Thermovision 900 infrared camera with filter • H2O emission • Thermal radiation from CO2 (4.31mm) and H2O (1.87mm) • Flame structure and equilibrium chemistry Limitations of Testing in n-g",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p48,0,"47 47 s-Flame • Durations in 2.2-s and 5.0-s drop towers insufficient to observe radiative extinction Limitations of Drop Towers Comparistion of Extinction Times for Different Mixtures 300 500 700 900 1100 1300 1500 1700 1900 2100 0 5 10 15 20 25 30 35 Time (s) Max Temp (K) 25%H2-20%CH4-55%N2, 0.01055g/s 50%H2-10%CH4-40%N2, 0.0081g/s 45%H2-55%N2, 0.001g/s `",,False
PSI-23,Science Documents,PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Science Documents/PSI-23_Science Documents_s-Flame SCR 200802.pdf,p49,0,"48 48 s-Flame • Preliminary indications of local radiative extinction observed in 5s drop tower, but • Need more time to observe quasi-steady flame spread prior to TOTAL radiative extinction • Multi-staged flames → kinetic extinction Limitations of Drop Towers 20%H2 / 25%CH4 / 55%N2",,False
