PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p1,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 1 Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021*) Prepared by: Dennis P. Stocker, NASA Glenn Research Center dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov, 216-433-2166 Prof. Chung K. Law, Princeton University Prof. Stephen D. Tse, Rutgers University A preliminary false-colored image of a hydrogen/helium flame in an oxygen/helium atmosphere, where the porous spherical burner and its supply tube can be seen within the flame. The bright glow of two 14-micron fibers used for Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) is also visible. *Data analysis and the publication of results is still underway, so it is expected that this report will be updated in the future with at least a revision of the list of papers and presentations.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p2,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 2 Table of Contents Page 1. Overview 3 2. Science Overview 3 A. Background 3 B. Rationale for Microgravity 4 C. Science Objectives 5 D. Science Success Criteria 7 3. Hardware Overview 9 A. General Description 9 B. Hardware Shortcomings 17 4. Preliminary Results 19 A. Changed Test Plans 19 B. Overview of Experiment Test Operations 20 C. Summary of Preliminary Results 25 D. Surprises and New Discoveries 29 E. Accomplishment of Approved Objectives 30 F. Additional Objectives Achieved 31 G. Impacts, Benefits, and Products 32 H. Patents 32 5. Follow-On Experiments 33 6. Appendices 34 A. Abbreviations and Acronyms 34 B. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increments 59 and 60 37 C. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increments 61 and 62 46 D. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increment 63 57 E. Operational Timeline 72 F. Astronauts and Cosmonauts who Conducted ISS Operations for s-Flame 74 G. ISS Operations per the Payload Weekly Science Summary 75 H. Crew Activity per Space Station Science Highlights 88 I. Crew Activity per the ISS Daily Summary Report 92 J. Crew Activity per the NASA Space Station Blog 97 K. ACME Operations per @ISS_Research on Twitter 100 L. ACME Operations per Astronauts on Twitter 104 M. Summary of the ISS data collection 106 N. Publications, Presentations, Awards, and Outreach 107 1. Publications and Presentations with ISS Results 107 2.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p2,1,Publications and Presentations (comprehensive list) 107 3. Awards 113 4. Outreach 113,,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p3,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 3 1. Overview The Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment is one of six independent experiments that make up the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project for which tests are conducted within the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on the International Space Station (ISS). The experiment’s Principal Investigator (PI) is Prof. Chung K. Law of Princeton University. The experiment has two Co-Investigators (Co-Is), namely Prof. Stephen D. Tse of Rutgers University and Dr. Kurt R. Sacksteder of the NASA Glenn Research Center. While Sacksteder was actively engaged in defining the experiment, he had transitioned to other NASA work long before the spaceflight operations limiting his overall contributions. Meanwhile, Dennis P. Stocker of the NASA Glenn Research Center is the Project Scientist (PS) for ACME including s-Flame and the rest of the ACME investigations. In preparatory drop testing, etc., Dr. Fumiaki Takahashi of the Case Western Reserve University (CWRU) served as the Deputy Project Scientist. For ACME’s spaceflight operations, the NASA science team includes Drs. Vedha Nayagam and Uday G. Hegde who are also with CWRU.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p3,1,"The s-Flames operations were conducted in two rounds of testing during Increments 60-63, where there was no testing during Increment 62 due to limited crew availability. More specifically, the first round of testing was conducted from Sept. 2019 to Jan. 2020, while the second-round testing – featuring helium rather than nitrogen dilution - occurred from May to July 2020. 2. Science Overview A. Background This background is the Executive Summary from the experiment’s Science Requirements Document (SRD) of Jan. 2010. Recognizing that most combustion engines and devices operate on the basis of diffusion flames, the proposed program aims to characterize and gain predictive capability of the structure and dynamics of diffusion flames in simple, well-defined flow fields such that the phenomena of interest can be studied without being unduly complicated and compromised by complex and sometimes non-quantifiable flow field effects. The basic flame configuration adopted for the investigation is the spherically symmetric diffusion flame generated by discharging a fuel gas from a porous spherical burner into a quiescent, oxidizing ambience in microgravity. The one- dimensional nature of the system facilitates data acquisition, data reduction, and computational simulation.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p3,2,"The primary goals of the program are two-fold and strongly interdependent. The first objective is to interrogate the fidelity of the chemical kinetic mechanisms and transport submodels used in the simulation of aerothermochemical phenomena, and consequently identify possible modifications. The second objective is to characterize and gain predictive capability on spherical diffusion flames.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p4,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 4 The space flight program consists of two main parts: (1) Mapping and characterization of the movement of spherical diffusion flames subsequent to ignition from a non-steady-state condition and their extinction boundaries due to direct finite-rate chemistry and indirect radiative heat loss. The emphases are on the relative importance of radiative emission and re-absorption, the examination of the adequacy of the detailed chemical kinetics mechanism and transport sub- models used, and the development of flame front instabilities that could modify the extinction boundaries. Results of the study are of relevance to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions. (2) Studies of the response of characteristically sooty flames to a non-steady-state initial condition with respect to the aspects given in (1). The studies will include characterizing soot onset as a function of residence time for expanding flames, as well as soot radiation and transport contributions in spreading, extinction, and instability. The project involves microgravity experimentation, computational simulation, and, where appropriate, theoretical analysis. Interpretation of all phenomena will emphasize the simultaneous and coupled influence of chemistry and transport.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p4,1,"B. Rationale for Microgravity This is the ‘Justification for Extended Duration Microgravity Environment’ section (2.5) of the experiment’s SRD from Jan. 2010. See the SRD itself for the references, etc. which are not included in this report. 2.5.1 Limitations of Terrestrial (1g laboratory) Testing We seek to make observations of truly one-dimensional flames whose structure can be studied without complications due to uncertainties in the flow pattern caused by buoyancy. Such fundamental study of flame structure and dynamics is best served via the elimination of buoyancy through the reduction of gravity. The minimum sizes of these flames imply that buoyancy would be significant if the experiments were conducted in normal gravity. Previous experimentation on earth has amply demonstrated this influence. Although studies of spherical diffusion flames were conducted in normal gravity using the low-pressure, inverse-flame arrangement (see §1), the conditions are limited for small flames, low pressures, low molecular weight fuels. Indeed, only the hydrogen flame can be considered to be nearly buoyancy free. With such constraints, normal-pressure, micro-gravity, hydrocarbon-based experiments are requisite.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p4,2,"One-dimensional spherical flames allow for the unambiguous study of such fundamental flame phenomena as the dynamics and extinction of diffusion flames (including the mechanisms of flame front unsteadiness), soot suppression/formation, and flame front instabilities, without gravity-induced baroclinicity and buoyancy, on the flame responses.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p5,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 5 2.5.2 Limitations of Drop Towers and Aircraft Extensive experimentation has been conducted in the 2.2-s drop tower, 5-s drop tower, and parabolic aircraft, as reviewed previously in §1.3. Although experimental data of good quality were obtained on the transient response of spherical diffusion flames in the 2.2-s drop tower facility, the duration was insufficient to observe radiative extinction. The numerical simulations indicate that, for the characteristic flames under study, more than 2.5s of microgravity time is needed. Subsequent parabolic aircraft experimentation afforded the additional low-gravity duration (up to 10s by free-floating the apparatus), as well as an experimental package incorporating rainbow schlieren (which cannot be contained within a package for the 2.2-s drop tower), although radiative extinction was still not observed. The unavoidable g-jitter associated with the facility, however, renders the observation less definitive. Note that even though the package was free-floated, umbilical restrictions could not be avoided. This g-jitter effect was substantiated by accelerometer data. 5-s drop tower experiments for a mixture composition of 20%H2 / 25%CH4 / 55%N2 did show events with local extinction toward the end of the drop (see Fig. 1.11). While these new results are extremely encouraging and suggest that radiative extinction can indeed be experimentally observed, the 5-s duration is still insufficient.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p5,1,"To obtain spherical symmetry for small burners (in order to minimize heat loss to the burner), as well as to ensure quasi-steady flame evolution up to radiative extinction (for the flame sizes to be studied), low mass flow rates are needed, which increase the characteristic time needed. Moreover, for other mixture compositions (such as 50%H2/10%CH4/40%N2 45%H2/55%N2), more than 5s of microgravity duration has been computationally predicted to be needed to achieve radiative extinction (see Fig. 1.12). Additional time is also needed to employ multi-staged flames (where the mass flow rate is changed) so that such phenomena as kinetic extinction can be studied (for example, by establishing a quasi-steady flame at a given flow rate and then reducing it so that the flame front moves inwardly and quasi-steadily until kinetic extinction). Finally, given the characteristic time for diffusion flame oscillation (see Fig. 1.14), which does not occur until the flame nears the quasi-steady extinction limits, extended microgravity duration becomes essential in order to study the phenomenon.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p5,2,"2.5.3 Justification for Space-Flight Facility As explained above, extended microgravity duration, of high quality, is needed to make all necessary flame structure measurements, allowing for the study of transient phenomena (such as flame oscillations and fuel vapor accumulation) and extinction processes. Moreover, at present, the advanced diagnostics found on the space-flight facility cannot be utilized in the drop tower facilities. Numerical simulations indicate that about 25s of microgravity is needed for non- extinguishing flames to reach 80% of their steady-state radius, for the fuel mixtures and flow rates examined. It is expected that within this duration, the aforementioned phenomenon can be duly investigated. The total experimental duration for a single case would be 25s. This duration is within the 25s bound imposed by constant volume combustion within the specified CIR dimensions (see §Appendix 5.1) regarding oxygen depletion for a “penetration depth” of 20cm. C. Science Objectives The proposed objectives text presented here is the ‘Experimental Objectives’ section (2.1) of the experiment’s SRD from Jan. 2010. Current experimental and theoretical capabilities make detailed, quantitative descriptions of laminar flames situated in well-defined flow fields conceivable provided that comprehensive",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p6,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 6 chemical-kinetic and transport mechanisms are available. The comprehensiveness of these mechanisms has been extensively tested for premixed but not diffusion flames, especially in simple, well-defined flow fields not complicated by buoyant flows. A burner-generated spherical diffusion flame provides a simple, one-dimensional flame geometry that permits a rigorous comparison between detailed theory and experimental verification. Existing theory and limited low-gravity experimental results suggest that quasi-steady spherical diffusion flames can exist with some combinations of fuel mixture supply rates and quiescent ambient oxidizers. With respect to the steady flame conditions, altering fuel supply rates to limit flame residence time induces a convective-kinetic extinction limit. Alternatively, very large residence times allow radiative heat losses and diffusive transport mechanisms to quench the flame. Near these dual flammability boundaries of quasi-steady flames, flame-front instabilities may appear before the flame is quenched.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p6,1,"We seek to investigate and improve the fidelity and comprehensiveness of the chemical kinetic mechanisms and transport submodels used in the simulation of aerothermochemical phenomena, and consequently identify possible modifications, by characterizing important unit combustion processes, including: (1) the dynamics and extinction of diffusion flames, with emphases on fuel vapor accumulation, radiation heat transfer, flame pulsation, and flame kinetics; and (2) the structure and response of characteristically sooty flames, with emphases on flame front stability and soot formation. Comprehensive predictive capability can be achieved by employing the simple flames of Process (1) to interrogate and suggest modifications of the kinetic and transport data bases, and then validate their adequacy in the chemically and aerodynamically more complex situation of Process (2). A series of experiments is proposed with the following specific objectives, in both soot-free and sooty flames, to compare with detailed computational simulation to assess transport properties and detailed chemical kinetics (including chemiluminescence): Objective A. Measure and characterize the transient structure (such as temperature and species distribution) of diffusion flames established at initially non-steady, spherical flame structures for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty), for different flow rates.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p6,2,"Objective B. Determine the quasi-steady convective/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (low system Damköhler numbers) and radiative/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (high system Damköhler numbers) for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty). Objective C. Determine the existence (for different Zeldovich number, Ze), onset (as a function Da), and nature (i.e. mode, frequency, amplitude) of pulsating instabilities theoretically predicted to occur in transient spherical diffusion flames using fuel/diluent mixtures that are above a critical Lewis number.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p7,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 7 D. Science Success Criteria The success criteria is from the s-Flame’ portion of the ‘Success Criteria’ section of the ACME Integrated Science Requirements Document (ISRD, ACME-SRD-001.B) from Sept. 2013, which is revision B of the version prepared for the Requirements Definition Review (RDR). 6.4.1. Minimum Success Minimum success is defined to mean sufficient scientific data return from the experiment to perform a direct comparison with the numerical flame simulation and publish a single archival journal article. This minimal level of success may be achieved by obtaining (for example): 1. Observations of transient flame phenomena leading toward steady state flames for 1 CH4 case and 1 C2H4 case. Such observations must include, at a minimum, the flow and boundary condition measurements, flame temperature data and image sequences from at least two orthogonal imaging systems; or 2. Observations of flame extinction at both low and high system Damköhler number for 1 case. Such observations must include, at a minimum, the flow and boundary condition measurements, flame temperature data and image sequences from at least two orthogonal imaging systems; or 3. Observations of spherical soot formation in 1 C2H4 case. Such observations must include, at a minimum, the flow and boundary condition measurements, flame temperature data and image sequences from at least two orthogonal imaging systems.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p7,1,"6.4.2. High Success High success is defined to mean sufficient scientific data return from the experiment to perform direct comparison with the numerical flame simulation resulting in multiple archival journal publications, but less return that defined for complete success. This high level of success may be achieved by obtaining (for example): 1. Observations of at least two instances of flame front instabilities including at least one hydrocarbon fuel. Such observations must include, at a minimum, the flow and boundary condition measurements, and data quantifying the flame front oscillations from image sequences from at least two of the three imaging systems and from the radiometer instrument; or 2. Combinations or extensions (in terms of number of mixture ratios for a single hydrocarbon fuel or number of hydrocarbon fuels) of at least two of items 1 through 4 defined for minimum success.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p7,2,"6.4.3. Complete Success Complete Success is defined as meeting all of the experiment objectives, including as a minimum: 1. Observations of transient flame phenomena leading toward steady state flames for at least three different mixture ratios of each of two hydrocarbon fuels and two diluents. Such observations must include, at a minimum, the flow and boundary condition measurements, flame temperature data, image sequences from two orthogonal imaging systems, and radiometer data; and 2. Observations of flame extinction at both low and high system Damköhler number for at least three different mixture ratios of each of two hydrocarbon fuels and two diluents. Such observations must include, at a minimum, the flow and boundary condition measurements, flame temperature data, image sequences from two orthogonal imaging systems, and radiometer data; and 3. Observations of at least two instances of flame front instabilities each including at least one hydrocarbon fuel. Such observations must include, at a minimum, the flow and boundary",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p8,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 8 condition measurements, and data quantifying the flame front oscillations from image sequences from at least two orthogonal imaging systems and from the radiometer instrument; and 4. Observations of soot formation. Such observations must include the flow and boundary condition measurements, flame temperature data, image sequences from all imaging systems, temperature data, and radiometer data.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p9,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 9 3. Hardware Overview A. General Description This section is copied from section 2.A of the April 2021 version of ‘ACME Engineering Implementation’ which should be consulted for additional details. This section provides a general description a general description of the ACME engineering from the ‘3.0 Experimental’ section of the following 2014 conference paper: Dennis P. Stocker, Fumiaki Takahashi, J. Mark Hickman, and Andrew C. Suttles, “Gaseous Non-Premixed Flame Research Planned for the International Space Station,” Spring Technical Meeting of the Central States Section of the Combustion Institute, Tulsa, OK, 16– 18 March 2014. IMPORTANT: The 2104 paper from which this section was extracted proceeded completion of the spaceflight hardware which was mostly launched in 2017. As a result, it does not accurately describe the final engineering in some regards. Therefore updates, clarifications, additional details, shortcomings, references, etc. have been included in boxed notes like this following the original description. As a minor note, the sizes of figures were adjusted in this report, but none were cropped.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p9,1,"As previously discussed, the ACME experiments will be conducted within the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) using a chamber insert, depicted in Figure 2, which was designed to accommodate gas delivery to a variety of exchangeable burners. Besides the chamber insert, other ACME-unique hardware includes a high-definition color camera and an avionics package, which is for data acquisition and control. Figure 2: Design of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) chamber insert that will be used in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on ISS.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p10,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 10 ACME’s chamber insert, which is ~0.4 m in diameter, provides common mounting for a variety of burners on the chamber axis (but only one at a time) and includes a retracting resistive igniter. As depicted in Figure 3, there will initially be gas-jet burners (with inner diameters ranging from 0.4 to 3.2 mm), a small coflow burner (where the two concentric tubes have inner diameters of 2.1 and 25 mm), porous spherical burners (with diameters ranging from 6.35 to 12.7 mm), and two flat porous burners for the BRE experiment (with inner diameters of 25 and 50 mm). Most burners will be equipped with a surface thermocouple, and each BRE burner will have two heat flux transducers. The internal pressure of the porous burners will be measured to allow monitoring of the pressure differential across the outlet surface. The igniter will have a crew-exchangeable tip and arm (to accommodate the various burners) and its stepper-motor drive will allow for precise positioning. Figure 3: ACME burner types, including from left to right, gas-jet burners, a co-flow burner, porous spherical burners, and flat porous burners for the BRE experiment.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p10,1,"Ultimately, the spherical burners were limited to a single diameter of 6.35 mm, and the fabricated gas-jet burners were limited to two sizes with nominal inner diameters of 1.3 and 2.1 mm. However, only the larger gas-jet burner was used during the spaceflight tests. The burners are addressed in detail in section 3.C. Unfortunately, the spherical burners have not always yielded spherically symmetric flames, presumably as a result of (1) greater burner flow through the joint between the porous sphere and supply tube, and (2) heat loss to the support tube (encompassing the supply tube) causing local flame quenching. Two types of igniter tips have been used during ACME testing. Most tests have been conducted using a loop igniter tip like those which had been successfully used in the droplet combustion experiments which preceded ACME research in the CIR. However, some tests were conducted with a coil igniter tip, where that included the second round testing for both the Burning Rate Emulator (BRE) and Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiments, with the exception of the latter’s first day testing. The CFI-G investigation will also use the coil igniter tip. For more information about the igniter tips, see section 3.D.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p10,2,"The chamber insert includes three mass flow controllers (visible on the left of Fig. 2) for the delivery of fuel, oxidizer or inert (e.g., helium or carbon dioxide), and nitrogen to the burner. The maximum fuel flow is 2 slpm (on a nitrogen basis), whereas much higher flows, e.g., 10 slpm (on a nitrogen basis), are possible for the oxidizer and nitrogen lines. The crew can exchange the mass flow controllers to provide appropriate ranges that maximize the flow accuracy. The gas delivery system enables on-orbit dilution of the fuel or oxidizer (but not both simultaneously), coflow, inverse flames (e.g., oxidizer delivery to the center of a coflow burner), and premixed flames,",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p11,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 11 although the latter are not planned for any of the current ACME experiments. Gases can also be delivered premixed to the space station where that is necessary in some circumstances, such as the blending of different fuel gases. CIR’s standard bottle sizes are 1, 2.25, and 3.8 liters but ACME will sometimes use smaller bottles as a hazard control to limit the deficient reactant (e.g., fuel) that can be released into the combustion chamber. Thus far, ACME tests were not conducted with carbon dioxide as a diluent. Furthermore, the fuel diluted with helium was premixed prior to launch rather than on orbit. Ultimately, oxidizer flows of up to 20 slpm (on a nitrogen basis) were possible, while the nitrogen flow was limited to a maximum of 5 slpm. ACME has not used gas bottles smaller than 1 liter as indicated above; the bottle fill pressure was instead limited as an additional hazard control.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p11,1,"ACME’s primary flame diagnostics are a suite of imaging systems including five video cameras and two illumination systems, of which three cameras are part of the CIR facility and are already in use on orbit. All but one of the cameras is mounted outside of the chamber. Collectively, the imaging systems will enable color, CH*, and OH* imaging; pyrometry; measurement of the soot volume fraction; and operational support. However, it is unlikely that all functions would be exercised at any one time as will become apparent in the subsequent discussion. Color imaging is provided by two cameras: a high-definition digital camera mounted outside of the chamber and a standard analog camera mounted on the chamber insert (with a turning mirror). Both cameras are part of the ACME hardware that is now in development. The analog camera signal will be downlinked in near real time to support the experiment operations. Light- emitting diodes within the chamber can be used to illuminate the igniter and burner to both support the operations and provide reference images. The 12-bit digital camera records at up to 30 full frames (1360x1024) per second, and is equipped with a motorized lens with independent control of the zoom, iris, and focus. The digital camera will be equipped with a filter assembly with four options, where selection of a clear “filter” allows for color imaging.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p11,2,"Color imaging can also be carried out with a blue-green BG7 Schott glass filter, which balances the color planes to avoid saturation in the red while maintaining sensitivity in the blue and green. The natural flame color can be approximately reconstructed, but the primary purpose of the filter is pyrometry and measurement of the soot volume fraction, again via the techniques of [23-24]. Alternately, CH* imaging can be conducted with this camera because the filter assembly includes a 430-nm bandpass filter with a 10-nm full width at half-maximum transmission (FWHM). A 450-nm bandpass filter (again with a 10-nm FWHM) can be used with the CH* filter to effectively subtract interfering broadband soot emission. However, the filter assembly rotates slowly so such subtraction effectively requires a separate test with each filter and of course reproducible flame conditions. The video from the analog operations (i.e., ops) camera, also referred to as the internal camera, is not archived in space. The video is instead recorded on the ground in the operations center. Its gain was sometimes adjusted for improved imaging of dim flames, e.g., approaching extinction.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p11,3,"The sensitivity of the color digital camera, often referenced as the ACME DataCam (i.e., Data Camera), to the dim microgravity flames was such that it was often used with long exposure times and thus low framing rates, for example on the order of 1 frame per second. The clear “filter” is of fused silica. Unfortunately, the thickness of the blue-green BG7 Schott glass filter and the added",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p12,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 12 antireflective (AR) coating caused too great a reduction in the red, so it does not balance the intensity of the three color planes as intended and thereby can limit the accuracy of temperature determination. Meanwhile, rather than the 450-nm bandpass filter described above, the high definition color camera was equipped with a bandpass filter for imaging formaldehyde emissions. For more information, see the Data Camera Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-010 Rev. A). Imaging of the dim hydrogen flames in the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment was particularly challenging with these, if not all, cameras.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p12,1,"Pyrometry can alternately be accomplished with CIR’s monochrome High-Bit Depth Multispectral (HiBMs) camera, equipped with a Liquid-Crystal Tunable Filter (LCTF), which allows for imaging at multiple spectra, but requires a quasi-steady flame for measurement at each desired band. This camera has a telecentric optical system and is recorded at 512x512 and 30 frames per second (fps). Although it has a 1024x1024 pixel array, binning has been necessary because of signal loss through the LCTF. Both soot pyrometry and Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) are possible, where ACME is equipped with a crew-replaceable array of 5 silicon carbide (SiC) fibers. The fibers are orthogonal to the flame axis and the array can be translated along the flame axis to the desired position downstream of the burner, e.g., based on the downlink of the analog video. During set up, the crew can manually orient the array so that it is orthogonal to either ACME’s color digital camera or the HiBMs camera with the LCTF. An alternate fiber array including a platinum wire will be used for inflight characterization, which in combination with preflight calibration will enable determination of the soot volume fraction [24].",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p12,2,"In contrast to the preceding paragraph, ACME did not use a HiBMs camera for pyrometry, but instead relied on use of ACME’s color DataCam for such temperature measurements. For an example of how this can be done, see the techniques described in the following papers: P.B. Kuhn, B. Ma, B.C. Connelly, M.D. Smooke, and M.B. Long, “Soot and Thin-filament Pyrometry Using a Color Digital Camera,” Proceedings of the Combustion Institute, 33 (2011) 743-750. B. Ma, M. B. Long, “Absolute light calibration using S-type thermocouples,” Proceedings of the Combustion Institute, 34 (2) (2013) 3531-3539. Thus far, TFP tests have been conducted with 14-micron fibers spaced apart by 5 mm for a total span of 20 mm (in the case of 5 intact ceramic fibers), neglecting optional translation of the array. Tests have been conducted using arrays with and without the platinum wire in the furthest downstream position. Tests have also been conducted with some of the fibers broken and subsequently removed, leaving only three fibers in the final testing for the BRE and Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame (CLD Flame) experiments and at least the initial testing for the Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p12,3,"CIR has a second HiBMs camera, without a filter, that will be used in combination with CIR’s illumination package for alternate measurements of the soot volume fraction. This will be accomplished via an extinction technique previously employed in microgravity [3,6,26], where the camera and the collimated light source (with its two fiber-coupled laser diodes) are positioned at opposite windows. The images are recorded at 1024x1024 and 30 fps. Thus far, the primary purpose of this combined system has been the measurement of the droplet diameter for determination of the droplet burning rate.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p13,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 13 A HiBMs camera without the Liquid Crystal Tunable Filter (LCTF) has been used throughout much, but not all, of ACME’s operations. In the first round of the CLD Flame experiment, it was used without a filter for flame imaging, but this proved to be largely unsuccessful because the camera is very insensitive to blue. Afterward, it was equipped with a red filter corresponding to the CIR illumination package for the rest of the ACME testing. Unfortunately, that filter is sufficiently broad that significant incandescence can pass through the filter limiting its utility for determinations of soot volume fraction via light extinction, e.g., through the technique described in the following papers: D.L. Urban, Z.G. Yuan, P.B. Sunderland, K.C., Lin, Z. Dai, and G.M. Faeth, “Smoke-Point Properties of Nonbuoyant Round Laminar Jet Diffusion Flames,” Proceedings of the Twenty-Eighth Symposium (International) on Combustion 28 (2000) 1965-1972. F.J. Diez, C. Aalburg, P.B. Sunderland, D.L. Urban, Z.-G. Yuan, G.M. Faeth, “Soot properties of laminar jet diffusion flames in microgravity,” Combustion and Flame, 156 (2009) 1514-1524. P.S. Greenberg and J.C. Ku, “Soot Volume Fraction Imaging,” Applied Optics, 36 (22) (1997) 5514- 5522. Nonetheless, soot volume fraction can also be determined from the color DataCam images.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p13,1,"During ACME operations, the HiBMs camera is typically archived at 15 fps because of data limits for transfer and downlink operations. And because operations are constrained by the data limits, image data from this camera are sometimes deleted without downlinking to enable additional testing. And in some testing, HiBMs images aren’t even acquired given their lesser value. Unfortunately, the HiBMs backlight illumination is significantly obstructed by the bracket for the electrode mesh further limiting its utility for the E-FIELD Flames experiment. This was a design trade-off necessitated primarily by ACME’s large number of cameras and other optical instruments. CIR’s Low-Light-Level Ultraviolet (LLL-UV) package is a monochrome camera with an intensifier (for 280-700 nm) and a 310-nm bandpass filter (with a 10-nm FWHM) for OH* imaging. Again as a result of the signal loss through the filter, binning is employed such that the OH* images are recorded at 512x512 and 30 fps. During ACME operations, the LLL-UV camera has only been used for the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p13,2,"While not discussed in the preceding section, another intensified camera was used throughout ACME testing, although its image data was sometimes deleted or not even acquired to allow the performance of additional tests. This was the Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) camera which was generally configured with its CH* filter, where an exception is some s-Flame testing with particularly dim flames. Due to design trade-offs, its Field of View (FOV) was partially obstructed by the arms of the Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) fiber array when it was moved inwards to the flame. More information about this camera can be found in its characterization report, CIR-RPT- 4600. The ACME chamber insert will also be equipped with non-imaging optical diagnostics including both thermopile radiometers and Photomultiplier Tubes (PMTs). There is a crew-exchangeable linear array of radiometers of which there are two versions supporting the initial set of",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p14,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 14 experiments. The array has a fixed position which is parallel to the chamber axis and centered (along the chamber length) relative to the chamber windows and thus most of the other instrumentation. One array is equipped with a single wide-view radiometer with two narrow- view radiometers that are downstream and two more that are upstream, where all views are orthogonal to the chamber axis, as depicted in Figure 4. The narrow-view radiometers are intended to support studies of gas-jet flames, where ACME can accommodate shorter burners, making the “lower” detectors useful. The other array, also seen in Fig. 4, provides for three wide- view measurements of the flame, where one view is masked to nominally block radiant emission from the burner. It was specifically designed for the BRE experiment but will be a resource available to other ACME experiments. Given that the radiometer array can be exchanged by the crew, alternate arrays could be developed. Figure 4: Crew-exchangeable radiometer arrays on the ACME chamber insert: (top) standard (ACME) array of 5 radiometers, and (bottom) array with three radiometers, designed for the BRE experiment, where the lowest detector is masked to avoid view of the burner.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p15,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 15 A third radiometer array was developed for the CFI-G experiment, where it is important to understand that only a single radiometer array can be used at a time. The narrow-view radiometers in the standard array are only fully sensitive in small spots, but they are each sensitive in a much greater Field of View (FOV) than represented in the preceding figure. They do not have lenses; their FOV is a result of an aperture where most of their FOV is within the penumbra which is only visible to a portion of the sensor. Figures representing the measured FOVs of the as-built standard and BRE radiometer arrays are found in section 3.F of this document. Unfortunately, the radiometers in the BRE array typically saturated in the first round of BRE testing, so the standard array was used in its second round. However, those radiometers similarly saturated in many of round 2 tests. In this regard, each of ACME’s BRE and standard radiometers have a fixed gain. Meanwhile, each sensor in the radiometer array developed for the CFI-G investigation has two fixed gains, i.e., a high gain and low gain. For more information about the standard radiometer array used for most ACME testing, see the Radiometer Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-011). Meanwhile, you can learn about the other radiometer arrays from the BRE Radiometer Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-012) and the Cool Flames Radiometer Characterization Report (CIR-RPT-4600).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p15,1,"An additional wide-view radiometer, that is fixed and not crew exchangeable, is positioned downstream of the burner and flame at 30o from the chamber axis. This detector is primarily intended to support the spherical flame experiments and will have a quicker response than the more sensitive detectors that will used in the linear arrays. Like the radiometers on the two linear arrays, it will have a spectral range of nominally 0.2-11 micron and its field of view will be established by an appropriately selected aperture. The FOV of the off-set radiometer is also shown in section 3.F. Learn more about this sensor from the Radiometer Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-011). To detect the quenching of dim flames, the chamber insert will be equipped with a set of three PMTs. Each PMT will have a wide-angle view, a sight line that is orthogonal to the chamber axis, and will be centered (along the chamber length) relative to the chamber windows so that it is aligned with most of the other instrumentation. The PMTs’ spectral range will be 230-700 nm, i.e., visible and near ultraviolet, but one will have a 310-nm bandpass filter and another will have a 430-nm bandpass filter (where both are with a 10-nm FWHM). The pair of detectors will enable measurement of OH* and CH* emission, respectively. The third PMT will be equipped with a neutral density filter to prevent saturation. Like many other components, the PMT array can be removed from the chamber insert by the crew.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p15,2,"Unlike the radiometers, each PMT had a readily adjustable gain. However, the PMT signals sometimes saturated when the gains were set too high. See the PMT Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-013) to learn more about the three Photomultiplier Tube (PMTs). The chamber insert is also equipped with six far-field thermocouples to measure the spread of the thermal field in the spherical flame experiments. They are of limited utility for other experiments because they are positioned upstream of the burner tip. Therefore, they will",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p16,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 16 generally not be installed by the crew to reduce the chance of accidental damage. There will be two versions of a four-thermocouple rake to allow a choice of near and far measurements. Other arrays could be designed to support future experiments, but they cannot extend downstream of the burner tip without interfering with other (e.g., optical) measurements. The two lone thermocouples, which are both at nominally 200 mm from the center of the spherical burner, have been installed throughout ACME testing. The tip of one was seen to be slightly bent early in testing, as discussed and depicted in section 3.G of this report. Only the short (or far) version of the radiometer rake has been used thus far, where factors include the need for crew time to switch between rakes and the heat loss that could result from thermocouples in the long (close) rake. The short (far) rake’s thermocouple tips should nominally be at 45, 60, 100, and 100 mm from the spherical burner center. For thermocouples at the same distance from the burner center, the angle between the rays from the burner center to the two thermocouples should be 90 degrees. The bead size of the thermocouples was not measured, but it is undoubtedly at least twice the size of the 0.2 mm diameter wire. The two thermocouples closest to the burner outlets on each four-thermocouple rake are type B, while the others are type K.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p16,1,"Experiment-unique hardware is also mounted on the chamber insert. For example, to accommodate the E-FIELD Flames experiment, the insert is equipped with a high-voltage supply and a crew-removable electrode mesh which can be set between +10 and -10 kV. The circular mesh is orthogonal to the chamber axis and is positioned (during set up) at either ~35 or ~50 mm downstream of the insulated burner. The ion current between the burner and electrode mesh will be determined via measurement of the voltage drop across a known resistor and application of Ohm’s Law. Additional images of the chamber insert can be seen in subsequent sections of this report. The hardware specific to E-FIELD Flames is discussed further in section 2.B. Additional supporting measurements will be made, where the chamber pressure is a simple example. The ACME experiments will use the CIR’s gas chromatograph for pre- and post-test analysis of the chamber atmosphere, e.g., for oxygen, nitrogen, carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide, and fuel gases. Acceleration measurements will be provided by the Space Acceleration Measurement System (SAMS) [27], which is the primary instrument for such measurements on ISS and previously served that role for the space shuttle.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p16,2,"The CIR’s Gas Chromatograph Instrumentation Package (GCIP) was not used for the CLD Flame or E-FIELD Flames investigations and thus far was only sometimes used during BRE, Flame Design, and s-Flame testing. Its use requires significant time and as such effectively reduces the number of tests that can be conducted. However, estimates of the chamber contents were computed assuming that the fuel in a normal flame test (for example) was either fully or not at all oxidized, e.g., for unsustained ignition in the latter case. The CIR facility was equipped with a 3-axis SAMS sensor head and the acceleration measurements, along with relevant documentation, are accessible via the home page for the Principal Investigator",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p17,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 17 Microgravity Services (PIMS), https://gipoc.grc.nasa.gov/wp/pims/home/. To be clear, the acceleration measurements are not archived with the ACME experiment data in the Physical Sciences Informatics (PSI) website, www.nasa.gov/PSI. All ACME experiments will be constant volume (i.e., rather than constant pressure) studies, where the chamber’s free volume will be about 90 liters. Burner flows will generally be constrained to limit the pressure rise, and tests will typically be 0.5-2 minutes in duration. The chamber’s free volume is approximately 83 liters depending on the installed hardware.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p17,1,"The ACME experiments will be configured, but not conducted, by the ISS crew. Instead, the experiments will be commanded from the ground, specifically from the Telescience Support Center (TSC) at the NASA Glenn Research Center in Cleveland, Ohio. They can be conducted in a nominally automated mode using pre-programmed scripts. However, it will also be possible to effectively operate the experiments in a “manual” mode, where changes can be made in response to the analog video downlink and the numerical data available in the telemetry stream. For example, a flow rate could be adjusted to approach a stability or soot limit. Step and ramp changes can be made in the flow rate(s) or electric field potential. The TFP fiber array can be translated uniformly through a soot-free flame to get a continuum measurement of the temperature field. In summary, ACME’s experiment control will allow for broad flexibility. The TSC is now also referred to as the Glenn ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC). Thus far, the ACME tests have always been conducted in a nominally automated mode. B. Hardware Shortcomings Key s-Flame shortcomings include image acquisition, limited imaging sensitivity (especially to the hydrogen flames), and the flow asymmetry from the burner (especially at high flow rates). Other shortcomings are discussed in this section and Appendices B to D, but the reader may want to refer to the ACME Engineering Implementation document for additional information.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p17,2,"At high flow rates, the porous spherical burner used for s-Flame did not produce spherically symmetric flames. While they were nominally spherical at moderate flow rates, the flame’s center became increasing offset from the burner center when testing with higher flow rates. More specifically, the flame shifted toward the supply tube indicating that the sintered attachment of the porous sphere to the supply tube is more ‘open’ than the pores of the sintered sphere. In other words, the flow from the burner is not spherically symmetric. At sufficiently high flow rates, the flame becomes obviously non-spherical, where it can appear to be ellipsoidal or even parabolic in shape. In the latter case, the flame locally quenches near the supply tube. When s-Flame’s first fuel bottle was installed on 30 July 2019 (19211), it was at the expected pressure, but the fuel pressure was observed to drop with time, indicating a leak. Troubleshooting, including testing with other fuel bottles, was carried out and it was concluded that the leak was related to the bottle’s quick connection and potentially caused by foreign debris. The bottle was returned to Earth unused and tested in the ground copy of the CIR, but a leak was not found in that ground testing. As a result of this anomaly, testing with a planned hydrogen/methane/nitrogen blend was not accomplished.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p18,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 18 When a fuel or diluent Mass Flow Controller (MFC) was used at a small fraction of its maximum flow rate, the actual flow rate sometimes differed from that desired. More importantly, small flow perturbations were observed in some s-Flame tests, where the flow irregularities were most apparent through their effect on Photomultiplier Tube (PMT) measurements, e.g., of OH* and CH* chemiluminescence. For example, a momentary increase in fuel flow, naturally leads to increased light from the flame. Steps were made to mitigate such behavior, e.g., by zero-ing the MFCs prior to each test, but they were not completely successful. Ignition of the flames in the second round, with helium rather than nitrogen dilution, presented a challenge. The properties of the two gases differ dramatically; for example, helium’s thermal conductivity is several times greater than that of nitrogen. A cautious approach was taken in increasing the igniter power on the first test day with helium (11 May 2020, i.e., 20132) because of concerns about potential failure of the igniter tip and the stalled operations that would result. The number of tests accomplished with that day’s conditions were limited as a result.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p18,1,"The standard igniter tip was subsequently replaced with a replaced with a coil, rather than loop, design which was believed to be more robust because of the thicker wire. The coil igniter was used successfully in subsequent testing, but still with caution where flames were not always ignited successfully. As a result, some tests with helium dilution were conducted in atmospheres with ~30% oxygen (by volume) instead of the planned ~21%, which as one would expect facilitated ignition. As a result, tests were conducted with an inconsistent mix of oxygen concentrations, which may yield valuable insights, but limits the planned test-to-test comparisons. The CIR’s Image Processing and Storage Unit for Gigabit Ethernet cameras (IPSU-G), which is used with the ACME Data Camera (DataCam) and Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) intensified camera, didn’t properly archive all of the image data on 13 Sept. 2019 (19256), where a large fraction of the image data was lost while flames were burning. After troubleshooting attempts, the IPSU-G was removed and replaced by astronaut Jessica Meir on 24 October 24 (19297) where subsequent check-out on Oct. 28 (19301) confirmed normal archival of the image data.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p18,2,"The DataCam images were mis-formatted on 18 Dec. 2019 (19352), where the cause is unknown but there were repeated operational issues with the CIR’s Image Processing and Storage Units (IPSUs) on that test day. Fortunately, the mis-formatting was not seen on subsequent test days and the problematic test day was nominally repeated on 24 Jan. 2020 (20024) to make up for the lost science. The ACME DataCam, generally the primary camera for ACME testing, is unfortunately not as sensitive as had been hoped to the dim microgravity flames. A critical challenge for s-Flame was in imaging the pure hydrogen and diluted hydrogen flames, which were not apparent or hardly visible to the DataCam and analog operations camera. This unfortunately inhibits assessment of three-dimensional flame symmetry because the CFI intensified camera and Low Light Level Ultraviolet (LLL-UV) cameras were opposite of each other. While infrequent, tests also sometimes aborted automatically, typically due to communication issues with an IPSU but sometimes because test parameters were accidentally assigned out-of- range values. Fortunately, such aborts occurred before the start of gas flow so little was lost other than the time in which the aborted tests were conducted.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p19,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 19 In contrast, a combination of factors led to testing being cut short on 3 June 2020 (20155). A high pressure reading (for a carrier gas) in the CIR’s Gas Chromatograph Instrumentation Package (GCIP) put the Fuel Oxidizer Management Assembly (FOMA) Control Unit, i.e. the FCU, in an off- nominal state. Rebooting the FCU inadvertently set the valve safety timers to zero, and they cannot be reset without venting and refilling the combustion chamber with the intended atmosphere. Those issues combined with an extended loss of commanding prevented further science operations on that day. However, a test day was added on 8 July 2020 (20190) to make up for the lost science. 4. Preliminary Results A. Changed Test Plans To limit experiment scope, ACME investigators were asked to develop test matrices including nominally 50 ignitions. The s-Flame investigators accordingly planned within that target limit whereas each of the other ACME experiments exceeded it, significantly so in most cases. To improve the balance between the ACME investigations, the decision was made to treat s-Flame’s highly desired tests, featuring diluted ethylene, as required. This was proposed by Dennis Stocker in 2015 (if not earlier) and adopted by 2016, where s-Flame was conducted on the ISS in 2019- 2020.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p19,1,"Long in advance of the launch of the ACME hardware, Stocker proposed that each experiment be conducted in two separate rounds to allow the investigators to dig into the results from past tests so that they could make the best use of the remaining testing and thereby maximize the scientific return. Although this plan was adopted, the limited crew availability in 2020 led to the decision to conduct s-Flame’s two rounds of testing sequentially. Despite this decision, the second round ISS testing with flames didn’t begin until 3.5 months after it was completed for the first round. As a result of this delay, the investigators did get a significant break between the test rounds even though ACME operations didn’t shift to a different experiment. In response to challenges with ignition during ISS tests for the preceding Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame (CLD Flame) experiment in late 2017, the decision was made to develop a coil igniter as an alternate to the standard loop type. Although it was later determined that the igniter had not been properly aligned, the decision was made to continue with the development of the coil igniter. When ignition was found to be a challenge in s-Flame’s second round testing featuring helium dilution, the decision was made to use the coil igniter for the remainder of those tests. The two igniter designs are discussed and depicted in the ACME Engineering Implementation document.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p19,2,"Given the ignition challenges in the second round, the decision was made to conduct some tests at elevated oxygen concentrations, whereas the original plan was to conduct all s-Flame tests with an oxygen concentration of nominally 21% (on a volume basis). This decision is discussed in the preceding section, 3.B Hardware Shortcomings. A few minor changes were made to the script used to run the ISS tests: 2020-01-14 Added ignition detection prior to igniter arm retraction 2020-03-11 burnerOverLimitSec variable changed to global scope 2020-03-13 Fixed ramp-holds not ramping to desired flow delta",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p20,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 20 The first change was implemented near the end of the first round of testing, while the other two changes were made between the two rounds. The final version of the script is found in the ACME/s-Flame Experimental Procedure document. B. Overview of Experiment Test Operations This section is copied from the introduction (section 1) of the April 2021 version of ‘ACME/s-Flame Experimental Procedure’ which should be consulted for additional details. The narrative description of the experimental procedure is an excerpt from the ‘Methods / Experimental’ section of the following conference paper. Y.-C. Chien, J. Tinajero, D. Stocker, U. Hegde, and D. Dunn-Rankin, “Electric Field Effects on Flames in Microgravity on the International Space Station,” 2018 Spring Technical Meeting of the Central States Section of The Combustion Institute, Minneapolis, MN, 20-22 May 2018. While the paper was written for the Electric-Field Effects on Laminar Diffusion Flames (E-FIELD Flames) experiment, much of the discussion applies to ACME in general, including the s-Flame investigation. The figure referenced in this excerpt is not included herein. Clarifications and additional information, not in the conference paper, are included in boxed notes like this.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p20,1,"The CIR experiments are configured, but not conducted, by the ISS crew. Thus far, ACME’s activity has included the majority of the crew members, regardless of their nationalities. Most often the astronauts and cosmonauts exchange gas bottles, mass flow controllers, and igniter tips. In the latter case, the igniter tip exchange can occur after failure or proactively before failure when the crew already has changes to make changes in the interior of the insert. While these activities may seem mundane, the crew play a critical role in setting up the hardware for each experiment. As an extreme example, there were crew activities on 8 separate days in Sept.-Oct. 2017 to configure the CIR and ACME hardware for the first ACME experiment, namely the Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame (CLD Flame) experiment. In contrast, only two crew days were required to reconfigure the hardware for the first E-FIELD Flames tests because of the initial use of the same coflow burner. Planning is critical because crew activity is scheduled three weeks in advance, for which detailed procedures must be prepared and uplinked to the ISS. Similarly, the s-Flame experiment was conducted using the same burner as the Flame Design investigation which preceded it. However, there was hardware reconfiguration for s-Flame to enable use of an additional intensified camera for imaging OH* chemiluminescence, namely the Low Light Level Ultraviolet (LLL-UV) camera.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p20,2,"All CIR tests are remotely commanded from the ground from NASA’s Glenn ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC) in Cleveland, Ohio. Typically, four to six people staff four consoles during the testing. The CIR hardware is controlled from the Rack Officer (RO) and Data Management Officer (DMO) consoles, and the ACME hardware is controlled from the Payload Developer (PD) console. Two shifts of personnel are scheduled to staff these three consoles on test days because they typically last 14 hours with the many pre- and post-testing activities. Given that the tests are remotely commanded, the operations are carried out in the day and evening in Cleveland, e.g., from 7 AM to 9 PM. A day’s tests, i.e., with flames, are normally conducted in a period of 10 hours of less. Tests are normally conducted on three weekdays per two weeks. On intervening weekdays, the image data is downlinked and the crew often swap one or both gas bottles.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p21,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 21 Transferring and downlinking data from a day’s tests also requires a 2-shift day for the RO and DMO consoles, where it is always necessary to verify that data was downlinked without errors and that data is not missing. Those two positions are also staffed to guide and oversee the crew activity. As a result, the CIR consoles are normally actively staffed on four weekdays per week. A team of four carry out the detailed planning for the CIR and ACME console staff, and the daily operational plans typically exceed 50 pages on days including testing.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p21,1,"The science console allows for review of the analog video and non-imaging telemetry (at 1 Hz) in near real time but doesn’t allow for commanding. In the case of E-FIELD Flames, arrangements were made so that the analog video is streamed to a secure computer at UC Irvine to allow the research team to participate in test operations and to recommend adjustments to the day’s planned parameters. That team does not see the telemetry during the testing, but is in communication via phone and e-mail with their NASA research partners at the GRC ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC). While there are many preparatory steps, the tests are ultimately conducted using an uploaded software script and parameter files. In advance of the test day, the UC Irvine science team plans the nearly 60 parameters in the voltage sweep script and specifies other parameters that are configured outside of the script, such as camera settings. It is also possible to effectively conduct tests in a ‘manual’ mode, where parameters are changed in response to the analog video downlink or telemetry. For example, the electric field potential can be adjusted to approach a stability or soot limit.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p21,2,"Provisions have been made to enable the investigators from all ACME experiments to remotely view the analog video while they communicated verbally with NASA science personnel in the operations center. Thus far, all ACME flame tests, including those for s-Flame and E-FIELD Flames, have been conducted in an automated manner using software scripts and extensive parameter files.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p21,3,"Typically, ten E-FIELD Flames tests are conducted per test day. Despite the short test durations, the low rate results from several unavoidable factors. After a test is conducted, the 125-Hz non- imaging data is downlinked for immediate review by the science team. A small sample of images are often downlinked to ensure that the camera exposures are appropriate, where the times for those images are typically selected based on non-imaging data to provide extreme examples. The review helps to verify parameters or plan for adjustments in subsequent tests, e.g., to select the voltage range after determining where the ion current saturates for the given flame conditions. After a test is conducted, a fan circulates the chamber atmosphere after which the chamber is vented (to space) back to atmospheric pressure, if needed. Midway through a day’s E-FIELD Flames tests, the atmosphere is circulated for 15 minutes through a filter cartridge filled with molecular sieves to capture any soot and adsorb at least a portion of the combustion products, e.g., carbon dioxide, water vapor, and incomplete combustion products. The tests are only conducted when both Ku and S band communication is available through NASA’s Tracking and Data Relay Satellite (TDRS) System. Depending on the various orbits, there are periodic communication outages with a loss of signal (LOS) that can be a short handover (from one satellite to another) or can last as long as 30 minutes.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p21,4,"In contrast to E-FIELD Flames, an average of 15 flames were ignited per test day for s-Flame, but they were of even shorter durations. The pressure rise with an s-Flame test was small and the chamber was rarely vented after a test unless the chamber atmosphere was being adjusted for",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p22,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 22 other purposes. In this regard, the chamber atmosphere was sometimes scrubbed for the s-Flame investigation by recirculating it through the adsorber cartridge, but this was not a regular routine as in the case of E-FIELD Flames. The chamber was also sometimes partially or completely vented and refilled during s-Flame operations, for example to adjust the atmosphere’s oxygen concentration. Unlike E-FIELD Flames, the s-Flame experiment also made use of the CIR’s Gas Chromatograph Instrumentational Package (GCIP) to measure species concentrations of the atmosphere in the combustion chamber. There is no sampling probe and measurements are only made before, between, or after tests when the chamber atmosphere was ‘stirred’ through use of a fan. For s- Flame, the GC measurements were made on most but not all test days, but they were limited in scope because of the significant time required. The GC measurements were sometimes only made at the end of the test day but were occasionally made earlier in the day, for example prior to an adjustment of the chamber atmosphere.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p22,1,"A variety of constraints limit the number of tests that can be performed, both overall and on a daily basis. The next combustion experiments in the CIR will feature solid fuels and focus on flame spread and material flammability. That follow-on plan limits the duration of ACME operations, where each ACME experiment is allotted four months for testing. Although it requires additional crew time, these allotments are split into two rounds so that the investigators have a break from testing for careful review of the early results so that they can maximize the scientific outcome of their second round. This paper reports on early results from the first round of E-FIELD Flames. In contrast to earlier plans, the two rounds of the s-Flame experiment occurred sequentially because of limited crew availability. Due to delays in commercial crew transport, the space station had three rather six crew members through much of 2020. As a result of the reduced crew size, the s-Flame investigators did get a 3-month break (in Feb. to April) between their two rounds of flame tests allowing them to study early results and prepare for the second round. Hardware issues also stretched out s-Flame operations in the latter half of 2019.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p22,2,"While nitrogen is available from the space station, the combustion testing is limited by the available gas bottles that were and are launched to support the tests. For E-FIELD Flames and most of ACME’s experiments, the test matrices used to plan those gas resources were established in 2010. At that time, NASA limited the scope of the ACME tests matrices with the intent of focused experiments with limited objectives rather than comprehensive experiments. As such, the general target was 50 tests per experiment. For most ACME experiments, the limiting gas constraint is the oxygen/nitrogen bottles used to fill the chamber. This resource constraint is exacerbated by the CIR because it is equipped with a pair of redundant safety timers to limit the fuel that can flow into the combustion chamber (where the timers can be set for the oxidizer flow in the case of inverse flame testing). The timers can only be reset by venting the combustion chamber. Therefore, the timer limit combined with other constraints effectively corresponds to the day’s maximum flow time for the fuel (or more generally, the limiting reactant). For E-FIELD Flames, the safety timers are set to the maximum value of 999 seconds, which corresponds to the 10 tests typically completed per day. The second round of the s-Flame experiment featured helium dilution of both the fuel and the oxidizer, so its testing was also limited by the oxygen/helium bottles launched for that purpose.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p23,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 23 The 999-second limit on the valve safety timers has thus far been common for all ACME testing. However, the timers can be reset to 999 by nearly completely venting the chamber. Sufficient time and gas resources are needed to vent and refill the chamber and still conduct tests afterward and that was only occasionally done for s-Flame. Another constraint is the available time in a day, where the console personnel and ISS resources, such as the video downlink, are planned for a limited number of hours. When necessary for the research, NASA does have continuous 24-hour/day operations for some non-CIR experiments. But as discussed, the ACME tests are typically two minutes or less in duration, so the testing is almost always limited to the planned durations. Data limits are yet another constraint, where the on-orbit hardware can store a limited amount of data. More importantly, 127,000 MB is the most that can be reasonably managed in a two- shift transfer and downlink day. Therefore, tests days are limited to this amount of data, where image data from select cameras is often archived at reduced rates to mitigate this challenge. Nonetheless, the limit sometimes forces decisions to abandon recorded images. This detailed description of operations and hardware provides the context under which the ACME experiments are conducted. …",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p24,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 24 Meanwhile, Table 1 is a summary log of the flames ignited, including a limited overview of the test conditions, where it is an excerpt from this report’s Appendix D. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increment 63. Note that it is comprehensive and includes the earlier testing conducted in the preceding increments.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p24,1,"Table 1. Flames ignited for the s-Flame experiment Test Day1 Year Month Date Atmosphere2 Fuel bottle Flames ignited3 First round of tests, with nitrogen-diluted fuel and oxygen 19254 2019 9 11 ~21% O2 with N2 50% C2H4 with N2 13 19256 13 16 19304 10 31 45% CH4 with N2 9 19308 11 4 ~30% O2 with N2 21 19352 12 18 ~21% O2, with N2 100% H2 10 20006 2020 1 6 44.4% H2 with CH4 17 20013 13 66.7% H2 with CH4 22 20024 24 100% H2 16 20029 29 50% C2H4 with N2 21 Second round of tests, with helium-diluted fuel and oxygen 20132 2020 5 11 ~21% O2 with He 25% H2, 20% CH4, with He 7 20136 15 20% H2, 25% CH4, with He 11 20142 21 ~30% O2 with He 30% H2, 15% CH4, with He 17 20155 6 3 ~30% and ~21% O2 with He 45% H2 with He 9 20160 8 45% CH4 with He 12 20164 12 ~30% O2 with He 20% C2H4 with He 11 20170 18 ~30% and ~21% O2 with He 25% C2H4 with He 20 20176 24 30% C2H4 with He 23 20190 7 8 45% H2 with He 21 1Test Day includes the year as the first two digits followed by the 3-digit day of the year. 2Atmosphere is ~1 atm but pressure increases and O2% drops with vitiation. 3Flames ignited is the number of nominally sustained flames ignited on that day (neglecting the day change on ISS).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p25,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 25 C. Summary of Preliminary Results The s-Flame experiment aims to characterize and gain predictive capability of the structure and dynamics of diffusion flames in simple, well-defined flow fields such that the phenomena of interest can be studied without being complicated by various flow field effects. The basic flame configuration adopted for the investigation is the spherically symmetric diffusion flame generated by discharging a fuel gas from a porous spherical burner into a quiescent, oxidizing atmosphere in microgravity. The theoretically one-dimensional nature of the system facilitates data acquisition, data reduction, and computational simulation. The s-Flame experiment uses the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) and the ACME hardware in the Destiny module of the ISS.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p25,1,"Specifically, the s-Flame tests are intended to: (i) Advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction, of both soot-free and sooty flames; (ii) Obtain experimental data in simple, well-defined flow fields and understand various key elemental diffusion flame processes; (iii) Identify the extinction limits for both radiative and convective extinction (i.e., at high and low system Damköhler numbers, respectively); (iv) Determine the existence, onset, and nature of pulsating instabilities that have been theoretically predicted to occur in such flames with fuel/diluent mixtures that are above a critical Lewis number; (v) Validate and/or improve accuracy of computational simulation including detailed chemistry and transport. The project has completed two (2) rounds of microgravity experimentation. Currently, detailed analyses of the acquired data, computational simulation, and theoretical analysis are ongoing. Interpretation of all phenomena will emphasize the simultaneous and coupled influence of chemistry and transport.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p25,2,"Table 1, on the preceding page, summarizes the tests conducted for two rounds of tests, which are meant to address the scientific objectives listed above. All experimental data address transient phenomenon, as the flame evolves from localized ignition towards “steady” behavior. The flames then evolve in a quasi-steady state manner (becoming history independent), after a short initial transient. Conventional gaseous fuels including hydrogen, methane, and ethylene are used. Hydrogen is the simplest fuel and its reaction mechanism is also reasonably established. Methane is the simplest hydrocarbon, and extensive studies have been conducted on its combustion chemistry. Ethylene is a typical sooty fuel and is also an important intermediate for the oxidation of higher hydrocarbons. Diluents mixed with fuel include nitrogen and helium to adjust mixture diffusivities and characteristic flame temperature. The rationale for the matrix of tests is to utilize various mixtures to assess both chemical and transport effects (including radiative) on flame behavior. Pure H2 and pure CH4 are utilized to assess kinetic mechanisms associated with each. Additionally, pure H2 only produces H2O as its radiative product, thereby isolating its radiative properties. Mixtures composed of both H2 and CH4 are used to examine their combined chemical and transport effects. N2 and He inerts which affect characteristic flame temperature and transport (molecular and radiative), in the same mole",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p26,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 26 fractions, are utilized for comparison. As seen in Table 1, the first round of tests examined nitrogen-diluted fuel burning in a nitrogen- diluted oxidizing atmosphere. Test day 19254 examines nitrogen-diluted C2H4 issuing into 21% O2 / 79% N2 atmosphere. C2H4 allows for the examination of a fuel with nominal unity Le number, setting a baseline standard for flame stretch and thermal diffusive stability effects. Additionally, its characteristic sooting nature (in normal gravity) directly addresses our basic science objectives focusing on spreading of flame sheets, dual extinction states, and flame-front instabilities. For a case of fuel-mixture of 20% C2H4 / 80% N2 at a flow rate of 3 cc/s, a seemingly quasi-steady flame is attained for extended duration. For the same mixture, higher flow rates of 5-10 cc/s result in radiative extinctions, corresponding to high system Damköhler number. Other mixtures of 25% C2H4 / 75% N2 and 30% C2H4 / 70% N2, also show radiative extinctions at higher flow rates. Since characteristic residence times will affect sooting tendencies, various flow rates are investigated for different mixtures. Soot is only observed during ignition, which goes away as the flames establish themselves, as seen for cases where low flow rates (e.g., 3 cc/s) with low ethylene contents (e.g., 25-30% C2H4) are ramped to high flow rates (e.g., 5-10 cc/s) with higher ethylene contents (e.g., 40-50% C2H4).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p26,1,"Soot will also enhance flame radiation, but our non-sooty flames already extinguish as they spread outwardly. Observations of such radiative extinction behavior at high flow rates is an achievement because such phenomenon was not observed conclusively in our ground-based studies, given the limited microgravity durations. Radiative reabsorption extends flame size at extinction. In ISS testing, radiative extinction has been found to be a common event, which was predicted computationally, but not definitively observed previously for our flame conditions.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p26,2,"Test day 19256 uses nitrogen-diluted C2H4 issuing into 21% O2 / 79% N2 atmosphere to examine kinetic extinction at low system Damköhler numbers. Similar to the radiative extinction cases, sufficient microgravity duration is needed to observe kinetic extinction because a near steady flame needs to be established first, before ramp down in flow rate to find properly the chemical kinetic extinction limit. For mixtures with 20-30% C2H4 content, spherical non-sooty flames are established at 3 cc/s; and kinetic extinctions are observed during ramp down before reaching 1 cc/s. For the same mixtures, radiative extinctions are observed when the non-sooty flames are established at 3 cc/s and the flow rates are ramped up before reaching 8 cc/s. Quantitative determination of the states of flame extinction, especially the dual extinction modes at high and low system Damköhler numbers have been achieved experimentally and are being compared to computational simulation.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p26,3,"Test day 19304 investigates 45% CH4 / 55% N2 mixtures issuing into 21% O2 / 79% N2 atmosphere. This pure-fuel methane case is used to compare with CH4/H2 blend mixtures. However, quasi- steady flames could not be established for the given fuel MFC (0.5 slpm). Consequently, the 19308 tests investigate the nitrogen-diluted methane flames in increased oxygen atmosphere, i.e., 30% O2 / 70% N2. Methane concentrations from 45% down to 15% are varied. For 45% CH4 / 55% N2, relatively long duration flames of ~15 s and ~10 s are established at flow rates of 2 cc/s and 3 cc/s, respectively. At larger flow rates of 5 and 9.4 cc/s, the flames expand to large radii and then extinguish, demonstrating radiative extinction at high system Damköhler number. Reduction of flow rate from 2 cc/s to 1 cc/s results in extinction during ramp down, indicating kinetic extinction at low system Damköhler number. For 30% CH4 / 70% N2, flow rates of 3-10 cc/s produce flames that expand to radiative extinction; and reduction of flow rate from 3 to 1 cc/s contracted to",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p27,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 27 kinetic extinction. Similar results are obtained for mixtures of 25% CH4 / 75% N2 and 20% CH4 / 80% N2. For 15% CH4 / 85% N2, flames are ignited, but extinguish soon after. These tests of 19308 suggest that like ethylene, methane flames can manifest both radiative and kinetic extinction for the conditions specified. We are currently mapping computationally the quasi-steady isola response for these flames to compare with the experimental extinction responses. Test day 19352 was intended to examine various concentrations of nitrogen-diluted H2 (45%-70%) issuing into air. However, various technical problems manifested that necessitated re-running of the tests on 20024 (as a make-up test day).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p27,1,"Test day 20006 examines 20% H2 / 25% CH4 / 55% N2 mixtures issuing into air. Mixture of methane and hydrogen will be compared to those of pure fuels of methane and hydrogen to address chemical kinetic and transport aspects. With regard to radiative extinction, mixture compositions of 20% H2 / 25% CH4 / 55% N2, 25% H2 / 20% CH4 / 55% N2, and 30% H2 / 15% CH4 / 55% N2 have been shown in computational simulations to be characterized by different radiative extinction times. These mixtures also avoid sooty ignition, which can otherwise result in asymmetrically trapped soot that deteriorates flame sphericity. For this mixture, the flames extinguish, some with wavy oscillating behavior just prior. However, it seems that even for very small flow rates, e.g., 2 cc/s, extinction is by the radiative mechanism during flame expansion. There was no parameter space in terms of flow rate and burner size to identify conclusively kinetic extinction during ramp down for these mixtures. Radiative extinction (with local flame extinction and re- ignition seems to occur prior to ultimate extinguishment) during flame expansion after ignition is observed.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p27,2,"Test day 20013 mainly investigates 30% H2 /15% CH4 / 55% N2 mixtures issuing into air. Again, mixture of methane and hydrogen are run to compare with pure fuels of methane and hydrogen. Note that 25% H2/ 20% CH4 / 55% N2 mixtures could not be run on the ISS because the mixture bottles had leaked. Radiative extinction is difficult to identify conclusively because flames with larger flow rates, e.g. 15 cc/s, seem to last, although with patchy extinction areas, until time out. Larger flow rates could not be examined either because of limited MFC range or distorted flame shape caused by burner limitations at high flow rates. Also, kinetic extinction during ramp down could not be identified conclusively, as some flames seem to extinguish because they contract into the burner.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p27,3,"Test day 20024 is a makeup day for 19352 – when the 0.1-slpm fuel MFC range was too low to examine radiative extinction and the analog and intensified cameras did not pick up the flame images at all or well – examining nitrogen-diluted H2. Here, a 0.5-slpm fuel MFC is used and only a fused silica filter is used for the intensified camera. 45%-15% H2/N2 balance mixtures are investigated, where flames at high flow rates, e.g., 10 cc/s, did not extinguish, appearing quasi- steady until time out. The hydrogen flames would likely need to reach a diameter much larger than that accommodated by the experiment to extinguish radiatively. The flames seem to extinguish during ramp down, but further analysis is needed to assess kinetic extinction at low Damköhler number. Test day 20029 uses and repeats and augments conditions of 19256 – when the images were corrupt – for nitrogen-diluted ethylene flames. Again, the results indicate both kinetic and radiative extinction phenomena under corresponding conditions. No sooting flames are observed.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p28,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 28 As seen in Table 1, the second round of tests replaces the inert, N2, with He, to better isolate Le>1 instabilities. However, higher characteristic flame temperatures (given the lower molar heat capacity of monatomic He versus that of diatomic N2) correspond to lower Zeldovich numbers, which would suppress oscillations based on Sivashinsky’s criterion for premixed flames. Moreover, lower Ze may also affect extinction processes. Test day 20132 examines helium-diluted fuel mixture of 25% H2 / 20% CH4 / 55% He issuing into an ambient of 21% O2 / 79% He. The high thermal conductivity of He drastically shifts the ignition parameters from that using N2 as inert in Round 1. The tests produced flames at 3-10 cc/s that expand outwardly quickly and extinguish. For 5-10 cc/s, the flames seemed to disappear and 're- ignite' with a ""flash"" before final extinction. We are currently investigating the flash phenomenon.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p28,1,"Test day 20136 investigates the fuel blend 20% H2 / 25% CH4 / 55% He issuing into 21% O2 / 79% He. For 5 cc/s and 7 cc/s, the mixture ignites but immediately extinguishes, with almost no observable flame-front expansion. For 10-17.5 cc/s, the mixtures ignite, expand quickly, and extinguish. The extinction mechanism is believed to be radiative and is being assessed with computational simulations. For the cases with a ramp down in flow rate, the flames seem to extinguish before ramp down. Thus, purely kinetic extinction could not be assessed. Only flow rates above 7 cc/s showed flame-front expansion leading to extinction, with 17.5 cc/s showing a flash or wavering of the flame surface before final extinction. Test day 20142 investigates the fuel blend 30% H2 / 15% CH4 / 55% He issuing into 30% O2/ 70% He. Elevated oxygen ambient facilitates ignition and results in smaller characteristic flame size. For 5-16.5 cc/s flow rates, flames expand to wavy/oscillatory radiative extinction. Ramp down cases did not allow for assessment of kinetic extinction.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p28,2,"Test day 20155 examines 45% H2 / 55% He issuing into 30% O2/ 70% He and 21% O2 / 79% He. Radiative and kinetic extinction processes are not conclusively identified for these cases because the automated protocol ended the tests as the burner temperature routinely reached 250°C. Presumably, the thermally-conductive He diluent augments heat transfer to the burner. Nevertheless, nice transient flames are produced, such that the transient flame structure (e.g., temperature and species distribution) can be characterized by the ACME diagnostics and compared with computational simulation and theoretical analysis, along with cross comparisons with the N2 inert cases of Round 1. Test day 20160 investigates 45% CH4 / 55% He issuing into 30% O2 / 70% He and 21% O2 / 79% He. For the 30% O2/ 70% He atmosphere, the 5-15 cc/s flow rates are sooty at ignition, but subsequently become non-sooty, expanding to radiative extinction. At 6 and 7 cc/s, flames ignite less sooty, expanding with no soot to radiative extinction. For tests comprising 21% O2 / 79% He atmosphere, at 5-15 cc/s, flames ignite sooty, but dissipate, and expand quickly to radiative extinction.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p28,3,"Test day 20164 studies ethylene-based flames, where 20% C2H4 / 80% He issues into 30% O2 / 70% He and 21% O2 / 79% He atmospheres. For 5 cc/s issuing into 30% O2 / 70% He, the flame expands and remains quasi-steady until the burner limit temperature (250°C) is reached and the experiment shuts down. At 6-15 cc/s, the flames expand to radiative extinction, with the higher flow rates at 10-15 cc/s exhibiting sooty ignition. Fuel flow of 5.5 cc/s seems to correspond to a",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p29,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 29 condition where the onset of oscillating/wavy extinction is concurrent with the burner reaching the 250°C temperature limit. For the cases with a ramp down in flow rate, the flames seem to extinguish during ramp down, but heat loss to the burner may be the mechanism for extinction rather than kinetic extinction. Ignition is not achieved for tests conducted with 21% O2 / 79% He atmosphere, for the ignition parameters utilized. Test day 20170 investigates 25% C2H4 / 75% He issuing into 30% O2 / 70% He and 21% O2 / 79% He. At 5 and 5.5 cc/s issuing into 30% O2 / 70% He, blue chemiluminescent flames are ignited, but eventually cut off because of the burner temperature limit being reached. At 6 cc/s, a blue chemiluminescent flame expands slowly to radiative extinction. At 7-15 cc/s, sooty ignition occurs, but the soot goes away as the flames expand toward radiative extinction. By ramping the flow rates up, starting at 5 cc/s, soot at ignition is minimized. Ramp down of the flow rate results in almost immediate extinction. For tests at 21% O2 / 79% He atmosphere, cases at 5-15 cc/s produce flames that expand quickly to extinction. Less soot is present at ignition compared to those for 30% O2 / 70% He atmospheres.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p29,1,"Test day 20176 explores 30% C2H4 / 70% He issuing into 30% O2 / 70% He and 21% O2 / 79% He. For 30% O2 / 70% He, at 5 cc/s, a long duration flame is established that eventually reaches the burner temperature limit. At 6-15 cc/s, the flames are sooty at ignition, but the soot dissipates; and the flames expand to radiative extinction. Ramping up the flow rates, starting at 5 cc/s, minimizes soot at ignition and results in flames that slowly expand to radiative extinction. For tests at 21% O2/ 79% He atmosphere, cases at 5-15 cc/s produce flames that expand to radiative extinction. Ramp down of the flow rate did not produce flames that could confirm kinetic extinction. Test day 20190 is a makeup of 20155, with 45% H2 / 55% He issuing into 30% O2 / 70% He and 21% O2 / 79% He. For both oxygen concentration environments, flow rates resulting in expanding flames lasted for a while but were cut off by automated protocol for burner temperature limit before radiative extinction. As for cases where the flow rates are ramped down, extinction detection is observed for both oxygen concentration environments. It is not clear if extinction is kinetic or from heat loss to the burner. Detailed analyses are needed. Tests with a reduced oxygen concentration atmosphere of 17.5% O2 / 82.5% He seems to show both radiative and kinetic extinction. Additional analyses are needed.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p29,2,"D. Surprises and New Discoveries Many discoveries are expected to arise as the data is analyzed and computational simulations are compared. Initial surprises and discoveries include: (1) Nitrogen-diluted methane and ethylene flames in air seem to demonstrate kinetic extinction at low flow rates (low system Da) and radiative extinction at high flow rates (high system Da). Such phenomena were not observed in our ground-based research, given the limited microgravity durations. Nevertheless, comparisons with computational simulations are needed to assess better the extinction behavior. Radiative reabsorption extends flame size at extinction, making the chamber size an important parameter. Surprisingly, radiative extinction has been found to be a common event in ISS testing, which was predicted computationally, but not observed previously for our flame conditions. Similarly, sufficient microgravity duration is needed to observe kinetic extinction because a near steady flame",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p30,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 30 need to be established first, before ramp down in flow rate to find properly the chemical kinetic extinction limit. The nitrogen-diluted methane flames were tested in air and enriched O2 environments. The enriched O2 environments produce smaller characteristic flames; nonetheless, both extinction limits appear to have been observed. (2) Ethylene flames which tend to soot under normal gravity conditions did not soot under microgravity conditions. Sooting only occurred during forced ignition from the hotwire, but soon dissipated. Inherent sooting was also not found for enriched oxidizer conditions or for either of the inerts, i.e., N2 and He, examined. (3) Nitrogen-diluted hydrogen flames seem to need to be at very large characteristic sizes to self- extinguish. As such, radiative extinction was not observed for the cases run, despite lowering the H2 concentration. However, kinetic extinction during ramp down seem to be exhibited for hydrogen flames. (4) Nitrogen-diluted hydrogen/methane mixture flames manifested radiative extinction, as predicted by computational simulations. However, kinetic extinction at low flow rates could not be identified conclusively. The characteristic extinction radius may be within the burner radius, precluding experimental identification. More analyses of the data are needed. (5) Helium-diluted cases had shifted extinction limits with respect to the nitrogen-diluted cases.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p30,1,"In general, kinetic extinction was difficult to observe for the conditions examined. Ignition was also severely affected by using He as diluent. More analyses are needed to assess instability near the flame limits, which was predicted to occur computationally. (6) The He inert cases are meant to better isolate pulsating instabilities that are theoretically predicted to occur in transient spherical diffusion flames using fuel/diluent mixtures that are above a critical Lewis number. However, such instabilities were not conclusively observed in the ISS tests, at least from the video images. Radiometer and PMT data will need to be examined in detail. (7) The highly thermal conductive nature of He seems to affect various processes, such as ignition and heat transfer to the burner (affecting isolation of kinetic extinction), despite higher characteristic flame temperatures. (8) Flame luminosity does not necessarily correspond with flame strength (or combustion intensity). E. Accomplishment of Approved Objectives The ISS experimental matrix was proposed to address the following specific objectives, in both soot-free and sooty flames. Comparisons with detailed computational simulation will permit assessment of transport properties and detailed chemical kinetics (including chemiluminescence). The accomplishment aspect is given after the statement of each objective.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p30,2,"Objective A. Measure and characterize the transient structure (such as temperature and species distribution) of diffusion flames established at initially non-steady, spherical flame structures for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty), for different flow rates. Transient flames, both expanding and contracting, were achieved for all fuel mixtures. Some flames appeared to be quasi-steady in their evolution from a non-steady ignition origin.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p31,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 31 Characterization of the flame structures is still being done, e.g., analyzing and processing data from thin-filament pyrometry to determine the temperature distribution. Species distribution is not explicitly measured and would need to be inferred from data such as radiometry. Characteristically sooty flames were not observed. Transient sooting only occurred during forced ignition; however, the ignition process and associated phenomena are being analyzed. Transient oscillatory/wavy response of the flame surface prior to extinction was often observed and is being analyzed. Objective B. Determine the quasi-steady convective/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (low system Damköhler numbers) and radiative/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (high system Damköhler numbers) for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p31,1,"Nitrogen-diluted ethylene and methane evinced both radiative and kinetic extinctions. Nitrogen- diluted hydrogen/methane blends only demonstrated radiative extinction for the parameters tested. Nitrogen-diluted hydrogen cases may have indicated kinetic extinction, but more analyses are needed. Helium-diluted hydrogen/methane blends may have shown radiative extinction, but more analyses are needed. Helium-diluted hydrogen displayed radiative extinction and showed kinetic extinction for oxygen-reduced environments. Helium-diluted ethylene and methane exhibited radiative extinction. None of the flames were inherently sooty, so the effect of soot on extinction could not be investigated. Objective C. Determine the existence (for different Zeldovich number, Ze), onset (as a function Da), and nature (i.e., mode, frequency, amplitude) of pulsating instabilities theoretically predicted to occur in transient spherical diffusion flames using fuel/diluent mixtures that are above a critical Lewis number. Many flames presented unstable behavior near the burner tube prior to extinction, likely because of lack of fuel mixture and heat loss in that region, rather than inherent pulsating instability. However, additional analyses, especially of PMT and radiometer data, are needed to assess the existent of pulsating instability, especially for helium as diluent.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p31,2,"F. Additional Objectives Achieved No additional objectives have yet to be achieved. However, as we analyze and interpret the data, we expect new discoveries and insights into the fundamental mechanisms. Computational simulations will augment some test cases, and comparisons with experimental data will allow for extraction of basic parameters and assessment of chemical kinetic mechanisms. The role of the inerts will be better revealed, as the tests showed that they are far from passive participants in the flame dynamics and responses. Some additional objectives that may be achieved in the future would involve the following aspects given below. (A) Some experimental cases involved initial ramp up with subsequent ramp down in the flow rate, which may reveal hysteresis in the flame response.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p32,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 32 (B) The acquired data will allow for investigation of flame ignition and transient dynamics in 2D and 3D geometry, along with analysis of the emergence and dynamics of 2D and 3D wave patterns for near-limit spherical diffusion flames, and analysis of their role in flame extinction. G. Impacts, Benefits, and Products The impact of this work consists of both practical and fundamental aspects. Practically, with diffusion flame burning as the basis in many combustion engines and devices, understanding the underlying dynamics and chemistry of diffusion flames is important in improving performance in terms of power, efficiency, pollutant emissions, and reliability. Fundamentally, the unique buoyancy-free environment offered by microgravity affords simple aerodynamic flow fields to acquire diffusion flame data of high fidelity, with the prospect of understanding unit thermochemical processes as well as quantifying the associated physico-chemical parameters. In analyzing and validating the data sets, the experimental data and corresponding computational simulations should yield quantitative and qualitative understanding of various elemental flame phenomena. In studying the transient motion of diffusion flames ignited from a non-steady-state initial condition, the significance of fuel accumulation will be evaluated, which was originally found to be important in droplet burning.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p32,1,"Here, with no droplet surface regression nor coupling of fuel flow to heat transfer from the flame to influence flame-front motion, the effect can be better isolated and the far-field unsteadiness assessed. Quantitative predictability of the flame motion permits assessment of the parameters and mechanisms involved in radiation loss and re- absorption as well as other transport parameters. The microgravity environment greatly facilitates the examination of pulsating instabilities, which can be hidden or coupled to other processes in normal gravity. Moreover, involvement of multi-dimensional effects, rather than the nominally 1-D geometry here, would further complicate the manifestation of the phenomenon. Near-limit phenomena, including extinction, is best evaluated and validated using our one- dimensional spherical diffusion flame (as realized in the microgravity environment), which is probably the most desirable flame configuration for study of this nature. Understanding extinction phenomenon is paramount to revealing basic flame processes, along with highlighting implications in fire safety.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p32,2,"The results are of strong interest to other groups working on diffusion flames, including those also doing ISS-based experiments. The main products will be (i) insights from fundamental characterization of transient dynamics and responses of such flames, (ii) categorization of processes that are either quasi-steady (e.g., expanding flame front motion toward steady state) or fully transient (e.g., pulsating instabilities prior to extinction), (iii) quantitative and qualitative mapping of extinction boundaries and unstable regimes, (iv) clarification of the role of diluents in various processes, and (v) additional understanding of ignition in non-premixed flames. Engines and devices on Earth can be improved in efficiency, pollutant emissions, and dependability from such products. H. Patents No patents have been filed from the s-Flame research, yet.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p33,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 33 5. Follow-On Experiments Although two rounds of testing have been completed, with no further testing planned, if a third round becomes available, there are two main types of experiments that we would like to implement. We would like to examine the same fuel mixtures (plus C3H8 as mentioned below) of the first two rounds, but with CO2 as inert, including that for the ambient. N2 and He are radiatively transparent gases; while CO2 is an optically participating gas. Use of CO2 should result in a characteristically lower flame temperature, increasing the Zeldovich number, Ze. At the same time, the highly reabsorptive CO2 species can minimize net radiative heat loss affecting flame dynamics and extinction. Objectives A, B, and C would be addressed by these experiments.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p33,1,"Additionally, characteristically sooty flames were not observed in the first two rounds for the fuel mixtures tested. As such, we would like to investigate using C3H8 as fuel, which was originally specified in the SRD that was approved [in 2002, but which was not part of the s-Flame plan under ACME, which didn’t begin until 2006]. Specific knowledge originally sought in the first two rounds with respect to sooty flames, which were not achieved consist of: (a) the transient and steady state behavior and structure of sooting spherical flames, (b) residence time effect on soot formation, with only curvature effects and no strain, and (c) the effects of soot radiation and transport on extinction, at constant mass flow rate. The results so far will be compared with computational simulations to address deficiencies in fundamental parameters and models. The aforementioned CO2 experiments are highly desired; however, experimentation with C3H8 is of utmost importance if we are to achieve our original objectives with respect to characteristically sooty flames, which were not attained for the fuel mixtures and conditions tested in the first two rounds.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p34,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 34 Appendix A. Abbreviations and Acronyms ##### ACME days are identified by year (2 digits) and the day of the year (3 digits, 001-366) #####x# ACME tests are identified by day (5 digits;",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p34,1,"see above), a letter, and a 1-digit number 0-g microgravity 1-g normal gravity (on the surface of the Earth) ACME Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (CIR project) ADP Acceptance Data Package ASGSR American Society for Gravitational and Space Research AVP avionics package BRE Burning Rate Emulator (an ACME experiment) C2H4 ethylene C3H8 propane CH2O formaldehyde CASIS Center for the Advancement of Science in Space CCD Charge-Coupled Device (such as a camera sensor) CFI Cool Flames Investigation (an MDCA experiment preceding ACME in the CIR) CFI-G Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (an ACME experiment) CH4 methane CIA Chamber Insert Assembly (inserted into the CIR combustion chamber) CIR Combustion Integrated Rack (ISS facility in which ACME experiments are conducted) CLD Flame Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame (an ACME experiments) CM Combustion Module (space shuttle precursor to the CIR) cm centimeter CO2 carn dioxide Co-I Co-Investigator CRS Commercial Resupply Service (a designation of ISS cargo missions) CSA Canadian Space Agency CSV comma-separated values CWRU Case Western Reserve University Da Damköhler number DataCam Data Camera (ACME’s primary camera which was characterized for pyrometry, etc.) E-FIELD Flames Electric-Field Effects on Laminar Diffusion Flames (an ACME experiment) EMI electromagnetic interference EMS Experiment Mounting Structure ERD Engineering Requirements Document ESA European Space Agency Fiji Fiji Is Just ImageJ (a version of ImageJ",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p34,2,preloaded with many plug-in features) Flame Design Flame Design (an ACME experiment) FOMA Fuel and Oxidizer Management Assembly (CIR’s gas management subsystem) FOV field of view fps frames per second GC Gas Chromatograph (see GCIP) GCIP Gas Chromatograph Instrumentation Package (for measuring CIR chamber contents) GigE Gigabit Ethernet (an interface standard for high performance cameras) GIPOC Glenn ISS Payload Operations Center (also known as the TSC) GMT Greenwich Mean Time (where most references in this report are the day) H2 hydrogen,,False
