PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p35,1,"used for the DataCam) HV high voltage HVPS High-Voltage Power Supply ICDERS International Colloquium on the Dynamics of Explosions and Reactive Systems ID inner diameter (e.g., of burner tube) ImageJ Image Processing and Analysis in Java (open-source image processing software) IPSU Image Processing and Storage Unit (CIR device for recording images on ISS) IPSU-G IPSU for GigE cameras (including the DataCam and CFI intensified camera) ISRD Integrated Science Requirements Document (for a set of experiments) ISS International Space Station JAXA Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency kPa kilopascal kV kilovolt LLL-UV Low Light Level Ultraviolet camera ((developed for the CIR facility) LSP Laminar Soot Processes (a shuttle study conducted in the Combustion Module) MDCA Multi-user Droplet Combustion Apparatus (a CIR chamber insert) MFC Mass Flow Controller Mist Water-Mist Fire Suppression Experiment (a Combustion Module experiment) MIUL Materials Identification/Usage List mm millimeter N2 nitrogen NSF National Science Foundation NTSC National Television System Committee O2 oxygen OMA2 Optical Multi-channel Analyzer 2 (open-source software for image processing) ops operations PARIS Passive Rack Isolation System (with which the CIR is equipped, but not used for ACME) PI Principal Investigator PIMS Principal Investigator Microgravity Services PMT Photomultiplier Tube (where ACME is equipped with three PMT modules) PS Project Scientist PSI Physical Science Informatics System psia pounds",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p35,2,"per square inch PT pressure transducer QD quick disconnect qty quantity RSA Russian Space Agency, i.e., Roscosmos s second SAMS Space Acceleration Measurement System SDP Safety Data Package s-Flame Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (an ACME experiment) SiC Silicon Carbide (material of the TFP fibers) slpm standard liters per minute (a measure of the mass or molar flow rate of a gas) SOFBALL Structure Of Flame Balls At Low Lewis-number (a Combustion Module study) SoFIE Solid Fuel Ignition and Extinction (project to follow ACME in the CIR facility) SORD Science Operations Requirements Document (an ACME supplement to the ISRD) SRD Science Requirements Document",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p36,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 36 TDRS Tracking and Data Relay Satellite (System) TFP Thin Filament Pyrometry (a method for gas-phase temperature measurements) TIFF or TIF Tagged Image File Format (for the CFI intensified, HiBMs, and LLL-UV cameras) TM thermistor TS Transport Stream (a video format) TSC Telescience Support Center (also known as the GIPOC) UV ultraviolet Ze Zeldovich number zero-g zero gravity, i.e., microgravity ZIN ZIN Technologies, Inc. (ACME engineering and operations provider) µA microamp While this list of abbreviations and acronyms is long, it is not comprehensive. Contact ACME Project Scientist Dennis Stocker (216-433-2166, dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov) for additional clarification.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p37,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 37 Appendix B. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increments 59 and 60 16 December 2019 30-day Operational Accomplishments Report … for the International Space Station (ISS) Increments 59 and 60 ACME Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments s-Flame Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames The s-Flame investigation is one of several independent experiments that are part of the ACME project, which uses a single modular set of hardware to carry out studies of flames burning gaseous fuels in the ISS’ Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). The initial days of s-Flame testing with flames were carried out in the final month of Increment 60. As a result, there is nothing to be reported for Increment 59. Prepared by the PI team and Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov), the ACME Project Scientist, who is the primary contact for s-Flame and ACME information. Principal Investigator (PI): Prof. C.K. (Ed) Law, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ Co-Investigator (Co-I): Prof. Stephen D. Tse, Rutgers University, Piscataway, NJ Lead collaborator: Dr. Vladimir V. Gubernov, P.N. Lebedev Physical Institute of the Russian Academy of Sciences, Moscow, Russia NASA Project Scientist (PS): Dennis P. Stocker, NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, OH",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p37,1,"I. Introduction Tests for the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment were conducted in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on the International Space Station, as part of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project. The purpose of s-Flame is to advance the ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction and instabilities, of both soot-free and sooty flames. The results may contribute to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions here on Earth. The spherical flames are ignited at non-steady conditions and allowed to transition naturally toward extinction. Tests are conducted with various inert diluents in both the fuel and chamber atmosphere. The fuel gases include hydrogen and methane mixtures for soot-free flames, and ethylene for sooty flames. One experiment objective is to identify the extinction limits for both radiative and chemical kinetic extinction, i.e., where the flame extinguishes from heat loss or is blown out, respectively. Another objective is to determine the existence, onset, and nature of",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p38,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 38 pulsating instabilities that have been theoretically predicted to occur in flames with certain fuel/inert mixtures. More specifically, s-Flame has the following experimental objectives: A. Measure and characterize the transient structure (such as temperature and species distribution) of diffusion flames established at initially non-steady, spherical flame structures for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty), for different flow rates. B. Determine the quasi-steady convective/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (low system Damköhler numbers) and radiative/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (high system Damköhler numbers) for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty). A. Determine the existence (for different Zeldovich number, Ze), onset (as a function Da), and nature (i.e. mode, frequency, amplitude) of pulsating instabilities theoretically predicted to occur in transient spherical diffusion flames using fuel/diluent mixtures that are above a critical Lewis number. As suggested by the experiment’s name, s-Flame features a spherically symmetric diffusion flame, which is generated by discharging a fuel gas from a porous spherical burner into a still oxygen/inert atmosphere in microgravity. The one-dimensional nature of the system facilitates data acquisition, data reduction, and computational simulation.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p38,1,"The space flight program consists of two main parts: (1) Mapping and characterization of the motion (e.g., expansion) of spherical diffusion flames subsequent to ignition and at extinction because of the finite-rate chemistry and radiative heat loss. The study is focused on the thermal radiation, the adequacy of the detailed chemistry and transport submodels used in computational modeling, and the development of flame front instabilities that could modify the extinction boundaries. (2) Studies of the response of characteristically sooty flames to ignition, the onset of soot in the expanding flames, the soot radiation, and the soot’s effects on the flame spread, extinction, and instability.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p38,2,"Microgravity studies of flames allow for a range of simplifications and provide various advantages over normal-gravity studies. For example, the natural flicker of flames, which is a buoyancy- driven (i.e., gravity-driven) instability, is eliminated yielding quasi-steady flames in microgravity. Length scales are increased in microgravity flames facilitating analysis of the flame structure. Momentum-dominated flames, which are important for most practical combustion, can be studied at low velocities to simplify analysis. Microgravity flames tend to have a much stronger sensitivity to their atmosphere and exhibit a much broader range of characteristics than normal- gravity flames because of the near absence of buoyant entrainment. The long residence times in microgravity flames can lead to strong soot production, but many microgravity flames are soot free. The absence of buoyancy makes microgravity flames advantageous for studies of limit and stability behavior where chemical kinetics are important. For example, chemi-ion species, electronically-excited chemiluminescent species, are at very low concentration in typical flames, making the reduced buoyancy environment valuable for evaluating the minor species chemistry.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p39,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 39 With these advantages, microgravity testing can lead to new understanding of the complex nature of combustion – regardless of the gravitational environment of the combustion application. II. Methods/Research Operations A. Discussion of Method/Protocol The s-Flame investigation and other ACME experiments are configured, but not conducted, by the ISS crew. The crew’s role is to set up the hardware, gas bottles, etc. for each set of tests. The most common changes include the gas bottles on the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) and the mass flow controllers on the modular ACME chamber insert. All testing is commanded from the ground, specifically from the Glenn ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC). The science portion of the testing is typically conducted in a nominally automated mode using pre-programmed scripts. However, it may alternately be conducted in a ‘manual’ mode, where changes are made to a control parameter in response to the analog video downlink and the numerical data available in the telemetry stream. But it is not expected that such control, which might be used to approach a stability or soot limit, will be used for s-Flame. Thus far, that has been the case for this experiment, with the possible mundane exception of ‘manually’ halting the test after the flame has extinguished. Testing is not conducted when thrusters fire or spacecraft dock or undock from ISS to ensure an acceptable acceleration environment.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p39,1,"Data is downlinked between each set of tests, i.e., day of testing, to allow for preliminary analysis and requisite planning for subsequent testing. III. Results A. Baselined Activities Completed – Pre-flight, In-flight, Post-Flight A total of 29 flames were ignited for the s-Flame experiment in its two days of flame testing during Increment 60. Note that the investigation’s science testing didn’t begin until September 2019, so no flames were ignited during Increment 59. The s-Flame experiment does not have living subjects like a life science experiment, but the tests are independently conducted with various gaseous fuels, including hydrogen, methane, hydrogen/methane blends, and ethylene. These are simple hydrocarbon gases for which the chemistry is relatively well understood. The Increment 60 tests were all conducted with ethylene (C2H4), which is typically highly sooting because of its carbon double bond, but where the fuel was diluted with nitrogen for these tests.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p40,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 40 No samples (e.g., of soot) were collected for s-Flame; all of the ISS results were electronically transmitted to the ground. That is furthermore the case for all currently planned ACME experiments. A summary log of the flames ignited for s-Flame during Increment 60, including a limited overview of the test conditions, is found in Table 1. Table 1. s-Flame log of flames ignited in Increment 60 (where the investigation did not begin until Increment 60) Test Day1 Year Month Date Atmosphere2 Chamber Pressure Fuel bottle Flames ignited2 First round of tests, with nitrogen-diluted fuel, in Sept. 2019 (Increment 60) 19254 2019 9 11 ~21% O2, with N2 ~1 atm 50% C2H4 with N2 13 19256 13 16 1Test Day includes the year as the first two digits followed by the 3-digit day of the year. 2Flames ignited is the number ignited on that day (neglecting the day change on ISS). Pre-flight and post-flight activities are generally not addressed in this report.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p40,1,"B. List of Preflight, In-flight, Postflight Anomalies 1. Mission anomalies When s-Flame’s first fuel bottle was installed on the CIR on July 30 (19211) it was at the expected pressure, but the fuel pressure was observed to drop with time, indicating a leak. Troubleshooting, including testing with other fuel bottles, was carried out and it was concluded that the issue was related to the first s-Flame fuel bottle, which contained a hydrogen/methane blend. It was concluded that the leak was related to the quick connection, where it might have been caused by foreign debris. The bottle was returned to Earth and tested in the ground copy of the CIR, but surprisingly a leak was not found in that testing. During the review of the leaked fuel bottle, it was determined that the bottles with the hydrogen/methane blends (H2/CH4) were over-filled in that the flammable and explosive limits could be reached if the fuel were to leak into the open volume of the CIR rack. A mistake had been made in the determination of the bottle pressure which would be safe in the event of a leak. The two fuels were considered individually instead of collectively using Le Chatelier's mixing rule. Therefore, before their use, the plan is to vent each H2/CH4 bottle to a pressure that will be safe if a bottle leaks into the CIR free volume.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p40,2,"The CIR’s IPSU-G Image Processing and Storage Unit for Gigabit Ethernet cameras, which is used with the ACME Data Camera (DataCam) and Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) intensified camera, didn’t properly archive all of the image data on Sept. 13",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p41,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 41 (19256). A large fraction of the image data while the flame was burning was lost. Trouble shooting was subsequently conducted to hopefully resolve the issue before further flame testing was conducted. Ultimately, the IPSU-G was removed and replaced by astronaut Jessica Meir on October 24 (19297) where subsequent check- out on Oct. 28 (19301) confirmed normal archival of the image data. Regular testing resumed on Oct. 31 (19304). 2. Any other difficulties in obtaining scientific data During Increment 60, one s-Flame test automatically aborted (due to the controlling software script), where that was prior to gas flow and the activation of the igniter – which is typical for other ACME experiments. As a result, there was little lost other than the time in the failed test attempt. ACME aborts normally occur because of communication issues with an IPSU, although in this case it was because of operator error. ACME aborts are infrequent, but there are sometimes days when they occur repeatedly.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p41,1,"Tests are of course also unsuccessful when the flame fails to ignite. Two tests on the first day of testing failed to ignite, where adjustments to the tests parameters associated with the relative timing of the fuel flow and igniter activation thereafter resulted in successful ignition, with the exception of the abort discussed above. For other ACME tests, ignition has sometime been unsuccessful because the settings for the automatic flame detection are such that detection threshold is reached because of the igniter glow rather than a flame. As a result of the detection, the hot-wire igniter is deactivated and retracted, which is of course an issue if the flame has yet to ignite. This is typically avoided by detecting ignition with measurement of the flame’s OH* chemiluminescence with a Photomultiplier Tube (PMT) module. The OH* chemiluminescence, in the ultraviolet at 310 nm, is a good indicator of an ignited flame rather than the glowing igniter, which emits primarily in the infrared and visible spectrum. However, the combination of igniter settings and PMT gains can lead to false ignition detection.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p41,2,"At high flow rates, the burner does not produce spherically symmetric flames. While nominally spherical at moderate flow rates, the flame’s center becomes increasing offset from the burner center. More specifically, the flame is shifted toward the supply tube indicating that the sintered attachment of the porous sphere to the supply tube is more ‘open’ than the pores of the spherical burner. In other words, the flow from the burner is not spherically symmetric. At sufficiently high flow rates, the flame becomes obviously non-spherical, where it can appear to be ellipsoidal or even parabolic in shape. In the latter case, the flame locally quenches near the supply tube. Small flow perturbations have been noticed in some s-Flame tests, where ACME’s Mass Flow Controllers (MFCs) used to control the flow of fuel, nitrogen, and oxidizer have unfortunately not always performed satisfactorily. The flow irregularities have been most apparent through their effect on the Photomultiplier Tube (PMT)",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p42,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 42 measurements, e.g., of OH* and CH* chemiluminescence. A momentary increase in fuel flow, as has occurred in some s-Flame tests, naturally leads to increased light from the flame. The MFCs are tested - without flames - after they are first installed. Adjustments are typically made to (1) the setting that controls when the valves open and (2) the Proportional-Integral-Derivative (PID) settings. This ACME activity has involved significant testing, where there are an assortment of MFCs with different flow ranges for each of the three gas types. While the MFC adjustments have been beneficial, they have not eliminated all gas flow problems. It was seen in prior ACME testing that nitrogen oscillations (if any) tend to damp with time, so a nitrogen pre- flow can be used so that such oscillations might end before the flame is ignited. As another measure, the MFCs are re-zeroed prior to each test, where that was another measure adopted in earlier ACME testing.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p42,1,"C. Completeness/Quality of Data As a result of the anomalies discussed in III.B.1, only two days of flame tests were conducted for the s-Flame investigation during Increment 60, whereas approximately 16 days of such testing are planned. However, 29 flames were successfully ignited which is significant in comparison to the 58 tests specified as required in the ACME Integrated Science Requirements Document (ISRD). Therefore a wealth of data was obtained for a limited set of test conditions. However, a large fraction of the key image data from the tests conducted on Sept. 13 (19256) was not archived (again as discussed in III.B.1). As a result, that test day will be repeated. Data quality is of course dependent on the experimental hardware. But neglecting that limitation on data quality, the cameras are sensitive to radiation where pixels gradually become ‘hot’ (i.e., dead) over time. While these bright spots are addressed in image post- processing, they are a degradation of the image quality. It is possible that the tests are subtly affected by the acceleration environment, but there is not obvious indication of this and it has thus far not been evaluated with available measurements from the Space Acceleration Measurement System (SAMS). In this regard, note that the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR), in which the experiment is conducted, is equipped with a Passive Rack Isolation System (PaRIS).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p42,2,"D. Status of Data Analysis Image files are currently being processed. Radial position of maximum CH* chemilumenscence is being plotted against time. Different radii are being considered and plotted together, as some flames are not completely symmetric. MFC and PMT data are",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p43,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 43 being compared with video images to assess and confirm experimental settings. Radiometer data is being reviewed. Analysis of the fiber pyrometry data has not begun. Computational simulations with detailed chemistry and transport of the test cases are underway. Time dependent simulations will be compared with the time-dependent experimental data. Steady-state simulations will produce the Isola curves mapping flame response versus system Damkhöler number for given fuel mixtures. The experimental cases will then be pin-pointed on the computational mappings to assess whether the experimental conditions were in quasi-steady state or near extinction limits, either kinetic or radiative. Oscillation behavior will be correlated to possible pulsating oscillations found in the simulations. E. Preliminary Research Findings The s-Flame experiment utilizes spherically-symmetric diffusion flames and their dynamics and responses to investigate fundamental processes, such as extinction, instability, and soot formation. ISS testing for 19254 and 19256 examined nitrogen- diluted ethylene flames in air. The experiments of 19254 revealed that a quasi-steady flame could be attained for extended duration at low flow rate, i.e., 3cc/s for 20% C2H4 / 80% N2. The flame was shut down by the automated system because the burner reached a cut-off temperature of 250°C.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p43,1,"Although the flame oscillated near the end of the experiment, it was likely due to influence of the burner tube. Comparisons with computation simulations will help to clarify the involved processes. For higher flow rates, e.g., 5-10cc/s, with the same mixture, radiative extinctions were observed. Generally, the flames showed unstable behavior near the burner tube prior to extinction, likely because of lack of fuel mixture and heat loss in that region. Other mixtures, e.g., 25% C2H4 / 75% N2 and 30% C2H4 / 70% N2, also showed radiative extinctions at higher flow rates. For some cases, the pyrometry fiber interacted with the flame, allowing extraction of temperature in on-going analyses. Soot was observed only during ignition, which went away as the flame established itself. To asses whether these nitrogen-diluted ethylene flames are non-sooty or sooty, cases were run where mixtures were ignited and established at low flow rates (e.g., 3 cc/s) with low ethylene contents (e.g., 25-30% C2H4) and then ramped to high flow rates (e.g., 5-10 cc/s) with higher ethylene contents (e.g., 40-50% C2H4). The flames all radiatively extinguished, but no sooting was observed. Observations of such radiative extinction behavior at high flow rates is an achievement because such phenomenon was not observed in our ground-based experiments, given the limited microgravity durations. Radiative reabsorption extends flame size at extinction.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p43,2,"In ISS testing, radiative extinction has been found to be a common event, which was predicted computationally, but not observed previously for our flame conditions. The experiments of 19256 were aimed at examining kinetic extinction. Similar to the radiative extinction cases, sufficient microgravity duration is needed to observe kinetic extinction because a near steady flame needs to be established first, before ramp down",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p44,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 44 in flow rate to find properly the chemical kinetic extinction limit. High flow rate cases (e.g., 13.5 cc/s, 15 cc/s) showed asymmetrical flames which radiatively extinguished. Flow rates were lowered so that quasi-steady flames could be established before ramp down in flow rate. The 15% C2H4 mixtures were not strong enough to sustain a quasi- steady flame at 3 cc/s before ramp down. However, for stronger mixtures, i.e., 20-30% C2H4, very nice flames were established at 3 cc/s; and kinetic extinctions were observed during ramp down before reaching 1 cc/s. Radiative extinctions for ramp up cases were also examined. Similarly, for stronger mixtures, i.e., 20-30% C2H4, very nice flames were established at 3 cc/s; and radiative extinctions were observed during ramp up before reaching 8 cc/s. Future tests will investigate soot formation processes, which have not been observed for the fuel mixtures and conditions tested thus far. Early ISS results were presented in the following presentations: 1. Chung K. Law and Stephen Tse, “ACME - s-Flame,” ISS Increments 61 & 62 Science Symposium, 17-19 Sept. 2019. (oral presentation) 2. Stephen D. Tse, Jonathan Shi, Delin Zhu, and Chung K. Law, “Dynamics of Burner- Generated Spherical Diffusion Flames In Microgravity,” 35th Annual Meeting of the American Society for Gravitational and Space Research, Denver, CO, 20-23 Nov. 2019. (poster presentation)",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p44,1,"F. Communication Imagery 1. Still Imagery Photos are available online at www.flickr.com/photos/space-flames. Although the Facebook page at www.facebook.com/space.flames is meant to provide updates on ACME and related combustion research, it is good source for recent photos, e.g., of flames, that may not be available on the Flickr site. Additional photographs may be requested from the Project Scientist, Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov). 2. Video ‘The Power of Studying Combustion on the ISS,’ which was developed with input from the ACME Project Scientist, is available at https://science.nasa.gov/science- news/news-articles/the-power-of-studying-combustion-on-the-iss. Video(s) describing the ACME project can be found at https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/acme/. Videos related to s-Flame and other microgravity combustion research can also be seen on Facebook at www.facebook.com/space.flames. Contact Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov) for access to those videos or for additional videos.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p45,0,ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 45 IV. Additional Comments from science team None.,,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p46,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 46 Appendix C. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increments 61 and 62 11 June 2020 30-day Operational Accomplishments Report … for the International Space Station (ISS) Increments 61 and 62 ACME Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments s-Flame Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames The s-Flame investigation is one of several independent experiments that are part of the ACME project, which uses a single modular set of hardware to carry out studies of flames burning gaseous fuels in the ISS’ Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). The s-Flame testing began at the end of Increment 60 and continued through Increment 61, where progress was limited by both technical issues and limited crew availability. Because of the latter, no tests were conducted during Increment 62. Prepared by the PI team and Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov), the ACME Project Scientist, who is the primary contact for s-Flame and ACME information. Principal Investigator (PI): Prof. C.K. (Ed) Law, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ Co-Investigator (Co-I): Prof. Stephen D. Tse, Rutgers University, Piscataway, NJ Lead collaborator: Dr. Vladimir V. Gubernov, P.N. Lebedev Physical Institute of the Russian Academy of Sciences, Moscow, Russia NASA Project Scientist (PS): Dennis P. Stocker, NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, OH",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p46,1,"I. Introduction Tests for the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment were conducted in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on the International Space Station, as part of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project. The purpose of s-Flame is to advance the ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction and instabilities, of both soot-free and sooty flames. The results may contribute to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions here on Earth. The spherical flames are ignited at non-steady conditions and allowed to transition naturally towards steady-state or extinction. Tests are conducted with various inert diluents in both the fuel and chamber (oxidizer) atmosphere. The fuel gases include hydrogen and methane mixtures for soot-free flames, and ethylene for sooty flames. One experimental objective is to identify the extinction limits for both radiative and chemical kinetic extinction, i.e., where the flame",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p47,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 47 extinguishes from radiative heat loss or is “blown out,” respectively. Another objective is to determine the existence, onset, and nature of pulsating instabilities that have been theoretically predicted to occur in flames with certain fuel/inert mixtures at near-limit conditions. More specifically, s-Flame has the following experimental objectives: A. Measure and characterize the transient structure (such as temperature and species distribution) of diffusion flames established at initially non-steady, spherical flame structures for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty), for different flow rates. B. Determine the quasi-steady convective/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (low system Damköhler numbers) and radiative/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (high system Damköhler numbers) for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty). B. Determine the existence (for different Zeldovich number, Ze), onset (as a function Da), and nature (i.e. mode, frequency, amplitude) of pulsating instabilities theoretically predicted to occur in transient spherical diffusion flames using fuel/diluent mixtures that are above a critical Lewis number, at near-limit conditions.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p47,1,"As suggested by the experiment’s name, s-Flame features a spherically symmetric diffusion flame, which is generated by discharging a fuel gas from a porous spherical burner into a still oxygen/inert atmosphere in microgravity. The one-dimensional nature of the system facilitates data acquisition, data reduction, and computational simulation. The space flight program consists of two main parts: (1) Mapping and characterization of the motion (e.g., expansion) of spherical diffusion flames subsequent to ignition and at extinction because of the finite-rate chemistry and radiative heat loss. The study is focused on the thermal radiation, the adequacy of the detailed chemistry and transport submodels used in computational modeling, and the development of flame front instabilities that could modify the extinction boundaries. (2) Studies of the response of characteristically sooty flames to ignition, the onset of soot in the expanding flames, the soot radiation, and the soot’s effects on the flame spread, extinction, and instability.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p47,2,"Microgravity studies of flames allow for a range of simplifications and provide various advantages over normal-gravity studies. For example, the natural flicker of flames, which is a buoyancy- driven (i.e., gravity-driven) instability, is eliminated yielding quasi-steady flames in microgravity. Characteristic length scales are increased in microgravity flames facilitating analysis of the flame structure. Momentum-dominated flames, which are important for most practical combustion systems, can be studied at low velocities to simplify analysis. Microgravity flames tend to have a much stronger sensitivity to their atmosphere and exhibit a much broader range of characteristics than normal-gravity flames because of the near absence of buoyant entrainment. The long residence times in microgravity flames can lead to strong soot production, but many microgravity flames are soot free. The absence of buoyancy makes microgravity flames advantageous for studies of limit and stability behavior where chemical kinetics are important. For example, chemi-ion species, electronically-excited chemiluminescent species, are at very low",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p48,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 48 concentration in typical flames, making the reduced buoyancy environment valuable for evaluating the minor species chemistry. With these advantages, microgravity testing can lead to new understanding of the complex nature of combustion – regardless of the gravitational environment of the combustion application. II. Methods/Research Operations A. Discussion of Method/Protocol The s-Flame investigation and other ACME experiments are configured, but not conducted, by the ISS crew. The crew’s role is to set up the hardware, gas bottles, etc. for each set of tests. The most common changes include the gas bottles on the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) and the mass flow controllers on the modular ACME chamber insert. All testing is commanded from the ground, specifically from the Glenn ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC). The science portion of the testing is typically conducted in a nominally automated mode using pre-programmed scripts. However, it may alternately be conducted in a ‘manual’ mode, where changes are made to a control parameter in response to the analog video downlink and the numerical data available in the telemetry stream. But it is not expected that such control, which might be used to approach a stability or soot limit, will be used for s-Flame. Thus far, that has been the case for this experiment, with the possible mundane exception of ‘manually’ halting the test after the flame has extinguished.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p48,1,"Testing is not conducted when thrusters fire or spacecraft dock or undock from ISS to ensure an acceptable acceleration environment. Data is downlinked between each set of tests, i.e., day of testing, to allow for preliminary analysis and requisite planning for subsequent testing. III. Results A. Baselined Activities Completed – Pre-flight, In-flight, Post-Flight Thus far, a total of 145 flames were ignited for the s-Flame experiment, with 29 and 116 respectively ignited during Increment 60 and 61. In this regard, science testing began at the end of Increment 60 and the first round of tests were completed during Increment 61. No tests were conducted in Increment 62 due to a lack of crew time. The s-Flame experiment does not have living subjects like a life science experiment, but the tests are independently conducted with various gaseous fuels, including hydrogen, methane, hydrogen/methane blends, and ethylene. These are simple hydrocarbon gases for which the chemistry is relatively well understood. The Increment 60 tests were all",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p49,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 49 conducted with ethylene (C2H4), which is typically highly sooting because of its carbon double bond (under normal gravity conditions), but where the fuel was diluted with nitrogen for these tests. No samples (e.g., of soot) were collected for s-Flame; all of the ISS results were electronically transmitted to the ground. That is furthermore the case for all currently planned ACME experiments. A summary log of the flames ignited for s-Flame thus far, including a limited overview of the test conditions, is found in Table 1. The first two test days occurred at the end of Increment 60. Table 1. s-Flame log of flames ignited thus far, i.e., in Increments 60-62 Test Day1 Year Month Date Atmosphere2 Chamber Pressure Fuel bottle Flames ignited2 First round of tests, with nitrogen-diluted fuel (in Increments 60-61) 19254 2019 9 11 ~21% O2, with N2 ~1 atm 50% C2H4 with N2 13 19256 13 16 19304 10 31 45% CH4 with N2 9 19308 11 4 ~30% O2, with N2 21 19352 12 18 ~21% O2, with N2 100% H2 10 20006 2020 1 6 44.4% H2 with CH4 17 20013 13 66.7% H2 with CH4 22 20024 24 100% H2 16 20029 29 50% C2H4 with N2 21 1Test Day includes the year as the first two digits followed by the 3-digit day of the year. 2Flames ignited is the number ignited on that day (neglecting the day change on ISS). Pre-flight and post-flight activities are generally not addressed in this report.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p49,1,"B. List of Preflight, In-flight, Postflight Anomalies 1. Mission anomalies The only significant anomaly to occur during this increment pair was the mis- formatting of image data from ACME’s primary camera on Dec. 18 (19352). The cause is unknown, but there were repeated operational issues with the CIR’s Image Processing and Storage Units (IPSUs) on that test day. Fortunately, the mis-formatting was not seen on subsequent test days and the problematic test day was nominally repeated on Jan. 24 (20024).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p50,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 50 However, work was carried out during Increment 61 to address an anomaly that occurred near the end of Increment 60. During testing on Sept. 13 (19256), the CIR’s IPSU-G Image Processing and Storage Unit for Gigabit Ethernet cameras, which is used with the ACME Data Camera (DataCam) and Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) intensified camera, didn’t properly archive all of the image data on Sept. 13 (19256). A large fraction of the image data was lost. The issue wasn’t resolved by remote trouble shooting and the IPSU-G was ultimately removed and replaced by astronaut Jessica Meir on October 24 (19297). Subsequent check-out on Oct. 28 (19301) confirmed normal archival of the image data, where regular testing resumed on Oct. 31 (19304). 2. Any other difficulties in obtaining scientific data Neglecting the anomalies, the most noteworthy challenge was in imaging the hydrogen and hydrogen-diluted flames, which were not apparent or hardly visible to the operations camera and ACME DataCam, where the latter is the primary camera for ACME testing. The testing with the extremely dim flames was limited to that conducted on Dec. 18 (19352) and Jan. 24 (20024), where the testing on the latter day was to make up for the earlier image mis-formatting discussed in the preceding section. This situation unfortunately inhibits assessment of the three-dimensional flame symmetry as the two intensified cameras are opposite of each other.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p50,1,"During Increment 61, several s-Flame tests automatically aborted (due to the controlling software script), where that was prior to gas flow and the activation of the igniter – which is typical for other ACME experiments. As a result, there was little lost other than the time in the failed test attempt. ACME aborts normally occur because of communication issues with an IPSU, and that happened for tests 19304A5, 19352D3, 19352P1, 20024N1, and 20024J1. A few other tests aborted because flow rates were accidentally specified outside of the range of the installed mass flow controller(s), where that included 19352A1, 19352D2, and 20013C2. ACME aborts are infrequent, but there are sometimes days when they occur repeatedly as can be seen here.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p50,2,"Tests are also unsuccessful when the flame fails to ignite, where that occurred during Increment 61 in tests 19304A4, 19304A6, 19308C1, and 20006A1. That can be corrected by adjusting various test parameters and other settings. In this case, adjustments were subsequently made to the ignition position (e.g., after 19304A4 and 19304A6), the relative timing of the fuel flow and igniter activation (e.g., after 19308C1), and the duration of the igniter activation (e.g., after 20006A1). As another example, failed ignition can result from false ignition detection and deactivation resulting from the combination of settings for ignition and detection, e.g., the gain for the Photomultiplier Tube (PMT) module measuring the OH* chemiluminescence. The latter, in the ultraviolet at 310 nm, is a good indicator of an ignited flame rather than the glowing igniter, which emits primarily in the infrared and visible spectrum.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p51,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 51 At high flow rates, the burner does not produce spherically symmetric flames. While nominally spherical at moderate flow rates, the flame’s center becomes increasing offset from the burner center. More specifically, the flame is shifted toward the supply tube indicating that the sintered attachment of the porous sphere to the supply tube is more ‘open’ than the pores of the spherical burner. In other words, the flow from the burner is not spherically symmetric. At sufficiently high flow rates, the flame becomes obviously non-spherical, where it can appear to be ellipsoidal or even parabolic in shape. In the latter case, the flame locally quenches near the supply tube. When at a small fraction of the maximum flow rate, Mass Flow Controller (MFC) performance has been seen to be poor during s-Flame testing, i.e., where actual flow rate differed from that desired. However, this was not a surprise to those familiar with the hardware.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p51,1,"Flow perturbations have been noticed in some s-Flame tests, where ACME’s MFCs used to control the flow of fuel, nitrogen, and oxidizer have unfortunately not always performed satisfactorily. In tests conducted using the 1-slpm fuel MFC (where the flow range is on a nitrogen basis), the flow has been seen to overshoot and undershoot when first opening. Small flow irregularities have been most apparent through their effect on the Photomultiplier Tube (PMT) measurements, e.g., of OH* and CH* chemiluminescence. A momentary increase in fuel flow, as has occurred in some s-Flame tests, naturally leads to increased light from the flame. The MFCs are tested - without flames - after they are first installed. Adjustments are typically made to (1) the setting that controls when the valves open and (2) the Proportional-Integral- Derivative (PID) settings. This ACME activity has involved significant testing, where there are an assortment of MFCs with different flow ranges for each of the three gas types. While the MFC adjustments have been beneficial, they have not eliminated all gas flow problems. It was seen in prior ACME testing that nitrogen oscillations (if any) tend to damp with time, so a nitrogen pre-flow can be used so that such oscillations might end before the flame is ignited. As another measure, the MFCs are re-zeroed prior to each test, where that was another measure adopted in earlier ACME testing.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p51,2,"C. Completeness/Quality of Data During Increments 60 and 61, nine days of tests were conducted during which 145 flames were ignited for the first round of testing. In contrast, only 29 tests were specified as required or highly desired in the ACME Integrated Science Requirements Document (ISRD) for that round. Therefore, a wealth of data was obtained for this round of testing which featured gaseous fuels diluted by nitrogen. However, there was a significant loss of data. Most importantly, the first fuel bottle appeared to leak on installation (in Increment 60) and later on-orbit testing (in Increment 61) with other hydrogen/methane blends suggests that its fuel mixture would have been ideal for achieving science success. Furthermore, there was significant image data loss on two different test days, specifically Sept. 13 (19256) and Dec. 18 (19352). As a result,",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p52,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 52 those test days were nominally repeated on Jan. 29 (20029) and Jan. 24 (20024), respectively (i.e., in a reversed order). For these reasons, a simple comparison of the total number of flames ignited (145) with that specified as required or highly desired (29) for the first round of testing is somewhat misleading. As discussed in the preceding section, the hydrogen and diluted-hydrogen flames were not apparent or extremely dim in imaging from the operations camera and ACME DataCam. Neglecting that factor, the cameras are sensitive to radiation where pixels gradually become ‘hot’ (i.e., dead) over time. While these bright spots are addressed in image post-processing, they are a degradation of the image quality. It is possible that the tests are subtly affected by the acceleration environment, but there is not obvious indication of this; and it has thus far not been evaluated with available measurements from the Space Acceleration Measurement System (SAMS).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p52,1,"D. Status of Data Analysis [provided by Prof. Stephen Tse, Rutgers U.] Image files are currently being processed. Radial positions of maximum CH* chemiluminescence is being plotted against time. Different radii are being considered and plotted together, as some flames are not completely symmetric. MFC and PMT data are being compared with video images to assess and confirm experimental settings. Radiometer data is being reviewed. Analyses of the fiber pyrometry data has not begun. Computational simulations with detailed chemistry and transport of the test cases are underway. Time-dependent simulations will be compared with the time-dependent experimental data. Steady-state simulations will produce the Isola curves mapping flame response versus system Damkhöler number for given fuel mixtures. The experimental cases will then be pin-pointed on the computational mappings to assess whether the experimental conditions are in quasi-steady state or near extinction limits, either kinetic or radiative. Oscillation behavior will be correlated to possible pulsating oscillations found in the simulations. E. Preliminary Research Findings [provided by Prof. Tse and lightly edited by Stocker] The s-Flame experiment utilizes spherically-symmetric diffusion flames and their dynamics and responses to investigate fundamental processes, such as extinction, instability, and soot formation.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p52,2,"The tests conducted on 19254 (near the end of Increment 60) revealed that a quasi-steady flame could be attained for extended duration at low flow rate, i.e., 3 cc/s for 20% C2H4/80% N2 (on a volume basis). The flame was shut down by the automated system because the burner reached a cut-off temperature of 250°C. Although the flame oscillated near the end of the test, it was likely due to influence of the burner tube. Comparisons with computation simulations will help to clarify the involved processes. For higher flow rates, e.g., 5-10 cc/s, with the same mixture, radiative extinctions were",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p53,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 53 observed. Generally, the flames showed unstable behavior near the burner tube prior to extinction, likely because of lack of fuel mixture and heat loss in that region. Other mixtures, e.g., 25% C2H4/75% N2 and 30% C2H4/70% N2, also showed radiative extinctions at higher flow rates. For some cases, the pyrometry fiber interacted with the flame, allowing determination of temperature in on-going analyses. Soot was observed only during ignition, which went away as the flame established itself. To assess whether these nitrogen-diluted ethylene flames are non-sooty or sooty, cases were run where mixtures were ignited and established at low flow rates (e.g., 3 cc/s) with low ethylene contents (e.g., 25-30% C2H4) and then ramped to high flow rates (e.g., 5-10 cc/s) with higher ethylene contents (e.g., 40-50% C2H4). The flames all radiatively extinguished, but no sooting was observed. Observations of such radiative extinction behavior at high flow rates is an achievement because such phenomenon was not observed in our ground- based studies, given the limited microgravity durations. Radiative reabsorption extends flame size at extinction. In ISS testing, radiative extinction has been found to be a common event, which was predicted computationally, but not observed previously for our flame conditions.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p53,1,"The 19256 tests (in Increment 60) were aimed at examining kinetic extinction. Similar to the radiative extinction cases, sufficient microgravity duration is needed to observe kinetic extinction because a near steady flame needs to be established first, before ramp down in flow rate to find properly the chemical kinetic extinction limit. High flow rate cases (e.g., 13.5 cc/s, 15 cc/s) showed asymmetrical flames which radiatively extinguished. Flow rates were lowered so that quasi-steady flames could be established before ramp down in flow rate. The 15% C2H4 mixtures were not strong enough to sustain a quasi-steady flame at 3 cc/s before ramp down. However, for stronger mixtures, i.e., 20-30% C2H4, very nice flames were established at 3 cc/s; and kinetic extinctions were observed during ramp down before reaching 1 cc/s. Radiative extinctions for ramp up cases were also examined. Similarly, for stronger mixtures, i.e., 20-30% C2H4, very nice flames were established at 3 cc/s; and radiative extinctions were observed during ramp up before reaching 8 cc/s.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p53,2,"Testing on 19304 investigated 45% CH4/55% N2 mixtures issuing into an air atmosphere. The results indicated that the flow rates specified by the fuel MFC (0.5 slpm) were in the regime of kinetic extinction. Quasi-steady flames could not be established. Furthermore, larger flow rates could not be provided by the MFC, so observations of radiative extinction could not be achieved. Even with a larger capacity MFC, characteristically larger flames would be expected to be asymmetric. As such, nitrogen-diluted methane flames were planned to be reexamined in increased oxygen atmosphere, i.e., 30% O2/70% N2. Consequently, the 19308 tests investigated nitrogen-diluted methane flames in 30% O2/70% N2 atmosphere. Various methane concentrations from 45% down to 15% were examined. For 45% CH4/55% N2, relatively long duration flames of ~15 s and ~10 s were established at flow rates of 2 cc/s and 3 cc/s, respectively. At larger flow rates of 5 and 9.4 cc/s, the flames expanded to large radii and then extinguished. These flames",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p54,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 54 appeared to demonstrate radiative extinction. Reduction of flow rate from 2 cc/s to 1 cc/s resulted in extinction during ramp down, indicating kinetic extinction. For 30% CH4/70% N2, flow rates of 3, 5, and 10 cc/s produced flames that expanded to extinction (assumed radiatively); while reduction of flow rate from 3 to 1 cc/s resulted in presumably kinetic extinction during ramp down. For 25% CH4/75% N2, flow rates of 5 and 10 cc/s yielded flames that expanded to extinction. For 20% CH4/80% N2, constant flow rate of 10 cc/s created a flame that expanded until extinction (assumed radiatively); while reduction of flow rate from 10 cc/s to 1 cc/s resulted in wavering extinction during ramp down, suggesting kinetic extinction. For 15% CH4/85% N2, flames were ignited, but extinguished very quickly. These tests of 19308 suggest that they could manifest both radiative and kinetic extinction for the conditions specified.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p54,1,"The 19352 tests examined various concentrations of H2 (45%-70%) diluted with N2 issuing into air. The installed fuel MFC had a maximum output of 0.1 slpm, which unfortunately provided a less-than-sufficient flow range where its use may have precluded the study of radiative extinction corresponding to larger flame sizes. Imaging the diluted hydrogen flames was a challenge, especially since the hobj image files from the ACME DataCam were mis-formatted. Furthermore, little could be seen in the analog ops imaging, e.g., where it, like the DataCam and Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) intensified camera, cannot image the ultraviolet OH* flame emission (at 310 nm). Analysis of images from the Low Light Level Ultraviolet (LLL-UV) camera and its OH* filter is ongoing. And as one might expect, little could be seen with the CFI intensified camera using its broadband CH2O filter. Quasi-steady flames seem to be present at low flow rates (e.g., 2.4 cc/s for 70% H2), as inferred from PMT, pyrometry fiber emission, and burner temperature observations. Kinetic extinction seems to occur as the flow rate is ramped down. There were various problems with the IPSU and some tests aborted. Using the 100% H2 bottle with a higher flow rate MFC was later performed for tests of 20024 (see below), which was a make-up test day added because of the mis-formatting of the DataCam image files.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p54,2,"The tests conducted 20006, using the 44.4% H2/55.6% CH4 bottle, examined 20% H2/25% CH4/55% N2 mixtures issuing into air. Flames extinguished, some with wavy oscillating behavior prior to extinction. However, it seems that even for very small flow rates, e.g., 2 cc/s, extinction was by the radiative mechanism during flame expansion. There was too little parameter room in terms of flow rate and burner size to identify conclusively kinetic extinction during ramp down for these mixtures. In short, radiative extinction (with local flame extinction and re-ignition seemed to occur prior to ultimate extinguishment) during flame expansion after ignition was observed. However, it is unclear whether ramping down the flow rate resulted in kinetic extinction. Detailed analyses of the data are needed. The various cameras seemed to capture the tests well. The 20013 tests, which used the 66.7% H2/33.3% CH4 bottle, mainly investigated 30% H2/15% CH4/55% N2 mixtures issuing into air. Radiative extinction was difficult to identify conclusively because flames with larger flow rates, e.g. 15 cc/s, seemed to last, although with patchy extinction areas, until time out. Larger flow rates could not be examined",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p55,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 55 either because of limited MFC range or distorted flame shape cause by burner limitations at high flow rates. Also, kinetic extinction during ramp down could not be identified conclusively, as some flames seemed to extinguish because they were ""going into the burner."" The 20024 tests using the 100% H2 bottle repeated and augmented conditions for those of 19352 where the 0.1-slpm fuel MFC range was too low to examine radiative extinction and the analog and intensified cameras did not pick up the flame images at all or well. Here, the 0.5-slpm fuel MFC was used and only a fused silica filter was used for the Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) intensified camera. 45%-15% H2/N2 balance mixtures were investigated. The flames at high flow rates, e.g., 10 cc/s, did not extinguish, appearing quasi-steady until time out. The pyrometry fibers glowed, perhaps making good flame- structure measurements, which will be evaluated subsequently. The flames seemed to extinguish during ramp down, but further analysis is needed to assess kinetic extinction. The tests conducted on 20029 used 50% C2H4/50% N2 and repeated and augmented conditions of 19256, when the images were not good. Again, the results indicated both kinetic and radiative extinction phenomena under corresponding conditions. However, no sooting flames were established, necessitating 100% C2H4 and an enriched oxygen atmosphere to facilitate sooting behavior in possible future tests.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p55,1,"In summary, selected preliminary findings from this testing include: o Cases run for nitrogen-diluted methane and ethylene flames and in air seemed to indicate kinetic extinction at low flow rates and radiative extinction at high flow rates. As previously mentioned above (and in our report for the preceding increment pair), such phenomena were not observed in our ground-based research, given the limited microgravity durations. Nevertheless, comparisons with computational simulations are needed to assess better the extinction behavior. o Cases run for nitrogen-diluted hydrogen flames seemed to show kinetic extinction at ramp down. However, for the mass flow rates that could be accommodated, radiative extinction was not observed. o Cases run for nitrogen-diluted hydrogen/methane mixture flames manifested extinction, which are presumed to be radiative. However, kinetic extinction at low flow rates could not be identified. More analyses of the data are needed. Early ISS results were presented in the following presentations: 1. Chung K. Law and Stephen Tse, “ACME - s-Flame,” ISS Increments 61 & 62 Science Symposium, 17-19 Sept. 2019. (oral presentation) 2. Stephen D. Tse, Jonathan Shi, Delin Zhu, and Chung K. Law, “Dynamics of Burner- Generated Spherical Diffusion Flames In Microgravity,” 35th Annual Meeting of the American Society for Gravitational and Space Research, Denver, CO, 20-23 Nov. 2019. (poster presentation)",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p56,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 56 F. Communication Imagery 1. Still Imagery Photos are available online at www.flickr.com/photos/space-flames. Although the Facebook page at www.facebook.com/space.flames is meant to provide updates on ACME and related combustion research, it is good source for recent photos, e.g., of flames, that may not be available on the Flickr site. Additional photographs may be requested from the Project Scientist, Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov). 2. Video ‘The Power of Studying Combustion on the ISS,’ which was developed with input from the ACME Project Scientist, is available at https://science.nasa.gov/science- news/news-articles/the-power-of-studying-combustion-on-the-iss. Video(s) describing the ACME project can be found at https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/acme/. Videos related to s-Flame and other microgravity combustion research can also be seen on Facebook at www.facebook.com/space.flames. Contact Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov) for access to those videos or for additional videos. IV. Additional Comments from science team None.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p57,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 57 Appendix D. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increment 63 16 Dec. 2020 30-day Operational Accomplishments Report … for the International Space Station (ISS) Increment 63 ACME Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments s-Flame Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames The s-Flame investigation is one of several independent experiments that are part of the ACME project, which uses a single modular set of hardware to carry out studies of flames burning gaseous fuels in the ISS’ Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). The first round of s-Flame tests was conducted during Increments 60 and 61, while the second round was conducted fully within Increment 63. There were no tests conducted in Increment 62 because of limited crew availability. Prepared by the PI team and Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov), the ACME Project Scientist, who is the primary contact for s-Flame and ACME information. Principal Investigator (PI): Prof. C.K. (Ed) Law, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ Co-Investigator (Co-I): Prof. Stephen D. Tse, Rutgers University, Piscataway, NJ Lead collaborator: Dr. Vladimir V. Gubernov, P.N. Lebedev Physical Institute of the Russian Academy of Sciences, Moscow, Russia NASA Project Scientist (PS): Dennis P. Stocker, NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, OH",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p57,1,"I. Introduction Tests for the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment were conducted in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on the International Space Station, as part of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project. The purpose of s-Flame is to advance the ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction and instabilities, of both soot-free and sooty flames. The results may contribute to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions here on Earth. The spherical flames are ignited at non-steady conditions and allowed to transition naturally towards steady-state or extinction. Tests are conducted with various inert diluents in both the fuel and chamber (oxidizer) atmosphere. The fuel gases include hydrogen and methane mixtures for soot-free flames, and ethylene for sooty flames. One experimental objective is to identify the extinction limits for both radiative and chemical kinetic extinction, i.e., where the flame extinguishes from radiative heat loss or is “blown out,” respectively. Another objective is to determine the existence, onset, and nature of pulsating instabilities that have been theoretically predicted to occur in flames with certain fuel/inert mixtures at near-limit conditions.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p58,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 58 More specifically, s-Flame has the following experimental objectives: A. Measure and characterize the transient structure (such as temperature and species distribution) of diffusion flames established at initially non-steady, spherical flame structures for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty), for different flow rates. B. Determine the quasi-steady convective/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (low system Damköhler numbers) and radiative/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (high system Damköhler numbers) for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty). C. Determine the existence (for different Zeldovich number, Ze), onset (as a function Da), and nature (i.e. mode, frequency, amplitude) of pulsating instabilities theoretically predicted to occur in transient spherical diffusion flames using fuel/diluent mixtures that are above a critical Lewis number, at near-limit conditions. As suggested by the experiment’s name, s-Flame features a spherically symmetric diffusion flame, which is generated by discharging a fuel gas from a porous spherical burner into a still oxygen/inert atmosphere in microgravity. The one-dimensional nature of the system facilitates data acquisition, data reduction, and computational simulation.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p58,1,"The space flight program consists of two main parts: (1) Mapping and characterization of the motion (e.g., expansion) of spherical diffusion flames subsequent to ignition and at extinction because of the finite-rate chemistry and radiative heat loss. The study is focused on the thermal radiation, the adequacy of the detailed chemistry and transport submodels used in computational modeling, and the development of flame front instabilities that could modify the extinction boundaries. (2) Studies of the response of characteristically sooty flames to ignition, the onset of soot in the expanding flames, the soot radiation, and the soot’s effects on the flame spread, extinction, and instability.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p58,2,"Microgravity studies of flames allow for a range of simplifications and provide various advantages over normal-gravity studies. For example, the natural flicker of flames, which is a buoyancy- driven (i.e., gravity-driven) instability, is eliminated yielding quasi-steady flames in microgravity. Characteristic length scales are increased in microgravity flames facilitating analysis of the flame structure. Momentum-dominated flames, which are important for most practical combustion systems, can be studied at low velocities to simplify analysis. Microgravity flames tend to have a much stronger sensitivity to their atmosphere and exhibit a much broader range of characteristics than normal-gravity flames because of the near absence of buoyant entrainment. The long residence times in microgravity flames can lead to strong soot production, but many microgravity flames are soot free. The absence of buoyancy makes microgravity flames advantageous for studies of limit and stability behavior where chemical kinetics are important. For example, chemi-ion species, electronically-excited chemiluminescent species, are at very low concentration in typical flames, making the reduced buoyancy environment valuable for evaluating the minor species chemistry. With these advantages, microgravity testing can lead to",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p59,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 59 new understanding of the complex nature of combustion – regardless of the gravitational environment of the combustion application. II. Methods/Research Operations A. Discussion of Method/Protocol The s-Flame investigation and other ACME experiments are configured, but not conducted, by the ISS crew. The crew’s role is to set up the hardware, gas bottles, etc. for each set of tests. The most common changes include the gas bottles on the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) and the mass flow controllers on the modular ACME chamber insert. All testing is commanded from the ground, specifically from the Glenn ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC). The science portion of the tests were all conducted in a nominally automated mode using pre-programmed scripts. As expected, no s-Flame tests were conducted in a ‘manual’ mode, where changes are made to a control parameter in response to the analog video downlink and the numerical data available in the telemetry stream. Thus far, that has been the case for all ACME tests, with the mundane exception of ‘manually’ halting the test after the flame has extinguished or perhaps if a temperature has reached a critical value. Testing is not conducted when thrusters fire or spacecraft dock or undock from ISS to ensure an acceptable acceleration environment.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p59,1,"Data is downlinked between each set of tests, i.e., day of testing, to allow for preliminary analysis and requisite planning for subsequent testing. III. Results A. Baselined Activities Completed – Pre-flight, In-flight, Post-Flight A total of 276 flames were ignited for the s-Flame experiment, with 29, 116, and 131 respectively ignited during Increments 60, 61, and 63. These numbers neglect some flames which quenched promptly after ignition. In this regard, science testing began at the end of Increment 60 and the first round of tests were completed during Increment 61. No tests were conducted in Increment 62 due to a lack of crew time, where the second round of tests were fully conducted during Increment 63. The tests were conducted in two rounds, where the first was conducted in increments 60 and 61 featured 145 flames (nominally) in which the fuel and oxygen (i.e., in the chamber atmosphere) were diluted with nitrogen. The second round conducted in increment 63 featured 131 flames (nominally) in which the fuel and oxygen were instead diluted with helium.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p60,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 60 The s-Flame experiment does not have living subjects like a life science experiment, but the tests are independently conducted with various gaseous fuels, including hydrogen, methane, hydrogen/methane blends, and ethylene. These are simple hydrocarbon gases for which the chemistry is relatively well understood. No samples (e.g., of soot) were collected for s-Flame; all of the ISS results were electronically transmitted to the ground. That is furthermore the case for all currently planned ACME experiments. A summary log of the flames ignited for s-Flame, including a limited overview of the test conditions, is found in Table 1.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p60,1,"Table 1. Flames ignited for the s-Flame experiment Test Day1 Year Month Date Atmosphere2 Fuel bottle Flames ignited3 First round of tests, with nitrogen-diluted fuel and oxygen 19254 2019 9 11 ~21% O2 with N2 50% C2H4 with N2 13 19256 13 16 19304 10 31 45% CH4 with N2 9 19308 11 4 ~30% O2 with N2 21 19352 12 18 ~21% O2, with N2 100% H2 10 20006 2020 1 6 44.4% H2 with CH4 17 20013 13 66.7% H2 with CH4 22 20024 24 100% H2 16 20029 29 50% C2H4 with N2 21 Second round of tests, with helium-diluted fuel and oxygen 20132 2020 5 11 ~21% O2 with He 25% H2, 20% CH4, with He 7 20136 15 20% H2, 25% CH4, with He 11 20142 21 ~30% O2 with He 30% H2, 15% CH4, with He 17 20155 6 3 ~30% and ~21% O2 with He 45% H2 with He 9 20160 8 45% CH4 with He 12 20164 12 ~30% O2 with He 20% C2H4 with He 11 20170 18 ~30% and ~21% O2 with He 25% C2H4 with He 20 20176 24 30% C2H4 with He 23 20190 7 8 45% H2 with He 21 1Test Day includes the year as the first two digits followed by the 3-digit day of the year. 2Atmosphere is ~1 atm but pressure increases and O2% drops with vitiation. 3Flames ignited is the number of nominally sustained flames ignited on that day (neglecting the day change on ISS).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p61,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 61 Pre-flight and post-flight activities are generally not addressed in this report. B. List of Preflight, In-flight, Postflight Anomalies 1. Mission anomalies While there were certainly issues with testing discussed in the next section, there were no significant anomalies during s-Flame’s Increment 63 testing. However, anomalies did occur during Increment 60 and 61 which led to corrective actions and/or make-up testing.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p61,1,"2. Any other difficulties in obtaining scientific data Ignition of the flames presented a significant challenge to the experiment during this increment. All of the flame tests were conducted where the fuel and chamber atmosphere were both diluted with helium rather than the nitrogen used in Increments 60 and 61. The two gases’ properties are dramatically different making comparison testing very desirable for manifestation of different phenomena. Helium’s thermal conductivity is notably several times greater than that of nitrogen, so considerably more energy and power was required to ignite the flames. The power level was slowly increased from test to test until ignition was finally achieved on the ninth test, where only seven flames were ignited of the 15 tests conducted on May 11 (20132), the first day for flames in Increment 63. The cautious approach was taken because of concerns that the igniter tip might fail (i.e., burn out) if too much power was applied. An igniter failure would have likely halted testing for weeks given the need to schedule the commander’s time to replace the igniter tip.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p61,2,"After that first test day, the decision was made to have the igniter replaced with a coil rather than loop design which was believed to be more robust because of the thicker wire. Furthermore, the igniter tip had not been replaced in months, and the tips do degrade with time, especially when they are not quickly retracted from the ignited flames. The coil igniter was used successfully but still with caution, where flames were not always ignited successfully. Subsequently, some tests were conducted in atmosphere with ~30% oxygen (by volume) instead of the planned ~21%. As one would expect, that proved to be helpful for achieving ignition. But the end result was that some tests were conducted with 21% oxygen, some at 30%, and some at both nominal concentrations. Fortuitously, this mixture of conditions may provide deeper insight into the mechanisms of flame dynamics and response. Tests are also unsuccessful when the flame fails to ignite, where that occurred, for example, during Increment 61 in tests 19304A4, 19304A6, 19308C1, and 20006A1. That can be corrected by adjusting various test parameters and other settings. In this case, adjustments were subsequently made to the ignition position (e.g., after 19304A4 and 19304A6), the relative timing of the fuel flow and igniter activation (e.g., after 19308C1), and the duration of the igniter activation (e.g., after 20006A1). As another example, failed ignition can result from false ignition detection and",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p62,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 62 deactivation resulting from the combination of settings for ignition and detection, e.g., the gain for the Photomultiplier Tube (PMT) module measuring the OH* chemiluminescence. The latter, in the ultraviolet at 310 nm, is a good indicator of an ignited flame rather than the glowing igniter, which emits primarily in the infrared and visible spectrum.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p62,1,"Testing on June 3 (20155) was cut short due to a combination of issues. The final test was aborted due to an error with an Image Processing and Storage Unit (IPSU). Consoles needed to be reset for the new increment, and there was an issue with the Fuel Oxidizer Management Assembly (FOMA) Control Unit, i.e. the FCU. Rebooting the FCU inadvertently set the valve safety timers to zero, which cannot be reset without venting and refilling the combustion chamber with the intended atmosphere. Those issues combined with an extended loss of commanding prevented further science operations on that day. Therefore, an additional test day (July 8, i.e., 20190) was added to make up for lost science, e.g., where only one test was successfully conducted at ~21% oxygen on June 3. The FCU was off nominal because of a high carrier gas pressure in the Gas Chromatograph Instrumentation Package (GCIP). Imaging of the hydrogen flames was often an issue, where they were not apparent or hardly visible to the operations camera and ACME DataCam, where the latter is the primary camera for ACME testing. The testing with the extremely dim flames was limited to that conducted on Dec. 18 (19352), Jan. 24 (20024), June 3 (20155), and July 8 (20190). This shortcoming unfortunately inhibits assessment of the three-dimensional flame symmetry as the two intensified cameras were opposite of each other.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p62,2,"The flow ranges of the selected Mass Flow Controllers (MFCs) was found to be a challenge, where it was discovered that the goal of studying both kinetic and radiative extinction was not always satisfactorily achieved because of flow rate constraints. At high flow rates, the burner does not produce spherically symmetric flames. While nominally spherical at moderate flow rates, the flame’s center becomes increasing offset from the burner center. More specifically, the flame is shifted toward the supply tube indicating that the sintered attachment of the porous sphere to the supply tube is more ‘open’ than the pores of the spherical burner. In other words, the flow from the burner is not spherically symmetric. At sufficiently high flow rates, the flame becomes obviously non-spherical, where it can appear to be ellipsoidal or even parabolic in shape. In the latter case, the flame locally quenches near the supply tube. When at a small fraction of the maximum flow rate, Mass Flow Controller (MFC) performance has been seen to be poor during s-Flame testing, i.e., where actual flow rate differed from that desired. However, this was not a surprise to those familiar with the hardware. Flow perturbations have been noticed in some s-Flame tests, where ACME’s MFCs used to control the flow of fuel, nitrogen, and oxidizer have unfortunately not always",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p63,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 63 performed satisfactorily. In tests conducted using the 1-slpm fuel MFC (where the flow range is on a nitrogen basis), the flow has been seen to overshoot and undershoot when first opening. Small flow irregularities have been most apparent through their effect on the Photomultiplier Tube (PMT) measurements, e.g., of OH* and CH* chemiluminescence. A momentary increase in fuel flow, as has occurred in some s-Flame tests, naturally leads to increased light from the flame. The MFCs are tested - without flames - after they are first installed. Adjustments are typically made to (1) the setting that controls when the valves open and (2) the Proportional-Integral- Derivative (PID) settings. This ACME activity has involved significant testing, where there is an assortment of MFCs with different flow ranges for each of the three gas types. While the MFC adjustments have been beneficial, they have not eliminated all gas flow problems. It was seen in prior ACME testing that nitrogen oscillations (if any) tend to damp with time, so a nitrogen pre-flow can be used so that such oscillations might end before the flame is ignited. As another measure, the MFCs are re-zeroed prior to each test, where that was another measure adopted in earlier ACME testing.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p63,1,"As mentioned previously, s-Flame tests have sometimes automatically aborted (because of the controlling software script), where that is normally prior to gas flow and the activation of the igniter – which is typical for other ACME experiments. As a result, there was little lost other than the available experimental time in the failed test attempt. ACME aborts normally occur because of communication issues with an IPSU, which for example happened for tests 19304A5, 19352D3, 19352P1, 20024N1, and 20024J1. A few other tests aborted because flow rates were accidentally specified outside of the range of the installed mass flow controller(s), where that included for example 19352A1, 19352D2, and 20013C2. ACME aborts are infrequent, but there are sometimes days when they occur repeatedly as can be seen here. C. Completeness/Quality of Data During Increments 60 and 61, nine days of tests were conducted during which 145 flames were ignited for the first round of testing. In contrast, only 29 tests were specified as required or highly desired in the ACME Integrated Science Requirements Document (ISRD) for that round. Therefore, a wealth of data was obtained for this round of testing which featured gaseous fuels diluted by nitrogen.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p63,2,"However, there was a significant loss of data. Most importantly, the first fuel bottle appeared to leak on installation (in Increment 60) and later on-orbit testing (in Increment 61) with other hydrogen/methane blends suggests that its fuel mixture would have been ideal for achieving science success. Furthermore, there was significant image data loss on two different test days, specifically Sept. 13 (19256) and Dec. 18 (19352). As a result, those test days were nominally repeated on Jan. 29 (20029) and Jan. 24 (20024), respectively (i.e., in a reversed order). For these reasons, a simple comparison of the total number of flames ignited (145) with that specified as required or highly desired (29) for the first round of testing is somewhat misleading.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p64,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 64 As discussed in the preceding section, the hydrogen and diluted-hydrogen flames were not apparent or extremely dim in imaging from the operations camera and ACME DataCam. Neglecting that factor, the cameras are sensitive to radiation where pixels gradually become ‘hot’ (i.e., dead) over time. While these bright spots are addressed in image post-processing, they are a degradation of the image quality. It is possible that the tests are subtly affected by the acceleration environment, but there is not obvious indication of this; and it has thus far not been evaluated with available measurements from the Space Acceleration Measurement System (SAMS). D. Status of Data Analysis [provided by Prof. Stephen Tse, Rutgers U.] Image files are currently being processed. Radial positions of maximum CH* chemiluminescence is being plotted against time. Different radii are being considered and plotted together, as some flames are not completely symmetric. MFC and PMT data are being compared with video images to assess and confirm experimental settings. Radiometer data is being reviewed. Analyses of the fiber pyrometry data are ongoing. MATLAB code is used to isolate fiber images, which are then color assessed for temperature.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p64,1,"Computational simulations with detailed chemistry and transport of the test cases are underway. Time-dependent simulations will be compared with the time-dependent experimental data, where tracking of the flamefront location is based on chemiluminescent emission. Steady-state simulations will produce the Isola curves mapping flame response versus system Damkhöler number for given fuel mixtures. The experimental cases will then be pin-pointed on the computational mappings to assess whether the experimental conditions are in quasi-steady state or near extinction limits, either kinetic or radiative. Oscillation behavior will be correlated to possible pulsating oscillations found in the simulations. E. Preliminary Research Findings [provided by Prof. Tse and lightly edited by Stocker] The s-Flame experiment utilizes spherically-symmetric diffusion flames and their dynamics and responses to investigate fundamental processes, such as extinction, instability, and soot formation.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p64,2,"The tests conducted on 19254 (near the end of Increment 60) revealed that a quasi-steady flame could be attained for extended duration at low flow rate, i.e., 3 cc/s for 20% C2H4/80% N2 (on a volume basis). The flame was shut down by the automated system because the burner reached a cut-off temperature of 250°C. Although the flame oscillated near the end of the test, it was likely due to influence of the burner tube. Comparisons with computation simulations will help to clarify the involved processes. For higher flow rates, e.g., 5-10 cc/s, with the same mixture, radiative extinctions were observed. Generally, the flames showed unstable behavior near the burner tube prior to extinction, likely because of lack of fuel mixture and heat loss in that region. Other",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p65,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 65 mixtures, e.g., 25% C2H4/75% N2 and 30% C2H4/70% N2, also showed radiative extinctions at higher flow rates. For some cases, the pyrometry fiber interacted with the flame, allowing determination of temperature in on-going analyses. Soot was observed only during ignition, which went away as the flame established itself. To assess whether these nitrogen-diluted ethylene flames are non-sooty or sooty, cases were run where mixtures were ignited and established at low flow rates (e.g., 3 cc/s) with low ethylene contents (e.g., 25-30% C2H4) and then ramped to high flow rates (e.g., 5-10 cc/s) with higher ethylene contents (e.g., 40-50% C2H4). The flames all radiatively extinguished, but no sooting was observed. Observations of such radiative extinction behavior at high flow rates is an achievement because such phenomenon was not observed in our ground- based studies, given the limited microgravity durations. Radiative reabsorption extends flame size at extinction. In ISS testing, radiative extinction has been found to be a common event, which was predicted computationally, but not observed previously for our flame conditions.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p65,1,"The 19256 tests (in Increment 60) were aimed at examining kinetic extinction. Similar to the radiative extinction cases, sufficient microgravity duration is needed to observe kinetic extinction because a near steady flame needs to be established first, before ramp down in flow rate to find properly the chemical kinetic extinction limit. High flow rate cases (e.g., 13.5 cc/s, 15 cc/s) showed asymmetrical flames which radiatively extinguished. Flow rates were lowered so that quasi-steady flames could be established before ramp down in flow rate. The 15% C2H4 mixtures were not strong enough to sustain a quasi-steady flame at 3 cc/s before ramp down. However, for stronger mixtures, i.e., 20-30% C2H4, very nice flames were established at 3 cc/s; and kinetic extinctions were observed during ramp down before reaching 1 cc/s. Radiative extinctions for ramp up cases were also examined. Similarly, for stronger mixtures, i.e., 20-30% C2H4, very nice flames were established at 3 cc/s; and radiative extinctions were observed during ramp up before reaching 8 cc/s.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p65,2,"Testing on 19304 investigated 45% CH4/55% N2 mixtures issuing into an air atmosphere. The results indicated that the flow rates specified by the fuel MFC (0.5 slpm) were in the regime of kinetic extinction. Quasi-steady flames could not be established. Furthermore, larger flow rates could not be provided by the MFC, so observations of radiative extinction could not be achieved. Even with a larger capacity MFC, characteristically larger flames would be expected to be asymmetric. As such, nitrogen-diluted methane flames were planned to be reexamined in increased oxygen atmosphere, i.e., 30% O2/70% N2. Consequently, the 19308 tests investigated nitrogen-diluted methane flames in 30% O2/70% N2 atmosphere. Various methane concentrations from 45% down to 15% were examined. For 45% CH4/55% N2, relatively long duration flames of ~15 s and ~10 s were established at flow rates of 2 cc/s and 3 cc/s, respectively. At larger flow rates of 5 and 9.4 cc/s, the flames expanded to large radii and then extinguished. These flames appeared to demonstrate radiative extinction. Reduction of flow rate from 2 cc/s to 1 cc/s resulted in extinction during ramp down, indicating kinetic extinction. For 30%",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p66,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 66 CH4/70% N2, flow rates of 3, 5, and 10 cc/s produced flames that expanded to extinction (assumed radiatively); while reduction of flow rate from 3 to 1 cc/s resulted in presumably kinetic extinction during ramp down. For 25% CH4/75% N2, flow rates of 5 and 10 cc/s yielded flames that expanded to extinction. For 20% CH4/80% N2, constant flow rate of 10 cc/s created a flame that expanded until extinction (assumed radiatively); while reduction of flow rate from 10 cc/s to 1 cc/s resulted in wavering extinction during ramp down, suggesting kinetic extinction. For 15% CH4/85% N2, flames were ignited, but extinguished very quickly. These tests of 19308 suggest that they could manifest both radiative and kinetic extinction for the conditions specified.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p66,1,"The 19352 tests examined various concentrations of H2 (45%-70%) diluted with N2 issuing into air. The installed fuel MFC had a maximum output of 0.1 slpm, which unfortunately provided a less-than-sufficient flow range where its use may have precluded the study of radiative extinction corresponding to larger flame sizes. Imaging the diluted hydrogen flames was a challenge, especially since the hobj image files from the ACME DataCam were mis-formatted. Furthermore, little could be seen in the analog ops imaging, e.g., where it, like the DataCam and Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) intensified camera, cannot image the ultraviolet OH* flame emission (at 310 nm). Analysis of images from the Low Light Level Ultraviolet (LLL-UV) camera and its OH* filter is ongoing. And as one might expect, little could be seen with the CFI intensified camera using its broadband CH2O filter. Quasi-steady flames seem to be present at low flow rates (e.g., 2.4 cc/s for 70% H2), as inferred from PMT, pyrometry fiber emission, and burner temperature observations. Kinetic extinction seems to occur as the flow rate is ramped down. There were various problems with the IPSU and some tests aborted. Using the 100% H2 bottle with a higher flow rate MFC was later performed for tests of 20024 (see below), which was a make-up test day added because of the mis-formatting of the DataCam image files.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p66,2,"The tests conducted on 20006, using the 44.4% H2/55.6% CH4 bottle, examined 20% H2/25% CH4/55% N2 mixtures issuing into air. Flames extinguished, some with wavy oscillating behavior prior to extinction. However, it seems that even for very small flow rates, e.g., 2 cc/s, extinction was by the radiative mechanism during flame expansion. There was too little parameter room in terms of flow rate and burner size to identify conclusively kinetic extinction during ramp down for these mixtures. In short, radiative extinction (with local flame extinction and re-ignition seemed to occur prior to ultimate extinguishment) during flame expansion after ignition was observed. However, it is unclear whether ramping down the flow rate resulted in kinetic extinction. Detailed analyses of the data are needed. The various cameras seemed to capture the tests well. The 20013 tests, which used the 66.7% H2/33.3% CH4 bottle, mainly investigated 30% H2/15% CH4/55% N2 mixtures issuing into air. Radiative extinction was difficult to identify conclusively because flames with larger flow rates, e.g. 15 cc/s, seemed to last, although with patchy extinction areas, until time out. Larger flow rates could not be examined either because of limited MFC range or distorted flame shape cause by burner limitations at high flow rates. Also, kinetic extinction during ramp down could not be identified",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p67,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 67 conclusively, as some flames seemed to extinguish because they were ""going into the burner."" The 20024 tests using the 100% H2 bottle repeated and augmented conditions for those of 19352 where the 0.1-slpm fuel MFC range was too low to examine radiative extinction and the analog and intensified cameras did not pick up the flame images at all or well. Here, the 0.5-slpm fuel MFC was used and only a fused silica filter was used for the Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) intensified camera. 45%-15% H2/N2 balance mixtures were investigated. The flames at high flow rates, e.g., 10 cc/s, did not extinguish, appearing quasi-steady until time out. The pyrometry fibers glowed, perhaps making good flame- structure measurements, which will be evaluated subsequently. The flames seemed to extinguish during ramp down, but further analysis is needed to assess kinetic extinction. The tests conducted on 20029 used 50% C2H4/50% N2 and repeated and augmented conditions of 19256, when the images were not good. Again, the results indicated both kinetic and radiative extinction phenomena under corresponding conditions. However, no sooting flames were established, necessitating 100% C2H4 and an enriched oxygen atmosphere to facilitate sooting behavior in possible future tests.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p67,1,"The 20132 tests kicked off a round of tests with helium-diluted fuel and oxidizer. The ambient was 21% O2 and 79% He. The fuel was composed of 25% H2, 20% CH4, and 55% He. Using He as inert made igniting the mixture much more difficult compared to that for the N2 inert cases of Round 1, using the same ignition parameters. The reason is believed to be due to the 6X higher value in thermal conductivity of He versus that of N2. As a result, ignition parameters were varied, e.g., with a longer preheat time and higher power, to achieve ignition for the He-diluted mixtures and ambient. The tests produced flames at 3, 5, 7, and 10 cc/s that expanded outwardly quickly and extinguished. For 5, 7, and 10 cc/s, the flames seemed to disappear and 're-ignite' with a ""flash"" before final extinction. We are currently investigating the flash phenomenon. Moreover, the radiation mechanism is not conclusive, as it appears different than for the N2-diluted cases. We did not have a chance to examine lowering of flow rates to assess kinetic extinction.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p67,2,"The 20136 tests examined 20% H2/25% CH4/55% He issuing into 21% O2/He. For 5 cc/s and 7 cc/s, the mixture ignites but immediately extinguishes, with almost no observable flame-front expansion. For 10, 13, 15, and 17.5 cc/s, the mixtures ignite, expand quickly, and extinguish. We believe that the extinction mechanism is radiative and will compare with computational simulations. For the cases with a ramp down in flow rate, the flames seemed to extinguish before ramp down. Thus, we were not able to assess purely kinetic extinction. Ignition parameters were varied to investigate thresholds for igniting the mixture for different flow rates. Only flow rates above 7 cc/s showed flame-front expansion leading to extinction, with 17.5 cc/s showing a flash or wavering of the flame before final extinction.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p68,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 68 The tests conducted on 20142 investigated 30% H2/15% CH4/55% He issuing into 30% O2/He. Elevated oxygen was approved for study, since ignition and extinction limits in previous testing were affected by the high thermal conductivity of He. For 5, 7, 10, 15, 13, and 16.5 cc/s flow rates, nice flames were established that expanded to wavy/oscillating extinction at the end, indicative of radiative extinction. For the cases with a ramp down in flow rate, the flames seemed to extinguish during the ignition flow rate stage before ramp down. Thus, we were not able to assess kinetic extinction.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p68,1,"The 20155 tests examined 45% H2/55% He issuing into 30% O2/He and 21% O2/He. Previous tests using He as inert had shown that much larger energy and power are needed to ignite the mixtures. For the 30% O2/H2 atmosphere, the tests produced flames at 10 and 14 cc/s (MFC limit of 1 SLMP) that expanded after ignition, but were extinguished by the automated protocol because the burner reached the 250°C temperature limit before any self-extinction could occur. The flames were not too large at the end. Presumably, the thermally-conductive He diluent facilitates heat transfer to the burner. For the cases with a ramp down in flow rate, i.e., starting at 10 and 14 cc/s and ramping down, the flames seemed to self-extinguish during ramp down. Flow rate at 5 cc/s results in flames that only existed briefly. Extinction at this lower flow rate is probably due to heat loss to the burner, rather than pure kinetic extinction. Ramp down from 14 cc/s towards 5 cc/s resulted in the burner reaching the 250°C limit during ramp down. More analyses are needed. Tests at 21% O2/He atmosphere were limited. Fuel flow of 5 cc/s showed flash ignition, but no sustained flame. Meanwhile, 10 cc/s resulted in a flame that seemed to expand to self-extinction.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p68,2,"Testing on 20160 investigated 45% CH4/55% He issuing into 30% O2/He and 21% O2/He. Without H2 in the mixture, even larger energy and power were needed to ignite the mixtures. For the 30% O2/H2 atmosphere, the 5, 10, and 15 cc/s flow rates were sooty only at ignition; and the subsequent non-sooty flames expanded to self-extinction. At 3 and 4 cc/s, the mixtures flashed at ignition, and extinguished almost immediately. It was not clear whether extinction was due to kinetic extinction or heat loss to the burner. At 6 and 7 cc/s, flames ignited with less soot, expanding with no soot to self-extinction. The case with a ramp down in flow rate could not conclusively identify kinetic extinction. More analysis is needed. Tests at 21% O2/He atmosphere needed even more energy and power for ignition. At 5, 7, 10, and 15 cc/s, flames ignited with soot, and expanded quickly to extinction.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p68,3,"The 20164 tests began study of ethylene-based flames, where 20% C2H4/80% He issued into 30% O2/He and 21% O2/He atmospheres. For 5 cc/s, the flame expanded and remained quasi-steady until the burner limit temperature (250°C) was reached. At 6, 7, 10, and 15 cc/s, the flames expanded to extinction, with the higher flow rates (i.e., 10 and 15 cc/s) exhibiting sooty ignition. Fuel flow of 5.5 cc/s seemed to correspond to a condition where the onset of oscillating extinction was simultaneous with the burner reaching the 250°C temperature limit. For the cases with a ramp down in flow rate, the flames seemed to extinguish during ramp down, but heat loss to the burner may be the",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p69,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 69 mechanism of extinction. Although 13 tests were conducted with the 21% O2/He atmosphere, ignition was not achieved. The tests performed on20170 investigated 25% C2H4/75% He issuing into 30% O2/He and 21% O2/He. At 5 and 5.5 cc/s, nice blue chemiluminescent flames were ignited, but eventually were cut off because of the burner temperature limit being reached. At 6 cc/s, a nice blue flame was ignited which expanded slowly to extinction. At 7, 10, and 15 cc/s, sooty ignition occurred, but as the flames expanded, the soot went away, eventually resulting in radiative extinction. By ramping the flow rates up, starting at 5 cc/s, soot at ignition was minimized, and the flames could expand to extinction. Ramp down of the flow rate resulted in almost immediate extinction. For tests at 21% O2/He atmosphere, cases at 5, 10, and 15 cc/s resulted in flames that expanded quickly to extinction. Less soot was present at ignition compared with that for the 30% O2/He atmosphere cases. Consistent ignition was not always achieved for the same conditions, which is puzzling.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p69,1,"Testing on 20176 explored 30% C2H4/70% He issuing into 30% O2/He and 21% O2/He. At 5 cc/s, a long duration flame was established that eventually reached the burner temperature limit. At 6, 7, 10, and 15 cc/s, the flames were sooty at ignition, but the soot dissipated; and they expanded to extinction. Ramping up the flow rates, starting at 5 cc/s, minimized soot at ignition and resulted in flames that slowly expanded to extinction. For tests at 21% O2/He atmosphere, cases at 5, 10, and 15 cc/s resulted in flames that expanded to extinction. At 4 cc/s, the flame lasted a while before eventual extinction. The 3 cc/s case did not ignite. Ramp down of the flow rate seemed to result in flames that extinguished quickly before the protocol could ramp down the flow rate.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p69,2,"The last testing of Round 2, which was a makeup of 20155, occurred on 20190, with 45% H2/55% He issuing into 30% O2/He and 21% O2/He. For both oxygen concentration environments, flow rates resulting in expanding flames lasted for a while, but were cut off by automated protocol before self-extinction when the burner temperature limit was reached. As for cases where the flow rates were ramped down, extinction detection was observed for both oxygen concentration environments. We are currently analyzing the data to determine if extinction was kinetic or due to heat loss to the burner. A last set of tests were conducted with a reduced oxygen concentration atmosphere, i.e., 17.5% O2/82.5% He. Some cases indicated that radiative and kinetic extinction were possibly observed. In summary, selected preliminary findings from this testing include: o Cases run for nitrogen-diluted methane and ethylene flames and in air seemed to indicate kinetic extinction at low flow rates and radiative extinction at high flow rates. As previously mentioned above (and in our report for the preceding increment pair), such phenomena were not observed in our ground-based research, given the limited microgravity durations. Nevertheless, comparisons with computational simulations are needed to assess better the extinction behavior.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p70,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 70 o Cases run for nitrogen-diluted hydrogen flames seemed to show kinetic extinction at ramp down. However, for the mass flow rates that could be accommodated, radiative extinction was not observed. o Cases run for nitrogen-diluted hydrogen/methane mixture flames manifested extinction, which are presumed to be radiative. However, kinetic extinction at low flow rates could not be identified. More analyses of the data are needed. o Cases for helium-diluted cases shifted the extinction limits with respect to the nitrogen-diluted cases. In general, kinetic extinction was difficult to observe for the conditions examined. More analyses are needed to assess instability near the flame limits, which was predicted to occur computationally.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p70,1,"Early ISS results were presented in the following presentations: 1. Chung K. Law and Stephen Tse, “ACME - s-Flame,” ISS Increments 61 & 62 Science Symposium, 17-19 Sept. 2019. (oral presentation) 2. Stephen D. Tse, Jonathan Shi, Delin Zhu, and Chung K. Law, “Dynamics of Burner- Generated Spherical Diffusion Flames In Microgravity,” 35th Annual Meeting of the American Society for Gravitational and Space Research, Denver, CO, 20-23 Nov. 2019. (poster presentation) 3. Jonathan Shi, Stephen D. Tse, Dennis Stocker, and Chung K. Law, “ISS Experiments of Gaseous Spherical Diffusion Flames,” 36th Annual Meeting of the American Society for Gravitational and Space Research, 5-6 Nov. 2020. (pre-recorded oral presentation) F. Communication Imagery 1. Still Imagery Photos are available online at www.flickr.com/photos/space-flames. Although the Facebook page at www.facebook.com/space.flames is meant to provide updates on ACME and related combustion research, it is good source for recent photos, e.g., of flames, that may not be available on the Flickr site. Additional photographs may be requested from the Project Scientist, Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov). 2. Video ‘The Power of Studying Combustion on the ISS,’ which was developed with input from the ACME Project Scientist, is available at https://science.nasa.gov/science- news/news-articles/the-power-of-studying-combustion-on-the-iss.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p70,2,Video(s) describing the ACME project can be found at https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/acme/. Videos related to s-Flame and other microgravity combustion research can also be seen on Facebook at www.facebook.com/space.flames.,,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p71,0,ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 71 Contact Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov) for access to those videos or for additional videos. IV. Additional Comments from science team Product references are included for clarification and do not indicate an endorsement by NASA or the federal government.,,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p72,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 72 Appendix E. Operational Timeline This summary timeline of s-Flame operations was prepared by Dennis Stocker, where the red text indicates the key days when flames were ignited. The dates for crew activities were based on plans, where it is possible that the dates for some simple tasks, e.g., hardware gather or stowage, may have shifted slightly. Date GMT Activity s-Flame round 1, featuring nitrogen dilution 2019 July 30 211 Bottle swaps by Koch July 31 212 Bottle trouble shooting by Hague Aug. 7 219 Bottle swap by Parmitano Aug. 13 225 Hardware gather, chamber insert reconfigure, and window cover removal for s-Flame by Koch Aug. 14 226 Hardware stow by Koch Aug. 15 227 Hardware check-out, etc. in preparation for s-Flame Sept. 9 252 Bottle swap by Koch Sept. 11 254 s-Flame tests using 50% C2H4 with N2 Sept. 13 256 s-Flame tests using 50% C2H4 with N2 Sept. 23 266 Swap of bottles and fuel Mass Flow Controller by Parmitano Sept. 25 268 MFC testing and tuning, launch of Skripochka, Meir, and Mansouri Sept. 27 270 IPSU-G diagnostic testing Oct. 2-3 275-276 IPSU-G diagnostic testing Oct. 24 297 IPSU-G replacement by Meir Oct. 28 301 IPSU-G checkout Oct. 31 304 s-Flame tests using 45% CH4 with N2 Nov. 4 308 s-Flame tests using 45% CH4 with N2 Nov. 18 322 GC sampling with check gas, file maintenance Nov. 19 323 Bottle swaps by Morgan Dec. 13 347 Swap of fuel and nitrogen Mass Flow Controllers by Morgan Dec.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p72,1,"18 352 s-Flame tests using 100% H2 Dec. 24 358 Swap of fuel and nitrogen Mass Flow Controllers by Parmitano 2020 Jan. 2 002 Bottle swap by Skripochka (with Parmitano’s assistance) Jan. 6 006 s-Flame tests using 44.4% H2 with CH4 Jan. 8 008 Bottle swap by Skripochka Jan. 13 013 s-Flame tests using 66.7% H2 with CH4 Jan. 17 017 Bottle swaps by Skripochka Jan. 24 024 s-Flame tests using 100% H2 Jan. 28 028 Bottle swap by Skripochka Jan. 29 029 s-Flame tests using 50% C2H4 with N2 s-Flame round 2, featuring helium dilution April 9 100 Launch of Cassidy, Ivanishin, and Vagner April 23 114 Swap of FOMA calibration unit by Cassidy April 24 115 FOMA calibration by Cassidy April 28 119 Swap of fuel Mass Flow Controller and setting of valve timers by Cassidy April 29 120 MFC testing & tuning",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p73,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 73 May 1 122 Bottle swap by Ivanishin (with Cassidy’s assistance) May 11 132 s-Flame tests using 25% H2 / 20% CH4, with He May 13 134 Bottle swaps by Ivanishin May 15 136 s-Flame tests using 20% H2 / 25% CH4, with He May 19 140 Bottle swaps by Ivanishin May 21 142 s-Flame tests using 30% H2 / 15% CH4, with He May 26 147 Bottle swaps by Ivanishin June 2 154 Swap of igniter tip (to coil type) and fuel Mass Flow Controller by Cassidy, hardware checkout June 3 155 s-Flame tests using 45% H2 with He June 4 156 Bottle swap by Ivanishin June 8 160 s-Flame tests using 45% CH4 with He June 10 162 Bottle swap by Ivanishin June 12 164 s-Flame tests using 20% C2H4 with He June 16 168 Bottle swaps by Ivanishin June 18 170 s-Flame tests using 25% C2H4 with He June 19 171 Bottle swap by Ivanishin June 24 176 s-Flame tests using 30% C2H4 with He July 6 188 Bottle swaps by Ivanishin July 8 190 s-Flame tests using 45% H2 with He",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p74,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 74 Appendix F. Astronauts and Cosmonauts who Conducted ISS Operations for s-Flame This list was prepared by Dennis Stocker from his log of ACME operations. Eight cosmonauts and astronauts participated in ISS operations for this investigation, where they are listed with the dates of their activity (at least as planned). They are listed by the number of days in which they conducted supporting on-orbit activities and alphabetically. The crew activity on each of those dates is very briefly summarized in the Appendix D. Additional information about the crew activity can be found in some of the subsequent appendices. Anatoly Ivanishin (RSA) 2020 122, 134, 140, 147, 156, 162, 168, 171, 188 9 days Chris Cassidy (NASA) 2020 114-115, 119, 122 (with Ivanishin), 154 5 days Christina Koch (NASA) 2019 211, 225-226, 252 4 days Oleg Skripochka (RSA) 2020 002, 008, 017, 028 4 days Luca Parmitano (ESA) 2019 219, 266, 358 3 days 2020 002 (with Skripochka) 1 day Drew Morgan (NASA) 2019 323, 347 2 days Nick Hague (NASA) 2019 212 1 day Jessica Meir (NASA) 2020 297 1 day Together, the 6 men and 2 women represented three space agencies and three nations, namely Italy, Russia, and the United States.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p75,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 75 Appendix G. ISS Operations per the Payload Weekly Science Summary This section is from Dennis Stocker’s log of ACME operations. s-Flame round 1, featuring nitrogen dilution 2019 Week 21: July 26 – August 1 On GMT 206, we performed the final test points for the first round of testing for ACME Flame Design. Test points were performed using ethylene fuel (sometimes diluted) in a chamber environment of air (21% oxygen, 79% nitrogen at 1 atm). The flames for these tests were short-lived and non-sooty—unlike the flames from the tests at higher oxygen concentrations. The goal of Flame Design is to improve our understanding of soot processes so that flames can be “designed” to be soot free. To prepare for the start of ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames), Christina Koch replaced the bottles on CIR Manifolds #2 and #4 on GMT 211. While monitoring the bottle pressures, it was noted that the pressure of the bottle at Manifold #4 was slowly decreasing. The rack was powered down, and Nick Hague and Luca Parmitano both helped with troubleshooting operations. Currently, a forward plan is being developed.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p75,1,"Week 22: August 2 - 8 On GMT 219, Luca Parmitano replaced the CIR Manifold #4 bottle, which was losing pressure while installed and the valve opened, with a known good bottle. While monitoring, the known good bottle maintained its pressure! Next week, we plan to continue transitioning to the next ACME experiment, s- Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames). Week 23: August 9 - 15 On GMT 225, Christina Koch configured the ACME Chamber Insert and the CIR Combustion Chamber to support the next ACME experiment, s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames). Week 27: September 6 - 12 On GMT 254, the first test points were performed for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames) with 13 spherical flames ignited. This investigation is the fifth to be conducted as part of the ACME project, which features studies of burner-stabilized flames of gaseous fuels. s-Flame’s goal is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction, of both soot-free and sooty flames—where today’s testing was focused on soot-free flames. Without the buoyant flows created by Earth’s gravity, flames in microgravity are typically spherical, which allows the analysis to focus on the combustion chemistry and details of the transport phenomena.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p75,2,"CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG In this test point video, a non-sooty (blue) flame is ignited with a retracting hot-wire igniter, where the gaseous fuel is flowing from a porous spherical burner. After ignition, the flame grows slightly as a result of the diminishing oxygen gradient. An array of 14-micron ceramic fibers is then moved toward the flame, where the temperature of the flame can be determined from the color of the fiber’s glow—where yellow is hotter than orange, and orange is hotter than red. While only a single fiber can be seen at first, a second becomes visible when the flame suddenly gets bigger",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p76,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 76 and engulfs the first fiber. The diluted ethylene flow to the burner was abruptly increased and made to be more fuel rich. As a result, the flame needed to grow to reach enough oxygen to burn all the fuel. Throughout the test, the spherical flame is open near the burner’s tube because of heat loss. As the flame grows, the opening around the tube increases (while the flame weakens and dims). The flame edge then pulls away from the burner tube and leaves just a small portion of the spherical shell. This large hole in the flame shell allows for fuel and air mixing and the flame momentarily reestablishes and brightens before it extinguishes. Week 28: September 13 - 19 On GMT 256, 16 spherical flames were successfully ignited for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames). Even though ethylene’s double bond tends to make it a sooty fuel, these nitrogen-diluted ethylene flames were all non-sooty (except for during ignition). For each test point, the harp-like array of 14-micron ceramic fibers was used to determine temperatures in or near the flame, while an array of thermocouples was used to measure the spread of the thermal field from the flame. ACME Flame Design Images",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p76,1,"These two images are of the same flame at nearly the same time. The image on the left is a false-colored image. The porous spherical burner can be seen in the middle of the flame. The flame shell surrounds the burner except at the fuel supply tube, which causes the flame to locally quench because of heat loss. A glowing ceramic fiber can be seen above the flame, where the fiber’s glow is used to determine the local temperature by comparison of the red, green, and blue color planes in a measurement technique known as pyrometry. The faint lines at the top of the flame suggest that the fiber may have locally quenched the flame even though it was above the flame. The image on the right is from an intensified camera looking at the emission from the excited molecule CH*, which emits a photon of blue 431-nm light when it drops to a lower energy state in a process referred to as chemiluminescence. The location of the burner sphere is noticeable due to the diminished light in the middle of the flame. The opening in the flame shell due to the burner’s tube is also noticeable. The",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p77,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 77 apparent opening in the top of the flame may be caused by the fiber array arm, which, in this camera’s view, blocks the flame. In microgravity, flames aren’t supplied oxygen through buoyant convention. As a result, low-momentum flames like these in a quiescent (still) atmosphere tend to be quite weak and self-extinguish. Microgravity is an extremely useful for environment for this experiment since a key objective is to study flame dynamics and extinction. Week 29: September 20 - 26 On GMT 266, Luca Parmitano replaced the ACME Fuel Controller and the bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4. These changes support the next ACME s-Flame test points, which will be in a chamber environment of air (21% oxygen, 79% nitrogen at 1 atm) and will use diluted methane fuel at faster flow rates. Week 5: October 25 - 31 On GMT 297 on the back of the CIR Optics Bench, Jessica Meir removed a failed hard drive from the FCF Image Processor and then replaced the FCF Image Processor. On GMT 301, the CIR was powered up and a checkout of the new FCF Image Processor was successful. We returned to performing test points later in the week!",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p77,1,"Week 6: November 1 - 7 On GMT 304 and 308, 30 spherical flames were successfully ignited for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames). These tests burned methane fuel diluted with nitrogen in atmospheres of 21% and 30% oxygen (with a balance of nitrogen). Methane is the primary component of natural gas. These test points focused on determining where the radiative heat loss or velocities are too high for the flame to be sustained, which are known as the radiative and convective extinction limits. Radiative heat transport is much more important under microgravity conditions because the hot gases don’t buoyantly rise from the flame. ACME s-Flame Images This figure shows three flames of 45% methane burning in 30% oxygen. The gaseous methane fuel flows from the porous sphere of the burner, which is visible in the flame’s center. The flow of fuel increases from left to right, which increases the size of the flame. The burner’s fuel supply tube is visible below the sphere. The flame loses heat to this tube causing an opening, which typically grows with time as the flame weakens. In the flame on the right, the opening is comparatively small, which signifies a stronger flame.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p78,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 78 In the left and center images, the glow of a 14-micron ceramic fiber is visible above the flame. Where the fiber is visibly glowing, the flame’s temperature at that point can be determined via color-ratio pyrometry, which uses a color camera characterized for that purpose. The faint glow of a second fiber further from the flame can be seen at the top of the center image. When the image on the right was captured, the fiber array was not yet inserted into the flame. Week 8: November 15 - 21 On GMT 323, Drew Morgan replaced the bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4 to support ACME s-Flame test points using pure hydrogen fuel in air (21% oxygen, 79% nitrogen at 1 atm). Week 12: December 13 - 19 On GMT 347, Drew Morgan replaced the ACME Bypass and Fuel Controllers to support the ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames) test points on GMT 352, which flowed nitrogen and fuel at slower flow rates.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p78,1,"Ten flames were successfully ignited on GMT 352. These tests were conducted with nitrogen-diluted hydrogen fuel making the flames very faint. The CIR is equipped with an intensified camera, which images these very faint flames by visualizing the ultraviolet emission of the OH molecule (at 310 nm) as it drops to a lower energy level. This process is known as chemiluminescence. Thus far, there has only been a preliminary review of some image data. These tests used hydrogen fuel in differing concentrations with both steady and ramped flow rates. The tests with ramped flow rates investigated extinction characteristics of the flame. Week 14: December 27 - January 2 On GMT 358, Luca Parmitano replaced the ACME Bypass and Fuel Controllers to support ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames) test points with faster nitrogen and fuel flow rates. On GMT 002, Oleg Skripochka and Luca Parmitano replaced the bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support ACME s-Flame test points using a hydrogen/methane fuel mixture in air (21% oxygen, 79% nitrogen at 1 atm).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p78,2,"2020 Week 15: January 3 - 9 On GMT 006, 17 flames were successfully ignited for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames), which uses spherically-symmetric diffusion flames to investigate fundamental processes, such as flame extinction. These tests were conducted with a mixture of hydrogen (20%), methane (25%), and nitrogen (55%) ejected from the porous spherical burner into a chamber atmosphere of air (21% oxygen, 79% nitrogen at 1 atm). The dim, soot-free flames exhibited local quenching due to radiative heat loss and reignition, which sometimes occurred repeatedly. In these cases, the dynamic parts of the broken flame sometimes appear to dance around the burner. CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG In this test point video, a non-sooty (blue) flame is ignited with a retracting hot-wire igniter. The harp-like array of 14-micron ceramic fibers moves in from the left to a fixed position. The glow from the fibers allows for a good estimation of the local temperature. The flame starts “dancing,” where it partially quenches due to radiative heat loss and the remaining flame stretches and circles around the burner through a repeated combination of quenching and reignition. The fuel",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p79,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 79 and oxygen, which are normally separated by the flame, mix where the flame quenches, making the local gas more flammable. Week 16: January 10 - 16 On GMT 013, 22 flames were successfully ignited for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames), which is a record number of sustained flames in a single day of testing for all the ACME experiments! Like the tests performed on GMT 006, these tests burned a mixture of hydrogen, methane, and nitrogen but with a greater hydrogen concentration relative to the methane concentration. Even though these flames were less dynamic than the “dancing” flames from GMT 006, they did break apart and create flamelets. The flamelets were about 1 cm across and were shaped like curved bands or circles rather than spheres or ellipsoids. This figure shows four different tests from GMT 013, where the flow rate of the hydrogen/methane fuel increases from left to right while the nitrogen flow rate remains constant. The burner, with its supply tube and porous sphere, is opposite the flame. The fuel and nitrogen both flow from the burner sphere into a chamber atmosphere of ~19% oxygen and nitrogen at a pressure of ~1 atm.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p79,1,"The bright horizontal lines in each flame are the glowing 14-micron ceramic fibers, where that glow can be used to determine the flame’s temperature. As the fuel flow rate increases, the flames become larger and separate flamelets form. For these tests, the flow rate was increased to explore flame stability and extinction limits. Week 17: January 17 - 23 On GMT 017, Oleg Skripochka replaced the bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4 to support ACME s-Flame test points using pure hydrogen fuel in air (21% oxygen, 79% nitrogen at 1 atm). Week 18: January 24 - 30 On GMT 024, 16 flames were successfully ignited for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames). These tests were conducted with nitrogen-diluted hydrogen fuel making the flames very faint. Even though the flames were barely visible, their presence was obvious due to the measurements of various instrumentation, including thermocouples, radiometers, and photomultiplier tubes. The glowing ceramic fibers were also a positive indication of a flame. The harp-like array of 14- micron ceramic fibers was translated into the flame to make optical measurements of the flame temperature from the hot fibers’ visible glow. Week 19: January 31 - February 6 On GMT 029, 21 flames were successfully ignited for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames). These were the final tests for the first round of testing for s-Flame. In this round, a total",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p80,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 80 of 145 flames were ignited using nitrogen-diluted gaseous fuels. In the upcoming second round, tests will be conducted with the same fuels but diluted with helium, which has much different transport properties than nitrogen. CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG In this test point video, a sooty (yellow) flame is ignited with a retracting hot-wire igniter. This flame is burning ethylene fuel diluted by nitrogen. Ethylene’s double bond tends to make flames sooty. However, the soot (which is black, but thermally glows yellow) quickly disappears, leaving a blue soot-free spherical flame. Following ignition, the flame grows to reach enough oxygen to consume the fuel. The harp-like array of 14-micron ceramic fibers moves in from the left to a fixed position. The glow from the fibers allows for a good estimation of the local temperature. These flames are a bit like bubbles, where the fuel is within the flame, the oxygen is outside of it, and the light, heat, and chemical products are emitted at the surface where they come together and react.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p80,1,"The base of the flame begins to retreat from the burner’s fuel supply tube due to heat loss. Both the flame and the glowing fibers can be seen to dim as the flame weakens. Eventually the base of the flame begins to oscillate. The flame locally quenches—creating a hole in the flame. This hole allows for increased mixing of the fuel and oxygen, which in turn allows the flame to temporarily recover. As the oscillations grow in strength, the flame goes out. s-Flame round 2, featuring helium dilution Week 2: April 17 - 23 On GMT 114, Chris Cassidy replaced the CIR FOMA Cal Unit on the back of the CIR Optics Bench. This activity allowed us to perform the calibration of the CIR’s FOMA (Fuel/Oxidizer Management Assembly) system. This calibration needs to be completed yearly to ensure that all the pressure transducers in the CIR are accurate. On GMT 115, in support of the CIR FOMA Calibration, Chris Cassidy closed and opened valves on the gas bottles installed in CIR. Week 3: April 24 - 30 On GMT 119, Chris Cassidy replaced the ACME Fuel Controller and set the CIR Valve Timers to support the upcoming second round of testing for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames) starting in a couple weeks.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p80,2,"s-Flame’s goal is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction, of both soot-free and sooty flames. Without the buoyant flows created by Earth’s gravity, flames in microgravity are typically spherical, which allows the analysis to focus on the combustion chemistry and details of the transport phenomena. Results may lead to more efficient and less polluting combustion technology for use on Earth.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p81,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 81 Week 4: May 1 - 7 On GMT 122, Anatoly Ivanishin and Chris Cassidy replaced three CIR Manifold Bottles to support the upcoming second round of testing for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames) starting next week. s-Flame’s goal is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction, of both soot-free and sooty flames. Without the buoyant flows created by Earth’s gravity, flames in microgravity are typically spherical, which allows the analysis to focus on the combustion chemistry and details of the transport phenomena. Results may lead to more efficient and less polluting combustion technology for use on Earth. Week 5: May 8 - 14 On GMT 132, we began the second round of testing for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames) with seven flames ignited using a hydrogen/methane fuel mixture diluted with helium. While the first round of testing used nitrogen to dilute the atmosphere and fuels, this round of testing uses helium. Helium is more diffusive and has a greater thermal conductivity than nitrogen. These factors can strongly influence combustion, which when coupled with the microgravity environment, can lead to new insight and understanding. On GMT 134, Anatoly Ivanishin replaced the bottles on the CIR Manifold #4 and the CIR Check Gas Manifold to continue supporting s-Flame test points.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p81,1,"CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG In this test point video, a porous sphere at the end of the burner’s fuel supply tube is used to create spherical flames. The gaseous fuel is ejected from this sphere, and the flame is ignited with a retracting hot-wire igniter. The flame “retreats” from the burner’s tube due to heat loss leaving just a flame “cap.” As the igniter retracts, a second ignition occurs while the flame “cap” is still burning. This second flame, which is large and spherical, is initially bright but quickly fades and extinguishes. We’re not sure what caused the second ignition, but regardless, the resulting data provides an opportunity to learn about the inner workings of flames. s-Flame’s goal is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction, of both soot-free and sooty flames. Without the buoyant flows created by Earth’s gravity, flames in microgravity are typically spherical, which allows the analysis to focus on the combustion chemistry and details of the transport phenomena. Results may lead to more efficient and less polluting combustion technology for use on Earth.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p81,2,"Week 6: May 15 - 21 On GMT 136, 11 flames were successfully ignited for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames). These tests burned a hydrogen/methane fuel mixture diluted with helium but with a different ratio of flammable gases than investigated in the first test day. The results from both days will be compared with those from a third ratio, which will be tested on our next test day. On GMT 140, Anatoly Ivanishin replaced the bottles at CIR Manifold #1 and CIR Manifold #4 to support ACME s-Flame test points using the third hydrogen/methane fuel mixture diluted with helium.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p82,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 82 ACME s-Flame Test Point Image In this false-colored preliminary image, the flame encompasses the porous spherical burner and its fuel supply tube. The bright lines at the top are 14-micron ceramic fibers that are glowing because of the flame’s heat. Where the fibers are visibly glowing, the flame’s temperature at that point can be determined by color-ratio pyrometry, which uses a color camera characterized for that purpose. Qualitatively, a yellow glow is hotter than orange, which is in turn hotter than red—just like metal in a forge. Given the extremely fine fibers, the measurement effectively provides the flame temperature, which can be compared with the results of computational modeling. s-Flame’s goal is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction, of both soot-free and sooty flames. Without the buoyant flows created by Earth’s gravity, flames in microgravity are typically spherical, which allows the analysis to focus on the combustion chemistry and details of the transport phenomena. Results may lead to more efficient and less polluting combustion technology for use on Earth.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p82,1,"Week 7: May 22 - 28 On GMT 142, 17 flames were successfully ignited for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames). These tests burned a hydrogen/methane fuel mixture diluted with helium but with a different ratio of flammable gases than investigated in the first two test days. These tests were performed in a chamber environment of 30% oxygen (the remainder was helium); whereas, the first two test days were performed in 21% oxygen. Flames are strongly dependent on the oxygen concentration, so these flames lasted longer before extinguishing than the earlier tests. On GMT 147, Anatoly Ivanishin replaced the bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4 to support ACME s-Flame test points using 45% hydrogen fuel diluted with helium. CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG Following ignition in this test point video, the spherical flame expands to reach enough oxygen to consume the fuel flowing from the burner. The flame retreats from the burner’s fuel supply tube due to heat loss leaving just a flame “cap.” The harp-like array of 14-micron ceramic fibers moves",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p83,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 83 in from the left to a fixed position. The glow from the fibers allows for a good estimation of the local temperature. As the flame opens, the fuel and oxygen, which are normally separated by the flame, mix together. This mixing allows the flame to temporarily recover. The flame flashes back three times before it fully extinguishes. s-Flame’s goal is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction, of both soot-free and sooty flames. Without the buoyant flows created by Earth’s gravity, flames in microgravity are typically spherical, which allows the analysis to focus on the combustion chemistry and details of the transport phenomena. Results may lead to more efficient and less polluting combustion technology for use on Earth.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p83,1,"Week 8: May 29 - June 4 In support of ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames) test points, Chris Cassidy replaced the ACME Igniter Tip and the ACME Fuel Controller on GMT 154. The next day, nine helium-diluted hydrogen flames were successfully ignited for s-Flame, where most of these tests were performed in a chamber environment of ~30% oxygen with a balance of helium. Previously, similar tests were performed with nitrogen dilution, but helium is dramatically different than nitrogen. Helium is more diffusive and has a greater thermal conductivity than nitrogen. Studying these differences can enable new learning, particularly when combined with the microgravity environment. On GMT 156, Anatoly Ivanishin replaced the bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support ACME s-Flame test points using methane fuel diluted with helium. ACME s-Flame Test Point Image One benefit of performing combustion research in microgravity is spherical flames. This image (preliminary and false-colored) shows a spherical flame of diluted hydrogen ~25 mm in diameter. Reducing the flame geometry to a single dimension dramatically simplifies analysis and modeling. It also enables computation focused on chemical kinetics and transport phenomena rather than fluid dynamics and flame structure.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p84,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 84 The two bright lines above the flame are 14-micron ceramic fibers, which are visibly glowing because of the flame’s heat. The temperature distribution along the glowing fiber can be quantified through Thin Filament Pyrometry, where the hue is indicative of the temperature. Flame measurements are also made with intensified cameras, thermopile detectors, photomultiplier tubes, and thermocouples. All this data is used to improve and validate flame modeling and through that enable improvements in practical terrestrial combustion. Week 9: June 5 - 11 On GMT 160, 12 helium-diluted methane flames were ignited in oxygen/helium atmospheres for ACME s- Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames). Compared to other gaseous hydrocarbon fuels, methane (CH4) is more difficult to ignite because of its lack of carbon-carbon bonds. Methane ignition hadn’t been an issue in earlier testing with nitrogen dilution, but it was challenging in this week’s tests because of helium’s much greater thermal conductivity. Nonetheless, the recently installed coiled igniter tip enabled us to ignite these flames.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p84,1,"The results from the current series of s-Flame tests will be compared with the results from the previous tests using nitrogen dilution. In that earlier testing, it was found that radiative extinction (i.e., extinction caused by radiative heat loss) is a common event, which had been predicted computationally. However, we did not observe this in preliminary ground-based testing. It was also seen in the on-orbit tests that radiative reabsorption extends the flame size at extinction. Another type of extinction was sometimes observed, where the gas velocities caused nitrogen-diluted ethylene flames (under certain conditions) to blow out. It will be interesting to see what the future comparisons will reveal about the nature of flames. On GMT 162, Anatoly Ivanishin replaced the bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support ACME s-Flame test points using 20% ethylene fuel diluted with helium.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p84,2,"Week 10: June 12 - 18 On GMT 164, 11 helium-diluted ethylene flames were ignited for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames). This is a shift in testing for the s-Flame experiment from non-sooty fuels (i.e., hydrogen, methane, or hydrogen/methane blends) to a sooty fuel. Ethylene (C2H4) tends to be a sooty fuel because it has a carbon double-bond. Soot forms when hot fuel combines with itself in the absence of oxygen, where the union of fuel molecules is facilitated by the double bond. The flames in these tests were sooty at ignition, but the soot quickly disappeared resulting in blue flames, where blue is the natural color of flames with hydrocarbon fuels. On GMT 168, Anatoly Ivanishin replaced the bottles at CIR Manifold #1, CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4 to support ACME s-Flame test points using 25% ethylene fuel diluted with helium. CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG This test point video runs at half speed to better show what is happening with the flame. A flame of 20% ethylene fuel ignites in a chamber atmosphere of about 30% oxygen (both diluted with helium). The flame is initially very bright. Although the soot particles are black, their glow depends on their temperature, where yellow is hotter than orange which is in turn hotter than red. While some soot clusters escape the flame, much of the soot becomes trapped within the flame by the temperature gradient.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p84,3,"After some time, the array of ceramic fibers is translated into the flame to determine the radial temperature distribution where the fibers are glowing. Despite their 14-micron diameter, the",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p85,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 85 fibers appear to cool or push away the soot because that side of the flame becomes increasingly blue. The flame becomes all blue and retreats from the fuel supply tube because of heat loss. The opening in the flame allows fuel and oxygen to directly mix, where they are normally separated by the presence of the flame shell. This mixing enhances flammability and the reinvigorated and locally brighter flame flashes back to the supply tube reestablishing the nominally spherical flame. But it is short-lived, and the flame quickly retreats again until it is just a “cap” on the left. Mixing again leads to another flame flash, but it quickly extinguishes due to radiative heat loss, heat loss to the supply tube, and conductive heat loss enhanced by the helium’s high thermal conductivity. Week 11: June 19 - 25 On GMT 170, 20 flames were ignited for ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames). These flames burned a 25% ethylene/75% helium blend; whereas, the flames on GMT 164 burned a 20% ethylene/80% helium blend. Comparing these data will enable analysis of the effects of this fuel/inert blend on flame dynamics, such as extinction behavior and structure. On GMT 171, Anatoly Ivanishin replaced the bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support ACME s-Flame test points using a 30% ethylene/70% helium blend. ACME s-Flame Test Point Image",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p85,1,"In this image (preliminary and false-colored), the flame on the right has a higher ethylene concentration than the flame on the left. As expected, the flame on the right is larger where the spherical flame expands until the fuel reaches enough oxygen to be consumed. These flames, which are non-premixed, are like bubbles in that the gaseous fuel is inside the bubble and the oxygen is outside the bubble. The flame shell, like the film of a bubble, is a thin sheet where the fuel and oxygen come together and react, producing light, heat, and chemical products, such as carbon dioxide and water vapor. Meanwhile, the helium—which readily diffuses—is free to cross the flame shell. 20% 25%",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p86,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 86 However, diffusion was minimal because the helium concentrations within the fuel and the surrounding atmosphere were similar. Both flames were bright and sooty at ignition, but these images are from late in each test when the flames became dim. The bright lines at the top are the 14-micron ceramic fibers used for optical determination of the flame temperature using a camera characterized for that purpose. s-Flame’s goal is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction, of both soot-free and sooty flames. Without the buoyant flows created by Earth’s gravity, flames in microgravity are typically spherical, which allows the analysis to focus on the combustion chemistry and details of the transport phenomena. Results may lead to more efficient and less polluting combustion technology for use on Earth. Week 12: June 26 - July 2 GMT 176 was a record-breaking day for ACME with 23 flames ignited—the most we’ve ignited in one day! We’ve now ignited over 250 flames for s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames) and over 1000 flames for ACME since it started in Nov. 2017. The tests on GMT 176 were performed with 30% ethylene in helium for comparison with the results from the two preceding test days, which burned 20% and 25% ethylene/helium blends.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p86,1,"CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG In this test point video, helium-diluted ethylene fuel is ignited with a low fuel flow rate to limit the soot produced with ignition. After ignition, the flow rate is tripled in a smooth ramp, so that the flame response can be observed. As expected, the flame quickly grows, where it must expand to reach enough oxygen to consume all the fuel. Before the fuel flow rate has completely tripled, the flame retreats from the burner’s fuel supply tube because of heat loss—creating a hole in the flame near the tube. This hole allows for increased mixing of the ethylene and oxygen, which are normally separated by the flame shell. This mixing causes the flame to momentarily reestablish around the burner tube with a bright flash of light. Mixing again leads to another flame flash, after which the flame fully extinguishes. The fuel flow rate did completely triple before the flame extinguished.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p86,2,"Although not visible in the video, the measured OH* and CH* chemiluminescence was relatively constant throughout this test with the important exceptions of ignition and the bright flashes of light prior to extinction. In contrast, the measured chemiluminescence declined with time in similar tests with constant fuel flow rates. OH* and CH* are molecules at an elevated energy state which emit a photon of a specific wavelength as their energy level drops a level. The CH* emission is the source of most of the blue in a hydrocarbon flame, while the OH* emission is in the ultraviolet. ACME uses the latter measurement as an indicator of ignition and extinction because it is insensitive to the hot-wire igniter and hot burned gases. Using this unique microgravity data, the s-Flame investigation seeks to test and improve computational models, which may enable improvements to practical combustion on Earth.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p87,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 87 Week 13: July 3 - 9 On GMT 188, Anatoly Ivanishin replaced the bottles at CIR Manifold #1, CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4 to support ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames) test points using 45% hydrogen fuel diluted with helium. On GMT 190, 21 sustained flames using this fuel were ignited for ACME s-Flame. These are the final test points for the second round of ACME s-Flame testing. Helium’s characteristics are dramatically different from that of nitrogen, which was used for dilution in the first round of s-Flame testing. ACME s-Flame Test Point Image In this image (preliminary and false-colored), the burner tube and porous sphere are visible in the middle of the flame. In this test, a hydrogen/helium blend was ejected into an oxygen/helium atmosphere from the porous sphere at the end of the burner. The two bright lines above the flame are 14-micron ceramic fibers, which are glowing because of the flame’s heat and are used for optical temperature measurements. s-Flame’s goal is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction, of both soot-free and sooty flames. Without the buoyant flows created by Earth’s gravity, flames in microgravity are typically spherical, which allows the analysis to focus on the combustion chemistry and details of the transport phenomena. Results may lead to more efficient and less polluting combustion technology for use on Earth.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p88,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 88 Appendix H. Crew Activity per Space Station Science Highlights The subsequent text and images are from the weekly highlights prepared by the Lead Increment Scientist and made available via e-mail or online via www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/station/research/. Note that hyperlinks were generally not included in the excerpts below, but they typically go to web pages accessible via https://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/station/research/experiments/explorer/. Those pages are secondary sources of information for the ACME project, for which the primary web site is https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/acme/. s-Flame round 1, featuring nitrogen dilution Week of July 29, 2019 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • ACME Flame Design, which studies the production and control of soot to optimize oxygen- enriched combustion and the design of robust, soot-free flames, is part of a series of independent ACME experiments using the orbiting laboratory’s Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). The note above is in error, where the first round of Flame Design operations ended with the data transfer and downlink on July 26. Crew activity during this week was in preparation for the s- Flame experiment.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p88,1,"Week of August 5, 2019 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • ACME Flame Design, which studies the production and control of soot to optimize oxygen- enriched combustion and the design of robust, soot-free flames, is part of a series of independent ACME experiments using the orbiting laboratory’s Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). The note above is in error, where the crew activity during this week was in preparation for the first round of the s-Flame experiment. Week of October 28, 2019 NASA astronaut Jessica Meir works on components of the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR), a platform for the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project. This set of six independent studies includes ACME Flame Design, which studies the production and control of soot to optimize oxygen- enriched combustion and the design of robust, soot-free flames. Credits: NASA",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p89,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 89 Week of January 13, 2020 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) is a set of six independent studies of gaseous flames conducted in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). ACME supports spacecraft fire prevention as well as improved fuel efficiency and reduced pollutant production in practical combustion on Earth. • The Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame), part of the ACME project, advances prediction of the structure and dynamics of soot-free and sooty flames. Results could contribute to engines with improved efficiency and reduced emissions on Earth. Week of January 27, 2020 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • The Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame), part of the ACME project, advances prediction of the structure and dynamics of soot-free and sooty flames. Results could contribute to engines with improved efficiency and reduced emissions on Earth. s-Flame round 2, featuring helium dilution",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p89,1,"Week of April 27, 2020 Predicting flame structure During the week, the crew prepared for continuation of runs for Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame). This investigation advances prediction of the structure and dynamics of soot- free and sooty flames, which could contribute to development of engines with improved efficiency and reduced emissions on Earth. S-Flame is part of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project, a series of independent studies of gaseous flames performed in the station’s Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). The project’s primary goals are to improve fuel efficiency and reduce pollutant production in routine fuel combustion activities on Earth. A secondary goal is improving spacecraft fire prevention through innovative research focused on materials flammability. NASA astronaut Chris Cassidy works on the Combustion Integrated Rack, a device that enables safe fuel, flame and soot studies in microgravity. Credits: NASA",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p90,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 90 Week of May 18, 2020 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) studies the structure and dynamics of soot-free and sooty flames. Findings could contribute to development of engines with improved efficiency and reduced emissions on Earth. S-Flame is part of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project, a series of independent studies of gaseous flames performed in the station’s Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). ROSCOSMOS cosmonaut Anatoly Ivanishin replaces a gas bottle in the Combustion Integration Rack (CIR) in preparation for flame studies controlled by the ground. Credits: NASA Week of May 25, 2020 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) studies the structure and dynamics of soot-free and sooty flames. Findings could contribute to development of engines with improved efficiency and reduced emissions on Earth. S-Flame is part of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project, a series of independent studies of gaseous flames performed in the station’s Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p90,1,"Week of June 1, 2020 Cleaner, more efficient flames During the week, the crew made changes for different runs of the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) investigation. This experiment is part of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project, a series of independent studies of gaseous flames performed in the station’s Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). In particular, s-Flame advances prediction of the structure and dynamics of soot-free and sooty flames with the goal of improving fuel efficiency and reducing pollutants in routine fuel combustion activities on Earth. It also could contribute to better spacecraft fire prevention through innovative research focused on materials flammability. Week of Jun 15, 2020 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) studies the structure and dynamics of soot-free and sooty flames. Findings could contribute to development of engines with improved efficiency and reduced emissions on Earth. S-Flame is part of the Advanced",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p91,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 91 Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project, a series of independent studies of gaseous flames performed in the station’s Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). Week of July 6, 2020 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) studies the structure and dynamics of soot-free and sooty flames. Findings could contribute to development of engines with improved efficiency and reduced emissions on Earth. S-Flame is part of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project, a series of independent studies of gaseous flames performed in the station’s Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p92,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 92 Appendix I. Crew Activity per the ISS Daily Summary Report The subsequent text was prepared by Dennis Stocker, where the source information is available from https://blogs.nasa.gov/stationreport. Text was added identifying the investigation’s two rounds, but s- Flame operations listed under the heading “All activities are complete unless otherwise noted” were not included in this report. Some days of crew activity were only listed in that way, so this section does not comprehensively documenting crew activity in support of the s-Flame experiment. s-Flame round 1, featuring nitrogen dilution 2019 July 30: GMT 211 Combustion Integration Rack (CIR)/ Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) Manifold Bottle Exchange: The crew replaced the 40% O2 gas bottle with a 30% O2 bottle. They also replaced the ethylene fuel bottle with a hydrogen/methane mixture fuel bottle. Telemetry showed a slow leak in the hydrogen/methane bottle and teams are discussing a recovery plan. Flame Design part 1 investigation is complete and preparations are in work for transition to the s-Flame investigation. The purpose of s-Flame is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction and instabilities, of both soot-free and sooty flames. The results may contribute to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions here on Earth.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p92,1,"2019 July 31: GMT 212 Combustion Integration Rack (CIR) Manifold Bottle Inspection: Following yesterday’s discovery of a slow leak, the fuel bottle connection was demated, inspected, cleaned, and remated to the CIR manifold. The pressure was subsequently monitored and the leak was reduced, but still present. The crew then looked for the leak source (suspected to be either the quick-disconnect or pressure relief valve) with the ultrasonic leak detector but were unable to isolate the source of the leak. The fuel bottle valve was closed and teams are discussing a forward plan. This fuel bottle will be used for the upcoming s-Flame investigation. 2019 August 13: GMT 225 Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME): The crew configured the ACME Chamber for s-Flame by partially reinstalling the chamber insert to replace the igniter tip, then reinstalled the chamber insert, replaced the fuel controller, and closed the Front End Cap and rack doors. The ACME experiment series includes five independent studies of gaseous flames. The primary goals of ACME are to improve fuel efficiency and reduce pollutant production in routine fuel combustion activities on Earth. Its secondary goal is to improve spacecraft fire prevention through innovative research focused on materials flammability.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p92,2,"2019 September 9: GMT 252 Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR): The crew replaced the Manifold 4 fuel and Nitrogen bottles in preparation for upcoming s-Flames experiment runs. The CIR includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion experiments in microgravity. 2019 September 23: GMT 266 Combustion Integration Rack (CIR)/ Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME): In support of the ongoing s-Flame experiment, the crew replaced the fuel and oxidizer manifold bottles as",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p93,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 93 well as the mass flow controller. The purpose of s-Flame is to advance the ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction and instabilities, of both soot-free and sooty flames. Results may contribute to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions here on Earth. S-Flame is one of five experiments hosted by CIR/ACME, which is designed to study gaseous flames. 2019 October 24: GMT 297 CIR (Combustion Integrated Rack): The crew did a full replacement of the FCF CIR image processor (IPSUG) due to a previous failure of the hard drive. The CIR includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion experiments in microgravity.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p93,1,"2019 November 19: GMT 323 CIR/ACME/s-Flames manifold bottle exchange: As a continuation of the s-Flame experiment, the crew performed a routine manifold bottle exchange. In this activity, the crew replaced a used 30% O2/70% N2 bottle with a new one of the same composition, and replaced the used 45% CH4/55% N2 fuel bottle with a 100% H2 fuel bottle. The purpose of s-Flame is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction and instabilities, of both soot-free and sooty flames. The results may contribute to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions here on Earth. S-Flame is one of five experiments hosted by CIR/ACME which is designed to study gaseous flames. 2019 December 13: GMT 347 Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME): The crew replaced the ACME Bypass Controller and the ACME Fuel Controller, in order to continue s-flame operations. The ACME experiment series being performed in the CIR includes five independent studies of gaseous flames. The primary goals of ACME are to improve fuel efficiency and reduce pollutant production in routine fuel combustion activities on Earth. Its secondary goal is to improve spacecraft fire prevention through innovative research focused on materials flammability.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p93,2,"2019 December 24: GMT 358 CIR/ACME (Combustion Integration Rack/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments): In support of the continuing s-Flame investigation, the crew exchanged the ACME Bypass Controller and the ACME Fuel Controller. These controllers allow different flow regimes to be used for the experiment. The purpose of s-Flame is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction and instabilities, of both soot-free and sooty flames. The results may contribute to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions here on Earth. S-Flame is one of five experiments hosted by CIR/ACME which is designed to study gaseous flames. 2020 2020 January 2: GMT 002 Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR): As a continuation of the s-Flame experiment, the crew performed manifold H2 and CH4 bottle exchanges. The CIR includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion experiments in microgravity.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p94,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 94 2020 January 8: GMT 008 Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR): As a continuation of the s-Flame experiment, the crew performed a manifold H2 and CH4 bottle exchanges. The CIR includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion experiments in microgravity. 2020 January 17: GMT 017 CIR/ACME (Combustion Integration Rack/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments): In support of the continuing s-Fames investigation, the crew replaced a used 3.8L Nitrogen/Oxygen manifold bottle with a new one. They also replaced the 1.0L hydrogen/methane manifold fuel bottle with a 100% hydrogen fuel bottle. The purpose of s-Flame is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction and instabilities, of both soot-free and sooty flames. The results may contribute to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions here on Earth. S-Flame is one of five experiments hosted by CIR/ACME, which is designed to study gaseous flames. 2020 January 28: GMT 028 CIR (Combustion Integrated Rack): The crew replaced the CIR manifold #4 with new H2 and C2H4/N2 gas bottles in preparation of continued s-Flame test runs. The CIR includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion experiments in microgravity.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p94,1,"s-Flame round 2, featuring helium dilution 2020 April 23: GMT 114 CIR (Combustion Integrated Rack): The crew performed a replacement of the CIR FOMA (Fuel Oxidizer Management Assembly) Calibration Unit in preparation for the next set of s-flame runs. The CIR includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion experiments in microgravity. 2020 April 24: GMT 115 CIR (Combustion Integrated Rack): The crew prepared the CIR to allow ground commanding of the FOMA (Fuel Oxidizer Management Assembly) Calibration. The CIR includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion experiments in microgravity. 2020 April 28: GMT 119 Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME): The crew replaced an ACME controller In preparation for the continuation of s-flame runs. The ACME experiment series being performed in the CIR includes five independent studies of gaseous flames. The primary goals of ACME are to improve fuel efficiency and reduce pollutant production in routine fuel combustion activities on Earth. Its secondary goal is to improve spacecraft fire prevention through innovative research focused on materials flammability.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p95,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 95 2020 May 1: GMT 122 Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR): The crew replaced Nitrogen/Oxygen manifold bottles in support of the continuing s-Flames investigation. The CIR includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion experiments in microgravity. 2020 May 13: GMT 134 Combustion Integration Rack/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (CIR/ACME) : As a continuation of the ACME/s-Flame part 2 investigation, the crew removed a nitrogen check gas bottle and replaced it with a helium check gas bottle. They also removed a H2/CH4/He fuel bottle and replaced it with one of the same gas mixture, but different percent composition. Following the installation of the gas bottles, it was noted the actual pressure in the helium check gas bottle was significantly lower than expected and the team is assessing the impacts. The purpose of s-Flame is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction and instabilities, of both soot-free and sooty flames. The results may contribute to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions here on Earth. S-Flame is one of five experiments hosted by CIR/ACME which is designed to study gaseous flames.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p95,1,2020 May 19: GMT 140 Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME): The crew replaced Nitrogen/Oxygen manifold bottles in support of the continuing s-Flames investigation. The ACME experiment series being performed in the CIR includes five independent studies of gaseous flames. The primary goals of ACME are to improve fuel efficiency and reduce pollutant production in routine fuel combustion activities on Earth. Its secondary goal is to improve spacecraft fire prevention through innovative research focused on materials flammability. 2020 May 26: GMT 147 Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME): The crew replaced Nitrogen/Oxygen manifold bottles in support of the continuing s-Flames investigation. The ACME experiment series being performed in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) includes five independent studies of gaseous flames. The primary goals of ACME are to improve fuel efficiency and reduce pollutant production in routine fuel combustion activities on Earth. Its secondary goal is to improve spacecraft fire prevention through innovative research focused on materials flammability.,,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p95,2,"2020 June 2: GMT 154 Combustion Integration Rack/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (CIR/ACME): In a continuation of the s-Flame part 2 investigation, the crew performed the necessary activities to change out a mass flow controller and igniter. The mass flow controller allows the ACME experimenters to study combustion under different gas flow regimes. The igniter was changed from a well-used hoop style igniter to a coil style igniter which can deliver more energy to ignite a gas mixture. A minor leak was seen in one of the coolant Quick Disconnect (QD) fittings when it was removed, but this is not expected to be an issue once installed again. The purpose of s-Flame is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction and instabilities, of both soot-free and sooty flames. The results may contribute to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions here on Earth. S-Flame is one of five experiments hosted by CIR/ACME which is designed to study gaseous flames.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p96,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 96 2020 June 4: GMT 156 Combustion Integration Rack/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (CIR/ACME): In support of the on-going s-Flame part 2 investigation, the crew removed a 45% H2/ 55% He fuel bottle and replaced it with a 45% CH4/ 55% He fuel bottle. The purpose of s-Flame is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction and instabilities, of both soot-free and sooty flames. The results may contribute to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions here on Earth. S-Flame is one of five experiments hosted by CIR/ACME which is designed to study gaseous flames. 2020 June 10: GMT 162 Combustion Integration Rack/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (CIR/ACME): In support of the on-going s-Flame part 2 investigation, the crew removed a 45% CH4/ 55% He fuel bottle and replaced it with a 20% CH2=CH2/ 80% He fuel bottle. The purpose of s-Flame is to advance our ability to predict the structure and dynamics, including extinction and instabilities, of both soot-free and sooty flames. The results may contribute to the development of lean-burn engines for improved efficiency and reduced pollutant emissions here on Earth. S-Flame is one of five experiments hosted by CIR/ACME which is designed to study gaseous flames.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p96,1,"2020 June 16: GMT 168 Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME): In support of the on-going s-Flame part 2 investigation, the crew replaced C2H4, He, and O2 gas bottles in preparation for upcoming test point runs. The ACME experiment series being performed in the CIR includes five independent studies of gaseous flames. The primary goals of ACME are to improve fuel efficiency and reduce pollutant production in routine fuel combustion activities on Earth. Its secondary goal is to improve spacecraft fire prevention through innovative research focused on materials flammability. 2020 June 19: GMT 171 Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME): In support of the on-going s-Flame part 2 investigation, the crew replaced the C2H4 fuel bottle in preparation for upcoming test point runs. The ACME experiment series being performed in the CIR includes five independent studies of gaseous flames. The primary goals of ACME are to improve fuel efficiency and reduce pollutant production in routine fuel combustion activities on Earth. Its secondary goal is to improve spacecraft fire prevention through innovative research focused on materials flammability.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p97,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 97 Appendix J. Crew Activity per the NASA Space Station Blog This section consists of excerpts from https://blogs.nasa.gov/spacestation/. Hyperlinks were generally not included, but clarifications have sometimes been added in red. s-Flame round 1, featuring nitrogen dilution 2019 August 13 – Crew Gets Ready for Next Spacewalk and New Spaceships Christina Koch of NASA serviced and replaced hardware that fuels experiments inside the Combustion Integrated Rack. 2019 September 23 - Japanese, Russian Rockets Prepare to Launch Cargo and Crew This Week Luca Parmitano of ESA (European Space Agency) replaced fuel bottles in the Combustion Integrated Rack …. 2019 October 24 - Crew Explores Space Biology, Reviews Particle Detector Repairs Meir later swapped out a failed computer hard drive that supports combustion experiments. 2019 October 29 - Spacesuits, Human Research, Robotics Training Ahead of Cargo Missions NASA astronaut Jessica Meir works to swap out a failed computer hard drive that supports experiments inside the Combustion Integrated Rack aboard the International Space Station’s U.S. Destiny laboratory module. 2020 January 8 - Spacewalk Preps Underway Amidst Space Research Cosmonauts Alexander Skvortsov and Oleg Skripochka split their time on Russian science and maintenance tasks. … Skripochka replaced fuel bottles for combustion research.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p97,1,"2020 January 17 – Nutrition Studies, Spacewalk Preps Ahead of SpaceX Crew Escape Test Fellow cosmonaut Oleg Skripochka serviced combustion research gear ….",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p98,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 98 s-Flame round 2, featuring helium dilution 2020 April 21 - Advanced Space Research Ahead of Weekend Cargo Mission In the afternoon, Cassidy moved over to the U.S. Destiny laboratory module and swapped computer parts inside the Fluids and Combustion Facility (FCF). The FCF consists of two refrigerator-sized research racks enabling safe observations of the behavior of fluids and flames in microgravity. Scientists use the data to design advanced fuel transfer systems and increase fire safety. While this may be true, Cassidy did not make such a change on the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) during April 2020. 2020 April 28 - Cygnus Prepped for Departure During Station Science and Upkeep Cassidy also opened up the Combustion Integrated Rack for maintenance replacing components in the research device that enables safe fuel, flame and soot studies in microgravity. 2020 April 29 - Spacesuit Work and Heart Research Fill Crew Day Expedition 63 Commander Chris Cassidy works in the Combustion Integrated Rack, a research device that enables safe fuel, flame and soot studies in microgravity. While Cassidy had worked on the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on April 28, he is not doing so in this photo (in which the CIR facility does not appear) and as such the caption is in error. In this photo, he appears to be working at a Materials Science Research Rack (MSRR).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p98,1,"2020 May 13 - Flying Robots, Ultrasound Eye Scans Top Science Schedule Ivanishin started his workday swapping fuel bottles inside the Combustion Integrated Rack which enables safe studies of fuels, flames and soot in microgravity. 2020 May 19 - Japan Ready for Cargo Launch, Crew Works Science and Emergency Procedures Veteran cosmonaut Anatoly Ivanishin continued servicing the Combustion Integrated Rack as he swapped out fuel bottles inside the device that enables fuel, flame and soot research in microgravity.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p99,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 99 2020 May 26 - Expedition 63 Awaits SpaceX Crew, Unpacks Japanese Cargo Veteran cosmonaut Anatoly Ivanishin swapped out more fuel bottles inside the Combustion Integrated Rack to maintain research operations inside the U.S. Destiny laboratory module. The refrigerator-sized research rack enables fuel, flame and soot research in microgravity. 2020 June 4 - Advanced Station Science Benefiting Humans Ivanishin was inside the U.S. Destiny laboratory swapping fuel bottles inside the Combustion Integrated Rack to enable safe fuel and flame science. 2020 June 10 - Station Buzzing With Science in Middle of Spacewalk Preps Today, the three NASA astronauts and two Roscosmos cosmonauts juggled a variety of experiments encompassing fluids, combustion, genetics and vision among others. … Russian Flight Engineer Anatoly Ivanishin … swapped fuel bottles inside the Combustion Integrated Rack to continue safe fuel and flame research. 2020 June 16 - Biology and Physics as Crew Enters BEAM, Preps for Spacewalk Tuesday’s science aboard the International Space Station encompassed life science, fluids and flames to help humans on Earth and in space. … Veteran cosmonaut Anatoly Ivanishin continued to service the Combustion Integrated Rack replacing fuel bottles to maintain safe fuel and flame research in the device. Insights could improve fire safety as well as combustion processes for Earth and space industries.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p99,1,"2020 June 19 - Crew Readies Satellite and Organizes Station Cosmonaut Anatoly Ivanishin changed out fuel bottles in the Combustion Integrated Rack this morning to continue the safe research of flames in microgravity. 2020 June 22 – Astronauts Check Spacewalk Tools and Suits, Study Space Physics Hurley spent the first half of his Monday working on fluid and combustion physics. He first explored how microfluidics can cause biochemical reactions in blood revealing mechanisms hidden on Earth. Next, he researched fabricating composite materials to learn how to repair and build structures on future space missions. While Hurley worked on fluid physics, the reference to combustion is presumably in error. At the least, he did not work on the ACME project on that day, if at all. 2020 July 6 - Cargo and Science Operations Start Work Week Cosmonaut Anatoly Ivanishin replaced fuel bottles inside the Combustion Integrated Rack to continue safe fuel and flame research.",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p100,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 100 Appendix K. ACME Operations per @ISS_Research on Twitter These tweets (from https://twitter.com/ISS_Research/) were from the period of s-Flame operations, i.e., from July 2019 to July 2020. However, please note that some are in regard to other ACME investigations. 2019 Sept. 10: GMT 253 2019 Sept. 12: GMT 255 This Flame Design video clip can be viewed at, and downloaded from, https://images.nasa.gov/details- jsc2019m000710_Designing-Flames-Aboard-ISS-MP4.html.",,False
