PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p104,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 104 Appendix L. ACME Operations per Astronauts on Twitter These tweets are from ISS crew members who conducted operations in support of the s-Flame experiment. Jessica Meir (@Astro_Jessica) on Twitter, https://twitter.com/astro_jessica 2019 Nov. 06: GMT 310",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p105,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 105 Chris Cassidy (@Astro_SEAL) on Twitter, https://twitter.com/astro_seal 2020 Sept. 1: GMT 245",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p106,0,"ACME/s-Flame Final Report (2021) 29 July 2021 106 Appendix M. Summary of the ISS data collection The s-Flame data is all digital and includes both image and non-image data, where this is true for the other ACME experiments. To be clear, there was no collection of soot or other physical samples that were subsequently transported to the ground. The s-Flame image data includes images from the following cameras: 1. ACME DataCam, used with a clear fused-silica ‘filter’ and various zoom and focus settings (but those settings were consistent within each individual test) 2. CFI intensified camera, used with CH*, formaldehyde, and UV fused silica filters, but where only a single filter was used in each test 3. Low Light Level Ultraviolet (LLL-UV) camera, equipped with an OH* filter 4. High-Bitdepth Multispectral (HiBMs) camera, equipped with a red filter corresponding to the opposing CIR illumination package, which was sometimes left off The raw DataCam images are in a Halcon Iconic Object (hobj) format, while the images from the CFI, HiBMs, and LLL-UV cameras are provided in a Tagged Image File (tif) format. In contrast, the analog video was recorded on the ground in a Transport Stream (ts) format. The key non-imaging data from a test is a comma-delimited file that is described in detail in the Software Interface Design Description (ACME-DOC-010 Rev. D).",,False
PSI-23,Reports,PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Reports/PSI-23_Reports_s_Flame_final_report_20210729.pdf,p106,1,The raw data is currently archived at NASA and work is underway to make it available publicly through NASA’s Physical Science Informatics System (PSI).,,False
