PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p15,0,ACME Critical Design Review– 20 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies SpaceDOC ACME Contract Status •ACME SpaceDOC involvement began in 2007 •ACME Science Concept Review was held in February 2008 •ACME Requirements Definition Review was held in May 2010 •ACME Preliminary Design Review was held in January 2011 •ACME Phase 0/1 Flight Safety Review (Integrated with CIR) was held in February 2012 •ACME Interim Design Review was held in June 2012 •BRE Requirements Definition Review and Preliminary Design Review (combined) was held in June 2013 •The ACME SpaceDOC work is assigned as incremental Delivery Orders (DOs) to ZIN Technologies under the SpaceDOC contract •Current Delivery Order contract began in October 2010 and continues through December 2013,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p16,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 21 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR RFA-001 Disposition RFA # 01 Originator: Anita Tenteris Originator Concurrence: 23 September 2013 Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale One of the criteria for PDR is a preliminary limited life items list. Just because other experiments may not have such a list does not mean ACME should not have such a list. ACME presents a unique situation where experiments will occur in the same chamber as the Cube is located as well as tubing, wiring, cooling lines, etc. The project should to start identifying their materials lists from the Computer- Aided Design (CAD) drawings so that compatibility issues/soft goods shelf life/limited life items can be identified early in the design cycle. Maybe testing would also be needed as well for materials with unknown compatibility? Identify the material compatibility and shelf life limits for the soft goods used in ACME and CIR It has been determined by the project that a materials analysis would not be done for PDR due to funding and resource limitations. The plan is to have this analysis and MIUL completed at CDR. Although the analysis was not complete at PDR, the ACME project is aware of potential issues with the Data Cube and other electronics and their compatibility with the gaseous fuels. These issues are currently being tracked in the Risk Management Implementation Tool database.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p17,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 22 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR RFA-002 Disposition RFA # 02 Originator: Anita Tenteris Originator Concurrence: 23 September 2013 Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale In many of the presentations, the presenters indicated CIR heritage for a choice of hardware. It seemed as though CIR was treated as a ‘black box’ that ACME hooks into. ACME fuels need to be compatible with CIR and also cause no issues to CIR. The following items need to be investigated further to assess compatibility: • ACME Venting compatibility with CIR hardware • Are the gases/liquids compatible with CIR hardware, particularly with the soft goods in the solenoid valves? The board was told the solenoid valves contained FKM o-ring. For the ACME gases, NBr is the recommended rubber for most of the gases, if not all. • What are the limited life items for CIR? Do any of the items need be replaced prior to ACME use? An assessment of the venting compatibility has been addressed and was deemed acceptable. As the project matures, CIR will assess compatibility of materials in their system with ACME gasses, products of combustion and any other applicable interactions. ACME anticipates this will be completed by CDR.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p18,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 23 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR RFA-003 Disposition RFA # 03 Originator: Al Linne Originator Concurrence: 7 November 2013 Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale There are no warnings to eliminate damage to the test hardware To minimize the likelihood of hardware damage, the flight & ground software designs should incorporate software requirements to identify invalid commands and command parameters that are outside of allowable limits and to alert operations personnel that invalid commands were entered.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p18,1,"A complete software command list for the ACME hardware has been derived from operational requirements for the ACME system. Operational limits have been defined for each command and incorporated into the software requirement set. The software requirements are documented in the Software section of the ACME Engineering Requirements Document (ERD) ACME-REQ-001. This list of signals is also documented in the ACME Data Dictionary Document (ACME-DOC-008). These documents will be available for NASA review and comment during the CDR. The approved operational limitations will be built into the ACME ground command station software at the TSC. Every command sent by an operator will be checked against these established limits. Commands that violate safe ranges will be rejected (on the ground) and the operator will receive an error message. This ground software will be verified, as part of the overall ACME test and verification campaign, to ensure no command can ever reach the ACME hardware that is outside of these safely established limits.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p19,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 24 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR RFA-004 Disposition RFA # 04 Originator: Al Linne Originator Concurrence: 7 November 2013 Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale A detonation analysis has not been completed. Perform a detonation analysis on the expected ACME gas mixtures. This should be done prior to the ACME Phase 0/1 Flight Safety Review, since it may inform some of the hazard analysis. Also, the results of this analysis could drive the consumables usage and could limit the amount of science performed. An ACME detonation analysis has been performed for the Inverse Flame Experiment, which is the the worst case hazard. It was chosen because of the possible oxygen interaction with ACME CIA hardware (i.e. materials compatibility). The analysis validated that the present configuration could safely meet requirements. The analysis has not impacted the design. A full detonation analysis of all experiments will be completed prior to the Phase 2 Flight Safety Review.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p20,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 25 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR RFA-005 Disposition RFA # 05 Originator: Al Linne Originator Concurrence: 7 November 2013 Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale A number of different oxygen percentages were discussed & identified in the PDR presentation and documentation. What oxygen percentage was used in the design and does this cover the ranges requested by the PI. Determine the worst case oxygen percentage (and pressure) that could potentially be in the CIR chamber with ACME installed, based on credible failures to CIR & ACME equipment and use this oxygen percentage as the design point for ACME. This will drive integrated safety/hazard analyses as well as ACME material compatibility. Also, based on the resulting worst case oxygen percentage determine if an oxygen compatibility assessment needs to performed for the ACME Chamber Insert Assembly. Oxygen compatibility with the ACME CIA has been assessed and is documented in the Draft Materials Identification/ Usage List (MIUL), which is a review item for the CDR. An Integrated Hazard Analysis has been completed and the worst-case oxygen percentages and pressures have been identified and addressed in the Hazard Report (see ACME Hazard Report 5, documented in ACME-SDP-001).",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p21,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 26 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR RFA-006 Disposition RFA # 06 Originator: Alan Linne, Nechelle Grant, Terry O’Malley Originator Concurrence: 25 October 2013 Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale CIR hazards mitigations are being used by ACME. It was not clear that the ACME team had reviewed the CIR hazards and confirmed the mitigation steps are applicable to ACME. There were no integrated hazards included in the ACME Phase 0/1 Flight Safety Data Package safety hazard analysis (ACME-SDP-001. The ACME and CIR teams should provide an integrated CIR/ACME Safety story. There are likely integrated safety hazards that exist in the integrated system and these should be identified and documented. Perform an integrated hazard analysis and prepare integrated hazard reports. The integrated CIR/ACME hazard analysis should be part of the Phase 0/1 Flight Safety Review. Also, a pressure switch, a solenoid valve, and a pressure regulator in the ACME system were included in the design on the N2 input to the chamber. Perform a integrated hazard analysis of this system (CIR & ACME hardware) to ensure system meets the intent of CIR hazard analyses. The Integrated Hazard Analysis has been completed with the participation of the CIR Team. An Integrated Safety Data Package has been prepared and was included as input to the ACME Phase 0/1 Flight Safety Review.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p22,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 27 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR RFA-007 Disposition RFA # 07 Originator: Terry O’Malley Originator Concurrence: 25 October 2013 Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale The ACME project plan did not provide sufficient detail of the end-to-end project planning. There was no evidence of a bottoms up cost estimate or a bottoms up schedule. Both of these are vital in ensuring the project has been thought through and that sufficient resources are available to successfully complete the project. The only budget shown was the program budget, and it may not be adequate complete the current scope of the ACME project. The ACME Systems Engineering Management Plan also did not provide sufficient detail of the end-to-end project technical management. It provided details through PDR/CDR, but not the project lifecycle. Update the Project Plan. Complete a bottoms up cost estimate and a bottoms up end-to-end schedule. Consider performing a parametric cost estimate. To be of value, these should be performed within 6 months of the PDR. Update the SEMP to include technical details through launch. Include things as spare required, environmental testing required, Ground/Flight Support Equipment etc. An updated Project Plan has been baselined, which includes a bottoms-up cost estimate and schedule. An independent cost estimate by the GRC Cost & Economic Analysis Office has been performed and is in-line with the project plan.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p22,1,Updates to the SEMP will be made regularly as more information becomes available to the project team. The SEMP is a living document that will be revisited as necessary.,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p23,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 28 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR RFA-008 Disposition RFA # 08 Originator: Terry O’Malley Originator Concurrence: 25 October 2013 Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale The ACME Engineering requirements document is incomplete. While it appears that the Science requirements have been thoroughly addressed, carrier requirements (both CIR & ISS) and safety and mission assurance have not been addressed in the ACME ERD. It is important to thoroughly identify all requirements which are imposed on the system and subsequently must be verified. A related concern is the number of requirements that must be verified. There are a large number of requirements on the system and the verification program could be extensive. Complete the ERD. This should be done within 6 months of the PDR to ensure the CDR design meets all requirements that are levied on the system. Environmental testing requirements should be included as part of this, waivers for fragile equipment should be identified early. When developing the verification program look for ways to combine & streamline the verification process. The document FCF-ICD-CIR-ACME addresses carrier requirements (both CIR and ISS). ACME-REQ-001 points there.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p23,1,"Safety and Mission Assurance requirements will be detailed in the ACME Hazards and Integrated CIR-ACME hazard reports. Following the Phase 0/1 Flight Safety Review, where these Hazard Reports are approved, the requirements will be incorporated into ACME-REQ-001. The verification process will group requirements by discipline identified to facilitate verification and validation.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p24,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 29 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR RFA-009 Disposition RFA # 09 Originator: Lisa Van Der Aar Originator Concurrence: 9 September 2013 Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale The project has done a good job of writing operational requirements, but they have not yet been vetted with the PIs. This concern stems from RDR when the PIs were assuming operational requirements that the GRC science and engineering teams were not aware of. This concern follows on from the ACME RDR-004 RFA which has not been closed out as of the PDR. It is imperative that: 1) the currently documented operational requirements are vetted with each of the PIs; 2) the engineering team talks with each of the PIs. This will ensure that both parties fullly understand what is meant by each of the requirements, and also give the engineering team a chance to uncover additional operational requirements. Close out the RFA ACME RDR-004. The Project Scientist, Dennis Stocker, has vetted the operations requirements documented in ACME-SRD-001 with the respective Pis. Dennis has also discussed the Ops requirements with the ACME engineering team to be sure they are well understood. The ERD revision including the updates with the operations requirements has been completed and released. This information should be sufficient to close RFA ACME-RDR-004.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p25,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 30 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR BRE RFA1 Disposition BRE RFA #1 Originator: Roger Schwarz Originator Concurrence: Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale The BRE Science Requirements, which will be incorporated in the ACME Integrated Science Document Revision B, were presented in a draft version (non-signed) at the review. While a draft (ready for signature) ISRD is applicable at RDR, by PDR the ISRD should be signed as it baselines the science requirements. Additional changes and additions were discussed at the review, and these changes could affect the BRE burner and radiometer design requirements. Expedite approval of the ACME Revision B Integrated Science Requirements Document, including any changes from RDR. Any delay could impact the engineering effort to design the burner and radiometer components of the BRE. The BRE Science Requirements documented in the Integrated Science Requirements Document have been baselined.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p26,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 31 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR BRE RFA2 Disposition BRE RFA 2 Originator: Scott Darpel Originator Concurrence: Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale Although this is specifically targeted at BRE, the ACME project in general is lacking a completed SAR matrix which is required at PDR. The project SEMP states that the project will not have its own SMAP, but will follow ZIN’s P30012. P30012 is being replace with the newer P30020, but in either case, following each document requires the individual projects to complete an applicability matrix which details what S&MA requirements they plan to fully meet, or those they intend to partially meet or feel are not applicable, along with a rationale The project is reported to be working on complying with P30020. BRE and all of ACME has to get approved and signed their SMAP applicability matrix. The ACME SAR Checklist has been completed",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p27,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 32 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR BRE RFA3 Disposition BRE RFA 3 Originator: Scott Darpel, Roger Schwarz Originator Concurrence: Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale The charge for the engineering board, as communicated via the convening memo, included an action to evaluate the project’s performance vs. plan on budget and schedule. The presentation package did not include sufficient information that would allow the board to make this evaluation. Specifically, as BRE is a late addition to the ACME project, the board is interested to learn what additional risks to budget and schedule were incurred. Provide the members of the engineering board with information that communicates the overall performance against plan with respect to budget and schedule. Specifically, the board would like to see what the addition of BRE did to the overall cost and schedule to ACME. The information should make it clear what risks may or may not have been levied upon the ACME project with the late inclusion of the BRE. Data to be provided by the ACME CDR",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p28,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 33 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies PDR BRE RFA4 Disposition BRE RFA 4 Originator: Nancy Hall Originator Concurrence: Statement of Concern Recommended Action Closure Rationale ACME project risk due to addition of the BRE experiment was not presented at PDR. The engineering chair was later given access to RMIT and the engineering panel reviewed the ACME risk status. . There is a risk covering the BRE science requirements, but the risk mitigation does not include details of the project’s forward plan to meet the BRE requirements at an acceptable level of risk and does not indicate that BRE requirements are not finalized Update the BRE risk to include mitigation steps to address changes to the BRE science requirements The risk regarding BRE science requirements was updated to reflect the desired information and has since been closed with the delivery of the BRE science requirements. (Risk ACME-022)",,False
