PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p145,0,ACME Critical Design Review– 150 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Structural Analysis – Open Work • Avionics Package – Crew Loading • Similar overall dimensions to MDCA • Stiffer Structure should produce positive margins of safety,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p146,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 151 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Depress / Repress • Unless part of a pressurized system, all volumes are designed to adequately vent due to station / vehicle depressurization • Vented Containers will be verified by the stored energy method (SDP STD-ACME-1 17.a) where the volume has a stored energy < 3,063 ft- lbs. Volumes with a stored energy > 3,063 ft-lbs will have a depress rate calculated. • Depress rates are calculated* for each volume to show that the volume’s depress rate is greater than maximum station / vehicle depress rate so no pressure load is generated • Maximum depress rate is defined in section 3.1.1.2.1.5 part B of SSP- 50835 Rev C as 13.3kPa/s (1.93psi/sec) *AIAA Journal, October 1986, Vol. 24, No. 10, pg 1709-1711, “Formulas for Venting or Charging Gas from a Single Volume”, H.T. Yang",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p147,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 152 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Rotating Equipment / Fracture Control • Rotating Machinery consists of linear and rotating motors • All Rotating Machinery is shown to have low kinetic energy significantly less than the fracture critical limit of 14,240 ft-lbs • Fracture Control is implemented on all safety critical hardware using the requirements defined in NASA- STD-5019 • All pressurized components will be non-fracture critical using the disposition of Non Hazardous Leak Before Burst NHLBB • All safety critical fasteners will be non-fracture critical using the failsafe disposition.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p148,0,ACME Critical Design Review– 153 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Thermal Analysis Bill Dial – Thermal Analyst,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p149,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 154 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Thermal Analysis • Safety Requirements - Touch Temperature less than 45oC • SSP 51700, 220, 2d. • FCF-IDD-CIR-ACME, 14.2.0 • Facility Requirements • FCF-IDD-CIR-ACME, 5.1.1.3.1 The UML/PIL cooling is limited to a flow rate of 1.4m3/min. • FCF-IDD-CIR-ACME, 5.1.1.3.2 The UML/PIL air inlet has a temperature range of 20-30oC. • FCF-IDD-CIR-ACME, 5.1.1.3.3 The UML/PIL mounted component shall have a flow impedance that yields 100±12Pa. • FCF-IDD-CIR-ACME, 5.6.4 The cooling water return shall be less than 49oC • Mission Success • GLPR 7120.5.30, 3.7.4. Thermal Analysis Requirements Thermal Analysis Report: ACME-ANA-003 (DRAFT)",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p150,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 155 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Thermal Analysis • There are three primary packages analyzed • Insert Assembly, S1101MFA1000 • Camera Package, S1101MFA2000 • Avionics Package, S1101MFA3000 • COTS Parts - PC104 Stack & Other COTS PCBs • Operational temperatures are based on ambient environment in 1g. Verify the forced air is equal to or greater than free convection. • Custom Electronic Components • Calculate the specific temperatures on these components to verify operating conditions are acceptable. • Thermal Uncertainty Margin • Use SMC-TR-06-11 (MIL-STD-1540) as a guideline; thermal margin on components is 11oC Thermal Analysis Approach",,False
