PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p207,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 212 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME SAR Checklist • The ACME SAR Checklist is ACME-PLN-011 and customizes the requirements from GLPR 7120.5.30, Space Assurance Requirements, to the scale and needs of the ACME project. • This document was created with consultation from ZIN Quality, the NASA Safety and Mission Assurance Office and with help from the Chief Engineer’s Office and either accepts requirements as applicable or provides justification as to why they are not. • Requirements deemed Not Applicable include those that require long term trend analysis, requirements for payloads exposed directly to space and those relating to reflight hardware. • Refer to ACME-PLN-011 for more information. These requirements are also included in the ACME Verification Tracking Log.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p208,0,ACME Critical Design Review– 213 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Risk Management •SpaceDOC Risk Management Plan P30015 •GRC RMIT Risk Management Database •Utilized to assist in the continuous risk management process. •Online input/processing/reporting of risk information. •Risk Mgmt Working Group meets monthly to perform risk management activities. •ACME Systems Lead supports this meeting. • 26 ACME Risks have been identified to this point in time (24 closed) • 2 Active Technical Risks • 14 Closed Technical Risks • 5 Closed Schedule Risks • 1 Closed Cost Risk • 3 Closed Management Risks • 1 Closed Safety Risk,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p209,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 214 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Risk Summary • Risk ID: ACME-014 • Risk Title: IPSU to IOP image transfer rates take too long • Risk Statement: Given that the current data transfer rates from the IPSU to the IOP is severely limited, transfer of ACME data may take an unacceptable amount of time and may reduce obtainable science for the allotted operational time on board ISS. • Likelihood: 4 • Consequence: 3 • Approach: Watch. Need to keep an eye on this and follow up with the CIR team to keep updated on transfer improvements. • Latest Status: 09/17/13 - The IPSU-G is going to have their PDR September 26th 2013. The IPSU-G will eliminate the need to transfer image data from an IPSU to the IOP for downlink because the IPSU-G will be able to directly downlink image data to the ground. A trigger for this risk would be the cancellation of the IPSU-G project that would require ACME to mitigate, accept, etc...",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p210,0,ACME Critical Design Review– 215 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Risk Summary • Risk ID: ACME-026 • Risk Title: ACME E-Filed EMI exceedences impact science • Risk Statement: Given that the current ACME design exceeds the carrier EMI requirement; then there is the possibility that design changes made to meet this EMI requirement causes a loss of science. • Likelihood: 2 • Consequence: 3 • Approach & Latest Status: Watch. 10/22/13 Report received and the team is waiting to perform EMI testing on the flight system.,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p211,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 216 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Product Assurance ZIN Product Assurance will be involved extensively with the ACME Investigation verification process. Product Assurance overview will consist of: Inspections Closing MWO’s Witnessing tests Performing Mandatory Inspection Points (MIPs) Signing drawings and documents Problem reports, failures, non-conformances, and corrective actions are captured through conformance with the ZIN QMS using the non-conformance and corrective action processes. ZIN Technologies, Inc maintains compliance to the AS9100 Aerospace Standard.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p212,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 217 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Software Product Assurance ACME Software Product Assurance Plan, ACME-PLN-005 •Review of software documentation. •Peer reviews as needed for ACME S/W life cycle activities. •S/W audits. •Oversight on ACME S/W verifications. •Nonconformance Reporting & Corrective Action Process – S/W issues recorded in SpaceDOC NCR system. •Support of software development lifecycle milestone activities (PDR, CDR, SAR, etc.).",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p213,0,ACME Critical Design Review– 218 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Reliability Analysis Results documented in ACME-ANA-001 Failures are mitigated using ORU’s and components that are known to have a high reliability. There are no critical components based on a redundancy screen assessment. Failures would be immediately apparent. Mitigations outlined. Reliability assessed to be sufficient to obtain required success criteria. Approximately 61 hours of required test point operation (not including startup/warm up times) Risk of non-ORU component failure within this period of time is minimal,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p214,0,ACME Critical Design Review– 219 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME ACME Safety Wayne Borrelli – ACME Safety/Quality Engineer,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p215,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 220 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Flight Safety •ACME Flight Phase 0/1 Flight Safety Data Package reviewed and approved by PSRP. All hazard reports signed. • Phase II FSDP Submittal to PSRP planned for 12/29/2013. • ACME Phase II Flight Safety Review is scheduled for February 12 & 13, 2014. • ACME PSE is currently David Cho",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p216,0,ACME Critical Design Review– 221 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports STD-ACME-1: JSC Form ISS_OE_1298 Payload Standard Hazard Control Report ACME-2: Leakage of Water Cooling System ACME-3: Rupture of Pressurized Systems ACME-4: Crew Exposure to Broken Materials ACME-5: High Oxygen Concentration Results in Fire ACME-6: Electrical Shock,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p217,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 222 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports •Flammable Materials – Review & approval of MIUL/MUAs utilizing MAPTIS, and flammability assessment. •Materials Offgassing – Review & approval of MIUL/MUAs utilizing MAPTIS, and offgas assessment. •Mechanical Hazards (Sharp Edges, Corners, and/or Protrusions) – Verify ACME hardware conforms to the approved drawings, including requirements to remove sharp corners and protrusions. •Touch Temperature – Perform Thermal analysis and testing of the ACME hardware to prove the maximum temperature ACME hardware reaches (worst case experiment), and cool down time needed to reach below 45 degrees C. •Shatterable Material Release – All optical glass components, radiometer lenses and photomultiplier tube optics will be vibration tested. All optical glass components will be packaged in clear plastic bags prior to flight, and inspected before removal. JSC Form ISS_OE_1298",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p218,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 223 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports •Nonionizing Radiation - Perform EMI/EMC testing using the test methods/procedures defined in SSP 30237 and SSP 30243, and prepare a report confirming requirements have been met. •Lasers – N/A: ACME hardware contains no lasers. •Noise Exposure – Perform acoustic testing per SSP 50021 to prove ACME hardware will not create noise exposure hazard causing damage to hardware, injury to crew, or interfere with communications or caution/alarm detection. •Battery Failure – Perform battery loaded / open circuit voltage measurements and leakage check under vacuum to DNA battery. Perform functional check of Lippert Board Assembly (with soldered button battery) and visual inspection of Lippert Board battery. •Capacitors used as energy storage devices - ACME does not utilize asymmetric, lithium-based or free electrolyte capacitors as energy storage devices. JSC Form ISS_OE_1298",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p219,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 224 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports •Electrical Power Distribution - Perform analysis of the ACME electrical design to verify that proper circuit protective devices and wire sizing are implemented, proper bonding and proper grounding and prepare a report confirming that ISS requirements from SSP 30240 and 30245 have been met. Perform inspection to verify that the as-built electrical circuitry is in accordance with approved drawings and parts lists. •Mating/demating Powered Connectors – Inspect hardware for design features that completely enclose or shroud pins. Inspect connectors for powered side terminating in sockets rather than pins. Inspect crew procedure inputs to ensure that a step is place to remove power from the CIR experiment power connector prior to mate/demate of ACME connectors. JSC Form ISS_OE_1298",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p220,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 225 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports •Rotating Equipment – Inspect drawings and hardware to ensure rotating equipment is contained. Certify that rotating parts do not exceed 8 inches in diameter and 8000 revolutions per minute. •Interference with Translation Paths – Perform analysis to verify that ACME hardware does not impede emergency IVA egress. •Structural Failure – Perform a structural analysis, and generate a Fracture Control Plan and a Structural Verification Plan, including analysis of kick loads and 0.2 g reboost. Inspect final flight stowage configuration. •Structural Failure of Sealed Containers – Perform an analysis of all sealed containers to insure that they do not contain hazardous substances. Analyze all sealed containers to ensure they contain less than 14,240 foot pounds of stored pressurized energy and can withstand a maximum delta pressure of 1.5 atm. JSC Form ISS_OE_1298",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p221,0,ACME Critical Design Review– 226 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports •Structural Failure of Vented Containers – Perform an analysis that demonstrates that the vented containers provide an internal to effective vent area ratio that results in a differential pressure loading of no greater than 0.01 psid. Perform inspection to insure that the volume and vent area are in accordance with the above analysis. JSC Form ISS_OE_1298,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p222,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 227 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports Title: Leakage of Water Cooling System Description: Leaks in the ACME Chamber Insert Assembly water lines results in damage to hardware or inadvertently contaminates the vacuum exhaust system. Excessive cooling water leakage can cause an automatic shut down of the ISS ITCS, a must work system. Controls: 1.Design to appropriate factors of safety. 2.Build in accordance with approved drawings and assemble per approved procedures. 3.Install accumulators, where necessary. 4.Utilize crew procedures to ensure proper assembly. 5.Utilize redundant seals in all quick disconnects. Unique Hazard Report ACME-2",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p223,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 228 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports Title: Rupture of Pressurized Systems Description: Rupture of pressurized system hardware results in injury to crewmembers or damage to lab module. Controls: 1.Design to appropriate factors of safety. 2.Build in accordance with approved drawings and assemble per approved procedures. 3.Install accumulators, where necessary. 4.Materials selected to avoid susceptibility to stress corrosion cracking in accordance with MAPTIS database. Unique Hazard Report ACME-3",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p224,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 229 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports Title: Crew Exposure to Broken Materials Description: Crew exposed to fragments of ceramic and silicon- carbide materials. Controls: 1.Igniter tips, fiber arrays, burner insulators, E-Field Mesh Insulator, and E-Field Power Supply Insulator will be designed to a Factor of Safety of 2.0. 2.Igniter tips, fiber arrays, burner insulators, E-Field Mesh Insulator, and E-Field Power Supply Insulator will be bagged for stowage. Crew procedures will include steps for inspection of hardware prior to removal from clear plastic bags for installation. Unique Hazard Report ACME-4",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p225,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 230 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports Title: High Oxygen Concentration Results in Fire Description: Fire resulting from material incompatibility with high oxygen concentrations. Note: Current Science Requirements specify an 85% oxygen bottle for Inverse Flame experiments. Controls: 1.All CIA materials will be analyzed and selected for compatibility with up to 40% oxygen. 2.To compensate for the lack of flammability test data of some materials over 30% oxygen levels, non-metallics used inside the chamber will be assessed in the final configuration per JSC 29353 (to minimize ignition sources and flame propagation paths). Unique Hazard Report ACME-5",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p226,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 231 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports 3. CIR solenoid valve SV5 is nominally closed and prevents flow of oxygen to ACME. CIR Ground Procedure will verify that, when 85% oxygen bottle is in use, the CIR chamber pressure is above 14.0 psia before a command is issued to open solenoid valve SV5. 4. ACME solenoid valves (SV A1, SV A4 and SV A5) are nominally closed and prevent flow of oxygen into CIR chamber. ACME Ground Procedure will verify that, when 85% oxygen bottle is in use, the CIR chamber pressure is above 14.0 psia before a command is issued to open any of solenoid valves SV A1, SV A4, or SV A5. 5.ACME Pressure Switch (PSA1) inhibits ignition in the chamber until a chamber pressure of 14.0 psia is reached. 6.Materials in high O2 areas of the gas system shall be selected for high O2 environment per the MAPTIS database, or if required, an MUA will be written and approved by GRC PPAD per M&P Intercenter agreement. Unique Hazard Report ACME-5",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p227,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 232 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports Title: Electrical Shock Description: Incidental contact by the crew with high voltage of +/- 10KVDC can lead to crew injury. Controls: 1.Proper design of the ACME power distribution system, including compliance with bonding/grounding requirements. 2.Provide two bond paths to ground as required by letter MA2-99-142. 3.Provide electrical isolation as required by SSP 57000. Unique Hazard Report ACME-6",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p228,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 233 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Phase II Hazard Reports 4. Utilize connectors of scoop-proof design that completely encloses the pin/socket contacts. Powered side of all ACME cable connectors above 32 VDC are terminated in sockets to provide protection against debris/inadvertent shorting. To prevent mis-mating, all connectors are of different sizes and/or keying. All connectors will be labeled. 5. At least one verifiable upstream inhibit exists. ACME will utilize the CIR Rack Power Switch (RPS) as its upstream inhibit. 6. Manufacturing, assembly processes, and fabrication shall be performed In accordance with approved drawings and procedures. 7. High Voltage circuitry will be designed with resistor divider networks that will discharge the high voltage signals within one second (both positive and negative voltages) to chassis ground. 8. Wire size derating analysis will be performed per Interpretation Letter TA-92-038. Unique Hazard Report ACME-6",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p229,0,ACME Critical Design Review– 234 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies CIR/ACME Integrated Safety Clarise Shinn – FCF Safety Engineer ACME,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p230,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 235 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies CIR/ACME Integrated Flight Safety • CIR/ACME Integrated Phase 0/1 Flight Safety Data Package submitted to the PSRP, December 2011 • CIR/ACME Integrated Phase 0/I Flight Safety Review, February 2012 • All Phase 0/I integrated hazard reports are approved by the PSRP • Safety Data Package was approved by the PSRP • No Action Items or Agreements from Phase 0/I Review • Phase II Safety Data Submittal to PSRP planned for 12/29/2013. • CIR/ACME Integrated Phase II Flight Safety Review is scheduled with ACME Phase II Review on February 12 & 13, 2014.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p231,0,ACME Critical Design Review– 236 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Integrated Phase II Hazard Reports FCF-ACME- 01 Release of Adsorber Cartridge Contents into Habitable Volume FCF-ACME- 02 Exposure of Crew to Toxic Byproducts During Chamber Operations FCF-ACME- 03 Overpressurization of CIR Pressurized System by ISS Nitrogen Supply FCF-ACME- 04 Adiabatic Combustion Ruptures CIR Combustion Chamber or CIR Exhaust Vent Package during ACME experiments (except for the Inverse Flame Experiment) FCF-ACME- 05 Adiabatic Combustion Ruptures CIR Combustion Chamber or CIR Exhaust Vent Package during ACME Inverse Flame Experiment,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p232,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 237 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Integrated Phase II Hazard Reports Title: Release of Adsorber Cartridge Contents into Habitable Volume Description: Generation of (toxicity hazard level = 0, see HMST) particles due to launch loads and subsequent release into habitable volume during Adsorber Cartridge replacement results in injury to crew Controls: 1. The Adsorber Cartridge is designed with a 40 micron sintered metal filter, which is smaller than the grain size (1680 microns) of chemicals, located at both the inlet and outlet ports, to keep particles from escaping. 2. The Adsorber Cartridge is designed so that each end of the Adsorber Cartridge has redundant seals QDs and a manual valve which seals the Adsorber Cartridge for removal. 3. Constituents of Adsorber Cartridge are limited to Silica Gel or Molecular Sieve for ACME. FCF-ACME-01",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p233,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 238 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Integrated Phase II Hazard Reports Title: Exposure of Crew to Toxic Byproducts During Chamber Operations Description: Carcinogenic soot particles from the combustion chamber contaminate the habitable atmosphere causing crew illness if inhaled or floating particles could cause injury to the eyes. Controls: 1. The soot production for ACME experiments will be below the SMAC limit and does not exceed Tox 0. 2. The chamber air will be circulated through a tested Adsorber Cartridge for 15 minutes until free floating soot is removed, prior to venting. 3. The chemicals selected for the Adsorber Cartridge will remove the soot and combustion by-products. 4. Each Adsorber Cartridge shall have a content label and serial number. 5. Approved crew procedures shall be used for installation and removal of an Adsorber Cartridge. (TBR) (This control is also covered in CIR Hazard Report, FCF-C05 Inadvertent Release of Toxic Fuels and Combustion By-products and CIR Hazard Report, FCF-C28 Inadvertent Venting of High Oxygen, Pre-mixed Gases or Fuel through ISS Vent) FCF-ACME-02",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p234,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 239 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Integrated Phase II Hazard Reports FCF-ACME-03 Title: Overpressurization of CIR pressurized system by ISS Nitrogen Supply Description: Overpressurization of the CIR pressurized system by the ISS nitrogen supply via the CIR nitrogen bypass line results in rupture of CIR hardware, damage to ISS hardware or injury to the crew. Controls: 1. The ACME nitrogen pressure system design will include a pressure relief valve for the CIR nitrogen bypass line, and will be set at 135 psia. Note: CIR provides the additional hazard controls for overpressurization due to the ISS nitrogen gas supply. See CIR Hazard Report FCF-C10, hazard controls, 3.4, 3.5, 3.6 and 3.8.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p235,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 240 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Integrated Phase II Hazard Reports FCF-ACME-04 Title: Adiabatic Combustion Ruptures CIR Combustion Chamber or CIR Exhaust Vent Package during ACME experiments (except for the Inverse Flame Experiment) Description: Rapid overpressurization during the combustion of ACME gaseous fuel and oxidizer/diluent exceeds the MDP of the CIR Combustion chamber and/or exhaust vent package resulting in damage to CIR or ISS hardware or injury to the crew. Note (on hazard report): 1. This hazard report works in conjunction with CIR hazard report FCF-C07, “Adiabatic Combustion ruptures the chamber walls, causing damage to station and/or injury to crew.“ Overpressurization due to combustion will be controlled by limiting the fuel volume in each gas bottle. The CIR Valve Timers will be placed in the fuel mode. The chamber will be pre-filled with gaseous oxidizer and gaseous fuel will flow through the ACME burner. 2. CIR is responsible for filling the experiment gas bottles. The ACME detonation analyses determine the limits on fuel volumes in each bottle. The fuel gas volumes will be chosen such that even if the entire contents of a bottle were to be released into the CIR Combustion Chamber, any adiabatic combustion would result in a pressure less than the MDP of the CIR Combustion chamber. The MDP of the CIR chamber is 135 psia. 3.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p235,1,The CIR bottle leakage hazard controls and verifications are covered in CIR hazard report FCF-C08 Leakage of Pressurized System. 4. The CIR system rupture hazard controls and verifications are covered in CIR hazard report FCF-C10 Rupture of Pressurized Systems.,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p236,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 241 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Integrated Phase II Hazard Reports FCF-ACME-04 Controls: 1. Fuel volume in each gas bottle will be limited so that adiabatic combustion of the total fuel bottle volume at the worst-case chamber environment cannot exceed the MDP of the chamber. 2. The chamber atmosphere will be vented between fuel gas bottle changeouts. 3. Procedures for gas bottle ground handling and filling will prevent filling a bottle with the incorrect constituent, or the incorrect volume or pressure. 4. The crew procedure steps will require the crew to confirm the fuel bottle serial number and contents with POIC after installation. 5. An ACME pressure sensitive switch will inhibit the ACME igniter if the ACME maximum operating pressure is exceeded.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p237,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 242 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Integrated Phase II Hazard Reports FCF-ACME-05 Title: Adiabatic Combustion Ruptures CIR Combustion Chamber or CIR Exhaust Vent Package during ACME Inverse Flame Experiment Description: Rapid overpressurization during the combustion of ACME gaseous fuel and oxidizer/diluent exceeds the MDP of the CIR Combustion chamber and/or exhaust vent package resulting in damage to CIR or ISS hardware or injury to the crew. Note (on hazard report) : 1. This hazard report works in conjunction with CIR hazard report FCF-C07, “Adiabatic Combustion ruptures the chamber walls, causing damage to station and/or injury to crew.“ Overpressurizaton due to combustion will be controlled by limiting the oxygen volume in each gas bottle. The CIR Valve Timers will be placed in the oxygen mode. The chamber will be pre-filled with gaseous fuel and gaseous oxygen will flow through the ACME burner. 2. CIR is responsible for filling the experiment gas bottles. The ACME detonation analyses determine the limits on oxygen volumes in each bottle. The oxygen gas volumes will be chosen such that even if the entire contents of a bottle were to be released into the CIR Combustion Chamber, any adiabatic combustion would result in a pressure less than the MDP of the CIR Combustion chamber. The MDP of the CIR chamber is 135 psia. 3.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p237,1,The CIR bottle leakage hazard controls and verifications are covered in CIR hazard report FCF- C08 Leakage of Pressurized System. 4. The CIR system rupture hazard controls and verifications are covered in CIR hazard report FCF- C10 Rupture of Pressurized Systems.,,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p238,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 243 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies Integrated Phase II Hazard Reports FCF-ACME-05 Controls: 1. Oxygen volume in each gas bottle will be limited so that adiabatic combustion of the total fuel bottle volume at the worst-case chamber environment cannot exceed the MDP of the chamber. 2. The chamber atmosphere will be vented between oxygen gas bottle changeouts. 3. Procedures for gas bottle ground handling and filling will prevent filling a bottle with the incorrect constituent, or the incorrect volume or pressure. 4. The crew procedure steps will require the crew to confirm the oxygen bottle serial number and contents with POIC after installation. 5. An ACME pressure sensitive switch will inhibit the ACME igniter if the ACME maximum operating pressure is exceeded.",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p239,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 244 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Readiness • All project requirements are documented and agreed to • ACME design reflects complete compliance with the project requirements (detailed in ACME-DOC-002D) • Drawing status (complete/total): •CIA – 295/375 •AVP – 92/92 •Data Camera – 93/109 •Total – 480/576 83% •222 Flight drawing ECOs have been processed as a result of EM build and testing • Engineering Model Build: AVP complete, CIA and Data Camera underway",,False
PSI-23,Engineering Documents,PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Engineering Documents/PSI-23_Engineering Documents_ACME CDR_Presentation_Package.pdf,p240,0,"ACME Critical Design Review– 245 Glenn Research Center ZIN Technologies ACME Readiness • Flight Model Build: Build on all 3 major packages has started for low risk items • Build documents and test plans have been generated • Functional, validation and verification test planning is complete spanning all levels of the build • All analyses (structural, thermal, reliability, materials, etc.) show no major issues with ACME design • Risks are identified and managed • Carrier integration has already begun and will continue per the integration schedule • All hazards have sufficient controls • Project planning shows adequate technical margins and resources exist to complete the project within budget and schedule",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p1,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 1 Gaseous Non-Premixed Flame Research Planned for the International Space Station Spring Technical Meeting of the Central States Section of The Combustion Institute Mar 16–18, 2014 Dennis P. Stocker1, Fumiaki Takahashi2, J. Mark Hickman1, and Andrew C. Suttles1 1NASA Glenn Research Center, [address removed], USA 2Case Western Reserve University, [address removed], USA",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p2,0,National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov Outline • Background – Why microgravity? – Gaseous non-premixed flames in space • ACME – Science – Hardware – Status • Questions 2,,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p3,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov Why Microgravity? • Effective elimination of buoyancy and buoyancy- induced flicker • Spherical flames • Momentum- dominated flames at low velocities • Increased length scales • Longer residence times - good for studies of soot • Stronger flame sensitivity to atmosphere • Studies of limit and stability behavior, where chemical kinetics dominate • Spacecraft fire safety 3 Background > Science > Hardware > Status",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p4,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov Gaseous Non-premixed Flames in Space 3 space shuttle and 2 ISS experiments thus far • LSP: Laminar Soot Processes – PI: G.M. Faeth (U. Michigan) – Combustion Module – STS-83 and STS-94 (1997), STS-107 (2003) • TGDF: Turbulent Gas-jet Diffusion Flames – PI: M.Y. Bahadori (SAIC) – Get Away Special Canister (GAS can) – STS-87 (1997) • ELF: Enclosed Laminar Flames – PI: L.-D. Chen (U. Iowa) – Middeck Glovebox (MGBX) – STS-87 (1997) • SPICE: Smoke Point In Coflow Experiment – PI: D.L. Urban (NASA Glenn) - initially G.M. Faeth – Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) – ISS Expeditions 18-19, 30 (2009, 2012) • SLICE: Structure & Liftoff In Combustion Experiment – PI: M.B. Long (Yale U.) – Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) – ISS Expedition 30 (2012) 4 Roger Crouch with CM-1 on STS-83 (top), Michael Barratt with SPICE (bottom) ISS Space Shuttle",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p5,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 5 Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments • Conduct 5+ distinct experiments using a single modular insert for the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on the ISS – The only existing CIR insert, MDCA, is for the FLEX droplet combustion experiments, which have been collectively operating on ISS since 2009 Koichi Wakata with MDCA in CIR (left), ISS (right)",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p6,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 5 Current ACME Experiments 11 Investigators and 7 Universities (+ NASA Glenn) • BRE – Burning Rate Emulator – PIs: James G. Quintiere, Peter B. Sunderland (both U. Maryland) • CLD Flame – Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame – PIs: Marshall Long, Mitchell Smooke (both Yale U.) • E-FIELD Flames – Electric-Field Effects on Laminar Diffusion Flames – PI: Derek Dunn-Rankin (UC Irvine) • Flame Design - a novel approach to clean, efficient diffusion flames – PIs: Richard L. Axelbaum (Washington U. in St. Louis), Beei-Huan Chao (U. Hawaii), Peter B. Sunderland (U. Maryland), David L. Urban (NASA Glenn) • s-Flame – Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames – PIs: C.K. Law (Princeton U.), Stephen D. Tse (Rutgers U.), Kurt R. Sacksteder (NASA Glenn) 6 Background > Science > Hardware > Status",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p7,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 7 BRE • Burning Rate Emulator – Jim Quintiere & Peter Sunderland (both U. Maryland) • Concept – Flat-plate burner with gaseous fuel flow simulating the vaporization and combustion of condensed fuels – Heat flux to the burner is measured, where the flux vaporizes condensed-phase fuels – Fuels to be simulated include paper, plastic, alcohols • Goals – Quantify the burning and extinction behavior of condensed fuels in microgravity – Quantify the effects of increased O2 concentrations, e.g., for exploration atmospheres, on µg combustion. • Why? – Spacecraft fire prevention through improved assessment for materials selection (currently based on 1g testing, where flames can exist in µg and not in 1g) Background > Science > Hardware > Status 50-mm diam. burner C2H4 (various flows) in air at 1 atm µg flame near end of 5.18 s drop",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p8,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 8 CLD Flame • Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame – Marshall B. Long & Mitchell D. Smooke (both Yale U.) • Concept – Lifted coflow (2D) flame, so little/no heat loss to burner • enabling improved modeling of the flame • Goals – Generation of modified kinetic mechanisms for hydrocarbon fuels that are able to model effectively diffusion flame structure under a larger parameter range than existing mechanisms. – Development of submodels for soot formation that are capable of predicting both high and low soot loading levels in hydrocarbon flames of various fuels. • Why? – Improved design capability through validation of combustion models over wider parameter range Background > Science > Hardware > Status Attached 100% CH4 flame on a 1.6-mm ID burner tube, with soot temperature, soot volume fraction below.",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p9,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 9 E-FIELD Flames • Electric-Field Effects on Laminar Diffusion Flames – Derek Dunn-Rankin (UC Irvine) • Concept – Gas-jet or coflow (2D) flame with high-voltage mesh (up to ±10 kV) electrode above (downstream of) burner • Goal – Understand chemi-ionization behavior and the resulting ion driven winds sufficiently well so that electrical properties of flames can be used to characterize (by monitoring ion current) and control them (via direct chemical or local convective influences). • Why? – Advanced control capability enabling improved combustion performance 1g, 0 kV, gas-jet 0g, -0.6 kV, gas-jet 0g, 0kv, 24 cm/s coflow Background > Science > Hardware > Status",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p10,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov Flame Design • A novel approach to clean, efficient diffusion flames – Rich Axelbaum (Washington U., St. Louis), Beei- Huan Chao (U. Hawaii), Peter Sunderland (U. Maryland), David L. Urban (NASA Glenn) • Concept – Spherical (1D) flame, normal and inverse flow config. • Goals – Evaluate the effects of flame structure on soot inception and flame extinction – Obtain a correlation of sooting limits with stoichiometric mixture fraction, Zst, and adiabatic temperature, Tad – Determine the importance of gas-phase oxidation on soot inception for high Zst flames – Demonstrate the existence of steady spherical flames – Identify pseudo-flammability limits for diffusion flames in terms of Zst and Tad • Why? – To reduce soot and NOx through nitrogen exchange Background > Science > Hardware > Status 18% C2H4 into 27% O2 18% C2H4 Into 28% O2 100% O2 into 12% C2H4 100% O2 into 13% C2H4 Inverse flames Normal flames 10",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p11,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 11 s-Flame • Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames – C.K. Law (Princeton U.), Stephen D. Tse (Rutgers U.), Kurt R. Sacksteder (NASA Glenn) • Concept – Spherical (1D) flame stabilized on a porous burner • Goals – Use spherical diffusion flames in ISS environment to: • obtain experimental data in simple, well-defined flow fields • understand various key elemental diffusion flame processes – Characterize transient structure, determine extinction limits, and check existence of theoretically predicted pulsating instabilities. – Validate and improve accuracy of computational simulation including detailed chemistry and transport • Why? – Improvements in the design of practical, high-efficiency, low- emission combustion systems on Earth flame evolution with time Background > Science > Hardware > Status",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p12,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov ACME Approach • ACME is not a technology demonstration, but is seeking to improve life on Earth via the path below. 12 Improved understanding of combustion fundamentals Improved numerical models of combustion processes Improved design tools Improved practical combustion devices ACME from ACME Background > Science > Hardware > Status Numerical prediction of flame structure [Yale U.]",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p13,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov Similarities of Current Experiments • Gaseous flame – fuel is a gas, e.g., methane and ethylene • Non-premixed (i.e., diffusion), flame – fuel and oxidizer (e.g., air) are on opposite sides of the reaction sheet – but premixed flames can be studied with ACME • Laminar flame – flow is smooth and not turbulent (i.e., w/o vortices) – but turbulent flames can be studied with ACME • 1D or 2D flame – but 3D flames can be studied with ACME 13 F O2 O2 O2 O2 O2 F F F F Normal flame Inverse flame Background > Science > Hardware > Status",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p14,0,National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 14 ACME Hardware avionics package digital color camera Background > Science > Hardware > Status modular insert burners gas bottles and filters,,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p15,0,National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov ACME Insert Background > Science > Hardware > Status 15,,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p16,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov • Delivers 3 gases from CIR to the chamber or burner • fuel (2 slpm max, N2 basis) • oxygen/inert mix or diluent (e.g., He or CO2) • nitrogen (from ISS) • Mass flow controllers (crew swappable to vary flow ranges) Gas Flow System Background > Science > Hardware > Status 16",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p17,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov Burners & Igniter • Four types of burners are planned for the first 5 experiments: • gas-jet burners (7) 0.4, 0.8, 1.3, 1.6, 2.1, 2.7 and 3.2 mm ID • spherical burners (3) 0.25, 0.375, 0.5 inch diameter, porous sintered metal • coflow burner (1) 2.1-mm ID inner tube, 25-mm ID outer tube • BRE burners (2) 25, 50 mm diameter, porous (w/ 2 heat flux transducers each) • Thermocouple for surface temperature on most burners • Burners will generally be electrically isolated • Hot-wire igniter, retracting, similar to that in use for MDCA • Igniter tips are replaceable, e.g., to match the burner type/size Background > Science > Hardware > Status Gas-jet, spherical, coflow, and BRE burners (left to right) Igniter, hot-wire 17",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p18,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov Electric Field • ±10kV at copper mesh above (i.e., downstream of) isolated burner • Ion current through flame is determined from Ohm’s law (I=V/R) and measurement of the voltage difference across a shunt resistor (in series with the flame) • copper mesh screen (removable) • high-voltage source (removable) • passive electronics (fixed) H.V. Chamber Nozzle Mesh electrode Shunt for current measurement, Rs Low voltage input, e.g., 0-24VDC Gas supply tubes Insulator Coflow burner with flame Electrode mesh Background > Science > Hardware > Status 18",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p19,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 19 Basic Imaging • Color Imaging for Ops – Analog color camera (removable) with 45-degree turning mirror – LEDs for optional illumination • Color Imaging for Data – Digital GigE color camera, 1.4 megapixel, 12 bit, 30 fps with motorized control of zoom, iris, and focus – 3 optional filters including a blue-green BG-7 filter (to balance light intensity for pyrometry), 430 and 450-nm for CH* (incl. soot subtraction) • Ultraviolet (UV) Imaging – Existing LLL-UV camera equipped with a 310-nm filter for imaging OH* – Field of view (FOV) options: 50-mm and 80-mm diameter Background > Science > Hardware > Status Color Camera Motorized Zoom Lens Electronics Enclosure 45° Fold Mirror Filters (swap via rotating barrel)",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p20,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 20 Advanced Imaging • Pyrometry for Hot Sooty and Soot-Free Regions – Determine temperature distribution from broadband thermal radiation, where the spectrum is indicative of the temp. using (1) GigE camera and/or (2) HiBMs camera with a liquid-crystal tunable filter. – Thin-Filament Pyrometry (TFP) to determine temperature profiles from the broadband thermal radiation. Array of 15-micron oxidized SiC fibers stretched across the flame with motor to allow for scanning or removal of the fibers from the flame. Alternate array includes a Pt wire for absolute light calibration. – HiBMs FOV options: 30 and 50-mm dia. • Soot Volume Fraction – Via (1) GigE camera or (2) CIR illumination package (using a 653-nm laser diode) and a second HiBMs camera – HiBMs FOV options: 30, 50, and 90-mm diameter TFP Soot vol. fraction HiBMs camera Soot pyrometry Background > Science > Hardware > Status High Bit-depth Multi-spectral (HiBMs)",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p21,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov Emission Measurements • Photomultiplier Tubes (PMTs) • Broadband, OH*, CH* (3 total) • Radiometers (up to 6 total) • Broadband thermal radiation via thermopile detectors • Wide and/or narrow angle FOVs • One fixed detector • An exchangeable array with 2 current options • std. array with 5 sensors • BRE array with 3 sensors Background > Science > Hardware > Status BRE Radiometer Array -10 Offset +30 Offset +60 Offset ~27° = 70 mm FOV ~27° = 70 mm FOV ~27° = 70 mm FOV Use knife-edge at the radiometer housing to block the burner from being “seen” Center 120mm FOV 30 mm Offset 2mm Spot -30 mm Offset 2mm Spot 60 mm Offset 2mm Spot -60 mm Offset 2mm Spot ~47° = 120 mm FOV ACME Radiometer Array 21",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p22,0,National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov Optical Layout 1 5 3 7 Radiometers Color Camera Single IPSU LLL-UV Stacked 2 IPSUs Illumination HiBMs (1 & 2) PMT Analog Background > Science > Hardware > Status GigE Gigabit Ethernet (color camera) HiBMs High Bit-depth Multi-spectral camera IPSU Image Processing and Storage Unit LLL-UV Low Light Level Ultraviolet camera PMT Photomultiplier Tube module R Radiometers 22,,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p23,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 23 Additional Diagnostics • Heat flux (BRE burners, on & off axis) • Temperature – Burner surface (with 0-2 TCs) – Far-field gas phase • 2 fixed thermocouples (TCs) • rake with 4 TCs is crew removable or swappable for different TC positions • Ion current (electric field tests only) • Pre- & post-test gas composition – via gas chromatograph – e.g., CO, CO2, CH4, C2H2, C2H4, N2, O2 MDCA and CIR Thermocouple rake and 2 fixed thermocouples",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p24,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 24 Monitoring Measurements • Chamber pressure • Gas flow rates • Burner pressure differential – for porous burners • Electrical potential – for tests with the electric field • Acceleration – i.e., effective gravity These measurements are required to verify boundary & initial conditions and thus enable modeling and analysis. Michael Barratt with MDCA in CIR",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p25,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 25 Nominal Operational Sequence • Flow > Ignite > Measure > Extinguish – Initiate burner flows at ignition condition – Ignite flame • Wait for flame to stabilize – If needed, adjust flow(s) to initial test condition • Wait for flame to stabilize – If desired: • increase or decrease burner flow(s) – to vary flow/velocity or concentration • activate and adjust electric field • translate TFP array – Stop burner flows • per extinction detection, test duration, etc. • Ops control can be automated or “manual” – Ground control, not astronaut operated Nicole Stott with CIR",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p26,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 26 ACME Status • Schedule • 2013 Critical Design Review (CDR) • 2014 (now) completion of detailed design for spaceflight hardware assembly of engineering models fabrication of spaceflight hardware • 2016-2019 ISS ops Mike Fossum with CIR (left), Satoshi Furukawa with CIR (right)",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p27,0,National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 27 Questions? Mike Fossum with MDCA in CIR,,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p28,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov 28 Acknowledgements • Investigators (7 universities and NASA Glenn) • Richard L. Axelbaum, Beei-Huan Chao, Derek Dunn-Rankin, C.K. Law, Marshall B. Long, James G. Quintiere, Kurt R. Sacksteder, Mitchell D. Smooke, Peter B. Sunderland, Stephen D. Tse, David L. Urban • NASA Glenn • ISS Research Program Manager Fred Kohl • ISS Research Project Manager Tom St. Onge • R&T: Microgravity Combustion David Urban • Microgravity Facilities Manager Eric Neumann • Universities Space Research Association • Imaging Jay Owens • ZIN Technologies, Inc. • ACME Lead Brian Borowski • Diagnostics Steve Lawn • Electrical/Electronics Tim Gobeli • Mechanical Adrian Drake • Software Mike Medved • Systems Chris Mroczka",,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p29,0,National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov BACKUP 29,,False
PSI-23,Presentations,PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Presentations/PSI-23_Presentations_ACME_CSSCI_20140317.pdf,p30,0,"National Aeronautics and Space Administration www.nasa.gov Why Study Combustion? • Energy – While we might associate fire with the Stone Age, we still make extensive use of combustion in our daily lives. • Electricity – about 70% in the U.S. from combustion • Heating of buildings, water, food, and in manufacturing processes • Transportation – Our reliance on imported fuel contributes to our national trade deficit and affects our national security. • Environment – Combustion is a source of greenhouse gases. – Soot contributes to global warming and is a health problem. • Fire Safety Given its pervasive use with annual U.S. fuel costs on the order of a trillion dollars even small improvements in combustion technology can significantly reduce fuel needs and pollution production! 30 Background > Science > Hardware > Status",,False
PSI-23,Experimental table,PSI-23_Experimental table_ACME s-Flame.csv,data/raw/files/PSI-23/Experimental table/PSI-23_Experimental table_ACME s-Flame.csv,all,0,sheet PSI-23_Experimental table_ACME s-Flame.csv: 19 non-empty rows header: Test Type | Test Day* | Year | Month | Date | Atmosphere* | Fuel Bottle | Flames Ignited* | Notes row 1: First round of tests with nitrogen-diluted fuel and oxygen | 19254 | 2019 | Sept. | 11 | ~21% O2 with N2 | 50% C2H4 with N2 | 13 | *Test Day is formatted YYDDD where YY identfies year and DDD identifies day of year (GMT). row 2: First round of tests with nitrogen-diluted fuel and oxygen | 19256 | 2019 | Sept. | 13 | ~21% O2 with N3 | 50% C2H4 with N3 | 16 | *Atmosphere is ~1 atm but pressure increases and O2% drops with vitiation. row 3: First round of tests with nitrogen-diluted fuel and oxygen | 19304 | 2019 | Oct. | 31 | ~21% O2 with N4 | 45% CH4 with N2 | 9 | *Flames Ignited is the number ignited on that day (neglecting the day change on ISS). row 4: First round of tests with nitrogen-diluted fuel and oxygen | 19308 | 2019 | Nov. | 4 | ~30% O2 with N2 | 45% CH4 with N3 | 21 | row 5: First round of tests with nitrogen-diluted fuel and oxygen | 19352 | 2019 | Dec. | 18 | ~21% O2 with N2 | 100% H2 | 10 |,,False
PSI-23,metadata,PSI-23_metadata_PSI-99_ACME s-Flame.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-23/metadata/PSI-23_metadata_PSI-99_ACME s-Flame.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,0,"ONTOLOGY SOURCE REFERENCE Term Source Name PSI EFO Term Source File https://psi.nasa.gov/ http://data.bioontology.org/ontologies/EFO Term Source Version 1.0 132 Term Source Description User defined terms Experimental Factor Ontology INVESTIGATION Investigation Identifier Investigation Title Investigation Description Investigation Submission Date Investigation Public Release Date INVESTIGATION PUBLICATIONS Investigation PubMed ID Investigation Publication DOI Investigation Publication Author List Investigation Publication Title Investigation Publication Status Investigation Publication Status Term Accession Number Investigation Publication Status Term Source REF INVESTIGATION CONTACTS Investigation Person Last Name Investigation Person First Name Investigation Person Mid Initials Investigation Person Email Investigation Person Phone Investigation Person Fax Investigation Person Address Investigation Person Affiliation Investigation Person Roles Investigation Person Roles Term Accession Number Investigation Person Roles Term Source REF STUDY Study Identifier PSI-23 Study Title Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments-Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames Study Description Study Submission Date 05-Apr-2018 Study Public Release Date 05-Apr-2018 Study File Name s_PSI-23.txt Comment[Acknowledgments] Comment[Approach] ""A series of experiments is proposed in which mixtures of various gaseous fuels and diluents will be issued into an oxidizing environment",,False
PSI-23,metadata,PSI-23_metadata_PSI-99_ACME s-Flame.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-23/metadata/PSI-23_metadata_PSI-99_ACME s-Flame.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,1,"through a spherical porous burner and ignited to obtain, nominally, a spherical, one-dimensional stabilized flame. Synchronized measurements are to be made including spectrally resolved images, flame and burner temperatures, radiative flux measurements, and system operating conditions (including reactant flow rates, ambient temperatures, pressures and acceleration levels, etc.). These data are to be used for comparisons of flame behavior and structure with detailed numerical simulations of the flames. Conventional gaseous fuels including hydrogen, methane, and ethylene will be used. Hydrogen is the simplest fuel and its reaction mechanism is also reasonably established. Methane is the simplest hydrocarbon and extensive studies have been conducted on its combustion chemistry. Ethylene is a typical sooty fuel and is also an important intermediate for the oxidation of higher hydrocarbons.",,False
PSI-23,metadata,PSI-23_metadata_PSI-99_ACME s-Flame.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-23/metadata/PSI-23_metadata_PSI-99_ACME s-Flame.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,2,"Diluents mixed with fuel are to include nitrogen and helium to adjust mixture diffusivities, and carbon dioxide to influence flame radiation characteristics."" Comment[DOI] 10.60555/k57n-p030 Comment[Data Source Accession] Comment[Data Source Link] Comment[Experiment Hardware] Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR), ACME Comment[Experiment Hardware Link] https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/, https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/combustion-science/acme/ Comment[Flight Platform] International Space Station (ISS) Comment[Funding] NCC3-1051, NNC06AA27A, NNX09AR03A, NNX15AC79A Comment[Hypothesis] ""The transient numerical simulation of spherical flames developed as part of this program includes detailed chemistry and transport models, especially radiation mechanisms. Each description of the flames obtained from the experiment, including measurements of aspects of the flame structure, growth or flame front velocities, and the specific test conditions where steady state flames, flame extinctions, flame front instabilities, soot suppression limits, etc., comprise a rigorous test of the detailed model. Together they provide a measure of the comprehensiveness of the chemical kinetic and transport models. The numerical simulation is also being used as a predictive tool to develop the science requirements for this experiment, especially the collection of test conditions and most aspects of the measurement systems.",,False
PSI-23,metadata,PSI-23_metadata_PSI-99_ACME s-Flame.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-23/metadata/PSI-23_metadata_PSI-99_ACME s-Flame.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,3,"Due to time and g-jitter limitations, little of the planned test matrix can be adequately explored in the ground based test facilities, and the flight experiment definition will rely on the best effort in the modeling work."" Comment[Identifiers] Comment[Investigation Acronym] ACME s-Flame Comment[Investigation End Date] 11-24-2019 Comment[Investigation Start Date] 02-05-2015 Comment[Managing NASA Center] Glenn Research Center (GRC) Comment[Mission End] Comment[Mission Link] Comment[Mission Name] Comment[Mission Start] Comment[Objective] ""A series of experiments is proposed with the following specific objectives, in both soot-free and sooty flames, to compare with detailed computational simulation to assess transport properties and detailed chemical kinetics (including chemiluminescence): Objective A. Measure and characterize the transient structure (such as temperature and species distribution) of diffusion flames established at initially non-steady, spherical flame structures for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty), for different flow rates. Objective B. Determine the quasi-steady convective/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (low system Damköhler numbers) and radiative/chemical-kinetic extinction limits (high system Damköhler numbers) for mixtures of different H2/CH4/diluent ratios (soot-free) and different C2H4/diluent ratios (sooty). Objective C.",,False
PSI-23,metadata,PSI-23_metadata_PSI-99_ACME s-Flame.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-23/metadata/PSI-23_metadata_PSI-99_ACME s-Flame.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,4,"Determine the existence (for different Zeldovich number, Ze), onset (as a function Da), and nature (i.e. mode, frequency, amplitude) of pulsating instabilities theoretically predicted to occur in transient spherical diffusion flames using fuel/diluent mixtures that are above a critical Lewis number."" Comment[Project Identifier] Comment[Project Link] Comment[Project Title] Comment[Project Type] Microgravity Investigation Comment[Proposal Title] Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames Comment[Research Area] Combustion Science Comment[Research Impacts] The impact of this work consists of both practical and fundamental aspects. Practically, with diffusion flame burning as the basis in many combustion engines and devices, understanding the underlying dynamics and chemistry of diffusion flames is important in improving performance in terms of power, efficiency, pollutant emissions, and reliability. Fundamentally, the unique buoyancy-free environment offered by microgravity affords simple aerodynamic flow fields to acquire diffusion flame data of high fidelity, with the prospect of understanding unit thermochemical processes as well as quantifying the associated physico-chemical parameters.",,False
