PSI-25,Science Documents,PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Science Documents/PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,p8,0,"8 BASS-II Science Overview PS: Dr. Paul Ferkul, NCSER PI: Dr. Sandra Olson, NASA GRC Co-PI: Prof. Subrata Bhattacharjee, SDSU Co-PI: Prof. James T’ien, CWRU Co-PI: Prof. Fletcher Miller, SDSU Co-PI: Prof. Carlos Fernandez-Pello, UCB PM: Robert Hawersaat, NASA GRC Engineering Team: ZIN Technologies, Inc.",,False
PSI-25,Science Documents,PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Science Documents/PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,p9,0,"9 BASS-II Science Overview Relevance/Impact: Burning and Suppression of Solids - II (BASS-II) tests the hypothesis that materials in microgravity, with adequate ventilation, burn as well if not better than the same material in normal gravity with other conditions being identical (pressure, oxygen concentration, temperature, etc.). Objectives: • To identify what is the worst case for material flammability in spacecraft environments, and how does that compare to the terrestrial upward burning used to screen the materials for safe use aboard spacecraft. • To determine how quickly a fire in space can grow and if the flames reach a finite size or continue to grow. This has implications for firefighting strategies in spacecraft. Primary variables: The main variables to be tested are the effects of ambient oxygen concentration, ventilation flow velocity, and fuel type, thickness, and geometry. Development Approach: BASS-II will utilize the on orbit BASS hardware launched originally for SPICE on STS-126. New samples, igniters, and more flash cards will provide new data. Crew is required to set up and operate the experiment. Video and data down-linked to the ground for evaluation.",,False
PSI-25,Science Documents,PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Science Documents/PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,p10,0,"10 BASS-II Science Overview PMMA spherical section Gaseous Nitrogen nozzle for MSG work volume vitiation BASS-II Assembly for PMMA Spherical section Igniter BASS-II Fuel Geometries (not to scale) 4-cm PMMA Spherical section (Forward stagnation region ignition and flame growth) 1 and 2-cm width fuel sheets -PMMA -different thicknesses - 1 or 2 sided burns - SIBAL fabric - Nomex- III fabric PMMA rods (black and clear), ¼”, 3/8”, and ½” diameters Airflow 2 cmIgniter Fuel 2-pin connectorFrame Alignment tab",,False
PSI-25,Science Documents,PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Science Documents/PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,p11,0,BASS-II Science Overview 11 Sample Mounting Each Solid Fuel Assembly is mounted inside the Experiment Assembly via a magnetic interface such that it can be installed and removed by the crew The top window supports the solid fuel mounting rail.,,False
PSI-25,Science Documents,PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Science Documents/PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,p12,0,"BASS-II Science Overview priority order test matrix per PI: Olson 12 BASS- II test Matrix PI: Olson Priority Test # O2 Flow(s) Sample #/type/description brief objective of test notes 1 21 5, 3, 2, 1, min 1/4"" rod, clear steady spread at condition(s) ignite at a consistent concurrent flow, then reduce to low flows and measure spread, repeat until extinguished. Determine solid response time.",,False
PSI-25,Science Documents,PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Science Documents/PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,p12,1,"2 21 5, etc 3/8"" rod, clear steady spread at condition(s) diameter effect, timescale estimate 3 16 5, 3, 2, 1 1/4"" rod, clear find low velocity limit wait at each flow for solid phase timescale 4 16 5, increase 1/4"" rod, clear find blowoff limit can increase flow every 5-10 seconds 5 15 5, decrease 1/4"" rod, clear find low velocity limit may re-use same rod for repeated tests, to preserve samples 6 15 5, increase 1/4"" rod clear find blowoff limit can increase flow every 5-10 seconds 7 17 or 14 5, decrease 1/4"" rod, clear find low velocity limit 8 17 or 14 5, increase 1/4"" rod, clear find blowoff limit can increase flow every 5-10 seconds 9 21 5, etc 1/2"" rod, clear steady spread at condition(s) diameter effect, timescale estimate 10 21 5,3,2,1, min 1/4"" rod, black steady spread at condition(s) to determine if results depend on material color, for comparisions with Pello results 11 tbd 5, decrease 3/8"" rod, clear find low velocity limit diameter effect 12 tbd 5, decrease 1/2"" rod, clear find low velocity limit diameter effect 13 tbd 5, decrease 1/4"" rod, black find low velocity limit color dependence on limit 14 tbd tbd 1/4"" rod, clear tbd possible repeat limit 15 tbd 5, decrease 3/8"" rod, clear find low velocity limit diameter effect 16 tbd 5, increase 3/8"" rod, clear find blowoff limit diameter effect 17 19 tbd 1/4"" rod, clear tbd 18 19 tbd 1/4"" rod, clear tbd 19 18 tbd 1/4"" rod, clear tbd 20 18 tbd 1/4"" rod, clear tbd Notes in the event of a non-ignition of the rod at low",,False
PSI-25,Science Documents,PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Science Documents/PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,p12,2,"O2, an opposed ignition will be attempted, followed by an ambient air opposed or concurrent test at TBD flow(s).",,False
