PSI-25,Science Documents,PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Science Documents/PSI-25_Science Documents_BASS-II_SRD.pdf,p16,0,16 BASS-II Science Overview priority order test matrix per PI: Fernandez-Pello,,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,0,"OBJECTIVE: Conduct BASS-2 (Burning and Suppression of Solids-2) operations by adding GN2 to the WV to reduce the O2 partial pressure in order to get a stable blue flame for longer periods of time. TOOLS: CSA-CP CDM Tweezers (if needed) Mini-Maglite PARTS: BASS-2 Samples per BASS-2 Test Matrix Digital Tape Case P/N 96M53038-1 1. MSG FACILITY CONFIG 1.1 ✓All operator rings, watches removed CMP 1.2 EXPERIMENT POWER OUTLETS sw OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V → ON (Hold switch in place for 1 second and manually return to center.) ✓OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V ON LED (three) On (green) 1.3 ✓FUNCTIONS AIR CIRC ON LED On (green) ✓FUNCTIONS WV ILLUM ON LED On (green) WV rear wall 1.4 ✓GN2 CLOSE () SPICE Exp. Assy 1.5 ✓FUEL OFF (toward front of WV) BASS Control Box 1.6 ✓IGN ENA OFF ✓GN2 ENA OFF ✓DATA REC OFF 1.7 Remove front MSG Glove Ring Assembly (two). 2. SAMPLE REMOVAL SPICE Exp. Assy 2.1 Release access window latches (two). Lower front lexan cover. Figure 1. - BASS Flat Sample. 2.2 If BASS flat sample installed, Disconnect ignitor wire and remove from Sample Frame Assembly [Dry Wipe] (Figure 1). Stow BASS flat sample in original packaging and trash Dry Wipe. Go to step 3 2.3 If BASS Ignitor installed, Remove BASS Ignitor (gently pull upwards). Place BASS Ignitor inside MSG WV (to preserve ignitor wire and bent configuration) [Kapton Tape]. 2.4 If BASS Sample installed, Remove BASS Sample from Sample Frame Assembly [Dry Wipe]. Remove soot from BASS Sample [Dry Wipe].",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,1,"Stow BASS Sample in original packaging and trash Dry Wipe. 3. SAMPLE INSTALLATION 3.1 ✓POIC for PI enablement, Sample Number and Test Point 3.2 Install BASS Sample X onto the Sample Frame Assembly per BASS Test Matrix . ✓Sample orientation per BASS Test Matrix Ignition Type (front or wake) 3.3 Install BASS Ignitor X (Ignitor tip towards the right so that the Ignitor leads are clear of the imaging views) onto SPICE Experiment Assembly per BASS Test Matrix. 3.4 IGNITOR lever → Pull forward ✓Ignitor wire is as close to the leading edge of the BASS Sample as possible without touching (within 1 mm). 3.5 Close front lexan cover on SPICE Experiment Assembly, engage latches. 4. STILL CAMERA SETUP Figure 2. - Still Camera. WV 4.1 ✓Still Camera Power Switch ON (Figure 2) Figure 3. - Still Camera - Rear View. 4.2 If Still Camera needs to be removed to perform camera settings: Pull and hold plunger on Still Camera. Remove Still Camera from SPICE Still Camera Mount and orient camera per Figure 3. 4.3 Date and Time set per date displayed on MSG Video Monitor 1: Press Still Camera MENU button (Figure 3). ✓SETUP MENU Screen is displayed If SETUP MENU not displayed, Using the Still Camera UP/DOWN Multi-Selector (MS) Button select the SETUP MENU Screen. Press the RIGHT MS Button to select. Using the UP/DOWN MS Button highlight TIME ZONE AND DATE. Press the RIGHT MS Button to select. Press UP/DOWN to select DATE AND TIME.",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,2,"Using the RIGHT MS Button select the desired parameter and use the UP/DOWN MS Button to change the parameter. Date should be entered as YYYY/MM/DD and Time should be current GMT as display on MSG Video Monitor 1 ±5 seconds. Press OK to accept. Press the LEFT MS Button to return to SETUP MENU Screen. Press the LEFT MS Button to access the left column menu bar. 4.4 CL Mode Shooting Speed check (1fps): Using the Still Camera UP/DOWN MS button, select CUSTOM SETTING MENU screen. pb RIGHT MS → to select sel SHOOTING/DISPLAY pb RIGHT MS → to select ✓CL MODE SHOOTING SPEED 1(fps) 4.5 File Number Sequence check ON: Using the Still Camera UP/DOWN MS Button, ✓FILE NO. SEQUENCE ON 4.6 Press MENU button until back LCD is blank. Figure 4. - Still Camera top LCD - FRAMES Available. Figure 5. - Still Camera Photo Display (Full Metadata) on Video Monitor. 4.7 Verify FRAMES Available on Microdrive = 146 (Figure 4) 4.8 Verify Full Metadata camera display mode: pb SHUTTER → Press firmly and hold for 5 seconds ✓Image data is displayed on the back LCD with the word ""RAW"" in the bottom right. (Figure 5) 4.9 If Still Camera was previously removed, Install Still Camera onto SPICE Still Camera Mount. ✓Spring Plunger fully engaged (Still Camera) 5. DARK FRAME CALIBRATION AND TEST POINT SETUP 5.1 If first test of the day, Install Still Camera lens cap. Perform steps 5.2 through 5.9 per the BASS Test Matrix, Dark Frame Calibration. Figure 7. - Still Camera - Bracket Display (Top LCD).",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,3,"5.2 pb Still Camera Fn or Pv button → Press and Hold (near bottom front of camera between grip and lens) ✓BKT display appears on Still Camera LCD (Figure 7) ✓9F on top left of display indicates 9 Frames will be taken for Bracket Sequence (Figure 7) 5.3 While pressing and holding Fn or Pv on Still Camera, Rotate Still Camera Main Command Dial (top left of camera while mounted, Figure 2) until appropriate Bracketing Frames value appears on LCD per BASS Test Matrix (Figure 7). Release Fn or Pv button. 5.4 While pressing and holding Fn or Pv button on Still Camera, Rotate Camera Sub-command Dial (bottom left of camera while mounted, Figure 2) until appropriate Exposure Bracket Value appears on LCD per BASS Test Matrix (Figure 7). Release Fn or Pv button. Figure 8. - Still Camera - Exposure Mode Adjustment. 5.5 pb Still Camera MODE button (Figure 8) → Rotate Main Command Dial to set Exposure Mode per BASS Test Matrix. Release MODE button. Figure 9. - Still Camera - Exposure Compensation (EV Setting). 5.6 If Camera Exposure Mode is A or S per the BASS Test Matrix, pb Exposure Compensation Button (Figure 9) → Press and Hold (EV setting will display on top LCD, Figure 9) While pressing Exposure Compensation Button adjust the Main Command Dial (Figure 9 above -- top left of Camera while mounted) to Exposure Compensation Value (EV) per BASS Test Matrix. 5.7 If Camera Exposure Mode is M per the BASS Test Matrix, Rotate Main Command Dial (Figure 8) → Shutter Speed (per BASS Test Matrix)",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,4,"5.8 Rotate Still Camera Sub-Command Dial (Figure 2)→F-Stop Value (per BASS Test Matrix) Figure 10. - Still Camera - Release Modes: S=Single, CL=Continuous Low, CH=Continuous High. 5.9 While pressing the MODE Release button, rotate Release Mode Selection per the BASS Test Matrix (Figure 10) Still Camera 5.10 ✓SPICE Feedthrough Cable P43 | SHUTTER J43 (left side) SLICE Control Box Assy 5.11 pb SHUTTER → Press firmly and hold for 5 seconds ✓Camera fires BKT sequence in rapid succession Release Shutter after timed count. ✓Image data appears on MSG Video Monitor 2 Figure 11. - Still Camera - Bracketing Progress Indicator Full. 5.12 On Still Camera top LCD, ✓Bracketing Progress Indicator full (ready for new sequence) Figure 11. 5.13 If first test of the day, Remove Still Camera lens cover, place on MSG Long Velcro Strip. Repeat step 5.2 to step 5.12 to setup the Still Camera for the next planned Test Point per the BASS Test Matrix. Figure 12. - Still Camera Filter. 5.14 Install or remove Still Camera Filter on the Still Camera (Figure 12) per the BASS Test Matrix. Stow Still Camera Filter on MSG Long Velcro Strip, if required. Figure 13. - Still Camera - LCD illuminator display. Figure 14. - LCD Output on Rear Display and MSG Video Monitor 2. Still Camera 5.15 Rotate Main Power switch momentarily clockwise to activate LCD illuminator display (Figure 13 and 14). 6. FAN FLOW CONSTRICTOR CONFIGURATION",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,5,"SPICE Exp. Assembly 6.1 Install or remove Fan Flow Constrictor(s) on the SPICE Experiment Assembly Fan Inlet, per the BASS Test Matrix. Stow Fan Flow Constrictor(s) on MSG Long Velcro Strip if required. SPICE Exp. Assembly 6.2 ✓Fan Inlet and Filter Outlet are clear of obstructions. 7. TEST PREP SPICE Exp. Assy 7.1 ✓Front lexan cover closed (latches engaged) 7.2 Activating the CSA-CP and CDM: MSG WV 7.2.1 Place CSA-CP and CDM on MSG Velcro Mounting Pad. CSA-CP CDM 7.2.2 pb MODE → Press (hold until 'RELEASE' displayed) 7.2.3 Wait 1 minute. On CSA-CP, verify display indicates readings for OXYGEN, HCN, HCL and CO. On CDM, verify display indicates readings for CO2. 7.3 Place Mini-Maglite on MSG Long Velcro Strip. MSG WV 7.4 Install front MSG Glove Ring Assembly (two) ✓MSG Gloves are out of the FOV of the cameras. 7.5 ✓All operator rings, watches removed CMP 7.6 sw FUNCTIONS AIR CIRC → OFF (Hold switch in place for 1 second and manually return to center.) ✓FUNCTIONS AIR CIRC OFF LED On (green, air circulation disabled) BASS Control Box 7.7 FAN FLOW → 10.0 GN2 FLOW → ✓POIC WV 7.8 Using front right MSG Glove Ring Assembly, GN2 → OPEN (CCW, approx 2 turns) SPICE Exp. Assy 7.9 sw FUEL → ON (push toward rear of WV to enable GN2 flow) 7.10 ✓DATA REC OFF ✓IGN ENA OFF sw GN2 ENA → ON 7.11 Notify POIC with the following: GMT time of GN2 flow start CSA-CP OXYGEN reading CSA-CP CO reading CDM CO2 reading 7.12 Using MSG Glove Ring Assembly, power off CSA-CP and CDM:",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,6,"pb MODE → Press (hold until 'RELEASE' displayed) ✓CSA-CP Off ✓CDM Off 7.13 ✓MSG Front Stray Light Cover is installed. Peel off middle viewing window. 7.14 Wait 25-225 minutes for WV to fill with GN2 per OSTPV. 8. TEST PREP CONCLUDE 8.1 ✓POIC for PI enablement and live video BASS Control Box 8.2 sw GN2 ENA → OFF 8.3 Notify POIC with GMT time of GN2 flow off. 8.4 FAN FLOW → 0.0 8.5 Powering the CSA-CP and CDM using the MSG Glove Ring Assembly: CSA-CP CDM 8.5.1 pb MODE → Press (hold until 'RELEASE' displayed) 8.5.2 Wait 1 minute. On CSA-CP, verify display indicates readings for OXYGEN, HCN, HCL and CO. On CDM, verify display indicates readings for CO2. 8.5.3 Notify POIC with the following: CSA-CP OXYGEN reading CSA-CP CO reading CDM CO2 reading 8.5.4 Power off CSA-CP and CDM: pb MODE → Press (hold until 'RELEASE' displayed) ✓CSA-CP Off ✓CDM Off CMP 8.6 sw FUNCTIONS WV ILLUM → OFF 9. FLAME TESTS 9.1 GN2 FLOW → rotate to value (per BASS Test Matrix) FAN FLOW → rotate to value (per BASS Test Matrix) 9.2 sw IGN ENA → ON ✓GN2 ENA OFF 9.3 Review step 9.4 for hardware location and sequence order. This step must be performed without delay or loss of science will occur. Figure 15. - SPICE Experiment Assembly - Flame Ignition. SPICE Exp. Assy 9.4 Locate ignitor lever (Figure 15). IGNITOR lever → Pull forward and hold (ignitor glows) Allow the IGNITOR lever to move backward until the ignitor is not glowing when flame appears on MSG Video Monitor 1 (up to 60 seconds).",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,7,"BASS Control Box pb SHUTTER → Press firmly and lock. Hold IGNITOR lever at mid-point for 10 seconds for ignitor to cool, and then fully release. 9.5 Adjust RADIOMETER GAIN until RAD value on MSG Video Monitor 1 is at highest value possible but RAD ≤ 1.0 9.6 After burning for approximately one minute, attempt to extinguish flame per PI direction. If extinguishing the flame by terminating air flow, Set FAN FLOW to 0.00 Wait for flame to extinguish. If extinguishing the flame using GN2, sw GN2 ENA → ON Increase GN2 flow by one turn. Observe flame for destabilization or extinction (wait 5 seconds). Repeat until flame extinguishes. BASS Control Box 9.7 sw IGN ENA → OFF sw GN2 ENA → OFF 9.8 Set FAN FLOW to maximum. Unlock and release SHUTTER button. WV rear wall 9.9 GN2 → CLOSE (CW) 9.10 Report RADIOMETER GAIN from step 9.5. 9.11 Powering the CSA-CP and CDM using the MSG Glove Ring Assembly: CSA-CP CDM 9.11.1 pb MODE → Press (hold until 'RELEASE' displayed) 9.11.2 Wait 1 minute. On CSA-CP, verify display indicates readings for OXYGEN, HCN, HCL and CO. On CDM, verify display indicates readings for CO2. 9.11.3 Notify POIC with the following: CSA-CP OXYGEN reading CSA-CP CO reading CDM CO2 reading 9.11.4 Power off CSA-CP and CDM: pb MODE → Press (hold until 'RELEASE' displayed) ✓CSA-CP Off ✓CDM Off 9.12 Photographing the Sample Post-Burn using the BASS Still Camera: 9.12.1 Using MSG Glove Ring Assembly, illuminate BASS sample [Mini-Maglite]. BASS Control Box 9.12.2 pb SHUTTER → Press",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,8,"9.12.3 Review image on MSG Video Monitor 2. 9.12.4 If needed, adjust Mini-Maglite angle and retake photo to capture/highlight sample details. 10. MSG WV PURGE SPICE Exp. Assy 10.1 sw FUEL → OFF (pull toward front of WV) CMP 10.2 sw FUNCTIONS AIR CIRC → ON (Hold switch in place for 1 second and manually return to center.) ✓FUNCTIONS AIR CIRC ON LED ON (green) 10.3 Remove front MSG Glove Ring Assembly (two) (MSG goes to OPEN mode) 10.4 Wait 3 minutes while Work Volume clears of combustion products before accessing the WV. 10.5 While waiting an additional 6 minutes with FAN FLOW at maximum for touch temperature, Per PI direction, perform 2.007 BASS FLASH CARD EXCHANGE, steps 2 to 4 (US PODF: MSG BASS) SPICE Exp. Assy 10.6 If required, adjust sample distance and/or BASS Ignitor per PI direction for next test point. 10.7 Install front MSG Glove Ring Assembly (two). 10.8 sw FUNCTIONS AIR CIRC → OFF (Hold switch in place for 1 second and manually return to center.) ✓FUNCTIONS AIR CIRC OFF LED On (green, air circulation disabled) 10.9 If additional reduced O2 tests planned for the day, Perform step 7 then exit procedure. If additional test in normal air planned for the day, Go to step 8.5 If all tests complete for the day, Go to step 11 11. FAN CALIBRATION SPICE Exp Assy 11.1 ✓Front Lexan Window closed (engage latches) Figure 16. - Fan Inlet/ Filter Outlet. 11.2 ✓POIC for Fan Flow Constrictor configuration (zero, one, or two installed)",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,9,"11.3 ✓Fan Inlet and Filter Outlet are clear of obstructions. (Figure 16) CMP 11.4 sw FUNCTIONS AIR CIRC → OFF (Hold switch in place for 1 second and manually return to center.) ✓FUNCTIONS AIR CIRC OFF LED On (green, air circulation disabled) BASS Control Box 11.5 Rotate FAN FLOW → 10.0 (Figure 17) Video Monitor 1 ✓POIC with AIR and FAN value on monitor after value stabilizes (approximately 1 minute) (Figure 18). Repeat with FAN FLOW of 8.0, 6.0, 4.0, 2.0 and 0.0. 12. MSG VIDEO TAPE EXCHANGE MSG Vid Drawer 12.1 Exchange the digital video tapes in MSG Video Recorders 1 and 2. Note video tape barcodes. If procedure steps needed, ✓MAIN POWER ON LED On ✓RECORDERS 1,2 LED On Slide MSG Video Drawer fully open. Release MSG Video Drawer lid latching mechanisms (two), Slide lid, secure. Open MSG Video Recorder 1,2 lid. On MSG Video Recorder 1,2, EJECT → Slide in direction of arrow Remove, lock Digital Video Tape (two). Label Digital Tapes with BASS and GMT date [Fine Point Sharpie]. Stow BASS Digital Tapes in ""MSG/BASS Used Tapes"" Ziplock Bag. Remove blank Digital Tape (two) from Digital Tape Case. Note tape barcode to report to POIC. Insert tape into MSG Video Recorder 1,2. pb TC Reset → press Close MSG Video Recorder 1,2 lid. Pull MSG Video Drawer lid forward. Secure MSG Video Drawer lid latching mechanisms (two). Close, lock MSG Video Drawer. 12.2 Notify POIC of blank video tape barcode number. 12.3 Stow: CSA-CP CDM Ground should update IMS for the following parts:",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,10,"BASS Used Sample To: Stow (step 2.4) BASS New Sample To: MSG (step 3.2) Table 1. Procedure Hazard Control List. NOTE 1. Steps 7, 8 and 9 will be repeated as required to complete multiple tests on a given day per PI direction. 2. Steps 9 requires S-Band and KU-Band for the PI to assist with test parameters. 3. The fan calibration (step 11) and the video tape exchange (step 12) are only required after the last flame test for the day has been completed. CAUTION To prevent degraded/blurred video image, avoid touching the lexan covers during sample removal. NOTE Previously burned BASS Samples may have some soot accumulation and should be removed from the SPICE Experiment Assembly using a Dry Wipe. * | *************************************************************************************************** * * * | If ignitor touches the BASS Sample, reposition the BASS Ignitor until the ignitor wire is within 1 mm of the surface of the sample without touching [Tweezers and/or Fingers]. * | *************************************************************************************************** NOTE The Still Camera can be removed for better access to the rear display, crew preference. The Still Camera is also displayed on MSG Video Monitor 2. NOTE It is highly possible that the date displayed on the MSG Video Monitor 1 is one day off from the actual date. It is very important that the date on the Still Camera match the displayed date.",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,11,"* | *************************************************************************************************** * * * | If CL MODE SHOOTING SPEED is not 1fps, pb RIGHT MS → to select sub-menu pb UP/DOWN MS to select → 1fps pb RIGHT MS to select → OK Press LEFT MS Button to exit and return to SHOOTING/DISPLAY * | *************************************************************************************************** * | *************************************************************************************************** * * * | If OFF pb RIGHT MS → to select sub-menu pb UP/DOWN MS to select → ON pb RIGHT MS to select → OK Press LEFT MS Button to exit and return to SHOOTING/DISPLAY * | *************************************************************************************************** * | *************************************************************************************************** * * * | If camera display mode is not Full Metadata, pb UP/DOWN → to select (See Figure 6) Figure 6. - Still Camera Photo Display (Partial Metadata) on Video Monitor. * | *************************************************************************************************** * | *************************************************************************************************** * * * | If 'BKT' icon does not appear on Still Camera LCD, pb Fn or Pv → Press, Hold while rotating Main Command dial until 'BKT' display appears on LCD ✓POIC if any problems encountered",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,12,"* | *************************************************************************************************** NOTE Camera exposure is optimally configured for the upcoming flame tests and as a result the deployed scale may not be visible in the image. NOTE In Figure 11 the bracketing is FULL when the BKT indicator is at zero. Figure 11 shows each of the four bracket settings at FULL. * | *************************************************************************************************** * * * | If Bracketing Progress Indicator is not full, pb SHUTTER → Press firmly and release Repeat as necessary until Bracketing Progress Indicator is full. * | *************************************************************************************************** NOTE 1. Audible beeps occur when the MODE pushbutton is pressed during unit activation. 2. Wait approximately 1 minute while unit runs self-check routine. A single beep occurs when the self-check routine is complete. NOTE MSG Air Circ must be OFF during GN2 fill and flame test. NOTE 1. Audible beeps occur when the MODE pushbutton is pressed during unit activation. 2. Wait approximately 1 minute while unit runs self-check routine. A single beep occurs when the self-check routine is complete. NOTE",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,13,"Steps 9 requires S-Band and KU-Band for the PI to talk directly to the crew and provide all guidance from this point. These steps, included in the procedure for situational awareness, will be repeated as required to complete multiple tests on a given day per PI direction. CAUTION 1. The following step will initiate the Still Camera to take an image once every second. A maximum of 100 images may be taken before the camera stops. Releasing the SHUTTER will also cause the camera to stop taking images. 2. The SLICE Ignitor takes time to cool after Ignition; to avoid damage to the ignitor or front window release the IGNITOR lever only to the point that the ignitor is not glowing for 10 seconds before fully releasing. In order to hold the IGNITOR lever and push the SHUTTER button simultaneously, Step 9.4 will be performed with right hand in the right MSG Glove Ring Assembly and the left hand on the SHUTTER button. * | *************************************************************************************************** * * * | If ignitor does not glow or flame does not appear: Unlock and release SHUTTER button. On BASS Control Box, sw IGN ENA → OFF ✓GN2 ENA OFF ✓POIC for re-iginition or ignitor reposition. * | *************************************************************************************************** NOTE 1. Audible beeps occur when the MODE pushbutton is pressed during unit activation.",,False
PSI-25,Engineering Documents,PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Engineering Documents/PSI-25_Engineering Documents_BASS-II_Ops.docx,all,14,"2. Wait approximately 1 minute while unit runs self-check routine. A single beep occurs when the self-check routine is complete. NOTE The MSG Stray Light Cover can be removed or folded down per crew preference to complete step 9.12. WARNING The MSG WV must be purged for 3 minutes after every ten BASS burns because of the concern of combustion byproducts from the Freon in the cabin air. Figure 17. - FAN FLOW Adjustment Knob. Value shown here is 2.36. | Figure 18. - MSG Video Monitor 1 Display. Hazard Report Number | Hazard Control Number | Procedure Step(s) STD-BASS-01 | 14/14b.6 | Go to step 1.6, step 7.10, step 9.2, step 9.7 STD-BASS-01 | 14/14b.8 | Go to step 9.4 BASS-U1 | 2/2.2 | Warning Box prior to step 10.1, step 10.2, step 10.3, step 10.4 BASS-U3 | 2/2.1 | Go to step 10.4, step 10.5 BASS-U4 | 1/1.1 | Go to step 1.6, step 7.10, step 9.2, step 9.7 BASS-U4 | 1/1.2 | Go to step 9.4 BASS-U4 | 1/1.3 | Go to step 3.5, step 7.1 BASS-U4 | 2/2.1 | Go to step 9.6 BASS-U4 | 2/2.2 | Go to step 9.6 MSG-Int-BASS-05 | 1/1.2 | Warning Box prior to step 10.1 and step 10.4 MSG-FL4 | 1/1.2 | Warning Box prior to step 10.1and step 10.4",,False
PSI-25,Experimental table,PSI-25_Experimental table_BASS-II.csv,data/raw/files/PSI-25/Experimental table/PSI-25_Experimental table_BASS-II.csv,all,0,sheet PSI-25_Experimental table_BASS-II.csv: 130 non-empty rows header: PI | Test # | Date | GMT | Sample # | Fuel Sample Material | Flow restrictor | Fan display | Air display | Radiometer gain (1000 250 50 10) | Total Frames Shot | Calibrated initial O2 % by vol | Calibrated final O2 % by vol | Initial CO2 % by vol | Final CO2 % by vol | Initial CO (ppm) | Final CO (ppm) row 1: Bhattacharjee | B1 | 2/19/2014 | 50 | 147 | 100 micron PMMA film 2 cm wide | 2 | 62 | 5 | 50 | 57 | 22.2 | 22.2 | 0.44 | 0.44 | 0 | 6 row 2: Bhattacharjee | B2 | 2/19/2014 | 50 | 155 | 200 micron PMMA film 2 cm wide | 2 | 62 | 5 | 50 | none | 22.2 | 1.6 | 0.44 | | 6 | row 3: Bhattacharjee | B3 | 2/19/2014 | 50 | 148 | 100 micron film 2 cm wide | 2 | 47 | 2 | 50 | 253 | 22.2 | 21.8 | 0.43 | 0.49 | 12 | 19 row 4: Bhattacharjee | B4 | 2/19/2014 | 50 | 159 | 300 micron PMMA film 2 cm wide | 2 | 61 | 5 | 50 | 137 | 22.2 | 22.2 | 0.44 | 0.51 | -1 | 10 row 5: Bhattacharjee | B5 | 2/19/2014 | 50 | 156 | 200 micron 2 cm wide | 2 | 59 | 5 | 50 | 206 | 22.2 | 21.9 | 0.43 | 0.43 | 0 | 12,,False
PSI-26,metadata,PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-26/metadata/PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,0,"ONTOLOGY SOURCE REFERENCE Term Source Name EFO PSI Term Source File http://data.bioontology.org/ontologies/EFO https://psi.nasa.gov/ Term Source Version 132 1.0 Term Source Description Experimental Factor Ontology User defined terms INVESTIGATION Investigation Identifier Investigation Title Investigation Description Investigation Submission Date Investigation Public Release Date INVESTIGATION PUBLICATIONS Investigation PubMed ID Investigation Publication DOI Investigation Publication Author List Investigation Publication Title Investigation Publication Status Investigation Publication Status Term Accession Number Investigation Publication Status Term Source REF INVESTIGATION CONTACTS Investigation Person Last Name Investigation Person First Name Investigation Person Mid Initials Investigation Person Email Investigation Person Phone Investigation Person Fax Investigation Person Address Investigation Person Affiliation Investigation Person Roles Investigation Person Roles Term Accession Number Investigation Person Roles Term Source REF STUDY Study Identifier PSI-26 Study Title Burning and Suppression of Solids Study Description Study Submission Date 30-Jun-2013 Study Public Release Date 30-Jun-2013 Study File Name s_PSI-26.txt Comment[Acknowledgments] Comment[Approach] ""The experiment was conducted aboard the International Space Station (ISS) inside the Microgravity Glove Box (MSG).",,False
PSI-26,metadata,PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-26/metadata/PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,1,"Custom-made fabric samples consisting of 75% cotton and 25% fiberglass (by mass) measuring were used as a fuel source as well as Nomex fabric and Ultem. A Kanthal wire (29-gage)igniter was used to initiate combustion at one end of the fabric. Airflow was controlled using a variable speed fan, with airflow speeds reaching as high as 40 cm/s. The airflow was directed along the fuel surface in either the opposed or concurrent direction. This investigation explored two primary air flow configurations; concurrent-flow and opposed-flow. With concurrent-flow air flows in the same direction as the flame spread and in opposed-flow air flows in the opposite direction of flame spread. Flames were observed in the top and edge profiles and data was collected on flame size, shape, color, spread rate, and potential flame quenching. The samples were placed in a small flow duct (76 mm x 76 mm cross section) that allowed for airflow across the fabric.",,False
PSI-26,metadata,PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-26/metadata/PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,2,"Other sample shapes were utilized as well including spheres and cylinders."" Comment[DOI] 10.60555/58h4-dy80 Comment[Data Source Accession] Comment[Data Source Link] Comment[Experiment Hardware] Microgravity Science Glovebox Comment[Experiment Hardware Link] https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/msg/ Comment[Flight Platform] International Space Station (ISS) Comment[Funding] Comment[Hypothesis] BASS experiments were based on the hypothesis that materials in microgravity, with adequate ventilation, burn as well if not better than the same material in normal gravity with other conditions being identical (pressure, oxygen concentration, temperature, etc.) To test this hypothesis with any but the thinnest materials requires extended microgravity time – longer than available in drop towers or parabolic aircraft. Comment[Identifiers] Comment[Investigation Acronym] BASS Comment[Investigation End Date] 06-30-2013 Comment[Investigation Start Date] 10-01-2009 Comment[Managing NASA Center] Glenn Research Center (GRC) Comment[Mission End] Comment[Mission Link] Comment[Mission Name] Comment[Mission Start] Comment[Objective] ""The two main objectives for BASS are to: 1.) Begin to bridge the gap between the normal gravity NASA-STD-6001 Test#1 method, ground-based microgravity tests, and actual material flammability in microgravity. The relation between these can be investigated by observing flames burning for the longer times available in flight.",,False
PSI-26,metadata,PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-26/metadata/PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,3,"Practical, realistic (thicker) fuels in typical geometries will be examined, including wake flames which have characteristics of a fire burning behind another object (for example, a fire burning in tight area which may not be easily accessible). 2.) Assess the effectiveness of an inert, gaseous extinguishing agent (similar to that used on ISS) in putting out flames over different materials, geometries, preheating, and flow."" Comment[Project Identifier] Comment[Project Link] Comment[Project Title] Comment[Project Type] Microgravity Investigation Comment[Proposal Title] Comment[Research Area] Combustion Science Comment[Research Impacts] ""Current NASA spacecraft materials selection is based on a standard test method (NASA–STD–6001 Test 1) that segregates material based on 1-g behavior without consideration of low gravity effects. A critical element of this understanding is the radiative heat emission from the flame. These results are used in first order models and predictions of heat release in spacecraft fires and as a means to extend heat release data from tests like the NASA cone calorimeter test (NASA–STD–6001 Test 2) to a performance-based material selection process. Using nitrogen as a flame suppressant in microgravity provides a direct link to current and planned extinguishment techniques. BASS results provide essential guidance to ground-based microgravity combustion research efforts.",,False
PSI-26,metadata,PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-26/metadata/PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,4,"Detailed combustion models are validated using the simpler flow environment afforded by tests in microgravity. Once validated, they can be used to build more complex combustion models needed to capture the important details of flames burning in normal gravity.",,False
PSI-26,metadata,PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-26/metadata/PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,5,"These models have wide applicability to the general understanding of many terrestrial combustion problems and will guide novel spacecraft design and ensure the safety of future crewed missions."" Comment[Space Program] National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) Comment[Study Grant Number] Comment[SubResearch Area] Spacecraft Fire Safety STUDY DESIGN DESCRIPTORS Study Design Type Flame Spread Fire safety Fire suppression system Forced Flow Flame Damkohler number Kinetic regime Boundary layer Study Design Type Term Accession Number https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ Study Design Type Term Source REF PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI STUDY PUBLICATIONS Study PubMed ID Study Publication DOI 10.2514/6.2006-941 10.1016/j.firesaf.2015.11.011 10.1016/j.proci.2014.07.017 10.1080/00102202.2014.900056 10.1504/PCFD.2012.048249 10.1299/jsmeatem.2015.14.289 10.1016/j.proci.2012.05.076 Study Publication Author List Spivey Reggie, Luz Paul Bhattacharjee Subrata, Laue Matthew, Carmignani Luca, Ferkul Paul, Olson Sandra Takahashi Shuhei, Ebisawa Tomoya, Bhattacharjee Subrata, Ihara Tadayoshi S Bhattacharjee, R Nagarkar, Y Nakamura Bhattacharjee Subrata, Paolini Christopher P., Miller Fletcher, Nagarkar Rohit Bhattacharjee Subrata, Cartnignani Luca JR Villaraza, L Carmignani, S Bhattacharjee P Ferkul, SL Olson, F Takahashi, M Endo, MC Johnston, JS Tien S Bhattacharjee, M",,False
PSI-26,metadata,PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-26/metadata/PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,6,"Bundy, C Paolini, G Patel, W Tran. GR Bornand, FJ Miller, JM Pepper, SL Olson, IS Wichman Study Publication Title The Microgravity Science Glovebox as a Platform for Exploration Research on the International Space Station Opposed-flow flame spread: A comparison of microgravity and normal gravity experiments to establish the thermal regime Simplified model for predicting difference between flammability limits of a thin material in normal gravity and microgravity environments A Correlation for an Effective Flow Velocity for Capturing the Boundary Layer Effect in Opposed-Flow Flame Spread over Thin Fuels Radiation signature in opposed-flow flame spread OS24-1 The Effect of Boundary Layer on Blow-Off Extinction in Opposed-Flow Flame Spread:",,False
PSI-26,metadata,PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-26/metadata/PSI-26_metadata_PSI-102_BASS.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,7,"Results of Experiments and Simplified Analysis(invited,Scale modeling on solid combustion,OS24 Scale modeling,FLUID AND THERMODYNAMICS) Role of Gas Radiation in the Mechanism of Opposed-Flow Flame Spread in a Microgravity Environment Thickness and Fuel Preheating Effects on Material Flammability in Microgravity from the BASS Experiment A novel apparatus for flame spread study Opposed-Flow Flame Spread in a Narrow Channel Apparatus over Thin PMMA Sheets Study Publication Status published published published published published published published published published published Study Publication Status Term Accession Number http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 Study Publication Status Term Source REF EFO EFO EFO EFO EFO EFO EFO EFO Comment[Study Publication Link] http://dx.doi.org/10.2514/6.2006-941 http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.firesaf.2015.11.011 http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.proci.2014.07.017 http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/00102202.2014.900056 http://dx.doi.org/10.1504/pcfd.2012.048249 http://dx.doi.org/10.1299/jsmeatem.2015.14.289",,False
