PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Flammability Aspects of Fabric_USNationalCombustion2013.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Flammability Aspects of Fabric_USNationalCombustion2013.pdf,p37,0,"Summary ♦ For the opposed flow configuration, the flame quickly reached steady spread for each flow speed, and the spread rate was fastest at an intermediate value of flow speed. ♦ These tests show the enhanced flammability in microgravity for this geometry, since, in normal gravity air, a flame self-extinguishes in the opposed flow geometry (downward flame spread). ♦ For the concurrent flow configuration, a limiting length and steady spread rate were obtained only in low flow speeds. For these conditions, flame spread rate increased linearly with increasing flow. ♦ This is the first time that detailed transient flame growth data was obtained in purely forced flows in microgravity. In addition, by decreasing concurrent flow speed to a very low value (around 1 cm/s), quenching extinction was observed.",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Flammability Aspects of Fabric_USNationalCombustion2013.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Flammability Aspects of Fabric_USNationalCombustion2013.pdf,p38,0,"Summary ♦ For the opposed flow configuration, the flame quickly reached steady spread for each flow speed, and the spread rate was fastest at an intermediate value of flow speed. ♦ These tests show the enhanced flammability in microgravity for this geometry, since, in normal gravity air, a flame self-extinguishes in the opposed flow geometry (downward flame spread). ♦ For the concurrent flow configuration, a limiting length and steady spread rate were obtained only in low flow speeds. For these conditions, flame spread rate increased linearly with increasing flow. ♦ This is the first time that detailed transient flame growth data was obtained in purely forced flows in microgravity. In addition, by decreasing concurrent flow speed to a very low value (around 1 cm/s), quenching extinction was observed. ♦ The valuable results from these long-duration experiments validate a number of theoretical predictions and also provide the data for a transient flame growth model under development.",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Flammability Aspects of Fabric_USNationalCombustion2013.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Flammability Aspects of Fabric_USNationalCombustion2013.pdf,p39,0,"Fumiaki Takahashi, National Center for Space Exploration Research Bob Hawersaat, NASA Glenn Research Center Jay Owens, National Center for Space Exploration Research Tibor Lorik, ZIN Technologies Chuck Bunnell, ZIN Technologies Dennis Siedlak, Georgia Institute of Technology Carol Reynolds, NASA Marshall Space Flight Center Don Pettit, NASA Johnson Space Center Joe Acaba, NASA Johnson Space Center Suni Williams,NASA Johnson Space Center Acknowledgments:",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p1,0,"SLICE/BASS SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS DESIGN REVIEW Prepared For: National Aeronautics and Space Administration John H. Glenn Research Center at Lewis Field Microgravity Science [address removed] Prepared By: ZIN Technologies, Incorporated [address removed] April 14, 2010",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p2,0,"SLICE HARDWARE OVERVIEW • SLICE will utilize the SPICE hardware currently on ISS – Experiment Assembly, Cables, Control Box, Video Camera • SLICE will refurbish the SPICE Gas Bottles for re-flight • SLICE will design, fabrication, assemble, test new hardware – 2.1 mm and 3.2 mm burner tubes – Upgraded Nikon Still Camera – Blue-Green Filter – Ceramic Pyrometry Fiber 4/14/2010 2",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p3,0,SPICE ON-ORBIT IN MSG 4/14/2010 3,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p4,0,SLICE HARDWARE OVERVIEW Gas Bottles in Case (1 of 2) Experiment Assembly Control Box Gas Bottle (1 of 12) Video Camera 4/14/2010 4 Ignitor/Burner Case,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p5,0,SLICE HARDWARE OVERVIEW (NEW) Burner Tube (2.1 mm & 3.2 mm) Ceramic Pyrometry Fiber D300s Still Camera LEXAN Window 4/14/2010 5,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p6,0,SLICE HARDWARE OVERVIEW 4/14/2010 6,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p7,0,"SLICE SCIENCE OVERVIEW OBJECTIVES • Identify the lift-off velocity limits in air for flames of methane, ethylene, and selected dilutions of each fuel as a function of the nozzle size. When possible, also identify the blow-out velocities (i.e., when the flame extinguishes). • Characterize the hysteresis of flame lifting and reattachment (i.e., stabilization) with variations in flow conditions as a function of the fuel and nozzle size. • Characterize the structure of the flame, especially its base (i.e., stabilizing region), from attached through lifted conditions as a function of the fuel, nozzle size, and flow conditions. 4/14/2010 7",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p8,0,"SLICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENTS • Fuel Nozzles – All nozzles are replaceable on-orbit by the crew – Fuel nozzle installed orientation is in the axis of the flow duct – Fuel nozzles are circular with a constant internal cross section – 2 new nozzles have been fabricated. – Off-the-shelf tubes have inner diameter with a tolerance of better than the required ±0.1 mm – The original SPICE nozzles (0.4, 0.8, and 1.6 mm) and the new SLICE nozzles will be measured such that the actual size is known with an accuracy of ±0.05 mm – Burner tubes are made of 304 Stainless Steel • FUELS – Project has procured gases with appropriate fuel type, purity, and concentration such that the gas is dry with a net amount of contaminants less than a mole and concentration known to within 0.005 mole fraction as verified by vendor certification documentation. – Gas volume (bottle pressure) has been maximized based on the minimum thermal environment such that condensation does not occur. – The 12 fuels bottles will be filled as shown in Slide 6. • Oxygen Concentration – Project will include steps in crew procedures to install a Compound Specific Analyzer (CSA-O2) into the MSG to measure the Oxyge concentration. There is also a Major Constituent Analyzer available to measure the Columbus Module concentration. 4/14/2010 8",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p9,0,SLICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENTS • Pressure – Cabin pressure will be measured by 4 pressure transducers in the Columbus module and the differential pressure between the cabin and MSG WV measured by 2 MSG transducers. • Acceleration – SLICE testing will be scheduled around planned thruster firing or spacecraft dock or undock. – The acceleration environment will be measured by SAMS during SLICE testing which will be installed into the MSG Work Volume. • Still Imaging – A Nikon D300s camera with a blue-green BG-7 filter that can installed or removed will be manifested for use with SLICE. – The Nikon D300s camera will be calibrated with a blackbody source • Calibration will be performed using the replacement LEXAN top window and with and without the BG-7 filter from 800 – 1200 ˚C in 100 ˚C increments. 4/14/2010 9,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p10,0,SLICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENTS • There shall be a single ceramic SiC fiber with a diameter of less than 100 and shall be known to within ± 5 microns that can be located downstream and orthogonal to the fuel nozzle. – The fiber used will be an 80 micron diameter fiber – The fiber will diameter will be measured using a calibrated measurement device capable of the required accuracy – Utilize the BASS Frame assembly and Flat Fuel Holder design – Distance from the nozzle is adjustable – Fiber is in the Field of View of the Still Camera 4/14/2010 10,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p11,0,"SLICE SUCCESS CRITERIA • Minimum Success -– Still images are acquired for at least 15 different lifted flames with different combinations of fuel flow and air velocity with a single fuel, or at least 10 different lifted flames each of two fuels, or at least 5 different lifted flames each of 3 or more fuels. • High Success -– Still images are acquired of at least 30 different lifted flames total from at least 3 different fuels. The still images are acquired with a blackbody characterized camera for soot pyrometry or liftoff limits are determined for at least one fuel. • Complete Success -– Testing conducted with all 12 fuel bottles, with identification of stability limits and smoke points (within the range of the SPICE velocity limits). Still images are taken of lifted flames with a blackbody characterized camera. 4/14/2010 11",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p12,0,SLICE PRODUCT VERIFICATION TESTING • D300s Still Camera – Functional Test – Soft Stow Vibration per 60054-TST-007 • 7.13 gRMS – Thermal Operating Test per 60054-TST-009 • 10 cycles from -10 to 45 ˚C – Black Body Calibration and Focus Setup using Replacement Top Window. – Materials Bake-out for 72 hours at 120 ˚F • The SPICE off-gas testing using the similar D100 camera as well as all other SPICE hardware has a total T value of 0.001152. Using this value for just the new BASS D300s camera will provide sufficient margin as well as allow the payload to remain below the 0.5 limit. • Burner Tubes – Perform dimensioning calibration. No verification testing is planned for the burner tubes. • Replacement Top Window – Anneal at 250˚F for 1 hour – Materials Bake-out for 72 hours at 120 ˚F 4/14/2010 12,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p13,0,SLICE PRODUCT VERIFICATION TESTING • Ceramic Pyrometry Fiber – Soft Stow Vibration per 60054-TST-007 • 7.13 gRMS – Materials Bake-out for 72 hours at 120 ˚F • Gas Bottles – Soft Stow Vibration per 60054-TST-007 • 7.13 gRMS – Pre and Post Vibe Helium Leak Test 4/14/2010 13,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p14,0,SAFETY VERIFICATIONS • SLICE is designated as a SPICE re-flight. • Safety Package Submitted to the PSRP 3/10/10 • Out of Board Approval is Expected • 19 Safety Verifications – 5 Closed Verifications-Gas Bottle Leak Test after Vibe – Depress/Repress Analysis for D300s Camera – Routed 3/9/10 – Shatterable Containment Analysis for D300s Camera & Filter – Routed 3/9/10 – Sharp Edge Drawing Analysis – Submit when all drawings are released – 4/10 – As-Built Inspection (7) – Upon Assembly Complete including verification that hardware is properly stowed for launch and gas bottles filled with approved gas - 5/10 – Materials Analysis (Materials Cert Letter) (2)– 5/10 • Compatibility of Ethane has been verified for Stainless Steel as well as the Fluorocarbon FKM o-rings. – Vibration Test for Shatterable Material (D300s Camera & Filter) – Crew Procedure Review for Hazard Control – 7/10 4/14/2010 14,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p15,0,INTEGRATION VERIFICATIONS • Utilize SPICE ICD as a baseline for the integration requirements with MSG • New verifications not addressed in the safety verifications include the following: – Fit Check for D300s Still Camera in MSG EU – Hardware weight and volume – Possible TIA for Still Camera EMI & Bonding not performed – Still Camera Video Interface Test in MSG EU – Labeling 4/14/2010 15,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p16,0,SCHEDULE HIGHLIGHTS (Open Work) • Still Camera Assembly including Filter – 4/15/10 – Vibration Test – 4/29/10 – Thermal Cycle Test – 5/15/10 – Black Body Calibration – 5/30/10 • Ceramic Pyrometry Fiber Assembly – 4/25/10 – Vibration Test – 4/29/10 • Burner Tube Calibration – 5/15/10 • SAR-1 – 5/26/10 • SAR-2 – 6/9/10 • Bench Review – 6/22/10 4/14/2010 16,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p17,0,17 ISS Research Project SLICE/BASS Risk Assessment Approach M-Mitigate W-Watch A-Accept R-Research Med High Low Criticality LxC Trend Decreasing Increasing Unchanged New 5 4 3 2 1 L I K E L I H O O D 5 1 2 3 4 CONSEQUENCES B-02 B-01 SL-01 SL-02 Rank Risk ID Approach Risk Title 1 B-01 M Flame Extinguishment of PMMA Spheres 2 B-02 M Crew Setup of Ignitor 3 SL-01 M Ethane Gas Usage 4 SL-02 M Camera EMI Test 5 6 7 8 9 10 4/14/2010,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p18,0,Risk Statement (Title & Detailed Description) Mitigation Strategy Risk ID Rank 1 L3 C4 L4 C3 2 L2 C3 3 SLICE-001 BASS-001 BASS-002 Ethane Gas Usage Given that there is a new gas (Ethane) in the science matrix there is the possibility that this gas will result in new safety hazard controls. Crew Setup of Ignitor Given that the operations scenario has the crew position the ignitor very near the fuel to initiate ignition there is the possibility they will experience difficulty and require several attempts at correct placement. Flame Extinguishment of PMMA Spheres Given that the PMMA spheres can burn for a significantly long time there is the possibility that it will be difficult to extinguish the flame. Ground testing has shown that placing the correctly designed ignitor near the sample does result in ignition of the solid fuel. Crew training and proper procedures should mitigate this risk. A zero-g facility 5 second drop tower test demonstrated the nitrogen extinguishment of the 2 cm PMMA sphere - successfully completed on 25 Feb 2010. (CLOSED),,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p18,1,The project has analyzed the fuel for safety impacts and found that this gas selection falls within the bounds of the previously used gases for LFL and SMAC. RISK ASSESSMENT SLICE-002 4 L2 C4 Camera EMI Test Given that the project proposes not to perform EMI testing on the replacement still camera there is the possibility that this approach will not be accepted resulting in a delay in hardware delivery. The camera design is similar to the original camera. The power supply and cable that powers the camera is on-orbit and therefore conducting an EMI test with non-flight hardware poses as much uncertainty as the camera itself. 4/14/2010 18,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p19,0,"BASS SLICE SR/DR Success Criteria and Evidence 4/14/2010 19 Critical Design Review Success Criteria Artifact Name and/or Number Status (include Section or Drawing Number) (Complete, Planned Completion Date, Not Applicable) 1 The detailed design is expected to meet the requirements with adequate margins at an acceptable level of risk. See drawing tree included in the Appendix 4/30/10 2 Interface control documents are sufficiently matured to proceed with fabrication, assembly, integration, and test, and plans are in place to manage any open items. SPICE-ICD Complete 3 High confidence exists in the product baseline, and adequate documentation exists or will exist in a timely manner to allow proceeding with fabrication, assembly, integration, and test. Previous flight had complete success. Complete 4 The product verification and product validation requirements and plans are complete. SR/DR Package. The package describes the planned product verifications. Also refer to the SpaceDoc PAP and SLICE and BASS Safety Packages (Summarized in this package) 4/30/10",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_SLICE_SysReqDesignReview.pdf,p20,0,"BASS SLICE SR/DR Success Criteria and Evidence 4/14/2010 20 Critical Design Review Success Criteria Artifact Name and/or Number Status (include Section or Drawing Number) (Complete, Planned Completion Date, Not Applicable) 5 The testing approach is comprehensive, and the planning for system assembly, integration, test, and launch site and mission operations is sufficient to progress into the next phase. SR/DR Package. The Project Plan contains the plan for system integration, test and mission operations plan. The Project Plan will be included in the package 4/14/10 6 Adequate technical and programmatic margins and resources exist to complete the development within budget, schedule, and risk constraints. SR/DR Package. Project Schedule and Budget is included in the package 4/14/10 7 Risks to mission success are understood and credibly assessed, and plans and resources exist to effectively manage them. SR/DR Package. Project Risks are included in the package 4/14/10 8 Safety and mission assurance (e.g., safety, reliability, maintainability, quality, and EEE parts) have been adequately addressed in system and operational designs, and any applicable S&MA products (e.g., PRA, system safety analysis and failure modes and effects analysis) have been approved. Safety Data Package 4/30/10",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p1,0,"SPICE, SLICE, and BASS Crew Training October, 2010 Chuck Bunnell Technical Lead Glenn Research Center [e-mail removed] [phone removed] Carol Reynolds MSG Investigations Lead Marshall Space Flight Center [e-mail removed] [phone removed] Robert Hawersaat Project Manager Glenn Research Center [e-mail removed] [phone removed] Dr. Marshall Long SLICE Principal Investigator Yale University Marshall.long@yale.edu (203)432-4229 Dr. Paul Ferkul BASS Principal Investigator Glenn Research Center paul.v.ferkul@nasa.gov (216)433-8107 Dr. David Urban SPICE Principal Investigator Glenn Research Center david.l.urban@nasa.gov (216)433-2835 1",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p2,0,"Training Objectives • A brief description of the SPICE, SLICE, and BASS investigations, predecessor experiments and science objectives. • An overview and description of the SPICE, SLICE, and BASS hardware. • An overview of the astronaut’s importance to the experiment, Micro-gravity Science Glovebox (MSG) interfaces, and science data collection operations. • Step-by-step discussion of the science data collection process, by completing portions of the crew procedures for each investigation 2",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p3,0,"INTRODUCTION • Three investigations will be performed: SPICE, SLICE, and BASS. • These investigations will be performed inside the Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG). • SPICE is based on the Enclosed Laminar Flames (ELF) hardware which flew on STS-87 in 1997. • SPICE hardware is already on-orbit. It has successfully completed all of its objectives with the collection of 200 data points in the first half of 2009. Spare gas onboard will now be used to collect additional data. New fuel is being flown on ULF5 to support the SLICE and BASS experiments. • SPICE hardware will also be used to perform SLICE and BASS. • SPICE burns different fuels to investigate the point at which they begin to smoke. • SLICE burns different fuels to investigate the structure of flames when lifted from the fuel nozzle. • BASS burns different materials to investigate how they burn in microgravity.",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p4,0,"The objective of the SPICE experiment is to find the laminar smoke-point properties of nonbuoyant jet diffusion flames (i.e., the properties of the largest laminar jet diffusion flames that do not emit soot) for two fuel types under different nozzle diameter / co-flow velocity configurations. SPICE will stabilize an enclosed laminar flame, measure the overall flame shape to validate the theoretical and numerical predictions, measure the flame stabilization heights, and measure the temperature field to verify flame structure predictions. SPICE Science Overview 4",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p5,0,"Target Setting: The target setting is just above the setting that produces a distinct edge (only a small portion of the tip will emit smoke). The smoke will probably emerge from the center of the tip but may emerge at the tip edges (horns) for the larger nozzle. The pictures show the onset of smoke points for various fuels on the KC-135. The size of the flame when it emits smoke is a fuel property. The expected size sequence will be Propylene, Ethylene, Propane and Methane (probably no smoke point). The diluted propylene smoke points will be longer than pure propylene. SPICE Smoke Point Determination Cue Card Background: The smoke point is the flame length, above which, soot particles are no longer consumed in the flame and instead emerge through the flame tip and glow as they cool. The glowing soot causes a change from a sharp edge to a gradual transition between the bright flame and black background.",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p5,1,"Advisory note: For the test points with the 0.4 mm nozzle, the possibility exists that the flame could blow off. When this happens, the blue flame-base no longer touches the nozzle. When the base of the flame starts to separate from the nozzle, reduce the flow rate until it is well attached as the smoke height is not achievable. 50% Propylene 1.6 mm nozzle, smoke point indicated by the arrow Ethylene, 1.6 mm nozzle, smoke point indicated by the arrow Propane, 0.8 mm nozzle, Smoke point indicated by the arrow Propane, 1.6 mm nozzle, Smoke point just below smaller flame Note: Relative sizes correspond to expected result Look for the transition between a distinct flame tip and dull red/orange breaking through the flame tip (indistinct tip).",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p6,0,"SLICE Science Background • The goal of the SLICE investigation is to investigate the structure of lifting and lifted flames; whereby the flame detaches from the nozzle and stabilizes at a downstream position. • The results of the SLICE experiment will be used to minimize risk and maximize the scientific return of the CLD (Coflow Laminar Diffusion) Flame experiment, which is one of the four ACME (Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments) investigations that are currently in development for conduct within the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). 6 The lifted nature of the flames can be discerned from the outward flare of the flame base in the example images below (which are not at the same scale) and the distance from the nozzle tip (which is not visible). Are CLD or ACME the approved names? I could not find them. (these payloads are in-development and haven’t touched the ops world yet)",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p7,0,"BASS Science Background • The BASS investigation tests the hypothesis that materials in microgravity burn as well if not better than the same material in normal gravity with other conditions being identical (ie. pressure, oxygen concentration, temperature). • To test this hypothesis with any but the thinnest materials requires extended microgravity time – longer than available in drop towers or parabolic aircraft. If this hypothesis is true, it implies serious safety concerns over the utility and margin of safety provided NASA-STD-6001 Test #1 (upward flammability test used to evaluate materials intended for use in the habitable environments of US spacecraft). Practical, realistic (thicker) fuels in typical geometries will be examined, including wake flames which have characteristics of a fire burning behind another object (for example, a fire burning in tight area which may not be easily accessible). • There are important differences in the suppression of fires in space compared to on Earth. On Earth it is understood that best results are generally obtained when the extinguisher “attacks” the base of the flame, which is both the stabilization point and the point of fresh air entrainment. For a fire burning in microgravity, the best point of application of suppressant may not be immediately apparent, especially for a partially obstructed flame or a wake-stabilized flame.",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p7,1,"Depending on the geometry of the flame and the characteristics of the extinguisher (distance from flame, dispersion angle) it is possible that the suppressant stream will be ineffective or might actually make the flame worse through the entrainment of oxygen. 7",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p8,0,"8 BASS Samples 1cm Flat Sample 2cm Sphere Sample Wake Flame Zero-g facility test burning 2-cm-diameter PMMA sphere in 30 cm/s airflow. Left: 1 g; Middle: 0 g, 1 s after drop; right: 0 g, 4 s after application of nitrogen extinguishing agent.",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p9,0,"Earth Applications • SPICE The smoke-point phenomena is a classical metric in the understanding of the heat release and spread rate of fires. It is commonly used in fire modeling on Earth and to understand the soot growth and emission by flames. The dominant characteristics of many flames of practical interest are nonbuoyant. SPICE seeks to extend our understanding by looking at the interaction of ambient flow with the smoke point, enabling us to better predict heat release from non-buoyant flames in practical combustors (e.g. jet engines and furnaces). • SLICE The twin purposes for the SLICE research are increased efficiency and reduced pollutant emission in practical combustion devices. More explicitly, the experiment is being conducted to advance combustion modeling capability, which “allows system designers to improve efficiency and reduce harmful pollutants in ways never before possible” (as stated by an external reviewer of the SLICE experiment). In other words, the SLICE research is being conducted to advance energy technology and improve our global environment. • BASS It is highly desirable to develop a new and/or improved Earth-based test method for material flammability. The data obtained in this experiment will begin to bridge the gap between the normal gravity method, ground-based microgravity tests, and actual material flammability in microgravity 9",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p10,0,SPICE Hardware installed in MSG Work Volume SPICE Hardware Overview 10,,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p11,0,SPICE science objectives are met solely through photo and video downlink. SPICE is a very crew intensive experiment as the crew must adjust fuel flow and fan speed as well as take photos. MSG Work Volume should be retracted throughout all SPICE operations except Setup and Stow. SPICE Hardware Overview (continued) 11,,False
