PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p26,0,"Major Unique Hazard Controls • After every 2 burns the MSG Work Volume must be purged for 3 minutes to promote air mixing to dilute any HF concentration byproduct produced during combustion. – MSG Glove Port open – MSG Air Cir ON • Each Gas Bottle Assembly must have a tie wrap valve restraint in place before stowing. • Prior to crew access into MSG, the Gas Bottle Assembly valve must be closed. • When the MSG Work Volume is open and a Gas Bottle Assembly is present, MSG Air Cir must be ON. • The inside of the Experiment Assembly must be permitted to cool to a temp below 49°C before crew access is permitted. – Wait time for SLICE is 1 minute (covered by 3 minute purge) – Wait time for BASS thin and candle samples is 2 minutes (covered by 3 minute purge) – Wait time for BASS sphere and thick samples is 9 minutes 26",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2010.pdf,p27,0,• ACME - Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments • BASS – Burning and Suppression of Solids • CLD - Coflow Laminar Diffusion • CIR - Combustion Integrated Rack • ELF- Enclosed Laminar Flames • MSG – Microgravity Science Glovebox • SLICE – Structure and Liftoff In Combustion Experiment • SPICE – Smoke Point in Coflow Experiment 27 Acronyms,,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p1,0,"SPICE, SLICE, and BASS Crew Training March 2011 Chuck Bunnell Technical Lead Glenn Research Center [e-mail removed] [phone removed] Carol Reynolds MSG Investigations Lead Marshall Space Flight Center [e-mail removed] [phone removed] Robert Hawersaat Project Manager Glenn Research Center [e-mail removed] [phone removed] Dr. Marshall Long SLICE Principal Investigator Yale University marshall.long@yale.edu (203) 432-4229 Dr. Paul Ferkul BASS Principal Investigator Glenn Research Center paul.v.ferkul@nasa.gov (216)433-8107 Dr. David Urban SPICE Principal Investigator Glenn Research Center david.l.urban@nasa.gov (216)433-2835 1",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p2,0,"Training Objectives • A brief description of the SPICE, SLICE, and BASS investigations, predecessor experiments and science objectives. • An overview and description of the SPICE, SLICE, and BASS hardware. • An overview of the astronaut’s importance to the experiment, Micro-gravity Science Glovebox (MSG) interfaces, and science data collection operations. • Step-by-step discussion of the science data collection process, by completing portions of the crew procedures for each investigation 2",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p3,0,"Introduction • Three investigations will be performed: SPICE, SLICE, and BASS. • These investigations will be performed inside the Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG). • SPICE is based on the Enclosed Laminar Flames (ELF) hardware which flew on STS-87 in 1997. • SPICE hardware is already on-orbit. It has successfully completed all of its objectives with the collection of 200 data points in the first half of 2009. New fuel is being flown on ULF5 to support the SLICE and BASS experiments. • SPICE hardware will also be used to perform SLICE and BASS. • SPICE burns different fuels to investigate the point at which they begin to smoke. • SLICE burns different fuels to investigate the structure of flames when lifted from the fuel nozzle. • BASS burns different materials to investigate how they burn in microgravity.",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p4,0,"The objective of the SPICE experiment is to find the laminar smoke-point properties of nonbuoyant jet diffusion flames (i.e., the properties of the largest laminar jet diffusion flames that do not emit soot) for two fuel types under different nozzle diameter / co-flow velocity configurations. SPICE will stabilize an enclosed laminar flame, measure the overall flame shape to validate the theoretical and numerical predictions, measure the flame stabilization heights, and measure the temperature field to verify flame structure predictions. SPICE Science Overview 4",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p5,0,"Target Setting: The target setting is just above the setting that produces a distinct edge (only a small portion of the tip will emit smoke). The smoke will probably emerge from the center of the tip but may emerge at the tip edges (horns) for the larger nozzle. The pictures show the onset of smoke points for various fuels on the KC-135. The size of the flame when it emits smoke is a fuel property. The expected size sequence will be Propylene, Ethylene, Propane and Methane (probably no smoke point). The diluted propylene smoke points will be longer than pure propylene. SPICE Smoke Point Determination Cue Card Background: The smoke point is the flame length, above which, soot particles are no longer consumed in the flame and instead emerge through the flame tip and glow as they cool. The glowing soot causes a change from a sharp edge to a gradual transition between the bright flame and black background.",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p5,1,"Advisory note: For the test points with the 0.4 mm nozzle, the possibility exists that the flame could blow off. When this happens, the blue flame-base no longer touches the nozzle. When the base of the flame starts to separate from the nozzle, reduce the flow rate until it is well attached as the smoke height is not achievable. 50% Propylene 1.6 mm nozzle, smoke point indicated by the arrow Ethylene, 1.6 mm nozzle, smoke point indicated by the arrow Propane, 0.8 mm nozzle, Smoke point indicated by the arrow Propane, 1.6 mm nozzle, Smoke point just below smaller flame Note: Relative sizes correspond to expected result Look for the transition between a distinct flame tip and dull red/orange breaking through the flame tip (indistinct tip).",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p6,0,"SLICE Science Background • The goal of the SLICE investigation is to investigate the structure of lifting and lifted flames; whereby the flame detaches from the nozzle and stabilizes at a downstream position. • The results of the SLICE experiment will be used to minimize risk and maximize the scientific return of the CLD (Coflow Laminar Diffusion) Flame experiment, which is one of the four ACME (Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments) investigations that are currently in development for conduct within the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). 6 The lifted nature of the flames can be discerned from the outward flare of the flame base in the example images below (which are not at the same scale) and the distance from the nozzle tip (which is not visible).",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p7,0,"BASS Science Background • The BASS investigation tests the hypothesis that materials in microgravity burn as well if not better than the same material in normal gravity with other conditions being identical (ie. pressure, oxygen concentration, temperature). • To test this hypothesis with any but the thinnest materials requires extended microgravity time – longer than available in drop towers or parabolic aircraft. If this hypothesis is true, it implies serious safety concerns over the utility and margin of safety provided NASA-STD-6001 Test #1 (upward flammability test used to evaluate materials intended for use in the habitable environments of US spacecraft). Practical, realistic (thicker) fuels in typical geometries will be examined, including wake flames which have characteristics of a fire burning behind another object (for example, a fire burning in tight area which may not be easily accessible). • There are important differences in the suppression of fires in space compared to on Earth. On Earth it is understood that best results are generally obtained when the extinguisher “attacks” the base of the flame, which is both the stabilization point and the point of fresh air entrainment. For a fire burning in microgravity, the best point of application of suppressant may not be immediately apparent, especially for a partially obstructed flame or a wake-stabilized flame.",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p7,1,"Depending on the geometry of the flame and the characteristics of the extinguisher (distance from flame, dispersion angle) it is possible that the suppressant stream will be ineffective or might actually make the flame worse through the entrainment of oxygen. 7",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p8,0,"8 BASS Samples 1cm Flat Sample 2cm Sphere Sample Wake Flame Zero-g facility test burning 2-cm-diameter PMMA sphere in 30 cm/s airflow. Left: 1 g; Middle: 0 g, 1 s after drop; right: 0 g, 4 s after application of nitrogen extinguishing agent. Double click each image below.",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p9,0,"Earth Applications • SPICE The smoke-point phenomena is a classical metric in the understanding of the heat release and spread rate of fires. It is commonly used in fire modeling on Earth and to understand the soot growth and emission by flames. The dominant characteristics of many flames of practical interest are nonbuoyant. SPICE seeks to extend our understanding by looking at the interaction of ambient flow with the smoke point, enabling us to better predict heat release from non-buoyant flames in practical combustors (e.g. jet engines and furnaces). • SLICE The twin purposes for the SLICE research are increased efficiency and reduced pollutant emission in practical combustion devices. More explicitly, the experiment is being conducted to advance combustion modeling capability, which “allows system designers to improve efficiency and reduce harmful pollutants in ways never before possible” (as stated by an external reviewer of the SLICE experiment). In other words, the SLICE research is being conducted to advance energy technology and improve our global environment. • BASS It is highly desirable to develop a new and/or improved Earth-based test method for material flammability. The data obtained in this experiment will begin to bridge the gap between the normal gravity method, ground-based microgravity tests, and actual material flammability in microgravity 9",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p10,0,SPICE Hardware installed in MSG Work Volume SPICE Hardware Overview 10,,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_Crew Training 2011.pdf,p11,0,SPICE science objectives are met solely through photo and video downlink. SPICE is a very crew intensive experiment as the crew must adjust fuel flow and fan speed as well as take photos. MSG Work Volume should be retracted throughout all SPICE operations except Setup and Stow. SPICE Hardware Overview (continued) 11,,False
