PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p11,0,SLICE HARDWARE OVERVIEW 4/14/2010 11,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p12,0,"SLICE SCIENCE OVERVIEW OBJECTIVES • Identify the lift-off velocity limits in air for flames of methane, ethylene, and selected dilutions of each fuel as a function of the nozzle size. When possible, also identify the blow-out velocities (i.e., when the flame extinguishes). • Characterize the hysteresis of flame lifting and reattachment (i.e., stabilization) with variations in flow conditions as a function of the fuel and nozzle size. • Characterize the structure of the flame, especially its base (i.e., stabilizing region), from attached through lifted conditions as a function of the fuel, nozzle size, and flow conditions. 4/14/2010 12",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p13,0,"SLICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENTS • Fuel Nozzles – All nozzles are replaceable on-orbit by the crew – Fuel nozzle installed orientation is in the axis of the flow duct – Fuel nozzles are circular with a constant internal cross section – 2 new nozzles have been fabricated. – Off-the-shelf tubes have inner diameter with a tolerance of better than the required ±0.1 mm – The original SPICE nozzles (0.4, 0.8, and 1.6 mm) and the new SLICE nozzles will be measured such that the actual size is known with an accuracy of ±0.05 mm – Burner tubes are made of 304 Stainless Steel • FUELS – Project has procured gases with appropriate fuel type, purity, and concentration such that the gas is dry with a net amount of contaminants less than a mole and concentration known to within ±0.005 mole fraction as verified by vendor certification documentation. – Gas volume (bottle pressure) has been maximized based on the minimum thermal environment such that condensation does not occur. – The 12 fuels bottles will be filled as shown in Slide 6. • Oxygen Concentration – Project will include steps in crew procedures to install a Compound Specific Analyzer (CSA-O2) into the MSG to measure the Oxygen concentration. There is also a Major Constituent Analyzer available to measure the Columbus Module concentration. 4/14/2010 13",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p14,0,SLICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENTS • Pressure – Cabin pressure will be measured by 4 pressure transducers in the Columbus module and the differential pressure between the cabin and MSG WV measured by 2 MSG transducers. • Acceleration – SLICE testing will be scheduled around planned thruster firing or spacecraft dock or undock. – The acceleration environment will be measured by SAMS during SLICE testing which will be installed into the MSG Work Volume. • Still Imaging – A Nikon D300s camera with a blue-green BG-7 filter that can installed or removed will be manifested for use with SLICE. – The Nikon D300s camera will be calibrated with a blackbody source • Calibration will be performed using the replacement LEXAN top window and with and without the BG‐7 filter from 800 – 1200 ˚C in 100 ˚C increments. 4/14/2010 14,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p15,0,SLICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENTS • There shall be a single ceramic SiC fiber with a diameter of less than 100 and shall be known to within ± 5 microns that can be located downstream and orthogonal to the fuel nozzle. – The fiber used will be an 80 micron diameter fiber – The fiber will diameter will be measured using a calibrated measurement device capable of the required accuracy – Utilize the BASS Frame assembly and Flat Fuel Holder design – Distance from the nozzle is adjustable – Fiber is in the Field of View of the Still Camera 4/14/2010 15,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p16,0,"SLICE SUCCESS CRITERIA • Minimum Success -– Still images are acquired for at least 15 different lifted flames with different combinations of fuel flow and air velocity with a single fuel, or at least 10 different lifted flames each of two fuels, or at least 5 different lifted flames each of 3 or more fuels. • High Success -– Still images are acquired of at least 30 different lifted flames total from at least 3 different fuels. The still images are acquired with a blackbody characterized camera for soot pyrometry or liftoff limits are determined for at least one fuel. • Complete Success -– Testing conducted with all 12 fuel bottles, with identification of stability limits and smoke points (within the range of the SPICE velocity limits). Still images are taken of lifted flames with a blackbody characterized camera. 4/14/2010 16",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p17,0,SLICE PI Endorsement 17,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p18,0,SLICE PRODUCT VERIFICATION TESTING • D300s Still Camera – Functional Test – Soft Stow Vibration per 60054-TST-007 - Complete – Radiated Emissions Test – RE02 – 5/25/10 • The original plan was to use similarity of design from the original camera however the EME panel on 5/13/10 informed the project that it will be required to perform a radiated emissions test. – Thermal Operating Test per 60054-TST-009 – 5/27/10 • 10 cycles from ‐10 to 45 ˚C – Materials Bake-out for 72 hours at 120 ˚F – 6/1/10 • The SPICE off‐gas testing using the similar D100 camera as well as all other SPICE hardware has a total T value of 0.001152. Using this value for just the new BASS D300s camera will provide sufficient margin as well as allow the payload to remain below the 0.5 limit. – Black Body Calibration and Focus Setup using Replacement Top Window. – 6/5/10 • Burner Tubes – Perform dimensioning calibration - Complete • Replacement Top Window – Soft Stow Vibration per 60054-TST-007 - Complete – Anneal at 250˚F for 1 hour - Complete – Materials Bake-out for 72 hours at 120 ˚F - Complete 4/14/2010 18,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p19,0,SLICE PRODUCT VERIFICATION TESTING • Ceramic Pyrometry Fiber – Materials Bake-out for 72 hours at 120 ˚F – 5/29/10 – Soft Stow Vibration per 60054-TST-007 – 6/3/10 • Engineering Vibe Test passed • Gas Bottles – Soft Stow Vibration per 60054-TST-007 - Complete – Pre and Post Vibe Helium Leak Test – Complete – Fill per Table above – 6/3/10 4/14/2010 19,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p20,0,SAFETY VERIFICATIONS • SLICE is designated as a SPICE re-flight. • Safety Package Submitted to the PSRP • Revised Package in review to include additional hardware and correct original SPICE hazards • Out of Board Approval is expected once verifications are closed • 72 Safety Verifications – 43 Closed Verifications – 12 Crew Procedure Controls – Procedures are being developed – 4 Materials Certification – MIUL draft in review. Updates need to be incorporated – 5 Proper Fill of Gas Bottles – 6/3/10 – 3 Fracture Control Summary Report – Routed to SWG – 2 As-Built Inspections – 6/11/10 – Shatterable Containment Analysis – 6/1/10 – Sharp Edge Drawing Analysis – 6/1/10 – Stowage Configuration – 6/20/10 4/14/2010 20,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p21,0,INTEGRATION VERIFICATIONS • Utilize SPICE ICD as a baseline for the integration requirements with MSG • New verifications not addressed in the safety verifications include the following: – Fit Check for D300s Still Camera in MSG EU - Complete – Hardware weight and volume – Complete @ KSC – Still Camera Video Interface Test in MSG EU - Complete – Labeling – Complete @ KSC • Physical and Functional Interface testing in the MSG EU with the new camera were successful. • Modified SPICE crew procedures were dry run to evaluate their status. Other than minor tweaks the procedures are adequate. 4/14/2010 21,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p22,0,SLICE Operations on ISS Crew Procedures 2.001 SLICE HARDWARE SETUP 2.002 SLICE SOOT REMOVAL 2.003 SLICE FAN CALIBRATION 2.004 SLICE OPS PREP 2.005 SLICE OPERATIONS 2.006 SLICE VIDEOTAPE EXCHANGE 2.007 SLICE FLASH CARD EXCHANGE 2.008 SLICE HARDWARE STOW All currently under development approaching final revision,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p23,0,SLICE Operations on ISS Typical test operation description Load new DVCAM tapes in recorders Load empty flash card in still camera Install desired fuel bottle Power up SLICE experiment Configure still camera Set fan flow and fuel flow on control box Start MSG video recording Start fuel flow and ignite Adjust fan/fuel as per test matrix Take bracketed still images per test matrix Repeat fan/fuel flow adjustments and take images per test matrix Stop fuel flow to extinguish flame Stop video recording Start MSG Work Volume purge after every other ignition Copy files from flash card to SSC for downlink,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p24,0,"SLICE Nominal Ops Flow SLICE Payload FAM SLICE PL Crew Conference MSG Facility Act MSG Video Equip Setup* SLICE Test Sequence (repeat for each fuel type) SLICE Stow MSG Facility Deact :15 :15 :10 :05 2:00 1:00 :25 SLICE Setup Sequence 1‐6 Seq 7 * We expect the external MSG Video Equipment to already be deployed, therefore this activity may not need to be scheduled. We are required to carry this activity in URC. Order of Operations SPICE Gas Bottle Assembly Number Fuel Type & Percentage 1st 4, 5, 6 Methane 100% @ 210 psi. 2nd 1 Ethylene 100% @ 210 psi. 3rd 12 Ethane 100% @ 210 psi. 4th 11 Propane 100% @ 110 psi. 5th 7, 8 Methane 70%, Diluent GN2 @ 210 psi. 6th 9, 10 Methane 40%, Diluent GN2 @ 210 psi. 7th 2, 3 Ethylene 20%, Diluent GN2 @ 210 psi",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p25,0,SCHEDULE HIGHLIGHTS (Open Work) • Still Camera Assembly including Filter – Radiated Emissions Test – RE02 – 5/25/10 – Thermal Operating Test per 60054-TST-009 – 5/27/10 – Materials Bake-out for 72 hours at 120 ˚F – 6/1/10 – Black Body Calibration – 6/5/10 • Ceramic Pyrometry Fiber Assembly – 5/27/10 – Materials Bake out – 5/29/10 – Soft Stow Vibration – 6/3/10 • SAR-2 – 6/9/10 • Bench Review – 6/22/10 4/14/2010 25,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p26,0,"SR/DR RFA & Actions • RFA Concern: The SR/DR Plan needs to address the system requirements • Recommended Action: The SR/DR plan needs to be updated to better reflect the systems requirements portion of the review. • Project Response: Rather than rewrite the plan, the science requirements, as well as the entrance and success criteria were adequately addressed during the SR/DR review. Slides were presented that discussed the science requirements and SR/DR success criteria. They are included in the RFA response. • ACTION Item: Verify that the new fuel (Ethane) is compatible with the materials it comes in contact, specifically the stainless steel and o-rings. • Compatibility of Ethane has been verified for Stainless Steel as well as the Fluorocarbon FKM o‐rings. • A Materials Certification Letter will be forthcoming upon review and acceptance of the MIUL by GRC. This is expected by 6/8/10. 4/14/2010 26",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p27,0,SLICE NCR Status • No Non-conformances were written against the SLICE project 4/14/2010 27,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p28,0,Risk ID: SLICE-002 Rank: 1 Risk Title: Camera EMI Test Exposure: Medium Status: Open Likelihood: 2 - Low Risk Owner: Tibor Lorik Consequence: 4 - High Date Opened: 04/06/2010 Time Frame: 4 - Near Risk Statement: Given that the project proposes not to perform EMI testing on the replacement still camera there is the possibility that this approach will not be accepted resulting in a delay in hardware delivery. Risk Originator: Tibor Lorik Date Submitted: 04/06/2010 Resolution Date: 05/27/2010 Classification: Schedule Milestone: The project plans to perform radiated emissions testing to qualify the hardware for flight as instructed by the EME panel Last Modified By: Tibor Lorik Phase: None Last Modified On: 05/14/2010 Subsystem: None Approach: Mitigate SLICE Risk Summary Risk ID: SLICE-001 Rank: - Risk Title: Ethane Gas Usage Exposure: Low Status: Closed (Mitigated) The project has analyzed the fuel for safety impacts and found that this gas selection falls within the bounds of the previously used gases for LFL and SMAC. Likelihood: 2 - Low Risk Owner: Tibor Lorik Consequence: 3 - Moderate Date Opened: 04/06/2010 Time Frame: 4 - Near Risk Statement: Given that there is a new gas (Ethane) in the science matrix there is the possibility that this gas will result in new safety hazard controls. Risk Originator: Tibor Lorik Date Submitted: 04/06/2010 Resolution Date: 05/17/2010 Classification: Safety Milestone: None Last Modified By: Tibor Lorik Phase: None Last Modified On:,,False
