PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p43,0,BASS Science Overview 43 BASS Test Matrix (Continued) Sample #Test ID Description Estimated Fuel Burned (mg) Flow speed (cm/s) Suppression 34 82 Candle (Japan wax) within tube (front ignition) 200 5 (temporarily 0) After 1 minute; Extinguishment will be initiated by gradually increasing the mass flow rate of nitrogen in a series of step increments 35 83 Candle (Japan wax) within tube (front ignition) 200 10 46 84 Candle (Japan wax) within tube (front ignition) 200 30 37 85 Candle (paraffin) within tube (front ignition) 200 5 (temporarily 0) 38 86 Candle (Japan wax) within tube (wake ignition) 200 5 (temporarily 0) 39 87 Candle (Japan wax) within tube (wake ignition) 200 10 40 88 Candle (Japan wax) within tube (wake ignition) 200 30 41 89 Candle (paraffin) within tube (wake ignition) 200 5 (temporarily 0),,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p44,0,"BASS SCIENCE REQUIREMENTS • General Requirements – Each Solid Fuel Assembly is mounted inside the Experiment Assembly via a magnetic interface such that it can be installed and removed by the crew – Solid fuel ignition will be done with a hot-wire (Kanthol) – Nitrogen gas to be used instead of fuel from 0 to 500 SCCM N2. Instead of connecting the Experiment Assembly to the gas bottle it is connected via the GN2 hose to the MSG GN2 interface. – 3.2 mm normal length and 3.2 mm short length burner tubes will be manifested – The Control Box will be modified to allow the fan flow adjustment to stop the fan when set to its minimum as a safety mechanism to extinguish flame. • Parameter Measurement – Measurement of the GN2 Flow rate, Fan Voltage, Co-Flow velocity, and flame radiation will be measured and overlaid on the Video. – The acquisition rate as measured by SPICE hardware is 4 Hz. – The measurement accuracy of ±10% was met by the SPICE hardware prior to its launch. • Imaging Requirements – The Field of view of the video can not be changed from SPICE. – The Still Image requirements are the same as for SPICE these were met. Enhanced capability is expected with the D300s. 4/14/2010 44",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p45,0,BASS SCIENCE REQUIREMENTS • Acceleration – BASS testing will be scheduled around planned thruster firing or spacecraft dock or undock. – The acceleration environment will be measured by SAMS during BASS testing which will be installed into the MSG Work Volume. • Oxygen Concentration – Project will include steps in crew procedures to install a Compound Specific Analyzer (CSA-O2) into the MSG to measure the Oxygen concentration. There is also a Major Constituent Analyzer available to measure the Columbus Module concentration. • Crew inserts fuel sample and adjusts ignitor and fuel base to desired position – Sample Frame Assembly is designed to allow for adjustment of the position of the fuel – Ignitor is a wire extension that the crew will be able to position as required to ignite the fuel 4/14/2010 45,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p46,0,BASS SUCCESS CRITERIA • Minimal Success: 20 total tests including: eight tests with flat samples (four materials at two different flow speeds); four tests with spherical PMMA samples (four different flow speeds with nitrogen extinguishment); four tests with candles (four different flow speeds with nitrogen extinguishment); and four tests of PMMA wake flames (four different flow speeds with nitrogen extinguishment). • Complete Success: 60 flames as indicated in test matrix. 4/14/2010 46,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p47,0,BASS PI Endorsement 47,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p48,0,BASS PRODUCT VERIFICATION TESTING • Sample Frame Assembly – Fit Check with Engineering Assembly - Complete – Soft Stow Vibration per 60054-TST-007 - Complete – Materials Bake-out for 72 hours at 120 ˚F - Complete – EMI Test for Magnet Strength - Complete • Fuel Samples Assembly (41 flight units) – Fit Check with Engineering Assembly - Complete – Soft Stow Vibration per 60054-TST-007 (Partially Complete) • GN2 Hose Assembly – Soft Stow Vibration per 60054-TST-007 - Complete – Pre and Post Vibe Helium Leak Test - Complete • BASS Control Box – Functional Test with Engineering Assembly - Complete – Soft Stow Vibration per 60054-TST-007 - Complete – Materials Bake-out for 72 hours at 120 ˚F – 6/1/10 • Ignitor Wire Extension Assembly – Functional Test with Engineering Assembly – 5/21/10 – Materials Bake-out for 72 hours at 120 ˚F – 6/1/104/14/2010 48,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p49,0,SAFETY VERIFICATIONS • BASS safety package utilizes the SPICE package but it is a stand-alone payload • Safety Package Submitted to PSRP. • Safety Review scheduled for 6/1/10 • 52 Safety Verifications – 25 Closed Verifications – 9 Crew Procedure Controls – Procedures are being developed – 4 Materials Certification – MIUL draft in review. Updates need to be incorporated – 4 BASS Control Box Test – 6/2/10 – 2 Solid Fuel Cool-down Time Test/Analysis – Report in Review – 2 ECLSS Assessment of Combustion By-products – 6/10 – 3 Fracture Control Summary Report – Shatterable Containment Analysis – 6/1/10 – Sharp Edge Drawing Analysis – 6/1/10 – Stowage Configuration – 6/20/10 4/14/2010 49,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p50,0,"INTEGRATION VERIFICATIONS • Utilize SPICE ICD as a baseline for the integration requirements with MSG • New verifications not addressed in the safety verifications include the following: – Fit Check for BASS Control Box & GN2 Hose in MSG EU - Complete – Hardware weight and volume - Complete @ KSC – Labeling - Complete @ KSC – Combustion By-products compatibility assessment with MSG Filters – Complete • Physical and Functional Interface testing in the MSG EU with the new Control Box were successful. • Preliminary BASS crew procedures were dry run to evaluate their status. The procedures generally are in good shape with a couple of issues. – The number of images expected to be acquired may limit the number of runs that can be performed in each session. There are eight 4 GB flash disks that will need to be transferred to a laptop, down-linked, and verified all were received prior to flash disks being erased to be reused. – The project is still evaluating the various modes available with the new camera of acquiring images 4/14/2010 50",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p51,0,BASS Operations on ISS 51 2.001 BASS HARDWARE SETUP 2.002 BASS GN2 LEAK TEST 2.003 BASS FAN CALIBRATION 2.004 BASS OPS PREP 2.005 BASS OPERATIONS 2.006 BASS VIDEOTAPE EXCHANGE 2.007 BASS FLASHCARD EXCHANGE 2.008 BASS HARDWARE STOW 2.009 BASS SETUP FOLLOWING SLICE OPS (All currently under development approaching final revision) Crew Procedures,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p52,0,52 BASS Operations on ISS • Load new DVCAM tapes in recorders • Load empty flash card in still camera • Install and configure BASS sample in Experiment Assembly • Power up BASS experiment • Configure still camera • Set fan flow and GN2 flow • Start MSG video recording • Start still camera • Ignite sample • Allow sample to burn for 60 seconds • Extinguish as per test matrix (some samples extinguish due to complete consumption) • Stop still camera • Stop video recording • Set fan flow to maximum and start MSG Work Volume purge • Wait per test matrix to allow cool down Typical Test Operation Description,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p53,0,"SLICE Payload FAM SLICE PL Crew Conference MSG Facility Act MSG Video Equip Setup* BASS Test Sequence (repeat for each fuel type) BASS Stow MSG Facility Deact :15 :15 :10 :05 2:00 1:00 :25 BASS Setup** Samples per test matrix Final Burn * We expect the external MSG Video Equipment to already be deployed, therefore this activity may not need to be scheduled. We are required to carry this activity in URC. ** If setup for the first time. Current plan transitions from SLICE which will reduce the time to 30 minutes BASS Camera Cal Tests Samples per test matrix BASS Operations on ISS BASS Nominal Ops Flow",,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p54,0,SCHEDULE HIGHLIGHTS (Open Work) • BASS Control Box Assembly – Thermal Cycle Test – 5/27/10 – Materials Bake out for 72 hours at 120 ˚F – 6/1/10 – Install Labels @ KSC • Flat Fuel Assembly – Test & Install Ignitors – 5/27/10 – Vibration Test – 6/3/10 • Wax & Flat PMMA Sample Assembly – Flight Wax Samples required redesign – 5/20/10 – Assemble Flight Units – 5/27/10 – Vibration Test – 6/3/10 • SAR-2 – 6/9/10 • Bench Review – 6/22/10 4/14/2010 54,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p55,0,BASS NCR Status • 1 Non-conformance issued against the BASS Payload • BASS-NCR-001: OPEN – 1 of the 4 Spherical 2 cm PMMA Samples had loosened after vibration testing. – Likely cause is that insufficient epoxy was applied – Closure will occur upon successful completion of a second vibration test planned for 6/3/10. 4/14/2010 55,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p56,0,Risk ID: BASS-002 Rank: 1 Risk Title: Crew Setup of Ignitor Exposure: Medium Status: Open Likelihood: 4 - High Risk Owner: Tibor Lorik Consequence: 3 - Moderate Date Opened: 04/06/2010 Time Frame: 3 - Mid Risk Statement: Given that the operations scenario has the crew position the ignitor very near the fuel to initiate ignition there is the possibility they will experience difficulty and require several attempts at correct placement. Risk Originator: Tibor Lorik Date Submitted: 04/06/2010 Resolution Date: 06/21/2010 Classification: Schedule Milestone: None Last Modified By: Tibor Lorik Phase: None Last Modified On: 05/03/2010 Subsystem: None Approach: Watch Risk ID: BASS-001 Rank: - Risk Title: Flame Extinguishment of PMMA Spheres Exposure: Medium Status: Closed (Mitigated) A zero‐g facility 5 second drop tower test demonstrated the nitrogen extinguishment of the 2 cm PMMA sphere ‐ successfully completed on 25 Feb 2010. Likelihood: 3 - Moderate Risk Owner: Tibor Lorik Consequence: 4 - High Date Opened: 04/06/2010 Time Frame: 5 - Imminent Risk Statement: Given that the PMMA spheres can burn for a significantly long time there is the possibility that it will be difficult to extinguish the flame. Risk Originator: Tibor Lorik Date Submitted: 04/06/2010 Resolution Date: 02/25/2010 Classification: Safety Milestone: None Last Modified By: Tibor Lorik Phase: None Last Modified On: 04/06/2010 Subsystem: None Approach: - BASS Risk Summary,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p57,0,Trend Legend (Based upon report start date of 04/04/2010) Upward Downward No Change New Likelihood 5 41 3 2 1 12345 Consequence Trend Rank ID Approach Title 1 BASS-002 Watch Crew Setup of Ignitor BASS Risk Matrix Chart,,False
PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_BASS_SysAcceptanceReview.1.pdf,p58,0,ZIN Quality Assessment 58 Quality SLICE/BASS followed the Zin Technology Quality Management System for engineering and building flight hardware. This For this re-flight SLICE/BASS: •Used Manufacturing Work Orders (MWO) prepared for cleaning and assembly operations •Use inspection and test documents prepared for in-process and final inspection/test activities – all completed plans are nominally reviewed/approved by a ZIN Tech Quality representative •Will achieve configuration verification through as-built inspections performed by a ZIN Tech’s Quality representative as part of the MWO process,,False
