PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,slide 12,0,"Science Background: Combustion of solid fuels in microgravity has been an active area of research Numerical models predict that 1-g buoyant flows profoundly affect fuel flammability Microgravity experiments burning thin fuels have verified this prediction, but ground-based facilities are too short to examine the burning of thicker fuels Flow speed (cm/s) O2 Mole Fraction 0.23 0.22 0.21 0.20 0.19 0.18 0.17 0.16 0.15 1 10 100 300 Flammable Not Flammable 1-g buoyant flow limit Typical Flammability Boundary for a Solid Fuel Burning And Suppression of Solids (BASS)",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,slide 13,0,"Hypotheses: Materials in microgravity, with adequate ventilation, may burn more readily compared to normal gravity with other conditions being identical (pressure, oxygen concentration, temperature, etc.) As a result, the 1-g test currently used by NASA to rate materials for space may need to be re-evaluated from a margin-of-safety point of view. Burning And Suppression of Solids (BASS)",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,slide 14,0,"Objective: Bridge the gap between normal gravity, NASA-STD-6001 Test # 1 method, ground based microgravity tests, and actual material flammability in microgravity. Material selection for spacecraft is based on 1-g tests Model and drop tower results suggest 1-g may not represent the most flammable condition in some cases Long duration tests will refine test criteria and modeling efforts Test the effectiveness of an inert, gaseous extinguishing agent (similar to that used on ISS) in putting out flames over different materials, geometries, and flow. Local extinguishment of flame has some advantages Geometry and flow can affect optimal suppression delivery Burning And Suppression of Solids (BASS)",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,slide 15,0,"Drop tower results and modeling: 2-cm PMMA sphere; 17% oxygen, 2 cm/s forced flow Experiment images and computer simulations show transition from 1-g to 0-g. Left contours: reaction rate (kgmol/m3/s); right contours: temperature (K) Burning And Suppression of Solids (BASS)",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,slide 16,0,"Why ISS for BASS? Relationship between ground test and microgravity test can be determined by observing flames burning for longer times. Practical, realistic fuels in typical geometries will be examined, including wake flames which have characteristics of a fire burning behind another object. Models can be validated and developed. Burning And Suppression of Solids (BASS)",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,slide 17,0,Science Applications for Space: The microgravity burning and extinction characteristics of a wide variety of fuel samples will be determined. The current NASA spacecraft materials selection is based on 1-g behavior without consideration of low gravity effects. These results will suggest improved strategies by linking actual long-duration burn behavior in microgravity to Earth-based selection methods. Guidance for strategies to extinguish accidental fires in microgravity will be provided. Burning And Suppression of Solids (BASS),,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,slide 18,0,"Science Applications for Earth: BASS results contribute to the combustion computational models used in the design of fire detection and suppression systems in microgravity and on Earth. Detailed combustion models are validated using the simpler flow environment afforded by tests in microgravity. Once validated, they can be used to build more complex combustion models needed to capture the important details of flames burning in normal gravity. These models have wide applicability to the general understanding of many terrestrial combustion problems. Burning And Suppression of Solids (BASS)",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,slide 19,0,"Differences from SLICE: Solid fuel samples burned (instead of gaseous jets) All but the flat samples will be burned multiple times The nozzle is used to deliver nitrogen (for extinguishment) in some cases The igniter typically needs to be positioned manually prior to the test Within 1 mm of fuel (but not touching fuel) when in position Out of flame zone and camera fields of view when retracted Immediately after ignition: don’t touch window when retracted, or hold away to cool for first ~ 10 s or it may stick to plastic door or break Burning And Suppression of Solids (BASS)",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,slide 20,0,"Differences from SLICE (con’t): Flow: Control box is different Allows potentiometer to go all the way to zero so the flow may be shut off gradually There is some fan hysteresis because the fan must start from a stopped position and thus overcome static coefficient of friction and resistance Nozzle flow is labeled “GN2” instead of “Fuel” (but note that screen display will still indicate “Fuel”) Fan calibration to be performed with 0, 1, and 2 screens; we will likely use mostly one of these configurations based on the calibration results Wax sample tests will be done last to minimize possible flow system blockage by wax re-condensation on downstream flow system Burning And Suppression of Solids (BASS)",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,slide 21,0,"Differences from SLICE (con’t): Brief test objective and test plan review prior to each days operations can be provided if desired Over the shoulder view is requested, if possible For each burn, N2 flow setting, fan airflow setting, and radiometer setting provided During burn, feedback on changes to airflow, N2, and photos will be provided Let sample cool after test Removes sample using tissue to avoid soot on hands, use tissue to remove soot from PMMA, candles, and slabs, and re-stow in bag Burning And Suppression of Solids (BASS)",,False
PSI-26,Presentations,PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Presentations/PSI-26_Presentations_BASS Overview.pptx,slide 22,0,"Flame photography and videography: 7 s brackets or up to 300 pictures per test, with retriggers every 100 For 1 fps tests, we request a countdown (3-2-1) to start of photos so we can time Ground will monitor time for 1 fps tests, and keep track of camera picture number Blue filter on camera for only candle tests Burning And Suppression of Solids (BASS)",,False
