PSI-26,Engineering Documents,PSI-26_Engineering Documents_Short Nozzle MFA10771.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Engineering Documents/PSI-26_Engineering Documents_Short Nozzle MFA10771.pdf,p1,1,"Part list: | Item | Qty | Part number | Description | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | 1 | S1182MFA0104 | 3.2 MM BURNER TUBE WELD ASSEMBLY, SPICE | Dimensions (mm): - Overall length 25.02 - Tube tip protrusion (3.18) reference - Body outer diameter (Ø7.7) reference NOTES: 1. CERTIFICATE OF CONFORMANCE REQUIRED FOR ALL COMMERCIAL PARTS AND ALL PROCESSES. 2. CLEAN AND PASSIVATE MACHINED SURFACES ONLY PER AMS-QQ-P-35. 3. CLEAN ALL SURFACES WITH ISOPROPYL ALCOHOL AND LET AIR DRY. WIPE WITH CLEAN COTTON SWABS OR LINT FREE CLOTH. 4. HANDLE WITH CLEAN LINT-FREE GLOVES. 5. BEFORE STORAGE, PROTECT WITH CONTAMINATION BARRIER. USE CLEAN, NEW, TRANSPARENT POLYETHYLENE BAG FOR SMALL ITEMS OR 4 MIL THICK MINIMUM POLYETHYLENE SHEET AND SEAL. IDENTIFY PART BY TAGGING WITH PART NUMBER AND SERIAL/LOT NUMBER AS SPECIFIED IN WORK ORDER PER SAE AS478, METHOD 35.",,True
PSI-26,Science Documents,PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Science Documents/PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,p1,0,February 2010 1 Science Requirements for Burning and Suppression of Solids “BASS” (Material Flammability in Low-Speed Air Flow at Microgravity Including an Assessment of Inert-Gas Extinguishing Agent: An ISS Glovebox Experiment Utilizing Existing SPICE Hardware with Minor Modifications) February 2010,,False
PSI-26,Science Documents,PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Science Documents/PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,p3,0,"February 2010 3 Background Flame spread behavior, flammability, and suppression data are of interest to NASA for application to fire safety efforts. The reduced buoyant flows, coupled with low-speed air circulation currents, create an environment quite different than normal Earth gravity. In addition, there are several oxidizer atmospheres proposed for crew-manned vehicles and habitats [NASA CxP-70024], which further complicates the problem. Understanding flammability and suppression in a variety of environments is especially important given NASA’s current exploration initiatives. There is some evidence for the hypothesis that materials in microgravity, with adequate ventilation, burn as well if not better than the same material in normal gravity with other conditions being identical (pressure, oxygen concentration, temperature, etc.) To test this hypothesis with any but the thinnest materials requires extended microgravity time – longer than available in drop towers or parabolic aircraft. If this hypothesis is true, it implies serious safety concerns over the utility and margin of safety provided NASA- STD-6001 Test #1.",,False
PSI-26,Science Documents,PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Science Documents/PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,p3,1,"There are important differences in the suppression of fires in space compared to on Earth. On Earth it is understood that best results are generally obtained when the extinguisher “attacks” the base of the flame, which is both the stabilization point and the point of fresh air entrainment. For a fire burning in microgravity, the best point of application of suppressant may not be immediately apparent, especially for a partially obstructed flame or a wake-stabilized flame. Depending on the geometry of the flame and the characteristics of the extinguisher (distance from flame, dispersion angle) it is possible that the suppressant stream will be ineffective or might actually make the flame worse through the entrainment of oxygen. Objectives There are two main objectives: 1.) Begin to bridge the gap between the normal gravity NASA-STD-6001 Test#1 method, ground-based microgravity tests, and actual material flammability in space. The relation between these can be investigated by observing flames burning for the longer times available in flight. Practical materials in typical geometries will be examined, including wake flames which have characteristics of a fire burning behind another object (for example, a fire burning in tight area which may not be easily accessible). Fuels can be significantly thicker than the thin sheets studied in ground-based facilities.",,False
PSI-26,Science Documents,PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Science Documents/PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,p4,0,"February 2010 4 2.) Assess the effectiveness of an inert, gaseous extinguishing agent (similar to that used on ISS) in putting out flames over different materials, geometries, preheating, and flow. Hardware changes The plan is to use the existing NASA glovebox SPICE hardware with four changes: 1.) Accommodation of replaceable solid fuel samples 2.) Igniter element adjustable (e.g. by bending wire leads) into the correct position 3.) Nitrogen gas jet for flame extinguishment 4.) Providing a way to shut off the flow completely The air co-flow is produced by using a fan to blow air through the duct. An anemometer is used to measure the actual flow speed. The airflow speed can be adjusted from 5 to 50 cm/s. Nitrogen is supplied through the nozzle from pressurized bottles via a mass flow controller, from 0 to 500 cc/min. A radiometer measures flame output. Video and still cameras are used to image the flame. Multiple fuel assemblies (total of 30, see test matrix) will be used. Figure 1 presents a possible concept for mounting to the existing nozzle structure. Another concept for mounting to the top window is shown in Figure 2. Front view Side view Figure 1. Possible Concept: Fuel Assembly for Flat Sample Solid fuel or wax- impregnated “wick” Fuel support Gas nozzle for suppression SPICE nozzle Embedded igniter Deployable igniter Air co-flow from SPICE duct Flat sample shown installed in SPICE hardware Fan Section Air Co- Flow",,False
PSI-26,Science Documents,PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Science Documents/PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,p5,0,"February 2010 5 There are three categories of sample: flat sheets, solid spheres, and candles within tubes (Figure 3). For the sheets, concurrent-flow spread rate and limiting flame length are the main interest. The cotton-fiberglass fabric blend SIBAL1 fuel is our leading candidate for the sample material, since it was specially developed just for this purpose. For some of these spread tests, the suppressant system will be engaged at a gradually 1 Solid Inflammability Boundary at Low Speed (Principal Investigator: James T’ien) was a previously funded flight project to examine the flammability of a thin solid material burning within an airflow tunnel in microgravity. Modified Top Lid Support posts attached to lid Cylindrical fuel assembly Connection SPICE nozzle Axial Adjustment Flat fuel assembly Figure 2. Fuel Sample Mounting Concept Candle within tube assembly",,False
PSI-26,Science Documents,PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Science Documents/PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,p6,0,"February 2010 6 increasing level until extinction is reached. A flat slab of fiberglass fabric saturated with wax wax and a one-sided flat PMMA sample are included. For the solid spheres, flame growth rate, flame shape, and luminosity are the main interests. Again, the suppression system will be engaged after some period of burning. Some of these samples will have inert hemispheres so that a wake flame can be investigated (the inert hemispheres prevent flames from spreading into the forward- stagnation region). Wake flames may occur in real situations where flames are confined behind obstacles and within tight spaces. The burning behavior of these samples and their suppression characteristics are of interest. For the candles within tubes, the effect of suppression amount on the flame structure at different flow speeds will be investigated. PMMA Sphere Fuel support arm Gas nozzle for suppression SPICE nozzle Fuel support arm locking nut Figure 3. a.) Fuel Assembly for PMMA Sphere Igniter Side view Front view b.) Fuel Geometries (not to scale) 1 and 2-cm PMMA spheres 1 and 2-cm width SIBAL fuel sheets 1-cm width flat wax and PMMA sample Candle within tube",,False
PSI-26,Science Documents,PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Science Documents/PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,p7,0,"February 2010 7 Science Requirements (with reference to SPICE SRD) Existing SPICE Requirement BASS Requirement General requirements Configuration: Round gas jet diffusion flame in a co-flowing oxidizer stream Configuration: Solid fuel materials on individual and exchangeable sample cards Pressure: Shuttle ambient pressure ---Same--- Ignition: Hot wire ---Same--- Fuel Flow Range: 0 to 500 SCCM N2 equivalent flow Nitrogen gas to be used instead of fuel: 0 to 500 SCCM N2 Oxidizer Velocity: 5 to 50 cm/s ---Same--- Nozzle Diameter: (0.4, 0.8, 1.6 mm) 2 required nozzles: Diameter: (3.2 mm normal length and 3.2 mm short length) Flame Length: 5 to 100 mm ---Same--- n/a Flow must be shut off completely as a safety mechanism to extinguish flame Parameters to be measured: (Accuracy +/- 10% of reading, 4 Hz) Fuel Mass Flow: 25 to 500 SCCM Nitrogen gas to be used instead of fuel: 25 to 500 SCCM N2 Co-flow Fan Voltage: 0.5 to 12 V ---Same--- Co-flow Velocity: 5 to 50 cm/s ---Same--- Flame Radiation: wavelength visible to near infra-red ---Same--- Imaging Requirements Video record of flame: color (FOV: 10 mm of the nozzle to the end of the duct) Video record of flame: color (FOV: 20 mm upstream of the fuel to the end of the duct) High resolution color images of flame: 2-3 per test (FOV: 10 mm of the nozzle to the end of the duct; resolution: 3000 by 2000 pixels or greater, 8 bits/pixel in 3 colors or greater). Images stored in raw (uncompressed) mode. ---Same---",,False
PSI-26,Science Documents,PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Science Documents/PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,p8,0,"February 2010 8 Existing SPICE Requirement BASS Requirement Acceleration Requirements n/a Testing not to be conducted during thruster fire or spacecraft dock or undock from ISS. Based on the SPICE results, the normal ISS operations provide an adequate acceleration environment. n/a When possible, the acceleration environment is to be measured by SAMS during testing. Ambient Oxygen Concentration Requirements n/a On each day of testing, the oxygen concentration of the ISS cabin atmosphere should be measured. Testing is restricted to oxygen concentrations at or above 21% O2. n/a When possible, the oxygen concentration within the MSG working volume shall be known during testing. It should be possible for a crewmember to measure the oxygen concentration within the MSG working volume with a CSA-CP (Compound Specific Analyzer – Combustion Products). Testing is restricted to oxygen concentrations at or above 21% O2. n/a The oxygen concentration can be measured by the MCA (Major Constituent Analyzer) or oxygen sensors in the Columbus module or Service Module (i.e., Zarya). The oxygen concentration could also be measured by a crewmember using a CSA-CP (Compound Specific Analyzer – Combustion Products). If possible, it is desired that the same O2 measurement sensor be used for all tests to eliminate variation between instruments.",,False
PSI-26,Science Documents,PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-26/Science Documents/PSI-26_Science Documents_SRD_BASS.pdf,p9,0,"February 2010 9 Existing SPICE Requirement BASS Requirement Operation requirements Crew sets the co-flow based upon the test matrix and ignites the flame. Once ignited the fuel flow is adjusted until the smoke height is found. High resolution digital flame images are taken above and below the smoke height. The total crew time is estimated to be 20 to 25 hours. Crew inserts fuel sample and adjusts igniter and fuel base to desired position. The co-flow air is set to the specified value. Once ignited the fuel is allowed to burn for about one minute. High resolution digital flame images are taken. Nitrogen flow is then initiated to extinguish the flame. Total crew time estimate: 30 hours. n/a Flow must be shut off completely as a safety mechanism to extinguish flame Downlink Real time video downlink is required for the first few tests and highly desired for subsequent tests. But, it is acceptable for the crew to perform several tests without downlink as long as the video is downlinked before the next series of tests. Video downlink will include fuel flow rate, radiometer readings, and co-flow rate. Real time video downlink is required for the first few tests and highly desired for subsequent tests. But, it is acceptable for the crew to perform several tests without downlink as long as the video is downlinked before the next series of tests. Video downlink will include nitrogen flow rate, radiometer readings, and co-flow rate.",,False
