PSI-39,metadata,PSI-39_metadata_PSI-118_CFI.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-39/metadata/PSI-39_metadata_PSI-118_CFI.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,0,ONTOLOGY SOURCE REFERENCE Term Source Name EFO PSI Term Source File http://data.bioontology.org/ontologies/EFO https://psi.nasa.gov/ Term Source Version 132 1.0 Term Source Description Experimental Factor Ontology User defined terms INVESTIGATION Investigation Identifier Investigation Title Investigation Description Investigation Submission Date Investigation Public Release Date INVESTIGATION PUBLICATIONS Investigation PubMed ID Investigation Publication DOI Investigation Publication Author List Investigation Publication Title Investigation Publication Status Investigation Publication Status Term Accession Number Investigation Publication Status Term Source REF INVESTIGATION CONTACTS Investigation Person Last Name Investigation Person First Name Investigation Person Mid Initials Investigation Person Email Investigation Person Phone Investigation Person Fax Investigation Person Address Investigation Person Affiliation Investigation Person Roles Investigation Person Roles Term Accession Number Investigation Person Roles Term Source REF STUDY Study Identifier PSI-39 Study Title Cool Flames Investigation Study Description Study Submission Date 18-Sep-2024 Study Public Release Date 18-Sep-2024 Study File Name s_PSI-39.txt Comment[Acknowledgments] Comment[Approach] The Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) contains the Multi-User Droplet Combustion Apparatus (MDCA) which facilitates the controlled combustion of fuel droplets.,,False
PSI-39,metadata,PSI-39_metadata_PSI-118_CFI.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-39/metadata/PSI-39_metadata_PSI-118_CFI.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,1,"A system of gas bottles and fuel syringes delivers the desired gas mixture to the combustion chamber ranging from 0.25 to 0.5 atm. Droplets of fuel are dispensed and suspended between hypodermic needles or tethered using silicon-carbide fibers. A hot-wire igniter is positioned near the droplet for ignition. After ignition the wire is retracted. Fuels observed include n-dodecane, farnesane, and mixtures of n-dodecane/iso-dodecane. Fuels diluted with molecular oxygen, atomic nitrogen, and helium to study the influence on flame extinction and dynamics. Flames were observed with a black-and-white camera for droplet size measurements. A UV camera was used for capturing the OH* radical emissions and flame size. Other high resolution cameras were used for monitoring and documentation. Narrow-band and wide-band radiometers for analyzed flame radiation.",,False
PSI-39,metadata,PSI-39_metadata_PSI-118_CFI.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-39/metadata/PSI-39_metadata_PSI-118_CFI.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,2,"Comment[DOI] 10.60555/m6pj-a914 Comment[Data Source Accession] Comment[Data Source Link] Comment[Experiment Hardware] Multi-User Droplet Combustion Apparatus (MDCA), Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) Comment[Experiment Hardware Link] https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/combustion-science/mdca/, https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/ Comment[Flight Platform] International Space Station (ISS) Comment[Funding] ISSNL National Laboratory - National Science Foundation (NL-NSF) Comment[Hypothesis] Flame combustion of liquid hydrocarbon fuels such as n-dodecane, n-dodecane/iso-dodecane mixtures, and faresane in microgravity can provide insights into low-temperature chemical kinetics, revealing critical parameters like droplet extinction diameters and flame dynamics that are not observable on Earth. Comment[Identifiers] Comment[Investigation Acronym] CFI Comment[Investigation End Date] 10-20-2021 Comment[Investigation Start Date] 10-01-2020 Comment[Managing NASA Center] Glenn Research Center (GRC) Comment[Mission End] Comment[Mission Link] Comment[Mission Name] Comment[Mission Start] Comment[Objective] ""The objectives of this investigation are to: 1) Examine the combustion behavior of n-dodecane and mixtures of n-dodecane/iso-dodecane at pressures ranging from 0.25 atm to 5 atm. 2) Analyze the effects of helium dilution on cool flame behavior. 3) Examine the behavior of combustion characteristics.",,False
PSI-39,metadata,PSI-39_metadata_PSI-118_CFI.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-39/metadata/PSI-39_metadata_PSI-118_CFI.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,3,"4) Investigate the relationship between droplet size, pressure, and flame extinction, and (5) understand the consequence of soot formation during cool flame combustion."" Comment[Project Identifier] Comment[Project Link] Comment[Project Title] Comment[Project Type] Microgravity Investigation Comment[Proposal Title] Comment[Research Area] Combustion Science Comment[Research Impacts] ""A basic understanding of cool flames is important for understanding ignition phenomena of practical fuels and fuel additives. Cool flames play a key role in the development and selection of new fuels and the design of advanced engines such as clean, high-efficiency Homogeneous Charge Compression Ignition (HCCI) engines. The research will also help combustion chemists to develop new computational models to compute combustion performance in for many practical applications on earth, including an understanding of how cool flame reactivity determines octane and cetane number for fuels in spark ignition and diesel engines.",,False
PSI-39,metadata,PSI-39_metadata_PSI-118_CFI.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-39/metadata/PSI-39_metadata_PSI-118_CFI.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,4,"Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) will provide critical validation for complex chemical kinetic mechanisms, advance the understanding of flame dynamics and instabilities in low-temperature combustion, and support the design of safer spacecraft systems by refining knowledge of combustion in microgravity environments."" Comment[Space Program] ISSNL National Laboratory - National Science Foundation (NL-NSF) Comment[Study Grant Number] Comment[SubResearch Area] Droplets STUDY DESIGN DESCRIPTORS Study Design Type Droplet combustion Alkane chemistry Extinction Negative Temperature Coefficient (NTC) Helium dilution Low-temperature chemistry n-dodecane cool flames hydrocarbon fuel combustion Flame dynamics Renewable jet fuel Study Design Type Term Accession Number https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ Study Design Type Term Source REF PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI STUDY PUBLICATIONS Study PubMed ID Study Publication DOI 10.1016/j.combustflame.2012.07.012 10.1016/j.combustflame.2015.01.012 10.2514/1.J054437 10.1080/13647830.2014.934296 10.1080/13647830.2016.1248494 10.1007/s12217-024-10115-x Study Publication Author List V Nayagam, DL Dietrich, PV Ferkul, MC Hicks, FA Williams V Nayagam, DL Dietrich, MC Hicks, FA Williams Nayagam Vedha, Dietrich Daniel L., Williams Forman A.",,False
PSI-39,metadata,PSI-39_metadata_PSI-118_CFI.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-39/metadata/PSI-39_metadata_PSI-118_CFI.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,5,"Paczko Guenter, Peters Norbert, Seshadri Kalyanasundaram, Williams Forman Arthur Seshadri Kalyanasundaram, Peters Norbert, Williams Forman A., Nayagam Vedha, Paczko Guenter Rachel Barry Dietrich Daniel, Krause Timmothy, Nayagam Vedha, Farouk Tanvir, Dryer Frederick, Williams Forman Study Publication Title Can cool flames support quasi-steady alkane droplet burning? Cool-flame extinction during n-alkane droplet combustion in microgravity Partial-Burning Regime for Quasi-Steady Droplet Combustion Supported by Cool Flames The role of cool-flame chemistry in quasi-steady combustion and extinction of n-heptane droplets Asymptotic analysis of quasi-steady n-heptane droplet combustion supported by cool-flame chemistry Science in Short:",,False
