PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p2,0,FLEX Hardware,,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p3,0,"FLEX Experiment Summary • Original of FLEX experiments was to observe and systematically study hot flame ignition and extinction in microgravity • A new phenomenon where a hot flame extinction leads to a low-temperature, “cool-flame” burning in microgravity observed for the first time • Cool flames are prevalent in FLEX experiments o Strong function of ambient pressure o Strong function of ambient environment – ambient oxygen mole fraction and diluent o Function of fuel composition • No systematic study of cool flames and their presence inferred and not observed 1.0 0.8 0.6 0.4 0.2 0.0 ( D / D0 )2 2.52.01.51.00.50.0 t / D0 2 ( s / mm2 ) 30 25 20 15 10 5 Df ( mm ) , Df / D d 20Watts 15 10 5 0 Flame Radiance ( W ) Thu, Mar 14, 2013 FLEX-500 P = 767.836 mmHg YO2 = 0.210 YN2 = 0.790 Heptane D0 = 3.610 mm Dext = 2.838 mm kavg = 0.485 mm2 /sec kavg, cool = 0.366 mm2 /sec Dext, cool = 1.146 mm Droplet history LLUV flame history LLUV flame standoff Wideband Radiometer Narrowband Power*10 Use the unique observation of quasi-steady burning and extinction of cool flames during microgravity droplet combustion to further our understanding of low temperature chemistry. Enhance the diagnostic capability of the CIR and MDCA to better study cool flame behavior.",,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p4,0,"Experiment Objectives – Cool Combustion, CFI 1. Further understanding of the combustion characteristics of normal alkanes, particularly in the low temperature region by conducting droplet combustion experiments in low gravity with fuels that supports cool flame burning and extinction. 2. Investigate the low temperature burning behavior of droplets consisting of pure fuels and bio-fuel constituents (and mixtures of them), as well as surrogate reference fuels to determine the relationship between the cool flame burning characteristics in microgravity droplet combustion and the octane/cetane behavior of the fuel. 3. Explore the low temperature chemistry of alkanes further by mixing additives to the fuel that disrupt the low temperature chemical pathways. [Ground-based only]",,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p5,0,Justification for Long-Duration Microgravity • Requires spherical symmetry to simplify mathematics ⌧k ⌧b = p gD3 ↵g ⌧m ⌧b = p gD3 Dg • Spherical symmetry requires g-levels less than 10-4g0 o Precludes experimentation using aircraft flying parabolic trajectories • Drop towers have appropriate g-levels o Currently limited to 5 seconds microgravity time • Proposed experiments involve: 1. Droplet growth and deployment (~ 10 seconds) 2. Droplet ignition and hot-flame burning (5 - 10 seconds) 3. Hot-flame extinction 4. Cool-flame burning (5 – 60 seconds) 5. Cool-flame extinction • Long-duration microgravity facilities are required for detailed parametric study,,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p6,0,"Requirements – Test Fuels • Objective 1: Study the ignition, hot-flame burning and extinction, cool flame burning and cool flame extinction of a model normal alkane fuel. o Normal dodecane • Objective 2: Investigate the low temperature burning of pure fuels, mixtures of pure fuels that are surrogates for real fuels and bio-fuel constituents to determine the relationship between a fuels octane/cetane behavior and the cool flame burning characteristics. o 2,6,10 trimethyldodecane (farnesane) o n-dodecane/2,6,10 trimethyldodecane mixtures (3 mixtures ea., ratios TBD) o n-dodecane/2,2,4,6,6 pentamethylheptane mixtures (3 mixtures ea., ratios TBD) o n-dodecane/iso-octane/n-propyl benzene/1,3,5 trimethyl benzene (2 mixtures ea.) o n-heptane/2,2,4 trimethylpentane mixtures",,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p7,0,"Requirements – Droplet Deployment and Ignition • No changes to MDCA deployment and ignition hardware • Reduce the size of the support fiber to 40 µm o Required in some cases to keep the droplet in the FOV o Allows an estimate of the hot-flame temperature in FLEX o 80 µm fiber in FLEX experiments created disturbances to droplet near cool flame extinction in almost all tests 1.0 0.8 0.6 0.4 0.2 0.0 ( D / D0 )2 2.01.51.00.50.0 t / D0 2 ( s / mm2 ) 35 30 25 20 15 10 5 Df ( mm ) , Df / D d kavg = 0.41 mm^2/sec kavg = 0.09 mm^2/sec Tue, Jun 15, 2010 FLEX-095 C08H201 Fiber-Supported P = 534.358 mmHg YO2 = 0.240 YN2 = 0.560 YCO2 = 0.200 Heptane D0 = 3.647 mm Dext, hot = 3.045 mm kavg, hot = 0.583 mm2 /sec kavg, cool = 0.41 mm2 /sec Dext, cool ~ 1.89 mm 1.0 0.8 0.6 0.4 0.2 0.0 ( D / D0 )2 2.01.51.00.50.0 t / D0 2 ( s / mm2 ) 30 25 20 15 10 5 Df ( mm ) , Df / D d FLEX-064 Thu, Mar 11, 2010 302F001 P = 764.593 mmHg YO2 = 0.175 YN2 = 0.815 YCO2 = 0.002 Heptane D0 = 2.715 mm Dext = 2.084 mm kavg = 0.600 mm2 /sec",,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p8,0,"Requirements – Ambient Environment • Pressure o Strongly influences cool flame burning and extinction – both hypo- and hyper-baric pressures o 0.5 – 3.0 atm required, up to 5 atm desired (hardware limit) • Oxygen Mole Fraction o 0.10 – 0.40, similar to the range in FLEX experiments • Ambient Diluent o Primarily nitrogen (Objective 1 and 2) o Helium and xenon (Objective 1) Ø Strongly influence cool flame burning and extinction • Misc. Ambient Requirements o Similar to FLEX requirements o Ambient humidity less than 10% (FLEX had lower requirement for methanol) o g-levels ≤ 10-5g0",,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p9,0,"Requirements – Droplet Imaging • Measure droplet size and shape, droplet dynamics, soot volume fraction, etc. as functions of time • No change to camera or backlight for new experiment necessary • Increase FOV from 30 mm (FLEX) to 50 mm (CFI) o Increases success rate by allowing for greater free deployment droplet drift o FLEX requires drift less than 1 mm/sec to stay in FOV o Reduces spatial resolution (30 µm to 50 µm)",,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p10,0,"Requirements – Hot Flame Imaging • Currently measure either OH* or CH chemiluminescence using intensified black and white camera o OH* (310 nm) images very dim, required pixel binning (2x2) to get good images, but reduced spatial resolution o CH (430 nm) images have improved contrast and reduced noise – possible to eliminate binning (increases data storage/downlink requirements) • Increase FOV from 50 mm (FLEX) to 80 mm (CFI) o Increases success rate by allowing for greater droplet drift • Current camera configuration acceptable for hot-flame imaging o Unlikely to be able to perform chemiluminescence of both the hot-flame and cool flame with current camera configuration • Require the capability to image the hot-flame o Either OH* or CH (preferred) chemiluminescence o 100 µm spatial resolution o 30 fps o Adjustable gain required (almost impossible to know appropriate gain based on ground-based testing)",,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p11,0,"Requirements – Cool Flame Imaging • Currently have no way to image cool flames on CIR/MDCA o Removing/changing filter and/or increasing gain on LLUV possible, no way to shield the camera from ignition or hot flame • Cool flame expected to be approximately ½ size of hot flame o FOV, spatial resolution and framing rate requirements similar to hot flame o Can use the same camera for both hot and cool flame imaging, but spectral ranges are different and required gain is much different • Based on literature search and FLEX radiometer data, cool flames are expected to be very dim 10 8 6 4 2 0 Relative Chemiluminescence Intensity ( a.u. ) 50004800460044004200400038003600 Wavelength ( angstroms )",,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p12,0,"Requirements – Color Camera Imaging • Necessary for droplet deployment, hardware diagnostics, etc. • Closest thing to being able to look into the chamber. • FLEX color camera with internal chamber light is acceptable for CFI o Zoom capability is critical. Zoom-in for droplet dispense, hardware diagnostics, igniter positioning. Zoom-out to view droplet burn. o Will not be able to image the cool flame o Must be able to image the vapor cloud and hot flame. o Must monitor radiation damage to image array over time • Require downlink of this view (recorded at TSC) during experiment. Also record on CIR because of image quality loss during ISS video compression).",,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p13,0,"Requirements – Flame Radiation 1.0 0.8 0.6 0.4 0.2 0.0 ( D / D0 )2 2.52.01.51.00.50.0 t / D0 2 ( s / mm2 ) 30 25 20 15 10 5 Df ( mm ) , Df / D d 20Watts 15 10 5 0 Flame Radiance ( W ) Thu, Mar 14, 2013 FLEX-500 P = 767.836 mmHg YO2 = 0.210 YN2 = 0.790 Heptane D0 = 3.610 mm Dext = 2.838 mm kavg = 0.485 mm2 /sec kavg, cool = 0.366 mm2 /sec Dext, cool = 1.146 mm Droplet history LLUV flame history LLUV flame standoff Wideband Radiometer Narrowband Power*10",,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p14,0,"Requirements – Flame Radiometer • Original FLEX radiometers not sensitive enough to adequately detect dim (hot) flames and had unacceptably low SNR • Starting in 2011, FLEX testing used replacement radiometers o Broadband: 0.6 – 10.0 µm sensitivity, images hot flame o Narrowband: 5.0 – 6.5 µm ( H2O ), sensitive to cool flame o 10 Hz frequency response, sampled at 100 Hz o Fixed gain, not sensitive to both hot and cool flames • Require flame radiometric measurements for CFI o Same wavelength bands as current radiometer package o Require auto-gain so each radiometer can capture both hot and cool flame Ø ~ 0.1 – 100 W flame radiance range Ø May not be able to capture flame radiance during re- ignition • Require at least 10 Hz sampling (same as FLEX) • Same FOV as FLEX radiometers 1.0 0.8 0.6 0.4 0.2 0.0 Fractional Transmission 56789 1 2 3 4 56789 10 2 3 4 56789 100 Wavelength ( µm ) 0.6 0.5 0.4 0.3 0.2 0.1 0.0 Fractional Transmission 4 5 6 7 8 9 10 Wavelength ( µm )",,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p15,0,"Desire – Flame (Hot and Cool) Pyrometry • Original intent of HiBMS-2/LCTF was SiC fiber pyrometry o Two-color pyrometry o Cycling time of LCTF too slow o Detector QE * LCTF transmission too small • Request capability to perform SiC pyrometry for fiber- supported CFI tests o Hot and cool flame, but not necessarily simultaneously • Requires changes to current HiBMS-2 configuration o Remove LCTF o Replace with wideband bandpass IR filter o Calibration insert to igniter circuit ??? 60 55 50 45 40 35 30 25 20 15 10 5 0 mm 605550454035302520151050 mm 1.0 0.8 0.6 0.4 0.2 0.0 Transmission, Reflectance ( fractional ) 120011001000900800700600500 Wavelength ( nm ) 0.5 0.4 0.3 0.2 0.1 0.0 Quantum Efficiency, Transmission (fractional) Sapphire Transmission BK7 Transmission SF11 Transmission LAH58 Transmission SF5 Transmission SK2 Transmission Detector Quantum Efficiency LCTF Transmission @ 700 nm LCTF Transmission @ 900 nm SF53 Transmission",,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p16,0,Requirements – Misc. • Require chamber pressure measurement o CIR/FOMA capability sufficient for CFI • Require chamber temperature measurement o CIR capability sufficient for CFI • CIR/FOMA gas mixing capability sufficient for the CFI experiments • All measurements time referenced to GMT within 0.03 sec o Current MDCA/CIR capability sufficient for CFI • Require measurement of g-level during experiment o Current SAMS capability sufficient for CFI • Require experiment isolation from ISS structure o PARIS sufficient for CFI,,False
PSI-39,Science Documents,PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-39/Science Documents/PSI-39_Science Documents_CFI_SCR.pdf,p17,0,"Experiment Procedures and Test Matrix • Experiment procedures similar to FLEX experiments o FLEX looked for flammability limits and did a few tests in gradually decreasing oxygen ambients o CFI will examine fewer ambients, but more tests in each ambient • Exact test matrix will be determined based on further analysis of FLEX data and companion theoretical/numerical analysis o Test matrix in SRD used for resource estimation o Require interactive experimentation, i.e., future experiments guided by the results of the current experiments Ø Requires timely downlink of experimental data. o Requires detailed interaction with engineering team Ø Tests planned to optimize resource utilization • Pressure range: 0.5 – 3.0 atm required (5.0 atm desired) • Ambient oxygen mole fraction: 0.10 – 0.40 • Ambient gas mixture: O2/N2, O2/He, O2/Xe, tertiary mixtures possible",,False
