PSI-47,metadata,PSI-47_metadata_PSI-126_DAFT_DAFT-2.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-47/metadata/PSI-47_metadata_PSI-126_DAFT_DAFT-2.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,0,"ONTOLOGY SOURCE REFERENCE Term Source Name PSI EFO Term Source File https://psi.nasa.gov/ http://data.bioontology.org/ontologies/EFO Term Source Version 1.0 132 Term Source Description User defined terms Experimental Factor Ontology INVESTIGATION Investigation Identifier Investigation Title Investigation Description Investigation Submission Date Investigation Public Release Date INVESTIGATION PUBLICATIONS Investigation PubMed ID Investigation Publication DOI Investigation Publication Author List Investigation Publication Title Investigation Publication Status Investigation Publication Status Term Accession Number Investigation Publication Status Term Source REF INVESTIGATION CONTACTS Investigation Person Last Name Investigation Person First Name Investigation Person Mid Initials Investigation Person Email Investigation Person Phone Investigation Person Fax Investigation Person Address Investigation Person Affiliation Investigation Person Roles Investigation Person Roles Term Accession Number Investigation Person Roles Term Source REF STUDY Study Identifier PSI-47 Study Title Dust and Aerosol Measurement Feasibility Test / Dust and Aerosol Measurement Feasibility Test-2 Study Description Study Submission Date 01-Jun-2009 Study Public Release Date 01-Jun-2009 Study File Name s_PSI-47.txt Comment[Acknowledgments] Comment[Approach] ""The experimental approach consists of the following components:",,False
PSI-47,metadata,PSI-47_metadata_PSI-126_DAFT_DAFT-2.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-47/metadata/PSI-47_metadata_PSI-126_DAFT_DAFT-2.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,1,"1) Modification of Instrumentation: TSI P-Trak was modified for use in microgravity by redesigning the condenser tube to use capillary forces for recycling isopropyl alcohol and removing the mercury switch responsible for detecting instrument orientation. 2) Aerosol Generation: Arizona Red Dust (ARD) was chosen as an aerosol standard due to its ease of dispersion. The ARD was aerosolized with GN2 to create particle samples. The aerosol was contained within 15-liter Mylar bags. 3) On-Orbit Testing: The modified P-Trak gathered data from counting aerosol particles and these data were compared to the DustTrak CNC specifically designed to work in microgravity."" Comment[DOI] 10.60555/ztgj-d202 Comment[Data Source Accession] Comment[Data Source Link] Comment[Experiment Hardware] TSI P-Trak Comment[Experiment Hardware Link] https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20050215648/downloads/20050215648.pdf Comment[Flight Platform] International Space Station (ISS) Comment[Funding] NASA Comment[Hypothesis] ""The TSI P-Trak operates by condensing isopropanol around aerosol particulates that are subsequently laser counted. It was hypothesized that the P-Trak would not work in microgravity due to two potential failure modes that exist. 1) the system would clog or become obscured by isopropanol, and 2) the internal air-flow dynamics of the optics chamber of the device will cause an over counting of particulates.",,False
PSI-47,metadata,PSI-47_metadata_PSI-126_DAFT_DAFT-2.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-47/metadata/PSI-47_metadata_PSI-126_DAFT_DAFT-2.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,2,"The TSI P-Trak was modified for microgravity use via using capillary action to recycle the isopropanol. Therefore, there are two basic hypotheses: 1) that the P-Trak, as modified for use in SAME, would function normally in microgravity. 2) that the aerosol created and filtered per the experiment design would be within the measurement capabilities of the test devices."" Comment[Identifiers] Comment[Investigation Acronym] DAFT / DAFT-2 Comment[Investigation End Date] 06-01-2009 Comment[Investigation Start Date] 03-01-2003 Comment[Managing NASA Center] Glenn Research Center (GRC) Comment[Mission End] Comment[Mission Link] Comment[Mission Name] Comment[Mission Start] Comment[Objective] ""The focus of DAFT / DAFT-2 was to mitigate the risk for the Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment (SAME). SAME relied on a commercially available condensate nuclei counter, the TSI P-Trak, to quantify smoke aerosols in a microgravity environment, however there were concerns that th P-Trak would not be reliable in microgravity as it relies on gravity to recycle isopropyl alcohol used in the measurement process. DAFT / DAFT-2 tests that a modified P-Trak particulate counter would be accurate in microgravity. Summarized objectives include:",,False
PSI-47,metadata,PSI-47_metadata_PSI-126_DAFT_DAFT-2.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-47/metadata/PSI-47_metadata_PSI-126_DAFT_DAFT-2.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,3,"1) Demonstrate the Functionality of the Modified TSI P-Trak in Microgravity 2) Compare the TSI P-Trak Performance against the DustTrak control 3) Create and Analyze Aerosol Standards in Microgravity 4) Ensure the reliability of future aerosol experiments"" Comment[Project Identifier] Comment[Project Link] Comment[Project Title] Comment[Project Type] Microgravity Investigation Comment[Proposal Title] Comment[Research Area] Combustion Science Comment[Research Impacts] Extended duration space flight missions with larger vessels will require fire detection systems with increased reliability and sensitivity. This research will impact the development of novel fire and smoke detection systems for the International Space Station and future spacecrafts. Additionally, the successful adaptation of the TSI P-Trak for microgravity can inform the design of instruments for use in other challenging Earth environments, such as submarines or underwater laboratories where accurate detection of smoke can save lives.",,False
PSI-47,metadata,PSI-47_metadata_PSI-126_DAFT_DAFT-2.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-47/metadata/PSI-47_metadata_PSI-126_DAFT_DAFT-2.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,4,"Comment[Space Program] National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) Comment[Study Grant Number] Comment[SubResearch Area] Spacecraft Fire Safety STUDY DESIGN DESCRIPTORS Study Design Type DAFT P-Trak particulate counter Condensate Nuclei Counter Capillary Fluid Motion Laser scattering aerosol device Combustion Fire safety Smoke Space hardware testing Fire prevention Study Design Type Term Accession Number https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ Study Design Type Term Source REF PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI STUDY PUBLICATIONS Study PubMed ID Study Publication DOI 10.2514/6.2005-341 10.4271/2005-01-2930 Study Publication Author List Ruff Gary, Urban David, King Merrill Urban David, Griffin DeVon, Ruff Gary, Cleary Thomas, Yang Jiann, Mulholland George, Yuan Zeng-guang Study Publication Title A Research Plan for Fire Prevention, Detection and Suppression in Crewed Exploration Systems Detection of Smoke from Microgravity Fires Study Publication Status published published Study Publication Status Term Accession Number http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 Study Publication Status Term Source REF EFO EFO Comment[Study Publication Link] http://dx.doi.org/10.2514/6.2005-341 http://dx.doi.org/10.4271/2005-01-2930 STUDY FACTORS Study Factor Name Study",,False
