PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p1,0,"1 Dust and Aerosol measurement Feasibility Test (DAFT) 30 Day Report Principal Investigator: avid Urban, NASA Glenn Research Center D Co-Investigators: George Mulholland, University of Maryland earch Zeng-guang Yuan, National Center for Space Exploration Res Jiann Yang, National Institute for Standards and Technology Thomas Cleary, National Institute for Standards and Technology",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p2,0,"2 A. Abstract be made vailable for launch within a six month period from the date of the request (March, 2003). his instrument was in question nd added significant technical risk to the future success of SAME. a risk mitigation xperiment to test the performance of the P-Trak in a microgravity environment. B. Hypothesis d and another device (the SI DustTrak), that was analyzed to be unaffected by the lack of gravity. (ARD, Fine) instead, in combination with ISS GN2 to create an aerosol for measurement urposes. filtered per e experiment design would be within the measurement capabilities of the test devices C. Objectives of Investigation odified TSI P-Trak functioned effectively and predictably in a icrogravity environment. . Methods/Research Operations ussion of Method/Protocol Final Research Report Template and Guidelines I. Introduction In the wake of the Columbia disaster, NASA H eadquarters put out a call for small, low upmass experiments that could be launched aboard the Russian Progress vehicle and which could a The Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment (SAME, formerly known as “Smoke”), which was being developed at the time and which is desi gned for operation aboard the ISS, required the utilization of a commercially available particulate counter, the TSI P-Trak, that is designed to use gravity to facilitate its operation. The microgravity performance of t a The Dust and Aerosol measurement Feasibility T est (DAFT) was designed as e",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p2,1,"Two potential failure modes were identified for the device: 1) that the particulate counting system, containing a nozzle, a laser diode and sensitive optics would clog or become obscured and 2) that the internal flow dynamics of the optics chamber of the device would cause an over counting of the particulates and give unreliable (high) readings. Therefore, an experiment was designed that would use a functionally flight like design and test it against an aerosol standar T The choice of the aerosol and the method used to create it were key items. Ideally, a mono dispersed aerosol would be used (0.7 – 1.3 microns), whic h would allow an easy comparison of the readings from the devices. However, due to the lack of development time, it was proposed to use Arizona Road Dust p Therefore, there are two basic hypotheses in DAFT: 1) that the TSI P-Trak, as modified for use in SAME, would function normally in microgravity, and 2) that the aerosol created and th The objective of DAFT was to minimize the risks of its parent experiment, SAME, by demonstrating that the m m II A. Disc In order to prepare the P-Trak for operation in microgravity, an analysis of its operation was performed. The P-Trak uses a small pump to draw the sample aerosol through a chamber saturated",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p3,0,"with alcohol vapor by means of a wick. That vapor/aerosol mix then passes through a cooling tube that allows the alcohol to condense on the particles in the aerosol, making droplets that are large enough to count w 3 ith a focused laser/photo cell combination. The condenser in the COTS device opes down, enabling the alcohol from the droplets that adhere to the tube wall to be recycled back ce. ther alterations to the device involved the removal of the mercury switch (for gravitationally he TSI DustTrak, a laser scattering device based on Mie scattering and included as a measurement tained vailable mono-dispersed particles. Therefore, Arizona oad Dust (fine), a NIST standard, was selected due to its known constitution and ease of created, the parameters tested and determined, and flight like hardware was reated and tested with the measurement instruments to develop the correlation curves discussed in by one of the xperiment’s Co-I’s (George Mulholland). The DAFT Impactor is a particle filter for large particles erosol is he experiment was conceived to disburse a similar aerosol on orbit and determine the device corr Trak’s measurement effectiveness. is was intended to be accomplished tilizing no more than 15 test points of aerosolized ARD in conjunction with a TSI DustTrak to e lim riments, the DAFT was d s: eries DAFT-3 ample generating and filtering equipment AFT-4 Contained an unmodified (but flight hardened) P-Trak, another alcohol wick and sl to the evaporator.",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p3,1,"In conjunction with fluid physics researchers at GRC, a modification to the cooling tube was developed that effectively moves liquid alcohol from the tip of the tube back upstream to the evaporator due to capillary action. This was the major modification that was made to the devi O sensitive tilt sensor), flight hardening and covering with foil tape for flammability concerns. T standard, was hardened in a similar fashion, but did not include a mercury switch. After some consideration, it was decided to use a 15 liter Mylar bag for the containment of the aerosol. Issues, including safety and the atomizing of the particulates (and the fluid that con them) precluded the use of commercially a R dispersion into a pressurized gas stream. A flight design was c the results section. One problem exists with this sample material, however. It contains a given number of particles in sizes above 2.5 microns, which is above the measurement limit of the P-Trak. To mitigate this factor, a device, the DAFT Impactor was developed based on design criteria supplied e (only) that was designed specifically for the combined flow rate of the test devices.",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p3,2,"The P-Trak gives a number count of the particles in an aerosol, in readings of pt/cc. Dustrak gives measurements of the total mass, in mg/m3. Since the distribution of the particulates in the a known to a certain confidence level, and the units had been correlated in testing on the ground, t elation in a microgravity environment to quantify the P- B. List & Description of All Functional Objectives As stated in previous sections, the functional objective of DAFT was to demonstrate the effective operation of the TSI P-Trak in a microgravity e nvironment. Th u provide a correlation to the readings obtained from the P-Trak. Due to th itations imposed to the allowa ble upmass for US based expe hardware ivided into the following four section DAFT-1 Contained the modified P-Trak, an Alcohol Wick and batt DAFT-2 Contained a DustTrak and batteries Contained the s D more batteries Since all of the hardware was not launched simultaneously, the objectives were modified accordingly. DAFT-1 and -2 were launched aboa rd Progress mission 16P, and an opportunity to",,False
