PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p4,1,"DAFT-0700 Contain DustTrak, cleaning tools, spare filters and misc. parts. P-Track Batteries DAFT-0701 6 - AA DAFT Fill Assembly DAFT-0301 f valves used to ags A custom made device, consisting of tubing fittings, filters, a regulator and pressure relie control and direct ISS GN2 for use in filling the Sample B with aerosol. DAFT Impactor DAFT-0401 e o sample aerosol of the larger Another custom made devic composed of fittings and filters, this device was used t extract the from the Sample Bags and eliminate most particles. DAFT Sample Bag DAFT-0501-01 thru -15 ngs. 15 liter COTS Mylar bags with tubing fitti DAFT Dust Holder DAFT-0516-01 thru -15 of two self te Each consists sealing QD’s that encapsula the ARD P-Track Alcohol Wick -02 DAFT-0601-2 Same as -01 P-Track -02 DAFT-0102 An unmodified COTS unit. Launched on ULF1.1 Dust Trak DAFT-0201 nother COTS TSI device, readings based on Mie scattering. Launched on 16P, A giving Dust Trak Batteries DAFT-0709 4 - C D. the batteries to each device, activated them, and performed the zero check. No anomalies were noted Sessions Table D.1 DAFT 1 & 2, Session 1, GMT 54, 2005 The first test point for DAFT was performed on February 22, 2005 during Increment 10. The hardware was set up per the procedure (nominal) in front of EXPRESS Rack 4. Leroy Chiao inserted",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p5,0,"5 ings, blocked tubing, etc.). The P-Trak was operated for approximately 12 minutes, and continued taking readings for the entire time. Near Real Time (NRT) p, r lly gave low readings. Overall, these readings are almost an order of magnitude below those that would normally 000 pt/cc for P-Trak), but were not totally ec SS air handling system. st both of the devices. Leroy was also asked to signal the ground team when he turned on the devices to ). for approximately 25 minutes; enough to capture all but the last few minutes of test point #4. Leroy gave a ‘thumbs up’ #2 Leroy was asked to mate and demate Velcro in front of the intake for the tubing that supplies samples to both of the devices. That data is provided in Section III C. Figures 3 and 4.Test points #3 and #4 were taken in Lab 1, adjacent to Port 6 and in the US Node 1, mid-cabin, T , ief d approximately three times longer than it is wide, and it is about 2 inches thick. From the video, it was clear that the foam was deformed from flat by about one inch along the shorter axis, (i.e.; warnings, beeping, unreasonable read video was available for most of the test, including set up, and the run time could be approximately synchronized with GMT through its use. The Payload Operations Director (POD) allowed the call up of a request, and Leroy was asked to relay readings from the P-Trak verbally, which he did. Those readings were low (2 – 5 pt/cc). The data for that test point can be seen in Section III C. Figures 1 and 2.",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p5,1,"A review of that data shows that the readings on both devices were very low for the entire test time, but that upon initial startu the addition and removal of the HEPA filter (zero check) is clearly indicated in the P-Trak data, and there appears to be some residual dust in the sampling tube that is drawn out during the test point. Fo the remainder of the point, some small variations can be seen, the DustTrak especia be seen in a typical laboratory environment ( ~ 4,000 – 6, unexp ted, due to the presence of a HEPA filter on the I D.2 DAFT 1 & 2, Session 2, GMT 63, 2005 On March 4, 2005, DAFT was given its second opportunity, which involved the performance of three additional test points. The DAFT ground team had made prior arrangements to revise the te procedure to include alternate techniques and locations in an attempt to realize higher readings on allow more precise time synchronization between the devices’ internal clocks and GMT. (Note: although the units both have internal clocks powered by a coin battery, these clocks drift over time Near Real Time (NRT) video was again provided, with a window that extended when he turned on each device at the beginning of this session, and through PAYCOM there were some real time communications regarding the activities during the test points. For test point respectively.",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p5,2,"D.3 DAFT-3, Session 1, GMT 229, 2006 The first test point for DAFT-3 and -4 was scheduled to be performed on August 18, 2006 (GM 229), during Increment 13. DAFT required NRT Video. During the hardware was set up procedure in front of EXPRESS Rack 5, Jeff Williams was in the process of unpacking the experiment equipment and held each item in front of the camera as he unpacked it. When he held up the DAFT Fill Assembly, the ground team noticed immediately that the HEPA filter that supports the pressure rel valves that protect the Sample Bag from being over filled, was missing. The POD was notified and requested to ask Jeff to return the Fill Assembly into the field of view, which he did, confirming the observation. Jeff noted that the HEPA Filter Subassembly was still within the foam enclosure. He then held up that enclosure, and noted that it was permanently deformed. The DAFT Fill Assembly is a series of linear tubing connections with ‘appendages’ connected to it by tees. The foam that encase it (minicell) is in line with the thickness. The foam is a ‘donut’ like cutout, with no strength in the direction of the deformation.",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p6,0,"6 devices, another anomaly occurred. When he had attached the P-Trak and attempted the data transfer, the message: “Error Communicating” popped up. e with the P-Trak and also once with the DustTrak. Backing ect a com port revealed that the EXPRESS Laptop being the As a result of the discovery of the broken part on the previous day, a special Safety telecom was ion would be required to provide the necessary protection for the crew. That telecom involved m Safety, t The DA follows: nately, with gray essure and use the remaining he Fill Assembly and scavenge the parts from the each potential fix, it was ne Wright) to LC was worked with the EXPRESS Software group. This issue was resolved when the EXPRESS group renamed the ports to be in lia , sked to direct PAYCOM to ask Jeff to voice down the real-time readings from the P-Trak and the DustTrak during the sampling period. This would allow the team to know immediately if the er there was Ku Band coverage. At that point, the nominal test plan was aborted. Jeff was asked to restow the Fill Assembly, and to revert to the procedure that was used for DAFT-1 &-2, taking a cabin air sample, which he did. Later, when he attempted to download the data from the The transfer was attempted one more tim up in the procedure and attempting to sel used did not have a “Com Port 1” within its list of available choices, which was a problem, since DAFT Software was configured to use that port.",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p6,1,"D.4 DAFT-3, GMT 230, 2006 convened to determine if the experiment could be performed, and if so, what level of repair or modificat embers of the PSRP, the ISS Cadre, DAFT Project Management and Safety, POIC he Crew Office and MER Safety. FT engineering team provided several options for repair. They are briefly described as Option 1: Cover the opening to the tubing system with a finger tip, or alter tape, and perform the experiment nominally. Option 2: Remove the fitting that supports the Gage subassembly, remove the high pr relief valve from that subassembly and replace it in that location; fitting to reinstall the low pressure relief valves, without the HEPA filter. Option 3: Disassemble the DAFT Impactor and remove the HEPA filter. Use that HEPA filter to replace the broken one on t Impactor that are necessary to deliver the sample to the devices. After a significant discussion regarding the implications and ramifications of determined that Option 1, using gray tape to cover the opening, would be the most desirable and did not pose an undue Safety risk to the crew. The engineering team worked with the designated Crew Procedures Engineer (Way develop the procedures to support that activity, and it was approved for use. Also, the issue regarding the labeling of the com ports on the E comp nce with the needs of the DAFT software. An ISS Payload Anomaly Report (PSR) number (DAFT-HW-01) was issued to describe, record and document the resolution of the broken DAFT Fill Assembly.",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p6,2,"D.5 DAFT-3, Session 1, GMT 233, 2006 Jeff accomplished the repair without incident and proceeded to perform the nominal test procedures. Since the DAFT ground team realized that this might be the only opportunity to perform the experiment and in order to insure that the test points would have the maximum experimental value POD was a concentration of the aerosol within the Sample Bags was appropriate to keep both of the instruments within range. It was extremely fortunate that that opportunity was provided to the Science and Engineering team. Near Real Time Video was also made available whenev",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p7,0,"7 The ground team requested that Alcohol Wick S/N 02 be used, to insure that there would be enough or The 2.5 micron Impactor was installed on the DustTrak from the Accessories kit. Trak go into Log Mode 2. Using voice up messaging with POD and PAYCOM, the ground team asked Jeff to make a second attempt, which he The number of requested toggles of the fill valve (per the Execution Notes), once the DAFT Dust as d in Appendix C, labeled as ‘Bag #1’. rted ment readings every in the ground team that the four test points from the previous testing (last year) should still have been oad procedure was then performed nominally, transferring test points 1 – 4 and those four files were placed on the ELC for lly once it was transferred from EXPRESS to the -1 and -2 over a year ago. III. Results alcohol to run all of the test points. Jeff was unable to open the container that it was in. The reason f that is unknown. He was directed to revert to using Alcohol Wick S/N 01. Jeff noted during set up that he could not make the Dust did, but that did not help. He was instructed to ignore the problem and move forward. Holder was installed onto the Fill Assembly, was four. Both of the instrument’s count values went to zero when the HEPA filter was installed on the DAFT Impactor inlet, which is nominal. Once the devices were attached, while performing DAFT Sample Extract, Jeff gave a reading from each instrument at 30 second intervals for the entire time that sampling was occurring.",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p7,1,"The data w recorded manually by the DAFT ground team. That data has been transcribed to an Excel file and is include The operations proceeded nominally from this point forward, although the P-Trak reportedly sta beeping sometime before Bag #2 was extracted. It is unknown if this was an actual low alcohol warning, or merely an artifact of drawing the Sample Low enough to cause a vacuum within the P- Trak. The HEPA filter was installed on the inlet of the DAFT Impactor before each test point and the devices were re-zeroed. Six toggles were used to introduce dust into Sample Bag #2. Eight toggles were used for Sample Bag #3. Jeff continued to provide call downs of the instru 30 seconds for all of the test points, and they were recorded in a similar fashion. The units were not turned off in between test points, and no removal or reinstallation of the P-Trak Alcohol Wick was performed until the end of the session. All of the used Sample Bags and Dust Holders were placed the trash at the end of the test period. The DAFT hardware was all restowed at the end of the testing, since the scheduled Data Download would not be performed for several hours. When Jeff attached the P-Trak to the ELC to download the data, it was clear that the fix implemented by the EXPRESS team had worked, the device recognized the Com Port, and reported that there were four test points ready for downloading.",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p7,2,"This did not seem to be right, since it was the opinion of stored, and the test numbers should have incremented starting at number 5. The devices were both queried for test points 5, 6 and 7, and no data was found. The DATA Downl downlink as a part of their housekeeping data. Unfortunately, the only data received electronica GRC TSC was the data obtained from the DAFT A. List of Pre-, In-, Postflight Anomalies Detailed in the Sessions section. B. Completeness/Quality of Data",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p8,0,"The data taken from DAFT 1 and 2 can be seen in Section III C. Figures 1 through 4. Test point #2 data (Figures 3 a 8 nd 4) shows that the P-Trak responded to the Velcro dust quite well, with rak readings, indicates then, that the mass of the average particle counted on orbit is higher implies that it is allowing a considerable number of larger particles to be ng the particles, one of the enous material; but since it contains a wide distribution of sizes, it is a mixture. The amount of the material that is enclosed in each Dus nd testing, it is estimated that only about 10% of the material contained within the Dust Holder is introduced into the aerosol, due to the irregular interior surfaces within it, the surface adhesion of the dust and other factors. Therefore, the net amount of sample delivered to the Sample Bags is about 25 milligrams. Since this amount is so small and is coming from a mixture, some variation in the average particle size between samples is expected. C. Tables, Graphs, Figures Index several readings above the 30,000 pt/cc mark (P-Trak has a maximum count of 500,000 pt/cc). Although there is no way to exactly correlate between the Velcro demates and the increases in the P-T the observations that the unit’s readings increased and decreased in a logical fashion, were within the unit’s prescribed range and there were no observed anomalies serve to indicate that the unit was functioning normally.",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p8,1,"DustTrak also responded well to the Velcro dust, but did not reach readings above 10% of its overall capability. Test points #3 and #4 were not appreciably different from Test point #1 for either device. Section III C. Figure 7 contains a chart developed from the ground testing that was performed in May of 2003, using the crew training hardware in a flight like manner. It can be seen from that graph that although there is a wide variation in the amount of aerosol that is contained within the various samples, the relationship between the DustTrak and P-Trak readings is very linear, meaning that the overall composition of the various aerosols created on the ground, regardless of concentration, is very similar with regard to the relationship between the number and the sizes of the particles entrained. A comparison between this chart and the one taken from the flight data, presented in Appendix C, shows that the data acquired on orbit falls outside of this ground based relationship. The data points are clustered to the left of the line (area), in a region where the count/mass ratio is lower than what was determined from ground testing. This than that determined on Earth. This observation makes sense since, on-orbit, without gravity to drive settling, more of the larger particles from the dust sample should remain entrained. It should be noted that, although the DAFT Impactor is designed to filter out the larger particles, it is only about 50% efficient;",,False
PSI-47,Reports,PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Reports/PSI-47_Reports_DAFT_30Day.PostFlightReport.pdf,p8,2,"which drawn into the devices. If the data collected on orbit had been to the lower right of the ground based testing line on the graph in Appendix D, it would have meant that more smaller particles would have been present; which is counter intuitive and would have indicated that the P-Trak may have been over counti postulated failure modes. The analysis of the average particulate size and mass shown on the spreadsheet in Figure 5 (provided by Zengguang Yuan) shows a good uniformity between the particulates sampled from the various bags. The average mass (assuming a material density of water) is 2.5 E-12 grams, with a range from 2 – 3 E-12. The calculated average diameter is 1.65 micron, ranging from 1.56 – 1.7 micron. The sample material, Arizona Road Dust (Fine) is a standardized, homog t Holder is small (0.25 gram). From grou",,False
