PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_60089MFHD509 P-Trak Condenser Tube Modification.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_60089MFHD509 P-Trak Condenser Tube Modification.pdf,p1,0,"Description: C-size modification drawing of an existing P-Trak condenser tube: a flanged tube whose bore is machined with 60 fine longitudinal knife-edge grooves. Section A-A (side), end view, Detail B (groove profile, scale 50/1) and Detail C (grooved bore end, scale 8/1). ### Title block - Title: P-TRAK CONDENSER TUBE MODIFICATION; next assembly: P-TRAK ASSEMBLY 60089MFHA500 - Drawing number: 60089MFHD509, size C, rev - (no revision letter; revisions table empty) - Organisation: National Aeronautics and Space Administration, John H. Glenn Research Center, Lewis Field, Cleveland, Ohio; ZIN Technologies - Project lead, quality, engineer, designer, reviewer: [name omitted], dated 6/21/05 to 6/22/05; safety: NA - Contract number: NAS3-99154; delivery order: MRDOC-074; file location: M:\MRDOC\60089_074; CAD package: Pro/E Wildfire - Scale 4/1; initial release date 6/22/05; sheet 1 of 1; bldg ZIN; area GRC; units: inch; discipline: mechanical - ""This drawing is under configuration control: do not revise without approved ECO"". Approved: [name omitted], date 6/22/05 ### Tolerances (unless otherwise specified, dimensions in inches) | | | |---|---| | .X | ± 0.03 | | .XX | ± 0.01 | | .XXX | ± 0.005 | | FRACTION | ± - | | ANGULAR | ± 0.50° | | BREAK EDGES | N/A | Dimensioning and tolerancing per ASME Y14.5M-1994.",,True
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_60089MFHD509 P-Trak Condenser Tube Modification.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_60089MFHD509 P-Trak Condenser Tube Modification.pdf,p1,1,"### Notes 1. EXISTING P-TRAK CONDENSER TUBE NO. 3001737-8, MODIFY AS SHOWN WHILE PROTECTING ALL OTHER EXISTING SURFACES FROM ANY DAMAGE. (MATL: ALUMINUM). 2. 16 Ra ALL MACHINED SURFACES. 3. CLEAN SURFACES WITH ISOPROPYL OR ETHYL ALCOHOL. AIR DRY. WIPE WITH CLEAN COTTON SWABS AND/OR LINT-FREE CLOTH. 4. HANDLE WITH CLEAN LINT FREE GLOVES. 5. BEFORE STORAGE, PROTECT WITH CONTAMINATION BARRIER. USE CLEAN, NEW, TRANSPARENT POLYETHYLENE BAG FOR SMALL ITEMS, OR 4 MIL THICK MINIMUM POLYETHYLENE SHEET AND SEAL. IDENTIFY BY TAGGING WITH PART NUMBER AND SERIAL/LOT NUMBER AS SPECIFIED IN WORK ORDER, PER SAE AS478, METHOD 35. ### Section A-A (dimensions in inches) - Overall length (1.438); grooved length 1.375 - Flange thickness (.080); (.050) - Tube outer diameter (Ø.307); bore (Ø.188); (Ø.250) - ""SEE DETAIL C"" at the grooved end ### End view - (Ø.376), (Ø.997) flange; ""SEE DETAIL B""; section line A-A ### Detail B (60 PLACES EQUALLY SPACED, SCALE 50/1) - Groove depth .0095/.0090 - R.0015 MAX at groove root - 30°0' and 60°0' groove angles - SHARP CORNERS (KNIFE EDGES) ### Detail C (SCALE 8/1) - 60X R.250 [?]; (1.375) grooved length",,True
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,p1,0,"2.011 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLING MGUEEXPRSDAFTN011 06May04 N011-1 DAFT/PB3-01-0677A Start_IMS OBJECTIVE: Dust and Aerosol measurement Feasibility Test (DAFT). This procedure utilizes an ambient air sample from ISS for measurement by the P-Trak and DustTrak. It initiates the P-Trak and DustTrak for sampling and shutdown. STATION PARTS: DAFT Accessories Kit P/N DAFT-0700 DustTrak P/N DAFT-0201 P-Trak-01 P/N DAFT-0101 Single Strap P/N PERS0041 Cable Straps [6] P/N 528-43074-1 NOTE 1. DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLING alternates between both the P-Trak and DustTrak hardware units. 2. This procedure contains Near Real-Time video downlink or LAB VTR recording requirements. 3. AOS required for POIC to configure onboard system for downlink or LAB VTR recording. 1. DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLING SETUP 1.1 Retrieve DustTrak. Start Video Activity 1.2 If video is required, Perform {P/TV 584 DAFT}. NOTE 1. DustTrak takes approximately ten seconds to warm up prior to use. 2. DustTrak displays during warm up appear quickly. DustTrak displays must be checked immediately after powering on the unit and at the end of the display sequence. DustTrak 1.3 pb ON/OFF → Press",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,p2,0,"2.011 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLING MGUEEXPRSDAFTN011 06May04 N011-2 DAFT/PB3-01-0677A 1.4 Verify DustTrak battery display is above 40%. 1.5 Tmpry stow DustTrak. 1.6 Retrieve P-Trak-01. NOTE P-Trak-01 takes approximately one minute to warm up prior to use. P-Trak-01 1.7 pb ON/OFF → Press 1.8 After P-Trak-01 has warmed up (one minute), √Displays are active and visible √Particles per cubic centimeter (PT/CC) display is active and reading (upper portion of screen) √Battery power indicator is at least half full (lower right corner of screen) * * * * * * * * * * * * * * ******************************************************** If P-Trak-01 is not in the listed states, pb ON/OFF → Press (off) Unstow P-Trak Batteries. Remove used P-Trak Batteries. Dispose of P-Trak Batteries in appropriate trash. Replace with new P-Trak Batteries. pb ON/OFF → Press (on) √P-Trak-01 Displays If still no joy, √POIC ******************************************************** 1.9 Verify no visual warnings appear (lower left corner of screen) or audible warnings occur. 1.10 Tmpry stow P-Trak-01.",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,p3,0,"2.011 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLING MGUEEXPRSDAFTN011 06May04 N011-3 DAFT/PB3-01-0677A NOTE 1. DustTrak displays appear immediately after pressing buttons on the unit then displays fade. 2. Test ID information from DustTrak must be logged. 3. DustTrak Xµm Adapter will be used for DustTrak 10µm Adapter, DustTrak 2.5µm Adapter or DustTrak 1µm Adapter. 2. DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK INITIATION 2.1 Retrieve DustTrak. DustTrak 2.2 pb SAMPLING MODE → Press once √LOG 1 is visible (upper middle portion of screen) 2.3 pb SAMPLE → Press √TEST ID number 2.4 √DustTrak display is RECORDING * * * * * * * * * **************************************************************** If DustTrak display does not display RECORDING Repeat steps 2.2 and 2.3. If still no joy, √POIC **************************************************************** 2.5 Log TEST ID number from DustTrak on DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLE LOG. P-Trak-01 2.6 pb Down arrow → Press twice √LOG MODE 1 is visible (lower left portion of screen) 2.7 pb ↵ (Enter) → Press √LOG MODE 1 is visible (lower left portion of screen) √↵ TO STOP is visible (lower right portion of screen) 2.8 Log TEST ID number from P-Trak-01 on DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLE LOG. 2.9 Retrieve the DAFT Accessories Kit.",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,p4,0,"2.011 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLING MGUEEXPRSDAFTN011 06May04 N011-4 DAFT/PB3-01-0677A DAFT Accessories 2.10 Unstow: Kit P-Trak HEPA Filter DAFT HEPA Filter 2.11 Tmpry stow: DAFT HEPA Filter DAFT Accessories Kit P-Trak-01 2.12 Connect P-Trak HEPA Filter to P-Trak-01. P-Trak HEPA Filter hose end →|← P-Trak Hose Adapter 2.13 Retrieve DAFT HEPA Filter. DustTrak 2.14 Connect DAFT HEPA Filter to DustTrak. DAFT HEPA Filter hose end →|← DustTrak Xµm Adapter per Execution Notes 2.15 Wait 15 seconds. P-Trak-01 2.16 Verify particles per cubic centimeter (PT/CC) display: <10 (upper portion of screen). DustTrak 2.17 Verify milligrams per meters cubed (mg/m3) display: -0.001 to 0.003 (left prominent display). P-Trak-01 2.18 Disconnect P-Trak HEPA Filter from P-Trak-01. P-Trak HEPA Filter hose end ←|→ P-Trak Hose Adapter DustTrak 2.19 Disconnect DAFT HEPA Filter from DustTrak. DAFT HEPA Filter hose end ←|→ DustTrak Xµm Adapter per Execution Notes 2.20 Tmpry stow: P-Trak HEPA Filter DAFT HEPA Filter P-Trak-01 DustTrak * * * * * * * **************************************************************** If continuous beeping is heard from P-Trak-01 or DustTrak, √POIC for ground troubleshooting ****************************************************************",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,p5,0,"2.011 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLING MGUEEXPRSDAFTN011 06May04 N011-5 DAFT/PB3-01-0677A 3. DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLING 3.1 Retrieve DAFT Accessories Kit. DAFT Accessories 3.2 Unstow DAFT Sample Hose. Kit Tmpry stow DAFT Accessories Kit. 3.3 Retrieve P-Trak-01. P-Trak-01 3.4 Connect DAFT Sample Hose to P-Trak-01. To P-Trak Hose Adapter →|← P-Trak Hose Adapter 3.5 Retrieve DustTrak. DustTrak 3.6 Connect DAFT Sample Hose to DustTrak. To DustTrak Adapter →|← DustTrak Xµm Adapter per Execution Notes NOTE 1. The Single Strap and Cable Straps can be used for a secure setup for DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK Sampling when the sample location is stationary. 2. {5.011 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK CONFIGURATION WITH VELCRO RESTRAINTS} can be used as reference for a stationary setup. 3.7 √P-Trak-01 and DustTrak with DAFT Sample Hose are secured together Refer to {5.009 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK HARDWARE ASSEMBLY}. NOTE 1. Execution Notes will provide sample location for Sampling. 2. Sampling will take approximately six minutes to complete. P-Trak-01 3.8 √Particles per cubic centimeter (PT/CC) displays active and Sample Extraction is recording (upper portion of screen) DustTrak 3.9 √Milligrams per meters cubed (mg/m3) displays active and Sample Extraction is recording (left prominent display) 3.10 Log GMT on DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLE LOG. 3.11 Using P-Trak-01 and DustTrak, sample ambient air per Execution Notes for approximately six minutes.",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,p6,0,2.011 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLING MGUEEXPRSDAFTN011 06May04 N011-6 DAFT/PB3-01-0677A 3.12 Log ambient air sample location on DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLE LOG. End Video Activity 4. DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLING SHUTDOWN P-Trak-01 4.1 Disconnect DAFT Sample Hose from P-Trak-01. To P-Trak Hose Adapter ←|→ P-Trak Hose Adapter DustTrak 4.2 Disconnect DAFT Sample Hose from DustTrak. To DustTrak Adapter ←|→ DustTrak Xµm Adapter per Execution Notes 4.3 Tmpry stow DAFT Sample Hose. 4.4 Retrieve P-Trak HEPA Filter. P-Trak-01 4.5 Connect P-Trak HEPA Filter to P-Trak-01. P-Trak HEPA Filter hose end →|← P-Trak Hose Adapter 4.6 Retrieve DAFT HEPA Filter. DustTrak 4.7 Connect DAFT HEPA Filter to DustTrak. DAFT HEPA Filter hose end →|← DustTrak Xµm Adapter per Execution Notes 4.8 Wait 15 seconds. 4.9 Verify particles per cubic centimeter (PT/CC) display: <10 (upper portion of screen). 4.10 Verify milligrams per meters cubed (mg/m3) display: -0.001 to 0.003 (left prominent display). P-Trak-01 4.11 Disconnect P-Trak HEPA Filter from P-Trak-01. P-Trak HEPA Filter hose end ←|→ P-Trak Hose Adapter DustTrak 4.12 Disconnect DAFT HEPA Filter from DustTrak. DAFT HEPA Filter hose end ←|→ DustTrak Xµm Adapter per Execution Notes 4.13 Tmpry stow: P-Trak HEPA Filter DAFT HEPA Filter P-Trak-01 4.14 pb ↵ (Enter) → Press pb ON/OFF → Press √P-Trak power − Off,,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Sampling.pdf,p7,0,"2.011 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLING MGUEEXPRSDAFTN011 06May04 N011-7 DAFT/PB3-01-0677A DustTrak 4.15 pb SAMPLE → Press pb ON/OFF → Press √DustTrak power − Off 4.16 √P-Trak-01 and DustTrak are secure 4.17 Retrieve: DAFT Accessories Kit P-Trak HEPA Filter DAFT HEPA Filter DAFT Sample Hose DAFT Accessories 4.18 Stow: Kit P-Trak HEPA Filter DAFT HEPA Filter DAFT Sample Hose 4.19 Stow or Tmpry stow DAFT Accessories Kit. RESTOW tools, parts, materials, as required, to original locations:",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,p1,0,"2.012 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK DATA TRANSFER MGUEEXPRSDAFTN012 06May04 N012-1 DAFT/PB3-01-0677 Start_IMS OBJECTIVE: Dust and Aerosol measurement Feasibility Test (DAFT). This procedure transfers the data from the P-Trak and DustTrak after sample collection has been completed. STATION PARTS: DAFT Accessories Kit P/N DAFT-0700 DustTrak P/N DAFT-0201 P-Trak-01 P/N DAFT-0101 P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01 P/N DAFT-0601-1 ELC 1. ALCOHOL WICK REMOVAL 1.1 Unstow P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01. 1.2 Retrieve P-Trak-01. 1.3 √P-Trak-01 power − Off CAUTION To reduce the amount of alcohol released into ISS, performance of the Alcohol Wick Removal (steps 1.4 to 1.8) must be limited to one minute. 1.4 On P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01, remove Alcohol Wick Cap. Tmpry stow Alcohol Wick Cap. 1.5 On P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01, remove For Storage Only Cap (turn 1/4 turn CCW). Tmpry stow: For Storage Only Cap P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,p2,0,2.012 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK DATA TRANSFER MGUEEXPRSDAFTN012 06May04 N012-2 DAFT/PB3-01-0677 1.6 Remove Alcohol Wick from P-Trak-01 (turn 1/4 turn CCW). Retrieve P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01. Insert Alcohol Wick into P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01 using the alignment pin (turn 1/4 turn CW). 1.7 Retrieve and replace Alcohol Wick Cap on P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01 (finger tighten). Tmpry stow P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01. 1.8 Retrieve and insert For Storage Only Cap into P-Trak-01 using the alignment pin (turn 1/4 turn CW). 1.9 Stow P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01. 2. TRANSFER P-TRAK DATA 2.1 Retrieve DAFT Accessories Kit. DAFT Accessories 2.2 Unstow DAFT Data Cable. Kit 2.3 Tmpry stow DAFT Accessories Kit. ELC 2.4 √ELC powered √EXPRESS Application not active 2.5 Connect DAFT Data Cable to ELC. DAFT Data Cable port →|← RS232 2.6 Retrieve P-Trak-01. P-Trak-01 2.7 Connect DAFT Data Cable to P-Trak-01. DAFT Data Cable port →|← RS232 2.8 pb ON/OFF → Press √P-Trak-01 power − On 2.9 Wait until P-Trak clock stops counting down (warming up). 2.10 Secure P-Trak-01. ELC 2.11 Double click DAFT Icon. 2.12 Dust and Aerosol Measurement Feasibility Test (DAFT) sel Setup,,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,p3,0,2.012 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK DATA TRANSFER MGUEEXPRSDAFTN012 06May04 N012-3 DAFT/PB3-01-0677 2.13 Setup sel DustTrak tab 'Communications Port' √COM1 selected 'Data Transfer Files' √Destination directory - H:\DAFT\DustTrak 2.14 sel P-Trak tab 'Communications Port' √COM1 selected 'Data Transfer Files' √Destination directory - H:\DAFT\PTrak 2.15 sel Logging tab 'Message Log' √Log messages to a file is selected √Base file name - MsgLg √Destination directory - H:\DAFT\MsgLog 'Raw Communication Log' √Log raw communication to a file is selected √Base file name - ComLg √Destination directory - H:\DAFT\CommLog 2.16 cmd − OK 2.17 Dust and Aerosol Measurement Feasibility Test (DAFT) sel P-Trak,,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,p4,0,"2.012 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK DATA TRANSFER MGUEEXPRSDAFTN012 06May04 N012-4 DAFT/PB3-01-0677 2.18 PTrak 2.19 'Data Transfer' sel Specific Enter Test ID number per Execution Notes. cmd − Transfer 'Progress' Verify 0 of 1 complete starts incrementing. Verify bar increments. When Progress completes (1 of 1 complete), 'Transferred Data' Verify Test ID Success? − Yes * * * * ******************************************************************* If Test ID Success? − No Log GMT and P-Trak Test ID number on DAFT TRANSFER LOG * PTrak * * * * * * * * * * * * * * 'Data Transfer' sel Specific Enter unsuccessful Test ID number cmd − Transfer Verify Test ID Success? − Yes If Test ID Success? − No Repeat up to two more times After third failed attempt, Log Test ID number(s) as Failed on DAFT TRANSFER LOG Notify POIC and report Failed Test ID number. ******************************************************************* Repeat for each Test ID per Execution Notes. P-Trak-01 2.20 pb ON/OFF → Press √P-Trak-01 power − Off 2.21 Disconnect DAFT Data Cable from P-Trak-01. DAFT Data Cable port ←|→ RS232 2.22 Tmpry stow P-Trak-01.",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,p5,0,"2.012 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK DATA TRANSFER MGUEEXPRSDAFTN012 06May04 N012-5 DAFT/PB3-01-0677 NOTE DustTrak Xµm Adapter will be used for DustTrak 10µm Adapter, DustTrak 2.5µm Adapter or DustTrak 1µm Adapter. 3. TRANSFER DUSTTRAK DATA 3.1 Retrieve DustTrak. DustTrak 3.2 Connect DAFT Data Cable to DustTrak. DAFT Data Cable port →|← RS232 3.3 pb ON/OFF → Press √DustTrak power − On 3.4 Wait 10 seconds while DustTrak warms up. 3.5 Secure DustTrak. ELC 3.6 Dust and Aerosol Measurement Feasibility Test (DAFT) sel DustTrak",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,p6,0,"2.012 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK DATA TRANSFER MGUEEXPRSDAFTN012 06May04 N012-6 DAFT/PB3-01-0677 3.7 DustTrak 3.8 'Data Transfer' sel Specific Enter Test ID number per Execution Notes. cmd − Transfer 'Progress' Verify 0 of 1 complete starts incrementing. Verify bar increments. When Progress completes (1 of 1 complete), 'Transferred Data' Verify Test ID Success? − Yes * * * * ******************************************************************* If Test ID Success? − No Log GMT and DustTrak Test ID number on DAFT TRANSFER LOG * DustTrak * * * * * * * * * * * * * * 'Data Transfer' sel Specific Enter unsuccessful Test ID number cmd − Transfer Verify Test ID Success? − Yes If Test ID Success? − No Repeat up to two more times After third failed attempt, Log Test ID number(s) as Failed on DAFT TRANSFER LOG Notify POIC and report Failed Test ID number. ******************************************************************* Repeat for each Test ID per Execution Notes. 3.9 cmd − Exit Exit 'Pressing the 'Yes' button will close the DAFT application and stop any transfers in progress. Are you sure you want to exit?' sel Yes DustTrak 3.10 pb ON/OFF → Press √DustTrak power − Off ELC 3.11 Disconnect DAFT Data Cable from ELC. DAFT Data Cable port ←|→ RS232",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.DataTransfer.pdf,p7,0,"2.012 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK DATA TRANSFER MGUEEXPRSDAFTN012 06May04 N012-7 DAFT/PB3-01-0677 DustTrak 3.12 Disconnect DAFT Data Cable from DustTrak. DAFT Data Cable port ←|→ RS232 Tmpry stow DAFT Data Cable. 3.13 Tmpry stow DustTrak. 3.14 Retrieve DAFT Accessories Kit. DAFT Accessories 3.15 Retrieve and stow DAFT Data Cable. Kit 3.16 Tmpry stow DAFT Accessories Kit. 3.17 √DustTrak Xµm Adapter number has been logged on DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLE LOG √All data (numbers) has been recorded on DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLE LOG for this experiment run RESTOW tools, parts, materials, as required, to original locations:",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Restow.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Restow.pdf,p1,0,2.013 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK RESTOW MGUEEXPRSDAFTN013 06May04 N013-1 DAFT/PB3-01-0677 Start_IMS OBJECTIVE: Dust and Aerosol measurement Feasibility Test (DAFT). This procedure disassembles and stows the P-Trak and DustTrak. It completes the DAFT sample collection process. STATION PARTS: P-Trak-01 P/N DAFT-0101 DustTrak P/N DAFT-0201 DAFT Accessories Kit P/N DAFT-0700 P-Trak Batteries [6] P/N DAFT-0708 Used P-Trak Batteries P/N DAFT-0718 DustTrak Batteries [4] P/N DAFT-0709 Used DustTrak Batteries P/N DAFT-0719 Single Strap P/N PERS0041 Cable Straps [6] P/N 528-43074-1 STATION TOOLS: 4X4 Ziplock Bag [2] P/N 528-21039-2 Fine Point Sharpie P/N 528-40674-1 Bar Code Kit P/N 582-21046-3 1. P-TRAK DISASSEMBLY 1.1 Retrieve P-Trak-01. 1.2 √P-Trak-01 power − Off P-Trak-01 1.3 Disconnect P-Trak Hose Adapter from P-Trak-01. P-Trak Hose Adapter ←|→ Inlet Tmpry stow P-Trak-01. 1.4 Retrieve DAFT Accessories Kit. DAFT Accessories 1.5 Stow P-Trak Hose Adapter. Kit 1.6 Tmpry stow DAFT Accessories Kit. 1.7 Retrieve P-Trak-01.,,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Restow.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Restow.pdf,p2,0,"2.013 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK RESTOW MGUEEXPRSDAFTN013 06May04 N013-2 DAFT/PB3-01-0677 P-Trak-01 1.8 √For Storage Only Cap is installed in P-Trak-01 * * * * * ******************************************************************* If For Storage Only Cap is not installed, Perform {2.006 DAFT P-TRAK SETUP}, steps 2.3 to 2.8, then: ******************************************************************* 1.9 Remove and Tmpry stow P-Trak Battery Cover. 1.10 Remove P-Trak battery pack from P-Trak-01 (slight prying motion). 1.11 Remove P-Trak Batteries (six). Tmpry stow P-Trak Batteries (six). 1.12 Replace P-Trak battery pack in P-Trak-01 (using the alignment mark or guide). 1.13 Retrieve and replace P-Trak Battery Cover. 1.14 Stow P-Trak-01. 1.15 If first P-Trak Batteries stowage, Unstow: 4X4 Ziplock Bag (2) Fine Point Sharpie Bar Code Kit Tmpry stow: 4X4 Ziplock Bag Bar Code Kit If not first P-Trak Batteries stowage, Unstow Used P-Trak Batteries (P/N DAFT-0718). Go to step 1.22. 1.16 Using Fine Point Sharpie, label 4X4 Ziplock Bag, Used P-Trak Batteries P/N DAFT-0718. Tmpry stow: 4X4 Ziplock Bag Fine Point Sharpie 1.17 Retrieve Bar Code Kit. Bar Code Kit 1.18 Remove IMS Bar Code Label. Tmpry stow Bar Code Kit.",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Restow.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Restow.pdf,p3,0,"2.013 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK RESTOW MGUEEXPRSDAFTN013 06May04 N013-3 DAFT/PB3-01-0677 1.19 Retrieve 4X4 Ziplock Bag. 1.20 Place IMS Bar Code Label on Used P-Trak Batteries. 1.21 Manually update IMS or Notify POIC with the new DAFT B/C number (as required). 1.22 Retrieve P-Trak Batteries (six). Place P-Trak Batteries (six) in Used P-Trak Batteries. 1.23 Stow Used P-Trak Batteries. NOTE DustTrak Xµm Adapter will be used for DustTrak 10µm Adapter, DustTrak 2.5µm Adapter or DustTrak 1µm Adapter. 2. DUSTTRAK DISASSEMBLY 2.1 Retrieve DustTrak. 2.2 √DustTrak power − Off DustTrak 2.3 Disconnect DustTrak Xµm Adapter from DustTrak. DustTrak Xµm Adapter ←|→ Inlet 2.4 Tmpry stow: DustTrak DustTrak Xµm Adapter 2.5 Retrieve DAFT Accessories Kit. DAFT Accessories 2.6 Retrieve and stow DustTrak Xµm Adapter. Kit 2.7 Tmpry stow DAFT Accessories Kit. DustTrak 2.8 Retrieve DustTrak. Remove (unscrew) and Tmpry stow DustTrak Battery Cover.",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Restow.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Restow.pdf,p4,0,"2.013 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK RESTOW MGUEEXPRSDAFTN013 06May04 N013-4 DAFT/PB3-01-0677 NOTE Move DustTrak battery pack wire to prevent obstruction of the DustTrak Battery Pack. 2.9 Remove DustTrak battery pack from DustTrak (slight prying motion). 2.10 Remove DustTrak Batteries (four). Tmpry stow DustTrak Batteries (four). NOTE 1. DustTrak battery pack wire should cover the DustTrak battery pack after replacement of DustTrak battery pack. 2. DustTrak Battery Cover must be properly inserted into the DustTrak for secure closure. 3. DustTrak Battery Cover closure is spring loaded. Slight pressure is needed to secure DustTrak Battery Cover. 2.11 Replace DustTrak battery pack in DustTrak. 2.12 Retrieve and replace DustTrak Battery Cover taking care not to pinch DustTrak battery pack wire. 2.13 Stow DustTrak. 2.14 If first DustTrak Batteries stowage, Retrieve: Fine Point Sharpie 4X4 Ziplock Bag If not first DustTrak Batteries stowage, Unstow Used DustTrak Batteries (P/N DAFT-0719). Go to step 2.21. 2.15 Using Fine Point Sharpie, label 4X4 Ziplock Bag, Used DustTrak Batteries P/N DAFT-0719. Tmpry stow Fine Point Sharpie. 2.16 Retrieve Bar Code Kit. Bar Code Kit 2.17 Remove IMS Bar Code Label. Tmpry stow Bar Code Kit. 2.18 Retrieve 4X4 Ziplock Bag.",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Restow.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.Restow.pdf,p5,0,"2.013 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK RESTOW MGUEEXPRSDAFTN013 06May04 N013-5 DAFT/PB3-01-0677 2.19 Place IMS Bar Code Label on Used DustTrak Batteries. 2.20 Manually update IMS or Notify POIC with the new DAFT B/C number (as required). 2.21 Retrieve DustTrak Batteries (four). Place DustTrak Batteries (four) in Used DustTrak Batteries. 2.22 Stow: Fine Point Sharpie DAFT Accessories Kit Used DustTrak Batteries Bar Code Kit (as required) Single Strap (as required) Cable Straps (six) (as required) RESTOW tools, parts, materials, as required, to original locations:",,False
PSI-47,Presentations,PSI-47_Presentations_Daft_FactSheet.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Presentations/PSI-47_Presentations_Daft_FactSheet.pdf,p1,0,"Dust and Aerosol Measurement Feasibility Test (DAFT) Principal Investigators : Dr. David Urban, NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, OH; (216) 433-2835; david.l.urban@nasa.gov Dr. Gary Ruff, NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, OH; (216) 433-5697; gary.a.ruff@nasa.gov Project Manager: William Sheredy, NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, OH; [phone removed]; [e-mail removed] Project Lead: Greg Funk, Zin Technologies, Cleveland, OH; [phone removed]; [e-mail removed] Operations Lead: Nathan Funk, Zin Technologies, Cleveland, OH; [phone removed]; [e-mail removed] National Aeronautics and Space Administration Glenn Research Center Cleveland, Ohio DAFT Isn’t As Crazy As It Sounds T he Dust and Aerosol Measurement Feasibility Test (DAFT) was designed to ensure that a modiﬁed P-Trak®—a key component of the forthcoming NASA Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment (SAME)—will perform properly in the unique enviroment of micro- gravity. If the P-Trak® performs as expected, the device will be used in SAME to provide data that will help scientists design better ﬁre detectors for future, long-duration, manned missions. The Challenge The P-Trak® is a commercial device that counts ultra- ﬁne particles (it can recognize particles as small as 0.02 micrometer in diameter). It works by passing particulate-laden air through a heated chamber of vaporous isopropyl alcohol.",,False
PSI-47,Presentations,PSI-47_Presentations_Daft_FactSheet.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Presentations/PSI-47_Presentations_Daft_FactSheet.pdf,p1,1,"The individual particles serve as “seeds” around which the alcohol condenses when cooled, forming droplets large enough to be detected and counted as they scatter light from a laser beam (see the schematic on the other side). The P-Trak® must be tested in microgravity prior to its use in SAME because its alcohol condenser was redesigned to work properly in mi- crogravity. (The original smooth-walled P-Trak® condenser—meant for Earth-based use—depends on gravity.) NASA scientists modiﬁed the unit’s condenser by forming microgrooves in its wall to increase the alcohol ﬂow back to the wick (see photo on right). This modiﬁca- tion was based on the knowledge gained from previous microgravity ﬂuids physics experiments conducted by NASA GRC researchers. The Plan The main components of the DAFT experiment—the P-Trak® and the DustTrak® aerosol monitoring devices—were delivered to the International Space Station (ISS) in February 2005. (The DustTrak® uses a sensor to determine the intensity of light being scattered by particles and translates that number into an aerosal mass measurement. It is being used to test the accuracy of the P-Trak® because it is insensitive to gravitational forces.) The ﬁrst set of tests were conducted in February and March 2005 and the results were encouraging. Importantly, the modiﬁed condenser performed as expected.",,False
PSI-47,Presentations,PSI-47_Presentations_Daft_FactSheet.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Presentations/PSI-47_Presentations_Daft_FactSheet.pdf,p1,2,"The second set of tests will be conducted after the balance of DAFT parts (including a calibrated aerosol source and an unmodiﬁed P-Trak®) are delivered to the ISS. The P-Trak® and its components (clockwise from the left: alcohol wick, wick container, container cap, P-Trak®, and battery pack.) A cross-section of the modiﬁed P-Trak® condenser chamber shows the microgrooves that NASA engineers cut on the inside diameter to improve the ﬂow of alcohol back to the wick in microgravity. An engineer holding the DustTrak® (top) and the P-Trak® in their approximate locations in the EXPRESS rack (the blue velcro board has been superceded by straps).",,False
PSI-47,Presentations,PSI-47_Presentations_Daft_FactSheet.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Presentations/PSI-47_Presentations_Daft_FactSheet.pdf,p2,0,"Research Procedure During the ISS tests conducted in 2005, astronauts performed several steps: Turned on the P-Trak® and the DustTrak® Attached High Efﬁciency Particulate Air (HEPA) ﬁlters to the inlets to “zero” the particle counters (since the HEPA ﬁlter removes all airborne particles, if the particle counters don’t read “0” something is amiss) Connected the devices to a common sampling hose Recorded the ISS environment for several minutes Reconnected the HEPA ﬁlters to “zero” the particle counters Turned off the P-Trak® and the DustTrak® The tests were conducted in the US laboratory module Destiny in front of EXPRESS Rack 4; in the modules’s aft end and port side; and in Node 1. (The EXPRESS Rack is a standardized payload rack system that transports, stores, and supports experiments aboard the ISS; Node 1 is a hub to which various modules are attached.) Three of the tests sampled undisturbed cabin air; the other test validated the instruments at high particulate levels by having an astronaut create airborne debris at the P- Trak® and DustTrak® inlets by separating pieces of Velcro®. Results So Far The three tests that sampled the undisturbed environment in the ISS showed very low levels of airborne particulates, averaging fewer than 0.005 mg/m3 from the DustTrak® and fewer than 15 particles/cm3 from the P-Trak® (these are typical readings at the baseline).",,False
PSI-47,Presentations,PSI-47_Presentations_Daft_FactSheet.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Presentations/PSI-47_Presentations_Daft_FactSheet.pdf,p2,1,"These numbers are dramatically lower than the values recorded in the Space Shuttle in an earlier experiment (~ .050 mg/m3). Lower levels are to be expected because the ISS US Lab has HEPA ﬁltration for the cabin air as compared to merely a ﬁne screen on the shuttle air handler. Furthermore, the typical shuttle crew of seven astronauts generates more airborne particulate than an ISS crew of two astronauts. Future DAFT Tests The next series of tests will take place once the remaining DAFT equipment is delivered to the ISS. These will be performed with Arizona Road Dust (ARD)—a standard aerosal test material of known particle size and distribution—and nitrogen aerosol inside a 15-liter Mylar® bag. Testing a known particulate will allow scientists to establish deﬁnitively how well the P-Trak® works in microgravity. Additional Information More experiment details—including periodic updates and photos—can be found on the DAFT Web site: http://microgravity.grc.nasa.gov/combustion/daft/daft_index.htm. 1. 2. 3. 4. 5. 6. DAFT: Research Procedure—Results So Far—Future DAFT Tests—Additional Information The DustTrak®—shown here with its battery pack—is insensitive to gravitational forces. It is used to determine the accuracy of the modiﬁed P-Trak®. P-Trak® DustTrak® Size (inches) 10.75 x 5.5 x 5.5 8.7 x 5.9 x 3.4 Weight (lbs) 3.8 3.3 Particle size range (mm) 0.02 to > 1.0 0.10 to 10 The P-Trak® and the DustTrak® compared.",,False
PSI-47,Presentations,PSI-47_Presentations_Daft_FactSheet.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Presentations/PSI-47_Presentations_Daft_FactSheet.pdf,p2,2,"As shown in this P-Trak® schematic, particle- laden air passes through a heated chamber containing vaporized alcohol and then into a cooled chamber where the alcohol droplets form around the particles. The particles are counted as they pass through a laser beam. ���� ������ ��������� �������� ��������� �������� ����������� ������������ �������� ������ ��������� ������ ����� ����� ������ �������� ����� ���������� ���������������",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.SetUp.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.SetUp.pdf,p1,0,"2.010 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SETUP MGUEEXPRSDAFTN010 06May04 N010-1 DAFT/PB3-01-0677A Start_IMS OBJECTIVE: Dust and Aerosol measurement Feasibility Test (DAFT). This procedure assembles the P-Trak and DustTrak. It prepares the P-Trak and DustTrak for future sample collection. STATION PARTS: DustTrak P/N DAFT-0201 P-Trak-01 P/N DAFT-0101 DAFT Accessories Kit P/N DAFT-0700 P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01 P/N DAFT-0601-1 P-Trak Batteries [6] P/N DAFT-0708 Used P-Trak Batteries P/N DAFT-0718 DustTrak Batteries [4] P/N DAFT-0709 Used DustTrak Batteries P/N DAFT-0719 NOTE 1. This procedure contains Near Real-Time video downlink or LAB VTR recording requirements. 2. AOS required for POIC to configure onboard system for downlink or LAB VTR recording. 1. P-TRAK ASSEMBLY 1.1 Unstow: P-Trak-01 P-Trak Batteries(Used P-Trak Batteries) (six) DAFT Accessories Kit Tmpry stow: P-Trak Batteries(Used P-Trak Batteries) (six) DAFT Accessories Kit Start Video Activity 1.2 If video is required, Perform {P/TV 584 DAFT}. P-Trak-01 1.3 Remove and Tmpry stow P-Trak Battery Cover. 1.4 Retrieve P-Trak Batteries(Used P-Trak Batteries) (six).",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.SetUp.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.SetUp.pdf,p2,0,"2.010 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SETUP MGUEEXPRSDAFTN010 06May04 N010-2 DAFT/PB3-01-0677A 1.5 Remove P-Trak battery pack from P-Trak-01 (slight prying motion). Install P-Trak Batteries(Used P-Trak Batteries) (six) (per marked orientation). 1.6 Replace P-Trak battery pack in P-Trak-01 (using the alignment mark or guide). 1.7 Retrieve and replace P-Trak Battery Cover. 1.8 Tmpry stow P-Trak-01. 1.9 Retrieve DAFT Accessories Kit. DAFT Accessories 1.10 Unstow P-Trak Hose Adapter. Kit Tmpry stow DAFT Accessories Kit. 1.11 Retrieve P-Trak-01. P-Trak-01 1.12 Connect P-Trak Hose Adapter to P-Trak-01. P-Trak Hose Adapter →|← Inlet Tmpry stow P-Trak-01. 2. ALCOHOL WICK INSERTION 2.1 Unstow P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01. 2.2 Retrieve P-Trak-01. CAUTION To reduce the amount of alcohol released into ISS, performance of the Alcohol Wick Insertion (steps 2.3 to 2.7) must be limited to one minute. P-Trak-01 2.3 Remove For Storage Only Cap (turn 1/4 turn CCW). Tmpry stow For Storage Only Cap. 2.4 On P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01, remove Alcohol Wick Cap. Tmpry stow Alcohol Wick Cap. 2.5 Remove Alcohol Wick from P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01 (turn 1/4 turn CCW). Tmpry stow P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01. Insert Alcohol Wick into P-Trak-01 using the alignment pin (turn 1/4 turn CW).",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.SetUp.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.SetUp.pdf,p3,0,"2.010 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SETUP MGUEEXPRSDAFTN010 06May04 N010-3 DAFT/PB3-01-0677A 2.6 Retrieve P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01. Retrieve and insert For Storage Only Cap into P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01 using the alignment pin (turn 1/4 turn CW). 2.7 Retrieve and replace Alcohol Wick Cap on P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01 (finger tighten). 2.8 Stow or Tmpry stow: per Execution Notes P-Trak-01 P-Trak Alcohol Wick Container-01 NOTE DustTrak Xµm Adapter will be used for DustTrak 10µm Adapter, DustTrak 2.5µm Adapter or DustTrak 1µm Adapter. 3. DUSTTRAK ASSEMBLY 3.1 Unstow: DustTrak DustTrak Batteries(Used DustTrak Batteries) (four) Tmpry stow DustTrak Batteries(Used DustTrak Batteries) (four). DustTrak 3.2 Remove (unscrew) and Tmpry stow DustTrak Battery Cover. Retrieve DustTrak Batteries(Used DustTrak Batteries) (four). NOTE Move DustTrak battery pack wire to prevent obstruction of the DustTrak battery pack. 3.3 Remove DustTrak battery pack from DustTrak (slight prying motion). Install DustTrak Batteries(Used DustTrak Batteries) (four) (per marked orientation). NOTE 1. DustTrak battery pack wire should cover the DustTrak battery pack after replacement of DustTrak battery pack. 2. DustTrak Battery Cover must be properly inserted into the DustTrak for secure closure. 3. DustTrak Battery Cover closure is spring loaded. Slight pressure is needed to secure DustTrak Battery Cover. 3.4 Replace DustTrak battery pack in DustTrak.",,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.SetUp.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.SetUp.pdf,p3,1,3.5 Retrieve and replace (hand tighten) DustTrak Battery Cover taking care not to pinch DustTrak battery pack wire.,,False
PSI-47,Engineering Documents,PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.SetUp.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Engineering Documents/PSI-47_Engineering Documents_Procedure.SetUp.pdf,p4,0,"2.010 DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SETUP MGUEEXPRSDAFTN010 06May04 N010-4 DAFT/PB3-01-0677A 3.6 Tmpry stow DustTrak. 3.7 Retrieve DAFT Accessories Kit. DAFT Accessories 3.8 Unstow DustTrak Xµm Adapter per Execution Notes. Kit Remove and discard tabbed Kapton Tape (covering adapter), as required. 3.9 Log DustTrak Xµm Adapter number on DAFT P-TRAK AND DUSTTRAK SAMPLE LOG. 3.10 Retrieve DustTrak. NOTE Over-tightening of the DustTrak Xµm Adapter may damage the O-ring. DustTrak 3.11 Connect DustTrak Xµm Adapter to DustTrak Inlet by turning the DustTrak Xµm Adapter approximately 2 1/2 turns CW (hand tighten). 3.12 Tmpry stow: DustTrak DAFT Accessories Kit End Video Activity RESTOW tools, parts, materials, as required, to original locations:",,False
PSI-47,Experimental table,PSI-47_Experimental table_DAFT.csv,data/raw/files/PSI-47/Experimental table/PSI-47_Experimental table_DAFT.csv,all,0,sheet PSI-47_Experimental table_DAFT.csv: 3 non-empty rows header: Session Name | Actual Day | Hardware Description | Sample Material | Sampled Location row 1: D1 | GMT 54 (2005) | Contained the modified P-Trak with Alcohol Wick and batteries | Arizona Road Dust (ARD Fine) in combination with ISS GN2 to create an aerosol for measurement purposes. | Sampled air near Express rack 4 row 2: D2 | GMT 63 (2005) | Contained DustTrak and batteries | Arizona Road Dust (ARD Fine) in combination with ISS GN2 to create an aerosol for measurement purposes. | Sampled separated Velcro and the air near Port rack 6 and in Node 1,,False
PSI-60,metadata,PSI-60_metadata_NEWID-5_Computational and Ex.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-60/metadata/PSI-60_metadata_NEWID-5_Computational and Ex.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,0,"ONTOLOGY SOURCE REFERENCE Term Source Name PSI EFO Term Source File https://psi.nasa.gov/ http://data.bioontology.org/ontologies/EFO Term Source Version 1.0 132 Term Source Description User defined terms Experimental Factor Ontology INVESTIGATION Investigation Identifier Investigation Title Investigation Description Investigation Submission Date Investigation Public Release Date INVESTIGATION PUBLICATIONS Investigation PubMed ID Investigation Publication DOI Investigation Publication Author List Investigation Publication Title Investigation Publication Status Investigation Publication Status Term Accession Number Investigation Publication Status Term Source REF INVESTIGATION CONTACTS Investigation Person Last Name Investigation Person First Name Investigation Person Mid Initials Investigation Person Email Investigation Person Phone Investigation Person Fax Investigation Person Address Investigation Person Affiliation Investigation Person Roles Investigation Person Roles Term Accession Number Investigation Person Roles Term Source REF STUDY Study Identifier PSI-60 Study Title Computational and Experimental Analysis of SPICE Data Sets Study Description Study Submission Date 29-Aug-2022 Study Public Release Date 30-Sep-2025 Study File Name s_PSI-60.txt Comment[Acknowledgments] Comment[Approach] The approach was to combine image processing, experimental diagnostics, and detailed soot modeling.",,False
PSI-60,metadata,PSI-60_metadata_NEWID-5_Computational and Ex.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-60/metadata/PSI-60_metadata_NEWID-5_Computational and Ex.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,1,"Raw Nikon NEF images were decoded into three color channels and analyzed using color-ratio pyrometry to extract soot temperature fields. Absolute light calibration from SPICE was then used to calculate soot volume fractions. For computational modeling, a coupled flow–species–soot system was implemented, modified to include radiation re-absorption and discrete-sectional aggregate formation. Simulations were performed for both Yale and ACME burner configurations under normal and microgravity conditions. Comparisons between experimental data and computed results guided sensitivity analyses of soot evolution parameters, including the effects of radiation, scattering, boundary conditions, and heat transfer to the burner structure. Comment[DOI] 10.60555/mzjm-yv57 Comment[Data Source Accession] Comment[Data Source Link] Comment[Experiment Hardware] Comment[Experiment Hardware Link] Comment[Flight Platform] Comment[Funding] 80NSSC19K1634 Comment[Hypothesis] Microgravity conditions would produce longer, more diffuse flames with lower temperatures and increased soot concentrations compared to normal gravity, and that careful integration of experimental temperature fields into the simulations would reduce discrepancies between measured and predicted soot distributions.",,False
PSI-60,metadata,PSI-60_metadata_NEWID-5_Computational and Ex.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-60/metadata/PSI-60_metadata_NEWID-5_Computational and Ex.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,2,"Comment[Identifiers] Comment[Investigation Acronym] Comment[Investigation End Date] 08-29-2022 Comment[Investigation Start Date] 06-05-2019 Comment[Managing NASA Center] Glenn Research Center (GRC) Comment[Mission End] Comment[Mission Link] Comment[Mission Name] Comment[Mission Start] Comment[Objective] This ground investigation sought to increase understanding of the issues that control the large discrepancy between soot formation in 1g and in 0g flames. The objective of this investigation was to integrate computational and experimental methods to derive temperature distributions and soot volume fractions from the Smoke Point in Coflow Experiment (SPICE) data available in PSI. By analyzing over 1600 raw DSLR images and incorporating advanced soot models, the study sought to improve understanding of soot formation, oxidation, radiative re-absorption, and aggregate growth in heavily sooting flames. The work further aimed to reconcile microgravity and normal gravity flame behavior, identify key physical and chemical processes influencing smoke point development, and provide a framework for evaluating model parameter sensitivity.",,False
PSI-60,metadata,PSI-60_metadata_NEWID-5_Computational and Ex.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-60/metadata/PSI-60_metadata_NEWID-5_Computational and Ex.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,3,"Comment[Project Identifier] Comment[Project Link] Comment[Project Title] Comment[Project Type] Ground Investigation Comment[Proposal Title] Computational and Experimental Analysis of SPICE Data Sets Comment[Research Area] Combustion Science Comment[Research Impacts] This study sought to enhance understanding of soot formation, flame structure, and radiative transport under microgravity and normal gravity, improving predictive models for combustion in both spacecraft and terrestrial systems. For space applications, the results support safer fire detection, suppression, and cleaner combustion technologies critical for long-duration missions. On Earth, the insights help reduce pollutant emissions, optimize combustion efficiency, and inform the design of cleaner energy and industrial systems. Comment[Space Program] National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA), PSI grant Comment[Study Grant Number] Comment[SubResearch Area] Gaseous STUDY DESIGN DESCRIPTORS Study Design Type SPICE Flames Soot Laminar diffusion flames Coflow Study Design Type Term Accession Number https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ Study Design Type Term Source REF PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI STUDY PUBLICATIONS Study PubMed ID Study Publication DOI 10.1080/00102202.2022.2041621 Study Publication Author List Dobbins Richard R., Tinjero Jesse, Squeo Joseph, Zhao Xinyu, Hall Robert J., Colket Meredith B., Long Marshall B., Smooke Mitchell D.",,False
PSI-60,metadata,PSI-60_metadata_NEWID-5_Computational and Ex.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-60/metadata/PSI-60_metadata_NEWID-5_Computational and Ex.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,4,Study Publication Title A Combined Experimental and Computational Study of Soot Formation in Normal and Microgravity Conditions (2022) Study Publication Status published Study Publication Status Term Accession Number http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 Study Publication Status Term Source REF EFO Comment[Study Publication Link] https://doi.org/10.1080/00102202.2022.2041621 STUDY FACTORS Study Factor Name Study Factor Type Study Factor Type Term Accession Number Study Factor Type Term Source REF STUDY ASSAYS Study Assay File Name Study Assay Measurement Type Study Assay Measurement Type Term Accession Number Study Assay Measurement Type Term Source REF Study Assay Technology Type Study Assay Technology Type Term Accession Number Study Assay Technology Type Term Source REF Study Assay Technology Platform STUDY PROTOCOLS Study Protocol Name Study Protocol Type Study Protocol Type Term Accession Number Study Protocol Type Term Source REF Study Protocol Description Study Protocol URI Study Protocol Version Study Protocol Parameters Name Study Protocol Parameters Name Term Accession Number Study Protocol Parameters Name Term Source REF Study Protocol Components Name Study Protocol Components Type Study Protocol Components Type Term Accession Number Study Protocol Components Type Term Source REF STUDY CONTACTS Study Person Last Name Long Smooke Dietrich Study Person First Name Marshall Mitchell Daniel Study Person Mid Initials B. D. L.,,False
