PSI-99,metadata,PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-99/metadata/PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,0,ONTOLOGY SOURCE REFERENCE Term Source Name EFO PSI Term Source File http://data.bioontology.org/ontologies/EFO https://psi.nasa.gov/ Term Source Version 132 1.0 Term Source Description Experimental Factor Ontology User defined terms INVESTIGATION Investigation Identifier Investigation Title Investigation Description Investigation Submission Date Investigation Public Release Date INVESTIGATION PUBLICATIONS Investigation PubMed ID Investigation Publication DOI Investigation Publication Author List Investigation Publication Title Investigation Publication Status Investigation Publication Status Term Accession Number Investigation Publication Status Term Source REF INVESTIGATION CONTACTS Investigation Person Last Name Investigation Person First Name Investigation Person Mid Initials Investigation Person Email Investigation Person Phone Investigation Person Fax Investigation Person Address Investigation Person Affiliation Investigation Person Roles Investigation Person Roles Term Accession Number Investigation Person Roles Term Source REF STUDY Study Identifier PSI-99 Study Title Spacecraft Fire Experiment-II Study Description Study Submission Date 18-Sep-2024 Study Public Release Date 18-Sep-2024 Study File Name s_PSI-99.txt Comment[Acknowledgments] Comment[Approach] The Saffire-II experiment was performed on the Orbital Science’s Cygnus resupply vehicle after it deberthed from the ISS.,,False
PSI-99,metadata,PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-99/metadata/PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,1,"As a follow on to the Saffire-I investigation, the Saffire-II investigation added more samples including SIBAL, silicone, structure PMMA, flat PMMA, and PMMA with Nomex. The Saffire-1I flight unit had a sample card installed in the middle of a large flow duct. Air is drawn through the flow duct by fans located at the top of the duct with flow straighteners at the bottom of the experiment module. Saffire-II has a sample card that contains 9 samples, each 5 cm x 29 cm. Depending on the sample material, the Saffire-II was conducted at an air flow velocity of either 20 or 30 cm/s, the flow being either concurrent or opposed. Once the sample was ignited flame images were captured using two cameras. In addition, thermocouples provided flow, flame, and plume temperatures, two radiometers recorded flame radiation, solid state sensors were used to measure pressure, CO2 and O2 mole faction, and two anemometer tracked flow velocity. All data was compressed, and subsequently downlinked. Details related to samples and experimental parameters are provided in the Experimental Details file in the Science document folder.",,False
PSI-99,metadata,PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-99/metadata/PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,2,"Comment[DOI] 10.60555/2chp-m469 Comment[Data Source Accession] Comment[Data Source Link] Comment[Experiment Hardware] Cygnus Pressurized Cargo Module, SAFFIRE Comment[Experiment Hardware Link] https://cdn.northropgrumman.com/-/media/wp-content/uploads/Cygnus-Fact-Sheet-1.pdf, https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20170000230/downloads/20170000230.pdf Comment[Flight Platform] Cygnus Spacecraft Comment[Funding] NASA Comment[Hypothesis] This investigation hypothesizes that due to large differences between fire behavior in normal and reduced gravity, the use of terrestrial fires and fire standards to design spacecraft fire safety systems presents an inherent risk to the vehicle because of the significant level of uncertainty. While this approach has been successful thus far, the uncertainty and risk will increase as exploration missions venture further from earth with considerably longer transit times for a safe return. There is a significant lack of microgravity data available for spacecraft designers on which to base their fire safety designs and procedures. To address this knowledge gap, this investigation proposed to conduct large-scale fire safety experiments in an expendable spacecraft without risk to a crew.",,False
PSI-99,metadata,PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-99/metadata/PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,3,"Comment[Identifiers] Comment[Investigation Acronym] SAFFIRE-II Comment[Investigation End Date] 04-01-2017 Comment[Investigation Start Date] 09-30-2016 Comment[Managing NASA Center] Glenn Research Center (GRC) Comment[Mission End] Comment[Mission Link] Comment[Mission Name] Comment[Mission Start] Comment[Objective] The objectives of SAFFIRE-II are to determine how rapidly a large scale fire grows in low-gravity and to investigate the low-g flammability limits compared to those obtained in NASA’s normal gravity material flammability screening test. Comment[Project Identifier] Comment[Project Link] Comment[Project Title] Comment[Project Type] Microgravity Investigation Comment[Proposal Title] Comment[Research Area] Combustion Science Comment[Research Impacts] The Saffire-II experiment provides a new way to study a realistic fire on an exploration vehicle, which has not been possible in the past because the risks are too high. Instruments on an unmanned cargo vessel measure oxygen, carbon dioxide, heat, pressure and flame growth, and two video cameras provide views of the flame. Results determine microgravity flammability limits for several spacecraft materials, help to validate NASA’s material selection criteria, and how microgravity and limited oxygen affect flame size. The investigation is crucial for the safety of current and future space missions.",,False
PSI-99,metadata,PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-99/metadata/PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,4,"Studying fire in small, sealed environments such as the Cygnus cargo supply vehicle benefits fire safety and prevention efforts on Earth, including inside mines, airplanes, or submarines. Comment[Space Program] National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) Comment[Study Grant Number] Comment[SubResearch Area] Surface Combustion STUDY DESIGN DESCRIPTORS Study Design Type fire fire safety Cygnus Nomex SAFFIRE SHS Smoldering Smoke Distribution Study Design Type Term Accession Number https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ Study Design Type Term Source REF PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI STUDY PUBLICATIONS Study PubMed ID 35197652 Study Publication DOI 10.1016/j.combustflame.2018.10.012 10.1016/j.combustflame.2018.10.016 10.1007/s10694-019-00863-3 10.1016/j.proci.2018.05.168 10.1016/j.firesaf.2020.102977 Study Publication Author List JE Niehaus, PV Ferkul, SA Gokoglu, GA Ruff W Schoren, GA Ruff, DL Urban Urban David L., Ferkul Paul, Olson Sandra, Ruff Gary A., Easton John, T'ien James S., Liao Ya-Ting T., Li Chengyao, Fernandez-Pello Carlos, Torero Jose L., Legros Guillaume, Eigenbrod Christian, Smirnov Nickolay, Fujita Osamu, Rouvreau Sébastien, Toth Balazs, Jomaas Grunde Thomsen Maria, Fernandez-Pello Carlos, Ruff Gary A., Urban David L.",,False
PSI-99,metadata,PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-99/metadata/PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,5,"Olson Sandra L., Urban David L., Ruff Gary A., Ferkul Paul V., Toth Balazs, Eigenbrod Christian, Meyer Florian, Jomaas Grunde Li Chengyao, Liao Ya-Ting T., T'ien James S., Urban David L., Ferkul Paul, Olson Sandra, Ruff Gary A., Easton John Wang Xiaoliang, Zhou Hao, Arnott W. Patrick, Meyer Marit E., Taylor Samuel, Firouzkouhi Hatef, Moosmüller Hans, Chow Judith C., Watson John G. Study Publication Title Buoyant Effects on the Flammability of Silicone Samples Planned for the Spacecraft Fire Experiment (Saffire), Conference: 45th International Conference on Environmental Systems ICES-2015-[293] 12-16 July 2015, Bellevue, Washington The Spacecraft Fire Experiment (Saffire) - Objectives, Development and Status Flame spread:",,False
PSI-99,metadata,PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-99/metadata/PSI-99_metadata_PSI-167_SAFFIRE-II.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,6,Effects of microgravity and scale Buoyancy effects on concurrent flame spread over thick PMMA Concurrent Flame Spread Over Two-Sided Thick PMMA Slabs in Microgravity Transient flame growth and spread processes over a large solid fabric in concurrent low-speed flows in microgravity – Model versus experiment Evaluation of gas and particle sensors for detecting spacecraft-relevant fire emissions Study Publication Status published published published published published published published Study Publication Status Term Accession Number http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 Study Publication Status Term Source REF EFO EFO EFO EFO EFO EFO Comment[Study Publication Link] https://www.researchgate.net/publication/282252038_Buoyant_Effects_on_the_Flammability_of_Silicone_Samples_Planned_for_the_Spacecraft_Fire_Safety_Experiment_Saffire https://ntrs.nasa.gov/search.jsp?R=20170001636 http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2018.10.012 http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2018.10.016 http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s10694-019-00863-3 http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.proci.2018.05.168 http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.firesaf.2020.102977 STUDY FACTORS Study Factor Name Study Factor Type Study Factor Type Term Accession Number Study Factor Type Term Source REF STUDY ASSAYS Study Assay,,False
