PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p6,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 1 1.0 INTRODUCTION 1.1 Scope This plan applies to all Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment (SAME) hardware, software, and operating procedures developed under the Microgravity Research, Development, and Operations Contract (MRDOC). This plan establishes the methods and responsibilities for verifying the SAME requirements. The requirements for the SAME project, including investigation, engineering, product assurance, safety, and carrier requirements, are in Appendix A of this document. The verification methods and sources of these requirements are also included in Appendix A. This plan defines the verification process, leading to certification of the SAME flight hardware. 1.2 Purpose This document defines the verification activities required to show that SAME complies with its requirements. These activities include the verification of investigation requirements from the Exploration Investigation Requirements Document (EIRD) for SAME, software engineering requirements from the Software Requirements Documents, product assurance requirements from the Project Product Assurance Plan (PAP), safety requirements from the Hazard Reports, and carrier requirements from the Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) Interface Control Document (ICD). This document includes all of the requirements to be verified by SAME and serves as the Engineering Requirements Document to organize and list all necessary requirements.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p6,1,"1.3 TBDs TBDs are used as a place-holder for requirements, verifications, or document numbers that are not known at the time of the latest release of this document. 2.0 DOCUMENTS 2.1 Applicable Documents The documents listed are applicable to the SAME project to the extent specified herein. Exploration Investigation Requirements Document for SAME a.k.a. Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment Standardized Hazard Report (STD-SAME-01) and Unique Hazard Reports (SAME-03, -04, -05, and -08) MSFC-ICD-3462 Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) Interface Control Document for SAME (Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment) MSFC-RQMT-2888 Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) Investigation Interface Requirements Document MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001 MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document NSTS 1700.7B Safety Policy and Requirements for Payloads Using the Space Transportation System",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p7,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 2 NSTS 1700.7B ISS Addendum Safety Policy and Requirements for Payloads Using the International Space Station NSTS/ISS 13830 Payload Safety Review and Data Submittal SAME-DOC-001 Baseline Concept Description Document SAME-DOC-004 Software Requirements Document SAME-DOC-005 Software Interface Control Document SAME-PLN-003 Product Assurance Plan SSP 50313 Display and Graphics Commonality Standard SSP 57000 Pressurized Payloads Interface Control Document 2.2 Reference Documents The documents listed are provided only as reference material for background information and are not imposed as requirements. SAME-PLN-002 Configura tion Management Plan 3.0 ACRONYMS AND ABBREVIATIONS EIRD Exploration Investigation Requirements Document EU Engineering Unit GRC Glenn Research Center HFIT Human Factors Integration Team ICD Interface Control Document ISS International Space Station JSC Johnson Space Center KSC Kennedy Space Center MRDOC Microgravity Research, Development, and Operations Contract MSG Microgravity Science Glovebox PSRP Payload Safety Review Panel QA Quality Assurance SAME Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment SARG Space Assurance and Requirements and Guidelines SVTL Safety Verification Tracking Log VDS Verification Definition Sheet",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p8,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 3 4.0 VERIFICATIONS 4.1 General The SAME Project Office will verify all the requirements imposed on the SAME System. The primary sites for verification activities will be the Glenn Research Center (GRC) and the item developer’s site unless otherwise directed by the MSG or International Space Station (ISS) programs. All requirements will be recorded in this document and disseminated to the design team. This document will provide traceability from the parent requirement through the subsequent derived requirements. It will also identify the verification source document and verification method. SAME will be building and verifying more than one set of flight hardware. Consequently, a certain number of verifications must be repeated for each serialized assembly. A subset of the requirements is designated as series requirements. 4.1.1 Verification Methods The methods used to complete the verifications are analysis, test, inspection and demonstration. The method(s) required for the SAME verifications are listed in the requirements matrix in Appendix A. The methods of verification are defined in MSFC-RQMT-2888 as stated in Sections 4.1.1.1, 4.1.1.2, 4.1.1.3, and 4.1.1.4. 4.1.1.1 Analysis Analysis is a technical evaluation that relates equipment design and use parameters for prediction of actual design and operation. Analysis may be used to verify requirements, provided established techniques used are adequate to yield confidence, or where testing is impractical.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p8,1,"Included in this category is analysis of similarity to items previously verified to the same criteria or more stringent criteria. Verification accomplished by analysis shall be evidenced by an analysis report. 4.1.1.2 Test Test is actual operation of equipment under simulated conditions or the subjection of equipment to specified environments to measure responses. Verification accomplished by test shall be evidenced by a test report. 4.1.1.3 Inspection Inspection is a physical evaluation of equipment and associated documentation. Inspection may be used to verify construction features, drawing compliance, workmanship, and physical condition. It includes determination of physical dimensions. Verification accomplished by inspection shall be evidenced by an inspection report. 4.1.1.4 Demonstration Demonstration is the qualitative determination of compliance with requirements by observation during actual operation or simulation under preplanned conditions and guidelines. Some human factor requirements where the method of closure is a demonstration require the investigation to be set up in its on-orbit configuration in the MSG Engineering Unit (EU) to verify that the requirement is satisfied.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p9,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 4 4.1.2 Tools (Requirements Matrix and Verification Tracking Log) A matrix has been compiled in an Excel spreadsheet to include all requirements to be satisfied by SAME. It identifies the source of each requirement, the discipline under which it falls, the verification method to be used for closure, and resources necessary to perform the verification. It also identifies series requirements. The matrix, in Appendix A, will be provided to the engineering team for use in verification activities, and will be included in the released (.pdf) version of this document. The verification tracking log (VTL) will be used to track the status of the verifications and the documentation provided as evidence for the completion of the verifications. It is an Excel workbook that includes five worksheets: the VTL itself, a statistics page, the current version of the requirements matrix, a summary of applicable ECOs, and a notes/explanation page. The VTL will not be configuration- controlled as it is a summary of the state of controlled documentation and it will not be included in the released version of this document. It will be available in the project team’s shared directory. 4.2 Planning 4.2.1 Verification Definition Sheets A Verification Definition Sheet (VDS) will be used to complete the carrier requirements from MSFC- ICD-3462. The corresponding VDS for each carrier requirement is listed in the requirements matrix and can be found in Appendix E of MSFC-RQMT-2888.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p9,1,"The VDS provides instructions, definitions, references, and guidelines for verification activities associated with each requirement. 4.2.2 Test Plans and Procedures The controlled requirements matrix will be distributed to the engineering team so that the requirements can be grouped into meaningful test plans or procedures that will allow for efficient testing and closure of the requirements. Test plans and procedures will be written to include the verification statements to ensure that the requirements are clearly stated, understood, and closed out by the test. Test plans and procedures containing carrier requirements will also contain the corresponding VDS. Test plans and procedures will be a project team effort lead by the Systems Engineering team. Plans and procedures will use exact language from the requirement sources to convey the objective of the test as completion of these verifications. The template of how the requirements will be incorporated into the documents is shown in Figure 1. This table, along with the introductory text, will be included in test plans and procedures intended for verification activities. 4.2.3 Implementation and Reporting 4.2.3.1 Implementation Test verifications in the matrix will be logically grouped into similar items that can be completed in a single test. The test plans and procedures will be written accordingly.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p9,2,"Some of the verifications will be closed on the functional model or other similar flight-like hardware when the testing of flight hardware is not necessary or cannot be accomplished due to the nature of the test. Otherwise, testing will be done on the flight hardware. The tests performed on non-flight hardware are indicated in the requirements matrix.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p10,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 5 Inspections will be done at the time of hardware assembly and will be done on flight hardware or flight drawings. Analyses will be done using the flight configuration of hardware for the calculations. Demonstrations will be done on the flight hardware. All Human Factors (HFIT) verifications will be done once the hardware is delivered to MSG and the HFIT evaluation is performed. Additional verifications require integrated setup or testing in the MSG EU and will be completed after the hardware is delivered to MSG. These verifications are identified in the requirements matrix. Figure 1: Example of Verification List in Documents 4.2.3.2 Reporting Test, analysis and inspection reports will be written as evidence that the tests, analyses and inspections were performed to satisfy the requirements. Reports will include the requirement statements, as shown in Figure 1, to indicate what verifications were performed and are closed by the report. Reports and other necessary documentation will be kept by the Systems Engineering team and Configuration Management as evidence that the verifications were performed. The final report generated against a carrier requirement will be a comprehensive report intended to close out the requirement, upon which a Certificate of Compliance is issued. Certificates of Compliance will be generated to close verifications by demonstration.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p11,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 6 4.3 Certificate of Compliance As the carrier verification activities are completed, the SAME team will generate and sign Certificates of Compliance. With the completion of all verifications identified in this document and their associated Certificates of Compliance, all of the requirements and verifications of MSG compatibility, functionality, and safety will have been met. 4.4 Maintenance of the Certificate of Compliance Any changes to SAME occurring after the verification activities are completed will be reviewed to assess their impacts on the individual requirements and their associated verification certificates. If such a review determines that re-verification is warranted, the changes to SAME and the proposed additional verification activities will be presented to the MSG payload program office for concurrence before verification activities are completed. 4.5 Requirement Sources 4.5.1 Investigation Requirements The investigation requirements for SAME are found in the EIRD for SAME. These, along with derived engineering responses to these requirements from the various engineering disciplines, can be found in the requirements matrix in this document. These engineering responses will be verified to show that the investigation requirements are met. The Project Scientist for SAME will approve all reports containing investigation verification close-outs.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p11,1,"4.5.2 Software Engineering Requirements Software engineering requirements can be found in SAME-DOC-004, Software Requirements Document, and are included in the requirements matrix in this document. These requirements include detailed responses to the investigation, safety, and MSG carrier requirements, and requirements to establish good engineering practices. 4.5.3 Product Assurance Requirements Product assurance requirements can be found in SAME-PLN-003, Product Assurance Plan. The requirements found in the Product Assurance Plan are derived from the Space Assurance Requirements and Guidelines (SARG) as they are applicable to the SAME project. Statements containing “shall” require a formal documented closure of the requirement, as defined in the Product Assurance Plan, and are included in the requirements matrix. Statements containing “will or must” are requirements to be fulfilled but do not require a formal documented closure, as defined in the Product Assurance Plan. The “will or must” requirements which have a direct impact on the SAME project budget, schedule, and design are included in the requirements matrix.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p11,2,"4.5.4 Safety Requirements SAME safety requirements are identified according to the payload safety process described in NSTS 1700.7B, Safety Policy and Requirements for Payloads Using the Space Transportation System, and NSTS 1700.7B ISS Addendum, Safety Policy and Requirements for Payloads Using the International Space Station, using NSTS/ISS 13830, Payload Safety Review and Data Submittal.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p12,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 7 The SAME safety requirements are found in the SAME hazard reports and are included in the requirements matrix in this document. The requirements are included in the matrix to provide visibility and to allow them to be grouped with other requirements in verification activities (i.e. test plans and procedures) for efficient completion. Although closure information will be included in the VTL, the safety requirements will be officially tracked in a Safety Verification Tracking Log (SVTL) and maintained by the SAME Safety Engineer. The SVTL and documentation used to close the safety verifications will be provided to the Payload Safety Review Panel (PSRP) for approval at the Phase III Safety Review. 4.5.5 MSG Carrier Requirements Requirements for interfacing with the MSG are found in MSFC-ICD-3462, Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) Interface Control Document for SAME, and are included in the requirements matrix in this document. MSFC-ICD-3462 contains a Verification Applicability Matrix to indicate what requirements from MSFC-RQMT-2888 are applicable to SAME. The Verification Applicability Matrix also identifies the appropriate verification method, the applicable VDS, and other comments associated with the completion of the verification. All information given in the Verification Applicability Matrix is duplicated in the requirements matrix in this document. The complete text of the requirements as well as the VDS is found in MSFC-RQMT-2888.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p12,1,"4.5.6 Space Shuttle and ISS Requirements MSG interfaces with Johnson Space Center (JSC) concerning payload interface requirements. Therefore, all Space Shuttle and ISS requirements should be documented in MSFC-ICD-3462, as identified in Section 4.5.4. 4.5.7 KSC Facility Requirements SAME is not planning to operate at Kennedy Space Center (KSC). 4.6 Staffing Implementation of the SAME verification process requires all members of the SAME team to support the verification activities. 4.6.1 Roles and Responsibilities The Systems Engineering team will prepare documentation necessary for the performance of verifications and the reporting of the results of verification activities. All plans and procedures for verification activities will be checked by a member of the Systems Engineering team to insure that the applicable requirements are included. One member of the Systems Engineering team will be responsible for updating the VTL to track the status of the verification activities and to include the document number for the reports generated to close the verifications. Hardware and Software Engineers will provide assistance writing test plans and procedures and performing verification activities. The Safety Engineer will track and maintain all safety verifications from the hazard reports in the SVTL. The Project Scientist will approve all documentation that completes an investigation requirement.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p12,2,Quality Assurance (QA) personnel will be involved if the requirement in question is a QA requirement.,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p13,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 8 Configuration Management will control documentation per SAME-PLN-002, Configuration Management Plan. 5.0 REQUIREMENTS MATRIX The requirements matrix listing the SAME requirements can be found in Appendix A of this document. When viewing the original electronic files of this document, the requirements matrix can be found in an Excel spreadsheet with the same name as this document. The hardcopy of this document and the .pdf version will include the requirements matrix at time of release in Appendix A. In addition to requirements to be verified, the matrix contains section titles and parent requirements that provide additional information to aid in the understanding of the verification activities. The items that are not verifiable requirements are designated in the matrix as indicated in Table 1. Table 1 is an explanation of the information contained in the requirements matrix. Table 1: Explanation of Verification Matrix Column Heading Explanation Requirement Number This column contains a unique identifying number that tracks the line item in the matrix. The requirements are numbered based on their source. Software requirements from SAME-DOC-004 that are derived as responses to other requirements are numbered according to the parent requirement.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p13,1,"000-099 – Investigation requirements from the EIRD 100-109 – Software requirements from SAME-DOC-004 110-199 – Product assurance requirements from SAME-PLN-003 200-299 – Safety requirements from the Standardized and Unique Hazard Reports 300-499 – Carrier requirements from MSFC-ICD-3462 Requirement Source This column lists the source of the requirement, where the text of the requirement can be found. There is no source listed for requirements derived from the investigation requirements. Their source is generally the Engineering Requirements Document, which is incorporated into this document. Discipline This is the discipline responsible for the completion of the requirement. Statements that are not verifiable requirements (i.e. section titles, descriptions, and parent requirements broken into verifiable sub-requirements) are indicated with “Title” in this column. Requirement This is the text of the requirement taken from the “Requirement Source.” In addition to requirements, this column includes the section titles and other information necessary for the completion of the requirement. System Response & Notes This column contains any notes necessary to explain the requirement. In the investigation requirements, this column further expands on the engineering response to the requirement. In the MSG carrier requirements, this column contains notes that were indicated in the ICD Verification Applicability Matrix.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p14,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 9 Column Heading Explanation Method This is the method required for verification. T = Test A = Analysis I = Inspection D = Demonstration NVR = No Verification Required (item is a title, description, or a parent requirement) PSRP = For MSG carrier requirements, this indicates that the verification is closed when the PSRP approves the corresponding safety verification. MSG requires no additional data be provided to them for closure. Series There is an “X” in this column if the requirement must be verified for serialized hardware. Safety There is an “X” in this column if the requirement came from one of the Hazard Reports, corresponds to a requirement from the Hazard Reports, or if it was indicated as a safety requirement in the ICD Verification Applicability Matrix. HFIT There is an “X” in this column if the requirement was indicated as an HFIT requirement in the ICD Verification Applicability Matrix. VDS # This column contains the Verification Definition Sheet (VDS) Number as indicated in the ICD Verification Applicability Matrix. The VDS contains information necessary to complete the verification and can be found in MSFC-RQMT-2888, Appendix E. Hardware F.M.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p14,1,Flt. An “X” in one of these columns indicates the classification of hardware that the final verification will be performed on. A hardware system will also be identified for each requirement marked here. Verification will be performed on the Functional Model or other flight-like hardware Verification will be performed on Flight Hardware System Full-up System Sub-System An “X” in one of these columns indicates the hardware system on which the verification activities will be performed. Verification will be performed on the complete SAME system. Verification will be performed on partial SAME system. GSE Required Certified Ground Support Equipment (GSE) is required to perform the verification. In MSG EU An “X” in this column indicates that the verification can be performed in the MSG EU. An “X*” in this column indicates that the verification must be performed in the MSG EU. Documents Flight Drawings Outside Source,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p14,2,"Documentation/ Procedures An “X” in these columns indicates documentation, rather than hardware, will be used to close the verification. Flight drawings will be used to perform the verification. Quality Assurance personnel will ensure that the hardware is built to the drawings. For software requirements, this indicates the code will be used to perform the verification. The documentation used will be provided from an outside source (e.g. NIST, MSG) Indicates documentation will be the source of information used to close the verification. This includes documents provided by SAME, outside documentation and crew procedures.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p15,0,SAME-PLN-005 Rev F A-0 APPENDIX A – VERIFICATION MATRIX (see SAME-PLN-005.xls),,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p16,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures 001 SAME EIRD 1 Title Smoke Generation Duct (Aging Chamber Inlet Elbow) NVR 001.01 SAME EIRD 1.a Title a) Configuration: Square or round duct with air flow through the duct. NVR 001.01.1 Mechanical Engineering The smoke generation duct shall be round with air flow through the duct. SAME Drawing #____________ I X 001.02 SAME EIRD 1.b Title b) Optical access: One view is required of the sample and the downstream flow. This view should be illuminated and imaged by a color video camera (visible light only). The orientation of the light relative to the imager should permit the visualization of the light scattered by the smoke. The duct has a port downstream of the sample, with a wide-angle camera mounted to view the sample and the first portion of the flow duct. Lighting will provide image visibility in the closed tube and has been demonstrated in 1-g with sample smoke. NVR 001.02.1 Mechanical Engineering 01. The duct shall have a port approximately 1 inch downstream of the sample. 02. Mounting shall be provided for the camera at the port. SAME Drawing #____________ See # 008.01.1 I X 001.02.2 Electrical Engineering Imaging Device Electrical 01. Power camera 02. Control camera 03. Record camera 04. Power LED's 01. Power to the camera is routed from the MSG Video + 12 VDC source. 02. Electronics will be provided to allow the DACU to control the camera. 03.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p16,1,"Video signal is routed to a transcoder, then to the MSG monitor and recorder. 04. Power is supplied to the LED's Reference SAME electrical schematic #__________________ See # 008.01.2 T XXX X 001.02.3 Software Engineering Provide software to control (if necessary) and record the images from the camera and the LED's See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.2.1.5, 4.4.5, and 5.3 NVR 001.02.3.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The DACU shall have the ability to control MSG video drawer functions by sending video command packets to the SPLC. The controllable video drawer functions are determined by the requirements of the video drawer command group, Section 4.4.5. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-5-1] See # 008.01.3.a TXXXX 001.02.3.b SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall accept a set of commands to perform MSG video drawer operations. The video drawer group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 6 of SAME-DOC-004. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-5-1-1] See # 008.01.3.b TXXXX 001.02.3.c SAME-DOC-004 5.3 Software Engineering The DACU shall control power to any combination of the camera lights. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-3-1-1-1] See # 008.01.3.c TXX 001.02.3.d SAME-DOC-004 5.3 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the gain and gamma settings of the camera. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-3-2-1-1] See # 008.01.3.d TXX 001.02.4 Diagnostics Imaging Device Specifications: 01. Allow for imaging of sample, heater wire, and smoke plume 02. Ability to verify no combustion beyond pyrolysis (LED's off) 03.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p16,2,"Verify sample type and placement 04. A ruled image in the field of view will be recorded with the flight system. A wide angle lens on a CCD color camera and illuminated by an LED in a side-scattering orientation provides a view of the centerline. See # 008.01.4 TXX 001.03 SAME EIRD 1.c Title c) Length: The straight section of the duct downstream of the sample shall be at least 30 cm long, and shall be known within 1 cm. NVR 001.03.1 Mechanical Engineering The straight section of the smoke generation duct length shall: 01. Be at least 30 cm long, length of duct to be provided from drawing 02. Fit within the confines of the MSG Working Volume Length will be maximized within MSG physical and operational constraints to meet requirements. See SAME Drawing #____________ A X 001.04 SAME EIRD 1.d Title d) Width: The test section width shall be based upon the sample size (see sample size below). The smallest duct dimension shall be twice the largest sample dimension. NVR Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required A-1",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p17,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 001.04.1 Mechanical Engineering The smoke generation duct width shall be at least twice as wide as the largest sample dimension. The sample size (see section 006) is not specified by science, rather it is determined by manufacturing requirements and 1-g testing. The requirement for double the width is easily met, and will be verified on the final design. See SAME Drawing #_________ for the layout and dimensions of the duct width I X 001.05 SAME EIRD 1.e Title e) Flow rate: 1 to 10 (+/- 0.5) cm/s average flow velocity. NVR 001.05.1 Mechanical Engineering The air flow will be provided by a pneumatically controlled piston system This system shall provide: 01. Control of the rate of piston movement 02. Fluid path and solenoids to route vacuum from MSG vacuum connection plus pressure from MSG GN2 03. Pressure transducers to monitor pressure differential 04. Mounting for mass flow controller, fluid path, and transducer The flow rate is determined by the speed of the piston in the Aging Chamber. The piston is moved pneumatically by a pressure differential driven by vacuum (aging chamber fill) or GN2 (aging chamber purge).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p17,1,"A software algorithm will determine and control the speed of the piston using data from a mass flow controller and a pressure transducer. System assembly and successful pyrolysis precludes inlet leaking. All ports must be sealed. Test procedure will include inspection for leaks. TX XX 001.05.2 Electrical Engineering Provide: 01. Power & control to piston system solenoids interfacing with vacuum and pressure. 02. Power & control to mass flow controller interfacing with vacuum and pressure. 03. Monitor output signal from mass flow controller interfacing with pressure (GN2) 04. Power to differential pressure transducer 05. Monitor output signal from differential pressure transducer See Electrical Schematic(s): SAME Drawing #____________ Power Summary Power to solenoid valves is 12 VDC Power to Differential XDCR is 28 Volts Power to MFC is 12 VDC Data and Control Summary 1 Analog output from MFC 1 Analog input to MFC 1 Analog output from Differential XDCR 8 discrete inputs to solenoids TX XX 001.05.3 Software Engineering Provide software to control: 01. Mass flow controller and solenoids Provide software to record data from: 02. Mass flow controller and differential pressure transducer output See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.4 NVR 001.05.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the aging chamber piston upward velocity to achieve 1 to 10 cm/s average flow velocity in the smoke generation duct.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p17,2,"[SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-1] T X X 001.05.3.b SAME-DOC-004 5.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the aging chamber piston upward movement by controlling a solenoid on the vacuum line, by sensing and controlling a mass flow controller on the vacuum line, and by sensing a pressure transducer monitoring the drive side pressure of the piston. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-2] See # 001.08.3.a TXX 001.06 SAME EIRD 1.f Title f) Transverse spatial flow variation: Induced maximum flow velocity (no sample present) shall be at the center of the duct with monotonic decrease to the walls. The ratio of the centerline flow velocity to average velocity shall be no greater than fully developed laminar flow +10%. NVR 001.06.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a round smoke generation duct and a positive displacement system. System design provides flow through sample generation section by creating pressure differential. Inlet screen on sample distributes flow over entire cross section. Simple analysis of laminar flow through a round duct will be used to verify. A X A-2",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p18,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 001.07 SAME EIRD 1.g Multiple g) Flow profiles well characterized (cold flow, no flow present) in ground based testing (hot wire anemometry adequate, test plan to be approved by project scientist). Ground based testing will characterize the flow profiles. This is not expected to change in μg. T & A X X X 001.07.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a test structure and instrumentation for characterizing cold flow spatial profiles in ground based testing. Flow profile will be measured using a hot wire anemometer at three cross sections of the duct located at 5mm increments along the duct. NVR 001.07.2 Electrical Engineering Provide power, data and control support for test structure and instrumentation to characterize flow profiles. Flow profile will be measured using a hot wire anemometer at three cross sections of the duct located at 5mm increments along the duct. NVR 001.07.3 Software Engineering Provide software to support test structure and instrumentation to characterize flow profiles. See SAME-DOC-004, section 8.0 NVR 001.07.3.a SAME-DOC-004 8.0 Software Engineering Software will be provided to support test structures and instrumentation to characterize flow profiles.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p18,1,[SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-1] Development Software NVR 001.08 SAME EIRD 1.h Title h) Temporal Flow variation: Less than +/- 10% variation of average velocity (measured with 1 Hz resolution). NVR 001.08.1 Mechanical Engineering Air flow will be provided by a pneumatically controlled piston system. The system shall contain a pressure transducer and a mass flow controller to determine the rate of piston movement. The design of the aging chamber and piston shall provide for smooth movement of the piston. Interior of aging chamber is a smooth aluminum surface and the piston is Rulon with Teflon o- rings that allow it to move smoothly in the chamber. The pressure transducer and mass flow controller provide information on the location of the piston in the chamber. Temporal flow variation will be verified from the MFC and pressure transducer readings. AXX 001.08.2 Electrical Engineering Power and control will be provided to a mass flow controller and a pressure transducer to determine and monitor the movement of the piston in the aging chamber. IXX 001.08.3 Software Engineering Provide software to control: 01. Mass flow controller and solenoids Provide software to record data from: 02.,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p18,2,"Mass flow controller and differential pressure transducer output See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.4 NVR 001.08.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the aging chamber piston upward movement by controlling a solenoid on the vacuum line, by sensing and controlling a mass flow controller on the vacuum line, and by sensing a pressure transducer monitoring the drive side pressure of the piston. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-2] See # 001.05.3.b TXX 001.09 SAME EIRD 1.i Multiple i) Flow linearity: for cold flow without sample present, flow stream lines (from the centerline to 3/4 of the distance to the wall) shall be linear (distance from wall shall not change more than 10% throughout the test sections). Can be validated by smoke tracers (1-g). Ground based testing will verify the flow linearity. This is not expected to change significantly in μg. The diameter variations at duct transitions shall be minimized. TXXX 001.09.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a test structure and instrumentation to validate flow stream lines The flow stream linearity will be characterized using video data provided by a camera located outside of the sample tube. A clear acrylic tube will be used to view the smoke tracers to show the streamlines in the Smoke Generation Duct.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p18,3,"NVR 001.09.2 Electrical Engineering Provide power, data and control support for test structure and instrumentation to validate flow stream lines The flow stream linearity will be characterized using video data provided by a camera located outside of the sample tube. A clear acrylic tube will be used to view the smoke tracers to show the streamlines in the Smoke Generation Duct. NVR 001.09.3 Software Engineering Provide software to support test structure and instrumentation to validate flow stream lines See SAME-DOC-004, section 8.0 NVR 001.09.3.a SAME-DOC-004 8.0 Software Engineering Software will be provided to support test structures and instrumentation to validate flow stream lines. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-2] Development Software NVR A-3",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p19,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 001.10 SAME EIRD 1.j Title j) Sample Location: Sample shall be located so its long dimension is oriented perpendicular to the flow and the sample shall be greater than 5 (10 preferred) times its small dimension from the duct entrance. Samples shall be centered on the duct axis +/- 2mm. NVR 001.10.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide: 01. Sample heater coil support with sample's long dimension perpendicular to the flow 02. Sample heater coil support 5-10 times the sample's small dimension away from the duct entrance 03. Sample heater coil support centered on the duct axis +/- 2mm 1, 02. SAME Drawing #____________ 03. Centerline requirement will be verified (inspection) on ground prior to packing for launch. IXXX 001.11 SAME EIRD 1.k Title k) Fan or flow generator: Either end of the duct is acceptable, duct outlet preferred. NVR 001.11.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide Mechanism for inducing flow through the smoke generation duct at the outlet end of the duct. Flow is induced by increasing the volume of the sample side of the Aging Chamber, drawing the smoke from the sample, through the Smoke Generation Duct, and into the Aging Chamber.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p19,1,"SAME Drawing #____________ I X 001.12 SAME EIRD 1.l Title l) Surface coating: Where feasible, objects in the camera field of view should be blackened and roughened to reduce stray reflections. Acceptance will be based on testing with flight-like unit. With the illumination on, it should be possible to discriminate punk smoke from the wall reflections. NVR 001.12.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide rough, black surface on the interior of the Smoke Generation Duct within field of view The duct will be aluminum, and the blackening of the visible area will be accomplished by roughened black anodizing or diffused black paint. I X 001.12.4 Diagnostics Verify that the punk smoke is visible in the Smoke Generation Duct Ground test will verify smoke visibility. T X X 001.13 SAME EIRD 1.m Title m) Particle loss by impaction, diffusion, thermophoresis, or electrostatic attraction shall be minimized and characterized by test and/or analysis in 1-g. (<5% desired) NVR 001.13.1 Mechanical Engineering 01. Design all smoke flow ducts such that: Use conductive surfaces (electrostatic) where possible Minimize interior surface area (fusion) Gradual bends/turns (impaction) Minimize thermal gradients (thermophoresis) Minimize obstructions (impaction) 02. Perform particle loss analysis 1. SAME Drawing #____________ 2. Analysis will be coordinated with science.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p19,2,"A X 002 SAME EIRD 2 Title Diagnostics Duct (Includes Aging Chamber Outlet Elbow, Sampling Duct Assembly, and Detector Duct Assembly) NVR 002.01 SAME EIRD 2.a Title a) Unswept volume minimized (< 5% desired) NVR 002.01.1 Mechanical Engineering Transitions must be gradual to minimize flow separations SAME Drawing #____________ Science will review the design to determine that unswept volume is minimized. IXXX 002.02 SAME EIRD 2.b Title b) Transit time minimized (<60 s desired) and unaffected by changes in the flow rate in the smoke generation duct. The transit time is a function of the number of diagnostics operating and the tube lengths and diameters. NVR 002.02.1 Mechanical Engineering Transit time shall be minimized through selection and routing of the tubing that delivers the sample to the diagnostics. A X A-4",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p20,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 002.03 SAME EIRD 2.c Title c) Smoke leaving the aging chamber shall be reliably sampled (< 10% error for each size range) by the diagnostics for the expected particle size ranges (0.02 to 1.0 µm, 1.0 to 2.5 µm, 2.5 to 6.0 µm) with total particle sampling error minimized and characterized by 1-g testing or analysis. NVR 002.03.1.a Mechanical Engineering Provide a ground test apparatus to test and characterize system Verification of this requirement will be testing or analysis performed by NIST. Inspection of their characterization will close the verification. IXXXXX 002.03.1.b Mechanical Engineering Provide an analysis to characterize the transit losses A X 002.04 SAME EIRD 2.d Multiple d) Temporal delay from the smoke source shall be well characterized. Required accuracy of temporal delay calibration: +/- 5 seconds in residence time for all duct flow rates. (Temporal delay includes transit time from the Smoke Aging Chamber to the diagnostics sampling point, transit time in the sampling tube and instrument response time) The temporal delay will be characterized using ground based testing to determine the time from the gate valve opening to the instrument response.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p20,1,"TXX 002.04.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a test structure and instrumentation for characterizing temporal delays in ground based testing NVR 002.04.2 Electrical Engineering Provide power, data and control support for test structure and instrumentation to characterize temporal delays NVR 002.04.3 Software Engineering Provide software to support test structure and instrumentation to characterize temporal delays See SAME-DOC-004, section 8.0 NVR 002.04.3.a SAME-DOC-004 8.0 Software Engineering Software will be provided to support test structures and instrumentation to characterize temporal delays of the smoke. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-4] Development Software NVR 002.05 SAME EIRD 2.e Title e) Volume sufficient to house the inlets to the diagnostics NVR 002.05.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide diagnostic ducting that is large enough to provide inlets to all diagnostic equipment listed in requirements 12 through 16 SAME Drawing #____________ I X 002.06 SAME EIRD 2.f Title f) Particle loss by impaction, diffusion, thermophoresis or electrostatic attraction shall be minimized and characterized by test and/or analysis in 1-g. (< 5% desired). NVR 002.06.1 Mechanical Engineering 01. Design all smoke flow ducts such that: Use conductive surfaces (electrostatic) where possible Minimize interior surface area (fusion) Gradual bends/turns (impaction) Minimize thermal gradients (thermophoresis) Minimize obstructions (impaction) 02. Perform particle loss analysis 1. SAME Drawing #____________ 2.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p20,2,"Analysis will be coordinated with science. A X 002.07 SAME EIRD 2.g Title g) Dilution air can be added as long as the smoke concentration is maintained in the operating range of the diagnostics. If dilution is used, either the dilution level must be known +/- 10% or the concentration entering the Smoke Aging Chamber must be known. NVR 002.07.1 Mechanical Engineering The positive displacement system of the aging chamber will allow for concentration of smoke to be known. A X 002.08 SAME EIRD 2.h Title h) The concentration seen by each of the diagnostics must be the same or at a known dilution level (consistent with achieving required instrument accuracy). Current design should support this requirement. NVR A-5",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p21,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 002.08.1 Mechanical Engineering Sample inlet locations for all diagnostic instruments will be on the same length of duct. Dilution will be provided to deliver smoke concentrations in the operating ranges of the diagnostics including: 01. Smoke and GN2 to dynamic diluter 02. Laminar flow element and voltage sensitive orifices to the smoke and GN2 inlets 03. Control and measurement instrumentation 04. Smoke and GN2 to static diluter Dynamic diluter features include: 01. Active dilution, constant draw from diagnostics duct while active with a dilution ratio variable from 100:1 to 10:1 02. Smoke volume accurately measured through a laminar flow element 03. Control loop provides self-adjusting flow to compensate for changes in the system Static diluter features include: 04. Static dilution ratio on the order of 4:1 that can be turned on or off T & I X X X 002.08.2 Electrical Engineering 01. Power for the Voltage Sensitive Orifice(s) (VSO) and the control/measurement instrumentation 02. Analog inputs/outputs for the VSO control and measurement instrumentation (including feedback from the Laminar Flow Element) 03.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p21,1,"Discrete outputs for the VSO control and measurement instrumentation TX X X 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p21,2,"See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR A-6",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p22,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p22,1,"See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p22,2,"See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR A-7",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p23,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p23,1,"See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 004.01.2 Electrical Engineering The aging chamber must have electronics to age the smoke to the specified time and then initiate the aging chamber evacuation into the diagnostics duct. The evacuation of the aging chamber is controlled by the MFC connecting to the GN2 fluid path. TX XX 004.01.3 Software Engineering Software must be in place to accept the timing parameter, and execute the aging/evacuation process. The residence time is an uplink parameter, programmable from 0 to 100 (or greater) minutes. After the appropriate time has elapsed, the software will issue a command to open the solenoid path to the GN2 and dispense the contents of the aging chamber. See SAME-DOC-004, section 4.5.1. NVR 004.01.3.a SAME-DOC-004 4.5 Software Engineering Residence time of smoke in the aging chamber shall be controllable from 0 to 100 (or greater) minutes per aging period.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p23,2,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-1-1-1] T X X 004.02 SAME EIRD 4.b Title b) Displaceable volume sufficient to provide enough sample gas to the diagnostics (including the thermal precipitator) to achieve a steady signal on all the diagnostic systems (based on the sample size and temporal resolution requirements of the diagnostics). NVR 004.02.1 Mechanical Engineering Aging chamber shall provide sufficient sample to achieve a minimum of 30 seconds of stable response with all diagnostics operating. See SAME Drawing ________________ A X 004.02.2 Electrical Engineering Electronic timing control must be provided to the diagnostics for the samples to be taken at the appropriate time such that the requirement is met. The diagnostics will respond to software commanding. TX XX 004.02.3 Software Engineering Software timing control must be provided to the diagnostics for the samples to be taken at the appropriate time such that the requirement is met. Software will command the diagnostics to take samples once the diagnostic duct has reached steady state. See SAME-DOC-004, section 4.5.1. NVR 004.02.3.a SAME-DOC-004 4.5 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the timing of diagnostic measurements such that a steady state signal is achieved on all instruments. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-1-1-3] T X X A-8",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p24,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 004.03 SAME EIRD 4.c Title c) Chamber design produces conditions where the rate of change of the number concentrations resulting from wall loss is less than 10% of the rate of change of the particle concentration from coagulation (in normal gravity for 1μm particles). Suggested approaches: minimize surface to volume, fabricate wall materials with conductive materials to reduce electrostatic attraction. Notes: Wall loss less than or equal to 10% of coagulation (curve vs. particle size). There are predictions in EIRD (Table 3-2). Suggested test: known concentration, age some known time, measure w/P-Trak and DustTrak. NVR 004.03.1 Mechanical Engineering Chamber must be designed to have conductive walls with minimized wall surfaces. See SAME Drawing _______________ I X 004.04 SAME EIRD 4.d Title d) The ability to store enough gas for multiple aging durations is strongly desired. NVR 004.04.1 Mechanical Engineering The aging chamber is nearly at the maximum volume that can be inserted into and accommodated within the MSG Working Volume. Therefore, the diagnostics duct must: 01. Be sized such that multiple samples from a single aging chamber fill will yield acceptable results. 02.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p24,1,"Have provisions for purging the system between the samples taken with different aging durations When multiple aging durations from a single aging chamber fill is desired, the STS smoke detector will not be used. The sample size required for the STS smoke detector is too large (5 L/min) to accommodate multiple agings. TXX 004.04.2 Electrical Engineering The electronics must: 01. Control multiple samples from a single aging chamber fill 02. Control purging between the samples taken with different aging durations Controls will be provided that will allow for multiple agings in the aging chamber and allow for purging of the diagnostics between samples. TX XX 004.04.3 Software Engineering The Software must: 01. Control multiple samples from a single aging chamber fill 02. Control purging between the samples taken with different aging durations Software will control the aging chamber to allow for multiple samples and will purge the diagnostics between samples. NVR 004.04.3.a SAME-DOC-004 4.5 Software Engineering The DACU shall allow the option of multiple smoke samples from the aging chamber with separate aging times. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-1-1-4] T X X 004.04.3.b SAME-DOC-004 4.5 Software Engineering The DACU shall purge the diagnostic duct between multiple smoke samples from the aging chamber. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-1-1-5] T X X 004.05 SAME EIRD 4.e Title e) The smoke in the Aging Chamber must be fully mixed within 10% of the residence time.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p24,2,"NVR 004.05.1 Mechanical Engineering The systems within the sample heating, smoke generation duct, and aging chamber must provide fully mixed smoke within 5 seconds (for the shortest residence time). Provide active (air flow) and passive (flow induced) mixing options. The mixing method is flow driven and additionally mixed with a fan. Air is drawn into the aging chamber across the smoking sample, drawing and mixing the smoke as it flows into the chamber. In microgravity, there is no mechanism for settling once the smoke is mixed. Ground testing will characterize in 1g. TXXX 004.05.2 Electrical Engineering Provide power and control to the mixing fan. See SAME Electrical Schematic #_____ T X X X 004.05.3 Software Engineering Provide software control to the mixing fan. See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.4 NVR 004.05.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall control a mixing fan in the aging chamber to be on during a specified portion of the residence time. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-5] T X X 005 SAME EIRD 5 Title Sample Heater (Heater Wire Coil) NVR 005.01 SAME EIRD 5.a Title a) Size: sample size and heater design will be established to achieve the required mass loss rate by controlling the sample surface temperature and geometry. NVR 005.01.1 Mechanical Engineering The sample will be sized to provide the required mass loss while meeting the length, width and position constraints levied elsewhere.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p24,3,"The heater is a resistance wire, wrapped in a spiral around the cylindrical sample. TXX 005.01.2 Electrical Engineering The electronics must: 01. Control the heater surface temperature 02. Control the duration of the burn A resistance heater is used, with the voltage programmed and the current measured for an accurate indication of heater temperature, based on ground testing. T X XXX A-9",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p25,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 005.01.3 Software Engineering The software must: 01. Control the heater surface temperature 02. Control the duration of the burn The software will cycle between a heating cycle and a voltage (current) measurement cycle, and adjust the voltage applied to the heater during the next heating cycle based on the voltage measurement. See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.2. NVR 005.01.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.2 Software Engineering The DACU in combination with the heater microcontroller shall control the sample surface temperature and duration of the heating. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-1] T X X X 005.02 SAME EIRD 5.b Title b) Heating rate: achieve target wire temperature (within required accuracy) in 5 seconds. NVR 005.02.1 Mechanical Engineering 01. The sample heater must be made with materials capable of reaching the target temperature within 5 seconds. 02. There must be ground test hardware to characterize the sample heater rate. Ground based testing will characterize temperature rise of heater. TXX 005.02.2 Electrical Engineering 01. The control circuitry must have the temporal resolution and timing capability to reach steady state temperature within 5 seconds. 02.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p25,1,"There must be electrical test hardware to characterize the sample heater rate. Ground based testing will characterize temperature rise of heater. TX X X 005.02.3 Software Engineering 01. The software must have the timing capability to control the heater to reach steady state temperature within 5 seconds. 02. There must be software to characterize the sample heater rate. Ground based testing will characterize temperature rise of heater. See SAME-DOC- 004, section 5.2. NVR 005.02.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.2 Software Engineering The DACU in combination with the heater microcontroller shall achieve steady state temperature of the heater within 5 seconds. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-2] T X X 005.02.3.b SAME-DOC-004 8.0 Software Engineering Software will be provided to support test structures and instrumentation to characterize the sample heater rate. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-5] Development Software NVR 005.03 SAME EIRD 5.c Title c) Temperature range, current estimate: 50-500 C. Actual values to be determined based upon the sample mass loss rate. NVR 005.03.1 Mechanical Engineering The heater element must operate over the temperature range of 50-500°C. Also see section 5.d The heater is stainless steel wire, which can operate over the temperature range of 50-500 oC. TXX 005.03.2 Electrical Engineering The electronics must be able to control the power to the heater element so that it will achieve the temperature range of 50- 500°C.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p25,2,The voltage output to the heater wire will have sufficient range and resolution to meet this requirement. A resistance vs. temperature calibration for stainless steel has been performed. AXX 005.03.3 Software Engineering The software must be able to control the power to the heater element so that it will achieve the temperature range of 50- 500°C. The software will provide software algorithm to convert voltage measured --> resistance --> temperature necessary for this control. See SAME-DOC-004 sections 4.5.2 and 5.2. NVR 005.03.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.2 Software Engineering The DACU in combination with the heater microcontroller shall set the temperature of the heater wire coil to any temperature between 50 and 500 degrees Celsius. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-3] T X X 005.04 SAME EIRD 5.d Multiple d) Temperature accuracy: reproducibility and accuracy of the temperature shall be such that the mass loss repeatability (item 005.06 below) is achieved. Wire physical characteristics plus sample attachments will dominate accuracy variations. Stainless steel wire will be used and techniques will be developed for sample attachment repeatability. A resistance vs. temperature calibration for stainless steel has been performed. TXXXX 005.04.1 Mechanical Engineering Test hardware will be provided to test and characterize the accuracy of the heater temperature. NVR 005.04.2 Electrical Engineering Test electronics will be provided to test and characterize the accuracy of the heater temperature.,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p25,3,"NVR 005.04.3 Software Engineering Test software will be provided to test and characterize the accuracy of the heater temperature. See SAME-DOC-004, section 8.0 NVR A-10",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p26,0,SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 005.04.3.a SAME-DOC-004 8.0 Software Engineering Software will be provided to test and characterize the accuracy of the heater temperature. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-6] Development Software NVR 005.05 SAME EIRD 5.e Title e) Heater design shall provide active temperature control to adapt to low-gravity environment NVR 005.05.1 Mechanical Engineering Heater must maintain contact with sample throughout its length along the sample. IXX 005.05.2 Electrical Engineering Electronics must provide feedback in a closed loop type of system so that the temperature of the heater can be monitored on-orbit. Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 The current sense circuit will provide active feedback of the calculated heater temperature. I X 005.05.3 Software Engineering Software must provide algorithm to evaluate and control the voltage (power) to the heater based on the measurements taken on-orbit. The software will provide software algorithm to convert voltage measured --> resistance --> temperature necessary for this control. See SAME-DOC-004 section 5.2. NVR 005.05.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.2 Software Engineering The DACU in combination with the heater microcontroller shall provide active temperature control of the heater.,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p26,1,"[SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-4] T X X 005.06 SAME EIRD 5.f Title f) Sample mass loss rate: Without igniting the samples, the samples shall produce sufficient mass, at the specified rate (see test matrix) to fill the aging chamber with the target mass concentration. This shall be demonstrated by ground based testing with the 95% confidence interval within +/- 50% of the target mass loss rate. The target concentration in the aging chamber shall be selected so that it is in the operating range of the diagnostic systems. Ground based testing will demonstrate that the samples produce sufficient mass to fill the Aging Chamber with the target mass concentration. NVR 005.06.1 Mechanical Engineering The Mechanical heater system must: 01. Not ignite the sample 02. Provide support for samples the correct size, as determined by ground testing 03. Have a test rig available for characterization of the mass loss rate Ground based testing will demonstrate that the samples produce sufficient mass to fill the Aging Chamber with the target mass concentration. TXX 005.06.2 Electrical Engineering The Electrical heater system must: 01. Not ignite the sample 02. Provide the correct power for the correct duration, as determined by ground testing 03. Have a test rig available for characterization of the mass loss rate The electrical heater temperature will be controlled so as not to ignite the sample, and a ground test rig will be supported electrically.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p26,2,"TX X X 005.06.3 Software Engineering The Software for the heater system must: 01. Control the power and the duration, as determined by ground testing 03. Have software to support a test rig for characterization of the mass loss rate The software will control the electrical heater temperature so as not to ignite the sample, and a ground test rig will be supported with software. See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.2. NVR 005.06.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.2 Software Engineering The DACU in combination with the heater microcontroller shall control the heater temperature and duration so as not to ignite the sample. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-5] T X X 005.06.3.b SAME-DOC-004 8.0 Software Engineering Software will be provided to support a test rig for characterization of mass loss rate for the sample heater system. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-7] Development Software NVR 006 SAME EIRD 6 Title Experimental Operating Conditions/Test samples NVR 006.01 SAME EIRD 6.a Title a) Spacecraft Material Samples (Teflon™ wire insulation material, Kapton™, silicone rubber, cellulose). NVR 006.01.1 Mechanical Engineering Test Samples of the specified materials must be procured. Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 Samples are as specified. IXX A-11",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p27,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 006.02 SAME EIRD 6.b Title b) Samples shall be small pieces of these materials selected to be representative of materials used in spacecraft. Sample geometry is unimportant other than through its interaction with the sample heater (based on testing in 1-g). NVR 006.02.1 Mechanical Engineering Test Samples of the specified materials must be procured. Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 Samples primarily will be rod shaped, with heater coiled along the length. See SAME Drawing ______________ IXX 006.03 SAME EIRD 6.c Title c) Reference sample design: Porous wick material supporting dibutyl phthalate (DBP). This liquid shall be vaporized by the sample heater producing an aerosol with predictable thermo physical properties. NVR 006.03.1 Mechanical Engineering Test Samples of the specified materials must be procured. Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 At least one sample will be DBP. IXX 006.04 SAME EIRD 6.d Title d) Mass loss for all samples to be established by determining the mass change in the sample. Accuracy: 0.0001 g. This will require reasonably expeditious sample return. NVR 006.04.1.a Mechanical Engineering The heater system must: 01. Provide for the removal and/or storage of the intact sample after heating. 02.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p27,1,"Provide for the intact transportation of the sample back to Earth IX X 006.04.1.b Mechanical Engineering Provide test procedure for post-flight sample removal and weighing Post flight ground based verification will measure μg mass loss. TXXX 006.04.5 Glovebox Expeditious return to Earth and to the scientists at GRC Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 This will be negotiated, and documented in the ICD. I XX 006.05 SAME EIRD 6.e Title e) Number of tests, 4 per sample required with an additional 4 tests strongly desired for repeats and parameter variation. (see test matrix in SAME EIRD). NVR 006.05.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide the appropriate number of sample carousels to accommodate the required number of tests Sample Carousels, data storage, samples, consumables, etc IXXX 006.05.5 Glovebox Provide documentation and negotiate resources to accommodate the required number of tests. This will be documented in the ICD (timeline, power, crew time, stowage, consumables). I XX 007 SAME EIRD 7 Title Microgravity Requirements NVR 007.01 SAME EIRD 7.a Title a) Less than 10-3 g at frequencies less than 30 Hz during sample heating interval, less than 10-2 at frequencies less than 30 Hz during smoke aging and diagnostics operation. This gravity requirement is not stringent enough to require ""quiet time"" nor on-orbit verification. NVR 007.01.5 Glovebox Glovebox should notify the crew to subdue high-g activities (such as bicycle) during the burn.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p27,2,"Restrictions on ISS operations and crew activities should be documented in the ICD. I XX 008 SAME EIRD 8 Title Video Monitoring Measurements NVR 008.01 SAME EIRD 8.a Title a) 1 view required (2nd orthogonal view desired) of the test section during the smoke generation interval. The duct has one port downstream of the sample. NVR 008.01.1 Mechanical Engineering 01. The duct shall have a port approximately 1 inch downstream of the sample. 02. Mounting shall be provided for the camera at the port. See # 001.02.1 I X 008.01.2 Electrical Engineering Imaging Device Electrical 01. Power camera 02. Control camera 03. Record camera 04. Power LED's See # 001.02.2 T X X X X X 008.01.3 Software Engineering Provide software to control (if necessary) and record the images from the camera and the LED's See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.2.1.5, 4.4.5, and 5.3 NVR A-12",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p28,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 008.01.3.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The DACU shall have the ability to control MSG video drawer functions by sending video command packets to the SPLC. The controllable video drawer functions are determined by the requirements of the video drawer command group, Section 4.4.5. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-5-1] See # 001.02.3.a TXXXX 008.01.3.b SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall accept a set of commands to perform MSG video drawer operations. The video drawer group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 6 of SAME-DOC-004. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-5-1-1] See # 001.02.3.b TXXXX 008.01.3.c SAME-DOC-004 5.3 Software Engineering The DACU shall control power to any combination of the camera lights. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-3-1-1-1] See # 001.02.3.c TXX 008.01.3.d SAME-DOC-004 5.3 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the gain and gamma settings of the camera. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-3-2-1-1] See # 001.02.3.d TXX 008.01.4 Diagnostics Imaging Device Specifications: 01. Allow for imaging of sample, heater wire, and smoke plume 02. Ability to verify no combustion beyond pyrolysis (LED's off) 03. Verify sample type and placement 04.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p28,1,"A ruled image in the field of view will be recorded with the flight system. See # 001.02.4 T X X 008.02 SAME EIRD 8.b Title b) 30 Hz recording NVR 008.02.4 Diagnostics The video system shall record at 30 Hz or faster. The frame rate of the camera is 30 fps. I X X 008.02.5 Glovebox The video shall interface with Glovebox Video recording and/or downlinking at a rate of 30 Hz minimum. TXXX 008.03 SAME EIRD 8.c Title c) Standard composite color video (equivalent to 3 color at 8 bits/color or better), able to image light scattered from smoke, compression acceptable with testing. NVR 008.03.4 Diagnostics The video system shall 01. Record with standard composite video equivalent to 3 colors at 8 bits (or better) 02. Image the light scattered from smoke The color camera supports this requirement. T X X 008.03.5 Glovebox The video interface shall: A. Digitize to 3 colors at 8 bits (preferred) OR B. Store and downlink analog with sufficient S/N to achieve 3 colors at 8 bits resolution (acceptable) Should be documented in the ICD. T & I X X X X X 008.04 SAME EIRD 8.d Title d) Field of view at duct midplane: i) Transverse: sample width plus 20% ii) Axial: starting at upstream side of sample projecting 3 cm downstream required, more desired NVR 008.04.1 Mechanical Engineering The port and camera mounting hardware shall allow for: 01. Transverse view of sample width +20% 02.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p28,2,"Axial minimum view of entire sample + 3 cm downstream The port/camera mounting hardware design will meet this requirement. Test will verify. TXX 008.04.4 Diagnostics The camera shall allow for imaging of: 01. Transverse view of sample width +20% 02. Axial minimum view of entire sample + 3 cm downstream The camera has a wide-angle lens mounted to view the sample and the first portion of the flow duct. TXX 008.05 SAME EIRD 8.e Title e) Resolution: 0.5 mm NVR 008.05.4 Diagnostics The video system shall provide an object resolution of 0.5 mm. The wide-angle characteristic of the lens may result in predictable non-linearity, but should support this requirement. TXX 008.06 SAME EIRD 8.f Title f) Depth of Field: depth of smoke plume. NVR 008.06.4 Diagnostics The camera must provide a depth of field at least equal to the smoke plume. The depth of the smoke plume is estimated to be the diameter of the sample/heater wire assembly. The current camera and mounting design should support this requirement. The estimate of the smoke plume depth is based on laminar flow and the camera view being close to the sample. TXX 009 SAME EIRD 9 Title Sample temperature measurement: NVR A-13",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p29,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 009.01 SAME EIRD 9.a Title a) Sampling Rate: 1 Hz NVR 009.01.3 Software Engineering The software must sample the temperature input at least once per second. The software will be designed to meet or exceed this requirement. See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.2. NVR 009.01.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.2 Software Engineering The DACU shall measure and store the sample temperature at least once per second. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-6] See # 009.02.3.a and # 009.03.3.a TXX 009.02 SAME EIRD 9.b Title b) Accuracy and precision: +/- 5 % of reading (Celsius temperature). NVR 009.02.2 Electrical Engineering The end to end accuracy must be calculated, and be no more than +/-5% of the reading. The sensor, conditioning, and A/D converter accuracies will be calculated for end-to-end accuracy. AXX 009.02.3 Software Engineering The software must contain calibration coefficients for the thermal sensor if these are supplied by the vendor, and use them to convert the signals to engineering units. The calibration coefficients will be provided to software, and software will incorporate them into the conversion algorithm. See SAME-DOC- 004, section 5.2.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p29,1,"NVR 009.02.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.2 Software Engineering The DACU shall measure and store the sample temperature at least once per second. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-6] See # 009.01.3.a and # 009.03.3.a TXX 009.03 SAME EIRD 9.c Title c) Location: Provide a temperature representative of the reacting region of the sample near the heater during sample heating. NVR 009.03.2 Electrical Engineering Provide electrical circuitry to measure the resistance of the heating element The temperature of the heater will be calculated by using the current sensing circuit (voltage measured across the resistor). Heater wire resistance is linear with temperature. TX XX 009.03.3 Software Engineering Provide software algorithm to convert voltage measured --> resistance --> temperature The software will provide software algorithm to convert voltage measured --> resistance --> temperature necessary for this control. See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.2. NVR 009.03.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.2 Software Engineering The DACU shall measure and store the sample temperature at least once per second. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-6] See # 009.01.3.a and # 009.02.3.a TXX 010 SAME EIRD 10 Title Gas Plume Temperature measurement NVR 010.01 SAME EIRD 10.a Title a) Sampling Rate: 1 Hz NVR 010.01.3 Software Engineering The software must sample the temperature input at least once per second. The software will be designed to meet or exceed this requirement. See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.2.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p29,2,"NVR 010.01.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.2 Software Engineering The DACU shall measure and store the gas plume temperature at least once per second. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-7] See # 010.02.3.a TXX 010.02 SAME EIRD 10.b Title b) Accuracy: +/- 5 % of reading NVR 010.02.2 Electrical Engineering The end to end accuracy must be calculated, and be no more than +/-5% of the reading. The flight sensor will be tested against a calibrated standard to determine its accuracy. The sensor, conditioning, and A/D converter accuracies will be calculated for end-to-end accuracy. See # 011.02.2.c T & A X X X 010.02.3 Software Engineering The software must contain calibration coefficients for the thermal sensor if these are supplied by the vendor, and use them to convert the signals to engineering units. The calibration coefficients will be provided to software, and software will incorporate them into the conversion algorithm. See SAME- DOC-004, section 5.2. NVR 010.02.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.2 Software Engineering The DACU shall measure and store the gas plume temperature at least once per second. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-7] See # 010.01.3.a TXX 010.03 SAME EIRD 10.c Title c) Location: in the center of the duct 1 to 3 cm from the sample NVR 010.03.1 Mechanical Engineering The sensor must be mounted in the duct, 1 to 3 cm from the sample. Due to location in the field of view, sensor must be black in color. Thermocouple probe is located in the duct.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p29,3,"See SAME Drawing _______________ IXXX 010.04 SAME EIRD 10.d Title d) Probe wire diameter: as small as possible, less than 0.010"" diameter NVR 010.04.1 Mechanical Engineering The temperature sensor probe must meet all other requirements and be less than 0.010"" diameter. The sensor has a diameter of 0.011"" and will not meet the size requirement. IXX A-14",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p30,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 011 SAME EIRD 11 Title Air flow speed in the duct (average), ambient humidity, pressure and temperature measurement NVR 011.01 SAME EIRD 11.a Title a) 0.1 Hz sampling rate NVR 011.01.3 Software Engineering Software shall be in place to record data from the sensors used to measure the air flow speed in the duct and ambient conditions at a sampling rate of at least 0.1 Hz. The air flow speed in the duct will be calculated from MFC settings and pressure transducer data used in the piston movement. The pressure transducer monitoring the diagnostics plenum will be used to measure ambient pressure. The gas plume temperature sensor will be used to measure the ambient temperature. NVR 011.01.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.12 Software Engineering The DACU shall record the MFC settings and the pressure transducer data at the rate of at least 0.1 Hz to be used in the calculation of the air flow speed. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-12-1-1-2] Data will be sampled at 1 Hz. TXX 011.01.3.b SAME-DOC-004 5.12 Software Engineering The DACU shall measure and store the ambient air pressure of the work volume at a rate of at least 0.1 Hz.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p30,1,"[SAME-SW-REQ-5-12-1-1-4] See # 011.02.3.b Data will be sampled at 1 Hz and will be measured before each test point for 30 seconds prior to sample heating. TXX 011.01.3.c SAME-DOC-004 5.12 Software Engineering The DACU shall measure and store the ambient air temperature of the work volume at a rate of at least 0.1 Hz. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-12-1-1-5] See # 003.04.3.a and # 011.02.3.c Data will be sampled at 1 Hz and will be measured before each test point for 30 seconds prior to sample heating. TXX 011.01.5 Glovebox Glovebox must supply humidity data at a sampling rate of at least 0.1 Hz. MSG resource, will be documented in the ICD. Data is available in the MSG Health and Status data, which is updated once per second. I XX 011.02 SAME EIRD 11.b Title b) Accuracy +/- 10% of reading NVR 011.02.2.a Electrical Engineering The end to end accuracy of the air flow speed system must be calculated and not be more than +/- 10% of the reading. The accuracies for the components in the air flow speed system will be calculated for end-to- end accuracy. AXX 011.02.2.b Electrical Engineering The end to end accuracy of the pressure transducer must be calculated, and be no more than +/- 10% of the reading. The accuracy of the pressure transducer that monitors the pressure in the diagnostics plenum will be calculated for end-to-end accuracy. AXX 011.02.2.c Electrical Engineering The end to end accuracy must be calculated, and be no more than +/-5% of the reading.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p30,2,"The flight sensor will be tested against a calibrated standard to determine its accuracy. Applicable to the gas plume temperature sensor. See # 010.02.2 T & A X X X 011.02.3 Software Engineering Software shall be in place to record data from the sensors used to measure the air flow speed in the duct and ambient conditions with an accuracy of at least +/- 10% of the reading. The air flow speed in the duct will be calculated from MFC settings and pressure transducer data used in the piston movement. The pressure transducer monitoring the diagnostics plenum will be used to measure ambient pressure. The gas plume temperature sensor will be used to measure the ambient temperature. NVR 011.02.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.12 Software Engineering The DACU shall record the air flow speed in the smoke generation duct. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-12-1-1-3] T X X 011.02.3.b SAME-DOC-004 5.12 Software Engineering The DACU shall measure and store the ambient air pressure of the work volume at a rate of at least 0.1 Hz. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-12-1-1-4] See # 011.01.3.b TXX 011.02.3.c SAME-DOC-004 5.12 Software Engineering The DACU shall measure and store the ambient air temperature of the work volume at a rate of at least 0.1 Hz. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-12-1-1-5] See # 003.04.3.a and # 011.01.3.c TXX 011.02.5 Glovebox Glovebox must supply humidity data with an accuracy of +/- 10% of the reading. MSG resource, will be documented in the ICD. Data is available in the MSG Health and Status data.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p30,3,"The tolerance of the sensor is +/- 10%, which does not meet the requirement. I XX 011.03 SAME EIRD 11.c Title c) Location: representative of the duct inlet conditions NVR A-15",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p31,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 011.03.1.a Mechanical Engineering The air flow speed in the duct will be calculated from the data used to control the piston movement in the aging chamber. The movement of the piston in the aging chamber determines the air flow speed in the smoke generation duct so that by knowing the speed of the piston, the air flow speed in the duct can be calculated. TXX 011.03.1.b Mechanical Engineering The pressure transducer monitoring the pressure in the diagnostics plenum shall be used to measure the ambient pressure in the MSG work volume. TXX 011.03.1.c Mechanical Engineering The SAME gas plume temperature sensor, located in the smoke generation duct, shall be used to measure the ambient temperature. TXX 011.03.5 Glovebox The ambient humidity measurement must be taken in the work volume of the Glovebox. MSG resource, will be documented in the ICD. Data is available in the MSG Health and Status data. Sensor is located on the back wall of the MSG work volume. I XX 011.04 SAME EIRD 11.d Title d) Range:",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p31,1,"Air flow 0-15 cm/s, humidity 10 to 90% RH, pressure 0.08 to 0.13 MPa, temperature 15 to 35 C NVR 011.04.1 Mechanical Engineering The pressure transducer used to measure ambient pressure in the work volume shall measure the pressure range of 0.08 MPa to 0.13 MPa. IXXX 011.04.2 Electrical Engineering The gas plume temperature sensor used to measure ambient temperature in the work volume shall measure the temperature range of 15°C to 35°C. IXXX 011.04.3 Software Engineering Software will use the data provided to control the movement of the piston in the aging chamber to determine the air flow speed in the duct. NVR 011.04.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.12 Software Engineering The DACU shall record the air flow speed in the smoke generation duct with a range of at least 0 to 15 cm/s using calibration coefficients as necessary. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-12-1-1-1] T X X 011.04.5 Glovebox Glovebox shall measure ambient conditions from 10% to 90% relative humidity in the work volume. MSG resource, will be documented in the ICD. Sensor data is available in the MSG Health and Status data. Measurement range of MSG sensor is 20% to 80% RH, which does not meet the requirement. I XX 012 SAME EIRD 12 Title Experimental Diagnostics Measurements - Number concentration measurement system (zeroth moment) The Number Concentration measurement is the first of the three moment measurements required by science. It measures the number of smoke particles/unit volume. NVR 012.01 SAME EIRD 12.a Title a) Location:",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p31,2,"in diagnostics duct NVR 012.01.1 Mechanical Engineering The Number Concentration system must extract a sample from the Sampling Duct Assembly. The sample is routed by flexible tube from the sampling duct to the Number Concentration system. See SAME Drawing #________________ IXX 012.02 SAME EIRD 12.b Title b) Expected levels at detector location: (104 to 3 × 107) part/cm3 (actual values will be based upon the suite of instruments, calibrated dilution is acceptable) NVR 012.02.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a system to dilute the smoke to the input range of the P- Trak. Dilution on the order of 60:1 required. Verification of this requirement will be testing performed by NIST. Inspection of their characterization will close the verification. IXXXXX 012.02.2 Electrical Engineering Provide electrical circuitry to support the dilution system Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 I X 012.02.3 Software Engineering Provide software to support the dilution system See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.11 NVR 012.02.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.11 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the dilution of smoke using analog input(s) and output(s) and discrete output(s) to a voltage sensitive orifice and measurement instrumentation. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-11-1-1-1] See # 002.08.3.b TXX 012.03 SAME EIRD 12.c Title c) Repeatability ± 4 % (95% confidence), accuracy ± 10% (95% confidence) NVR A-16",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p32,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 012.03.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a ground test apparatus to measure and characterize repeatability Verification of this requirement will be testing performed by NIST. Inspection of their characterization will close the verification. IXXXXX 012.04 SAME EIRD 12.d Title d) Temporal resolution: 5 s NVR 012.04.2 Electrical Engineering The electrical characteristics for the number concentration system must have a response time which allows for a 5 second temporal resolution. This requirement will be verified by inspection of the instrument specification. IX X X 012.05 SAME EIRD 12.e Title e) Sampling rate 0.2 Hz NVR 012.05.3 Software Engineering The software must sample the number concentration system at least once every 5 seconds. P-Trak can meet this requirement. See SAME- DOC-004, section 5.7. NVR 012.05.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.7 Software Engineering The DACU shall sample P-Trak data at a minimum rate of 0.2 Hz. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-7-2-1-2] Data will be sampled at 1 Hz. TXX 012.06 SAME EIRD 12.f Title f) Particle size sensitivity: 0.05 µm to 1 µm NVR 012.06.1 Mechanical Engineering Number concentration system must meet this particle size sensitivity with some margin. P-Trak is unreliable above 1 µm.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p32,1,"Verification will be analysis performed by NIST. Inspection of their report will close the verification. IX X XX XX 012.07 SAME EIRD 12.g Title g) System calibrated on the ground for monodisperse aerosols over expected range. NVR 012.07.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a ground test apparatus to test and characterize system Verification of this requirement will be testing performed by NIST. Inspection of their characterization will close the verification. IXXXXX 012.08 SAME EIRD 12.h Title h) Suggested approach: TSI P-Trak™, dilution will be required for smaller particles due to increased number count. NVR 012.08.1 Mechanical Engineering Supply a number concentration measurement system equal to or better than the TSI P-Trak™ A TSI P-Trak™ will be used unless eliminated due to microgravity testing. I X 012.08.2 Electrical Engineering Provide electrical support for a TSI P-Trak See electrical schematic #_____ I X 012.08.3 Software Engineering Provide software support for a TSI P-Trak See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.4.7 and 5.7 NVR 012.08.3.a SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall accept a set of commands to activate the keys on the P-Trak. The P-Trak group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 8 of SAME-DOC-004. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-7-1-1] T X X 012.08.3.b SAME-DOC-004 5.7 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the P-Trak via an RS-232 serial port.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p32,2,"[SAME-SW-REQ-5-7-1-1-1] T X X 012.08.3.c SAME-DOC-004 5.7 Software Engineering The DACU shall turn the P-Trak on and off by controlling its momentary on/off switch via custom electronics. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-7-1-1-2] T X X 012.08.3.d SAME-DOC-004 5.7 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the P-Trak keypad using custom electronics. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-7-1-1-3] T X X 012.08.3.e SAME-DOC-004 5.7 Software Engineering The DACU shall receive data from the P-Trak via an RS-232 serial port. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-7-2-1-1] T X X 013 SAME EIRD 13 Title First Moment measurement System The First Moment measurement is the second of three moment measurements required by science. It measures the total of the particle diameters. (length/unit volume) NVR 013.01 SAME EIRD 13.a Title a) Location: in diagnostics duct NVR 013.01.1 Mechanical Engineering The First Moment measurement system must extract a sample from the Sampling Duct Assembly. The sample is routed by flexible tube from the sampling duct to the First Moment measurement system. I X 013.02 SAME EIRD 13.b Title b) Expected levels at detector location: 0.01 to 7.5 m/cm3 (actual values will be based upon the suite of instruments, calibrated dilution is acceptable). NVR 013.02.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide the appropriate levels at the detector for its proper operation TXX A-17",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p33,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 013.03 SAME EIRD 13.c Title c) Repeatability ± 4 %, accuracy ± 10% NVR 013.03.1 Mechanical Engineering Verify the repeatability and accuracy of the First Moment System Verification of this requirement will be analysis performed by NIST. Inspection of their report will close the verification. IX X XX XX 013.04 SAME EIRD 13.d Title d) Temporal resolution 5 s NVR 013.04.2 Electrical Engineering The electrical characteristics for the first moment measurement system must have a response time which allows for a 5 second temporal resolution. Verification will be done by inspection of instrument specification. IXX 013.05 SAME EIRD 13.e Title e) Sampling rate 0.2 Hz NVR 013.05.3 Software Engineering The software must sample the first moment measurement system at least once every 5 seconds. The First Moment Measurement System can meet this requirement. See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.8. NVR 013.05.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.8 Software Engineering The DACU shall record measurements from the ionization detector at a minimum rate of 0.2 Hz. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-8-1-1-1] See # 013.08.3.a Data will be sampled at 1 Hz.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p33,1,"TXX 013.06 SAME EIRD 13.f Title f) Particle size sensitivity 0.05 µm to 4 µm NVR 013.06.1 Mechanical Engineering First moment measurement system must meet this particle size sensitivity with some margin. First Alert Ion detector will need to be calibrated to meet this requirement. NIST will be calibrating the instrument, ZIN will inspect the calibration certification. IX X XX XX 013.07 SAME EIRD 13.g Title g) System calibrated on the ground for monodisperse aerosols over expected range. NVR 013.07.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a ground test apparatus to test and characterize system Verification of this requirement will be testing performed by NIST. Inspection of their characterization will close the verification. IXXXXX 013.08 SAME EIRD 13.h Title h) Produce measurement that can be correlated with the first moment. Suggested approach: ionization sensor from residential smoke detector NVR 013.08.1 Mechanical Engineering Supply a first moment measurement equal to or better than a residential smoke detector ionization sensor An ionization sensor from a ""First Alert"" smoke detector will be mounted in the Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure. I X 013.08.2 Electrical Engineering Provide electrical support for a residential smoke detector ionization sensor An ionization sensor from a ""First Alert"" smoke detector will be mounted in the Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p33,2,"I X 013.08.3 Software Engineering Provide software support for a residential smoke detector ionization sensor See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.8 NVR 013.08.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.8 Software Engineering The DACU shall record measurements from the ionization detector at a minimum rate of 0.2 Hz. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-8-1-1-1] See # 013.05.3.a Data will be sampled at 1 Hz. TXX 014 SAME EIRD 14 Title Mass concentration measurement system (third moment) The Mass Concentration measurement is the third of the three moment measurements required by science. It measures the mass of smoke particles/unit volume. NVR 014.01 SAME EIRD 14.a Title a) Location: in diagnostics duct (inlet to aging chamber desired) NVR 014.01.1 Mechanical Engineering The Mass Concentration measurement system must extract a sample from the Sampling Duct Assembly. The sample is routed by flexible tube from the diagnostics duct to the Mass Concentration measurement system. I X 014.02 SAME EIRD 14.b Title b) Expected levels at detector location: 0.1 mg/m3 to 400 mg/m3 (actual values will be based upon the suite of instruments, calibrated dilution is acceptable). NVR 014.02.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide the appropriate levels at the detector for its proper operation. Dilution on the order of 4:1 required. TXX 014.03 SAME EIRD 14.c Title c) Repeatability ± 4 %, accuracy ± 10% NVR A-18",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p34,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 014.03.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a ground test apparatus to measure and characterize repeatability Verification of this requirement will be testing performed by NIST. Inspection of their characterization will close the verification. IXXXXX 014.04 SAME EIRD 14.d Title d) Temporal resolution 5 s NVR 014.04.2 Electrical Engineering The electrical characteristics for the number concentration system must have a response time which allows for a 5 second temporal resolution. Verification of this requirement will be done by inspection of instrument specifications. IXX 014.05 SAME EIRD 14.e Title e) Sampling rate 0.2 Hz NVR 014.05.3 Software Engineering The software must sample the mass concentration system at least once every 5 seconds. The Mass Concentration Measurement System can meet this requirement. See SAME-DOC- 004, section 5.6. NVR 014.05.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.6 Software Engineering The DACU shall sample DustTrak data at a minimum rate of 0.2 Hz. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-2-1-2] Data will be sampled at 1 Hz. TXX 014.06 SAME EIRD 14.f Title f) Particle size sensitivity 0.05 µm to 4 µm NVR 014.06.1 Mechanical Engineering Mass concentration system must meet this particle size sensitivity with some margin.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p34,1,"DustTrak™ is mass dependent and not necessarily sensitive to smallest particles, but it will detect them if enough are present. Verification of this requirement will be analysis performed by NIST. Inspection of their report will close the verification. IX X XX XX 014.07 SAME EIRD 14.g Title g) System calibrated on the ground for monodisperse aerosols over expected range. NVR 014.07.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a ground test apparatus to test and characterize system Verification of this requirement will be testing performed by NIST. Inspection of their characterization will close the verification. IXXXXX 014.08 SAME EIRD 14.h Title h) Produce measurement that can be correlated with the third moment (mass concentration) Suggested approach: TSI DustTrak™ NVR 014.08.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide mounting for a DustTrak Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 See SAME drawing #_______ I X 014.08.2 Electrical Engineering Provide electrical support for a DustTrak Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 See electrical schematic #_____ I X 014.08.3 Software Engineering Provide software support for a DustTrak See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.4.6 and 5.6 NVR 014.08.3.a SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall accept a set of commands to activate the keys on the DustTrak. The DustTrak group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 7 of SAME-DOC-004.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p34,2,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-6-1-1] See # 014.09.3.a TXX 014.08.3.b SAME-DOC-004 5.6 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the DustTrak via an RS-232 serial port. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-1-1-1] See # 014.09.3.b TXX 014.08.3.c SAME-DOC-004 5.6 Software Engineering The DACU shall turn the DustTrak on and off by controlling the on/off keypad using custom electronics. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-1-1-2] See # 014.09.3.c TXX 014.08.3.d SAME-DOC-004 5.6 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the DustTrak keypad using custom electronics. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-1-1-3] See # 014.09.3.d TXX 014.08.3.e SAME-DOC-004 5.6 Software Engineering The DACU shall receive data from the DustTrak via an RS-232 serial port. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-2-1-1] See # 014.09.3.e TXX 014.09 SAME EIRD 14.i Title i) Measurement of this moment at more than one particle size cutoff (e.g. using impactors at the inlets of multiple DustTraks) is desired. Either this requirement or section 17 can be met for a given test. NVR 014.09.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide more than one size impactor and two DustTraks, and design the capability to measure from two units simultaneously or exchange the impactors on-orbit. I X 014.09.2 Electrical Engineering Provide electrical support for a second DustTrak Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 See electrical schematic #_____ I X A-19",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p35,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 014.09.3 Software Engineering Provide software support for a second DustTrak See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.4.6 and 5.6 NVR 014.09.3.a SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall accept a set of commands to activate the keys on the DustTrak. The DustTrak group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 7 of SAME-DOC-004. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-6-1-1] See # 014.08.3.a TXX 014.09.3.b SAME-DOC-004 5.6 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the DustTrak via an RS-232 serial port. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-1-1-1] See # 014.08.3.b TXX 014.09.3.c SAME-DOC-004 5.6 Software Engineering The DACU shall turn the DustTrak on and off by controlling the on/off keypad using custom electronics. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-1-1-2] See # 014.08.3.c TXX 014.09.3.d SAME-DOC-004 5.6 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the DustTrak keypad using custom electronics. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-1-1-3] See # 014.08.3.d TXX 014.09.3.e SAME-DOC-004 5.6 Software Engineering The DACU shall receive data from the DustTrak via an RS-232 serial port.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p35,1,"[SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-2-1-1] See # 014.08.3.e TXX 015 SAME EIRD 15 Title Thermal Precipitator NVR 015.01 SAME EIRD 15.a Title a) Collect samples of smoke particles on Transmission Electron Microscope (TEM) grids NVR 015.01.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide removable TEM grids and procedure for grid removal TEM grids are mounted in the Thermal Precipitator Module. See SAME Drawing # __________ and Operating Procedure ___________ I XX 015.02 SAME EIRD 15.b Title b) Sampled locations, required location: 5 to 20 cm from sample; desired location: at exit from aging chamber. Samples will be taken directly from the aging chamber, per science's direction. NVR 015.02.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide TEM grids in the specified locations Position of sample ports will be coordinated with science, documented, and signed off by science. I X 015.03 SAME EIRD 15.c Multiple c) If sample is aspirated from the duct, sample lines must cause limited particle loss due to diffusion or impaction. Sample line transit time must be characterized (+/- 5 sec). Design incorporates best fluid system possible to achieve this requirement. Ground Testing is needed to characterize. T & A X X X 015.03.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide sample system to limit particle loss due to diffusion or impaction. Provide ground test apparatus to characterize. NVR 015.03.2 Electrical Engineering Provide electrical support for a ground test apparatus to test and characterize transit time.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p35,2,"NVR 015.03.3 Software Engineering Provide software support for a ground test apparatus to test and characterize transit time. See SAME-DOC-004, section 8.0 NVR 015.03.3.a SAME-DOC-004 8.0 Software Engineering Software will be provided to support a ground test apparatus to test and characterize thermal precipitator sample line transit times. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-8] Development Software NVR 015.03.3.b SAME-DOC-004 8.0 Software Engineering Software will be provided to support a ground test apparatus to test and characterize thermal precipitator sampling efficiency and particle loss. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-9] Development Software NVR 015.04 SAME EIRD 15.d Title d) At grid location, flow must be 1 cm/s (+/-20%) with a thermal gradient of 200 (+/-40) °C/mm above the grid. NVR 015.04.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide flow of 1 cm/s and thermal gradient as specified Current design of controlling flow through the system meets this requirement. Will be verified by analysis. A X 015.04.2 Electrical Engineering Provide control electronics for heaters and valves of Thermal Precipitators Characterize grid heaters. A X 015.04.3 Software Engineering Provide software control for heaters and valves of Thermal Precipitators See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.5 NVR 015.04.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.5 Software Engineering Using digital outputs the DACU shall control the valves of the thermal precipitator module so that at most only one valve is open at a time.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p35,3,[SAME-SW-REQ-5-5-1-1-1] T X X 015.04.3.b SAME-DOC-004 5.5 Software Engineering Using a digital output the DACU shall control power to one thermal precipitator module heater at a time. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-5-2-1-1] T X X 015.05 SAME EIRD 15.e Title e) TEM grid attachment: grids must be removable without damage or dislodging particles as demonstrated by ground- based testing. NVR 015.05.1 Mechanical Engineering Perform ground test to show that the grids are removable without damaging the grids or dislodging the particles. Ground based testing started using flight-like thermal precipitator and TEM grids to determine the proper grid removal procedure. TXX A-20,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p36,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 015.06 SAME EIRD 15.f Title f) Handling consideration: Grids must be protected from contamination before and after the tests. NVR 015.06.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide protection for TEM grids before and after the tests. See SAME Drawing #_________ for the TEM protection design. Between runs, there is no flow or temperature delta, so no cross contamination will occur. IX X 015.07 SAME EIRD 15.g Title g) Grids must be returned for TEM analysis NVR 015.07.5 Glovebox The Thermal Precipitator Modules must be removed, stowed, and subsequently returned to Earth for analysis. This should be documented in the ICD. I XX 015.08 SAME EIRD 15.h Title h) Grid type: 3 mm copper TEM grid, Formvar/carbon coated 300 mesh. NVR 015.08.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide grids as specified Can be accommodated in current design. I X X 015.09 SAME EIRD 15.i Multiple i) System performance (sampling efficiency and particle loss) must be demonstrated by 1-g testing (less than 20% variation in number count loss between 0.5, 1, and 2 μm test aerosols, in precipitator).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p36,1,"NVR 015.09.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a ground test apparatus to test and characterize system Verification of this requirement will be testing performed by NIST. Inspection of their characterization will close the verification. IXXXXX 016 SAME EIRD 16 Title Space craft smoke detectors (desired) NVR 016.01 SAME EIRD 16.a Title a) Location: in diagnostics duct NVR 016.01.1 Mechanical Engineering Locate the spacecraft smoke detectors in the diagnostics duct Both detectors are mounted in the Detector Duct Assembly. See SAME Drawing #_______________ I X 016.02 SAME EIRD 16.b Title b) Sampling rate 5 Hz NVR 016.02.3 Software Engineering The software will sample the spacecraft smoke detectors at least once per second. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 5.9 and 5.10 NVR 016.02.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.9 Software Engineering The DACU shall sample the output of the ISS detector at a sampling rate of 1 Hz or greater. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-9-1-1-2] See # 016.04.3.b TXX 016.02.3.b SAME-DOC-004 5.10 Software Engineering The DACU shall sample the output of the STS detector at a sampling rate of 1 Hz or greater. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-10-1-1-2] See # 016.03.3.b TXX 016.03 SAME EIRD 16.c Title c) Shuttle system (ionization):",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p36,2,"Brunswick DefenseTM Engineering Model (supplied by PI) NVR 016.03.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide mounting for the Brunswick Defense™ detector Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 See SAME drawing #_______ I X 016.03.2 Electrical Engineering Provide electrical support for the Brunswick Defense detector Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 See SAME Electrical Schematic #_____ I X 016.03.3 Software Engineering Provide software support for the Brunswick Defense detector See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.10 NVR 016.03.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.10 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the STS detector. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-10-1-1-1] T X X 016.03.3.b SAME-DOC-004 5.10 Software Engineering The DACU shall sample the output of the STS detector at a sampling rate of 1 Hz or greater. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-10-1-1-2] See # 016.02.3.b TXX 016.04 SAME EIRD 16.d Title d) ISS system (scattering): Allied Signal/HoneywellTM Engineering Model (supplied by PI) NVR 016.04.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide mounting for an Allied Signal/HoneywellTM Engineering Model Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 See SAME drawing #_______ I X 016.04.2 Electrical Engineering Provide electrical support for an Allied Signal/HoneywellTM Engineering Model Deleted by SAME-ECO-119 See electrical schematic #_____ I X 016.04.3 Software Engineering Provide software support for an Allied Signal/HoneywellTM Engineering Model See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.9 NVR 016.04.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.9 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the ISS detector.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p36,3,[SAME-SW-REQ-5-9-1-1-1] T X X A-21,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p37,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 016.04.3.b SAME-DOC-004 5.9 Software Engineering The DACU shall sample the output of the ISS detector at a sampling rate of 1 Hz or greater. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-9-1-1-2] See # 016.02.3.a TXX 017 SAME EIRD 17 Title Gaseous Constituent Sensors (desired) Either this requirement or requirement 14.i can be met for a given test. This requirement is not implemented at this time, interfaces are provided for future use. NVR 017.01 SAME EIRD 17.a Title a) Location: in the diagnostics duct NVR 017.02 SAME EIRD 17.b Title b) Sampling Rate: 5 Hz NVR 017.03 SAME EIRD 17.c Title c) Suggested system: CO and CO2 sensors such as provided by Makel Engineering. Alternate suggested system: E-nose, developed by NASA JPL NVR 100 SAME-DOC-004 4.1 Title System Status Requirements NVR 100.01 SAME-DOC-004 4.1 Software Engineering The DACU software shall update its internal status once per second. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-1-1-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXX 100.02 SAME-DOC-004 4.1 Software Engineering The DACU shall illuminate the OK STATUS LED while the CPU is properly running. If the software detects an internal fault or error condition, the LED shall be extinguished.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p37,1,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-1-2-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 6.4 TXX 100.03 SAME-DOC-004 4.1 Software Engineering The DACU shall illuminate the TEST RUNNING LED while a test is running. Upon completion, the LED shall be extinguished. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-1-2-1-2] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 6.4 TXX 101 SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Title Data Communication Requirements NVR 101.01 SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering Once per second, the DACU shall transmit to the SPLC a health and status packet containing information such as voltages, currents, and pressures necessary to verify safe and proper operation. Included in this information are the C&W flags which indicate nominal or off-nominal operation to the rack systems. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-3-1] Derived from MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document Final Integrated Test TXXX 101.02 SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The Cycle Counter field in the health and status packet shall be incremented once per second up to the value 0xffff, then roll over to 0. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-3-3] Derived from MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document TXXX 101.03 SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering Once per second, the DACU shall transmit to the SPLC a low rate telemetry packet representing the current state of the instrument.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p37,2,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-4-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 Final Integrated Test TXXX 101.04 SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering All data files stored on the DACU local storage shall be automatically forwarded to the MLC via FTP for backup and downlink. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-2-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXXX 101.05 SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The DACU will automatically switch the Ethernet source from MLC→ISS to MLC→SAME when communication to the MLC is required. When the transaction is completed, the original MLC→ISS connection will be restored. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-2-2-3] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 Final Integrated Test TXXX 101.06 SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The DACU shall provide the ability to remove SAME files from the FTP server as needed to ensure proper storage space utilization. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-2-3-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 Final Integrated Test TXXX 101.07 SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The DACU shall provide a telnet console login session on an RS232 port. All administrative features and capabilities available via telnet shall be supported. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-3-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 Final Integrated Test TXX 101.08 SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The telnet console RS232 serial port settings shall be 19200 baud, no parity, 8 data bits, and 1 stop bit.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p37,3,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-3-1-2] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 Final Integrated Test TXX 102 SAME-DOC-004 4.3 Title Data Storage Requirements NVR 102.01 SAME-DOC-004 4.3 Software Engineering The DACU shall store all files on a media that can preserve all generated files for extraction after payload down-mass. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-1-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.2 TXX A-22",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p38,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 102.02 SAME-DOC-004 4.3 Software Engineering The storage media shall have a capacity of at least 520 megabytes. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-1-1-2] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.2 TXX 102.03 SAME-DOC-004 4.3 Software Engineering All files created shall be organized into a hierarchical directory structure based on the date that files were created. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-2-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.2 TXX 102.04 SAME-DOC-004 4.3 Software Engineering All files created shall have unique file names. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-2-2-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.2 TXX 102.05 SAME-DOC-004 4.3 Software Engineering Once per second data relating to the status of the instrument shall be stored to a file. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.2 TXX 102.06 SAME-DOC-004 4.3 Software Engineering Each second of housekeeping data stored to a file shall be time- tagged. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-1-2] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.2 TXX 102.07 SAME-DOC-004 4.3 Software Engineering Messages generated by the instrument shall be stored to a file.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p38,1,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-2-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.2 TXX 102.08 SAME-DOC-004 4.3 Software Engineering Each message stored to a file shall be time-tagged. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-2-2] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.2 TXX 102.09 SAME-DOC-004 4.3 Software Engineering Experiment results shall be stored to a file. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-3-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.2 TXX 102.10 SAME-DOC-004 4.3 Software Engineering Experiment results stored to a file shall be time-tagged. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-3-2] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.2 TXX 103 SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Title Command Requirements NVR 103.01 SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering Commands shall be organized by group and identifier, thus allowing logical grouping to ease user interface operations and functional implementation. Deleted by SAME-ECO-033 [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 I X 103.02 SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering Command groups shall include those listed in Table 2 of SAME- DOC-004. Deleted by SAME-ECO-033 [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-2] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXX 103.03 SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering At the start of execution of a command the DACU shall generate and downlink a message that includes the name of the command and its parameters.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p38,2,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-3] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXX 103.04 SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering At the successful completion of a command the DACU shall generate and downlink a message announcing the successful completion. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-4] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXX 103.05 SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall perform out-of-bound checking on all command parameters and reject commands with out-of-bound parameters. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-5] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXX 103.06 SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The bounds for command parameters shall be those specified in SAME-DOC-005, Software Interface Control Document. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-6] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXX 103.07 SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall allow for suspension of bound checking on command parameters. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-7] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXX 103.08 SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall indicate when a command is being executed with bound checking suspended. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-8] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXX 103.09 SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall accept a set of commands to perform system level operations. The system group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 3 of SAME-DOC-004.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p38,3,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-2-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXX 103.10 SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall accept a set of commands to perform automated tests. The test group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 4 of SAME-DOC-004. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-3-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXX 103.11 SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall accept a set of commands to configure test parameters. The test configuration group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 5 of SAME-DOC-004. All operations are for a specified test. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-4-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXX 104 SAME-DOC-004 4.5 Title Experiment Sequencing Requirements NVR 104.01 SAME-DOC-004 4.5 Software Engineering There shall be predefined tests with parameters as determined by the Principal Investigator. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-3-1-2] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 6.4 TXX A-23",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p39,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 104.02 SAME-DOC-004 4.5 Software Engineering Additional tests shall be possible to define with parameters determined by uplink commands. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-3-1-3] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 6.4 TXX 104.03 SAME-DOC-004 4.5 Software Engineering It shall be possible to confirm all parameters for a specific test. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-3-1-4] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 TXX 105 SAME-DOC-004 5.1 Title Sample Carousel Subsystem Requirements NVR 105.01 SAME-DOC-004 5.1 Software Engineering The DACU shall control a motor to rotate the sample carousel to a specified sample number. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-1-1-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 4.1 TXX 105.02 SAME-DOC-004 5.1 Software Engineering The DACU shall use four proximity sensors to confirm correct positioning of the correct sample carousel. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-1-1-1-2] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 4.1 TXX 105.03 SAME-DOC-004 5.1 Software Engineering The DACU shall mate the selected sample tube with the smoke generation duct and the sample contacts with heater wire coil contacts by controlling a linear actuator.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p39,1,"[SAME-SW-REQ-5-1-2-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 4.1 TXX 105.04 SAME-DOC-004 5.1 Software Engineering The DACU shall confirm correct mating of a sample tube and sample contacts by monitoring the resistance between the two heater wire coil contacts. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-1-2-1-2] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 4.1 TXX 106 SAME-DOC-004 5.4 Title Aging Chamber Subsystem Requirements NVR 106.01 SAME-DOC-004 5.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the aging chamber piston downward velocity in order to provide smoke at the rate required for the particular diagnostics in use. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-3] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 4.1 TXX 106.02 SAME-DOC-004 5.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the aging chamber piston downward movement by controlling a solenoid on the GN2 line, by sensing and controlling a mass flow controller on the GN2 line, and by sensing a pressure transducer monitoring the sample side pressure of the piston. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-4] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 4.1 TXX 107 SAME-DOC-004 6.1 Title Maintenance Mode Operation Requirements NVR 107.01 SAME-DOC-004 6.1 Software Engineering In the event of a loss of the ability to write to the data hard drive, the system shall continue to accept uplink commands and transmit status data to ensure safe operation.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p39,2,"[SAME-SW-REQ-6-1-1-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 Final Integrated Test TXX 107.02 SAME-DOC-004 6.1 Software Engineering In the event of a loss of Ethernet communications between the DACU and the MLC, operations shall continue. Data recorded on the flight avionics may be manually commanded for retransmission once communication is regained. [SAME-SW-REQ-6-1-1-1-2] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 5.1 Final Integrated Test TXXX 108 SAME-DOC-004 6.3 Title Automatic Safing NVR 108.01 SAME-DOC-004 6.3 Software Engineering In the event of a GN2 regulated pressure reading of over 21 psig, the DACU shall automatically shut off the main GN2 valve. [SAME-SW-REQ-6-3-1-1-1] Response to #204.3.3.1 TX XX 108.02 SAME-DOC-004 6.3 Software Engineering In the event of a vacuum pressure reading of over 19 psia, the DACU shall automatically shut off all connections to the ISS VES. [SAME-SW-REQ-6-3-1-1-2] Response to #204.3.6.1 TX XX 109 SAME-DOC-004 7.0 Title Crew Display Requirements NVR 109.01 SAME-DOC-004 7.1 Software Engineering The crew display shall meet all applicable standards defined in SSP 50313, Display and Graphics Commonality Standard. [SAME-SW-REQ-7-1-1-1-1] Derived from SSP 57000, Section 4.3.3.8.1 Inspection and Test by PDRP T & I X X X 109.02 SAME-DOC-004 7.3 Software Engineering The crew display shall include a subset of the full instrument command list.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p39,3,"[SAME-SW-REQ-7-3-1-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 4.3 TXXX 109.03 SAME-DOC-004 7.3 Software Engineering The crew display shall display a subset of the full instrument health and status and/or low-rate telemetry data. [SAME-SW-REQ-7-3-2-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 4.3 TXXX 109.04 SAME-DOC-004 7.4 Software Engineering For off-nominal operations the crew display shall include a feature that allows it to run in enhanced mode with an expanded command list and data display. Deleted by SAME-ECO-033 [SAME-SW-REQ-7-4-1-1-1] Derived from SAME-DOC-001, Section 4.3 TXXX A-24",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p40,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 110.03 SAME-PLN-003 1.3 Systems Engineering Use of Previously Designed, Fabricated, or Flown Hardware The Project Manager will provide documented evidence as to how the flight worthiness and integrity of previously flown and/or fabricated hardware intended for reuse has been maintained. NVR 130.02.a SAME-PLN-003 3.2 Systems Engineering Overall Verification Program The verifications for SAME will be contained in the [Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document, SAME-PLN- 005]. The verifications shall be prepared for demonstrating compliance with project verification requirements for each deliverable item. Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 This document will be prepared and released in accordance with the configuration management approach defined in SAME-PLN-002. A X 130.02.b SAME-PLN-003 3.2 Multiple Overall Verification Program Verification procedures will be prepared for each functional and environmental test activity as appropriate at the component, subsystem, and experiment levels. These procedures will describe the configuration of the article, specific environmental parameters, and how each activity contained in the verification plan will be implemented.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p40,1,"These procedures will be prepared and released in accordance with the SAME defined configuration management approach. NVR 130.02.c SAME-PLN-003 3.2 Multiple Overall Verification Program Verification reports for specific tests, analyses, inspections, and validations will be compiled by the responsible SAME personnel. These reports will be prepared and released in accordance with the SAME defined configuration management approach, SAME- PLN-002. NVR 130.03 SAME-PLN-003 3.3 Electrical Engineering Electrical Verification Requirements A functional checkout will be conducted before and after environmental tests, as defined in the verification plan. NVR 130.04.01 SAME-PLN-003 3.4.1 Mechanical Engineering Safety Critical and Fracture Critical Structures All structural elements including associated interfaces, fasteners, and welds in the primary load path are considered Safety Critical Structures (SCS) and shall be analyzed per the guidelines of NASA-STD-5003 and SSP 52005. Fracture Critical components are a subset of SCS and are defined as ones whose failure would present catastrophic hazards, i.e. they are not fail-safe. Through analysis SAME has determined that there are no Fracture Critical Components as part of the payload. A X 130.04.02 SAME-PLN-003 3.4.2 Mechanical Engineering Structural Loads SAME will demonstrate compliance with structural loads requirements by conducting the appropriate inspections, analyses, and/or tests.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p40,2,"Flight hardware will be designed to maintain structural integrity and functionality during all phases of the expected life cycle. Verification of the structures and systems will consider static and dynamic loads encountered during assembly, testing, transportation, launch, ascent, space operations, descent and landing. NASA-STD-5002 will be utilized for defining methodologies, practices and requirements for conducting load analyses in the verification plan. NVR 130.04.03 SAME-PLN-003 3.4.3 Mechanical Engineering Factors of Safety NASA-STD-5001 and SSP 52005 will be used to establish design and test factors, as well as service life factors, to be used for flight hardware development and verification. NVR 130.04.04 SAME-PLN-003 3.4.4 Mechanical Engineering Margins of Safety All structural elements that are critical for safety and mission assurance shall be shown by analysis to have positive margins of safety. The minimum margins of safety for all credible failure modes shall be determined. A X A-25",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p41,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 130.04.05 SAME-PLN-003 3.4.5 Mechanical Engineering Fracture Control SAME does not have any fracture critical hardware. However, if that determination is changed SAME will follow the guidelines in SSP-52005, and a Fracture Control Program will be implemented to meet the requirements of NASA-STD-5003 and SSP 30558. NVR 130.04.07 SAME-PLN-003 3.4.7 Mechanical Engineering Strength Testing The SAME Project will verify structural integrity by analysis alone without strength testing using the criteria and design factors specified in SSP 52005. Criteria and rationale will be reviewed and approved by the Payload Carrier/Integrator. For payloads launched on the Space Shuttle such as SAME, the Space Shuttle Payload Safety Review Panel will approve these criteria. NVR 130.04.08.a SAME-PLN-003 3.4.8 Mechanical Engineering Vibroacoustics All SAME flight hardware will be flown in a stowed configuration, packed in foam. SAME will use a combination of random and sinusoidal vibration testing (Protoflight vibration) to demonstrate the ability of the system to withstand launch requirements. See table in SAME-PLN-003, Section 3.4.8 for major system components and their vibration testing levels.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p41,1,"NVR 130.04.08.b SAME-PLN-003 3.4.8 Mechanical Engineering Vibroacoustics For a Class D experiment such as SAME, the workmanship vibration testing is a guideline. Vibration sensitive components, such as optical components or particle sensors, and irreplaceable mission critical parts will see qualification program levels of vibration testing while in the “soft” stowage configuration that they will see for launch. See table in SAME-PLN-003, Section 3.4.8 for major system components and their vibration testing levels. NVR 130.04.08.c SAME-PLN-003 3.4.8 Mechanical/ Electrical Engineering Vibroacoustics The custom boards that are part of the SAME experiment will be subjected to a workmanship level of testing. The remaining electronics boards will only be tested as a part of the final assembly (during the final, protoflight vibe). This testing will be performed to reveal inadequacies in workmanship. See table in SAME-PLN-003, Section 3.4.8 for major system components and their vibration testing levels. NVR 130.04.08.d SAME-PLN-003 3.4.8 Mechanical Engineering Vibroacoustics To validate the SLA material integrity, a mass model mockup of the largest rapid prototype component assembly, the Fluids Control Unit, will be hard mounted and tested to workmanship levels. This SLA material mockup will be tested without plating in order to characterize the worst case behavior of the raw material, and to allow more complete post vibration inspection.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p41,2,"See table in SAME-PLN-003, Section 3.4.8 for major system components and their vibration testing levels. NVR 130.04.08.e SAME-PLN-003 3.4.8 Mechanical Engineering Vibroacoustics Low-Level sine-sweep testing may be performed on engineering hardware to determine natural frequencies and provide finite element model validation, if the project manager specifies. See table in SAME-PLN-003, Section 3.4.8 for major system components and their vibration testing levels. NVR 130.04.09 SAME-PLN-003 3.4.9 Mechanical Engineering Shock (Mechanical and Pyro) The SAME Project will perform shock testing and/or analysis per carrier/integrator requirements. MSFC-RQMT-2888, MSG Investigation Interface Requirements Document contains no shock requirements. NVR 130.04.10 SAME-PLN-003 3.4.10 Mechanical Engineering Mechanical Function Verification testing shall be performed to demonstrate that the installation of each mechanical device is correct and that no problems exist that will prevent the proper operation of the mechanism during mission life. Flight system functional test. T X X X 130.04.11 SAME-PLN-003 3.4.11 Mechanical Engineering Pressure Profile Payload structures containing trapped volumes will be designed to maintain positive margins of safety per NSTS 1700.7 and ISS Addendum, NSTS 18798, and SSP 52005 when exposed to the worst-case depressurization/re-pressurization environments based on carrier location. Verification shall be by analysis. A X A-26",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p42,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 130.04.12.a SAME-PLN-003 3.4.12 Mechanical Engineering Fastener Integrity The SAME Project shall comply with fastener requirements outlined in “Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) Investigation Interface Requirements Document”, MSFC-RQMT-2888, section 3.1.1.3 – Attachment Provisions. SAME shall use either MSG provided M6 x 1.0 pitch fasteners or provide its own M6 x 1.0 pitch fasteners for interface with the M6 inserts. SAME shall provide a means of keeping the fastener captive, either physically or operationally. I X 130.04.12.b SAME-PLN-003 3.4.12 Mechanical Engineering Fastener Integrity The SAME project shall obtain approved certified fasteners from a NASA center whenever possible. In the event that a fastener that cannot be provided from the bonded storage of a NASA center late in the project, SAME will order certified fasteners from a ZIN-Tech approved vendor as accepted by GRC waiver. Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 I XX 130.04.13 SAME-PLN-003 3.4.13 Mechanical Engineering Mass Properties The SAME Project shall analyze mass properties using MIL- HDBK-1811 as a guideline.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p42,1,"Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 A X X X 130.04.14 SAME-PLN-003 3.4.14 Multiple Ground Support Equipment All Ground Support Equipment (GSE) shipped to KSC will meet the requirements of the SAME Ground Safety Package. This document will include all applicable sections of NASA-STD-5005 and KHB 1700.7. All GSE to be used at the Glenn Research Center will meet the requirements of the NASA Glenn Safety Manual. At this time there no GSE planned for shipment to KSC. NVR 130.05 SAME-PLN-003 3.5 Electrical Engineering Electromagnetic Compatibility (EMC) Requirements The final EMI qualification testing will be done with the fully assembled SAME Experiment, in its various operating modes, as required by the MSG/SAME ICD (EMI 2888 test compliment). For risk mitigation, subassemblies suspected of emissions and susceptibilities will be tested at the box level. ORU change-outs (P-Trak and the Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure) will be made during conducted emissions and radiated emissions only. NVR 130.07 SAME-PLN-003 3.7 Mechanical Engineering Vacuum, Thermal, and Humidity Requirements Thermal tests and/or analysis will demonstrate the system under consideration will: a) Properly operate in the flight environment, specifically temperature. b) Properly control the thermal environment of temperature sensitive items or levy that requirement on an integrator. c) Survive the temperature and humidity conditions of transportation (e.g.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p42,2,"truck, plane, and space vehicle), storage and pre and post conditions of the carrier. These tests will also act as an environmental screening to stimulate latent defects to maximize infant mortality. NVR 130.07.2 SAME-PLN-003 3.7.2 Mechanical Engineering [Thermal] Testing Levels Testing will be performed per SARG Table 3.7.2-1. Modifications to the number of thermal cycles and temperature range may be made based on carrier requirements and/or hardware requirements. These adjustments will be pre-approved by GRC. NVR 130.07.3.1 SAME-PLN-003 3.7.3.1 Electrical Engineering Thermal Cycling Only the electronic boards of the SAME system will be thermal cycled. This will help ensure that problems with latent manufacturing defects are addressed. This also includes the boards for the P-TRAK and DustTrak commercial units that are part of the payload. The system will undergo functional test at the end of the thermal cycling test. See table in SAME-PLN-003, Section 3.7.3.1 for thermal cycling information. Thermal cycling will be done at ambient pressure per SAME-PLN-003, Section 3.7.3.1.1 NVR A-27",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p43,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 130.07.4 SAME-PLN-003 3.7.4 Mechanical/ Electrical Engineering Description of Applicable [Thermal] Analysis The SAME project shall perform Thermal Analysis on all systems and identify the following: a) Heat sources and their magnitude b) Methods employed to dissipate the heat from the sources c) List of operating temperature ranges of the components d) Environmental conditions and design criteria e) Assessment of the thermal design and identification of additional analysis needed. f) Evaluation of the susceptibility to humidity extremes. g) Identification of any special testing requirements or conditions. This minimum analysis will be completed by PDR. A X 130.08.1 SAME-PLN-003 3.8.1 Systems Engineering Burn-In Tests Systems under test that contain electronic (EEE) parts shall be burned-in to stimulate infant mortality failures. A minimum of one hundred hours of failure-free operation is required for SAME components, subsystems or systems prior to hardware turnover. The one hundred hours includes operational time accumulated during any functional and environmental testing. Burn-in will be accomplished and recorded by way of system testing. Log books will contain operating times.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p43,1,"AX X 130.08.2.a SAME-PLN-003 3.8.2 Multiple Mission Simulation Test Flight and flight spare hardware will be subjected to mission simulation testing to provide confidence for both nominal and off- nominal operations. The hardware will also be run for extended periods of time, in part or in whole, to gain confidence that subsystems and functionality are sufficiently robust to meet the design goals. NVR 130.08.2.b SAME-PLN-003 3.8.2 Multiple Mission Simulation Test Flight hardware shall complete an operational test, which exercises all equipment and software commands. A test plan for the scope and duration of this test shall be coordinated with the Principal Investigators and/or Project Scientists to verify the correct experiment operations. TX X X 130.08.3 SAME-PLN-003 3.8.3 Multiple End-to-End Compatibility Test An end-to-end compatibility test shall be conducted on the complete operational system in the final mission configuration, as closely as possible. This test would include the flight system; the flight operational software, the Carrier/carrier simulator, and the mission operations system, including the ground processing equipment and software in order to fully demonstrate operational compatibility and the ability of the entire system to perform as required during the mission. The SAME project will develop a joint test plan with the MSG facility to define end-to-end interface tests with the MSG Ground Integration Unit (GIU).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p43,2,"TX X X 140.01 SAME-PLN-003 4.1 Safety System Safety Requirements The SAME Project Office (PO) shall conduct appropriate safety analyses both at the system and subsystem levels. Each system and subsystem shall be evaluated to determine the applicability of each technical safety requirement. SAME will implement a system safety program that will assure the identification and control of hazards during design, fabrication, test, transportation, ground operations, launch, and retrieval. The SAME system safety program will be tailored to meet carrier-specific requirements, as outlined in the SARG. SAME will also meet the interface and safety requirements for Microgravity Science Glovebox as required by the MSG Interface Control Document (ICD) for SAME and the SAME Flight Safety Data Package (SAME-SDP- 002). A XXX A-28",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p44,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 150.01 SAME-PLN-003 5.1 Multiple EEE and Mechanical Parts Control - General Requirements SAME will implement a control plan for EEE parts that is based on the ZIN QMS. This requires that parts are traceable and controlled over the life of the project. Additionally, the project will select parts for mission success based on NPD 8730.2. See SAME-PLN-003, Section 5.2 and 5.3 for details regarding EEE and Mechanical Control. NVR 150.02.4 SAME-PLN-003 5.2.4 Electrical Engineering Derating All EEE parts shall be used in accordance with NASA De-rating Guidelines. SAME EEE parts shall be de-rated per the requirements of SSP 30312 and NASA TM 102179 as specified in SSP 57000 or SSP 57003. Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 A X 150.02.8.a SAME-PLN-003 5.2.8 QA / Electrical Engineering Inspection Prior To Assembly EEE parts will be inspected prior to their assembly into flight systems or subsystems to ensure they are free of any debris, defects or other manufacturing faults that would interfere with their form, fit, and function. SAME will identify all safety or mission critical parts.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p44,1,"Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 I X X 150.02.8.b SAME-PLN-003 5.2.8 QA / Electrical Engineering Inspection Prior to Assembly EEE parts that are safety or mission critical shall be tested to verify and certify their electrical performance and protect against counterfeit or noncompliant parts prior to assembly into flight systems or subsystems. Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 Parts include the heater control circuit, solenoids, and DACU. TXX 150.03.2.a SAME-PLN-003 5.3.2 QA / Mechanical Engineering Inspection Prior To Assembly Per ZIN QMS procedure P10001 Inspection and Test Procedure, all mechanical parts shall be inspected prior to their assembly into flight systems or subsystems to ensure they are free of any debris, defect or other material or substance that would interfere with their function. Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 I X X X 150.03.2.b SAME-PLN-003 5.3.2 Mechanical Engineering Inspection Prior To Assembly SAME will identify all safety or mission critical parts. Mechanical parts that are safety or mission critical shall be tested to verify and certify their performance and protect against counterfeit or noncompliant parts prior to assembly into flight systems or subsystems Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 Reference SAME-ANA-001 for list of critical parts. TXX 150.03.2.c SAME-PLN-003 5.3.2 Mechanical Engineering Inspection Prior To Assembly All mechanical parts that provide rotational, translational or other movements shall be tested for full range of motion, and inspected for freedom of motion.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p44,2,"T & I X X 150.06 SAME-PLN-003 5.6 Multiple Parts Identification List The SAME Project will identify and maintain traceability by part number, name or description, manufacturer or CAGE, serial number, lot date code, quantity, and next higher assembly for all parts. The parts list will be submitted at CDR (as-designed parts list) and as part of the Acceptance Data Package (ADP) (as-built parts list). NVR 150.07 SAME-PLN-003 5.7 Systems Engineering Parts Risk Evaluation The risks associated with part selection will be tracked with the SAME Continuous Risk Management Plan, SAME-PLN-007. NVR 160.01.a SAME-PLN-003 6.1 Mechanical Engineering Materials and Processes Requirements - General Requirements SAME shall compile a Material Identification Usage List (MIUL) during the design process and maintain it current to the as-built configuration. The MIUL will identify individual material applications specified in design drawings; their respective pertinent material ratings; and other pertinent material application features essential for evaluation of the material application. A XX A-29",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p45,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 160.01.b SAME-PLN-003 6.1 Mechanical Engineering Materials and Processes Requirements - General Requirements All materials not ""A"" rated per MSFC-HDBK-527 or MAPTIS shall require an MUA and supporting documentation of the test results or analysis. SAME shall prepare Volatile Usage Agreements (VUA) as required per SSP 30233 for use of specific volatile organic compounds, primarily alcohols. A XX 160.01.c SAME-PLN-003 6.1 Mechanical Engineering Materials and Processes Requirements - General Requirements SAME shall prepare the appropriate materials-related information, including a certification letter for the acceptance of flight payload materials that will support NASA/GRC’s material certification of the experiment hardware. All manned space flight hardware certified from GRC shall be per the M&P Inter Center Agreements (ICA) established with Marshall Space Flight Center (MSFC), Johnson Space Flight Center (JSC), and Goddard Space Flight Center (GSFC). An M&P certification letter is approved and issued by the GRC Certifying Official to document compliance with the ICA.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p45,1,"Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 A XX X 160.02 SAME-PLN-003 6.2 Mechanical Engineering Materials and Processes Requirements - Specific Requirements This section addresses the M&P requirements needed to assure all mission success and safety related areas are addressed. See SAME-PLN-003 for the requirements. NVR 160.02.1.1 SAME-PLN-003 6.2.1.1 Mechanical Engineering Corrosion (Internal and External Environments) The SAME project will finish all metallic parts, assemblies, and equipment, including spares to provide protection from corrosion in accordance with the requirements of MSFC-SPEC-250. Materials not rated ""A"" in MAPTIS will have the required protection specified by the SAME project and verified by the SAME quality assurance lead. NVR 160.02.1.4.a SAME-PLN-003 6.2.1.4 Mechanical Engineering Flammability (Applicable to All Vehicle Areas) The project will design with materials that meet the flammability requirements of NSTS 1700.7B, section 209.2. “The minimum use of flammable materials will be the preferred means of hazard reduction.” NVR 160.02.1.4.b SAME-PLN-003 6.2.1.4 Electrical Engineering Flammability (Applicable to All Vehicle Areas) Electrical wire insulation will be made of Teflon-based materials and meet the requirements of JSC 29353. The M&P representative will be contacted regarding utilization of any wires or cables that do not meet of these specifications.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p45,2,"NVR 160.02.1.4.c SAME-PLN-003 6.2.1.4 Electrical Engineering Flammability (Applicable to All Vehicle Areas) Electrical wiring cable ties shall be made from ECTFE (Halar) material to provide an efficient means of hazard reduction. This is in preference to expending resources on verifying the spacing of flammable cable tie materials allowed by JSC 29353. Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 I X 160.02.1.5 SAME-PLN-003 6.2.1.5 Mechanical Engineering Fluid Compatibility All materials shall meet the fluid systems compatibility requirements of NSTS 1700.7B, paragraph 209.1. NASA-STD- 6001, Test 15 ""Reactivity of Materials in Aerospace Fluids,"" shall be performed to evaluate any changes resulting from exposure of a material to an aerospace fluid that makes the fluid or material unusable. When materials can be exposed to hazardous fluids by a credible single barrier failure, an engineering evaluation and analysis of test data will be conducted to demonstrate the acceptability of the configuration. Deleted by SAME-ECO-202 No exposure of any SAME hardware to any hazardous fluids can result from a single failure. TXX 160.02.1.6 SAME-PLN-003 6.2.1.6 Mechanical Engineering Materials Off-Gassing in Habitable Areas Off gas testing will be performed on the flight hardware at MSFC during shipment to KSC. NVR A-30",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p46,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 170.01.a SAME-PLN-003 7.1 Systems Engineering Reliability, Availability & Maintainability - General Requirements SAME will plan and implement a reliability program that interacts effectively with other program disciplines, including systems engineering, hardware design, and product assurance. The preferred development approach is to develop prototype hardware/software to provide the final flight design for experiment hardware/software. The reliability effort will be conducted throughout the design process, to the final design of the flight hardware. Much of the reliability will be demonstrated through component and assembly testing. Reliability and availability analyses will be performed concurrently with design to indicate areas of risk, promote design improvements, and increase system reliability and supportability. NVR 170.01.b SAME-PLN-003 7.1 Systems Engineering Reliability, Availability & Maintainability - General Requirements All non-conformances that occur during build-up or testing of flight hardware shall be reported and tracked to completion per the ZIN-Tech Quality Management System (QMS).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p46,1,"Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 A X 170.02.1.a SAME-PLN-003 7.2.1 Systems Engineering Reliability Allocation and Prediction Analysis The SAME project shall perform a low-fidelity reliability prediction and failure mode analysis. The system level reliability for the mission duration and duty cycle will be improved to the greatest extent possible with a combination of highly reliable components and flight spares complemented with alternate (off-nominal) procedures. A X 170.02.1.b SAME-PLN-003 7.2.1 Systems Engineering Reliability Allocation and Prediction Analysis When using a commercial off-the-shelf (COTS) component, SAME will procure the optimal reliability component. The selection will be based on the following: 1) Acceptable vendor screening program to the project requirements (burn-in, thermal cycle and vibration levels) 2) Acceptable vendor quality control program 3) A Mean-Time Between Failure (MTBF) which yields a reliability of 90% or greater for the expected life cycle of the component, whenever this data and these parts are available from the vendor 4) Radiation hardness and minimized EMI susceptibility, as needed NVR 170.02.2 SAME-PLN-003 7.2.2 Systems Engineering Burn-In Periods The SAME project shall provide a statement of the burn-in periods (both total burn-in and failure free) that will be required for all equipment prior to delivery, and how the periods relate to the overall equipment life.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p46,2,"Experiment run times will be provided (log books.) AX X 170.02.3 SAME-PLN-003 7.2.3 Systems Engineering Failure Modes and Effects Analysis and Critical Items List The SAME project will perform a Failure Analysis, performed on the major assembly/component level in order to determine the criticality of its internal hardware devices, relative to mission success and safety. Critical items are those components or subassemblies whose failure modes are safety critical or mission- success critical. In those instances where critical failure modes cannot be designed out of the intended system, the hardware will be designed for minimum risk or a spare component will be flown. NVR A-31",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p47,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 170.02.4 SAME-PLN-003 7.2.4 Systems Engineering Single Point Failure List There are no single point failures for loss of life or vehicle in the SAME Experiment. Any single point failures that could result in injury, loss of life, vehicle, or science mission, will be determined from the Hazards Analysis and the FMEA. Single point failures that would compromise mission success will be determined and documented as a result of the FMEA and will become part of the SAME risk list to be accepted with rationale, watched, or mitigated as they are evaluated. NVR 170.02.5 SAME-PLN-003 7.2.5 Systems Engineering Failure Detection SAME shall provide a description of the automatic or manual failure detection, isolation and recovery methods supported by the design under analysis. Will be contained in crew procedures as malfunction procedures. A XX 170.02.8 SAME-PLN-003 7.2.8 Systems Engineering Worst Case Analysis Worst-case analysis will be performed by the project on all circuits that support safety critical functions. The most sensitive design parameters, including those that are subject to variations that could degrade performance, will be included in the analysis.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p47,1,"NVR 170.02.9 SAME-PLN-003 7.2.9 Systems Engineering Trend Analysis The project will assess component, subsystem and system reliability and safety risk by identifying variables through testing and non-conformance reports. NVR 170.03.2 SAME-PLN-003 7.3.2 Systems Engineering Sparing and Logistics Planning The SAME project will build and maintain spare subsystem components as required by the customer. The spare unit will be composed of flight parts and will be identical to the flight unit. The Fit/Functional Model hardware is intended to be composed of some flight-like parts and procedures. This unit may be available for sparing of some parts or subsystems. Information and support will be provided to the Project Manager for sparing and logistics planning. NVR 170.04.1 SAME-PLN-003 7.4.1 Systems Engineering Maintainability Allocation and Prediction Analysis The project shall perform a maintainability allocation and prediction assessment. Spare hardware will be flown, as mass allocations allow, where failures are likely and unavoidable. Procedures shall be developed to troubleshoot, fix, or work around malfunctions. A X 170.04.2 SAME-PLN-003 7.4.2 QA / Multiple Limited Life Items Limited storage life items have a specified storage time limit (shelf-life), which is less its planned storage time. Examples of limited storage-life items are solid chemicals, chemical mixtures and/or solutions, solvents, lubricants, epoxies, or even hardware devices.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p47,2,"The project controls limited storage-life items by the expiration date indicated on any limited storage-life item. Whether dated by the manufacturer or by the project at receiving/creation, any expiration date is checked before use and recorded in the process plan or log book. For safety critical operations, this is validated by a QA sign-off. NVR 170.04.3 SAME-PLN-003 7.4.3 Systems Engineering Qualitative Maintainability Analysis The Project shall perform qualitative maintainability analysis to assure that the system is maintainable and that there is an effective maintenance capability to restore functions to their full capacity throughout the intended operational life of the system. The following maintenance design features are required: See SAME-PLN-003 for list of design features. A X A-32",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p48,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 170.04.4 SAME-PLN-003 7.4.4 Multiple Provisions For Failure Detection SAME has provisions for failure detection, annunciation, and recovery processes which include manual control of the automated processes. When functionality is restored it is confirmed. There are no catastrophic or critical hazards which require failure detection. NVR 180.02 SAME-PLN-003 8.2 QA Quality Assurance Requirements - General Requirements SAME will maintain an effective and timely Quality Assurance (QA) Program, which assures that quality requirements are met through control of design, operations, processes, procedures, testing, and inspection. The SAME Quality Assurance Program utilizes and is governed by the ZIN Technologies, Inc. Quality Management System (QMS), which is ISO 9001 certified. NVR 180.04.a SAME-PLN-003 8.4 CM Configuration Management and Verification All documents, drawings and revisions, which define and verify the system, shall be kept under configuration control. The SAME Configuration Management Plan (SAME-PLN-002) specifies responsibilities and addresses the following: See SAME-PLN-003 for more details.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p48,1,"Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 I X 180.04.b SAME-PLN-003 8.4 CM Configuration Management and Verification The System configuration items shall be placed under configuration control and base-lined at the earliest possible time. This may be at the fit/functional, qual or flight model stage, but in all cases, the system shall be baselined by CDR. Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 I X 180.04.c SAME-PLN-003 8.4 CM Configuration Management and Verification The responsible project configuration control personnel shall assure that documents are kept current, and when changes are made, they are made promptly and include changes to all associated documentation and the system. The Configuration Management Plan shall assure that only the latest drawings, including all changes, are used for the fabrication, assembly, testing and inspection of all components. Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 I XX 180.07.2 SAME-PLN-003 8.7.2 QA Training for Personnel As required by the applicable standards, only individuals qualified and/or certified to perform these activities shall be permitted to perform the work and inspection of flight hardware. This applies to assembling electrical cables, fiber optics cables, soldering, conformal coating and welding. Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 Zin's Quality System I X 180.07.4 SAME-PLN-003 8.7.4 QA Bonded Storage The SAME Project shall maintain a controlled bonded storage area, which is capable of storing flight material, parts and assemblies.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p48,2,"The level and type of environmental control shall be defined based on the specifics of the flight material, parts and assemblies being stored. The environmental control shall minimally protect the flight items from excessive temperatures and humidity, and from contamination. Electrostatic discharge (ESD) control shall be implemented for ESD sensitive parts as defined in Section 8.8. Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 Zin's Quality System I X 180.07.5 SAME-PLN-003 8.7.5 QA Records of Inspections and Tests The logbooks shall document all actions taken on the component, and shall provide for easily accessible total operating time of the component under control. Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 Zin's Quality System I X 180.08.1 SAME-PLN-003 8.8.1 QA Contamination Control Plan The SAME Project will use W09010 Contamination Control and Cleanliness Requirements as its contamination control plan. NVR A-33",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p49,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 180.09 SAME-PLN-003 8.9 QA Electrostatic Discharge Prevention SAME personnel are trained in ESD principles and control, as specified in ZIN W09007 Electrostatic Discharge (ESD) Control. This implements and maintains a program that complies with NASA-STD-8739.7. NVR 180.10 SAME-PLN-003 8.10 QA Nonconformance and Problem Reporting and Control SAME will operate a closed-loop nonconformance control system for failures and discrepancies and provide documentation of each nonconformance per the ZIN P13001 Procedure for the Control of Nonconforming Product. In this process ZIN F13001 Nonconformance Report is initiated. ZIN Quality monitors the disposition of the Nonconformance. The non-conformance is reviewed and closed-out by the responsible engineer, ZIN quality assurance, the SAME project task lead, and SAME Project Manager. This procedure also levees requirements for waivers and deviations based on the disposition of the non-conformance.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p49,1,"NVR 180.12 SAME-PLN-003 8.12 Systems Engineering Inspection and Test of Stored Limited-Life Hardware In the event that limited-life hardware items are stored or stocked, a test plan or line-item on a Process Plan will be developed which assures that limited-life items have not been degraded or damaged during storage. The plan will address proper handling, including environmental conditions, to mitigate damage or prolong life, and testing to assure the stored items meet required specifications. Limited-life items not meeting the requirements set forth in the plan will be considered nonconforming, and handled in accordance with paragraph 8.9. Limited life items will be identified on a list and a log of their remaining life maintained. (The definition and requirements for limited life items are shown in Section 7.4.2.) NVR 180.13 SAME-PLN-003 8.13 QA Metrology The SAME project will use the metrology program provided by ZIN P11001 Procedure for the Control of Inspection, Measuring, and Test Equipment. NVR 180.14 SAME-PLN-003 8.14 QA Handling, Preservation, Marking, Packaging, Packing and Transportation SAME will meet the requirements for handling, preservation, marking, packaging, packing, and transportation of all products as outlined in the developer’s QMS ZIN P15001 Procedure for Handling, Storage, Packaging, Preservation and Delivery.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p49,2,NVR 180.14.1 SAME-PLN-003 8.14.1 QA Control of Government Property by Contractors SAME will meet the requirements for Government property control as outlined in the developer’s QMS procedure ZIN P07001 Procedure for the Control of Customer-Supplied Products. This procedure meets all the requirements of SARG 8.13.1. NVR 180.15 SAME-PLN-003 8.15 QA / CM Configuration Verification The SAME Project Configuration Management (CM) System will be capable of assuring that as-built hardware conforms to the design documentation. The Quality Management Office will provide assessment of the CM system during construction of the engineering and flight systems. Formal verification and sign-off that flight as-built systems conform to as-designed documentation will be the responsibility of the project. NVR A-34,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p50,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 180.16.a SAME-PLN-003 8.16 QA Acceptance of Flight System Prior to the Pre-ship Review, the SAME project will assure that all deliverable end-items are in accordance with program/project requirements. The Acceptance Data Package will be submitted for approval and includes as a minimum the following: See SAME PLN-003 for list of included items. NVR 180.16.b SAME-PLN-003 8.16 Systems Engineering Acceptance of Flight System Acceptance Data Packages will be in accordance with SSP 30695 NVR 180.18 SAME-PLN-003 8.18 CM Control of Quality Records The SAME project shall use the procedure established by ZIN P16001, Procedure for the Control of Quality Records to manage the identification, collection, indexing, access, filing, storage, maintenance, and disposition of quality records. Quality records shall be maintained to demonstrate conformance to specified requirements and the effective operation of the quality system.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p50,1,"Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 I X 190.01 SAME-PLN-003 9.1 Systems Engineering Continuous Risk Management - General Requirements Continuous Risk Management activities shall be completed as addressed in the SAME Continuous Risk Management Plan, SAME-PLN-007 Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 Certificate of Compliance A X 190.02 SAME-PLN-003 10.1 QA / Software Engineering Software Assurance - General Requirements Software Assurance activities shall be completed as addressed in the SAME Software Management and Assurance Plan, SAME- PLN-006 Deleted by SAME-ECO-108 Certificate of Compliance A X 201.01.a Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 1.a Title Structural Failure (payloads must comply with the listed requirements for all phases of flight) Note: Locker and Soft Stowage items only. Designed to meet the standard modular locker stowage requirements of NSTS 21000-IDD-MDK or equivalent IDD SHI- SMP-S0001 BSL NVR X 201.01.a.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 1.a.1 Mechanical Engineering Zin Tech Engineering performs a weight test on the SAME hardware to assure that it meets Spacehab SHI-SMP-S0001 BSL IDD and ICD limits. For items over 30 lbs, a CG test will also be performed. TXX X X X 201.02 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 2 Title Structural Failure of Sealed Containers Note: The 1230 form is not applicable for “sealed container”(s) employed in levels of containment control of hazardous fluid Sealed containers must meet the criteria of NASA-STD- 5003, Para.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p50,2,"4.2.2.4.2.3a, contain a substance which is not a hazard if released, be made of conventional metals (e.g. Al, inconel, monel, steel or titanium), contain less than 14,240 foot-pounds (19,130 Joules) of stored energy due to pressure, and have a maximum delta pressure of 1.5 atm. NVR X 201.02.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 2.1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering Project reviews P-Trak Refill Container Assembly design to verify that it meets sealed container criteria of NASA-STD-5003, Para 4.2.2.4.2.3a. Note: This hazard is applicable to the P-Trak Refill Container Assembly, which is the only sealed container in SAME flight hardware. AX X 201.02.2 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 2.2 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA certifies that the P-Trak Refill Container Assembly is manufactured/assembled in accordance with approved drawings, parts lists and procedures. Note: This hazard is applicable to the P-Trak Refill Container Assembly, which is the only sealed container in SAME flight hardware. IXX X X X A-35",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p51,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 201.03 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 3.b Title Structural Failure of Vented Containers For intentionally vented containers, vents are sized to maintain a 1.4 factor of safety for Shuttle or a 1.5 factor of safety for Station with respect to pressure loads. Meets all of the applicable pressure rates defined for one or more of the following. b) Station environment – SSP 52005, paragraph 4.1.12 or equivalent payload specific ICD . NVR X 201.03.b.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 3(b).1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Engineering performs an analysis to verify that the vented containers (Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure, P-Trak Enclosure, Data Acquisition Control Unit (DACU), Power Distribution & Control Box, Fluid Control Unit, Dynamic Diluter, Thermal Precipitator, and the Camera Controller) can withstand the MPLM depressurization/repressurization rates of 890/800 Pa/sec (7.75/6.96 psi/min, MSFC-RQMT-2888, Section 3.1.1.2b).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p51,1,"AX X 201.03.b.2 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 3(b).2 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA inspects the as-built hardware (Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure, P-Trak Enclosure, Data Acquisition Control Unit (DACU), Power Distribution & Control Box, Fluid Control Unit, Dynamic Diluter, Thermal Precipitator, and the Camera Controller) to ensure that they are built per design drawings. IXX X X X 201.04.ad Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 4.ad Title Sharp Edges, Corners, and/or Protrusions Meets the intent of one or more of the following: a) NASA-STD-3000 / SSP 50005 d) NSTS 07700 Vol. XIV App. 9 (IVA hardware) / SSP 57000 NVR X 201.04.ad.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 4(a,d).1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering reviews drawings of SAME hardware to ensure that requirements to remove sharp corners, edges, and protrusions are included. Note: During the possible malfunction scenario of rotating the carousel by hand the crew will access the same part of the carousel they normally access for insertion into the holder which will have been verified to meet the requirements. AX X 201.04.ad.2 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 4(a,d).2 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA performs sharp-edge inspection of SAME flight hardware and record results in an inspection report. Note: During the possible malfunction scenario of rotating the carousel by hand the crew will access the same part of the carousel they normally access for insertion into the holder which will have been verified to meet the requirements.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p51,2,"IXX XX 201.05.a Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 5.a Title Shatterable Material Release a) All materials are contained. See Table: SAME Shatterable Materials attached to Standardized Hazard Report, STD-SAME-01, for list of shatterable materials. NVR X 201.05.a.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 5(a).1 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA ensures that provisions for containment (Lexan covers, Kapton tape) for SAME Experiment Support Plate components with shatterable materials (Smoke Generation Tube with LEDs, camera wide angle lens, and LED converging lens; and Dynamic Diagnostics Assembly with the ISS Smoke Detector) have been enacted and prepare a report confirming that design requirements have been met. IXX X X 201.05.a.2 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 5(a).2 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech QA inspects for provisions for containment by the Kapton tape cover over the LCDs (P-Trak and DustTrak displays). IXX X X 201.05.a.3 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 5(a).3 Operations ZIN Tech Engineering inspects flight crew procedure inputs for steps to perform on orbit inspection of the items containing glass for breakage prior to usage - the hardware will be examined by the crew for glass splinters through transparent Lexan covers before unpacking. IX X 201.05.a.4 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 5(a).4 Operations ZIN Tech Engineering inspects flight crew procedure inputs for steps to reinstall Lexan covers on the items with glass upon removal from the MSG after experiment completion. IX X A-36",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p52,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 201.05.b Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 5.b Title Shatterable Material Release b) Optical glass (i.e. lenses, filters, etc.) components of crew cabin experiment hardware that are non-stressed (no delta pressure) and have passed both a vibration test at flight levels and a post-test visual inspection. See Table: SAME Shatterable Materials attached to Standardized Hazard Report, STD-SAME-01, for list of shatterable materials. NVR X 201.05.b.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 5(b).1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering performs SAME hardware vibration test while in the soft stowage at protoflight levels (Maximum Expected Flight Level + 3dB) in stowed configuration and prepares a report confirming test was successfully completed. TXX X X 201.05.b.2 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 5(b).2 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA performs a post-test visual inspection to verify ability to survive launch loads (no glass breakage) and record results in inspection report.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p52,1,"IXX X X 201.06.ab Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 6.ab Title Flammable Materials a) A-rated materials selected from MAPTIS, or b) Flammability assessment per NSTS 22648 NVR X 201.06.ab.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 6(a,b).1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering compiles a Material Identification/Usage List (MIUL) and generates Material Usage Agreements (MUAs) to substantiate usage of all non-A rated materials. Any required flammability assessment per NSTS 22648 will be included in usage rationale of the MUAs. AX XX 201.06.ab.2 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 6(a,b).2 NASA/GRC NASA/GRC/SMAD/RMO reviews MIULs and MUAs and, when approved, provides a Material Certification Letter in accordance with M&P Intercenter Agreements. AX XX 201.07.a Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 7.a Title Materials Offgassing a) Offgassing tests of assembled article per NHB 8060.1 and/or NASA-STD-6001 NVR X 201.07.a.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 7(a).1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering arranges for off-gas testing of the flight unit in accordance with NASA-STD-6001, Test 7, and prepares test report. TX XX XX 201.07.a.2 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 7(a).2 NASA/GRC NASA/GRC/SMAD/RMO reviews off-gas test report and, when report is approved, provides a Material Certification Letter in accordance with M&P Intercenter Agreements. AX XX 201.08.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 8.1.ac Title Nonionizing Radiation Non-transmitters Meets all that apply:",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p52,2,"a) Pass ICD-2-19001, 10.7.3.2.2 / SSP 30238 EMI compatibility testing, or c) ISS/EMEP approved TIA NVR X 201.08.1.ac Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 8.1(a,c) Electrical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering performs EMI/EMC testing using the test methods/procedures defined in SSP 30238 per requirements in MSFC-RQMT-2888E and prepares a report confirming requirements have been met. TXX XX 201.08.1.c Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 8.1(c) Electrical Engineering TIA (approved by NSTS/EMEP) is processed if required. A X XX 201.08.2 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 8.2.ab Title Nonionizing Radiation Lasers a) Beams are totally contained at the maximum possible power and there is no crew access, or b) Meet ANSI Z136.1-2000 for class 1, 2, or 3a Lasers (as measured at the source). NVR X 201.08.2.ab Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 8.2(a,b) Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering reviews vendor documentation to verify that the laser beams are fully contained, there is no crew access to the lasers (DustTrak, P-Trak and ISS smoke detector) at any time, and the lasers meet ANSI Z136.1-2000 standards for Class 1, and prepares a report that all requirements above have been met. IX XX X A-37",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p53,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 201.09.b Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 9.b Title Battery Failure b) Li-CFx, Li-Iodine, Li-MnO2, which have a capacity of 300 mAh or less, and no more than 3 cells per common circuit. Pass acceptance tests which include open circuit & loaded voltage measurements, visual examination, and leakage check under vacuum (e.g. 6 hours at 0.1 psia). NVR X 201.09.b.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 9.b.1 Electrical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering conducts battery acceptance tests (flight button batteries have to undergo vacuum leak checks following with visual inspection and load check - open circuit & loaded voltage measurements), and prepares a report with test results. TXX X X 201.09.b.2 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 9.b.2 QA / Electrical Engineering ZIN QA performs a visual inspection of the batteries and records results in the inspection report after the acceptance test. IXX X X 201.10.a Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 10.a Title Touch Temperature a) Within IVA touch temperature range of –18 Degrees C. (0 Degrees F.) and 49 Degrees C. (120 Degrees F.) and satisfies the intentional contact constraints of letter MA2-95- 048 (if applicable).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p53,1,NVR X 201.10.a.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 10.a.1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering conducts the test to verify that the maximum temperature of the P-Trak Enclosure and Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure during experiment operation is such that touch temperature never exceeds a limit of 49°C. TX X X 201.10.a.2 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 10.a.2 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering conducts thermal precipitator test in the worst case condition to prove that the housing does not exceed touch temperature limit of 49°C. TX X X 201.10.a.3 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 10.a.3 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering conducts carousel (smoke tubes) test in the worst case (sample was completely burned) condition to prove that it does not exceed touch temperature limit of 49°C. TX X X 201.10.a.4 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 10.a.4 Electrical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering conducts the sample heater wire test to prove that it cools down below touch temperature within 91 seconds after current cut off. TX X X 201.10.a.5 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 10.a.5 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering conducts a test to prove that access time to a sample heater wire is greater than 91 seconds. AX XX X X 201.10.a.6 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 10.a.6 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech QA performs SAME hardware inspection to prove that the sample heater wire in operational configuration is not accessible.,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p53,2,"IX XX 201.10.a.7 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 10.a.7 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Engineering performs a thermal analysis (Fluids Control Unit, DACU, and Power Distribution and Control Unit) to verify that touch temperature under nominal conditions does not exceed touch temperature limit of 49° C. A & T X X 201.10.a.8 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 10.a.8 Electrical Engineering ZIN Engineering performs a test of the thermal shutdown control that monitors key components and removes power in the event of overtemperature due to failure of a component or loss of MSG cooling. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO-229 T X XXX 201.11.b Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 11.b Title Electrical Power Distribution b) Station-powered payloads – Meets station interface circuit protection requirements of SSP 57000 and payload circuit protection requirements of Letter TA-92-038. NVR X 201.11.b.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 11b.1 Electrical Engineering ZIN Engineering performs analysis of SAME electrical design to verify that proper circuit protective devices and wire sizing are implemented (per TA-92-038) and prepares a report confirming that ISS requirements from SSP 57000 and MSG requirements MSFC-RQMT-2888 have been met. AX X 201.11.b.2 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 11b.2 Glovebox MSG IIT reviews the integrated schematic indicating bonding and grounding methodology for compliance with SSP 30245 and SSP 30240. AX XX A-38",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p54,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 201.11.b.3 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 11b.3 QA / Electrical Engineering ZIN Tech Quality verifies that the as-built electrical circuitry is in accordance with approved drawings and parts lists and record results in an inspection report. IXX XX X 201.13 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 13 Title Rotating Equipment Rotating equipment meets criteria of NASA-STD-5003 for obvious containment. NVR X 201.13.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 13 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering reviews the SAME design to assure that the rotating equipment meets the obvious containment requirement (smaller than 8 inches in diameter max rotational velocity lower than 8000 RPM) of NASA-STD-5003, Section 4.2.2.2 (and SSP 52005 para 5.3.1.2. A.). See attached table of rotating equipment which includes diameters and velocities. See Table: SAME Rotating Equipment Table attached to Standardized Hazard Report, STD- SAME-01. AX X 201.15.b Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 15.b Title Contingency Return and Rapid Safing Shuttle Environment: b) If SPACEHAB transfer item - can establish a safe for return configuration within 3 min. (see paragraph 5 of letter MA2-96-190).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p54,1,NVR X 201.15.b.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 15(b) Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering performs analysis of SAME hardware to ensure that safe for return configuration can be established within 3 minutes for SPACEHAB transfer operations and prepares report confirming that requirements have been met. AX XX X 201.15.c Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 15.c Title Contingency Return and Rapid Safing Station Environment: c) Payload design does not impede emergency IVA egress to the remaining adjacent pressurized volumes. NVR X 201.15.c.1 Hazard Report STD-SAME-01 15(c) Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering performs analysis of SAME hardware and planned ISS flight operations and shows that the design does not impede emergency IVA egress to adjacent pressurized volumes and prepare report confirming that requirements have been met. AX XX X 203 Hazard Report SAME-03 Title Electrical Shock NVR X 203.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-03 1.1 Title MSG output feed (J302) shall be switched off by the crew using only approved crew procedures prior to mating/demating of connectors. NVR X 203.1.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-03 1.1.1 Operations ZIN Tech Engineering inspects crew procedures inputs to insure that a step is in place to remove power from the MSG power feed using the Control and Monitoring Panel (CMP). Reference PHR MSG-G2 control 4.3 and 6.2.,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p54,2,IX X 203.1.2 Hazard Report SAME-03 1.2 Title Connectors shall employ design features that completely enclose or shroud the pins and sockets during making/breaking of electrical contact. NVR X 203.1.2.1 Hazard Report SAME-03 1.2.1 Electrical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering inspects the SAME connectors for design features that completely enclose or shroud the pins and sockets. IX XX X 203.1.3 Hazard Report SAME-03 1.3 Title The powered side of the connector shall be terminated in sockets rather than pins. NVR X 203.1.3.1 Hazard Report SAME-03 1.3.1 Electrical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering inspects the SAME connectors for powered side terminating in sockets rather than pins. IX XX X 203.1.4 Hazard Report SAME-03 1.4 Title Connectors shall be keyed to prevent mis-mate. NVR X 203.1.4.1 Hazard Report SAME-03 1.4.1 Electrical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering inspects the SAME connectors for keying to prevent mis-mate. IX XX X A-39,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p55,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 203.1.5.1 Hazard Report SAME-03 1.5.1 Electrical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering inspects for a loop back of inhibit lines internal to SAME Experiment Support Plate. IXX X X X 203.2.1 Hazard Report SAME-03 2.1 Title Proper design of the SAME electrical system - 120VDC source will be isolated from case. NVR X 203.2.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-03 2.1.1 Electrical Engineering ZIN Engineering reviews the SAME electrical system design bonding and grounding methodology for compliance with SSP 30245 and SSP 30240. AX X 203.2.1.2 Hazard Report SAME-03 2.1.2 Glovebox MSG IIT reviews SAME electrical system design. A X X X 203.2.1.3 Hazard Report SAME-03 2.1.3 Electrical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering performs isolation test for the 120VDC source. TXX XX 203.2.2 Hazard Report SAME-03 2.2 Title Compliance with interpretation letter MA2-99-142, On-orbit Bonding and Grounding for satisfying bonding and grounding requirements when hardware installation occurs on orbit. NVR X 203.2.2.1 Hazard Report SAME-03 2.2.1 Electrical Engineering ZIN Engineering analyzes the design for compliance with the interpretation letter MA2-99-142 requirements to prove redundant bond paths are free of credible common cause failure modes.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p55,1,"AX X 203.2.2.2 Hazard Report SAME-03 2.2.2 Electrical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering performs integrated bonding test in the MSG Engineering Unit (per MSFC-RQMT-2888, Section 3.2.4.2). TXX XX X* 203.2.2.3 Hazard Report SAME-03 2.2.3 QA / Electrical Engineering ZIN Tech QA verifies that the as-built electrical circuitry is in accordance with approved drawings and parts lists and record results in an inspection report. IXX XX X 204 Hazard Report SAME-04 Title Rupture/leakage of Pressure Systems NVR X 204.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 1.1 Title Pressure system hardware shall be designed to the following factors of safety (FS): Lines Fittings (<1.5 in.) FS = 4 x respective MDP; other system components FS = 2.5 x respective MDP. See table of Fluid System Components attached to this Hazard Report (Table 4-2 in the Phase II SDP) for a list of components with factors of safety. NVR X 204.1.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 1.1.1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering performs an analysis to verify that SAME pressure system design is compliant to SSP 52005 Section 5.1.3 requirements. AX X 204.1.1.2 Hazard Report SAME-04 1.1.2 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech QA performs an inspection to insure flight hardware has been built and assembled in accordance with approved design drawings, parts lists, and procedures. I X X XXX X 204.1.1.3 Hazard Report SAME-04 1.1.3 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering conducts proof test of high pressure lines, fittings and components to 1.5 x MDP.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p55,2,Proof pressure test also to be performed on all hard flex lines and a representative sample of the low pressure non-metal tubing. Flight system hardware shall be leak tested at 1.0 x MDP. TXX X X 204.1.2 Hazard Report SAME-04 1.2 Title Design precludes mis-mating of pressure/vacuum hoses. NVR X 204.1.2.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 1.2.1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering performs SAME design analysis to verify that it includes features to preclude mis-mating of pressure/vacuum hoses. AX X 204.1.2.2 Hazard Report SAME-04 1.2.2 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech QA inspects SAME as-built hardware to verify that it has been built per approved drawings and procedures. IXX XX X A-40,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p56,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 204.2.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 2.1 Title Materials shall be selected to avoid stress corrosion cracking, in accordance with MSFC-HDBK-527/JSC 09604 and MAPTIS Database or if required, an MUA shall be submitted to NASA/GRC SMAD for review and approval, per M&P Intercenter agreement. NVR X 204.2.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 2.1.1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering generates the MIULs and MUAs and submits to NASA/GRC/SMAD/RMO for review per GRC Materials & Processes (M&P) Intercenter Agreement. AX XX 204.2.1.2 Hazard Report SAME-04 2.1.2 NASA/GRC NASA/GRC/SMAD/RMO reviews MIULs and MUAs and, if approved, provides a Material Certification Letter in accordance with M&P Intercenter Agreements. AX XX 204.2.2 Hazard Report SAME-04 2.2 Title Hardware shall be designed and built per the Fracture Control Requirements of NASA-STD-5003, SSP 30558, and SSP 52005. NVR X 204.2.2.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 2.2.1 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA performs ""as-built"" equipment inspection against final drawings to insure compliance with Fracture Control Plan. IXX XX X 204.2.2.2 Hazard Report SAME-04 2.2.2 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Engineering prepares Fracture Control Summary Report and submits it to JSC/ES4 for review.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p56,1,AX XX 204.2.2.3 Hazard Report SAME-04 2.2.3 JSC/ES4 JSC/ES4 reviews and approves the Fracture Control Summary Report for Phase III. IX XX 204.3.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.1 Title A regulator will reduce the ISS GN2 supply pressure (worst case 200 psi) to 15 psi. NVR X 204.3.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.1.1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Engineering conducts regulator test to verify that it reduces inlet pressure to 15 psig. TXX X X 204.3.1.2 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.1.2 QA / Mechanical Engineering Zin QA inspects flight regulator for locking of setting. I X X X X 204.3.2 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.2 Title Two relief valves will keep the pressure below 21 psi downstream. NVR X 204.3.2.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.2.1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Engineering conducts both relief valves test to verify that they control pressure below 21 psig. TXX X X 204.3.2.2 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.2.2 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA inspects SAME flight hardware for presence of two relief valves. IX XX 204.3.2.3 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.2.3 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA shall inspect flight relief valves for locking of setting. I X X X X 204.3.2.4 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.2.4 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Engineering performs an assessment of the relief valve comparing storage and on orbit time to time necessary between relief tests to prove adequate time to assure valve pressure does not drift.,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p56,2,"AX X 204.3.3 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.3 Title A pressure transducer signal processor will control a solenoid valve to keep the pressure below 21 psi downstream. NVR X 204.3.3.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.3.1 Mechanical/ Software Engineering ZIN Engineering conducts processor, pressure transducer and solenoid test to verify that they control pressure in event of failure of regulator. TXX XX 204.3.4 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.4 Title Components are protected from ISS VES back pressure of 40 psi by check valve NVR X 204.3.4.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.4.1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Engineering tests the check valve for proper function. T X X X X 204.3.5 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.5 Title Components are protected from ISS VES back pressure of 40 psi by a relief valve set at 20 psi. NVR X A-41",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p57,0,SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 204.3.5.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.5.1 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Engineering tests the relief valve for proper function. T X X X X 204.3.6 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.6 Title Components are protected from ISS VES back pressure of 40 psi by solenoid valve controlled by DACU with input from pressure transducer. NVR X 204.3.6.1 Hazard Report SAME-04 3.6.1 Mechanical/ Software Engineering ZIN Engineering tests the solenoid/DACU/pressure transducer system for proper function. TXX XX 205 Hazard Report SAME-05 Title Crew Exposure to Excessive Ionizing Radiation NVR X 205.1.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-05 1.1.1 Title Source is below acceptable limits for radiation source. NVR X 205.1.1.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-05 1.1.1.1 JSC JSC Radiation Working Group approves radiation JSC Form 44 attached [to Unique Hazard Report SAME-05]. AX XX 205.1.2.1 Hazard Report SAME-05 1.2.1 Title Commercial containment device (First Alert Smoke detector foil pack and module) is not modified. See attached manufacturer's information. See attachment to Unique Hazard Report SAME-05.,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p57,1,"NVR X 205.1.2.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-05 1.2.1.1 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA performs visual examination of the smoke detector installed in the Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure housing to verify that the device is properly contained and not accessible to the crew, that there is not any indication of changes and/or leakage and record all results in an inspection report. IXX X X 205.1.3.1 Hazard Report SAME-05 1.3.1 Title Commercial containment device is also contained inside of Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure housing and is inaccessible to the crew. NVR X 205.1.3.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-05 1.3.1.1 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA verifies that the smoke detector is installed in the Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure housing per approved design documents and record results in an inspection report. IXX X X X 205.1.3.2 Hazard Report SAME-05 1.3.2 Title Smoke detector is handled on ground using approved procedures. NVR X 205.1.3.2.1 Hazard Report SAME-05 1.3.2.1 QA / Electrical Engineering ZIN QA inspects and approves ground procedures used for handling ionizing radiation source (from smoke detector). IX X 205.2.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-05 2.1.1 Title Ionization portion of the Unit is not modified and is maintained by NASA Shuttle Logistics Depot (NSLD) at KSC services. NVR X 205.2.1.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-05 2.1.1.1 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA inspects the flight STS detector for damage before stowage and inspect paperwork concerning maintenance.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p57,2,"IX XX X 208 Hazard Report SAME-08 Title Release of the Pyrolysis Products into the Habitable Environment NVR X 208.1 Hazard Report SAME-08 1 Title Use of small amount of toxic materials, which do not have a potential to produce a hazardous amount of pyrolysis products. NVR X 208.1.1 Hazard Report SAME-08 1.1 NASA/JSC NASA/JSC/Toxicology Group determines toxicological level of SAME sample materials and their pyrolysis products. AX XX 208.1.2 Hazard Report SAME-08 1.2 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA performs an inspection to verify that type and quantity of sample materials comply with experiment protocol as consistent with the performed Toxicological assessment. IXX X X 208.2 Hazard Report SAME-08 2 Title The SAME hardware contains HEPA filters to trap the pyrolysed sample products (particulate). NVR X 208.2.1 Hazard Report SAME-08 2.1 QA / Mechanical Engineering ZIN QA performs an inspection of the SAME hardware effluent outlets for inclusion of the HEPA filters. IX XX X A-42",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p58,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 208.2.2 Hazard Report SAME-08 2.2 Mechanical Engineering ZIN Tech Engineering performs an analysis of the MSG system filters for compatibility with the SAME sample materials and their pyrolysis products. AX XX 311.1.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.1.1.a Mechanical Engineering Load Requirements Investigations will be designed to withstand the launch and landing conditions when stowed in the MPLM, Middeck or other transportation vehicles. For design consideration the Investigation will use the acceleration loads defined in Table I when stowed in the MPLM or Middeck. These loads are based on a worst case condition of the Middeck and MPLM. The IIT will provide the investigator with the acceleration and random vibration data of the carrier the investigation is manifested on. Additional requirements or data may be required depending on the configuration of the investigation and the requirements of the carrier. It is assumed that the loads imposed by the random vibration environment on stowed items in foam can be neglected in design consideration. The Investigation will conduct a workmanship test, for instance on electrical components or mechanisms, based on the random vibration environment defined in Table I.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p58,1,The Investigation must ensure that the hardware will survive the workmanship random vibration level. NVR 311.1.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.1.1.b Mechanical Engineering Load Requirements Investigations will be limited to ground transportation accelerations of 80% of the flight loads identified in paragraph 3.1.1.1.a of this IIRD. NVR 311.1.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.1.1.c Mechanical Engineering Load Requirements Investigations shall be designed to withstand the on-orbit loads of 0.2 Gs acting in any direction. T or A ST-001 X X X 311.1.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.1.1.d Mechanical Engineering Load Requirements Investigation hardware located outside of the WV shall be designed to maintain positive margins of safety when exposed to (ultimate and yield) the crew-induced loads as defined in Table II. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A X ST-002 311.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.1.2.a Mechanical Engineering Additional Investigation Requirements Investigations shall be limited to a control weight for on-orbit operations of 200 kg (440 lbs). MSG provided equipment in the WV shall be included in this on-orbit weight limit. Investigation control mass shall be documented in the Investigation Increment Specific ICD. T & A ME-001 X X 311.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.1.2.b Mechanical Engineering Additional Investigation Requirements Investigations shall be designed to withstand the MPLM depress rate of 890 Pa/sec (7.75 psi/min) and repress rate of 800 Pa/sec (6.96 psi/min).,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p58,2,"A X ST-003 X 311.2.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.1.2.c Mechanical Engineering Additional Investigation Requirements Investigation equipment will be designed not to create a hazard when exposed to the Portable Fire Extinguisher (PFE) discharge. NVR 311.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.1.3 Mechanical Engineering Attachment Provisions The Investigation shall participate in a physical and integrated interface verification of the investigation hardware at least once during the development and integration process per the MSG Investigation Integration Plan, MSFC-PLAN-3052. The integrated test requires that the investigation test in its flight configuration and verify all interfaces with the MSG. Investigation size relative to the MSG work volume may require an earlier fit check to ensure that margins are not compromised. These tests will require delivery of the investigation hardware to MSFC. A Customer Agreement per MPR4000.1 is necessary for IIT to process any investigation hardware at MSFC. See MSFC- RQMT-2888 for more information. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & I X ME-046 X X X* A-43",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p59,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 312.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.2.1 Mechanical Engineering Quasi-Steady Requirements For frequencies below 0.01 Hz, Investigations shall limit unbalanced transitional average impulse to generate less than 10 lb-s (44.8 N-s) within any 10 to 500 second period along any ISS coordinate system vector. There are two sources of Quasi- Steady disturbances to consider: venting and mass motion. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 T or A EN-005 312.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.2.2 Mechanical Engineering Vibratory Requirements Between 0.01 Hz and 300 Hz, Investigations shall limit vibration so that the limits of Figure 6 and Table III are not exceeded using the force method. The total force applied to the MSG Work Volume at all interface point will be calculated to be the root- summed squared (RSS) of all axes, within each third octave band, during the worst case 100 second interval. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 T or A EN-005 312.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.2.3 Mechanical Engineering Transient Requirements Investigations shall limit force applied to the MSG over any ten second period to an impulse of no greater than 10 lb-s (44.5 N- s).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p59,1,"Investigations shall limit their peak force applied to the MSG to less than 1000 lb (4448 N) for any duration. Note: Meeting the transient requirements does not obviate the need to also meet the 100 second vibration requirement of 3.1.2.2 for vibration included in and following the transient disturbance. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 T or A EN-005 313.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.3 Mechanical Engineering Stowage Input Requirements Investigations requiring individual stowage shall address their requirements for stowage planning. Stowage items requiring Late or Early access, special environmental control or orientation requirements while in stowage will be worked with the MSG IIT on a case by case basis. The Investigation's stowage requirements shall be documented in the Unique Investigation ICD. A ME-001 X X X 313.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.3 Mechanical Engineering Stowage Input Requirements Investigations shall provide the IIT with engineering sketches or drawings showing the individual hardware items to be stowed dimensions, part number and part name. These drawings will reside in the Unique Investigation ICD and shall be used to determine that the volume allocation in the ICD is not exceeded. A ME-001 X X X 313.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.3 Mechanical Engineering Stowage Input Requirements Investigations shall provide the IIT with the mass of each individual hardware item being stowed.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p59,2,"The mass of the hardware items shall be included in the Unique Investigation ICD and shall be used to determine that the mass allocation in the ICD is not exceeded. Hardware items weighing greater than 30 lbs (13.6 kg) being stowed in the Middeck and 50 lbs (22.7 kg) in the MPLM shall provide center of gravity parameters. Moment of inertia and products of inertia are required for items above 50 lbs. The investigator is referred to NSTS 21000-IDD-MDK for size constraints concerning the Middeck lockers and SSP 50467, Cargo Stowage Technical manual: Pressurized Volume, for additional stowage interface information. Need center of gravity. T & A X ME-001 X X X X X 313.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.1.3 Mechanical Engineering Stowage Input Requirements Any waste/trash generated by the Investigations shall be identified for planning of dedicated handling & treatment. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 119; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A ME-001 X X X A-44",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p60,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 321 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.1 Electrical Engineering Electrical Power Characteristics The total power consumption from all Investigation voltages shall not exceed 1000 Watts (i.e. if an Investigation is using 120 volts at 8.3 amps, then none of the other voltages available to the Investigation can be used at that time). Investigation power requirements are documented in the Investigation ICD and verified by a power draw test that the values documented in the ICD are not exceeded. The Investigation hardware connected to any of the above power sources are required to be compatible with characteristics prescribed herein. “Compatible” is defined as not producing an unsafe condition or one that may result in damage to ISS equipment, the MSG integrated rack, or Investigation hardware. Close prior to integrated test. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T X EL-006 X X X X* X X 321.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.1.1 Electrical Engineering Steady-State Voltage Characteristics The Investigation Electrical Power Consuming Equipment (EPCE) shall be compatible with steady-state voltage limits defined in Table IV. Close prior to integrated test. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p60,1,"T EL-001 X X X* 321.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.1.2.1 Electrical Engineering Ripple Voltage and Noise The Investigation EPCE shall operate and be compatible with the RPDA time domain ripple voltage and noise levels defined in Table V. A EL-002 X X 321.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.1.2.2 Electrical Engineering Ripple Voltage Spectrum The Investigation EPCE connected to the 120 Vdc WV primary power source shall operate and be compatible with the ripple voltage spectrum as shown in Figure 8. Note: This limit is 6dB below the EMC CS-01, CS-02 requirement in 3.2.4.4 up to 30MHz. A EL-002 X X 321.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.1.3 Electrical Engineering Transient Voltages The Investigation EPCE connected to the 120 Vdc WV primary power source shall operate and be compatible with the limits of magnitude and duration for the voltage transients as shown in Figure 9. The envelope shown in this figure applies to the transient responses exclusive of any periodic ripple and/or random noise components that may be present. T or A EL-003 X X X 321.3.1.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.1.3.1.a Electrical Engineering Fault Clearing and Protection The Investigation EPCE connected to the 120 Vdc WV primary power source shall be safe and not suffer damage with the fault clearing and protection transient voltage limits shown in Figure 10.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p60,2,"A EL-004 X X 321.3.1.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.1.3.1.b Electrical Engineering Fault Clearing and Protection The Investigation EPCE connected to the 28 Vdc WV secondary power source shall operate and be compatible with the limits of magnitude and duration for the voltage transients as shown in Figure 11. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A EL-004 321.3.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.1.3.2 Electrical Engineering Non-normal Voltage Range The Investigation EPCE connected to the 120 Vdc WV primary power source shall not produce an unsafe condition or one that could result in damage to ISS equipment, MSG equipment or Investigation hardware with the non-normal voltage characteristics: a. Maximum over-voltage of +165 Vdc for 10 sec. b. Undervoltage of +102 Vdc for an indefinite period of time. A EL-005 X 322.1.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.1.a Electrical Engineering Primary Power Connector The WV Investigation primary power connector (J302) is supplied on a MS27656P17F6SA connector in the WV rear wall as shown in Figure 2. The connector characteristics and contact assignments are listed in Table VI. The nominal voltage levels provided via this outlet are the ones available at the UIP reduced by a voltage drop inside the MSG of ~ 1.5VDC which results in nominal levels between 114.5 to 124.5 VDC. NVR A-45",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p61,0,SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 322.1.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.1.b Electrical Engineering Primary Power Connector The Investigation provided connector (P302) shall be of type MS27467T17F6PA and shall be compatible with the contact assignments defined in Table VI. Close prior to integrated test. Demonstration done in EU. I & D EL-007 X* X 322.1.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.1.c Electrical Engineering Primary Power Connector The primary power connector contains an inhibit circuit via a solid state switch in the CMP (see Figure 12). Contacts D and E shall be bridged inside the Investigation circuitry to assure proper operation of the inhibit. It is required that the investigation place the inhibit link in the connector. Close prior to integrated test. Demonstration done in EU. I & D EL-007 X X X* 322.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.2.a Electrical Engineering Secondary Power Connector The WV Investigation secondary power connectors (J303/J325) are supplied on two MS27656P21F11S connectors in the WV rear wall as shown in Figure 2. The connector characteristics and contact assignments are listed in Tables VII and VIII.,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p61,1,NVR 322.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.2.b Electrical Engineering Secondary Power Connector The Investigation provided connectors (P303/P325) shall be of type MS27467T21F11P and shall be compatible with the contact assignments defined in Tables VII and VIII. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. Demonstration done in EU. I & D EL-007 322.2.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.2.c Electrical Engineering Secondary Power Connector The secondary power connectors contains an inhibit circuit via a solid state switch in the CMP (see Figure 12). Contacts H and K shall be bridged inside the Investigation circuitry to assure proper operation of the inhibit. It is required that the investigation place the inhibit link in the connector. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. Demonstration done in EU. I & D EL-007 322.2.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.2.d Electrical Engineering Secondary Power Connector Investigations will always apply 20% of the load being applied on the -12V line to the +12V line when using the -12V alone. NVR 322.3.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.3.a Electrical Engineering Surge Current Investigation hardware connected to the 120 VDC WV primary power source available in the WV shall not exceed the surge current limits at the power inputs as defined in Figures 13 and 14. The duration of the surge current will not exceed 10 ms. These requirements apply to all operating modes and changes including power-up and power-down. Note:,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p61,2,"The MSG 120 VDC switching hardware does not provide soft ON/OFF characteristics. Investigation hardware with capacitive input filters may have to incorporate a soft start design in order to meet the ISS surge current requirements. Surge current measurements shall be taken during the integrated test, for all investigations using the primary power source, with the MSG engineering unit to insure ISS requirements are met for Surge Current. Close prior to integrated test. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & A EL-010 X X X X* 322.3.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.3.b Electrical Engineering Surge Current Investigation hardware connected to the 28 Vdc WV secondary power source available in the WV shall limit their maximum inrush current to ≤ 2 x INOM (Investigation’s nominal operating current draw) for a duration of ≤ 10 µs. This requirement applies to all operating modes and changes including power-up and power-down. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & A EL-010 322.3.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.3.c Electrical Engineering Surge Current Investigation hardware connected to the ±12V & 5V secondary power source available in the WV shall limit their maximum inrush current to 2X nominal operating current for up to 10ms. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & A EL-010 A-46",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p62,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 322.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.4 Electrical Engineering Reverse Current Investigation hardware connected to the 120 Vdc primary power source available in the WV must comply with the requirements defined in paragraph 3.2.2.4.1 or 3.2.2.4.2 for the reverse current into the upstream power source. Note: Investigations having input connected capacitances, the aggregate total of which is less than 25 micro-farads, are considered meeting the limits of paragraph 3.2.2.4.1 for US Type I RPCMs. Input connected capacitors are those which, by virtue of circuit arrangement, are able to discharge through the power source when the power source is short circuited. Series connected rectifier diodes and resistors greater than 10 Ohms are sufficient to block such discharges. NVR 322.4.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.4.1 Electrical Engineering Reverse Current Limits Investigations using the 120V DC primary power source shall limit reverse current transients that can occur when a hard fault occurs across the power source within the applicable transient envelope shown in Figures 15, 16 and 17. For purposes of this interface definition, the fault is 10 milli Ohms or less applied within 2 micro-seconds or less.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p62,1,"For the investigation hardware exhibiting reverse current transient peaks within ±100 Amperes, the fault resistance is 40 milli Ohms or less. A EL-011 X 322.4.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.4.2 Electrical Engineering Transients Partially Contained Within the Envelope If the reverse current on the 120V DC primary power source exceeds the envelope limits defined in paragraph 3.2.2.4.1 for one or more short time intervals, the transient peak current and the ratio of the times of intersection with the envelope shall satisfy the following inequality: (ipk/ie)2 x ln(t2/t1) ≤ 1 where ln represents the natural logarithm, t1 and t2 correspond to the beginning and end times, respectively, of each interval when the transient is outside the envelope, ipk is the peak of the transient occurring between t2 and t1, and ie is the point on the envelope at the time of the peak ipk. For multiple intervals in which the envelope is exceeded, the left-hand side of this expression will be evaluated for each interval and the sum of all such results will total less than unity. See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information. A EL-011 X 322.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.5 Title Circuit Protection Devices The RPDA 120 Vdc output modules provide local and remote over-current protection for the 120 Vdc WV primary power outlets and secondary WV power outlets. After tripping no automatic reset will be performed.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p62,2,NVR 322.5.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.5.a Electrical Engineering Circuit Protection Devices Investigation hardware connected to the 120 Vdc WV primary power source shall be compatible with the trip characteristics for the 120 Vdc power outlet given in Figure 19. Close prior to integrated test. A X EL-012 X 322.5.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.5.b Electrical Engineering Circuit Protection Devices Investigation hardware connected to the 28 Vdc WV secondary power source shall be compatible with the trip characteristics of the 28 Vdc WV secondary power source specified in Figure 20. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. A X EL-012 322.5.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.5.c Electrical Engineering Circuit Protection Devices Investigation hardware connected to the 5V secondary power source shall be compatible with a maximum inrush current of 2X nominal operating current for up to 10ms. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. A X EL-012 322.5.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.5.d Electrical Engineering Circuit Protection Devices Investigation hardware connected to the ±12V secondary power source shall be compatible with a maximum inrush current of 2X nominal operating current for up to 10ms. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. A X EL-012 A-47,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p63,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 322.7 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.7 Electrical Engineering Investigation Complex Load Impedances The load impedance presented by the investigation hardware connected to the 120 VDC interface shall not exceed the bounds defined in Figure 21 for input over the frequency range of 50 Hz to 100kHz. The magnitude component of the EPCE input impedance should not be less than the minimum defined in Figure 21. At frequencies where the magnitude component of the EPCE input impedance is less than the defined minimum, the phase component of the input impedance shall not exceed the bounds defined in this Figure. Impedance measurements shall be taken during the integrated test, for all investigations using the primary power source, with the MSG engineering unit to insure ISS requirements are met for Complex Load Impedance. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p63,1,"T EL-014 X X X* 322.8 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.8 Electrical Engineering Large Signal Stability The investigation hardware connected to the 120 VDC interface shall maintain stability with the ISS electrical power system interface by damping a transient response to 10 percent of the maximum response amplitude within 1.0 millisecond, and remaining below 10 percent thereafter under the following conditions: 1. The rise time/fall time (between 10 and 90 percent of the amplitude) of the input voltage pulse is less than 10 microseconds. 2. The voltage pulse is to be varied from 100 to 150 microseconds in duration. Note: Figure 22 is used to clarify the above requirement. Large Signal Stability measurements shall be taken during the integrated test, for all investigations using the primary power source, with the MSG engineering unit to insure ISS requirements are met for Large Signal Stability. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & A EL-023 X X X* 322.9 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.2.9 Electrical Engineering Electrical Load-Stand Alone Stability An Investigation connected to the RPDA power sources available in the WV shall provide local stability by meeting the following conducted susceptibility requirements: a. Section 3.2.4.4.2.2.1 (CS01) b. Section 3.2.4.4.2.2.2 (CS02) c.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p63,2,"Section 3.2.4.4.2.2.3 (CS06) A EL-016 X X 323.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.3.1 Electrical Engineering Wire Derating Derating criteria for Investigation hardware at and downstream of the RPDA shall be per NASA Technical Memo (TM) 102179 as interpreted by NSTS 18798, TA-92-038. A X EL-017 X 323.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.3.2 Electrical Engineering Loss of Power Investigations shall fail safe in the event of total or partial loss of power regardless of the availability of back-up power in accordance with paragraph 200.4a of NSTS 1700.7, ISS Addendum. Investigations shall remain safe until nominal operations are returned or investigation is terminated. Closed when safety verification is closed. (PSRP) A X (Safety) X X X 324.0 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4 Electrical Engineering Electromagnetic Compatibility (EMC) Investigation hardware shall meet the EMC requirements specified herein. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.1.0.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.a Electrical Engineering Electrical Grounding/Isolation Definition of Overall Grounding Concept: Investigation hardware connected to the RPDA power sources available in the WV shall be designed to prevent intentional electrical current from flowing in ground references except under fault conditions. Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 XX X A-48",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p64,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 324.1.0.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.b Electrical Engineering Electrical Grounding/Isolation Definition of Overall Grounding Concept: The electrical design shall be such that the electromagnetic environment due to grounding is not configuration dependent. Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 XX X 324.1.0.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.c Electrical Engineering Electrical Grounding/Isolation Definition of Overall Grounding Concept: Equipment external electrical signal and power grounds shall be dc isolated from each other at the Orbital Replaceable Unit (ORU) level. Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 XX X 324.1.0.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.d Electrical Engineering Electrical Grounding/Isolation Definition of Overall Grounding Concept: Each separately derived electrical power source shall be electrically connected to structure at no more than one point. Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 XX X 324.1.0.e MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.e Electrical Engineering Electrical Grounding/Isolation Definition of Overall Grounding Concept: Analog and digital signal grounds external to a system, subsystem, or equipment shall be electrically isolated from each other at the ORU level.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p64,1,"Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 XX X 324.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.1 Title Electrical Power For the purpose of this document, when ‘primary’ power is mentioned, it is referring to the MSG Facility-Furnished 120Vdc experiment interface while ‘secondary’ power refers to the MSG Facility-Furnished 28Vdc, ±12Vdc and 5Vdc interfaces. NVR 324.1.1.1.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.1.1.a Electrical Engineering Primary and Secondary Electrical Power The MSG primary and secondary electrical power systems are distributed single point grounded by ISS or MSG. The Investigation shall not ground the primary or secondary returns. Users of power shall be direct current (dc) isolated such that the primary electrical power ground configuration is not dependent on the presence or absence of flight elements, systems, subsystems, equipment, or users. Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 XX X 324.1.1.1.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.1.1.b Electrical Engineering Primary and Secondary Electrical Power Primary and secondary electrical power is single point grounded in the MSG facility rack. All users of these primary and secondary powers must keep the primary and secondary power high side and return isolated by ≥ 1MΩ from case and structure.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p64,2,"NVR 324.1.1.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.1.2.a Electrical Engineering Tertiary or Converted Electrical Power Tertiary power is any experiment-converted power that is converted from and isolated (by 1 megohm or more) from the MSG Facility 120 Vdc Primary Power or the MSG Facility 28 Vdc, ± 12 Vdc, or 5 Vdc Secondary Power to any other voltage used in the experiment equipment. a. Tertiary Power shall be isolated by 1 megohm or more from MSG Facility 120 Vdc Primary Power and MSG Facility 28 Vdc, ± 12 Vdc, and 5 Vdc Secondary Power. Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 XX X 324.1.1.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.1.2.b Electrical Engineering Tertiary or Converted Electrical Power b. When tertiary power is used only in the box where it is converted the developer has the option of either grounding the tertiary power to the case (or just outside the box) or leaving the tertiary power floating (not connected to case or structure). Reminder: see a) above. NVR 324.1.1.2.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.1.2.c Electrical Engineering Tertiary or Converted Electrical Power c. When tertiary power is used in another box it shall be grounded either to the box or nearby structure where the power is converted (the preferred method) or to the box (or nearby structure) where the power is used. The power shall be single point grounded. Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 XX X A-49",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p65,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 324.1.1.2.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.1.2.d Electrical Engineering Tertiary or Converted Electrical Power d. The developer has the option of grounding the tertiary power to the case inside the box or it may be grounded outside the box by a wire directly connected from the return to the nearest available structure. NVR 324.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.2 Title Returns NVR 324.1.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.2.1 Electrical Engineering Signal Circuit Return Grounding Signal circuit conductors shall be dc isolated from chassis, structure, and any other grounded power return, by a minimum of 1 megohm, individually, except at the circuit’s single point ground. This single point is usually the ground for the power supply that provides power to the signal’s sending end. An exception to the 1 megohm requirement for balanced circuits is found in paragraph 3.2.4.1.2.6. An exception for RF signals on coaxial cable is found in paragraph 3.2.4.1.2.7. Close prior to integrated test.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p65,1,"T & A X EL-020 EL-021 XX X 324.1.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.2.2 Electrical Engineering Alternating Current Power Return A neutral return wire shall accompany the alternating current input wires to individual equipment loads in the distribution of power. For the three-phase Wye ac power system the neutral shall be single point grounded at the power source. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 229; Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 324.1.2.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.2.3 Electrical Engineering Analog, Differential Circuit Return Each differential analog circuit shall employ a separate return. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 229; Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 324.1.2.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.2.4 Electrical Engineering Discrete Returns Low-level discrete signals shall use individual returns. Low level usually means signals with a supply voltage of approximately 5 volts. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 229; Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 324.1.2.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.2.5 Electrical Engineering Pulse or Clock Circuit Returns All digital, pulse, or clock circuits that do not use fiber-optic cabling shall use individual returns. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 229; Close prior to integrated test.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p65,2,"T & A X EL-020 EL-021 324.1.2.6 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.2.6 Electrical Engineering Returns, Signals Below Four Megahertz (an exception to 3.2.4.1.2.1) Balanced signal circuits external to equipment with frequency content below four megahertz shall be isolated from chassis, structure, and any other power return by a minimum of 6,000 ohms, individually (i.e., measured per connection, pin, wire, etc.). A balanced circuit means that the impedances from high and low sides to case or structure are equal. Typical circuits have balanced receiving ends and unbalanced sending ends. If the impedance through the sending end is very low (1%) compared with the isolation impedance at the receiving end, it is considered a balanced circuit. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 229; Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 324.1.2.7 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.1.2.7 Electrical Engineering Signals Equal to and Above Four Megahertz (an exception to 3.2.4.1.2.1) Signal circuits with frequency components equal to or above four megahertz shall use controlled impedance transmission and reception media such as shielded twisted 72-ohm cable, ""twin- ax"" cable, ""tri-ax"" cable, or ""co-ax"" cable. Circuits using ""twin-ax"" cable shall be balanced and referenced to primary structure at a single point.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p65,3,"""Tri-ax"" cable shall use the center and inner shield conductors for unbalanced transmission, referenced to primary structure at a single point with the outer shield multipoint grounded as an ""overshield"". dc isolated, single-ended circuits coupled by coaxial cable with the shield terminated 360 degrees at each end and at available intermediate points, shall be permitted for signals with the lowest frequency component equal to or above 4 MHz. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 229; Close prior to integrated test. T & A X EL-020 EL-021 A-50",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p66,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 324.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2 Electrical Engineering Electrical Bonding Investigation hardware connected to the RPDA power sources available in the WV shall be bonded in accordance with the following requirements. Electrical bonding is the process of providing good electrical connection across mechanical interfaces to minimize electrical potential differences between equipment and individual parts of structure. Good electrical bonding provides: Fault current paths for protection against fire and personnel shock, Provides a current path for radio frequencies for proper operation of filters and shields, and a means to prevent or safely discharge static charges. Electrical bonds are classified according to the purpose for the bond. There may be more than one purpose for bonding a specific interface, and the bond shall meet the requirements of each applicable class. Notes shall be provided on assembly drawings indicating the applicable class or classes. The material used in the MSG work volume is 7075 aluminum nickel struck. A bonding test shall be performed during integrated testing with the Engineering Unit. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p66,1,"T & I & A X EL-020 EL-022 X X X* X 324.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.1 Electrical Engineering Classes of Application Where a single bond is used to serve two or more classes of application, the design shall conform to the more stringent requirement of bonding. Investigation hardware providers shall assess the function of the device, equipment, structure or interface in question and apply the appropriate class of bond. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & I & A X EL-020 EL-022 X X X* X 324.2.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.1.1 Electrical Engineering Class H Bonding (Shock and Fault Protection) Fault current, due to short circuits to equipment case or structure, may cause shock or fire hazards. All electrically conductive equipment cases that may develop potentials due to short circuits shall be electrically bonded to structure. The bond shall minimize shock hazard voltages and allow proper operation of circuit protection devices such as circuit breakers or fuses. Bonding of structural joints in the fault current return path shall provide for the maximum current that may be delivered by the power supply until the fuse or circuit breaker disconnects. For personnel and fire safety the fault current return path shall be capable of conducting a minimum of 500% overload current for a time period of 0.5 second. Typical personnel protection breakers will trip within 0.2 second after a hard short to case.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p66,2,"Resultant voltage on the enclosure of the affected component must not exceed 4.5 volts, and no fire or damage to the bond is allowed. See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & I & A X X EL-020 EL-022 X X X* X A-51",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p67,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 324.2.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.1.2 Electrical Engineering Class R Bonding All electrical components which use or produce electromagnetic energy shall be installed to provide a continuous low impedance path from the equipment enclosure to the conductive structure. The Investigation shall demonstrate by test or analysis that the proposed bonding method results in a dc resistance of less than 2.5 milliohms across each faying surface in the bond path from enclosure to structure and a total bond path impedance of less than 100 milliohms up to a frequency of 1 megahertz. Tests have shown that bonding joints that consist of direct metal-to-metal contact over a large area (greater than 1 square inch) of the mating surfaces meet the RF impedance requirement. The bond from the equipment enclosure to the mounting plate furnished with the equipment shall also comply with these requirements, except that a suitable flat ground strap may be used across any necessary vibration isolators or other environment isolators. See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p67,1,"T & I & A X X EL-020 EL-022 X X X* X 324.2.1.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.1.3 Title Class S Bonding (Static Charge ) NVR 324.2.1.3.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.1.3.1 Electrical Engineering Conducting Structural Items All electrically conductive structural items having an area greater than 100 square centimeters which carry fluids in motion, or otherwise are subject to frictional charging or plasma–induced current flow or charging, shall have a mechanically secure conducting path to conductive structure. The resistance of the path shall be less than 1 ohm. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & I & A X X EL-020 EL-022 X X X* X 324.2.1.3.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.1.3.3 Electrical Engineering Conductive Mechanical Subassemblies/Parts All moving parts having a surface area greater than 100 square centimeters and which are subject to frictional charging (charging mechanism required), e.g., gears, cams, rotary joints, etc., shall be equipped with a charge bleed-off mechanism. This mechanism may take the form of bleed wire, wiper strap, conductive lubricant, etc. The bleed-off path shall not exceed 1000 ohms to conductive structure. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p67,2,"T & I & A X X EL-020 EL-022 X X X* X 324.2.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.4 Electrical Engineering Methods of Bonding Equipment and structure with metal-to-metal joints that are joined by processes that transform the mated surfaces into one piece of metal, such as by welding or brazing, are considered permanent and inherently bonded. Semi-permanent joints are held together by screws, rivets, clamps, etc. Semi-permanent mating surfaces shall be cleaned of all insulating material before connection to provide a good electrical bond. Clamping pressure across the joint shall be adequate to assure a secure mechanical connection. Fasteners or their threads shall not be used as primary bonding paths. Self-tapping screws; zinc-plated bolts, nuts, or screws; star, anodized, or zinc-plated washers; or any cadmium-plated hardware shall not be used for bonding purposes. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & I & A X X EL-020 EL-022 X X X* X A-52",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p68,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 324.2.4.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.4.1.2 Electrical Engineering Jumpers/Straps Bond straps or jumpers may be used to meet Class “H” or “S” bonding requirements. The usefulness of bond straps or jumpers for Class “R” bonds is very limited. Straps for Class “R” bonds shall be used only as a last resort. Bonding straps installed across shock mounts or other suspension or support devices shall not impede the performance of the mounting device. They shall be capable of withstanding the anticipated motion and vibration requirements without suffering metal fatigue or other failure. Extra care should be utilized in the attachment of the ends of bonding straps to prevent arcing or other means of electrical noise generation with movements of the strap. Bond straps or wires shall be sized to carry fault current. T & I & A X X EL-020 EL-022 X X X* X 324.2.4.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.4.2 Electrical Engineering Bonding Connections Bonding connections shall be installed so that vibration, expansion, contraction, or relative movement incident to normal service use will not break or loosen the connection to such an extent that the resistance will vary during the movement.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p68,1,"Bonding connections shall be located in a protected area, or accessible locations to permit rapid inspection or replacement. The following conditions shall also apply: See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & I & A X X EL-020 EL-022 X X X* X 324.2.4.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.4.5 Electrical Engineering Dissimilar Metals When the joining of dissimilar metals cannot be avoided, the most anodic (higher in the galvanic series) of the metals should be replaceable. Where the parts are not replaceable, the electrolyte contact area of the most anodic metal should be larger than that of the cathodic metal. Select small parts such as bolts and nuts of material compatible with the cathode. An intermediate metal should be placed between two metals that are far apart in the galvanic series. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & I & A X EL-020 EL-022 X X X* X 324.2.4.8 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.4.8 Electrical Engineering Unapproved Bonding Methods Antifriction bearings, wire-mesh vibration cushion mounts, or lubricated bushings shall not be used as a bonding path. Piano hinges shall not be relied upon a bonding path. Chemical conversion coatings shall not be used on internal removable bonding surfaces. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p68,2,"T & I & A X EL-020 EL-022 X X X* X 324.2.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.5 Electrical Engineering Bonding Faying Surface Preparation The mating, (faying), surfaces of all electrically bonded metal-to- metal joints shall be cleaned of all nonconductive materials and protected against corrosion. T & I & A X X EL-020 EL-022 XX X 324.2.6.3.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.2.6.3.2 Electrical Engineering Aluminum Alloy For nonremovable bonding surfaces, aluminum alloys shall be treated with: a chemical conversion coating conforming to Class 3 of MIL–C–5541. For removable bonding surfaces internal to the ISS, plated metallic finishes are required. Nickel plating is preferred, with adequate corrosion protection provided to counteract the dissimilar metal couple created. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & I & A X EL-020 EL-022 X X X* X 324.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3 Electrical Engineering Cable/Wire Design and Control Requirements The following information is provided to the investigator for the reduction of electromagnetic effects in the wiring. This can be accomplished by isolating incompatible circuits via wire cable bundling, routing, shielding, separation, and wire treatment requirements presented herein. NVR 324.3.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1 Title Characteristics NVR 324.3.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.1 Title Circuit Classification The following criteria will be applied to determine the appropriate circuit classification for each circuit. NVR A-53",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p69,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 324.3.1.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.1.1 Electrical Engineering Frequency or Rise/Fall Time For Table IX, EMC Classification, Wire Type, and Shield Grounding classification purposes, the fundamental component of steady state operation will determine the frequency of the circuit unless the rise/fall time (pulsed wave forms) is less than 10 microseconds. If the rise/fall time is less than 10 microseconds and the voltage is less than 6 volts, the circuit shall be classified as Radio Frequency (RF) regardless of the fundamental frequency. Power circuits which are switched on and off will be classified as HO or EO even if their rise/fall times are less than 10 microseconds. NVR 324.3.1.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.1.2 Electrical Engineering Impedance The actual impedance of the interconnecting circuit, i.e., the complex ""Z"" that includes resistance, inductance and capacitance, will be used to classify the source and load impedances using Table IX. These source and load impedances determine the magnetic and electric field coupling mode category.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p69,1,"The equivalent circuit pickup resistance, i.e., the sum of the load and equivalent reactive/resistive components, will be used to identify potential areas for magnetic or electric field coupling. NVR 324.3.1.1.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.1.3 Electrical Engineering Voltage The maximum peak-to-peak voltage appearing at the source of each circuit will be used to determine the circuit classification using Table IX. NVR 324.3.1.1.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.1.4 Electrical Engineering Sensitivity If the interface circuit is susceptible to magnetic or electric field coupling that can cause induced noise with amplitudes less than the source voltage that will affect measurement and/or conversion accuracy, circuit classification will be based upon circuit sensitivity considerations rather than upon frequency, rise/fall time, impedance, or voltage. Circuits assigned a classification based on sensitivity rather than on source voltage will be identified on all wiring diagrams containing such circuits. NVR 324.3.1.1.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.1.5 Electrical Engineering Signal Type and Wire Type Table IX describes the classification of signal types and required wire type to control cable coupled interference. Cable and wire treatment will be based on Table IX and the wire/cable and isolation requirements.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p69,2,NVR 324.3.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2 Electrical Engineering Classification/Wiring Procedure The following requirements will be applied to determine the classification of wiring treatment and installation of all interface circuit types: Step 1 - Circuit Classification Step 2 - Determination of Interface Wiring Requirements Step 3 - Bundling of All Coded Circuits Step 4 - Redundancy Requirements Step 5 - Installation of Bundles See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for steps to determine the classification NVR 324.3.1.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.1 Title Determination of Interface Wiring Requirements The following criteria will be used to determine the wiring requirements for each circuit. NVR A-54,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p70,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 324.3.1.2.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.1.1 Electrical Engineering Wire Type The categorization of each circuit in terms of frequency, impedance, voltage and/or sensitivity to assure proper EMEEC classification will permit the selection of wire type as specified in Table IX. The wire types given in Table IX are general in nature and do not alleviate the responsible design groups from specifying the wire size, allowable capacitance, and attenuation characteristics. Specific details on selected wire types will be included in applicable procurement specifications and assembly drawings. NVR 324.3.1.2.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.1.2 Electrical Engineering Signal Types Requiring Controlled Impedance Characteristics Serial digital, data bus, video, and clock circuits operating below 4 megahertz (MHz) will use controlled impedance wiring. RF circuits, including clock or data circuits with signal content of 4 MHz or above, will use fiber-optic, twinaxial or triaxial cable to maintain the requirements for isolation and single point references. Coaxial cable will be permitted for signals with frequency content above 4 MHz where dc isolation is maintained.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p70,1,"NVR 324.3.1.2.1.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.1.3 Electrical Engineering Shield Grounding Requirements Shields shall be terminated at both ends and at intermediate break points directly to structure or chassis, through connector backshells or direct wire connection per the methodology specified in MSG IIRD Section 3.2.4.3.1.2.4. T & I & A X EL-021 X X X 324.3.1.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.2 Electrical Engineering Implementation of Coding and Bundling All circuits routed together in a bundle will be of the same classification. Circuits classified by sensitivity will be analyzed to determine if the source voltage will be detrimental to other circuits in the bundle and if it is necessary to isolate such circuit wiring from other wires in the classification. NVR 324.3.1.2.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.3 Electrical Engineering Implementation of Bundle Installation The bundles which have been formed and coded will be installed using the following requirements to provide the required electrical isolation between different signal levels. NVR 324.3.1.2.3.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.3.1 Electrical Engineering Physical Isolation of Bundles With Different Codes Each bundle type will be physically isolated from all other bundles of a different bundle code. This separation provides electromagnetic coupling isolation between unlike bundles and circuits carrying different redundancy codes.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p70,2,"NVR 324.3.1.2.3.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.3.2 Electrical Engineering Separation Requirements Each bundle type of one code will be physically separated from other bundles to meet the 20-dB isolation requirement of MSG IIRD Section 3.2.4.3.1.2. Metallic channel separation will be permitted in lieu of physical separation, provided that the channel separator height is no less than the largest cable bundle diameter requiring separation and that analysis shows that the channels provide the required 20-dB isolation. The application of such metallic barriers in lieu of physical separation will be identified on all wiring diagrams containing these circuits. Cable bundle placement in all wire trays will also be determined using minimum to-maximum voltage or sensitivity requirements of Table IX, e.g., EO and RF cable bundles will have maximum separation in the placement of adjacent wire bundles. NVR 324.3.1.2.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.4 Electrical Engineering Shields System interconnections shall terminate overall cable shields peripherally. A & I EL-021 X X X A-55",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p71,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 324.3.1.2.4.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.4.1 Electrical Engineering Terminations Radio Frequency Interference (RFI) backshells with individual shield grounding provisions shall be used for multiple RF shield terminations. The length of the termination-to-ground lead for RF circuits shall be the minimum practical and shall not exceed 3 inches. The preferred method is to connect the shield peripherally to the backshell of an RF connector. This requires a continuous low-impedance electrical bond path through both halves of the connector shell, the connector-to-chassis interface, and the chassis-to-ground. All electrical connectors not engaged during mission shall be covered with a conductive cap. High impedance wires shall be terminated with a low impedance. A & I X EL-021 X X X 324.3.1.2.4.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.4.2 Electrical Engineering Breakouts Where RFI backshells with individual shield grounding provisions are required for multiple shield terminations, RF circuit shields shall be broken out such that no more than 2 inches of wiring is exposed; and, the wiring must be contained within the connector metal backshell covering.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p71,1,"NVR 324.3.1.2.4.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.4.3 Electrical Engineering Grounding of Radio Frequency Circuit Shields RF circuit shields shall be structure grounded as often as possible. This requirement can be satisfied by shield grounding to electrically conductive connector backshells at source and load and at any intermediate breakpoints. A EL-021 X 324.3.1.2.4.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.4.4 Electrical Engineering Internal Equipment Shields Shields originating and terminating within the same equipment shall be grounded therein. A EL-021 X 324.3.1.2.4.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.3.1.2.4.5 Electrical Engineering Grounding The shield ground shall be as specified in Table IX. A EL-021 X 324.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4 Electrical Engineering Electromagnetic Emission and Susceptibility Requirements Investigation hardware emissions and susceptibilities shall comply with these requirements. Testing of the equipment to ensure compliance to the requirements of this document shall be performed using the test methods given in Appendix B. Alternately, the investigation may choose to accept a minimal increase of EMI risk with a somewhat less stringent Electric Field Radiated Susceptibility (RS03) requirement on equipment considered to be non-safety critical to the vehicle and crew. The tailored RS03 requirement, shown below, will hereafter be denoted RS03PL. See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p71,2,"T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.1 Electrical Engineering Self-Compatibility The Equipment Under Test (EUT) shall not malfunction and performance shall not be degraded during Electromagnetic Interference (EMI) testing. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2 Electrical Engineering Equipment Emission and Susceptibility Limits This paragraph defines emission and susceptibility test limits. General EMI test techniques are contained in Appendix B of the MSG IIRD. Approval of design procedures and techniques does not relieve the Investigation of the responsibility of meeting the emission, and susceptibility test limits. A waiver is required for equipment which cannot meet the emission and susceptibility test requirements. The threshold of susceptibility shall be determined for equipment unable to meet the susceptibility test limits. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.1 Electrical Engineering Conducted Emissions Wiring between two or more Orbital Replacement Unit (ORU) shall be exempt from the conducted emission test requirements provided the specific ORUs are tested as a single unit. Wiring external to the group of ORUs tested as a unit shall meet the test limit requirements of this document. T & A X X EL-020 X X X A-56",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p72,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 324.4.2.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.1.1 Title CE01, Conducted Emissions Direct current primary power (120V), low frequency, 30 hertz (Hz) to 15 kilohertz (kHz). See MSG IIRD Section 3.2.4.4.2.1.2.2 for details of direct current secondary power (28V, ±12V, 5V) frequency, 30 Hz to 50 MHz. NVR 324.4.2.1.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.1.1.1 Title Applicability CE01 is applicable only for narrowband emissions between 30 Hz and 15 kHz on the 120V primary. NVR 324.4.2.1.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.1.1.2 Electrical Engineering CE01 Limits, 120V DC Electromagnetic emissions shall not appear on dc leads in excess of the following values as shown below. The emission limit shown below is for equipment drawing one amp or less. For equipment drawing more than one amp, the limit, in decibels (dB) as shown in Table X shall be raised by 20 x log I, where I equals the total dc current used by the equipment under test. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.2.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.1.2 Electrical Engineering CE03, Conducted Emissions Direct current primary power (120V) leads, 15 kHz to 50 megahertz (MHz). Direct current secondary power (28V, ±12V, 5V) frequency, 30 Hz to 50 MHz.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p72,1,"NVR 324.4.2.1.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.1.2.1 Electrical Engineering Applicability CE03 is applicable only for narrowband emissions between 15 kHz and 50 MHz on the 120V primary; CE01/CE03 is applicable for narrowband emissions between 30 Hz and 50 MHz on the secondary on dc leads which obtain power from other sources or provide power to other equipment, distribution panels or subsystems. NVR 324.4.2.1.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.1.2.2 Electrical Engineering CE03 Limits, 120V DC and CE01/CE03 Limits, +28V DC, ±12V DC and 5V DC Electromagnetic emissions shall not appear on dc power leads in excess of the following values as shown below for narrowband emissions: The limit shown below is for equipment drawing one amp or less. For equipment drawing more than one amp, the limit as shown in Table XI shall be raised by 20 x log I, where I equals the total dc current used by the equipment under test. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.2.1.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.1.3 Electrical Engineering CE07, Conducted Emissions Direct current power leads, spikes, time domain. NVR 324.4.2.1.3.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.1.3.1 Electrical Engineering Applicability CE07 is applicable for dc input power leads. Investigations using the MSG secondary power only are not required to perform this test. NVR 324.4.2.1.3.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.1.3.2 Electrical Engineering CE07 Limits CE07 on/off and mode switching transients shall not exceed the envelope defined by the following values listed in Table XII.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p72,2,"Repetitive on/off and mode switching transients shall not occur more frequently than every 100 milliseconds. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.2 Title Conducted Susceptibility NVR 324.4.2.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.2.1 Electrical Engineering CS01, Conducted Susceptibility Direct current power leads, 30 Hz to 50 kHz. NVR 324.4.2.2.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.2.1.1 Electrical Engineering Applicability CS01 is applicable to equipment and subsystems using dc power. NVR 324.4.2.2.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.2.1.2 Electrical Engineering CS01 Limits The EUT shall not exhibit any malfunction, degradation of performance, or deviation from specified indications beyond the tolerances indicated in the individual equipment or subsystem specification when subjected to electromagnetic energy injected onto its power leads less than or equal to the values as shown in Table XIII. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.2.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.2.2 Electrical Engineering CS02, Conducted Susceptibility Direct current power leads, 50 kHz to 50 MHz. NVR A-57",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p73,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 324.4.2.2.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.2.2.1 Electrical Engineering Applicability CS02 is applicable between 50 kHz and 50 MHz for equipment and subsystem dc power leads, including power returns which are not grounded internally to the equipment or subsystem. NVR 324.4.2.2.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.2.2.2 Electrical Engineering CS02 Limits CS02 Limits, 120V DC: The EUT shall not exhibit any malfunction, degradation of performance or deviation from specified indications beyond the tolerances indicated in the individual equipment or subsystem specification when subjected to 1 Vrms (for the 120V interface) from a 50-ohm source. The test signal shall be applied to the equipment power line near the equipment input terminals. Alternate CS02 Limits for 120V loads: The requirement is also met under the following condition: A 1 Watt source of 50-ohms impedance cannot develop the required voltage at the EUT power input terminals, and the EUT is not susceptible to the output of the signal source. CS02 Limits, +28V DC, ±12V DC and 5V DC: Investigations using the secondary power interfaces shall meet this requirement, but use the injection voltage of 0.1 volts rms for the Secondary Power Source (28V, +12V and 5V).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p73,1,"Alternate CS02 Limits for 28 Volt, ±12 Volt and +5 volt Loads: See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.2.2.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.2.3 Electrical Engineering CS06, Conducted Susceptibility Spikes, power leads. NVR 324.4.2.2.3.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.2.3.1 Electrical Engineering Applicability CS06 is applicable to equipment and subsystem dc power leads, including grounds and returns which are not grounded internally to the equipment or subsystem. Investigations using the MSG secondary power only are not required to perform this test. NVR 324.4.2.2.3.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.2.3.2 Electrical Engineering CS06 Limits The EUT shall not exhibit any malfunction, degradation of performance or deviation from specified indications beyond the tolerances indicated in the individual equipment or subsystem specification when the test spikes, each having the waveform shown on Figure 23, are applied sequentially to the dc power input leads. The values of E and t are given below. Each spike shall be superimposed on the powerline voltage waveform. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.2.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.3 Title Radiated Emissions NVR 324.4.2.3.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.3.1 Electrical Engineering RE02, Radiated Emissions Electric field, 14 kHz to 10 GHz, 13.5-15.5 GHz (narrowband).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p73,2,"NVR 324.4.2.3.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.3.1.1 Electrical Engineering Applicability RE02 is applicable for radiated emissions from equipment and subsystems, cables (including control, pulse, IF, power and antenna transmission lines) and interconnecting wiring of the test sample; for narrowband emissions, it applies at the fundamental frequencies and all spurious emissions including harmonics, but does not apply for radiation from antennas. This requirement is applicable for narrowband emissions from 14 kHz to 10 GHz and 13.5-15.5 GHz. NVR 324.4.2.3.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.3.1.2 Electrical Engineering RE02 Limits E-field emissions shall not be radiated in excess of those specified in the following paragraphs. Above 30 MHz, the limits shall be met for both horizontally and vertically polarized waves. Measurement shall be made in the peak detector mode. T & A X X EL-020 X X X A-58",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p74,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 324.4.2.3.1.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.3.1.2.1 Electrical Engineering Narrowband Electric Field Emissions Narrowband Eñfield emissions shall not be radiated in excess of the values as shown in Table XIV and in Figure 24 at the required test distance of 1 m. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.2.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.4 Title Radiated Susceptibility NVR 324.4.2.4.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.4.1 Electrical Engineering RS02, Radiated Susceptibility Magnetic induction field. NVR 324.4.2.4.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.4.1.1 Electrical Engineering Applicability RS02 is applicable for all equipment and subsystems. These susceptibility signals are electromagnetically coupled into the equipment or subsystem wiring. NVR 324.4.2.4.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.4.1.2 Electrical Engineering RS02 Limits The EUT shall not exhibit any malfunction, degradation of performance, or deviation from specified indications beyond the tolerances indicated in the individual equipment or subsystem specification when subjected sequentially to the test spikes, shown in Figure 23, each having the waveform with the values of E and t are given below:",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p74,1,"• Spike #1 E = ± Twice the nominal line voltage, t = 10 microseconds ± 20 percent • Spike #2 E = ± Twice the nominal line voltage, t = 0.15 microseconds ± 20 percent. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.2.4.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.4.2 Electrical Engineering RS03, Radiated Susceptibility Electric field, 14 kHz to 20 GHz. NVR 324.4.2.4.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.4.2.1 Electrical Engineering Applicability RS03 is applicable for all equipment and subsystems between 14 kHz and 20 GHz. Above 10 GHz, this requirement applies only at specific frequencies and amplitudes known to be present at the Space Station. Below 10 GHz, this requirement shall be increased only at specific frequencies and amplitudes known to be present at the ISS. Module shielding effectiveness can be used to limit the levels applied. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.2.4.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.4.2.2 Electrical Engineering RS03 Limits The EUT shall not exhibit any malfunction, degradation of performance, or deviation, from specified indications beyond the tolerances indicated in the individual equipment or subsystem specification when subjected to the radiated electric fields less than or equal to those specified herein. Above 30 MHz, the requirement shall be met for both horizontally and vertically polarized waves. As a minimum, the levels shown in Table XV apply at either the specific frequencies stated or across the ranges stated.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p74,2,"T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.4.2.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.5 Title Leakage Emissions NVR 324.4.2.5.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.5.1 Electrical Engineering LE01, AC Power User Leakage Current NVR 324.4.2.5.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.5.1.1 Electrical Engineering Applicability LE01 is applicable for all equipment and subsystems that use ac power. NVR 324.4.2.5.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.5.1.2 Electrical Engineering LE01 Limits The leakage current for all equipment and subsystems using ac power, as measured between chassis and input power, at the power frequency, shall not exceed 5 milliamperes. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 T & A X EL-020 A-59",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p75,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 324.4.2.6 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.4.2.6 Electrical Engineering Testing Requirements The test requirements and techniques of Appendix B shall be used to determine compliance with the applicable emission and susceptibility test limit requirements of this document. When an EUT susceptibility is noted, the thresholds of susceptibility shall be determined. Equipment that is intended to be operated as a subsystem shall be tested as such to the applicable emission and susceptibility limits whenever practical. T & A X X EL-020 X X X 324.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.5 Electrical Engineering Electrostatic Discharge All unpowered Investigation equipment shall not be damaged by Electrostatic Discharge (ESD) equal to or less than 4,000 V to the case or any pin on external connectors. EPCE that may be damaged by ESD between 4,000 and 15,000 V shall have a label affixed to the case in a location clearly visible in the installed position. Handling of EPCE susceptible to ESD up to 15,000 V shall be in accordance with MIL-STD-1686. These voltages are the result of charges that may be accumulated and discharged from ground personnel or crewmembers during equipment installation or removal.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p75,1,"Analysis and Inspection will be used to close this requirement. A & I or T & I X EL-024 X X X 324.6 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.6 Electrical Engineering Alternating Current (AC) Magnetic Fields Investigation containing devices that intentionally generate magnetic fields (electromagnets) shall not generate AC magnetic fields that exceed the levels given below. This requirement applies at a distance of 7 centimeters (cm) from a point on the enclosure of the rack or equipment case nearest the source of the field. Frequency Magnitude (dBpT) 30 Hz 140 30 Hz to 3.5 kHz Falling 26.5 dB/decade from 140 to 85 3.5kHz to 50 kHz 85 Note: Requirements are not applicable to solenoid valves, solenoid relays, and electric motors with current of less than 1 Amp. T or A X EL-020 X X X X 324.7 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.7 Electrical Engineering Direct Current (DC) Magnetic Fields Investigation containing devices that intentionally generate magnetic fields (permanent magnet and electromagnets) shall not generate DC magnetic fields that exceed 170 dB above a picotesla (dBpT). The requirement applies at a distance of 7 cm from a point on the enclosure of the rack or equipment case nearest the source of the field. Note: Requirements are not applicable to solenoid valves, solenoid relays, and electric motors with power consumption of less than 120 Watts.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p75,2,"T & A X X EL-020 X X X X 324.8 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.4.8 Electrical Engineering Corona Investigation electrical and electronic subsystems, equipment, and systems with voltages greater than 190 volts or equipment containing gaseous mixture, other than those present in the pressurized module, shall be designed to preclude damaging or destructive corona in its operating environment. Guidance for meeting the corona requirement is found in MSFC-STD-531, High Voltage Design Criteria. Submit statement to MSG saying that investigation will not have any voltages greater than 190V. T & A X EL-042 X X 325 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.5 Title Payload Electrical Safety Requirements NVR 325.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.5.1 Electrical Engineering Mating/Demating Of Powered Connectors The Investigation shall meet the electrical safety requirements as defined in NSTS 1700.7 ISS Addendum and paragraph 221 for Electrical Connections. Payloads shall comply with the requirements for mating/demating of powered connectors specified in NSTS 18798, MA2-99-170. Investigation crew procedures shall direct the operator to interrupt power via the CMP prior to mating/demating of connectors. Closed when safety verification is closed. I (PSRP) X (Safety) X X X A-60",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p76,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 325.3.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.5.3.a Electrical Engineering Power Switches/Controls The following power switches/controls requirements apply to power interfaces with open circuit voltage exceeding 30 volts rms or dc nominal (32 volts rms or dc maximum). a. Switches/controls performing on/off power functions for an Investigation connected to any WV power interface shall open (dead-face), all supply circuit conductors except the power return and the equipment grounding conductor while in the power-off position. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A X EL-029 325.3.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.5.3.b Electrical Engineering Power Switches/Controls The following power switches/controls requirements apply to power interfaces with open circuit voltage exceeding 30 volts rms or dc nominal (32 volts rms or dc maximum). b. Power-off markings and/or indications shall be used only if all parts, with the exception of overcurrent devices and associated EMI filters, are disconnected from the supply circuit.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p76,1,"N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A X EL-029 325.3.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.2.5.3.c Electrical Engineering Power Switches/Controls The following power switches/controls requirements apply to power interfaces with open circuit voltage exceeding 30 volts rms or dc nominal (32 volts rms or dc maximum). c. Standby, charging, or other descriptive nomenclature shall be used to indicate that the supply circuit is not completely disconnected for this power condition. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A X EL-029 330 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3 Software Engineering Command and Data Handling Requirements The MSG provides an Investigation with the ability to communicate with five different systems: MSG C&DH Systems 1) The Rack Controller (RC) which is also called the Standard Payload Computer (SPLC) 2) Experiment Control Board (ECB) 3) The MSG Laptop Computer (MLC) 4) The Video Drawer (VD) 5) The ISS Payload Ethernet Hub Gateway (PEHG) There are seven different interfaces (six hardware and one software) available to an Investigation. These interfaces are listed in Table XVII and shown in Figure 25. Table XVIII contains a list of C&DH related hardware that can be made available to an investigation. See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information. NVR 330.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering A single bi-directional RS422 channel between the DACU and the SPLC shall be used to communicate with the MSG systems.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p76,2,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-1-1] Final Integrated Test TXXX 331 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1 Software Engineering RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface The RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface is an RS422 serial interface that allows an investigation to communicate directly with the Rack Controller (RC). There are two RS422 - Direct Experiment Interfaces in the Work Volume, designated WV1 and WV2. NVR 331.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.1 Electrical Engineering RS422 Hardware Requirements The hardware verification items for the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface are contained in IIT-CD-01, Appendix E of the MSG IIRD. NVR 331.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.1.1 Electrical Engineering RS422 Cable Characteristics RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface wiring characteristics shall be in accordance with Table XIX. Close prior to integrated test. I CD-01 X X 331.1.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.1.2.a Electrical Engineering WV RS-422 Direct Experiment Interface Connector/Pin Assignments The WV RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface connectors (J305 and J311) are both type MS27656P9F35S, located on the WV rear wall as shown in Figure 2. The RS422 connection is a 4-wire point-to-point link of two users. The connector characteristics and pin assignments are shown in Tables XX and XXI. NVR A-61",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p77,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 331.1.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.1.2.b Electrical Engineering WV RS-422 Direct Experiment Interface Connector/Pin Assignments The Investigation provided RS-422 connector (P305/P311) shall be of type MS27467T9F35P, and shall be compatible with the pin assignments shown in Tables XX and XXI. Close prior to integrated test. I X CD-01 X 331.1.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.1.3 Electrical Engineering RS422 Signal Characteristics Investigation hardware that requires connectivity to the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to the electrical characteristics specified in the Electrical Industry Standard RS422B specification. Close prior to integrated test. I X CD-01 X X 331.1.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.1.4 Software Engineering RS422 Port Settings Port settings for the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface are fixed and are defined as follows: Parameter Setting Baud 19200 Data Bits 8 Parity None Stop Bits 1 Investigations that interface to the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall operate at these port settings. Close prior to integrated test. I CD-01 X 331.1.4.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering RS422 serial port settings shall be 19200 baud, no parity, 8 data bits, and 1 stop bit.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p77,1,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-1-2] I X 331.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2 Software Engineering RS422 Software Requirements The software verification items for the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface are contained in IIT-CD-02, Appendix E of the MSG IIRD. NVR 331.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.1 Title RS422 Protocols NVR 331.2.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.1.1 Software Engineering RS422 Byte and Bit Order Investigation hardware that requires connectivity to a RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 1.4.3 Byte and Bit Order, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-02 X X X* 331.2.1.1.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering All data that the DACU transmits to and accepts from the SPLC shall conform to the byte and bit order as specified in MSG- ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document, section 1.4.3. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-2-2] T X X X 331.2.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.1.2 Software Engineering RS422 Word Alignment Investigation hardware that requires connectivity to a RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 2.3 Word Alignment, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-02 X X X* 331.2.1.2.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering All data that the DACU transmits to and accepts from the SPLC shall conform to the word alignment as specified in MSG-ORIGIN- IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document, section 2.3.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p77,2,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-2-3] T X X X 331.2.1.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.1.3 Software Engineering ESTEC Data Link Format (EDLF) Investigation hardware that requires connectivity to a RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 3.5.1.1 ESTEC Data Link Format Description, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-02 X X X* 331.2.1.3.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering All data that the DACU transmits to and accepts from the SPLC shall conform to the ESTEC Data Link Format as specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document, section 3.5.1.1. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-2-1] T X X X 331.2.1.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.1.4 Software Engineering RC Required Headers Investigation hardware that requires connectivity to a RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 3.1 External Interface Description (Block Definitions) & MSG Headers, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-02 X X X* 331.2.1.4.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering See # 331.2.2.1.1.0.a, # 331.2.2.1.2.0.a, # 331.2.2.2.2.0.a, # 331.2.2.2.3.0.a, # 331.2.2.2.6.0.a, # 337.2.1.0.a [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-7-1] 331.2.2.1.1.0.a [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-6-1] 331.2.2.1.2.0.a [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-3-2] 331.2.2.2.2.0.a [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-4-2] 331.2.2.2.3.0.a [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-5-2] 331.2.2.2.6.0.a [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-5-2] 337.2.1.0.a TXXX A-62",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p78,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 331.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.2 Software Engineering RS422 Message Types Each RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface supports messages from the RC to the WV and messages from the WV to the RC. RC To WV WV to RC Experiment Commands Command Acknowledges Time of Day (TOD) Data Experiment H&S File Transfers Experiment LRT File Transfers Log Messages MSG Video Commands NVR 331.2.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.2.1 Title RC to WV Messages NVR 331.2.2.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.2.1.1 Software Engineering RS422 Experiment Commands Experiment Commands transmitted via the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 3.1.5.3 Experiment Commands, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-02 X X X* 331.2.2.1.1.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The DACU shall accept experiment command packets from the SPLC when they adhere to the format specified in MSG-ORIGIN- IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p78,1,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-7-1] See # 331.2.1.4.0.a TXXX 331.2.2.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.2.1.2 Software Engineering Time of Day (TOD) Data Time of day data transmitted via the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 3.1.5.6 TOD Data, of MSG- ORIGIN-IC-0001. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-02 X X X* 331.2.2.1.2.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The DACU shall accept time of day packets from the SPLC when they adhere to the format specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-6-1] See # 331.2.1.4.0.a TXXX 331.2.2.1.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.2.1.3 Software Engineering RS422 File Transfers, RC to WV Files transfers that are accomplished via the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 3.1.5.4 of MSG- ORIGIN-IC-0001. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-02 331.2.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.2.2 Title WV to RC Messages NVR 331.2.2.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.2.2.1 Software Engineering RS422 Command Acknowledges Command Acknowledges transmitted via the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 3.1.5.5 Command Acknowledges and Log Messages, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p78,2,"T CD-02 331.2.2.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.2.2.2 Software Engineering RS422 Experiment Health and Status Experiment Health and Status data transmitted via the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 3.1.5.2 Experiment Health and Status Data, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. The Health and Status Data an investigation is required to send is defined by the PSRP during the Safety Review process, see section 3.11 for additional information concerning Health and Status Data and Caution and Warning. Safety data is included in the Experiment Health and Status data and shall conform to Section 3.1.5.7 Safety Data, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-02 X X X* 331.2.2.2.2.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The content of the health and status packet shall conform to the format specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-3-2] See # 331.2.1.4.0.a TXXX 331.2.2.2.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.2.2.3 Software Engineering RS422 Experiment Low Rate Telemetry Experiment Low Rate Telemetry data transmitted via the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 3.1.5.1 Experiment Low Rate Telemetry Data, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p78,3,"T CD-02 X X X* 331.2.2.2.3.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The content of the low rate telemetry packet shall conform to the format specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-4-2] See # 331.2.1.4.0.a TXXX A-63",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p79,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 331.2.2.2.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.2.2.4 Software Engineering RS422 File Transfers, WV to RC Files transfers from the WV to the RC that are accomplished via the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 3.1.5.4 File Transfers, WV to RC, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-02 331.2.2.2.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.2.2.5 Software Engineering RS422 Log Messages Log Messages transmitted via the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 3.1.5.5 Command Acknowledges and Log Messages, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-02 331.2.2.2.6 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.1.2.2.2.6 Software Engineering RS422 MSG Video Commands MSG Video Commands transmitted via the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface shall conform to Section 3.1.8, of MSG- ORIGIN-IC-0001. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-02 X X X* 331.2.2.2.6.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The content of video drawer packets issued by the DACU shall conform to the format specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p79,1,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-5-2] See # 331.2.1.4.0.a and # 337.2.1.0.a TXXX 332 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.2 Software Engineering Investigation I/O Interface The MSG provides 8 analog inputs, 8 discrete inputs, and 8 digital outputs, split equally between two connectors in the Work Volume. When properly configured and enabled, the Experiment Control Board (ECB) reads the input lines and transmits the data to the RC. The RC in turn transmits the data to the ISS via the Low Rate Data Link (LRDL) in a Low Rate Telemetry (LRT) packet. The digital output lines are controlled via commands to the RC. NVR 332.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.2.1 Electrical Engineering I/O Hardware Requirements The hardware verification items for the Investigation I/O Interface are contained in IIT-CD-03, Appendix E of the MSG IIRD. NVR 332.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.2.1.1 Electrical Engineering I/O Cable Characteristics The Investigation I/O Interface wiring shall be 22 AWG TSP wire and all shields shall be tied to pin 9. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. I CD-03 332.1.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.2.1.2.a Electrical Engineering I/O Connector/Pin Assignments The WV Investigation I/O connectors (J301/J319) are supplied on two MS27656P15F35SA connectors in the WV rear wall as shown in Figure 2. The connector characteristics and contact assignments are listed in Table XXII and XXIII.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p79,2,"NVR 332.1.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.2.1.2.b Electrical Engineering I/O Connector/Pin Assignments The Investigation provided connectors (P301/P319) shall be of type MS27467T15F35PA and shall be compatible with the contact assignments defined in Tables XXII and XXIII. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. I CD-03 332.1.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.2.1.3 Electrical Engineering I/O Signal Characteristics Investigation hardware that requires connectivity to the Investigation I/O Interface shall conform to the electrical characteristics specified below: • Analog Inputs (Investigation data) - differential, -9 to +9 V (read with 12 bits of resolution) • Discrete Inputs (Investigation data) - single ended, 0-0.8 V for logical 0, 3.6-10 V for logical 1 • Digital Out (Investigation commanding) - single ended, 0-0.8 V for logical 0, 3.6-10 V for logical 1 N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. T CD-03 332.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.2.2 Software Engineering I/O Software Requirements There are no software verification items levied on an investigation for the Investigation I/O interface. However, there are software verification items to ensure that configuration and control commands are correct and that telemetry data can be correctly transmitted to the ground. The software verification items for the Investigation I/O Interface are contained in IIT-CD- 04, Appendix E of the MSG IIRD. NVR A-64",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p80,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 332.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.2.2.1 Software Engineering I/O Experiment Telemetry Details on the telemetry formats for the Investigation I/O Interface are found in 3.1.7.1 Experiment Telemetry Data of MSG-ORIGIN- IC-0001. NVR 332.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.2.2.2 Software Engineering I/O Configuration and Control Commands The Investigation I/O Interface configuration and control commands sent to the RC shall be in accordance with ECB 3.1.7.2 Configuration and Control commands of MSG-ORIGIN-IC- 0001. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-04 332.2.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.2.2.3 Software Engineering I/O Log Messages The Investigation I/O interface will generate a log message when an error is detected. Log messages are defined in 3.1.16.1 Log Messages of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. NVR 333 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.3 Electrical Engineering MLC Serial Interface The MLC provides a Serial Interface that is available to an investigation. The MLC Serial Interface is an EIA Standard RS232C asynchronous serial I/O port. Detailed specifications on RS232C can be found in EIA STD RS-232C.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p80,1,"NVR 333.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.3.1 Electrical Engineering MLC Serial Interface Hardware Requirements The hardware verification items for the MLC Serial Interface are contained in IIT-CD-05, Appendix E of the MSG IIRD. NVR 333.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.3.1.1 Electrical Engineering MLC Serial Interface Cable Characteristics MLC Serial Interface wiring characteristics shall be in accordance with Table XXIV. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. I CD-05 333.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.3.1.2 Electrical Engineering MLC Serial Interface Connector/Pin Assignments The MLC is typically located outside the work volume due to space limitations. However, it may be located inside, under special circumstances. NVR 333.1.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.3.1.2.1 Electrical Engineering MLC Outside the Work Volume When the MLC is to be used outside the work volume, the Investigation may connect to either the J404 feed-through port in the front of the MSG WV, or the J706 connector located on the CMP or to the J312 connector located on the WV rear wall. In either case, the Investigation provided connector (P404, P706 or P312), shall be of type MS27467T9F35P, and shall be compatible with the connector and pin assignments identified in Table XXV. The MSG provides two cables for use with MLC outside the work volume: W201 - converts RS232 to RS422 signal levels (the converter is powered from the serial lines).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p80,2,"This cable allows the MLC outside the work volume to communicate RS422 to an investigation inside the work volume. The investigation must provide a cable from the J404 or J312 connector to the experiment. W205 - allows MLC outside the work volume to communicate RS232 to an investigation inside the work volume. The investigation must provide a cable from the J404 or J312 connector to the experiment. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-229; Close prior to integrated test. I CD-05 333.1.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.3.1.2.2 Electrical Engineering MLC Inside the Work Volume When the investigation is using the MLC inside the WV, the investigation may use the W201 or W205 cables provided by the MSG, or, the investigation may provide a cable that connects directly to the DB9 connector provided on the MLC. The investigation supplied connectors shall be of type DB9 Female, and shall be compatible with the contact assignments defined in Table XXVI. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. I CD-05 333.1.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.3.1.3 Electrical Engineering MLC Serial Interface Signal Characteristics Investigation hardware that requires connectivity to the MLC Serial Interface shall meet the electrical characteristics in accordance with the Electrical Industry Standard RS232C specification. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. I CD-05 A-65",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p81,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 333.1.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.3.1.4 Software Engineering MLC Serial Interface Port Settings Investigations that interface to the MLC Serial Interface shall operate within the port settings defined in Table XXVII. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. I CD-05 333.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.3.2 Software Engineering MLC Serial Interface Software Requirements The MLC allows for an investigation to communicate directly to the MLC serial port, but strongly encourages investigations to use the MLC supplied server software. The MLC Server (MLCS) software allows an application to interface to the MLC Serial Interface without interfering with other MLC operations (see section 3.3.5 MLC Software Interface). Investigations interfacing to the MLC Serial Interface via the MLCS shall conform to Section 4.6 Experiment Software Interfaces, of MSFC-RQMT- 3098. Investigations that use the MLC Serial Interface by means other than the MLCS shall coordinate with the IIT to obtain additional MLC software requirements and constraints. The software verification items for the RS422 - Direct Experiment Interface are contained in IIT-CD-06, Appendix E of the MSG IIRD.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p81,1,"N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-06 334 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.4 Software Engineering MLC Ethernet Interface The MLC Ethernet Interface may be used for two purposes: 1) Transfer data directly to the ISS via the LAN1 MRDL 2) Data transfer from an investigation to MLC storage via FTP services (In order to utilize the FTP services of the laptop, the investigation must use a cross wired cable wired I.A.W. the IEEE 802.3.) Investigations need to be aware that SAMS also uses Ethernet for rack-to-rack communications. If SAMS is required, LAN1 and LAN2 will not be available inside the work volume (LAN1 and LAN2 are both on the J320 connector, but the cable does not break out LAN1 for investigation use). NVR 334.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.4.1 Electrical Engineering MLC Ethernet Interface Hardware Requirements The hardware verification items for the MLC Ethernet Interface are contained in IIT-CD-07, Appendix E of the MSG IIRD. NVR 334.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.4.1.1 Electrical Engineering MLC Ethernet Interface Cable Characteristics The Investigation shall meet the cable characteristics given in Table XXVIII. Close prior to integrated test. I CD-07 X 334.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.4.1.2 Electrical Engineering MLC Ethernet Interface Connector/Pin Assignments In order to utilize the FTP services of the laptop, the experiment shall provide a cross wired cable that conforms to the IEEE 802.3 standards.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p81,2,"Cables W208, W203, and W210 are provided by the MSG. These cables allow the MLC to communicate to the ISS LAN1 via the MRDL. Close prior to integrated test. I X CD-07 X 334.1.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.4.1.3 Electrical Engineering MLC Ethernet Interface Signal Characteristics Investigations that require connectivity to MLC via the MLC Ethernet shall meet the electrical characteristics of Ethernet in accordance with ISO/IEC 8802-3 with the following exceptions: IEC Publication 60 High-Voltage Test Techniques IEC Publication 380 Safety of Electrically Energized Office Machines IEC Publication 435 Safety of Data Processing Equipment IEC Publication 950 Safety of Information Technology Equipment, Including Electrical Business Equipment Close prior to integrated test. I X CD-07 X A-66",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p82,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 334.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.4.2 Software Engineering MLC Ethernet Interface Software Requirements Investigations communicating via the MLC Ethernet shall use the MLC Software Interface (see section 3.3.5) and shall conform to Section 4.6 Experiment Software Interfaces, of MSFC-RQMT- 3098. The hardware verification items for the MLC Ethernet Interface are contained in IIT-CD-08, Appendix E of the MSG IIRD. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-08 X X X* 335 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.5 Software Engineering MLC Software Interface The MSG Laptop Computer (MLC) is an IBM A31p model laptop computer. The MLC has a 1.8 GHz processor with 1GB of dynamic random access memory. A 60GB Hard Disk Drive is provided for use by the operating system, the MLC Server Software, Investigation Application Software, and for data storage. A 1.44 MB Floppy Disk Drive and a DVD-ROM/CD-RW are provided as additional data storage devices. The MLC also may be configured to support dual Ethernet connections by the use of a PCMCIA card that would occupy the only vacant PCMCIA slot. See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p82,1,"NVR 335.0.a SAME-DOC-004 7.2 Software Engineering The crew display shall be able to run on the MSG Laptop Computer with features and operating system as described in MSFC-RQMT-2888, Microgravity Science Glovebox Investigation Interface Requirements Document, Section 3.3.5. [SAME-SW-REQ-7-2-1-1-1] T X X 335.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.5.1 Software Engineering MLCS RS232 Interface The MLCS provides software support for serial interfaces. The MLCS software supports the following baud rates: 57600, 38400, 19200 and 9600. The data format supported is 8 data bits, 1 start bit, 1 stop bit and no parity. The MLCS provides support for a RTS/CTS protocol, a dead band sync protocol, or the ESTEC Data Link Format (EDLF) protocol. Data transfers utilizing the MLCS RS232 interface shall be compliant with Section 4.5.1 RS232/RS422 Serial Interface, of MSFC-RQMT-3098. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-09 335.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.5.2 Software Engineering Electrical Engineering MLCS Ethernet Interface The MLCS provides FTP software support for the Ethernet interface via the Microsoft Internet Information Server (See section 3.3.5.3 MLCS Socket Interface, for MRDL software support). Investigations requiring FTP access to the MLC shall: 1) Interface to the MLCS via FTP at IP address [ip removed], port 21 (standard FTP port) 2) Provide a cross wired cable wired I.A.W. the IEEE 802.3 (no Ethernet hub is provided).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p82,2,"Anonymous login is supported and the user is logged into C:\InetPub\ftproot directory with read/write privileges. Additional information is provided in Section 4.5.2 TCP/IP Ethernet Interface, of MSFC-RQMT-3098. Data transfers utilizing the MLCS Ethernet Interface shall be compliant with Section 4.5.2 TCP/IP Ethernet Interface, of MSFC-RQMT-3098. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-09 X X X* 335.2.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering A dedicated standard 802.3 (10BaseT) Ethernet connection from the MLC to the DACU shall be used to perform the FTP transfer. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-2-2-1] T X X X 335.2.0.b SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering Communications with the FTP server shall be done utilizing a server IP address of [ip removed], port 21. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-2-2-2] T X X X 335.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.5.3 Software Engineering MLCS Socket Interface The MLCS provides applications software with interfaces to the MLC hardware and translation of the 1553 and MRDL interfaces. The interface requirements and a summary of the software interfaces of this software are documented in Section 4.6 Experiment Software Interfaces, of MSFC-RQMT-3098, Section 4.6. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-09 X X X* A-67",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p83,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 335.3.0.a SAME-DOC-004 7.2 Software Engineering The crew display shall send commands from the MLC to the instrument using the MLC Server Socket Interface. [SAME-SW-REQ-7-2-2-1-1] T X X 335.3.0.b SAME-DOC-004 7.2 Software Engineering The crew display on the MLC shall receive health and status data and low-rate telemetry from the instrument using the MLC Server Socket Interface. [SAME-SW-REQ-7-2-2-1-2] T X X 335.3.0.c SAME-DOC-004 7.2 Software Engineering The crew display shall meet the MLC Server Socket Interface requirements documented in MSFC-RQMT-3098, Software Requirements Specification (SRS) for Microgravity Science Glovebox Laptop Computer Server Software, Section 4.6. [SAME-SW-REQ-7-2-2-1-3] T X X 336 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.6 Software Engineering ISS LAN1/LAN2 Interface The Investigation is provided a direct interface to the ISS LAN1 and LAN2 Ethernet via J320 on the WV rear wall. Investigations need to be aware that SAMS also uses Ethernet for rack-to-rack communications, and MSG does not provided an Ethernet Bridge so all Ethernet traffic will be seen at the interface.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p83,1,"If SAMS is required, LAN1 and LAN2 will not be available inside the work volume (LAN1 and LAN2 are both on the J320 connector, but the cable does not break out LAN connections separately for investigation use). It should be noted that Investigations within the MSG WV are also provided one Ethernet interface (LAN2), which is not supported by the MSG DHS, but instead is a direct connection to the ISS Ethernet. NVR 336.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.6.1 Electrical Engineering Ethernet Interface Hardware Requirements The hardware verification items for the Investigation Ethernet Interface are contained in IIT-CD-10, IIT-CD-11, and IIT-CD-12, Appendix E of the MSG IIRD. NVR 336.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.6.1.1 Electrical Engineering Ethernet Interface Cable Characteristics The Investigation shall meet the cable characteristics given in Table XXIX. Close prior to integrated test. I CD-12 X 336.1.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.6.1.2.a Electrical Engineering Ethernet Interface Connector/Pin Assignments The WV Investigation Ethernet connector (J320) is supplied on a D38999/20FG75SN type connector on the WV rear wall as shown in Figure 2. The connector characteristics and contact assignments are defined in Tables XXX. NVR 336.1.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.6.1.2.b Electrical Engineering Ethernet Interface Connector/Pin Assignments The Investigation provided connector (P320) shall be of type D38999/26FG75PN and shall be compatible with the contact assignments defined in Tables XXX. Close prior to integrated test.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p83,2,"I X CD-12 X 336.1.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.6.1.3 Electrical Engineering Ethernet Interface Signal Characteristics Payloads that require connectivity to the Ethernet shall meet the electrical characteristics of Ethernet in accordance with ISO/IEC 8802-3 with the following exceptions: IEC Publication 60 High-Voltage Test Techniques IEC Publication 380 Safety of Electrically Energized Office Machines IEC Publication 435 Safety of Data Processing Equipment IEC Publication 950 Safety of Information Technology Equipment, Including Electrical Business Equipment Close prior to integrated test. T & I CD-12 X X 336.1.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.6.1.4 Electrical Engineering Ethernet Interface Connectivity Ethernet connectivity shall conform to section 3.3 MRDL, of SSP 52050. T & I CD-11 X X X 336.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.6.2 Software Engineering Ethernet Interface Software Requirements The software verification items for the Ethernet- Direct Experiment Interface are contained in IIT-CD-10 and IIT-CD-11, Appendix E of the MSG IIRD. NVR A-68",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p84,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 336.2.1.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.6.2.1.a Software Engineering Ethernet Interface Protocol Investigations that communicate via the Ethernet shall conform to Section 3.3, Medium Rate Data Link of SSP 52050. a. Investigations that communicate via the Ethernet shall conform to ISO/IEC 8802-3 10-Base-T protocol in accordance with Section 3.3, Medium Rate Data Link (MRDL) of SSP 52050. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. T CD-10 336.2.1.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.6.2.1.b Software Engineering Ethernet Interface Protocol Investigations that communicate via the Ethernet shall conform to Section 3.3, Medium Rate Data Link of SSP 52050. b. Investigations sending data to the ground through the USOS Space to Ground Link shall use the CCSDS protocol and gateway protocol in Section 3.3.4, Gateway Protocol and 3.3.7 Packet Length in SSP 52050. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. T CD-10 336.2.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.6.2.2.a Software Engineering Ethernet Interface Address Investigations implementing Ethernet shall have a (unique) IEEE issued Ethernet Media Access Control (MAC) physical address (MAC Address), for each Ethernet attachment.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p84,1,"N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. T & A CD-11 336.2.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.6.2.2.b Software Engineering Ethernet Interface Address The MAC address shall be set prior to the Ethernet terminal going active. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Close prior to integrated test. T & A CD-11 337 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.7 Electrical Engineering Video Interface A video system is provided in the MSG for investigation use. The entire system is described in the MSG Capabilities Manual, MSFC-HDBK-3051. NVR 337.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.7.1 Electrical Engineering Video Interface Hardware Requirements The hardware verification items for the Video Interface are contained in IIT-CD-13, Appendix E of the MSG IIRD. NVR 337.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.7.1.1 Electrical Engineering Video Interface Cable Characteristics Video cables shall be compatible with NTSC EIA RS170A standards. Close prior to integrated test. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T & I CD-13 X X X X* X 337.1.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.7.1.2 Electrical Engineering Video Interface Connector/Pin Assignments For Investigation provided cameras, MSG provides video/camera feed-through connectors on three separate ports located in the upper front left (3 connectors) and right (3 connectors) and bottom right (3 connectors) corners of the WV window. The connector type for the feed-through connectors (A, B, C) are SGJ- 2B-319-CLL-PV (LEMO), female contacts located on the WV inside.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p84,2,"The investigation provided connector (A, B, C) shall be of type FGG-2B-319-CLCD and shall be compatible with the contact assignments defined in Table XXXI when using the MSG video system. Connector markings and pin assignments shall be worked with the IIT and documented in the Unique Investigation ICD for the feed-through connectors. Close prior to integrated test. SAME will provide a connector of type FGG-2B-319-CLAD-82Z. I X CD-13 X X X 337.1.3.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.7.1.3 Electrical Engineering Video Interface Signal Characteristics For cameras powered through the standard video drawer connections, a 1A fuse with a de-rated current value of 0.65A protects the 12V power provided to the camera. The power consumption of an investigation provided camera shall not exceed 7.8 Watts. As an alternative, investigations may power their cameras from internal secondary power through the J303 or J325 connectors inside the WV. If this is done, only the normal secondary power current protection is provided. Investigators may wish to insert additional protection for their camera. Close prior to integrated test. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-13 X X X X* X A-69",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p85,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 337.1.3.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.7.1.3 Electrical Engineering Video Interface Signal Characteristics Investigation-provided cameras shall be National Television Standard Committee (NTSC) video standard EIA RS-170A cameras. The investigation will provide both Composite and YC signals to the 19-pin LEMO connector inside the Work Volume. The pinning of the investigation camera cable to the feed-through depends on the video requirements and type of camera being used. The Composite video signal is required to view camera images on MSG monitors, and for video downlinking. The video recorders require the YC video signal. Close prior to integrated test. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. A X CD-13 X X X X* X 337.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.7.2 Software Engineering Video Interface Software Requirements There are no software verification items levied on an investigation for the Video Interface. However, there are software verification items to ensure that configuration and control commands are correct and can be successfully transmitted to the Video System. The software verification items for the Video Interface are contained in IIT-CD-14, Appendix E of the MSG IIRD.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p85,1,"NVR 337.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.7.2.1 Software Engineering Video Commands Commands sent to the Video subsystem shall conform to Section 3.1.8.1 Video Commands, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-14 X X X* 337.2.1.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The content of video drawer packets issued by the DACU shall conform to the format specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-5-2] See # 331.2.1.4.0.a and # 331.2.2.2.6.0.a TXX 337.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.7.2.2 Software Engineering Video Log Messages The Video Interface will generate a log message when an error is detected. Video Log messages are defined in 3.1.8.2 Video Log Messages and Command Acknowledges Messages, of MSG- ORIGIN-IC-0001. NVR 337.2.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.7.2.3 Software Engineering Video Health and Status The MSG video system provides health and status information and is transmitted to the ISS in the MSG Health and Status packet. The video system health and status data is defined in 3.1.8.3 Video Health and Status of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001. NVR 338 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.3.8 Software Engineering Real Time Health and Status Monitoring Ground displays that will be used to monitor the health and status of MSG or an Investigation shall be validated/verified during C&DH testing or by the playback of real time data.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p85,2,"This portion of the C&DH testing shall be used to verify that the data is being displayed accurately and usefully on the displays. All error conditions that can be verified via ground testing shall be included in this verification. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T CD-15 X X X* 340 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.4 Mechanical Engineering Thermal Control Interface Requirements The MSG Thermal Control Subsystem (TCS) provides both water cooling and avionics air cooling for MSG and Investigation hardware. The paragraphs below define the interface characteristics for the MSG TCS interfaces that are provided to the Investigation within the WV. NVR 341 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.4.1 Mechanical Engineering Investigation Thermal Interfaces Thermal cooling for Investigation hardware within the WV is provided by the WV Air Circulation System (ACS) and a coldplate which is mounted in the floor of the WV. Together, these provide a total 1,000 W of heat rejection for Investigation equipment inside the WV. NVR A-70",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p86,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 341.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.4.1.1 Electrical Engineering WV Air Circulation System (ACS) The WV air circulation is established by the MSG Air Handling Unit (AHU). The AHU heat exchanger provides 200 W of investigation cooling, at an airflow rate of 1200 l/min and a maximum velocity of 0.044 m/s at the centerline of the WV. The airflow can be varied between 15% and 100% depending on fan speed settings. The rate and velocity is calculated without an Investigation inside the WV. Investigation power draw data shall be used to determine if the AHU heat exchanger is capable of removing the dissipated heat load. The filtration system provides for particulate removal, filtration of single spillage (see section 3.7.3), and oxidation of CO to CO2 by means of a built-in catalyst. Due to the hydroscopic nature of the filters, the humidity inside the WV will be maintained at or below cabin level. Figure 26 provides block diagram of the MSG air loops. A FD-002 X X 341.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.4.1.2 Mechanical Engineering WV Coldplate Investigation cooling is also provided via a 350 mm x 400 mm coldplate embedded in the WV floor. Figure 29 provides a block diagram of the MSG water coolant loops.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p86,1,"The coldplate is located on the left side of the WV floor as indicated in Figure 2. Investigations that do not use the coldplate to dissipate heat are not required to meet the thermal requirements of section 3.4.1.2.1 of the MSG IIRD. NVR 341.2.1.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.4.1.2.1.a Mechanical Engineering Investigation Coldplate Mounting Requirements The Investigation baseplate shall maintain a flatness of less than 0.2mm and have a surface finish of 125 microinches on the surface in contact with the work volume coldplate. I X FD-003 X 341.2.1.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.4.1.2.1.b Mechanical Engineering Investigation Coldplate Mounting Requirements The Investigation shall utilize M6 threaded captive fasteners to attach hardware to the work volume coldplate. Investigations using the coldplate to dissipate heat shall tighten their bolts in according with their thermal requirements but not to exceed 60 in- lbs of torque. If a special tool is required to torque the fasteners the Investigation shall provide it. Refer to Figure 2 for mounting hole locations. I FD-003 X X X 341.2.1.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.4.1.2.1.c Mechanical Engineering Investigation Coldplate Mounting Requirements The Investigation shall provide, to the maximum extent possible, an equal heat distribution over the coldplate surface. A FD-003 X X 341.2.1.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.4.1.2.1.d Electrical Engineering Investigation Coldplate Mounting Requirements Total heat dissipation to the coldplate shall be no more than 800 Watts.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p86,2,"The predicted MSG coldplate temperatures are shown in Figure 27 and Figure 28. These predicted coldplate temperatures are based on a MSG Rack coolant inlet temperature of 18.3 C. These temperatures are a function of experiment heat dissipation to the coldplate, experiment heat dissipation to Glovebox air, and coldplate coolant flow rate. Coolant flows to the MSG rack equipment, heat exchangers, and the MSG coldplate in series. The heat dissipation to Glovebox air and the heat dissipation of other rack equipment influence the temperature of the coolant at the inlet of the MSG coldplate and the coldplate temperature. See MSFC- RQMT-2888 for more information. A FD-003 X X A-71",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p87,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 341.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.4.1.3 Mechanical Engineering Airlock Air Circulation System No active thermal conditioning of the AL is provided. The AL ACS is part of the Glovebox ACS and provides conditioning of the AL atmosphere. Therefore, no powered operations will be conducted in the AL. The AL airflow rate is 1 l/s with an air inlet area of 0.005 m2 . The fan speed can be adjusted at the CMP. NVR 341.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.4.1.4 Mechanical Engineering Loss of MSG Cooling or Services Investigations shall be designed to maintain fault tolerance or safety margins consistent with the hazard potential, e. g. touch temperature, without ground or flight crew intervention, in the event of sudden loss or temporary interruption of the MSG water coolant loop, avionics air cooling or the vacuum and nitrogen services in accordance with NSTS 1700.7, ISS Addendum. Investigations that can not prove, by analysis, that loss of cooling does not produce a credible fire hazard, shall provide temperature sensors and parameter monitoring that will remove power from the payload in the case that the temperature goes above safe limits, see section 3.8.1.1, Investigation Monitoring and Detection.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p87,1,"A X FD-004 X X 350 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5 Title Vacuum System Requirements The MSG provides the Investigation a direct interface connection to the ISS Vacuum Exhaust System (VES) and Vacuum Resource System (VRS) via quick disconnects (QDs) and manual shut-off valves located on the rear wall of WV. The system becomes active after opening the shut-off valve inside the WV. Figure 30 provides a block diagram of the MSG to ISS Vacuum System interface. NVR 351 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1 Title Vacuum Exhaust System NVR 351.1.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.1.a Mechanical Engineering VES Physical Interface The WV VES QD is supplied on a 0.5"" swagelock body, part # SS-QC8-B1-810K6, and white in color located on the WV rear wall as indicated in Figure 2. The Investigation provided QD shall be a swagelock stem, part # SS-QC8-D1-810K6 and shall be compatible with the WV VES QD. In the part number the D indicates a double end shut-off and the K indicates the keying. The Demonstration shall use the MSG EU to verify that connectors physically mate. SAME will provide a QD with part number SS-QC8-D-810K6. D FD-014 X X X* 351.1.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.1.b Mechanical Engineering VES Physical Interface The maximum leak rate of the investigation volumes connected to the VES shall be 2 x 10-3 scc/sec.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p87,2,"T FD-014 X X 351.1.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.1.c Mechanical Engineering VES Physical Interface The Investigation shall ensure that the VES manual shut-off valve will not be blocked during operations as shown in Appendix G of the MSG IIRD. Demonstration can be closed during physical or integrated testing with the EU. D FD-014 X X X 351.1.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.1.d Mechanical Engineering VES Physical Interface Gases released by the Investigation shall be within the same restrictions as for exhaust gases defined in MSG IIRD Section 3.5.1.5. See VDS # FD-018, FD-019, and FD-020. A FD-014 X X X 351.1.e MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.1.e Mechanical Engineering VES Physical Interface The Investigation shall incorporate into the crew procedures a procedure that directs the operator to monitor the pressure sensor between the manual shut-off valve and the QD to insure leak tightness of QD interface for each unique vacuum hose during the first connection to the VES. I FD-014 X 351.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.2.a Mechanical Engineering Input Pressure Limit Investigations shall limit their vented exhaust gas to a pressure of 276 kPa (40 psia) or less at the WV interface. A X FD-015 X A-72",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p88,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 351.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.2.b Mechanical Engineering Input Pressure Limit Investigation volumes connected to the VES shall be designed to the maximum design pressure (MDP) of at least 276 kPa (40 psia) with a safety factor of 1.5 based on MDP. Lines and fittings used to interface with the WV QD shall have an ultimate factor of safety equal to or greater than 4.0 based on MDP if the outside diameter < 1.5"" and 2.0 if > 1.5"". Components connected to the VES shall have an ultimate of 2.5 based on MDP. A X FD-015 X 351.2.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.2.c Mechanical Engineering Input Pressure Limit Investigations shall protect against failure conditions which would exceed the VES max design pressure of 276 kPa (40 psia). A X FD-015 X 351.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.3 Mechanical Engineering Input Temperature Limit The initial temperature range of Investigation exhaust gases shall be between 16°C (60°F) and 45°C (113°F) A FD-016 X 351.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.4 Mechanical Engineering Input Dewpoint Limit The initial dewpoint of Investigation exhaust gases shall be limited to 16°C (60°F) or less. Note: Dewpoint temperature (saturation temperature) is a function of dry bulb temperature and relative humidity.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p88,1,"Psychrometric charts may be referenced for a graphical depiction of this relationship. A FD-017 X 351.5.0.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.5.a Mechanical Engineering Acceptable Exhaust Gases Investigation exhaust gases vented into the VES shall be compatible with the wetted surface materials of the U.S. Lab. Materials in the wetted surfaces of the U.S. Lab which will be in contact with user payload waste gases are limited to: Stainless steel 321 and 450, Titanium 6A1-4V, Fluorocarbon rubber (Viton) (in Pirani gage transducer), Glass, Platinum-iridium alloy, Gold plated brass (in cold cathode transducer), Ceramic, Aluminum (in cold cathode transducer), Tetrafluoreeothylene (Teflon). Note: The Investigation is responsible for providing containment, storage, and transport hardware for gases that are incompatible with the vacuum exhaust or external environment. Where applicable, containment hardware for incompatible gases must meet the redundant container requirements specified in, NSTS 1700.7, ISS Addendum, section 209.1b. A X FD-018 X 351.5.0.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.5.b Mechanical Engineering Acceptable Exhaust Gases Investigation vented exhaust gases shall be non-reactive with other vent gas mixture constituents.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p88,2,A X FD-018 X X X 351.5.0.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.5.c Mechanical Engineering Acceptable Exhaust Gases Investigations venting to the VES or VRS shall provide a means of removing gases that would adhere to the tubing walls at a wall temperature of 4°C (40°F) and at a pressure of 10-3 torr. A X FD-018 X X 351.5.0.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.5.d Mechanical Engineering Acceptable Exhaust Gases Investigations venting to the VES or VRS shall remove particulates from the vent gases that are larger than 100 micrometers in size. A X FD-018 X X X 351.5.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.5.1 Mechanical Engineering Acceptable Gases-Initial List A list of acceptable exhaust gases with verified compatibility to the U.S. Lab wetted materials is specified in Appendix D. A list of unacceptable gases that are not compatible with the U.S. Lab wetted materials is specified in Appendix D. NVR A-73,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p89,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 351.5.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.5.2.a Mechanical Engineering External Contamination Control Investigation exhaust gases shall be compatible with the molecular column density, particulate, and deposition on external Space Station surfaces. a. The molecular column density created by the presence of ISS contamination sources along any unobstructed line of sight shall not exceed 1x1014 molecular-cm-2 for individual released species. A FD-019 X X X 351.5.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.5.2.b Mechanical Engineering External Contamination Control Investigation exhaust gases shall be compatible with the molecular column density, particulate, and deposition on external Space Station surfaces. b. The release of particulates from the ISS shall be limited to one particle 100 microns or larger per orbit per 1x10-5 steradian field of view as seen by a 1 meter diameter aperture telescope. A FD-019 X X X 351.5.2.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.5.2.c Mechanical Engineering External Contamination Control Investigation exhaust gases shall be compatible with the molecular column density, particulate, and deposition on external Space Station surfaces. c.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p89,1,"The flux of molecules emanating from the ISS shall be limited such that the 300K mass deposition rate on sampling surfaces shall be limited to 1x10-14g-cm-2-sec-1 (daily average). A FD-019 X X X 351.5.2.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.5.2.d Mechanical Engineering External Contamination Control Investigation exhaust gases shall be compatible with the molecular column density, particulate, and deposition on external Space Station surfaces. d. Investigations shall specify the material(s) to be dumped through the vacuum line. A FD-019 X X X 351.5.2.e MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.5.2.e Mechanical Engineering External Contamination Control Investigation exhaust gases shall be compatible with the molecular column density, particulate, and deposition on external Space Station surfaces. e. Investigations shall provide an absorbing filter for toxic materials that are dumped through the VES. A FD-019 X X X 351.5.4.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.5.4.a Mechanical Engineering Limit Amount of Vented Gases Investigations shall limit the total mass of vented gases (Mtot, kg) or the initial pressure (Po, psi) and volume (V, liters) of their vented gases, over any 9-minute period to one of the following limits: Mtot ≤ 0.0344(MolWt)1/2 or PoV ≤ 12606 / (MolWt)1/2 where MolWt is the average molecular weight of the vented gases. Note: Figure 31, total mass venting limit, illustrates the application of the equations in subparagraph A for the venting of air with an average molecular weight of 28.8 kg/kg-mole.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p89,2,"The purpose of these limits is to ensure that the Control Moment Gyros are not saturated, or ISS attitude rate limits are not exceeded, due to momentum caused by vent gas impingement upon ISS structures such as arrays and radiators. A FD-019 X X A-74",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p90,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 351.5.4.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.1.5.4.b Mechanical Engineering Limit Amount of Vented Gases Investigations shall limit the total mass of vented gases (Mtot, kg) or the initial pressure (Po, psi) and volume (V, liters) of their vented gases, over any 110-minute period to one of the following limits: Mtot ≤ 0.0549(MolWt)1/2 or PoV ≤ 20107 / (MolWt)1/2 where MolWt is the average molecular weight of the vented gases. Note: Figure 32, Pressure-volume venting limit, illustrates the application of the equations in subparagraph B for the venting of air with an average molecular weight of 28.8 kg/kg-mole. The purpose of these limits is to ensure that the Control Moment Gyros are not saturated, or ISS attitude rate limits are not exceeded, due to momentum caused by vent gas impingement upon ISS structures such as arrays and radiators. A FD-019 X X 352 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.2 Title Vacuum Resource System NVR 352.1.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.2.1.a Mechanical Engineering VRS Physical Interface The WV Investigation VRS QD is supplied on a 0.5"" swagelock body, part # SS-QC8-B1-810K1, and black in color located on the WV rear wall as indicated in Figure 2.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p90,1,"The Investigation provided QD shall be a swagelock stem, part # SS-QC8-D1-810K1 and shall be compatible with the WV VRS QD. In the part number the D indicates a double end shut-off and the K indicates the keying. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 D FD-021 352.1.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.2.1.b Mechanical Engineering VRS Physical Interface The maximum leak rate of the investigation volumes connected to the VRS shall be 2 x 10-3 scc/sec. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 T FD-021 352.1.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.2.1.c Mechanical Engineering VRS Physical Interface The Investigation shall ensure that the VRS manual shut-off valve will not be blocked during experiment operations. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 D FD-021 352.1.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.2.1.d Mechanical Engineering VRS Physical Interface Gases released by the Investigation shall be within the same restrictions as for exhaust gases defined in MSG IIRD Section 3.5.1.5. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 T & D FD-021 352.1.e MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.2.1.e Mechanical Engineering VRS Physical Interface The Investigation shall incorporate into the crew procedures a procedure that directs the operator to monitor the pressure sensor between the manual shut-off valve and the QD to insure leak tightness of QD interface for each unique vacuum hose during the first connection to the VRS.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p90,2,"N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I FD-021 352.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.2.2.a Mechanical Engineering Input Pressure Limit Investigations shall limit their vented VRS gas to a pressure of 10- 3 torr or less at the WV interface. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 T X FD-022 352.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.2.2.b Mechanical Engineering Input Pressure Limit Investigation volumes connected to the VRS shall be designed to the maximum design pressure (MDP) of at least 276 kPa (40 psia) with a safety factor of 1.5 based on MDP. Lines and fittings used to interface with the WV QD shall have an ultimate factor of safety equal to or greater than 4.0 based on MDP if the outside diameter <1.5"" and 2.0 if > 1.5"". Components connected to the VRS shall have an ultimate of 2.5 based on MDP. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A X FD-022 352.2.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.2.2.c Mechanical Engineering Input Pressure Limit Investigations shall protect against failure conditions which would exceed the VRS max design pressure of 276 kPa (40 psia). N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A X FD-022 352.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.2.3 Mechanical Engineering VRS Through-Put Limit The Investigation shall limit the gas throughput to the VRS to less than 1.2 x 10-3 scc/second. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 T FD-023 A-75",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p91,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 353 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.5.3 Mechanical Engineering Vacuum Outgassing Requirements Investigation non-metallic materials, which are exposed to pressures less than 1 torr via the MSG vacuum systems, shall meet the vacuum outgassing requirements of paragraph 4.2.7 of SSP 30233 and either of the following criteria: a. Non-metallic materials have an “A” rating for thermal vacuum stability in the Materials and Processes Technical Information System (MAPTIS) database. b. Non-metallic materials have an “B” rating for thermal vacuum stability in the MAPTIS database, but have been cured to meet the “A” requirements and the cure data is specified in the materials list. I FD-023 X X 360 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.6 Title Pressurized Gas Requirements NVR 361 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.6.1 Mechanical Engineering Nitrogen Interface Requirements The MSG provides the Investigation a direct interface connection to the ISS Nitrogen via a quick disconnect (QD), and a needle valve located on the rear wall of the WV. The system becomes active after opening the needle valve close to the CD. The MSG to ISS nitrogen interface is shown in Figure 30.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p91,1,"NVR 361.1.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.6.1.1.a Mechanical Engineering Nitrogen Physical Interface The WV Investigation nitrogen QD is supplied on a 0.25"" swagelock body, part #SS-QC4-B1-400, no key, and no color, located on the WV rear wall as shown in Figure 2. The Investigation provided nitrogen QD shall be a swagelock stem, part #SS-QC4-D1-400 and shall be compatible with the WV GN2 QD. In the part number the D indicates a double end shut-off. SAME will provide a QD with part number SS-QC4-D-400. I X FD-027 X 361.1.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.6.1.1.b Mechanical Engineering Nitrogen Physical Interface The maximum leak rate of the nitrogen connection and any permeation through materials from the WV QD to the Investigation shall be no greater than 10-3 scc/sec at MDP. All Nitrogen flowing past the GN2 valve is considered usage. The investigation allocation for GN2 will comprise leakage and usage. T FD-027 X X 361.1.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.6.1.1.c Mechanical Engineering Nitrogen Physical Interface Investigation fluid system connecting to the MSG nitrogen system shall be charged with nitrogen that meets the requirements specified in SSP 30573 Table 4.1-2.13, to a pressure no greater than 70 psia (480 kPa). Dispositioned changed to A by SAME-ECO- 202;",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p91,2,"reference MSFC-ICD-3642 T & I FD-027 361.1.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.6.1.1.d Mechanical Engineering Nitrogen Physical Interface Investigation fluid system connecting to the MSG nitrogen system shall meet the fluid system cleanliness levels specified in SSP 30573, Table 4.1-1.6, Operational Fluids T & I FD-027 X X 361.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.6.1.2 Mechanical Engineering Nitrogen Flow Control The MSG facility provides an orifice to control the flow of nitrogen so it does not exceed the ISS requirement of 5.43 kg/hr (12 lbm/hr) at an operating pressure of 517 to 931 kPa (75 to 135 psia). Investigations will provide their own means of controlling the flow of nitrogen if required. Test can be closed during physical or integrated testing with the EU. T FD-024 X X 361.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.6.1.3 Mechanical Engineering Nitrogen Interface Design Pressure The maximum design pressure (MDP) of the Investigation nitrogen system shall be 1,379 kPa (200 psia). Investigation volumes connected to the nitrogen system shall be designed with a safety factor 1.5 based on MDP. Lines and fittings used to interface with the WV QD shall have an ultimate factor of safety equal to or greater than 4.0 based on MDP if the outside diameter < 1.5"" and 2.0 if > 1.5"". Components connected to the nitrogen system shall have an ultimate of 2.5 based on MDP. T & A X FD-025 X X X X A-76",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p92,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 361.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.6.1.4 Mechanical Engineering Nitrogen Interface Temperature The Investigation nitrogen system will be designed for a nitrogen supply temperature range of 15.6°C to 45°C (60°F to 113°F). NVR 361.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.6.1.5 Mechanical Engineering Nitrogen Operational Constraints Investigations using the nitrogen system shall incorporate the following controls into their procedures: a. Oxygen sensor, provided by the investigation b. The crew is required to open a side port or front gloveport while fans are in Normal Mode so fresh cabin air can be sucked into the WV, filtered, and released. Investigation crew procedures shall direct the operator to purge the WV for two minutes prior to egress. c. Verify readings of oxygen sensor is above 10.5% O2 before opening the WV. d. Investigation crew procedures shall direct the operator to close the needle valve prior to QD mate/demate. Crew procedures to direct operator to monitor the pressure sensor between manual shut-off valve and QD to insure leak tightness of QD interface for each unique nitrogen hose during the first connection to the nitrogen system.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p92,1,"Items b and d are applicable Items a and c are not Disposition changed by SAME-ECO-202 SAME is not providing an oxygen sensor. I X FD-024 X X 362 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.6.2 Mechanical Engineering Pressurized Gas Systems Pressurized gas systems with a total expanded gas volume exceeding 400 liters at standard conditions shall limit the gas flow after a single failure to less than 240 standard liter per minute after 400 liters at standard conditions has been released to the cabin air. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A X FD-028 370 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7 Title Environment Interface Requirements NVR 371 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.1 Title Atmosphere Requirements NVR 371.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.1.1 Mechanical Engineering Pressure The Investigation shall be safe when exposed to the pressure transitions between 0 to 104.8 kPa (0 to 15.2 psia). Closed when safety verification is closed. T or A (PSRP) X (Safety) X X X 371.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.1.2 Mechanical Engineering Temperature The Investigation shall be safe when exposed to the temperatures of 10 to 46°C (50 to 115°F). Investigation must be safe for all potential temperature conditions related from failures to ISS, MSG, or investigation hardware. Closed when safety verification is closed.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p92,2,"T or A (PSRP) X (Safety) X X X 371.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.1.3 Mechanical Engineering Humidity The Investigation shall be designed to not cause condensation when exposed to a dewpoint of 4.4° to 15.6°C (40° to 60°F) and a relative humidity from 25% to 75% except when condensation is an intended operation of the Investigation. For reference, Figure 70 in Appendix H of the MSG IIRD depicts the temperature/humidity envelope defined by these dewpoints and relative humidity ranges for air (21% oxygen, 79% nitrogen) at one atmosphere pressure (14.7 psia). A EN-001 X 372.3.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.2.3.1 Electrical Engineering ICP Outlet Interface Requirements The WV ICP (J304) connector located on the WV rear wall and the ICP OUTLET (J334) connector located on the ICP are MS27656P15F35S type connectors. The connector characteristics and contact assignments are listed in Tables XXXII and XXXIII. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Requirements a. thru f. are not listed due to applicability being N/A. D & I & T EL-037 373.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.3.a Mechanical Engineering WV Environment The WV is capable of providing a Class 100K environment as defined in FED-STD-209. The WV provides a removal efficiency of 99.97% for particulate matter of 0.3 microns or larger in aerodynamic diameter. a. Investigations shall be safe when exposed to a WV negative pressure of ≥1.3 mbar relative to cabin pressure. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T or A X EN-007 X X X* A-77",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p93,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 373.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.3.b Mechanical Engineering WV Environment Investigations shall not cause an overpressurization of the WV, that being 20 mbar negative delta p and 15 mbar positive delta p. Investigations shall be two fault tolerant from preventing an overpressurization of the WV. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T or A X EN-007 X X X* 373.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.3.c Mechanical Engineering WV Environment Investigations that provide only one layer of containment for liquids in the WV shall limit the amount of liquid it contains to no more than 50 cc or the quantity stated in Table XXXIV, whichever is less, when using a fan speed setting of 4. These materials are compatible with the MSG WV, but Station may limit the use of them. Materials that could be released in the WV and any byproducts given off (etc. from combustion experiments) by the Investigation not listed in Table XXXIV, volumes larger than what is specified in Table XXXIV or require a fan speed setting greater that 4 shall be approved by the IIT. Materials that could be released in the WV shall be compatible with MSG hardware as defined MSG IIRD Section 3.9.1.2.b. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p93,1,"T or A X MP-004 X X X* 373.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.3.d Mechanical Engineering WV Environment Investigation hardware mounted in the WV shall physically fit within the available space and shall not protrude into the MSG WV exclusion zones, as defined in Appendix G. Flexible cables and hoses will be exempt from the exclusion zone requirements. In addition, Investigations may request a drawing package from the MSG IIT that illustrates the MSG WV dimensions and exclusion zones. Verification of the requirement shall be accomplished by a physical fit check with MSG. Investigations should also do a developmental fit check early in their design process. Note: These exclusion zones are primarily related to physical interference and airflow. Investigations are also required to meet human factors requirements specified in MSG IIRD Section 3.10. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T ME-046 X X X* 373.e MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.3.e Mechanical Engineering WV Environment Investigation hardware transferred to the MSG WV via the Side Loading Ports shall be designed to fit through a 406 mm diameter opening. Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T ME-046 X X X* 373.f MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.3.f Mechanical Engineering WV Environment Investigation hardware transferred to the MSG WV via the Airlock shall be designed to fit through the MSG Airlock front door and shall not protrude into the MSG Airlock exclusion zones, as defined in Appendix G.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p93,2,"Verification of this requirement shall be accomplished by a physical fit check with MSG. Investigations should also do a developmental fit check early in their design process. Investigations will work with the IIT for transfer requirements/procedures and tray location/dimensions when using the airlock. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Must test with EU, test results to be provided to IPIM. T ME-046 374 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.4 Title Investigation Use of Cabin Atmosphere NVR 374.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.4.1 Mechanical Engineering Active Air Exchange Active air exchange with the cabin atmosphere by Investigation shall be limited to air exchange for specimen metabolic purposes and for mass conservation purposes. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I EN-002 374.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.4.2 Mechanical Engineering Oxygen Consumption Investigations consumption of atmospheric oxygen shall not exceed 1.08 kg per day (2.38 lbs/day). A EN-003 X X A-78",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p94,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 374.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.4.3 Mechanical Engineering Chemical Releases Investigations that use chemicals that would create a toxicity problem or cause a hazard to ISS or MSG hardware if released shall provide adequate containment. Containment shall be provided by the use of a pressure vessel or the use of two or three redundantly sealed containers, depending on the toxicological hazard for a chemical with a vapor pressure below 15 psia. The Investigation must assure that each level of containment will not leak under the maximum use conditions (i.e. vibration, temperature, pressure, etc.). The release of methanol, ethanol, isopropyl alcohol, n-propyl alcohol, n-butyl alcohol, acetone, ethylene glycol, and propylene glycol into the ISS habitable environment shall be prohibited. The release of these compounds due to normal materials offgassing is exempt from this requirement. Closed when safety verification is closed. A & I (PSRP) X (Safety) X X X 374.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.4.4 Mechanical Engineering Cabin Air Heat Leak The sensible heat leak to the cabin air from the investigation hardware operating outside the WV shall be no greater than the maximum values identified in the Unique Investigation ICD.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p94,1,"These limits represent the total cabin air heat load capacity when the cabin temperature is at 18°C (65°F). N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A EN-002 375 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.5 Title Ionizing Radiation Requirements NVR 375.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.5.1 Electrical Engineering Investigation Contained or Generated Ionizing Radiation Investigations containing or using radioactive materials or that generate ionizing radiation shall comply with NSTS 1700.7, ISS Addendum, paragraph 212.1. Closed when safety verification is closed. A (PSRP) X (Safety) X X X 375.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.5.2 Electrical Engineering Ionizing Radiation Dose Investigations should expect a total dose of ionizing radiation of 30 Rads (Si) per year. NVR 375.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.5.3 Electrical Engineering Single Event Effect (SEE) Ionizing Radiation Investigations will be designed to not produce an unsafe condition or one that could cause damage to the MSG as a result of exposure to SEE ionizing radiation. Exposure levels are specified in SSP 30512, paragraph 3.2.1, assuming exposure levels with a shield thickness of 25.4 mm (1000 mils). NVR 376 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.7.6 Title Additional Environmental Conditions The environmental information provided in Appendix H is for design and analysis purposes. NVR 380 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.8 Mechanical Engineering Fire Protection Interface Requirements Investigations must not constitute an uncontrolled fire hazard to the ISS or other payloads.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p94,2,"The minimum use of flammable materials shall be the preferred means of hazard reduction. Closed when safety verification is closed. A (PSRP) X (Safety) X X X 381.1.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.8.1.1.a Mechanical Engineering Investigation Monitoring and Detection Fire detection shall be required where an investigation provides potential ignition sources and/or flammable materials. Some Investigations may, with hazard analysis and approval of the PSRP, rely on the fire detection capabilities of the MSG, as provided in section 3.8.1.c. Closed when safety verification is closed. A (PSRP) X (Safety) X A-79",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p95,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 381.1.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.8.1.1.b Mechanical Engineering Investigation Monitoring and Detection Parameter monitoring may be implemented by the Investigation to meet the fire detection requirement, if the MSG does not provide adequate coverage. This monitoring shall provide warning to the ISS, via the SPLC, prior to the initiation of a combustion event in order to allow timely action by the crew and/or ground control. This parameter monitoring may be used in conjunction with the MSG fire detection capability as stated in section 3.8.1.c. The Investigation shall comply with section 3.3.1.2.2.2.2 when implementing fire detection/annunciation requirements. Closed when safety verification is closed. A (PSRP) X (Safety) X 381.1.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.8.1.1.c Mechanical Engineering Investigation Monitoring and Detection Investigations that can not prove, by analysis, that loss of cooling does not produce a credible fire hazard, shall provide temperature sensors and parameters monitoring that will remove power from the payload in the case that the temperature goes above safe limits, as described in section 3.8.1.c. Closed when safety verification is closed.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p95,1,A (PSRP) X (Safety) X 381.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.8.1.2.a Mechanical Engineering Investigation Fire Suppression The WV provides a fire suppression port for use of the ISS PFE. The fire suppression port is covered with a fire port sticker. Figure 33 shows the location of the WV fire suppression port. Investigation shall provide a means for fire containment for any equipment that poses a credible risk of fire outside the envelope identified in paragraph 3.8.1. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 119; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A & I X ME-055 X X 381.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.8.1.2.b Mechanical Engineering Investigation Fire Suppression The WV provides a fire suppression port for use of the ISS PFE. The fire suppression port is covered with a fire port sticker. Figure 33 shows the location of the WV fire suppression port. Investigation hardware located outside the WV shall work with the IIT and PSRP on a case-by-case basis for fire detection and suppression requirements. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 D X ME-055 381.2.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.8.1.2.c Mechanical Engineering Investigation Fire Suppression The WV provides a fire suppression port for use of the ISS PFE. The fire suppression port is covered with a fire port sticker. Figure 33 shows the location of the WV fire suppression port. Investigations shall use materials and parts that meet the material requirements specified in paragraph 3.9 of the MSG IIRD.,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p95,2,"I X ME-055 X X X 391 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.9.1 Mechanical Engineering Materials and Parts Use and Selection Investigations shall use materials and parts that meet the materials requirements specified in NSTS 1700.7, ISS Addendum. This requirement also applies to all Commercial/Off the shelf (COTS) parts used in the investigation. For those NASA centers which participate in the NASA Materials and Processes Intercenter Agreement for ISS payloads, the Intercenter Agreement baselines the process for selection and certification of materials used in payload hardware to the safety requirements of NSTS 1700.7 and NSTS 1700.7 ISS Addendum, paragraphs 208.3 and 209 in their entirety. Whenever possible, materials should be selected that have already been shown to meet the applicable acceptance test criteria. Existing test data are compiled in the NASA Marshall Space Flight Center (MSFC) Materials and Process Technical Information System (MAPTIS) electronic database. See MSFC- RQMT-2888 for more information. Closed when safety verification is closed. A & I (PSRP) X (Safety) X X 391.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.9.1.2.a Mechanical Engineering Additional Investigation Material Requirements All Investigator hardware shall meet the toxicity offgassing acceptance requirements of NASA-STD-6001, Test 7. T & A X MP-004 X X X X A-80",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p96,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 391.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.9.1.2.b Mechanical Engineering Additional Investigation Material Requirements Investigations shall verify that any materials they could release into the WV, including sample material and byproducts of the investigation, are compatible with the MSG WV surfaces, Air Lock surfaces, filters, gloves and any other supplied hardware. The list of the WV materials to be analyzed is provided in Appendix F of the MSG IIRD. A X MP-004 X X 391.2.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.9.1.2.c Mechanical Engineering Additional Investigation Material Requirements Investigations shall maintain two or more levels of containment, depending on the toxicological hazard determined by the PSRP, during operations in the MSG if its materials or byproducts meet any of the following conditions. 1) they constitute a catastrophic toxic hazard according to the PSRP; 2) they are incompatible with the MSG hardware or other hardware that will occupy the MSG during the investigation's operations; 3) their volumes would saturate the MSG air filters or otherwise exceed the ability of the MSG to contain these materials if they were released into the WV. The PSRP may require a means of detecting an unintentional release of the material.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p96,1,"A X MP-004 X X 391.2.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.9.1.2.d Mechanical Engineering Additional Investigation Material Requirements Investigations using materials that are not listed in Table XXXIV shall provide their own cleaning tools/wipes etc., in case of spillage and take care of waste disposal. Investigation tools used for cleaning shall be approved by the IIT to insure the MSG WV will not be damaged. The investigation shall include in their crew procedures the wipe down of the accessible surfaces in the WV if released materials or byproducts will leave any residue on the WV surfaces and the equipment it contains to remove any resulting contamination. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 119; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A X MP-004 X X 391.2.e MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.9.1.2.e Mechanical Engineering Additional Investigation Material Requirements Investigations that cause condensation when exposed to the nominal ISS atmosphere as described in section 3.7.1.3 shall use fungus resistant materials. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A X MP-003 391.2.f MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.9.1.2.f Mechanical Engineering Additional Investigation Material Requirements Investigations will develop crew procedure steps for cleaning up liquid spillage or other release of materials within the WV, as defined in MSG IIRD Section 3.10.8.5.h., j. and k.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p96,2,"N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X MP-004 391.2.g MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.9.1.2.g Mechanical Engineering Additional Investigation Material Requirements The Investigation shall provide disclosure of the contents (including flammability, pH, toxicity) of all substances/samples including proprietary material used in or produced by the investigation to the JSC Toxicology Group in accordance with NSTS/ISS 13830 and JSC 27472. The Investigation shall comply with the toxic labeling standards in NSTS 07700, Volume XIV. Closed with submission of toxicological report. I X MP-004 X X 392 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.9.2 Mechanical Engineering Cleanliness Investigations shall conform to Visibly Clean-Sensitive (VC-S) cleanliness requirements. Visibly clean is defined as the absence of all particulate and nonparticulate matter visible to the normal unaided eye. I X X MP-002 X X X A-81",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p97,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 411 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.1 Mechanical Engineering Strength Requirements Forces and torque's required to remove, replace, operate, control, and maintain payload hardware and equipment on-orbit shall be equal to or less than the strength values given below. For operation and control of payload hardware equipment: a) Grip Strength - To remove, replace and operate payload hardware, grip strength required shall be less than 254 N (57lbf). b) Linear Forces - Linear forces required to operate or control hardware or equipment shall be less than the strength values for the 5th percentile female, defined as 50% of the strength values shown in Figure 34. c) Torques - Torques required to operate or control hardware or equipment shall be less than the strength values for the 5th percentile female, defined as 60% of the calculated 5th percentile male capability shown in Figure 35. Can be closed during crew training by crew usability acceptance. A or D X ST-005 X X X 412.1.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.2.1.1 Mechanical Engineering Closures or Covers Investigations closure/cover shall be provided for any area of the payload that is not designed for routine cleaning.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p97,1,"N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-007 412.1.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.2.1.2.a Mechanical Engineering Built-In Control Investigation containers of liquids or particulate matter shall have built-in equipment/methods for control of vaporization, material overflow, or spills. I X X X ME-008 X X X X X 412.1.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.2.1.2.b Mechanical Engineering Built-In Control Investigation capture elements, including grids, screens, or filter surfaces shall be accessible for replacement or cleaning without dispersion of the trapped materials. A or D X X ME-008 X X X 412.1.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.2.1.3 Mechanical Engineering One-handed Operation Investigation cleaning equipment and supplies shall be designed for one-handed operation or use. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 D X ME-009 412.2.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.2.2.1 Mechanical Engineering Continuous/Incidental Contact - High Temperature If the temperature of any Investigations hardware surface may exceed 49° C (120° F), and the surface may be exposed to continuous or incidental contact with the crewmember’s bare skin, then the investigation shall provide protective equipment to the crew and warning labels at the surface site as described in, NSTS 18798 Interpretation letter MA2-95-048. Closed when safety verification is closed.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p97,2,"A (PSRP) X (Safety) X X X 412.2.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.2.2.2 Mechanical Engineering Continuous/Incidental Contact - Low Temperature If the temperature of any Investigations surface may fall below - 18° C (0° F), and the surface may be exposed to continuous or incidental contact with the crewmember’s bare skin, then the investigation shall provide protective equipment to the crew and warning labels at the surface site as described in, NSTS 18798 Interpretation letter MA2-95-048. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Closed when safety verification is closed. A (PSRP) X (Safety) 412.2.3.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.2.2.3.a Mechanical Engineering Acoustic Requirements Investigations shall comply with the acoustic requirements in Table XXXV. The investigation shall perform an acoustics test on the hardware to verify the requirements are met. The sound pressure limits given in Table XXXV have the noise attenuation that the WV provides incorporated into them. Investigations not complying with the acoustic requirements shall require a waiver. T X EN-006 X X X A-82",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p98,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 412.2.3.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.2.2.3.b Mechanical Engineering Acoustic Requirements Investigations shall not exceed the A-weighted sound pressure level SPL limits during the Maximum Noise Duration as specified in Table XXXVI when the equipment is operating in the loudest expected configuration and mode of operation that can occur on orbit under planned operations. The Maximum Noise Duration is the total time that the investigation produces noise above the Continuous Noise Limits during a 24 hour time period. This duration is the governing factor in determining the allowable Intermittent Noise Limits. See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information. T X EN-006 X X X 412.2.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.2.2.4 Mechanical Engineering Lighting Design Investigation lighting sources shall be dimmable. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 D X ME-043 413.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.1 Mechanical Engineering Equipment Mounting Investigations items used during nominal operations and planned maintenance shall be designed, labeled, or marked to protect against improper installation. Demonstration can be closed during physical or integrated testing with the EU.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p98,1,"A or D X ME-011 X X X 413.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.2 Mechanical Engineering Unique Tools Investigation provided unique tools shall meet the applicable requirements defined as applicable in the MSG IIRD. Power tools shall be designed so the battery packs can be replaced and have a level-of-charge indicator or an indicator as to when the battery pack is required to be replaced or recharged. Dispositioned changed to A by SAME-ECO- 202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A X ME-016 413.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3 Title Connectors Investigation connectors shall meet the following requirements. NVR 413.3.01 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.1 Mechanical Engineering One-Handed Operation All Investigation connectors, whether operated by hand or tool, shall be designed and placed so they can be mated/demated using either hand. Must close with investigation set up in EU. D X ME-017 X X X* 413.3.02 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.2 Mechanical Engineering Accessibility Investigation connectors shall be designed so it is possible to mate/demate individual connectors without having to remove or mate/demate other connectors. Electrical connectors and cable installations shall permit disconnection and reconnection without damage to wiring connectors. Must close with investigation set up in EU. May require a waiver for sequential mating of electrical connectors.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p98,2,"D X ME-018 X X X* 413.3.03 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.3 Electrical Engineering Ease of Disconnect Electrical connectors shall require no more than two turns to disconnect. A X ME-017 X X X 413.3.04 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.4 Electrical Engineering Self Locking Payload electrical connectors shall provide a self-locking feature. A X ME-017 X X X X 413.3.05 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.5 Mechanical Engineering Connector Arrangement Space between connectors and adjacent obstructions shall be a minimum of 25mm (1inch) for access. Connectors in a single row or staggered rows which are removed sequentially by the crew shall provide 25mm (1inch) of clearance from other connectors and/or adjacent obstructions for 270 degrees of sweep around each connector beginning at the start of its removal/replacement sequence. Must close with investigation set up in EU. I X X ME-018 X X X* X 413.3.06 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.6 Electrical Engineering Arc Containment Electrical connector plugs shall be designed to confine/isolate the mate/demate electrical arcs or sparks. A X EL-026 X 413.3.07 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.7 Electrical Engineering Connector Protection Protection shall be provided for all demated connectors against physical damage and contamination. A X X ME-019 X X X 413.3.08 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.8 Electrical Engineering Connector Shape Investigations connectors shall use different connector shapes, sizes or keying to prevent mating connectors when lines differ in content.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p98,3,A X ME-019 X X X X A-83,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p99,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 413.3.09 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.9 Mechanical Engineering Fluid and Gas Line Connectors Investigations fluid and gas connectors that are mated and demated shall be located and configured so that they can be visually inspected for leakage. A X FD-001 X X X X 413.3.10 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.10 Mechanical Engineering Coding Both halves of mating connectors shall display a code or identifier that is unique to that connection. The labels or codes on connectors shall be located so they are visible when connected or disconnected. I X X ME-020 X X X 413.3.11 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.11 Electrical Engineering Pin Identification Each pin shall be uniquely identifiable in each electrical plug and each electrical receptacle, except for MSG facility and investigations, which shall have the option of either labeling every 10th pin or providing procedures/specifications that allow the pins to be identified. Crew procedures shall be provided through the OCR process for any flight maintenance required on- orbit to repair a connector. Close prior to integrated test.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p99,1,"I X X EL-007 X X X 413.3.12 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.12 Mechanical Engineering Orientation Grouped plugs and receptacles shall be oriented so that the aligning pins or equivalent devices are in the same relative position. A X ME-020 X X X X 413.3.13 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.3.13 Mechanical Engineering Alignment Marks or Guide Pins Mating parts shall have alignment marks in a visible location during mating or guide pins (or their equivalent). Must close with investigation set up in EU. I X X ME-020 X X X* 413.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.4 Title Fasteners Investigation provided fasteners that are removed on-orbit shall meet the following requirements. NVR 413.4.01 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.4.1 Mechanical Engineering Non-Threaded Fasteners Status Indication An indication of correct engagement (hooking, latch fastening, or proper positioning of interfacing parts) of non-threaded fasteners shall be provided. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I or D X ME-023 413.4.02 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.4.2 Mechanical Engineering Mounting Bolt/Fastener Spacing Clearance around fasteners to permit fastener hand threading (if necessary) shall be a minimum of 0.5 inches for the entire circumference of the bolt head and a minimum of 1.5 inches over 180 degrees of the bolt head and provide the tool handle sweep as seen in Figure 36. Must close with investigation set up in EU.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p99,2,"I X X ME-024 X X X* 413.4.03 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.4.3 Mechanical Engineering Multiple Fasteners When several fasteners are used on one item they shall be of identical type. I X X ME-025 X X X 413.4.04 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.4.4 Mechanical Engineering Captive Fasteners All fasteners planned to be installed and/or removed on-orbit shall be captive when disengaged. A X ME-026 X X X 413.4.05 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.4.5 Mechanical Engineering Quick Release Fasteners Quick release fasteners shall require a maximum of one complete turn to operate (quarter-turn fasteners are preferred). Quick release fasteners shall be positive locking in open and closed positions. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-026 413.4.06 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.4.6 Mechanical Engineering Threaded Fasteners Only right handed threads shall be used. I X X ME-026 X X X 413.4.07 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.4.7 Mechanical Engineering Over Center Latches Nonself-latching - Over center latches shall include a provision to prevent undesired latch element realignment, interface, or reengagement. Latch lock - Latch catches shall have locking features. Latch handles - If the latch has a handle, the latch handle and latch release shall be operable by one hand. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-027 413.4.08 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.4.8 Mechanical Engineering Winghead Fasteners Winghead fasteners shall fold down and be retained flush with surfaces. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-026 A-84",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p100,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 413.4.09 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.4.9 Mechanical Engineering Fastener Head Type Hex type external or internal grip or combination head fasteners shall be used where on-orbit crew actuation is planned. If a smooth surface is required, flush or oval head internal hex grip fasteners shall be used for fastening. Slotted fasteners shall not be used to carry launch loads for hard-mounted equipment. Slotted fasteners are allowed in nonstructural applications (e.g., computer data connectors, stowed commercial equipment). Dispositioned changed to A by SAME-ECO- 202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-028 413.4.10 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.4.10 Mechanical Engineering One-Handed Actuation Fasteners planned to be removed or installed on-orbit shall be designed and placed so they can be mated/demated using either hand. Must close with investigation set up in EU. D X ME-029 X X X* 413.4.12 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.3.4.12 Mechanical Engineering Access Holes Covers or shields through which mounting fasteners must pass for attachment to the basic chassis of the unit shall have holes for passage of the fastener without precise alignment (and hand or necessary tool if either is required to replace).",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p100,1,"N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Must close with investigation set up in EU. I X ME-024 414 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4 Mechanical Engineering Controls and Displays Investigation controls and displays shall meet the following requirements. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X 414.01 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.1 Mechanical Engineering Controls Spacing Design Requirements All spacing between controls and adjacent obstructions shall meet the minimum requirements for ungloved operations as shown in Figure 37. When operations require gloved hands the preferred requirements will be met. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-030 414.02 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.2 Mechanical Engineering Protective Methods Payloads shall provide protection against accidental control actuation using one or more of the protective methods listed in sub-paragraphs a. through c. below. Infrequently used controls (i.e. those used for calibration) should be separated from frequently used controls. Leverlock switches or switch covers are strongly recommended for switches related to mission success. Switch guards may not be sufficient to prevent accidental actuation. Note: Displays and controls used only for maintenance and adjustments, which could disrupt normal operations if activated, should be protected during normal operations, e.g., by being located separately or guarded/covered. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202;",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p100,2,"reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-031 414.02.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.2.a Mechanical Engineering Recess, shield, or otherwise surround the controls by physical barriers. The control shall be entirely contained within the envelope described by the recess or barrier. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-031 414.02.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.2.b Mechanical Engineering Cover or guard the controls. Safety or lock wire shall not be used. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-031 414.02.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.2.c Mechanical Engineering Cover guards when open shall not cover or obscure the protected control or adjacent controls. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-031 414.02.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.2.d Mechanical Engineering Locate and orient the controls so that the operator is not likely to strike or move them accidentally in the normal sequence of control movements. NVR 414.02.e MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.2.e Mechanical Engineering Provide the controls with interlocks so that extra movement (e.g., lifting switch out of a locked detent position) or the prior operation of a related or locking control is required. NVR 414.02.f MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.2.f Mechanical Engineering Provide the controls with resistance (i.e., viscous or coulomb friction, spring-loading, or inertia) so that definite or sustained effort is required for actuation.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p100,3,"NVR 414.02.g MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.2.g Mechanical Engineering Provide the controls with a lock to prevent the control from passing through a position without delay when strict sequential actuation is necessary (i.e., the control moved only to the next position, then delayed). NVR 414.03 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.3 Mechanical Engineering Noninterference Investigation provided protective devices shall not cover or obscure other displays or controls. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-030 A-85",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p101,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 414.04 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.4 Mechanical Engineering Barrier Guards Barrier guard spacing shall adhere to the requirements for use with the toggle switches, rotary switches, and thumbwheels as shown in Figures 37 and 38. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-030 414.05 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.5 Mechanical Engineering Recessed Switch Protection When a barrier guard is not used, rotary switches that control critical functions shall be recessed as shown in Figure 38. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-031 414.06 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.6 Mechanical Engineering Position Indication When payload switch protective covers are used, control position shall be evident without requiring cover removal. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-032 414.07 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.7 Mechanical Engineering Hidden Controls Controls that cannot be directly viewed will be avoided. If present, hidden controls shall be guarded to protect against inadvertent actuation.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p101,1,"N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-031 414.08 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.8 Mechanical Engineering Hand Controllers Hand controllers, excluding trackballs and mouse, shall have a separate on/off control to prevent inadvertent actuation when the controller is not in use. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-031 414.09.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.9.a Mechanical Engineering Valve Controls Requirements for design of investigation valve controls are defined as follows: Low-Torque Valves - Valves requiring 1 N-m (10 in-lb) or less for operation are classified as ""low-torque"" valves and shall be provided with a ""central pivot"" type handle, 5.5 cm (2.25 in) or less in diameter. (see Figure 39) N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-033 414.09.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.9.b Mechanical Engineering Valve Controls Requirements for design of investigation valve controls are defined as follows: Intermediate-Torque Valves - Valves requiring between 1 and 2 N-m (10 and 20 in-lb) for operation are classified as ""intermediate torque"" valves and shall be provided with a ""central pivot"" type handle, 5.5 cm (2.25 in) or greater in diameter, or a ""lever end pivot type"" handle, 7.5 cm (3 in) or greater in length. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-033 414.09.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.9.c Mechanical Engineering Valve Controls Requirements for design of investigation valve controls are defined as follows:",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p101,2,"High-Torque Valves - Valves requiring 2 N-m (20 in-lb) or more for operation are classified as ""high-torque"" valves and shall be provided ""lever type"" handles greater than 7.5 cm (3 in) or greater in length. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-033 414.09.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.9.d Mechanical Engineering Valve Controls Requirements for design of investigation valve controls are defined as follows: Handle Dimensions - Valve handles shall adhere to the clearances and dimensions illustrated in Figure 39, Valve Handle- Central Pivot Type and Figure 40, Valve Handle-Lever Type. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-033 414.09.e MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.9.e Mechanical Engineering Valve Controls Requirements for design of investigation valve controls are defined as follows: Rotary Valve Controls - Rotary valve controls shall open the valve with a counter-clockwise motion. N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-033 414.10 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.4.10 Mechanical Engineering Toggle Switches Dimensions for a standard toggle switch shall conform to the values presented in Figure 41. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X ME-034 415.1.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.5.1.a Mechanical Engineering Stowage Container Contents Restraints Investigation container (i.e. small parts box, sample holder) contents shall be restrained in such a way that the items do not float when the container is opened or closed. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 202;",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p101,3,reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Foam and stowage containers provided by MSG. A & I X ME-036 X X X X X A-86,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p102,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 415.1.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.5.1.b Mechanical Engineering Stowage Container Contents Restraints Investigation container contents shall be restrained in a way such that the items do not jam the drawer when the container is opened or closed. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Foam and stowage containers provided by MSG. D X ME-036 X X X X 415.1.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.5.1.c Mechanical Engineering Stowage Container Contents Restraints Investigation container contents shall be restrained in such a way that the contents can be removed/replaced without using a tool. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Foam and stowage containers provided by MSG. D X ME-036 X X X X 415.2.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.5.2.a Mechanical Engineering Stowage and Equipment Containers Investigation container (i.e. small parts box, sample holder) latches, handles, and operating mechanisms shall be designed to be latched/unlatched and opened/closed with one hand by the 95th percentile American male to the 5th percentile female. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Foam and stowage containers provided by MSG.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p102,1,"I X ME-027 X X X X X 415.2.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.5.2.b Mechanical Engineering Stowage and Equipment Containers The design of latches shall be such that their status (locked/unlocked) can be determined through visual inspection. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Foam and stowage containers provided by MSG. I X ME-027 X X X X 415.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.5.3 Mechanical Engineering Captive Parts Investigation equipment shall be designed in such a manner to ensure that all unrestrained parts (e.g., locking pins, knobs, handles, lens covers, access plates, or similar devices) that may be temporarily removed on orbit will be tethered or otherwise held captive. The MSG provides Velcro Strips that may be attached within the WV for restraining investigation equipment. I X X ME-036 X X X 415.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.5.4 Mechanical Engineering Handles and Restraints Investigation hardware items that are larger than 1ft3 shall be provided with handles or other suitable means of grasping, tethering, carrying, and restraining. I or D X ME-037 X X X X 415.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.5.5 Mechanical Engineering Handle Location/Front Access Handles and grasp areas shall be placed on the accessible surface of a payload item consistent with the removal direction. I X X ME-037 X X X X 416 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.6 Mechanical Engineering Handle Dimensions Handles for movable or portable units shall be designed in accordance with the minimum applicable dimensions in Figure 42.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p102,2,"I or D X ME-037 X X X X 416.1.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.6.1.a Mechanical Engineering Non-Fixed Handles Design Requirements Hinged, foldout, or attachable (i.e., non-fixed) handles shall comply with the following: a. Nonfixed handles shall have a stop position for holding the handle perpendicular to the surface on which it is mounted. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A & D X ME-037 416.1.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.6.1.b Mechanical Engineering Non-Fixed Handles Design Requirements Hinged, foldout, or attachable (i.e., non-fixed) handles shall comply with the following: b. Nonfixed handles shall be capable of being placed in the use position by one hand and shall be capable of being removed or stowed with one hand. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 D X ME-037 416.1.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.6.1.c Mechanical Engineering Non-Fixed Handles Design Requirements Hinged, foldout, or attachable (i.e., non-fixed) handles shall comply with the following: c. Attached/removable handles shall incorporate tactile and/or visual indication of locked/unlocked status. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I & D X ME-037 A-87",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p103,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 417 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.7 Mechanical Engineering Identification Labeling The development of labels shall be a joint process requiring the cooperative efforts of ISS Payload Label Approval Team (IPLAT) at JSC, MSG Investigation Integration Team and the MSG Investigator. Investigation elements, loose equipment, stowage containers, consumables, crew accessible connectors and cables, switches, indicators, and controls shall be labeled. Labels are markings of any from (including IMS barcodes) such as decals and placards, which can be adhered, “silk screened”, engraved, or otherwise applied directly to the hardware. The Investigator shall provide label drawings, label location drawings and information sufficient to enable IPLAT to determine that the labeling instructions are met. The labeling instructions are found in Appendix C of MSFC-RQMT-2888. See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information. I X ME-057 X X X 418.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.2 Electrical Engineering Mismatched The design of electrical connectors shall make it impossible to inadvertently reverse a connection or mate the wrong connectors if a hazardous condition can be created.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p103,1,"Payload and on-orbit support equipment, wire harnesses, and connectors shall be designed such that no blind connections or disconnections must be made during payload installation, operation, removal, or maintenance on orbit or during ground operations at KSC unless the design includes scoop proof connectors or other protective features. For payload equipment, for which mismating or cross- connection may damage ISS or MSG provided equipment, plugs, and receptacles (connectors), shall be selected and applied such that they cannot be mismatched or cross-connected in the intended system as well as adjacent systems. Although identification markings or labels are required, the use of identification alone is not sufficient to preclude mismating. See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information. Disposition changed to A (applicable) by SAME- ECO-229 Demonstration can be closed during physical or integrated testing with the EU. I or D X X ME-019 X X X X 418.3.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.3.1 Electrical Engineering Device Accessibility An overload protective device shall not be accessible without opening a door or cover, except that an operating handle or operating button of a circuit breaker, the cap of an extractor-type fuse holder, and similar parts may project outside the enclosure.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p103,2,Disposition changed to A (applicable) by SAME- ECO-229 I X EL-013 X X X X 418.3.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.3.2 Electrical Engineering Extractor-Type Fuse Holder The design of the extractor-type fuse holder shall be such that the fuse is extracted when the cap is removed. Demonstration can be closed during physical or integrated testing with the EU. D X EL-013 X X X 418.3.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.3.3 Electrical Engineering Overload Protection Location Overload protection (fuses and circuit breakers) intended to be manually replaced or physically reset on-orbit shall be located where they can be seen and replaced or reset without removing other components. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 119; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X EL-013 X X X 418.3.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.3.4 Electrical Engineering Overload Protection Identification Each overload protector (fuse or circuit breaker) intended to be manually replaced or physically reset on-orbit shall be readily identified or keyed for its proper value. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X EL-013 X X X 418.3.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.3.5 Electrical Engineering Automatic Restart Protection Controls shall be employed that prevent automatic restarting after an overload-initiated Shutdown.,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p103,3,A X EL-013 X X X 418.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.4 Mechanical Engineering Sharp Edges and Corners Protection Investigation design within a pressurized module shall protect crewmembers from sharp edges and corners during all crew operations. Translation paths and adjacent equipment shall be designed to minimize the possibility of entanglement or injury to crewmembers. Closed when safety verification is closed. I (PSRP) X X X (Safety) X X X X A-88,,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p104,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 418.4.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.4.1 Mechanical Engineering Holes Holes that are round or slotted in the range of 10.0 to 25.0 mm (0.4 to 1.0 in.) shall be covered. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A & I X X ME-007 X X X X 418.4.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.4.2 Mechanical Engineering Latches Latches that pivot, retract, or flex so that a gap of less than 35 mm (1.4 in) exists shall be designed to prevent entrapment of a crewmember's appendage. Disposition changed to A (applicable) by SAME- ECO-229 I X X ME-027 X X X X 418.4.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.4.3 Mechanical Engineering Screws and Bolts Threaded ends of screws and bolts accessible by the crew and extending more than 3.0 mm (0.12 in) shall be capped to protect against sharp threads. A & I X X X ME-026 X X X X X 418.4.4 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.4.4 Mechanical Engineering Securing Pins Securing pins shall be designed to prevent their inadvertently backing out above the handhold surface.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p104,1,"N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A X X ME-053 418.4.5 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.4.5 Mechanical Engineering Levers, Cranks, Hooks, and Controls Levers, cranks, hooks, and controls shall not be located where they can pinch, snag, or cut the crewmembers or their clothing. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 A & I X X ME-053 X X X X 418.4.6 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.4.6 Mechanical Engineering Burrs Exposed surfaces shall be free of burrs. I X X X ME-053 X X X 418.4.7 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.4.7 Mechanical Engineering Lockwire Investigations shall not use safety/lock wires on any fasteners. Disposition changed to N/A by SAME-ECO- 202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X X ST-009 X X X 418.5.a MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.5.a Operations Procedural Hazard Controls Investigations shall be responsible for using crew procedures to control the MSG hazards specified below. Depending on the investigation, some of these hazards may not be applicable. a. Investigation crew procedures shall have a step(s) that directs the crew to purge the MSG work volume for 2 minutes following the use of the Nitrogen system. If the MSG is in normal mode, then the work volume can be purged by opening a sideport or gloveport. (Reference Payload Hazard Report (PHR) MSG-FL4, control 1) I X CP-001 X 418.5.b MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.5.b Operations Procedural Hazard Controls Investigations shall be responsible for using crew procedures to control the MSG hazards specified below.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p104,2,"Depending on the investigation, some of these hazards may not be applicable. b. Investigation crew procedures shall have a step(s) that directs the crew to verify all experiment power outlets are OFF prior to any mating/demating of connectors in the work volume. (Reference PHR MSG-G2, control 6.2) I X CP-001 X 418.5.c MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.5.c Operations Procedural Hazard Controls Investigations shall be responsible for using crew procedures to control the MSG hazards specified below. Depending on the investigation, some of these hazards may not be applicable. c. Investigation crew procedures shall be written such that any ICP mating occurs prior to experiment activation and any ICP demating occurs after experiment deactivation. (Reference PHR MSG-G2, control 4.3) N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X CP-001 A-89",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p105,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 418.5.d MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.5.d Operations Procedural Hazard Controls Investigations shall be responsible for using crew procedures to control the MSG hazards specified below. Depending on the investigation, some of these hazards may not be applicable. d. Investigation crew procedures shall contain the following text whenever the investigation requires the core facility to be extracted or inserted: ""While holding the MSG Short Handrail with the left hand, use right hand to: Push knob, slide Core Facility to retracted (inserted) position. Pull knob (secures locking mechanism) √Core Facility position securely locked"" (Reference PHR MSG- G4, control 2.2) I X CP-001 X 418.5.e MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.5.e Operations Procedural Hazard Controls Investigations shall be responsible for using crew procedures to control the MSG hazards specified below. Depending on the investigation, some of these hazards may not be applicable. e. Investigation crew procedures shall contain the following text whenever the MSG Airlock door is being opened or closed:",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p105,1,"""MSG AL Front Door left latches (two) → OPEN Open MSG AL Front Door (door swings to right)"" (Reference PHR MSG-G4 control 2.3) N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X CP-001 418.5.f MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.5.f Operations Procedural Hazard Controls Investigations shall be responsible for using crew procedures to control the MSG hazards specified below. Depending on the investigation, some of these hazards may not be applicable. f. Investigation crew procedures shall have a step(s) that instructs the crew to inspect the MSG gloves prior to the start of experiment operations. (Reference PHR MSG-GB1, control 4.3) N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 No gloves are required. I X CP-001 418.5.g MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.5.g Operations Procedural Hazard Controls Investigations shall be responsible for using crew procedures to control the MSG hazards specified below. Depending on the investigation, some of these hazards may not be applicable. g. Investigation crew procedures shall have a step(s) that instruct the crew to insert a closing cap on the MSG glove port should a glove rupture. (Reference PHR MSG-GB1, control 4.5) N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 No gloves are required. I X CP-001 418.5.h MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.5.h Operations Procedural Hazard Controls Investigations shall be responsible for using crew procedures to control the MSG hazards specified below. Depending on the investigation, some of these hazards may not be applicable. h.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p105,2,"Investigations shall develop maintenance crew procedures in the event of a material spill or leak. (Reference PHR MSG-GB1, control 11.1) I X CP-001 X 418.5.i MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.5.i Operations Procedural Hazard Controls Investigations shall be responsible for using crew procedures to control the MSG hazards specified below. Depending on the investigation, some of these hazards may not be applicable. i. Investigation crew procedures shall have a step(s) that instructs the crew to close the vacuum resource/exhaust valve in the event of leakage of vacuum resource or vacuum exhaust lines. (Reference PHR MSG-GB3, control 3.3) I X CP-001 X A-90",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p106,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 418.5.j MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.5.j Operations Procedural Hazard Controls Investigations shall be responsible for using crew procedures to control the MSG hazards specified below. Depending on the investigation, some of these hazards may not be applicable. j. Investigation crew procedures shall have a step(s) that instructs the crew to notify the POIC should any type of leak or spill occur. (Reference PHR MSG-IntFL02, control 1.2 & MSG-Int- FL03, control 1.3) I X CP-001 X 418.5.k MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.8.5.k Operations Procedural Hazard Controls Investigations shall be responsible for using crew procedures to control the MSG hazards specified below. Depending on the investigation, some of these hazards may not be applicable. k. Investigation crew procedures shall be developed for instructing the crew on how to use the investigation clean up equipment. (Reference PHR MSG-Int-FL03, control 1.2) N/A - reference MSFC-ICD-3642 I X CP-001 419 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.9 Mechanical Engineering Payload In-Flight Maintenance Investigation hardware designed to be maintainable shall use Space Station provided onboard tools. A list of available tools for on-orbit use can be found at:",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p106,1,"http://iss- www.jsc.nasa.gov/ss/issapt/opsipt/mresup/mresup_home. html A X ME-003 X X X X 421 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.10.11 Mechanical Engineering Egress Investigations egress requirements shall be in accordance with 1700.7, ISS Addendum, paragraph 205. Disposition changed to A by SAME-ECO-202; reference MSFC-ICD-3642 Closed when safety verification is closed. (PSRP) X X (Safety) X X X X 431 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.11 Multiple Safety Investigations shall comply with the safety requirements contained in section 4.3 of MSFC-RQMT-2888. The following paragraphs define what data is considered Health and Status Data and information on the Caution and Warning classifications supported by the MSG C&DH system. See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information. Closed when safety verification is closed. (PSRP) X (Safety) X X X 431.0.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The C&W flags fields in the health and status packet shall be asserted only upon the conditions listed in Section 6.2 of SAME- DOC-004. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-3-4] T X X X 431.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.11 Software Engineering Safety Investigations shall format the caution and warning word in accordance with the Experiment Health and Status Data for the SPLC and MLC, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001 as a warning when the investigation sensors detect the following conditions: See MSFC- RQMT-2888 for conditions.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p106,2,"(PSRP) X (Safety) X X X 431.1.a SAME-DOC-004 6.2.1 Software Engineering There are no conditions that constitute a ""Class 2 - Warning"" condition and a subsequent set of the Warning bit in the Health and Status packet payload ECW. NVR 431.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.11 Software Engineering Safety Investigations shall format the caution and warning word in accordance with the Experiment Health and Status Data for the SPLC and MLC, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001 as a caution when the investigation sensors detect the following conditions: See MSFC- RQMT-2888 for conditions. (PSRP) X (Safety) X X X 431.2.a SAME-DOC-004 6.2.2 Software Engineering There are no conditions that constitute a ""Class 3 - Caution"" condition and a subsequent set of the Caution bit in the Health and Status packet payload ECW. NVR 431.3 MSFC-RQMT-2888 3.11 Software Engineering Safety Investigations that require an advisory shall format the caution and warning word in accordance with the Experiment Health and Status Data for the SPLC and MLC, of MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001 as an advisory. Advisories are set for the following conditions: See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for conditions. (PSRP) X (Safety) X X X A-91",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p107,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 431.3.a SAME-DOC-004 6.2.3 Software Engineering There are no conditions that constitute a ""Class 4 - Advisory"" condition and a subsequent set of the Advisory bit in the Health and Status packet payload ECW. NVR 443 MSFC-RQMT-2888 4.3 Title Safety Verification Requirements NVR 443.1 MSFC-RQMT-2888 4.3.1 Multiple Flight Safety requirements are found in NSTS 1700.7, Safety Policy and Requirements for Payloads Using the Space Transportation System and NSTS 1700.7 ISS Addendum, Safety Policy and Requirements for Payloads Using the International Space Station. Information on Payload Safety Reviews and required data is found in NSTS 13830, Payload Safety Review and Data Submittal Requirements. The investigator shall submit safety verification data as specified by the investigator developed payload hazard reports. See MSFC-RQMT-2888 for more information. (PSRP) X (Safety) X 443.2 MSFC-RQMT-2888 4.3.2 Multiple Ground Ground safety requirements are found in KHB 1700.7, Space Shuttle Payload Ground Safety Handbook. Information on Ground Safety Reviews and required data is found in NSTS 13830, Payload Safety Review and Data Submittal Requirements.",,False
PSI-101,Raw Data,SAME_VPRED.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Raw Data/SAME_VPRED.pdf,p107,1,"The investigator shall submit ground safety verification data as specified by the investigator developed ground safety hazard reports. (PSRP) X (Safety) X 450 MSFC-RQMT-2888 C.3.4.10 Mechanical Engineering Barcoding Investigators will coordinate with the MSG IIT and the IIT will coordinate with NASA/JSC organization OC for Inventory Management System (IMS) barcodes. All equipment shall have an IMS barcode in accordance with SSP 50007. IMS barcode placeholders shall be present on engineering drawings, if the standard hardware ID label (with IMS barcode included) is not used. If the IMS barcode is ordered from the DDPF, the Decal Catalog decal part number should be included in a note on the engineering drawing. I X ME-041 X X A-92",,False
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,PSI-101_Engineering Documents_60089MFHD509 P-Trak Condenser Tube Modification.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_60089MFHD509 P-Trak Condenser Tube Modification.pdf,p1,0,"Description: C-size modification drawing of an existing P-Trak condenser tube: a flanged tube whose bore is machined with 60 fine longitudinal knife-edge grooves. Section A-A (side), end view, Detail B (groove profile, scale 50/1) and Detail C (grooved bore end, scale 8/1). ### Title block - Title: P-TRAK CONDENSER TUBE MODIFICATION; next assembly: P-TRAK ASSEMBLY 60089MFHA500 - Drawing number: 60089MFHD509, size C, rev - (no revision letter; revisions table empty) - Organisation: National Aeronautics and Space Administration, John H. Glenn Research Center, Lewis Field, Cleveland, Ohio; ZIN Technologies - Project lead, quality, engineer, designer, reviewer: [name omitted], dated 6/21/05 to 6/22/05; safety: NA - Contract number: NAS3-99154; delivery order: MRDOC-074; file location: M:\MRDOC\60089_074; CAD package: Pro/E Wildfire - Scale 4/1; initial release date 6/22/05; sheet 1 of 1; bldg ZIN; area GRC; units: inch; discipline: mechanical - ""This drawing is under configuration control: do not revise without approved ECO"". Approved: [name omitted], date 6/22/05 ### Tolerances (unless otherwise specified, dimensions in inches) | | | |---|---| | .X | ± 0.03 | | .XX | ± 0.01 | | .XXX | ± 0.005 | | FRACTION | ± - | | ANGULAR | ± 0.50° | | BREAK EDGES | N/A | Dimensioning and tolerancing per ASME Y14.5M-1994.",,True
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,PSI-101_Engineering Documents_60089MFHD509 P-Trak Condenser Tube Modification.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_60089MFHD509 P-Trak Condenser Tube Modification.pdf,p1,1,"### Notes 1. EXISTING P-TRAK CONDENSER TUBE NO. 3001737-8, MODIFY AS SHOWN WHILE PROTECTING ALL OTHER EXISTING SURFACES FROM ANY DAMAGE. (MATL: ALUMINUM). 2. 16 Ra ALL MACHINED SURFACES. 3. CLEAN SURFACES WITH ISOPROPYL OR ETHYL ALCOHOL. AIR DRY. WIPE WITH CLEAN COTTON SWABS AND/OR LINT-FREE CLOTH. 4. HANDLE WITH CLEAN LINT FREE GLOVES. 5. BEFORE STORAGE, PROTECT WITH CONTAMINATION BARRIER. USE CLEAN, NEW, TRANSPARENT POLYETHYLENE BAG FOR SMALL ITEMS, OR 4 MIL THICK MINIMUM POLYETHYLENE SHEET AND SEAL. IDENTIFY BY TAGGING WITH PART NUMBER AND SERIAL/LOT NUMBER AS SPECIFIED IN WORK ORDER, PER SAE AS478, METHOD 35. ### Section A-A (dimensions in inches) - Overall length (1.438); grooved length 1.375 - Flange thickness (.080); (.050) - Tube outer diameter (Ø.307); bore (Ø.188); (Ø.250) - ""SEE DETAIL C"" at the grooved end ### End view - (Ø.376), (Ø.997) flange; ""SEE DETAIL B""; section line A-A ### Detail B (60 PLACES EQUALLY SPACED, SCALE 50/1) - Groove depth .0095/.0090 - R.0015 MAX at groove root - 30°0' and 60°0' groove angles - SHARP CORNERS (KNIFE EDGES) ### Detail C (SCALE 8/1) - 60X R.250 [?]; (1.375) grooved length",,True
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,PSI-101_Engineering Documents_60089MFHA150-B Aging Chamber Assembly.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_60089MFHA150-B Aging Chamber Assembly.pdf,p1,0,"Description: D-size assembly drawing of the SAME aging chamber assembly: cylindrical chamber with top plate, piston, fan assembly, inlet elbow/LED board, inlet and outlet gate valves and outlet elbow. Several elevation views, a top view, a bottom view, an isometric view (""THIS VIEW SHOWN WITH TOP PLATE REMOVED""), with item balloons and torque callouts. Commercial parts list and drawing list at left, notes at upper right. ### Title block - Title: AGING CHAMBER ASSEMBLY; next assembly: SAME IN MSG ASSEMBLY 60089MFHA000 - Drawing number: 60089MFHA150, size D, rev B - Organisation: National Aeronautics and Space Administration, John H. Glenn Research Center, Lewis Field, Cleveland, Ohio; ZIN Technologies - Project lead / quality / safety / engineer / designer / reviewer: [name omitted], dated 07/18/05 (project lead 07/18/06 [?]) - Contract number: NAS3-99154; delivery order: MRDOC-074; file location: M:\MRDOC\60089_074; CAD package: Pro/E Wildfire - Scale 1/4; initial release date 07/18/05; sheet 1 of 1; bldg ZIN; area GRC; units: inch; discipline: mechanical - ""This drawing is under configuration control: do not revise without approved ECO"". Approved: [name omitted], date 12/19/06 ### Revisions | CHG | ZONE | DESCRIPTION | APP/DATE | |---|---|---|---| | A | - | INCORPORATED SAME-ECO-134 | [name omitted] 06/22/06 | | B | - | INCORPORATED SAME-ECO-182 | [name omitted] 12/19/06 |",,True
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,PSI-101_Engineering Documents_60089MFHA150-B Aging Chamber Assembly.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_60089MFHA150-B Aging Chamber Assembly.pdf,p1,1,"### Tolerances (unless otherwise specified, dimensions in inches) .X ± 0.03; .XX ± 0.01; .XXX ± 0.005; FRACTION ± -; ANGULAR ± 0.50°; BREAK EDGES N/A. Dimensioning and tolerancing per ASME Y14.5M-1994. ### Notes 1. CERTIFICATE OF CONFORMANCE REQUIRED FOR ALL COMMERCIAL PARTS. 2. SAFETY CRITICAL ASSEMBLY (PRESSURE). 3. SAFETY CRITICAL FASTENERS (ITEM C9) TO BE INSTALLED PER MSFC-STD-486B. 4. (flag) APPLY THREADLOCKING COMPOUND PER MIL-S-46163 TO THREADED FASTENERS. 5. (flag) APPLY TWO COMPLETE WRAPS OF TEFLON TAPE TO FITTING THREADS IN DIRECTION OF THREAD. DO NOT OVERLAP FIRST THREAD AT END OF FITTING. FIRMLY PRESS TAPE IN PLACE AT OVERLAP POINT. 6. (flag) FOLLOWING FINAL TORQUE OF FASTENERS, APPLY RTV (ITEM C13) TO EXPOSED THREADS WHERE REQUIRED TO PREVENT SHARP EDGES. 7. (flag) SECURE ALL FLEXIBLE TUBING CONNECTIONS WITH HALAR TIE WRAPS (ITEM C18). 8. (flag) SECURE FITTING WITH KAPTON TAPE IN LOCATION SHOWN, FOR STORAGE ONLY. 9. (flag) OVERWRAP FLEXIBLE TUBING WITH VITON SHRINK WRAP. SIZE ACCORDING TO FIT. 10. CLEAN ALL SURFACES WITH ISOPROPYL ALCOHOL AND LET AIR DRY. WIPE WITH CLEAN COTTON SWABS AND/OR LINT FREE CLOTH. 11. HANDLE WITH CLEAN LINT FREE GLOVES. 12. BEFORE STORAGE, PROTECT WITH CONTAMINATION BARRIER. USE CLEAN, NEW, TRANSPARENT POLYETHYLENE BAG FOR SMALL ITEMS OR 4 MIL THICK MINIMUM POLYETHYLENE SHEET AND SEAL. IDENTIFY PART BY TAGGING WITH PART NUMBER AND SERIAL/LOT NUMBER AS SPECIFIED IN WORK ORDER PER SAE AS478, METHOD 35.",,True
