PSI-101,Engineering Documents,PSI-101_Engineering Documents_60089MFHA411 Teflon Ignitor Wire Coil Assembly.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_60089MFHA411 Teflon Ignitor Wire Coil Assembly.pdf,p1,1,"### Notes 1. MATERIAL: TYPE 304 STAINLESS STEEL SAFETY LOCKWIRE (.020 DIA), McMASTER-CARR PART No 8860K12 AND AS NOTED IN FIELD OF DRAWING. CERTIFICATE OF CONFORMANCE REQUIRED. 2. CLEAN ALL SURFACES WITH ISOPROPYL OR ETHYL ALCOHOL AND LET AIR DRY. WIPE WITH CLEAN COTTON SWABS AND/OR LINT FREE CLOTH. 3. HANDLE WITH CLEAN LINT FREE GLOVES. 4. BEFORE STORAGE, PROTECT WITH CONTAMINATION BARRIER. USE CLEAN, NEW, TRANSPARENT POLYETHYLENE BAG FOR SMALL ITEMS OR 4 MIL THICK MINIMUM POLYETHYLENE SHEET AND SEAL. IDENTIFY PART BY TAGGING WITH PART NUMBER AND SERIAL/LOT NUMBER AS SPECIFIED IN WORK ORDER PER SAE AS478, METHOD 35. ### Field of drawing (dimensions in inches) - Ø.020 WIRE FORMED AS SHOWN, PITCH 32/INCH, APPROX 15 COILS, Ø.160 ID - Coil length (.453); lead spacing .31; .072 - End view: (Ø.160); 6X R.06; 2X .44; 2X .69 - SIZE 22D CONNECTOR PIN, ALLIED ELECTRONICS PART NO. [phone removed]/204370-2, CRIMPED ON WIRE LEAD, 2 REQD",,True
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,"PSI-101_Engineering Documents_Fluids Control Unit, Schematic, left side-1.pdf","data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_Fluids Control Unit, Schematic, left side-1.pdf",p1,0,"Description: Scanned left half of the SAME fluids system schematic (drawing 60089MFHP000, rev E per the matching right half): the Fluids Control Unit (nitrogen supply and vacuum manifolds), the aging chamber smoke piston, and the piping, actuator, control element and item designation symbol legends along the bottom. No title block on this half. Some lines in the vacuum section are drawn as bold dashed lines (see general note 05 on the right half: components designated with bold dashed lines have a maximum design pressure of 19.7 psia).",,True
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,"PSI-101_Engineering Documents_Fluids Control Unit, Schematic, left side-1.pdf","data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_Fluids Control Unit, Schematic, left side-1.pdf",p1,1,"### Fluids Control Unit (see 60089MFHA220 for detail) Nitrogen supply path (heavy line, 1/8"" OD SS): - MSG GN2 QD -> PN08 / PN01 quick disconnect (PN10, PN09 quick disconnect with shut-off and cap outside the unit) -> PN05 filter (F) -> PN04 ""RELIEF VALVE 1, 220 PSIG WITH MANUAL OVERRIDE"" -> PN02 PT -> PN03 solenoid valve -> PN06 pressure regulator, 15 PSIG - Downstream of PN06 (1/8"" ID TYGON): PN12 PT; PN13, PN14, PN16 relief valves, each 20 PSIG; solenoid valves PN07, PN24 (-> PN25 orifice), PN15, PN27 (-> PN28 orifice) - PN07 feeds solenoid valves BN08 and BN09 (bold dashed line from BN08/BN09 down to the vacuum section) - Mass flow controller BN19 between solenoid valves BN18 and BN17; BN20 solenoid valve to VENT; BN02 PT; BN16 solenoid valve - Lines leave through quick disconnects: BN03 VACUUM/GN2 QD1; BN23 VACUUM/GN2 QD2 Vacuum section (bold dashed lines): - VM06 solenoid valve -> VM05 orifice -> VM07 orifice -> AT16 filter (F) -> quick disconnect - VR12, VR11, VR06 solenoid valves to quick disconnects; VR04 PT - VM09 solenoid valve -> VM10 orifice -> AT20 PT -> AT11 filter (F) -> quick disconnect - VR02 ""RELIEF VALVE 2, 5 PSIG WITH MANUAL OVERRIDE"" - VR20 relief valve, 5 PSIG - 1/8"" ID TYGON -> VR01 ""MSG VACUUM QUICK DISCONNECT""; VR09 check valve; VR19 MSG VACUUM QD; VR13, VR14 quick disconnect; ""MSG SHUT-OFF VALVE"" - VR10 VACUUM QD1",,True
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,"PSI-101_Engineering Documents_Fluids Control Unit, Schematic, left side-1.pdf","data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_Fluids Control Unit, Schematic, left side-1.pdf",p1,2,"### Aging chamber (see 60089MFHA150 for detail) - SMOKE PISTON: SAMPLE SIDE (top) / DRIVE/VAC SIDE (bottom) - Sample side valves with actuators BN21 and BN22; line ""FROM MSG WORK VOLUME"" - BN25 VACUUM/GN2 QD1 on the drive/vac side; BN23 VACUUM/GN2 QD2 ### Symbol legends (bottom edge) Piping component symbols: butterfly valve; check valve; gate valve; relief valve; ball valve; voltage sensitive orifice; directional valves 2-way, 3-way, 4-way; pressure regulator; loader; cylinder regulator; turb flowmeter; orifice; venturi; rupture disk; quick disconnect; press gauge; filter; strainer; heat exch; pump; motor; quick disconnect with shut-off. Actuator symbols: cylinder; hand; pneu control; rotary motor; solenoid. Primary control element symbols: EP elec-pneu xdcr; EH elec-hydr xdcr; LLS liq level switch; LLT liq level xdcr; LS limit switch; PDS press diff switch; PDT press diff xdcr; PS pressure switch; PT pressure xdcr; TC thermocouple; TS temp switch. Item designations: locally mounted; control room mounted; altn control room mounted; ref drawing. Instrument letter designations table cut off at the right edge [illegible].",,True
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,"PSI-101_Engineering Documents_80089MFHP000-E SAME Fluids System Schematic, right side-1.pdf","data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_80089MFHP000-E SAME Fluids System Schematic, right side-1.pdf",p1,0,"Description: Scanned right half of the SAME fluids system schematic, rev E: Dynamic Diagnostics Assembly, Dynamic Diluter, P-Trak enclosure, Commercial Diagnostics Enclosures A and B, Thermal Precipitator Module, balloon legend, general notes, instrument legends, revisions and title block. The drawing number in the title block reads 60089MFHP000 (the file name says 80089MFHP000). ### Title block - Title: SAME FLUIDS SYSTEM SCHEMATIC; next assembly: SMOKE AEROSOL MEASUREMENT EXPERIMENT 60089MFHP000 - Drawing number: 60089MFHP000, size E, rev E - Organisation: National Aeronautics and Space Administration, John H. Glenn Research Center, Lewis Field, Cleveland, Ohio; ZIN Technologies - Project lead, quality, safety, engineer, designer, reviewer: [name omitted], dated 7/21/05 to 7/26/05 - Contract number: NAS3-99154; delivery order: MRDOC-074; file location: M:\MRDOC\60089_074 [?]; CAD package: AutoCAD Mechanical 2004 DX - Scale, official date and sheet fields [illegible]; units: inch; discipline: mechanical; ""AutoCad 2004"" - Tolerances: .X ±; .XX ±; .XXX ±; FRACTION ±; ANGULAR ± NA; BREAK EDGES NA. Dimensioning and tolerancing per ASME Y14.5M-1994. - ""This drawing is under configuration control: do not revise without approved ECO"". Approved: [name omitted], date 10/11/06",,True
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,"PSI-101_Engineering Documents_80089MFHP000-E SAME Fluids System Schematic, right side-1.pdf","data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_80089MFHP000-E SAME Fluids System Schematic, right side-1.pdf",p1,1,"### Revisions | CHG | ZONE | DESCRIPTION | APP/DATE | |---|---|---|---| | A | H7,8 E,D 5&6 B8 | REV. A INCORPORATED ECO No. SAME-ECO-077 | [name omitted] 11/23/05 | | B | F8, D6, G1 | REV. B INCORPORATED ECO No. SAME-ECO-090 | [name omitted] 1/13/06 | | C | B8 | REV. C INCORPORATED ECO No. SAME-ECO-095 | [name omitted] 1/27/06 | | D | | REV. D INCORPORATED ECO No. SAME-ECO-156 | [name omitted] 7/17/06 | | E | A8 B8 | REV. E INCORPORATED ECO No. SAME-ECO-212 | [name omitted] 10/11/06 | ### General notes 01 REFER TO CP SHEETS FOR COMPONENT INFORMATION 02 MAXIMUM DESIGN PRESSURE OF COMPONENTS UPSTREAM OF AND INCLUDING PRESSURE REGULATOR (PN06) IS 200 PSIA 03 MAXIMUM DESIGN PRESSURE OF COMPONENTS DIRECTLY CONNECTED TO THE DYNAMIC DIAGNOSTICS ASSEMBLY INCLUDING THE DYNAMIC DILUTER IS 15.7 PSIA 04 MAXIMUM DESIGN PRESSURE OF COMPONENTS DOWNSTREAM OF AND INCLUDING CHECK VALVE (VR09,) IS 40 PSIA 05 MAXIMUM DESIGN PRESSURE OF COMPONENTS DESIGNATED WITH BOLD DASHED LINES IS 19.7 PSIA 06 ALL COMPONENTS NOT MENTIONED IN ABOVE NOTES HAVE A MAXIMUM DESIGN PRESSURE OF 29.7 PSIA 07 REFERENCES TO ASSEMBLY DRAWINGS ARE NOTED ON THE SCHEMATIC 08 REFER TO SAME-TST-035 FOR PROOF PRESSURE AND LEAK TESTING 09 TUBING AND LINES ARE SHOWN ON COMPONENT ASSEMBLY DRAWINGS OR INTERFACE CONNECTION DRAWING",,True
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,"PSI-101_Engineering Documents_80089MFHP000-E SAME Fluids System Schematic, right side-1.pdf","data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_80089MFHP000-E SAME Fluids System Schematic, right side-1.pdf",p1,2,"### Balloon legend | PRESSURE | GAS | |---|---| | P - POSITIVE | N - NITROGEN | | V - VACUUM | M - MSG ENVELOPE | | B - BOTH (P OR V) | R - N AND/OR MSG ENV | | A - NEAR ATM | T - MSG ENV. AND/OR SMOKE | | | U - ANY | ### Dynamic Diagnostics Assembly (see 60089MDBA165 for detail) - AT05 inlet ""FROM MSG WORK VOLUME"" -> ISS SMOKE DETECTOR -> STS SMOKE DETECTOR -> AT06 filter (F) -> TO MSG RECIRCULATION FANS - AT13 SAMPLE OULET QD (as written) between the detectors ### Dynamic Diluter (see 60089MFHA180 for detail) - AT03 (SAMPLE QD2) -> AT07 -> LFE with AT08 PT across it -> AU01 STATIC MIXER -> AU02 SAMPLE QD1 - PN20 GN2 QD -> PN23 VSO ### P-Trak enclosure (see 60089MFHA500 for detail) - AU03 (SAMPLE QD) -> OPTICS CHAMBER -> AU05 filter -> VR07 orifice -> VR05 VACUUM QD; bypass AU06 filter -> VR08 orifice ### Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure (A) (see 60089MFHA600 for detail) - SAMPLE INLET 1 (AU07) -> AU08 filter -> OPTICS CHAMBER -> AU09 filter -> VR15 orifice -> VR16 VACUUM QD - SAMPLE INLET 2 (AT04) -> AT09 ION -> AT10 filter -> VM04 orifice ### Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure (B) (see 60089MFHA600 for detail) - SAMPLE INLET 1 (AU10) -> AU11 filter -> OPTICS CHAMBER -> AU12 filter -> VR17 orifice -> VR18 VACUUM QD - SAMPLE INLET 2 (AT17) -> AT18 ION -> AT19 filter -> VM13 orifice ### Thermal Precipitator Module (TPM) (see 60089MFHA300 for detail) - SAMPLE INLET 1 (AT14) and SAMPLE INLET 2 (AT15), each to 6 solenoid-valved sample lines (12 total) -> VM08 VACUUM QD",,True
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,"PSI-101_Engineering Documents_80089MFHP000-E SAME Fluids System Schematic, right side-1.pdf","data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_80089MFHP000-E SAME Fluids System Schematic, right side-1.pdf",p1,3,"### Legends (bottom edge) Instrument letter designations (partly cut off at left): first letter B burner, E emergency, F flow, H hand, L level, P pressure, S speed, T temperature, Z position, J power, U surge; succeeding letter A alarm, C control, D differential, H high, I indicate, L low, P permissive, R record, S shutdown, Y datalog. Instrument symbols: general; prgm logic controller; data collector facility; prgm process controller; fluids system labels.",,True
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,PSI-101_Engineering Documents_SAME_Wire_Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_SAME_Wire_Report.pdf,p1,0,"Smoke Experiment aka Characterization of Smoke Particulate for Spacecraft Fire Detection Engineering Report Characterization of Ignitor Wire Candidates Submitted by: ZIN Technologies, Inc Len Miller April 2002",,False
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,PSI-101_Engineering Documents_SAME_Wire_Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_SAME_Wire_Report.pdf,p2,0,"SMOKE Experiment Ignitor WIre Characterization Study April 2002 Purpose: The purpose of this report is to document the results of a study conducted in search of the ideal type of wire to be used for the hot wire ignitors for the Smoke Experiment. Objective: The objective of this study was to find a wire with a consistent, linear resistance versus temperature characteristic and with a “significant” slope. Scope: This study was conducted in the Phase A Laboratory (Bldg 77, Room 154A) on commercially available wire. The ignitor manufacturing technique and the pyrolization of samples is beyond the scope of this study. Procedure: Each type of wire was mounted on a test fixture. The test fixture was then placed into a ThermCraft laboratory oven. The oven was programmed to ramp up to a set point, hold the set point for fifteen minutes, and then ramp to the next set point until the final temperature of approximately 800 oC was attained. (See oven cycle graph in Appendix C.) Resistance readings were taken near the end of each “dwell” period. Several of the wire types were subjected to additional tests to examine the effects of the previous thermal cycle(s). The test fixture held approximately thirty-four inches (0.86 meters) of wire. A stainless steel sheathed type-k thermocouple (Omega Part No. KQSS-116U-24) was placed inside the oven in close proximity to the wire under test.",,False
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,PSI-101_Engineering Documents_SAME_Wire_Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_SAME_Wire_Report.pdf,p2,1,Leads of high temperature furnace wire (#18 AWG) were brought out of the oven to enable the test conductor to measure the resistance of the wire under test. The resistance of the leads and the terminations was characterized by subjection the fixture and leads to a thermal cycle with a copper bus bar shorting the terminals of the fixture. The fixture resistance was subtracted from the total resistance reading for each test point to determine the net resistance of the wire under test. The results were recorded in an Excel Workbook. Several different data plots were prepared and studied.,,False
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,PSI-101_Engineering Documents_SAME_Wire_Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_SAME_Wire_Report.pdf,p3,0,"SMOKE Experiment Ignitor WIre Characterization Study April 2002 Summary and Conclusions: This study examined the characteristics of eight types of wire: anodized mild steel, pure iron, Alomega (similar to Alumel), -Stainless steel, pure platinum, platinum-rhodium, tungster-5%rhenium, and tungsten-26%rhenium. Traditional heating wires, Nichrome and Kanthal were also included for comparison. Pure platinum has close-to-ideal characteristics. However the cost of the material may be prohibitive. Alomega and stainless steel ran a close second. Mild steel, iron and tungsten-composites oxidized in the oven and their characteristics changed significantly from one thermal cycle to the next. Nichrome and Kanthal have too flat a slope to meet the objectives. Further testing will be conducted to determine the optimum wire type and wire diameter. Representative test samples will by pyrolized using various ignitor configurations. The quality and quantity of the smoke will be visually evaluated. Test Results: The tests were conducted in the Phase A Lab from February 6, 2002 through March 20 2002, by Len Miller and Nathan Funk of Zin Technologies, Inc. The individual results for each test were tabulated and plotted. For those wires subjected to more than one thermal cycle, the comparative results were plotted. Finally, an overall plot of the combined results was prepared. The entire Excel Workbook, with tabulated test results and plots thereof, is included in Appendix A of this report.",,False
PSI-101,Engineering Documents,PSI-101_Engineering Documents_SAME_Wire_Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Engineering Documents/PSI-101_Engineering Documents_SAME_Wire_Report.pdf,p3,1,Photographs of the laboratory apparatus are in Appendix B and a graph of the thermal cycle is in Appendix C.,,False
