PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p6,0,SAME-DOC-003 Rev A vi LIST OF TABLES Table 1 – Software Documentation per Level of Effort ........................................................................... 3 Table 2 – Software Records per Level of Effort ....................................................................................... 4 LIST OF FIGURES Figure 1 - Basic Software Lifecycle ........................................................................................................... 4 Figure 2 – Software Development Environment ...................................................................................... 7,,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p7,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 1 1.0 INTRODUCTION The Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment (SAME) instrument is an experiment payload planned for the Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) located on the International Space Station (ISS). Thr oughout this document the term “project” is used to refer to the SAME project in its role as payload developer. 1.1 Purpose The purpose of this Software Description Document is three-fold: (1) to describe the Software Management Plan (SMP) for the project (2) to identify what product specification and procedure documentation will be produced as part of the software effort for the project, and (3) to identify and describe the records that will be produced as part of the software effort. This document is inte nded to be used as a roadmap of the software development effort, describing some aspects of the road within the document itself and, as applicable, pointing to other software documents and records which will be completed as the project progresses. The basis for this document, including structure, are two Glenn Research Center (GRC) Manuals, GRC - P2.6.4 and GRC -M2.6.4. Refer to these documents for more detailed background information and terminology definitions. 1.2 Scope This Software Description Document establishes the project's management processes for the software to be developed by the project to satisfy its requirements.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p7,1,"It also contains requirements to be satisfied by the providers of all software purchased, contractually acquired, developed, or maintained for the support or execution of the project. And e xcept for software and hardware interfaces used to estimate capabilities and schedules for the availability of support resources, it does not apply to software applications that provide company-wide capabilities not specific to this project. The term ""software"" as used in this document includes code, documentation, associated data, and ""firmware"", which is software installed in a medium that cannot be dynamically changed. The project software is comprised of several software “packages”, each executing on a separate platform. Located within the MSG will be a microcontroller -based data acquisition and control unit (DACU) that is responsible for direct control of the experiment. Connected to this controlle r is the MSG Laptop Computer (MLC) which will contain software to provide a crew interface into the DACU. Ground support software will be developed to collect and analyze on -orbit data at the GRC Telescience Support Center (TS C). Test software may be dev eloped to assist in the development and testing of hardware subsystems. This document is applicable to all of these packages. Any s pecific exceptions will be identified in the appropriate section.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p8,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 2 1.3 Applicable Documents [1] GRC-P2.6.4 Implementation – Software Development [2] GRC-M2.6.4 Glenn Research Center Software Development Manual [3] GRC-P2.10.2 Software Product Assurance [4] SAME-PLN-001 Project Plan [5] SAME-PLN-002 Configuration Management Plan [6] SAME-PLN-003 Product Assurance Plan [7] SAME-PLN-006 Software Assurance Plan [8] SAME-PLN-008 Ground Support Equipment Plan [9] SAME-DOC-001 Concept Description Document [10] SAME-DOC-002 Engineering Requirements Document [11] SAME-DOC-004 Software Requirements Document [12] SAME-DOC-005 Software Interface Control Document [13] SAME-DOC-006 Software Design Document [14] SAME-DOC-007 Operations Manual [15] SAME-DOC-010 Ground Software Version Description Document [16] SAME-DOC-011 Flight Software Version Description Document [17] SAME-TST-001 Software Acceptance Test [18] 60081-STD-0066 BXF Software Coding Standard [19] QM001 ZIN Technologies Quality Manual, especially P04003, P13001, and P14001. [20] MSFC-RQMT-2888 Microgravity Science Glovebox Investigation Interface Requirements Document",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p9,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 3 2.0 SOFTWARE MANAGEMENT PLAN 2.1 Management Approach SAME will establish a software development program based on GRC -M2.6.4, Glenn Research Center Software Development Manual . Per the guidelines outlined in that document, SAME software is categorized as a low to medium control level effort. In defining the Management Approach, SAME will follow the guidelines set forth by GRC -M2.6.4 and use a medium control level software effort for software products that will be utilized onboard ISS and a low level software effort for all other SAME software products. Table 1 and Table 2 are reproduc ed from GRC-P2.6.4, Implementation—Software Development , and show the documentation and records suggested for each level of software development effort. Using the medium level effort column, the location of each identified piece of documentation for the project is indicated in the right-most column.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p9,1,Table 1 – Software Documentation per Level of Effort Software Documentation for: Low Medium High Critical Location Management Plan Development Activities Plan ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ & Verification Plan ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ & Validation Plan ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ & Organizational and Technical Interface Descriptions ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ & Acquisition Activities Plan □ □ - Training Development Plan ▲ ▲ - Assurance Plan ▲ ▲ ▲ * Risk Management Plan □ ▲ ▲ & * Configuration Management Plan ▲ ▲ ▲ * Delivery and Operational Transition Plan ▲ ▲ - Product Specification Concept documentation ▲ ▲ - Requirements documentation ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ * Design documentation ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ * Version description □ ▲ ▲ * User’s guide □ □ □ □ - Operational Procedures Manual □ □ □ □ * Procedures Testing Procedures ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ & * Safety Assurance Procedures □ ▲ - Security and Privacy Procedures □ ▲ - Certification Procedures □ □ ▲ - Key: ▲Required □ Optional & Text supplied in this document * Text supplied in a referenced document - Not being used for this project,,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p10,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 4 Table 2 – Software Records per Level of Effort Software Records: Low Medium High Critical Location Project Approval / Work Authorization ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ * Design Review Records ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ & Review or Board Records □ ▲ ▲ ▲ * Problem Reports / Corrective Action Reports □ □ ▲ ▲ & * Verification and Validation Results ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ & * Design Change Documentation ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ & Release Authorization ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ & Key: ▲Required □ Optional & Text supplied in this document * Text supplied in a referenced document - Not being used for this project Additional high-level characterization of the software effort is required by procedure P04003, Design Control Procedure for Software, of QM001, ZIN Technologies Quality Manual. Following the guidelines of P04003 it is determined that the Software Risk is Medium (see Section 2.8 for details). The Software Cost is Medium, meaning that software design and development cost would be equal to or greater than 10 percent of hardware design and development cost, but less than hardware design and development cost. The Software Complexity is also Medium , meaning that the s oftware design and develo pment can be accomplished by the Lead Software Engineer on a full -time support bas is, perhaps with occasional support of another software engineer. 2.2 Development Activities Plan The Development Activities Plan describes the path and standards to follow in getting from concept to finished product.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p10,1,"2.2.1 Lifecycle Approach The project will follow the conventional GRC Basic Software Lifecycle as shown below: Figure 1 - Basic Software Lifecycle The following is a summary of the software tasks, deliverables, and processes performed during each Life Cycle phase. Requirements: Document requirements . Perform any agreed upon i nspections and/or reviews. Acceptance Test Plan is started at this time. Design: Develop the design; document the design. Perform any rapid prototyping, and then capture the results in the design documentation. Write software system -level test plans and procedures. Perform agreed upon inspections and reviews. Requirements User Software Design Development System Testing Release Support Requirements User Software Requirements User Software Design Development System Testing Release Support",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p11,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 5 Development: Implement the design, i.e. , code the software. Devise an d perform unit -level testing. Perform any agreed upon inspections or walk- throughs. Finalize and release i ntegration and s oftware system-level test procedures. Perform informal regression testing as features are added. System Testing : Perform s oftware system-level testing, record errors, and fix problems . Document changes to the design, to the requirements, and to the code. Release: Perform any agreed upon customer demonstrations/tests. Record and store the version of the release. Finalize, review and sign- off on any user documents . Get customer agreement on delivery —a release authorization. For High and Critical Control Level, a version description document is required. It is highly recommended for Medium control software efforts as well. This project w ill supply a version description document. Support: Provide agreed upon level of version support to customers. Record and address any problems. Review requests for added features. Determine the need for updates and follow this procedure for any additional development. (Updates to the flight software are not possible for this project, but changes to ground support software and others are not unlikely). Provide operation assistance to users of the software. 2.2.2 Schedule The software development schedule is closely tied to the project schedule. See SAME-PLN-001, Project Plan.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p11,1,"Following are s oftware development tasks and milestones described in relation to the project milestones and phases. 2.2.2.1 SCR/RDR Phase During this project phase the software requirements document and supporting software interface control document will be drafted. The software management plan and the software assurance plan will be created and approved. 2.2.2.2 PDR Phase During this project phase the software requirements document and software interface control document will be finalized and approved. The software design document will be started with emphasis on the architectural design. Development hardware and software will be acquired and assembled into the development system . The design document wi ll be completed and signed off after a software design review in time for the PDR itself. Preliminary test plans will be written and reviewed. Coding of software will begin with emphasis on supporting individual sub- systems of the functional model which i s to be completed by PDR. Functional model software will be baselined. 2.2.2.3 CDR Phase During this project phase the flight, crew, and ground software will continue to be developed with testing of sub-systems as hardware is available. The software acceptance t est will be created, approved, and run on completed software with a complete system . Flight and ground software is baselined and a Software Readiness Review is conducted .",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p11,2,"Approval of the crew display by NASA’s Payload Display Review Team, including a usability test, will be acquired. Integrated testing with MSG, as required, will be performed. The operations manual will be written. Software will support the mission sequence test and respond to scientist request s for changes as necessary. Final loading of software, release and validation will be performed in time for the Verification Readiness Review (VRR).",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p12,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 6 2.2.2.4 Post Pre-ship Review During this period ground software and tools supporting ground operations and data processing can be created or enhanced. The Operations Manual will also be enhanced. 2.2.2.5 Mission Operations During mission operations the software team will support operations, trouble-shooting, and further create or enhance ground software as desired. Downlinked data will be archived. 2.2.2.6 Post Mission Operations After the mission is over, downlinked data will be safely stored. Data storage media brought back from the ISS, if any, will be backed up and data will be retrieved, compared to downlinked data, and safely stored. 2.2.3 Release Process A project software release is a complete package of every software module created that is necessary to operate the onboard payload system, payload crew display, payload ground control system, or any test support software (see SAME-PLN-008, Ground Support Equipment Plan). A software release is under version control and configuration management in such a way that the project can say for certain that the software that is being used is the software that was tested. If necessary, a past or present configuration can be reproduced using the records generated during a software release and the files stored in the software repository or configuration management system. Refer to SAME-PLN-002, Configuration Management Plan, for software release requirements.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p12,1,"2.2.4 Simulations Where possible and as available, ISS program simulators provided to the project will be utilized. As required for validation and assurance, the project m ay develop its own software and/or hardware simulators. An MSG software simulator has already been developed by ZIN and will be used in place of MSG during development and testing. 2.2.5 Development Environment Figure 2 shows a block diagram of the software development environment. Aspects of this environment are described below.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p13,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 7 Figure 2 – Software Development Environment 2.2.5.1 Hardware Project software will be developed on an x86 computer running Windows XP or better using the tools listed below. Compiled embedded control software and the operating system image for the DACU will be transferred to the DACU CPU ’s data storage over a private ethernet network. The DACU CPU is an x86 single board computer located on a PC/104 electronics board. The DACU can be run by resetting or re-powering or, in debug m ode, by initiating payload software from the development computer. An RS422 interface between the development computer and DACU CPU simulates and tests the communication link between the payload and MSG. 2.2.5.2 Operating Systems Embedded control software for the DACU will be developed to run under the QNX Real -Time Operating System (RTOS) by QNX Software Systems Ltd. (The QNX RTOS is also known as Neutrino.) It is a scalable, POSIX compliant, deterministic, and multi-threaded operating system. Custom project software for the MSG Laptop Computer will be developed to run under Windows NT 4.0, or as directed by MSG (an upgrade is possible in the near future). Custom project software for ground control at the TSC will be developed to run under Windows XP and will use the Telescience Research Kit (TreK) from MSFC to communicate with ISS command and data interfaces.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p13,1,"Development Computer Momentics IDE s/w Dev Pkg RS422 card Ethernet SAME Cmd & Data Application (Gnd s/w) MSG Simulator Version Control Client PC/104 Development Enclosure Power Supply Ethernet MicroDrive (2GB) DACU CPU Board(s) whose s/w is under development RS422 port Support Hardware s/w load, debug Experiment files ftp’d data cmds RS232 port RS232 port Telnet diagnostics DiskOnChip storage (optional) Contains Neutrino OS, payload software, and generated data files. Ethernet To Software Repository",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p14,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 8 2.2.5.3 Languages Wherever possible the project will use C++ as the programming language. 2.2.5.4 Coding Standard The project will use 60081-STD-0066, BXF Software Coding Standard, to provide coding guidelines to promote style consistency, ease of reading, and maintainability by different programmers. 2.2.5.5 Compilers Embedded control software for the DACU will be compiled using QNX Momentics Integrated Development Environment (IDE) version 6.x.x by QNX Software Systems Ltd. Custom project software for the MSG Laptop Computer and custom software for ground control will be developed using Microsoft Visual Studio .NET. 2.2.5.6 Version Control / Software Repository All project software, including low control level software, will be placed under version control. Version control allows the project to maintain a database of the source code, library modules, and COTS packages. This database will provide change tracking information and simultaneous user access. The version control database will be automatically archived using the existing ZIN network backup system. Version control will be achieved using Subversion by tigris.org. 2.3 Verification and Validation Plan Verification addresses whatever steps a project is going to use to assure that the requirements are completely and correctly incorporated in the software product. Validation states how the project intends to get confirmation that what is being designed, built, and delivered meets those requirements.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p14,1,"The project will perform verification by inspection. SAME-DOC-006, Software Design Document , will provide traceability to each specific requirement in SAME-DOC-004, Software Requirements Document. Verification will be completed prio r to sour ce code implementation and will involve a design review as specified in SAME-PLN-006, Software Assurance Plan. The project will perform validation by testing. SAME-TST-001, Software Acceptance Test , will be developed and executed to ensure that the softwa re performs as expected . In addition to nominal operations, this test will validate appropriate reaction, as defined by the software requirements, to off - nominal and boundary conditions. A mission sequence test will be developed and executed at the project level to ensure validation of the entire system , hardware and software . The crew display will be additionally validated by a usability test at Johnson Space Center (JSC). Integrated testing of the experiment with MSG will be performed as well, as requ ired by MSFC-RQMT-2888, MSG Investigation Interface Requirements Document. Prior to, and in-between releases of software, informal module testing will be performed to ensure functionality of each module. 2.4 Organizational and Technical Interface Descriptions This section describes all interfaces between the development organization and the users, customers, product assurance, and suppliers.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p14,2,Interface descriptions define the boundaries of responsibility for each part of the software product and the way that technical information will be transmitted between all parties.,,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p15,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 9 2.4.1 Project Manager (GRC) The GRC Project Manager is responsible for the overall management of the project including milestone reviews, cost review, schedule, technical status, issues, and technical interchange to assure understanding of all requirements 2.4.2 Principal Investigator and Science Team (GRC, NCMR, NIST) The Principal Investigator ’s (PI) team is responsible for specifying the science requirements for the experiment. These requirements will be captured in SAME-DOC-002, Engineering Requirements Document. The science team will review and accept the verification and validation of the SAME software. 2.4.3 Project Lead/Lead Engineer (ZIN) The Project Lead will provide a management function for the moni toring, control, and reporting of the project effort. The Project Lead shall provide to NASA reporting and insight into the project status, and technical performance. Acting also as the Project Lead Engineer, the Project Lead is responsible for coordinating issues between the various project disciplines. 2.4.4 Lead Software Engineer (ZIN) The Lead Software Engineer is responsible for software requirements gathering, overall design, implementation and testing of the various software systems. The Lead Software E ngineer ensures that software is provided for system verifications, such as environmental testing. The Lead Software Engineer coordinates with the Ground Operations Engineers to achieve needed ground software support.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p15,1,"The Software Lead is also responsible for maintaining the software schedules. 2.4.5 Ground Operations Engineers (ZIN and TSC) The Ground Operations Engineers assist the Lead Software Engineer in obtaining the necessary command and data interfaces at the TSC. The Ground Operations Engineers also provide ground software and procedures used for viewing data of the MSG host system onboard ISS. 2.4.6 GRC Safety and Mission Assurance Directorate (SMAD) SMAD is responsible for NASA programmatic oversight related to Safety, Reliability, Quality Assurance and Security. It provides advice and guidance in the planning and implementation of Security and supports flight projects to assure all safety requirements are met and mission success is consistent with the Center's goals. 2.4.7 ZIN Quality Assurance ZIN Quality Assurance is responsible for implementation of the ZIN quality system during the life of the project. It is responsible for ensuring that the quality assurance requirements identified in SAME -PLN- 006, Software Assurance Plan, and in SAME-PLN-003, Product Assurance Plan, are met.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p16,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 10 2.4.8 ZIN Configuration Management Officer The ZIN Configuration Management Officer is responsible for implementation and maintenance of Configuration Management on both project s oftware and s oftware documents. These duties shall be performed in accordance with SAME-PLN-002, Configuration Management Plan. 2.5 Acquisition Activities Plan Not required for medium control level software. 2.6 Training Development Plan Not required for medium control level software. 2.7 Software Assurance Plan In order to ensure conformance of software to established requirements, development methodologies and standards a software assurance plan shall be developed. Refer to SAME -PLN-006, Software Assurance Plan. 2.8 Risk Management Plan Risk management is addressed at the project level through SAME-PLN-001, Project Plan . However, procedure P04003, Design Control Procedure for Software , of QM001, ZIN Technologies Quality Manual requires that the software risk level be assessed, and that will be done here. Following the guidelines o f P04003 it is determined that the software risk for the project is Medium. A medium level of risk is characterized as “In -use software failure risks significant property damage or significant negative impact upon the customer ”. The influencing point in this decision is the fact that samples are destroyed in testing and therefore science data losses during the test cannot be recovered by re-running the sample test.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p16,1,"There is not a risk of significant property damage, only of negative impact upon the customer. 2.9 Configuration Management Plan For released software the configuration management plan is contained within SAME -PLN-002, Configuration Management Plan, which also contains requirements for releasing software. Prior to release, software will be stored and tracked using the version control method specified in Section 2.2.5.6. 2.10 Delivery and Operational Transition Plan Not required for medium control level software.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p17,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 11 3.0 PRODUCT SPECIFICATION There are number of software- related pr oduct specification documents recommended by GRC -P2.6.4. The manner in which these are implemented by the project are listed below. 3.1 Concept Documentation Not required for medium control level software. Refer to SAME-DOC-001, Concept Description Document, for the project-level concept. 3.2 Requirements Documentation The project will develop SAME-DOC-004, Software Requirements Document , which will contain all software requirements pertaining to systems, facility, safety, operations, telemetry, software interfac es, functionality and performance. This document will also contain any limitations or constraints on the system and will specify the required responses to off-nominal and boundary conditions. 3.3 Design Documentation The project will develop SAME -DOC-006, Software Design Document, which will contain both the architectural software design and the detailed software design. It is expected that these sections will be added at separate times. The architectural design will lay out the basic structure for the softwa re defining the high level interfaces between software and hardware and any other systems as well as define the domains, objects, modules, functions and operations needed to meet the requirements. Operational scenarios and use cases will be documented as part of the design to assure the design covers all the aspects of operation.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p17,1,"Timing diagrams and flow charts will be used to convey the architecture and the real -time command and data flow. Network diagrams may be used to describe the physical and data in teractions on the network in the final installation. The detailed software design will specify details that are worked within and between the modules, classes, and/or objects. For Object Oriented Design (OOD), the public versus private sectors will be determined. Parameter and variable types and values will be established. Global versus private flags, variables, and parameters will be determined. Methods, attributes, calculations, and algorithms will be captured. 3.4 Version Description The project will create Version Description Documents (VDD) to capture the as- built state of released software. A VDD will be necessary for each ground support software and flight software release and will be captured in SAME -DOC-010, Ground Software Version Description Document , and SAME -DOC- 011, Flight Software Version Description Document, respectively. VDDs are not required for test support software. The VDD will indicate compatability with other package versions. For example, “This version of ground software will work with versions 1.0–2.5 of flight software.” Additional requirements for the VDD are listed in SAME -PLN-002, Configuration Management Plan. It is suggested that the VDD contain the sections shown below.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p18,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 12 • Section 1. Scope A heading. • Section 1.1 Identification Identifies the Title, Version, and software number (assigned per the Configuration Management Plan) of the software. This section also includes a brief description of the software and software author contact information. • Section 1.2 Purpose E.g., “The purpose of this VDD is to provide unique information that describes and specifies the version of software identified in section 1.1.”. • Section 1.3 Product Information A heading. • Section 1.3.1 Verification Indentifies verification date, location, test procedure, and any other pertinent details. • Section 1.3.2 Virus Scan Optional. Identifies the virus scan program name and version, virus definition version and date, date of scan, and results of the virus scan. • Section 2 Reference Documents Identifies documents that may be useful to the reader. • Section 3 Version Description A heading. • Section 3.1 Inventory of Materials Released This section identifies any physical media that contains the released software. For a compact disc it shows what text is printed on the label. • Section 3.2 Inventory of Software Released A heading. • Section 3.2.1 Compact Disc Inventory A list of all files that are part of the released software. Includes file name, file date and time, size in bytes, and checksum. • Section 3.2.2 Install Directory Inventory A list of all files that are present in the installation directory after the software is installed.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p18,1,"Includes file name, file date and time, size in bytes, and checksum. Also describes any changes to the system registry, as applicable. • Section 3.2.3 Source Code Inventory List of source code for the released software or instructions for locating the source code on company computers. E.g., “list of files available by request.” For each file include at least the last modification date.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p19,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 13 • Section 3.3 Changes Implemented A list of changes made since the last version of released software. Identification of old and new version numbers. • Section 3.4 Installation Instructions Describes how to install the software on a host computer. • Section 3.5 Deinstallation Instructions Describes how to remove the software from a host computer. • Section 3.6 Known Issues Identifies any unresolved bugs or necessary work-arounds. 3.5 User’s Guide Optional for medium control level software. Any user guide type instructions will be included in the Operational Procedures Manual. 3.6 Operational Procedures Manual The project will develop SAME -DOC-007, Operations Manual, to describe how to operate the SAME instrument and to document how the hardware responds to the software commands. Any ground control- related procedures that are not included in other ZIN or TSC documents may be included in the Operations Manual as well.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p20,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 14 4.0 PROCEDURES See the Validation and Software Assurance Plan in previous sections for the use of proc edures by the project. This section describes the content of the procedures. 4.1 Testing Procedures The project will develop SAME-TST-001, Software Acceptance Test , which will perform all internal validation tasks on released software. This procedure will c ontain a requirements matrix which will indicate the source of each requirement and the step (s) in the procedure in which it is tested. The procedure will clearly delineate pass/fail criteria for each test within the procedure. The results of the procedure will be documented in a report numbered according to SAME -PLN-002, Configuration Management Plan. The mission sequence test procedure for full instrument validation for the PI is specified at the project level. Any integrated testing with MSG will follow procedures developed by MSG with input from the project. Procedures for u sability testing of the crew display software will be developed by JSC personnel with input from the project. 4.2 Safety Assurance Procedures Not required for medium control level software. 4.3 Security and Privacy Procedures Not required for medium control level software. 4.4 Certification Procedures Not required for medium control level software.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p21,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 15 5.0 RECORDS This section describes how the recommended software records listed in Table 2 are implemented and handled in this project. Most of these records are a result of following subsections of the software management plan, Section 2.0. 5.1 Project Approval/Work Authorization A record of approval/work authorization for the software effort of the project is part of the record for the entire project. Refer to the project level document, SAME-PLN-001, Project Plan. 5.2 Design Review Records During software design reviews, with occurrence as dictated by SAME -PLN-006, Software Assurance Plan, minutes will be taken and a summary report will be generated that is signed off by all design review members. At this point SAME -DOC-006, Software Design Document , will be modified to either reference a document containi ng the summary report or the report content itself will be appended to SAME-DOC-006. In addition, for each review a memo describing the results of the review will be sent to the GRC Project Manager. Minutes from other software meetings will be stored and backed-up electronically. 5.3 Review or Board Records Record of project level reviews and board response, for example the Verification Readiness Review and the Pre-Ship Review, are kept at the project level according to SAME-PLN-001, Project Plan.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p21,1,"5.4 Problem Reports / Corrective Action Reports As specified in SAME-PLN-006, Software Assurance Plan, the project will record and track to closure any problems found in software using procedures P13001, Procedure for The Control of Nonconforming Product, and P14001, Corrective and Preventive Action Procedure, of QM001, ZIN Technologies Quality Manual. The project shall use the non-conformance tracking and corrective action reporting as described in the Quality Manual once the software has passed software acceptance testing. 5.5 Verification and Validation Results Verification results consist of the record from the software design review. See Section 5.2 for more information. Internal validation results for the project software releases will be in the form of a signed Software Acceptance Test Report . Results of the validation accomplished by the mission sequence test will be generated at the project level according to SAME-PLN-003, Product Assurance Plan. External validation results, such as from the crew display usability test and any integrated MSG testing may exist as a memo or e -mail, in which case the text of the results will be stored electronically in a backed-up folder.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p22,0,"SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 16 5.6 Design Change Documentation Requests for changes to the software design and the software itself will be maintained in an electronic log by the lead software engineer. This log will include the date and origin of the request, description of the request, and the disposition: incomplete, in process, completed with date, or cance lled with date. The log will be stored in a backed-up folder. A r ecord of design changes will also be kept in the version control database in the form of comments entered at the time that source code changes are checked in or when COTS packages are updated or replaced. 5.7 Release Authorization A memo or e -mail from the Project L ead/Lead Engineer or Project Manager is required to authorize the release of a new software version. This may be in the context of the Software Lead announcing the upcoming release by e-mail and the Project Lead /Lead Engineer or Project Manager responding in approval.",,False
PSI-101,Reference Data,PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-101/Reference Data/PSI-101_Reference Data_SAME_Software Description Document_003 Rev A.pdf,p23,0,SAME-DOC-003 Rev A 17 6.0 ABBREVIATIONS AND ACRONYMNS CDR Critical Design Review COTS Commercial Off-The-Shelf CPU Central Processing Unit DACU Data Acquisition Control Unit GB Gigabyte GRC Glenn Research Center IDE Integrated Development Environment ISS International Space Station JSC Johnson Space Center MLC Microgravity Science Glovebox Laptop Computer MSFC Marshall Space Flight Center MSG Microgravity Science Glovebox OOD Object Oriented Design OS Operating System PDR Preliminary Design Review POSIX Portable Operating System Interface PSRP Payload Safety Review Panel RDR Requirements Definition Review RTOS Real-Time Operating System SAAD Safety and Assurance Directorate SAME Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment SCR Science Concept Review SMP Software Management Plan TReK Telescience Resource Kit TSC Telescience Support Center VDD Version Description Document,,False
