PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p7,0,vii Table of Contents Abstract……………………………………………………………………………...iii Acknowledgments……………………………………….…………………………...v Table of Contents…………………………………………………………………...vii List of Tables…………………………………………………………………...…..viii List of Figures……………………………………..………………………………..viii Nomenclature………………………………………………………………………...ix Acronyms…………………………….……………………………………………....ix 1. Introduction……………………………………………………………………...1 2. Smoke Background………………………………………………………………1 3. SAME Experiment……………………………………………………………….4 A. The Moment Method…………………………………………………………..4 A.1. Principle of the Moment Method………………………………………....4 A.2. Implementation of the Moment Method in SAME Experiment………….8 A.3. Uncertainty Analysis……………………………………..……………….8 B. Instrumentation………………………….……………….…………………….9 C. Instrument Calibration……………………………………………………..…12 4. Flight Tests……………………………………………………………………..14 5. Test Results and Discussion…………………………………………………….15 6. Closing Remarks………………………………………………………………..19 References…………………………………………………………………………….20 Publication List……………………………………………………………………….21 Appendix A. Log of SAME Flight Tests…………………………………………..A-1 Appendix B. (2009 ICES Paper)…………………………………………………...B-1 Appendix C. (2009 AIAA Paper)………………………………………………….C-1 Appendix D. (2008 ICES Paper)…………………………………………………..D-1 Appendix E. (2008 AIAA Paper)………………………………………………….E-1,,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p8,0,viii List of Tables Table 1. Results of SAME flight tests List of figures Figure 1. Brunswick Defense smoke detector used in the NASA orbiter fleet. Figure 2. Allied Signal/Honeywell light-scattering smoke detector used in the ISS. Figure 3. Diameters for a log-normal distribution with Dg = 1.0 m and g = 1.6. Figure 4. Schematic of the SAME hardware. Figure 5. Photograph of a sample carousel. Figure 6. Thermal Precipitator Module. Figure 7. P-trak Calibration results with Mono disperse DOP droplets. Figure 8. Ion Chamber Calibration results with Mono disperse DOP droplets. Figure 9. DustTrak results with Mono disperse DOP droplets. Figure 10. SAME Experiment installed in the ISS MSG Facility. Figure 11. Instrument traces for a typical silicone rubber run. Figure 12. Instrument traces for a typical Teflon run. Figure 13. Instrument traces for a typical smoke aging run for silicone rubber. Figure 14. TEM images for Lamp wick (test point 32) and Teflon (test point 26).,,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p9,0,"ix Nomenclature d -- Count mean diameter or arithmetic mean diameter md -- Diameter of average mass D -- Diameter of particles Dg -- Geometry mean diameter Dp -- A type of characteristic diameter depending on the value of p fN(D) -- Frequency of particle count distribution function (zeroth moment distribution function) fi(D) -- Frequency of the i-th moment distribution function Fi(D) -- Cumulative i-th moment distribution function L -- Letter for length dimension Mi -- The i-th moment N(D) -- Number of particles per unit volume with diameters equal or less than D Nt -- Total number of particles per unit volume u(x) -- Absolute uncertainty of variable x, with the same unit as variable x ur(x) -- Relative uncertainty of variable x, dimensionless. ur(x) = u(x)/x g -- Geometric standard deviation of a lognormal distribution Acronyms CMD: Count Mean Diameter CSD: Comparative Soot Diagnostics Experiment DACU: Data Acquisition and Control Unit DAFT: Dust Aerosol measurement Feasibility Test, a precursor project of SAME GMD: Geometric Mean Diameter GSD: Geometric Standard Deviation ISS: International Space Station MSG: Microgravity Science Glovebox SAME: Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment STS: Space Transportation System TEM: Transmission Electron Microscope",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p10,0,x,,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p11,0,"1 1. Introduction Appropriate design of fire detection systems require s knowledge of both the expected signature of the events to be detected and the bac kground levels of the measured parame ters. Terrestrial fire detection systems have been developed based on extensive study of terrestrial fires. 1, 2 Unfortunately, there is no corresponding data set for spacecraft fires and cons equently current spacecraft fire detectors were developed based upon experience on normal gravity fi re behavior and propertie s. This approach was necessary given the absence of data concerning microgravity smoke particulate. However planned spacecraft and their planned missions outside of low earth-orbit will requi re increased reliability of fire detection systems in addition to robust false alar m resistance. Extended lunar-surface missions will require systems that are not influenced by lunar dust and other nuisance dust sources. The absence of buoyant flow increas es the residence time in microgra vity fires causing substantial changes in flame structure. Given the large changes in fire beha vior seen in reduced gravity, it is reasonable to expect that the signatures and detection requirements of these fires will change. There are a number of factors that can be expected to aff ect the particle size distri bution of the smoke from spacecraft fires.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p11,1,"The absence of buoyant flow in low-gravity increases the residence time in microgravity fires and increases the transit ti me from the reaction zone to the detector. 3 The materials used in spacecraft are different from those typically used in terrestrial applications where smoke properties were previously evaluate d. More importantly, the limited options available to respond to a spacecraft fire increase the importance of detecting the fire in an earlier stage of its development than is typical for terrestrial applications. All of these effects can be expected to change the smoke particle size distribution. The objective of this work was to ma ke sufficient measurements of smoke from spacecraft fires to enable improved design of future detectors. 2. Smoke Background",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p11,2,"Smoke is a general term that encompasses aerosol materials produced by a number of processes. In particular for non-flaming combustion, the aerosol incl udes recondensed original material or pyrolysis products that can be either liquid or solid. For flaming combustion, the aerosol includes soot particulate, condensed water vapor, and ash particles. Soot part icles dominate the smoke pa rticulate in established flaming fires while unburned pyrolysis products and recondensed polymer frag ments are produced by smoldering and pyrolysis in the early stage of fi re growth. Given the constrained space on any spacecraft, the target for the fire detection system is necessarily the early phase and not established flaming fires; consequently, the primary target for detection is the pyrolysis products and not the soot. The background regarding fire detection in US spacecraft is summarized in this paper but is discussed in more detail in papers by Friedman and Urban 4, 5 In the Mercury, Gemini a nd Apollo missions, the crew quarters were limited and mission durat ions were short, consequently it was considered reasonable that the astronauts would rapidly detect any fire. The S kylab module, however, included approximately 30 UV-sensing fire detectors. 4 These devices were limited to line-of -sight and were reported to have difficulties with false alarms.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p11,3,Details of the ISS and STS detector require ments are described by Steisslinger et al. 6 A brief overview of the requirements is given below.,,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p12,0,"2 Space Shuttle Detectors: At the time the Space Shuttle Orbiter (Space Tran sportation System (STS)) was developed, smoke detection for terrestrial applic ations was becoming commonplace. 1 In particular, ionization detectors were readily available but photoelectric (scattering or obscuration detectors) were generally unavailable due to the difficulty of producing stable light sources. There were no data available concerning the smoke or dust particle size dist ribution in low-gravity and the database for normal-gravity smoke characteristics was a fraction of what is available today. Consequently, the STS smoke detector (Fig. 1) employed a variant of the accepted ground-based approach (ionization detectors). The Orbiter has nine particle-ionization smoke detectors in avionics cooling air return lines in the mid- deck and flight deck and the SpaceLab had six additional partic le-ionization smoke detectors in the avionics lines. 7 While the design rationale is not completely known, Celesco™ (later Brunswick Defense™) based their design on data that suggested incipient fires could be discriminated by looking for particulate in the 0.4 μm to 0.7 μm range.8 The use of a pump provided the opportunity to use a particle separator that re jected particulate larger than 1 μm. This air pump increased the power requirements a nd reduced the operational lif e of the detector.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p12,1,"The STS detector as built was designed to alarm at a particle loading of 2 mg/m 3 for 5 seconds (based on 1 micron particles) or a 0.022 mg/m3/s rise in concentration for 20 seconds9. ISS Detectors: The International Space Station (ISS) smoke detector de sign took advantage of the availability of stable laser-diode light sources, mirroring a tr end in terrestrial detectors toward light scattering devices. This provided the opportunity to produce a detector that required substantially less power, (1.5 Watts versus 9 Watts for the orbiter detector) 6 and, with no moving parts, was quiet er with a much longer operational life. The choice was supported by data indicating that early smoldering fires produce larger particulate than established flaming fires. 1 The ISS detectors (Fig. 2) are lase r-diode, forward-scattering, smoke or particulate detectors. The sensitivity extends down to 0.3 μm but is greatest for particles larger than 1 μm. The current requirements for the ISS call for tw o detectors in the open ar ea of the module, and detectors in racks that have cooling air-flow.10",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p12,2,The design developed for the ISS by Allied Signal™ (Fi g. 2) consists of a 2-pass laser diode to sense forward scattered light ( 30 degrees). There is also a light tran smission sensor to monitor the beam intensity. The system is designed to alarm base d on the magnitude of th e scattered light signal referenced to the beam intensity. The system is less se nsitive to particles smaller than the wavelength of the laser (near IR) than it is to larger particles. The mini mum reported sensitivity is 0.3 μm.6 The ISS detector was designed to alarm at 1% obscuration per foot using an Underwriters Laboratory (UL) Figure 1 . Brunswick Defense™ smoke detector used in the NASA orbiter fleet. The inlet is on the right and the gas is expelled out the small plate on the top.,,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p13,0,"3 smoke box and white light extinction. This was implemented using a transfer standard detector and a 0.5 m polystyrene-bead aerosol system that was used to set the amplifiers on each unit. The transfer standard was calibrated in the smoke box and then used to set the levels with the aerosol system. As a result of these de sign decisions, the ISS and the Orbiter detectors have particle sensitivities that do not overlap. In both cases the designs were based upon the best available data at the time. However, due to the complete absence of low-g data concerning the nature of particulate and radiant emission from incipient and fully developed low-g fires, different conclusions were drawn concerning the optimal design for spacecraft fire detection. As described by Friedman 4 there have been six overheat and failed component failures in the NASA Orbiter fleet in addition to several similar incidents that have occurred on the ISS. None of these events spread into a real fire but as mission durations in crease, the likelihood of failures increases. The experience on Mir in 1997 has shown that fa ilure of oxygen generation systems can have significant consequences. As a result, impr oved understanding of spac ecraft fire detection is critically needed.11",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p13,1,"Previous work on smoke particles from low-gravity sources by Urban et al. 5 indicated that the particulate produced by low-gravity fl ames (soot or unburned fuel partic les) tends to have larger size particles than in normal gravity. Results from the CSD (Comparative Soot Diagnostics) Experiment 5 which studied smoke properties in low-gravity from several spacecraft materials suggested that liquid smoke particles could achieve sizes larger than 1 µm while solid particulate remained in the sub- micrometer range. However, the CSD experiment di d not produce sufficient data concerning the size of the liquid smoke particles to guide detector design. The combined im pact of these lim ited results and physical expectations is that, ba sed on the increased sm oke residence time in low gravity, direct knowledge of low-g combustion partic ulate is needed for the confident design of smoke detectors for spacecraft rather than extrapolation from 1-g data Any effort to detect fires must be able to discriminate against the ambient background and nuisance signals. In the case of smoke detection, the background dust aerosol conditions must be considered. The only spacecraft background aerosol particulate measurem ents to date were made on the Orbiter on STS- 32 in 199012 and on the ISS.5",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p13,2,Figure 2. Allied Signal/Honeywell light-scattering smoke detector used in the ISS. The near IR laser beam emerges from the enclosure into the top assemb ly (A) and is reflected by two mirrors (one visible at top right (B)) and is then reflected back to the sensors in the enclosure (C). One sensor detects the forward scattered light and is referenced by another sensor that looks directly at the incident beam.,,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p14,0,"4 The STS measurements included the results from two cascade impactors and a light scattering device. The two impactors reported a bimodal particle size distribution with ~ 40% of the particles in each of the 2.5 μm to 10 μm and >100 μm ranges. The other two ranges <2.5 μm and 10 μm to 100 μm were very lightly populated. Each impact or sampled approximately 15 m 3 of air over approximately 30 hours. These results showed substantially higher concentr ations than typical indoor measurements (over a factor of 5 for the two most populat ed size ranges). These data were supported by the li ght scattering instrument which made 17 measurements in 12 loca tions, all of these reported mass concentrations ranging from 50 μg/m3 to 70 μg/m3. The ISS results of Urban et al. 5 were obtained from a feasibility experiment to validate the operation of an aerosol particle counter in low-gravity (DAFT). The measurements were made in the US Lab on Increment 10 and the particle count levels were at the noise level for the instrument (less than 5 particles/cm 3). The low particle levels in the < 2.5 μm bin for the STS and the low overall numbers for the ISS suggest a zone of opportunity for spacecraft fire detection since typical normal gravity fires produce su bstantial particulate in this size range 5,2 However more complete particle size statistics are needed. 3. SAME Experiment",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p14,1,"To address the limited data from the CSD experime nt concerning the likely size of spacecraft smoke particulate, the Smoke Aerosol Measurement E xperiment (SAME) was developed. The SAME experiment sought to avoid the problems experi enced by the CSD experiment by obtaining the particulate size statistics on-orbit with a reduced dependence upon sample return to Earth. This is a challenging endeavor because existing aerosol instrumentation is typica lly too large to be compatible with spacecraft experiment constraints. Thus, to obtai n particulate size statistics with existing aerosol instrumentation would require the return of numerous samples to Earth. As will be described below, an alternative approach was employed that used three discrete instruments to measure separate moments of the size distribution. When combine d, these moments provide useful a ggregate statistics of the size distribution if a lognormal distri bution is assumed. The measurem ents were made using smoke generated by overheated spacecraft materials in much the same manner as the CSD experiment however the sample temperature, flow field, and particle aging time were more rigorously controlled. A. The Moment Method",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p14,2,"The approach used by the SAME experiment is termed the ‘moment method’ for convenience. 13 This approach consists of measuring three moments of the size distribution (the zeroth, first and third moment) on orbit, using several small, light -weight instruments. By applyi ng the properties of the lognormal distribution to the measured moments, the total number concentration, Nt, the geometric mean diameter, Dg, and the geometric standard deviation, g, of the aerosol can be estimated. Once these three parameters are known, the size distribution of the m easured aerosol is uniquely defined, assuming that the distribution is lognormal. In what follows, we will explain how the moment method works. A.1. Principle of the moment method The number (or count) distribution of an aerosol descri bes the number of particles that fall into a certain size range in a unit volume of aerosol. The number distribution is sometimes also called size distribution. Mathematically, a distribution function can be expresse d in four different, but related, forms. First, a distribution function can be expre ssed as a frequency (or density) di stribution function or a cumulative distribution function. Then, each of the two can be expressed in terms of its normalized form or non-",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p15,0,"5 normalized form. The normalized forms of any dist ribution function are much like the probability functions in statistics. To avoid future confusion, we will define these distribution functions first. The particle number distribution function of an aerosol, fN(D), is defined as follows: (1) dD dNDf N )( , where dN is the number of particles per unit volume having diameters between D and D + dD. Here, the subscript N in fN(D) denotes particle numbers. The function fN(D) is a non-normalized frequency distribution function wi th a dimension of [ L-4]. The corresponding cumulativ e distribution function, F(D), is defined as a definite integral of fN(D) as follows: (1-a)  D N dfDF 0 )()(  As the upper limit D of the integral in Eq. (1-a) approaches infinity, F(D) approaches the total number of particles in a unit volume, denoted by Nt. Thus, F(D) is a non-normalized cumulative distribution function, with a dimension of [ L-3]. When fN(D) or F(D) is normalized by Nt, one gets its normalized counterpart. Note that Nt has a dimension of [ L-3], thus the normalized cumulative distribution function is always dimensionless whereas the normalized frequency distribution function has a dimension of [L-1]. In this report, unless otherwise stated, we will use non-normalized distribution functions, either in their frequency forms or in their cumulative forms.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p15,1,"Based on fN(D), distribution functions of va rious properties of a given aerosol can be defined. For instance, the distribution func tions of particle diameter, fD(D), surface area, fA(D), and particle volume, fV(D) (or mass distribution function, fm(D) if the particle material density is constant) are defined in the following manner. (2) )()()( 1 DDfdD dNDDfDf ND  (2a) )()()( 22 2 DfDdD dNDDfDf NA   (2b) )(66)(6)( 33 3 DfDdD dNDDfDf NV   (2c) )(66)(6)( 33 3 DfDdD dNDDfDf Nm   It can be seen that to address va rious aerosol property distributions, it is convenient to define a moment distribution function fi(D) as follows. (2d) ... 2, 1, ,0 )()(  iDfDDf N i i When i = 0, f0(D) and the number (or count) distribution function, fN(D) are identical. Because equation (2d) is similar to the calculation of moment in mechanics, fi(D) is conventionally called the i-th moment",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p16,0,"6 distribution function of the aerosol. Note that each fi(D) is a frequency distribution function. The corresponding cumulative distribution function, Fi(D), is as follows. (2e) .... 2, 1, 0, )()( 0  D N i i idfDF  When the upper limit of the above integral approaches infinity, the integral of (2e) returns a single value, which is called the i-th moment of the distribution function fN(D), denoted by Mi. That is (2f) ... 2, 1, 0, ,)()( 0    idfFM N i ii  The dimensions of fi(D) are [ Li-4] and those of Fi(D) and Mi are [ Li-3]. Note that for the sake of generalization, we do not set a limit on the number of moments now, although the highest moment used in SAME is the third moment. If fN(D) is an arbitrary function, all its moments are in dependent of each other making it impossible to reconstruct fN(D) from a finite number of its moments. However, if fN(D) is a function that can be uniquely defined by a finite numbe r of parameters, only that same number of its moments are independent and they can be arbitr arily selected. From any set of independent moments, one can solve for the parameters that defi ne the distribution function, fN(D). Thus, this type of distribution function, fN(D), can always be constructed if the independent moments are known. This is the underlying idea of the moment method. The lognormal distribution function below is widely accepted for de scribing the size distribution of aerosols14, 15.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p16,1,"(3)           g g g t N DD D NDf  2 2 ln2 lnlnexp ln2 )( , where Nt is the total number of particles in a unit volume, or total number concentration of the aerosol, and parameters Dg and g are the geometric mean diameter and geometric standard deviation, respectively, defined by (4) dDDfDND n t g    0 ln1ln and (5)  5.0 0 2 lnln1 1ln          dDDfDDN Ng t g The lognormal distribution is related to the well know n normal (or Gaussian) distribution function in that lnD is used as the independe nt variable in the Gaus sian distribution, including dN/d(lnD) that defines fN(D), because the particle diameter D can never take negative values.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p17,0,"7 The SAME project assumes a lognorm al distribution function for the aer osol size distri bution. Since a lognormal distribution function is uni quely defined by three parameters, Nt, Dg and g, only three moments are needed to reconstruc t the distribution function. In SAME , these three moments are chosen to be the zeroth moment, the first moment and the third moment. Based on Eq. (2f), the zeroth moment, M0 is simply equal to Nt, whereas the first moment, M1, can be thought of as the “diameter concentration” or integrated diameter per unit volume. The third moment, M3, is proportional, but not equal, to the total volume or mass of the particles per unit volume of the aerosol. From these three moments, two characteristic diameters are defined. They are a) the count (arithmetic) mean diameter, which is equal to M1/M0 and denoted by D0.5 or d and b) the diameter of average mass 16, which is equal to (M3/M0)1/3 and denoted by D1.5 or md . The subscripts of 0.5 and 1.5 used for these two diameters follow an existing naming convention. There is a unique feature of the lognormal distribut ion, which is describe d by the Hatch-Choate conversion equations 17. The diameters for various moment dist ributions are related to the geometric mean diameter, Dg, and the geometric standard deviation, g, via the following equation.14, 15",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p17,1,"(6)  ggp pDD 2lnexp In equation (6), p is a parameter defining the meaning of diameter Dp. For instance, when p = 0.5, Dp is the count mean diameter, whereas when p = 1.5, Dp is the diameter of average mass. This feature of the lognormal distribution greatly simplif ies the application of the mome nt method in finding the aerosol size distribution. Figure 3 shows a typical lognormal distribution alon g with various characteristic diameters, where Dg=1.0m and g= 1.6. Differing from Eq. (3), the y- axis in Fig. 3 is normalized by Nt, i.e., fN(D)/Nt. In this distribution, the correspondi ng values of the count mean diameter, D0.5, and the diameter of average mass, D1.5, can be computed from equation (6). They are 1.117 m and 1.393 m, respectively. Figure 3: Diameters for a log-normal distribution with Dg=1.0 m and g= 1.6.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p18,0,"8 In the case of the SAME experime nt, our objective is to obtain Dg, g, and Nt based on measured moments, M0, M1 and M3 with D0.5 and D1.5 being intermediate parameters. For this purpose, equation (6) is rearranged to provide the following explicit expressions for Dg and g. (7) 5.1 3 5.0 D DDg  and (8)     5.05.15.05.1 /lnexplnlnexp DDDDg  Thus, once the three moments, M0, M1 and M3, are obtained, we can compute D0.5 and D1.5. Using equations (7) and (8), we can get the corresponding values for Dg and g. When Dg, g and Nt (i.e., M0) are known, a lognormal distribution function, fN(D) can be uniquely defined for the measured aerosol. Validation of this approach is disc ussed in Cleary, Weinert and Mulholland. 13 This moment method provides a strong basis for the design of the SAME experiment. A.2. Implementation of the moment method in SAME experiment",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p18,1,"In the SAME experiment, the zeroth moment, M0, was measured using a c ondensation nuclei counter called the P-Trak TM (TSI Inc.). The first moment, M1, was approximately pr oportional to the output signal of an ionization detector, which was a modified ionization chamber taken from a commercially- available smoke detector. The third moment, M3, was inferred from the li ght scattered by the smoke aerosol when illuminated by a red laser. For particles in th e size range of 0.2 μm to 2.0 μm, , the light scattering signal at 90 degrees from a red laser is approximately proportional to the third moment. The instrument used to obtain the third moment is called a DustTrakTM (TSI Inc.). Calibration measurements were made for the DustTrak to account for th e departure of the scattering signal from the 3 rd moment. More detailed information of instruments and thei r operations can be found in the Instruments section below. The instruments used in SAME were compact enough to be installed in the ISS MSG. Extensive calibrations of these instruments were performed at NIST to ensure that correct values of each moment were obtained from the instrument output sign als. The results of these calibrations showed that the instrument signals correlate well with corresponding moments ex cept for the third moment. The third moment was found to be a function of the DustTr ak output and the particle diameters. This led to an iterative procedure to find the best-fit results of Dg and g.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p18,2,"A.3. Uncertainty Analysis Inevitably there are uncertainties in the measured moments. It is inte resting to know the contribution of the uncertainty of each moment to the uncertainties of Dg and g. To do so, we need to express Dg and g in terms of the three moments. By substituting D0.5 and D1.5 by M0, M1 and M3 in equations (7) and (8), we have (9) 6/1 3 5.1 1 3/4 0  MMMDg and",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p19,0,"9 (10)               1 3/1 3 3/2 0lnexp M MM g The law of propagation of uncertainty 18 for the case of independent variables (i.e., there is no covariance) states (11) i n i i c xux fyu 2 2 1 2 )(         , where u(xi) is the uncertainty of independent variable xi, n is the number of independent variables, and uc(y) is the combined uncertainty of dependent variable y = f(xi). Uncertainties can be expressed in terms of an absolute value of the variable or, in a relative sense, the ratio of the uncertainty to the value of the variable. Since relative uncertainties are easier to use, we will discuss relative uncertainties in the following text. A subscript r will be added to the letter u to indicate relative uncertainties. Then Eq.(11) can be rewritten as (11a) ir n i i i rc xux f y xyu 2 2 1 2 )(         In our case, y is either Dg or g and xi (i = 1, 2, 3) are M0, M1 and M3, respectively. Substituting Eqs. (9) and (10) into Eq. (11a), we obtain the relative combined uncertainties of Dg and g as follows.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p19,1,"(12)    3 2 1 2 0 22 36 125.29 16 MuMuMuDu rrrgrc  and (13)    3 2 1 2 0 2 2 2 25.025.2 ln9 1 MuMuMuu rrr g grc   Equations (12) and (13) show the relative uncertainties of Dg and g as functions relative uncertainties of M0, M1 and M3. It is interesting that both equations (12) and (13) show the uncertainty of M3 contributes the least to the combined uncertainties of Dg and g, whereas that of M1 contributes the most. B. Instrumentation The moment measurements were made using an a ssembly of three separate instruments. Two are industrial hygiene instruments manufactured by TSI and one is a modified residential smoke detector. The zeroth-moment instrument is a condensation nuclei counter, the P- Trak™ (TSI Inc.). This device operates by passing the aerosol-laden air stream through a region satura ted with isopropanol vapor and then into a cooler region where the vapor condenses onto the particles increasing their diameter such that they can be readily counted by a light scattering devi ce. This instrument is very robust and operates over a range of 0 to 10 5 particles/cm3 and 20 nm to 1 μm diameter. Some diluti on is required since the smoke concentration in SAME ranges from about 0.5 × 10 6 to 5 × 10 6 particles/cm3. There was also a concern that the isopropanol condensate woul d not return to the wick in low-gravity. 5 To mitigate this",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p20,0,"10 issue, the condensing section of the device was modified with very small grooves to improve conductance of the condensate back to the wick. These changes were tested in a separate space experiment with good results indicating the modified device could be used successfully in low gravity.5 The first-moment instrument is the ionization cham ber taken from a resident ial smoke detector. This device uses an alpha-particle emitter to generate ions in a region within a DC electric field. Normally, the ions drift toward an electrode inside the ioni zation chamber producing a current. When particulate are present, the ions attach themselves to the pa rticles thereby charging the particles. Because the charged particles have a much smaller mobility than ions alone, the current is reduced. The required particle concentrations are on the order of 105 particles/cm3, thus no sample dilution was required.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p20,1,"The third-moment instrument is a light scattering de vice, the DustTrak™ (TSI In c.). The device uses a 90 degree light scattering signal to quantify the aerosol mass density. For terrestrial dust particulate, this signal correlates well with the mass concentration, however additional comp ensation was needed to account for the range of particle sizes observed in the SAME experiment. The device operating range is from 0.001 mg/m3 to 100 mg /m3. These devices are equipped with an aerodynamic impactor at the inlet which captures particles larger than the selected size. The SAME experiment included 2 DustTraks™, one with a 1 μm impactor and the other with a 10 μm impactor. The differen ce in the signal from the two devices provided a measure of the fraction of the particulate that was larger than 1 μm. In some cases dilution was required owing to the high smoke concentration levels. A schematic of the assembled hardware appears in Fig. 4. The system was inst alled in the Microgravity Science Glovebox, an ISS facility that provides ma ny resources including: containment, power, data, video and uplink commanding. Smoke was generated by resistively heating small samples of spacecraft materials. Sample heating wa s achieved by heating a steel wire that was wrapped around an approximately 3 mm diameter cylinder of the sample material. The steel wire was resistively heated and Figure 4. Schematic of the SAME hardware.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p21,0,"11 the wire resistance was monitored to control its temperature. This technique was found to produce repeatable weight loss from sample to sample in laboratory tests. Groups of six pre- weighed samples were installed in carousels (Fig. 5) that the crew installe d in the SAME hardware. For each test, a new sample was moved into place by rotating one of the six sample chambers in the carousel into the flow duct and the sample heating and the flow were synchronized to provide a steady air stream over the sample during the heating interval. The system was designed to provide air fl ow rates ranging from 2 to 8 cm/s.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p21,1,"To emulate a variety of fire scenarios, tests were conducted at different sample temperatures, air flow rates, and smoke aging durations. The sample heating time is approximately 60 seconds. During this interval, controlled flow was induced by a moving piston in the aging chamber which drew the smoke into the chamber. The smoke was held in the chamber for a pre-determined time, allowing the particles to coagulate. After a spec ified aging time, the smoke was then pushed by the piston into the diagnostics duct where the moment in struments made their measurements. Also installed in the diagnostics duct were space s huttle and ISS smoke detectors. Th eir output signals were used to compare with the results of the moment method. As the aging chamber was filled and as the aerosol wa s pushed out to the moment instruments, a small aerosol stream was drawn off to the thermal precipitator, (Fig. 6). In side the precipitator, the aerosol passes between a Transmission Electron Microscope ( TEM) grid and a heated wire. Thermophoretic",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p21,2,"Figure 6. Thermal Precipitator Module. Left image is a photogr aph of a partially disassembled Thermal Precipitator Module with the inlet manifold exposed on the left, the hot wire leads in the red Vespel block in the middle and the outlet valves on the right. The drawing on the right disp lays the position of the hot wi re above the TEM grid with the air flow passing over both. The heated wire drives aerosol particles onto the grid via thermophoresis. Heated Wire TEM Grid Air Flow Figure 5. Photograph of a sample carousel. Carousel contains an assortment of Teflon, Kapton, lamp wick, and silicone rubber. The samples were supported and heated by the same stainless steel wire coil.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p22,0,12 forces moved particles to the grid surface where they adhered. After the mission the grids were removed from the assembly and examined in a TEM to obtain an independent determination of the particle size and morphology. C. Instrument Calibration,,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p22,1,"Calibration of these instruments was essential to prope rly interpret the flight data and was performed on the ground before the flight. Calibra tion was accomplished using two different aerosol generators; one generates mono-disperse particle using dioctyl phthalate (DOP) according to the approach by Mulholland and Liu 19 and the other uses polysty rene spheres. The monodi sperse droplet generator functioned by producing a spray of DOP diluted with isopropanol wh ich is then evaporated and recondensed producing monodisperse droplets. The droplet size is controlled by the DOP dilution level. The generator will operate stably for tens of minutes. The aerosol from the generator was sampled simultaneously by the SAME instru ment under test and a reference instrument. For the number count, the reference instrument was a TSI 3022 particle count er, for the mass concentration of the aerosol, a Tapered Element Oscillating Microbalance and for th e first moment a TSI Elect rical Aerosol Detector was used. The results for the P-Trak are shown in Fi g. 7. As the number c oncentration increases, the effect of the particle diameter becomes more evid ent. Separate correlations were developed for each particle size and the closes t correlation was used to analyze the flight data based on the initial estimates of the average particle size.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p22,2,"The first moment device, the ion chamber, showed very lit tle effect of particle si ze, as seen in Fig 8. Consequently a single correlation was used for all particle sizes. The third moment device (TSI- DustTrak®) is theoretically predicted to show non- monotonic behavior as par ticle size is increased. Figure 7. P-trak Calibration results with monodisperse DOP droplets. 0 100000 200000 300000 400000 500000 600000 0 50000 100000 150000 200000 250000 300000 350000 Ptrak output (number count/cc) TSI 3022A reference (number count/ cc) 100 nm 600 nm 1200 nm",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p23,0,"13 While a satisfactory prediction of the DustTrak® response was obtaine d using liquid droplets of dioctyl phthalate, (Fig. 9) the uncertainty associated with the Mie scattering calculations due to the particle refractive index estimate, the potential of agglomer ated particles, and deviation from assumed log- Figure 8. Ion Chamber Calibration results with monodisperse DOP droplets. 0 50 100 150 200 250 300 350 400 450 500 4.555.566.57 Ionization Chamber output (volts) EAD Output (first moment) 100 nm 150 nm 300 nm 600 nm 750 nm 1200 nm Figure 9. DustTrak results with monodisperse DOP droplets. 0 20 40 60 80 100 120 140 160 180 0 50 100 150 200 250 300 DustTrak output (mg/m3) TEOM (mg / m3) 100 nm 150 nm 300 nm 600 nm 750 nm 1200 nm",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p24,0,"14 normal size distribution must be assessed further for the smoke samp les here. In the results reported here, the DustTrak® response was directly calib rated, for each smoke source, against mass concentration measurements using a tapered element oscillating microbalance. This approach is valid as long as the size distribution of th e sample does not change significa ntly. Using estimated material densities, the diameter of average mass was computed for each sample. Equation 8 was used to compute the geometric standard deviation from the arithmetic mean diameter and the diameter of average mass, and Equation 7 was used to compute the GMD. 4. Flight Tests The SAME hardware installed in the ISS MSG is s hown in Fig 10. The SAME tests were performed on board ISS in September and early October of 2007. Overall 30 sample materials were tested. These were comprised of six samples each of 5 materials: Teflon™, Kapton™, silicone rubber, cellulose, and dibutyl-phthalate deposited on a por ous wick. These samples were test ed at different airflow rates, pyrolysis temperatures, and smoke aging durations.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p24,1,"Realizing that crew time was the most precious I SS resource, SAME was desi gned to be operated both autonomously and from the ground to the greatest exte nt possible. The SAME hardware was organized into a number of smaller modular un its to facilitate stow age for launch, provide for easy change out, increase the reliability a nd reduce risk. The downside of this st rategy was that the assembly of the experiment involved many connections and required approximately 5 hours of crew time. Once installed, however, SAME was easily and effectively operated fr om the Tele Science Center (TSC) at the Glenn Research Center (GRC), and this ca pability enabled the ground team to achieve roughl y a 50% greater number of test points (and data) than was originally planned. The on orbit operations were scheduled around the availability of the crew for installing the next Sample Carousel and Thermal Precipitator (and removing and stowing the used ones). Typically, the crew would install the next units during the morning, and operati ons would be scheduled for later that day. SAME would be nominally scheduled by the ISS staff for two test points on each of the following days. Figure 10. SAME Experiment installed in the ISS MSG Facility.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p25,0,"15 Because the allowed operations windows were sufficiently large, the ground team was frequently able to perform four test points in the allowed time. The staffing level of the ground team varied between two and five me mbers from the engineering team with additional support from up to three members of the Science team. Most of the time, there would be one person operating the linked co mputer that controlled the expe riment, one providing verbal communication to the ISS cadre an d doing data reduction and a third recording video and performing other miscellaneous functions. Uplink parameter sheets were generated by the Science team and most times, emailed to the engineering team the day before operations, although when additional unplanned test points were performed during a session, the Science team provided a hard copy of the parameters during the interim between test points. These sheets were printed and checked, and in most cas es, signed. The parameters were entered into the ground computer by a team member and then the en try was checked both by means of a checksum and by the other member on consol before uplinking them to the DACU. The test points were scheduled by the ground team to coincide with Ku and S band coverage so that video of the heating and the measurement data coul d be obtained Near Real Time (NRT) and recorded on the ground.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p25,1,"Because the data reduction software was already in place, processing of the data could be done immediately after the test point had been completed, and in all cases, before the next test point was performed. Periodically during the overall operations, downloads from the DACU were commanded as a backup to the NRT data to insure that the most complete compilation was available. Detailed chronological record of the SAME flight tests can be found in Appendix A. 5. Test Results and Discussions The analog signals from various instruments were digitized at 1 Hz by an 8 bit A/D converter. Figure 11 contains the signal traces from a typical run for silicone rubber. The difference in the signals for the two DustTrak’s indicates that a substan tial portion of the aerosol mass is in particles that are larger than 1 μm. For comparison, Fig. 12 contains similar data for a Teflon run where little difference is evident between the two DustTrak instruments, indicating that the bulk of the aerosol mass is in particles below 1 μm. Figure 13 shows that the effect of is a substantial decrease in the number concentration (Ptrak) while the mass concentrations (DustTraks) remain relatively stea dy. This behavior is consistent with particle coagulation with limited wall loss. It is also inte resting to compare the response of the ISS and STS detectors for silicone rubber and Teflon. In figure 11, both the ISS de tector and the STS detectors show strong signals.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p25,2,"(Note that the presence of smoke yi elds a positive deflection of the ISS signal and a negative deflection on the STS signal). This is reasonable because the silicone rubber smoke has a larger diameter of average mass in Table 1. In figure 12, the STS detector provided a strong signal, whereas the signal from the ISS detector was more erratic, because the Teflon smoke is comprised primarily of particles having diameters less than 1 m, as mentioned above. This s hows that the smoke detectors used in STS and ISS have different response to particle size range. Results from the moment instruments for baseline runs (no aging) and 720 second aging runs are summarized in Table 1.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p26,0,16 Figure 12. Instrument traces for a typical Teflon run. Base levels are at the beginning and end of the trace. The actual sampling interval is between 74025 and 74060 seconds. The small difference between DustTrak A and DustTrak B is indicative that the most of the particle distribution is smaller than 1 μm (the cutoff for the impactor on DustTrak A). Teflon Test 25 GMT 268 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 73975 74025 74075 74125 74175 74225 74275 Time (Seconds) -10 0 10 20 30 40 50 60 70 80 ISSDetectorScatterMeasurement(Volts) STSDetectorMeasurement(Volts) IonDetectorBMeasurement(Volts) PTrakMeasurement(PtPerCC/1000) (right) DustTrakAMeasurement(MgPerM3) (right) DustTrakBMeasurement(MgPerM3) (right) Figure 11. Instrument traces for a typical silicone rubber run. Base levels are at th e beginning and end of the trace. The actual sampling interval is between 125 and 160 seconds. The diffe rences in the rise times of each instrument are due to the smoke transit time to the instrument. The large difference between DustTrak A and DustTrak B is indicative of the substantial portion of the particle distribution that is larger than 1μm (the cutoff for the impactor on DustTrak A). Silicone Run 24 GMT 267 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 75 125 175 225 275 325 375 0 10 20 30 40 50 60 70 80 ISSDetectorScatterMeasurement(Volts) STSDetectorMeasurement(Volts) IonDetectorBMeasurement(Volts) PTrakMeasurement(PtPerCC)/1000 (right) DustTrakAMeasurement(MgPerM3) (right) DustTrakBMeasurement(MgPerM3) (right),,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p27,0,"17 Table 1. Results of SAME flight tests Geom.Mean Diam. (nm) Arithmetic Mean Diam. (nm) Diam. of Average Mass (nm) σg Kapton 80 105 179 2.08 Kapton aged 155 186 267 1.82 Lamp wick 186 227 341 1.89 Lamp wick aged 258 308 438 1.81 Silicone 189 257 472 2.18 Silicone aged 352 422 606 1.83 Teflon 170 205 297 1.84 Teflon aged 228 273 392 1.82 The Teflon and Kapton partic les are very small with Teflon showing limited increase in the size from the diameter of average mass compar ed to the arithmetic mean diameter. The lamp wick results exhibit substantially larger diameter of av erage mass. As reported previously, 12 the DustTrak results for lamp wick and silicone demonstrated su bstantial portions of the particle distribut ion possessed particle diameters larger than 1 m. The small diameters for the Kapton and Teflon will make detection of this smoke challenging for light scattering devices, on the other hand the large sizes seen with the lamp wick and silicone would generate very large signals on a lig ht scattering system, sugge sting that detection of these particles against the background environment will require a detection system capable of measuring more than one moment of the particle size distributi on. The aging results show substantial increase in the average particle sizes w ith them rising to over 1 m for the aged silicone di ameter of average mass. Since the aging can be considered to be representative of smoke emerging from a closed volume (e.g. an",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p27,1,"Figure 13. Instrument traces for a typical smoke aging run for silicone rubber. Base levels are at the beginning and end of the trace. The first sampling interval is between 5200 and 5250 seconds and the post aging sampling interval is between 6000 and 6050 seconds. Consistent with particle coagulation with limited wall loss, the overall number count (Ptrak) decreases substantially while the mass concentrations (DustTraks) remain relatively steady. GMT 267 Silicone Run 23 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 5100 5300 5500 5700 5900 6100 6300 6500 Time (sec) -10 10 30 50 70 90 110 130 ISSDetectorScatterMeasurement(Volts) IonDetectorBMeasurement(Volts) PTrakMeasurement(PtPerCC)/1000 (right scale) DustTrakAMeasurement(MgPerM3) (right scale) DustTrakBMeasurement(MgPerM3) (right scale)",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p28,0,"18 avionics enclosure), fire sensors mu st consider large particles in addition to the smaller particles seen with Kapton. In general, since the mass increases with the cube of the diameter, the larger particles do not affect the count mean diameter (CMD) as much as they affect the light scattering signa l which scales with the particle mass. Consequently, although the CMD for silicone rubber is 189 nm, almost half the particle mass is larger than 1 m, as evidenced by the ratio of the Dust Trak instruments. The lamp wick shows similar behavior. On the other hand, the Kapton and Teflon show substan tially narrower particle size distributions with little mass in particles larger than 1m. These low populations of larger particles are consistent with the less effective detection by light scat tering devices which was seen for so me of the Teflon tests and with the signals obtained from the STS a nd ISS detectors. All of the tests conducted in this series of experiments show strong signals on the Space Shuttle detector (ionization) despite the larger particle size distributions seen for the lamp wick (cellulose) and silicone rubber. The CSD experiment5 reported poor detection by the Space Shuttle detector for non- flaming overheat of cellulose and silicone rubber (indicating larger particle sizes).",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p28,1,"The reduced signal on the Space Shuttle detector for CSD suggests that under some reasonable pre-fire conditions the smoke size distribution can extend to diameters beyond those seen in the SAME experiment. The TEM grids recovered from the thermal precipi tators were unfortunately contaminated with extraneous particles whose origin was believed to be somewhere in the assembly of the thermal precipitators. Despite the contamination, the lamp wick and Teflon samples pr oduced distinct particles consistent with ground based experience. Typical particles are shown in Fig. 14. Figure 14: Test point 32 lamp wick Test point 26, Teflon",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p29,0,"19 Several conference papers were prepared during the analysis of the data obtained from SAME and are included in the appendices. These provide additional details on several intermediate steps in the data analysis process that led to the final results described in this report. 6. Closing Remarks The SAME project successf ully implemented the moment method de scribed in this report, under the volume constraints of the ISS MSG, to determine th e size distribution of th e smokes generated from 5 tested materials in well controlled conditions of heat ing temperature, heating tim e and air flow rate. In combination with the knowledge gained from prev ious CSD experiment, it becomes obvious that the detection of smoke across the size range seen in SA ME and CSD, while still discriminating against dust (which tends to be large), will prob ably require a detection system that can discriminate the particle size distribution to at least a limited degree. Two possible effective means of achieving this size discrimination are to employ a detector that measures more than one mo ment of the size distribution or to use a detector that can classify the particles into size ranges.",,False
PSI-102,Reports,PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reports/PSI-102_Reports_SAME_Final Science Report.pdf,p30,0,"20 References 1. Bukowski, R.W. and G.W. Mulholland, “Smoke Detector Design and Smoke Properties,” NBS Technical Note 973, 1978. 2. Bukowski, R. W, R. D. Peacock, J. D. Averill, T. G. Cleary, N. P. Bryner, W. D. Walton, P. A. Reneke, and E. D. Kuligowski, “Performance of Ho me Smoke Alarms, Analysis of the Response of Several Available Technologies in Residential Fire Settings,” NIST Technical Note 1455, December 2003. 3. Brooker, J. E., Urban, D.L., Ruff, G.A., “I SS Destiny Laboratory Sm oke Detection Model”, 07ICES-113, 2007 International Conference on Environmental Systems, Chicago, Ill. July 2007. 4. Friedman, R., “Fire Safety Practices and Needs in Human-Crew Spacecraft,” Journal of Applied Fire Science, Vol. 2, 1992, pp. 243-259. 5. Urban, D.L., Griffin D., Ruff, G.A., Cleary, T., Ya ng, J., Mulholland, G., Yuan, Z.G., “Detection of Smoke from Microgravity Fires”, 2005 Internat ional Conference on Environmental Systems, Rome Italy, Paper # 2005-01-2930 July 2005, SAE Transactions, pp 375-384. 6. Steisslinger, H. R., D. M. Hoy, J. A. McLin and E. C. Thomas., “Comparison Testing of the Space Shuttle Orbiter and Space Station Freedom Smoke Detectors,” SAE Paper 932291, 1993. 7. Martin, C.E. and R.C. DaLee, “Spacecraft Fire Detection and Suppression (FDS) Systems: An Overview and Recommendations for Future Flights,” SAE Technical Paper 932166, 23rd International Conference on Environmental Systems, Colorado Springs, CO, July 12-15, 1993. 8.",,False
