PSI-102,Presentations,PSI-102_Presentations_SAME2_FlightVSGroundTestResults.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Presentations/PSI-102_Presentations_SAME2_FlightVSGroundTestResults.pptx,slide 27,0,Conclusions 27 Particle sizes ranged from 100 to 600 nm Consistent with a log-normal distribution Particle sizes increase substantially with aging Particle dimensions increase substantially as air flow was decreased TEM showed a significant range of distinct particle morphologies For lampwick and silicone approximately 40% of the aerosol mass had aerodynamic diameters greater than 1 μm however Teflon and Kapton were mostly less than 1 μm,,False
PSI-102,Presentations,PSI-102_Presentations_SAME2_FlightVSGroundTestResults.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Presentations/PSI-102_Presentations_SAME2_FlightVSGroundTestResults.pptx,slide 28,0,Conclusions 28 Ground based testing at 8 cm/s showed particle dimensions very close to the flight results ISS Detector showed reduced detection performance for Kapton and Teflon due to the small size STS Detector fared slightly better for Kapton and Teflon Spacecraft fire conditions include an even wider array of materials and conditions Spacecraft background aerosols can be quite large (false alarms),,False
PSI-102,Presentations,PSI-102_Presentations_SAME2_FlightVSGroundTestResults.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Presentations/PSI-102_Presentations_SAME2_FlightVSGroundTestResults.pptx,slide 29,0,Conclusions 29 It is improbable that a single-moment device can be sensitive across the size spectrum and resist false alarms Detectors that can normalize the concentration for the particle size may solve this problem,,False
