PSI-102,Presentations,PSI-102_Presentations_SAME-2_ParticleMorphology and Size Results.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Presentations/PSI-102_Presentations_SAME-2_ParticleMorphology and Size Results.pptx,slide 27,0,27 Moment Results - Teflon,,False
PSI-102,Presentations,PSI-102_Presentations_SAME-2_ParticleMorphology and Size Results.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Presentations/PSI-102_Presentations_SAME-2_ParticleMorphology and Size Results.pptx,slide 28,0,28 MPASS Results,,False
PSI-102,Presentations,PSI-102_Presentations_SAME-2_ParticleMorphology and Size Results.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Presentations/PSI-102_Presentations_SAME-2_ParticleMorphology and Size Results.pptx,slide 29,0,"29 Conclusions For the conditions tested, the materials emit most of their particulate in the submicron range. A valid database now exists for design of future smoke detectors Particle dimensions vary strongly as a result of aging or flow residence time and temperature (for Kapton) Particle morphology varies widely across the range of expected sample materials. Dependence on a single angle scattering device will be difficult. MPASS – multi-moment approach shows promise for size segregating detector",,False
PSI-102,Presentations,PSI-102_Presentations_SAME-2_ParticleMorphology and Size Results.pptx,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Presentations/PSI-102_Presentations_SAME-2_ParticleMorphology and Size Results.pptx,slide 30,0,"30 Implications and Future Work Dust rejection may be achievable by detecting at the sub-micron level. Given the effects of aging and the slow mixing seen in spacecraft, it is probably unwise to focus entirely on small particle sizes for fire detection Size selective smoke detection will improve reliability and false alarm rejection. Either by classifying the particles or by measuring multiple moments. Addition of gaseous species detection could be helpful but requires further study.",,False
