PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p5,1,3 4.1.1 VERIFICATION METHODS................................................................................................. 3 4.1.2 TOOLS (REQUIREMENTS MATRIX AND VERIFICATION TRACKING LOG)............ 4 4.2 PLANNING................................................................................................................................... 4 4.2.1 VERIFICATION DEFINITION SHEETS.............................................................................. 4 4.2.2 TEST PLANS AND PROCEDURES ..................................................................................... 4 4.2.3 IMPLEMENTATION AND REPORTING ............................................................................ 4 4.3 CERTIFICATE OF COMPLIANCE............................................................................................. 6 4.4 MAINTENANCE OF THE CERTIFICATE OF COMPLIANCE................................................ 6 4.5 REQUIREMENT SOURCES........................................................................................................ 6 4.5.1 INVESTIGATION REQUIREMENTS .................................................................................. 6 4.5.2 SOFTWARE ENGINEERING REQUIREMENTS................................................................ 6 4.5.3 PRODUCT ASSURANCE REQUIREMENTS......................................................................,,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p5,2,6 4.5.4 SAFETY REQUIREMENTS.................................................................................................. 6 4.5.5 MSG CARRIER REQUIREMENTS ...................................................................................... 7 4.5.6 SPACE SHUTTLE AND ISS REQUIREMENTS ................................................................. 7 4.5.7 KSC FACILITY REQUIREMENTS ...................................................................................... 7 4.6 STAFFING .................................................................................................................................... 7 4.6.1 ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES....................................................................................... 7 5.0 REQUIREMENTS MATRIX ..................................................................................................... 8 APPENDIX A – VERIFICATION MATRIX......................................................................................... A-0,,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p6,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 1 1.0 INTRODUCTION 1.1 Scope This plan applies to all Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment (SAME) hardware, software, and operating procedures developed under the Microgravity Research, Development, and Operations Contract (MRDOC). This plan establishes the methods and responsibilities for verifying the SAME requirements. The requirements for the SAME project, including investigation, engineering, product assurance, safety, and carrier requirements, are in Appendix A of this document. The verification methods and sources of these requirements are also included in Appendix A. This plan defines the verification process, leading to certification of the SAME flight hardware. 1.2 Purpose This document defines the verification activities required to show that SAME complies with its requirements. These activities include the verification of investigation requirements from the Exploration Investigation Requirements Document (EIRD) for SAME, software engineering requirements from the Software Requirements Documents, product assurance requirements from the Project Product Assurance Plan (PAP), safety requirements from the Hazard Reports, and carrier requirements from the Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) Interface Control Document (ICD). This document includes all of the requirements to be verified by SAME and serves as the Engineering Requirements Document to organize and list all necessary requirements.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p6,1,"1.3 TBDs TBDs are used as a place-holder for requirements, verifications, or document numbers that are not known at the time of the latest release of this document. 2.0 DOCUMENTS 2.1 Applicable Documents The documents listed are applicable to the SAME project to the extent specified herein. Exploration Investigation Requirements Document for SAME a.k.a. Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment Standardized Hazard Report (STD-SAME-01) and Unique Hazard Reports (SAME-03, -04, -05, and -08) MSFC-ICD-3462 Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) Interface Control Document for SAME (Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment) MSFC-RQMT-2888 Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) Investigation Interface Requirements Document MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001 MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document NSTS 1700.7B Safety Policy and Requirements for Payloads Using the Space Transportation System",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p7,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 2 NSTS 1700.7B ISS Addendum Safety Policy and Requirements for Payloads Using the International Space Station NSTS/ISS 13830 Payload Safety Review and Data Submittal SAME-DOC-001 Baseline Concept Description Document SAME-DOC-004 Software Requirements Document SAME-DOC-005 Software Interface Control Document SAME-PLN-003 Product Assurance Plan SSP 50313 Display and Graphics Commonality Standard SSP 57000 Pressurized Payloads Interface Control Document 2.2 Reference Documents The documents listed are provided only as reference material for background information and are not imposed as requirements. SAME-PLN-002 Configura tion Management Plan 3.0 ACRONYMS AND ABBREVIATIONS EIRD Exploration Investigation Requirements Document EU Engineering Unit GRC Glenn Research Center HFIT Human Factors Integration Team ICD Interface Control Document ISS International Space Station JSC Johnson Space Center KSC Kennedy Space Center MRDOC Microgravity Research, Development, and Operations Contract MSG Microgravity Science Glovebox PSRP Payload Safety Review Panel QA Quality Assurance SAME Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment SARG Space Assurance and Requirements and Guidelines SVTL Safety Verification Tracking Log VDS Verification Definition Sheet",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p8,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 3 4.0 VERIFICATIONS 4.1 General The SAME Project Office will verify all the requirements imposed on the SAME System. The primary sites for verification activities will be the Glenn Research Center (GRC) and the item developer’s site unless otherwise directed by the MSG or International Space Station (ISS) programs. All requirements will be recorded in this document and disseminated to the design team. This document will provide traceability from the parent requirement through the subsequent derived requirements. It will also identify the verification source document and verification method. SAME will be building and verifying more than one set of flight hardware. Consequently, a certain number of verifications must be repeated for each serialized assembly. A subset of the requirements is designated as series requirements. 4.1.1 Verification Methods The methods used to complete the verifications are analysis, test, inspection and demonstration. The method(s) required for the SAME verifications are listed in the requirements matrix in Appendix A. The methods of verification are defined in MSFC-RQMT-2888 as stated in Sections 4.1.1.1, 4.1.1.2, 4.1.1.3, and 4.1.1.4. 4.1.1.1 Analysis Analysis is a technical evaluation that relates equipment design and use parameters for prediction of actual design and operation. Analysis may be used to verify requirements, provided established techniques used are adequate to yield confidence, or where testing is impractical.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p8,1,"Included in this category is analysis of similarity to items previously verified to the same criteria or more stringent criteria. Verification accomplished by analysis shall be evidenced by an analysis report. 4.1.1.2 Test Test is actual operation of equipment under simulated conditions or the subjection of equipment to specified environments to measure responses. Verification accomplished by test shall be evidenced by a test report. 4.1.1.3 Inspection Inspection is a physical evaluation of equipment and associated documentation. Inspection may be used to verify construction features, drawing compliance, workmanship, and physical condition. It includes determination of physical dimensions. Verification accomplished by inspection shall be evidenced by an inspection report. 4.1.1.4 Demonstration Demonstration is the qualitative determination of compliance with requirements by observation during actual operation or simulation under preplanned conditions and guidelines. Some human factor requirements where the method of closure is a demonstration require the investigation to be set up in its on-orbit configuration in the MSG Engineering Unit (EU) to verify that the requirement is satisfied.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p9,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 4 4.1.2 Tools (Requirements Matrix and Verification Tracking Log) A matrix has been compiled in an Excel spreadsheet to include all requirements to be satisfied by SAME. It identifies the source of each requirement, the discipline under which it falls, the verification method to be used for closure, and resources necessary to perform the verification. It also identifies series requirements. The matrix, in Appendix A, will be provided to the engineering team for use in verification activities, and will be included in the released (.pdf) version of this document. The verification tracking log (VTL) will be used to track the status of the verifications and the documentation provided as evidence for the completion of the verifications. It is an Excel workbook that includes five worksheets: the VTL itself, a statistics page, the current version of the requirements matrix, a summary of applicable ECOs, and a notes/explanation page. The VTL will not be configuration- controlled as it is a summary of the state of controlled documentation and it will not be included in the released version of this document. It will be available in the project team’s shared directory. 4.2 Planning 4.2.1 Verification Definition Sheets A Verification Definition Sheet (VDS) will be used to complete the carrier requirements from MSFC- ICD-3462. The corresponding VDS for each carrier requirement is listed in the requirements matrix and can be found in Appendix E of MSFC-RQMT-2888.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p9,1,"The VDS provides instructions, definitions, references, and guidelines for verification activities associated with each requirement. 4.2.2 Test Plans and Procedures The controlled requirements matrix will be distributed to the engineering team so that the requirements can be grouped into meaningful test plans or procedures that will allow for efficient testing and closure of the requirements. Test plans and procedures will be written to include the verification statements to ensure that the requirements are clearly stated, understood, and closed out by the test. Test plans and procedures containing carrier requirements will also contain the corresponding VDS. Test plans and procedures will be a project team effort lead by the Systems Engineering team. Plans and procedures will use exact language from the requirement sources to convey the objective of the test as completion of these verifications. The template of how the requirements will be incorporated into the documents is shown in Figure 1. This table, along with the introductory text, will be included in test plans and procedures intended for verification activities. 4.2.3 Implementation and Reporting 4.2.3.1 Implementation Test verifications in the matrix will be logically grouped into similar items that can be completed in a single test. The test plans and procedures will be written accordingly.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p9,2,"Some of the verifications will be closed on the functional model or other similar flight-like hardware when the testing of flight hardware is not necessary or cannot be accomplished due to the nature of the test. Otherwise, testing will be done on the flight hardware. The tests performed on non-flight hardware are indicated in the requirements matrix.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p10,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 5 Inspections will be done at the time of hardware assembly and will be done on flight hardware or flight drawings. Analyses will be done using the flight configuration of hardware for the calculations. Demonstrations will be done on the flight hardware. All Human Factors (HFIT) verifications will be done once the hardware is delivered to MSG and the HFIT evaluation is performed. Additional verifications require integrated setup or testing in the MSG EU and will be completed after the hardware is delivered to MSG. These verifications are identified in the requirements matrix. Figure 1: Example of Verification List in Documents 4.2.3.2 Reporting Test, analysis and inspection reports will be written as evidence that the tests, analyses and inspections were performed to satisfy the requirements. Reports will include the requirement statements, as shown in Figure 1, to indicate what verifications were performed and are closed by the report. Reports and other necessary documentation will be kept by the Systems Engineering team and Configuration Management as evidence that the verifications were performed. The final report generated against a carrier requirement will be a comprehensive report intended to close out the requirement, upon which a Certificate of Compliance is issued. Certificates of Compliance will be generated to close verifications by demonstration.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p11,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 6 4.3 Certificate of Compliance As the carrier verification activities are completed, the SAME team will generate and sign Certificates of Compliance. With the completion of all verifications identified in this document and their associated Certificates of Compliance, all of the requirements and verifications of MSG compatibility, functionality, and safety will have been met. 4.4 Maintenance of the Certificate of Compliance Any changes to SAME occurring after the verification activities are completed will be reviewed to assess their impacts on the individual requirements and their associated verification certificates. If such a review determines that re-verification is warranted, the changes to SAME and the proposed additional verification activities will be presented to the MSG payload program office for concurrence before verification activities are completed. 4.5 Requirement Sources 4.5.1 Investigation Requirements The investigation requirements for SAME are found in the EIRD for SAME. These, along with derived engineering responses to these requirements from the various engineering disciplines, can be found in the requirements matrix in this document. These engineering responses will be verified to show that the investigation requirements are met. The Project Scientist for SAME will approve all reports containing investigation verification close-outs.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p11,1,"4.5.2 Software Engineering Requirements Software engineering requirements can be found in SAME-DOC-004, Software Requirements Document, and are included in the requirements matrix in this document. These requirements include detailed responses to the investigation, safety, and MSG carrier requirements, and requirements to establish good engineering practices. 4.5.3 Product Assurance Requirements Product assurance requirements can be found in SAME-PLN-003, Product Assurance Plan. The requirements found in the Product Assurance Plan are derived from the Space Assurance Requirements and Guidelines (SARG) as they are applicable to the SAME project. Statements containing “shall” require a formal documented closure of the requirement, as defined in the Product Assurance Plan, and are included in the requirements matrix. Statements containing “will or must” are requirements to be fulfilled but do not require a formal documented closure, as defined in the Product Assurance Plan. The “will or must” requirements which have a direct impact on the SAME project budget, schedule, and design are included in the requirements matrix.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p11,2,"4.5.4 Safety Requirements SAME safety requirements are identified according to the payload safety process described in NSTS 1700.7B, Safety Policy and Requirements for Payloads Using the Space Transportation System, and NSTS 1700.7B ISS Addendum, Safety Policy and Requirements for Payloads Using the International Space Station, using NSTS/ISS 13830, Payload Safety Review and Data Submittal.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p12,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 7 The SAME safety requirements are found in the SAME hazard reports and are included in the requirements matrix in this document. The requirements are included in the matrix to provide visibility and to allow them to be grouped with other requirements in verification activities (i.e. test plans and procedures) for efficient completion. Although closure information will be included in the VTL, the safety requirements will be officially tracked in a Safety Verification Tracking Log (SVTL) and maintained by the SAME Safety Engineer. The SVTL and documentation used to close the safety verifications will be provided to the Payload Safety Review Panel (PSRP) for approval at the Phase III Safety Review. 4.5.5 MSG Carrier Requirements Requirements for interfacing with the MSG are found in MSFC-ICD-3462, Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) Interface Control Document for SAME, and are included in the requirements matrix in this document. MSFC-ICD-3462 contains a Verification Applicability Matrix to indicate what requirements from MSFC-RQMT-2888 are applicable to SAME. The Verification Applicability Matrix also identifies the appropriate verification method, the applicable VDS, and other comments associated with the completion of the verification. All information given in the Verification Applicability Matrix is duplicated in the requirements matrix in this document. The complete text of the requirements as well as the VDS is found in MSFC-RQMT-2888.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p12,1,"4.5.6 Space Shuttle and ISS Requirements MSG interfaces with Johnson Space Center (JSC) concerning payload interface requirements. Therefore, all Space Shuttle and ISS requirements should be documented in MSFC-ICD-3462, as identified in Section 4.5.4. 4.5.7 KSC Facility Requirements SAME is not planning to operate at Kennedy Space Center (KSC). 4.6 Staffing Implementation of the SAME verification process requires all members of the SAME team to support the verification activities. 4.6.1 Roles and Responsibilities The Systems Engineering team will prepare documentation necessary for the performance of verifications and the reporting of the results of verification activities. All plans and procedures for verification activities will be checked by a member of the Systems Engineering team to insure that the applicable requirements are included. One member of the Systems Engineering team will be responsible for updating the VTL to track the status of the verification activities and to include the document number for the reports generated to close the verifications. Hardware and Software Engineers will provide assistance writing test plans and procedures and performing verification activities. The Safety Engineer will track and maintain all safety verifications from the hazard reports in the SVTL. The Project Scientist will approve all documentation that completes an investigation requirement.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p12,2,Quality Assurance (QA) personnel will be involved if the requirement in question is a QA requirement.,,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p13,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 8 Configuration Management will control documentation per SAME-PLN-002, Configuration Management Plan. 5.0 REQUIREMENTS MATRIX The requirements matrix listing the SAME requirements can be found in Appendix A of this document. When viewing the original electronic files of this document, the requirements matrix can be found in an Excel spreadsheet with the same name as this document. The hardcopy of this document and the .pdf version will include the requirements matrix at time of release in Appendix A. In addition to requirements to be verified, the matrix contains section titles and parent requirements that provide additional information to aid in the understanding of the verification activities. The items that are not verifiable requirements are designated in the matrix as indicated in Table 1. Table 1 is an explanation of the information contained in the requirements matrix. Table 1: Explanation of Verification Matrix Column Heading Explanation Requirement Number This column contains a unique identifying number that tracks the line item in the matrix. The requirements are numbered based on their source. Software requirements from SAME-DOC-004 that are derived as responses to other requirements are numbered according to the parent requirement.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p13,1,"000-099 – Investigation requirements from the EIRD 100-109 – Software requirements from SAME-DOC-004 110-199 – Product assurance requirements from SAME-PLN-003 200-299 – Safety requirements from the Standardized and Unique Hazard Reports 300-499 – Carrier requirements from MSFC-ICD-3462 Requirement Source This column lists the source of the requirement, where the text of the requirement can be found. There is no source listed for requirements derived from the investigation requirements. Their source is generally the Engineering Requirements Document, which is incorporated into this document. Discipline This is the discipline responsible for the completion of the requirement. Statements that are not verifiable requirements (i.e. section titles, descriptions, and parent requirements broken into verifiable sub-requirements) are indicated with “Title” in this column. Requirement This is the text of the requirement taken from the “Requirement Source.” In addition to requirements, this column includes the section titles and other information necessary for the completion of the requirement. System Response & Notes This column contains any notes necessary to explain the requirement. In the investigation requirements, this column further expands on the engineering response to the requirement. In the MSG carrier requirements, this column contains notes that were indicated in the ICD Verification Applicability Matrix.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p14,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F 9 Column Heading Explanation Method This is the method required for verification. T = Test A = Analysis I = Inspection D = Demonstration NVR = No Verification Required (item is a title, description, or a parent requirement) PSRP = For MSG carrier requirements, this indicates that the verification is closed when the PSRP approves the corresponding safety verification. MSG requires no additional data be provided to them for closure. Series There is an “X” in this column if the requirement must be verified for serialized hardware. Safety There is an “X” in this column if the requirement came from one of the Hazard Reports, corresponds to a requirement from the Hazard Reports, or if it was indicated as a safety requirement in the ICD Verification Applicability Matrix. HFIT There is an “X” in this column if the requirement was indicated as an HFIT requirement in the ICD Verification Applicability Matrix. VDS # This column contains the Verification Definition Sheet (VDS) Number as indicated in the ICD Verification Applicability Matrix. The VDS contains information necessary to complete the verification and can be found in MSFC-RQMT-2888, Appendix E. Hardware F.M.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p14,1,Flt. An “X” in one of these columns indicates the classification of hardware that the final verification will be performed on. A hardware system will also be identified for each requirement marked here. Verification will be performed on the Functional Model or other flight-like hardware Verification will be performed on Flight Hardware System Full-up System Sub-System An “X” in one of these columns indicates the hardware system on which the verification activities will be performed. Verification will be performed on the complete SAME system. Verification will be performed on partial SAME system. GSE Required Certified Ground Support Equipment (GSE) is required to perform the verification. In MSG EU An “X” in this column indicates that the verification can be performed in the MSG EU. An “X*” in this column indicates that the verification must be performed in the MSG EU. Documents Flight Drawings Outside Source,,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p14,2,"Documentation/ Procedures An “X” in these columns indicates documentation, rather than hardware, will be used to close the verification. Flight drawings will be used to perform the verification. Quality Assurance personnel will ensure that the hardware is built to the drawings. For software requirements, this indicates the code will be used to perform the verification. The documentation used will be provided from an outside source (e.g. NIST, MSG) Indicates documentation will be the source of information used to close the verification. This includes documents provided by SAME, outside documentation and crew procedures.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p15,0,SAME-PLN-005 Rev F A-0 APPENDIX A – VERIFICATION MATRIX (see SAME-PLN-005.xls),,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p16,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures 001 SAME EIRD 1 Title Smoke Generation Duct (Aging Chamber Inlet Elbow) NVR 001.01 SAME EIRD 1.a Title a) Configuration: Square or round duct with air flow through the duct. NVR 001.01.1 Mechanical Engineering The smoke generation duct shall be round with air flow through the duct. SAME Drawing #____________ I X 001.02 SAME EIRD 1.b Title b) Optical access: One view is required of the sample and the downstream flow. This view should be illuminated and imaged by a color video camera (visible light only). The orientation of the light relative to the imager should permit the visualization of the light scattered by the smoke. The duct has a port downstream of the sample, with a wide-angle camera mounted to view the sample and the first portion of the flow duct. Lighting will provide image visibility in the closed tube and has been demonstrated in 1-g with sample smoke. NVR 001.02.1 Mechanical Engineering 01. The duct shall have a port approximately 1 inch downstream of the sample. 02. Mounting shall be provided for the camera at the port. SAME Drawing #____________ See # 008.01.1 I X 001.02.2 Electrical Engineering Imaging Device Electrical 01. Power camera 02. Control camera 03. Record camera 04. Power LED's 01. Power to the camera is routed from the MSG Video + 12 VDC source. 02. Electronics will be provided to allow the DACU to control the camera. 03.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p16,1,"Video signal is routed to a transcoder, then to the MSG monitor and recorder. 04. Power is supplied to the LED's Reference SAME electrical schematic #__________________ See # 008.01.2 T XXX X 001.02.3 Software Engineering Provide software to control (if necessary) and record the images from the camera and the LED's See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.2.1.5, 4.4.5, and 5.3 NVR 001.02.3.a SAME-DOC-004 4.2 Software Engineering The DACU shall have the ability to control MSG video drawer functions by sending video command packets to the SPLC. The controllable video drawer functions are determined by the requirements of the video drawer command group, Section 4.4.5. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-5-1] See # 008.01.3.a TXXXX 001.02.3.b SAME-DOC-004 4.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall accept a set of commands to perform MSG video drawer operations. The video drawer group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 6 of SAME-DOC-004. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-5-1-1] See # 008.01.3.b TXXXX 001.02.3.c SAME-DOC-004 5.3 Software Engineering The DACU shall control power to any combination of the camera lights. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-3-1-1-1] See # 008.01.3.c TXX 001.02.3.d SAME-DOC-004 5.3 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the gain and gamma settings of the camera. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-3-2-1-1] See # 008.01.3.d TXX 001.02.4 Diagnostics Imaging Device Specifications: 01. Allow for imaging of sample, heater wire, and smoke plume 02. Ability to verify no combustion beyond pyrolysis (LED's off) 03.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p16,2,"Verify sample type and placement 04. A ruled image in the field of view will be recorded with the flight system. A wide angle lens on a CCD color camera and illuminated by an LED in a side-scattering orientation provides a view of the centerline. See # 008.01.4 TXX 001.03 SAME EIRD 1.c Title c) Length: The straight section of the duct downstream of the sample shall be at least 30 cm long, and shall be known within 1 cm. NVR 001.03.1 Mechanical Engineering The straight section of the smoke generation duct length shall: 01. Be at least 30 cm long, length of duct to be provided from drawing 02. Fit within the confines of the MSG Working Volume Length will be maximized within MSG physical and operational constraints to meet requirements. See SAME Drawing #____________ A X 001.04 SAME EIRD 1.d Title d) Width: The test section width shall be based upon the sample size (see sample size below). The smallest duct dimension shall be twice the largest sample dimension. NVR Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required A-1",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p17,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 001.04.1 Mechanical Engineering The smoke generation duct width shall be at least twice as wide as the largest sample dimension. The sample size (see section 006) is not specified by science, rather it is determined by manufacturing requirements and 1-g testing. The requirement for double the width is easily met, and will be verified on the final design. See SAME Drawing #_________ for the layout and dimensions of the duct width I X 001.05 SAME EIRD 1.e Title e) Flow rate: 1 to 10 (+/- 0.5) cm/s average flow velocity. NVR 001.05.1 Mechanical Engineering The air flow will be provided by a pneumatically controlled piston system This system shall provide: 01. Control of the rate of piston movement 02. Fluid path and solenoids to route vacuum from MSG vacuum connection plus pressure from MSG GN2 03. Pressure transducers to monitor pressure differential 04. Mounting for mass flow controller, fluid path, and transducer The flow rate is determined by the speed of the piston in the Aging Chamber. The piston is moved pneumatically by a pressure differential driven by vacuum (aging chamber fill) or GN2 (aging chamber purge).",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p17,1,"A software algorithm will determine and control the speed of the piston using data from a mass flow controller and a pressure transducer. System assembly and successful pyrolysis precludes inlet leaking. All ports must be sealed. Test procedure will include inspection for leaks. TX XX 001.05.2 Electrical Engineering Provide: 01. Power & control to piston system solenoids interfacing with vacuum and pressure. 02. Power & control to mass flow controller interfacing with vacuum and pressure. 03. Monitor output signal from mass flow controller interfacing with pressure (GN2) 04. Power to differential pressure transducer 05. Monitor output signal from differential pressure transducer See Electrical Schematic(s): SAME Drawing #____________ Power Summary Power to solenoid valves is 12 VDC Power to Differential XDCR is 28 Volts Power to MFC is 12 VDC Data and Control Summary 1 Analog output from MFC 1 Analog input to MFC 1 Analog output from Differential XDCR 8 discrete inputs to solenoids TX XX 001.05.3 Software Engineering Provide software to control: 01. Mass flow controller and solenoids Provide software to record data from: 02. Mass flow controller and differential pressure transducer output See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.4 NVR 001.05.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the aging chamber piston upward velocity to achieve 1 to 10 cm/s average flow velocity in the smoke generation duct.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p17,2,"[SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-1] T X X 001.05.3.b SAME-DOC-004 5.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the aging chamber piston upward movement by controlling a solenoid on the vacuum line, by sensing and controlling a mass flow controller on the vacuum line, and by sensing a pressure transducer monitoring the drive side pressure of the piston. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-2] See # 001.08.3.a TXX 001.06 SAME EIRD 1.f Title f) Transverse spatial flow variation: Induced maximum flow velocity (no sample present) shall be at the center of the duct with monotonic decrease to the walls. The ratio of the centerline flow velocity to average velocity shall be no greater than fully developed laminar flow +10%. NVR 001.06.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a round smoke generation duct and a positive displacement system. System design provides flow through sample generation section by creating pressure differential. Inlet screen on sample distributes flow over entire cross section. Simple analysis of laminar flow through a round duct will be used to verify. A X A-2",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p18,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 001.07 SAME EIRD 1.g Multiple g) Flow profiles well characterized (cold flow, no flow present) in ground based testing (hot wire anemometry adequate, test plan to be approved by project scientist). Ground based testing will characterize the flow profiles. This is not expected to change in μg. T & A X X X 001.07.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a test structure and instrumentation for characterizing cold flow spatial profiles in ground based testing. Flow profile will be measured using a hot wire anemometer at three cross sections of the duct located at 5mm increments along the duct. NVR 001.07.2 Electrical Engineering Provide power, data and control support for test structure and instrumentation to characterize flow profiles. Flow profile will be measured using a hot wire anemometer at three cross sections of the duct located at 5mm increments along the duct. NVR 001.07.3 Software Engineering Provide software to support test structure and instrumentation to characterize flow profiles. See SAME-DOC-004, section 8.0 NVR 001.07.3.a SAME-DOC-004 8.0 Software Engineering Software will be provided to support test structures and instrumentation to characterize flow profiles.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p18,1,[SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-1] Development Software NVR 001.08 SAME EIRD 1.h Title h) Temporal Flow variation: Less than +/- 10% variation of average velocity (measured with 1 Hz resolution). NVR 001.08.1 Mechanical Engineering Air flow will be provided by a pneumatically controlled piston system. The system shall contain a pressure transducer and a mass flow controller to determine the rate of piston movement. The design of the aging chamber and piston shall provide for smooth movement of the piston. Interior of aging chamber is a smooth aluminum surface and the piston is Rulon with Teflon o- rings that allow it to move smoothly in the chamber. The pressure transducer and mass flow controller provide information on the location of the piston in the chamber. Temporal flow variation will be verified from the MFC and pressure transducer readings. AXX 001.08.2 Electrical Engineering Power and control will be provided to a mass flow controller and a pressure transducer to determine and monitor the movement of the piston in the aging chamber. IXX 001.08.3 Software Engineering Provide software to control: 01. Mass flow controller and solenoids Provide software to record data from: 02.,,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p18,2,"Mass flow controller and differential pressure transducer output See SAME-DOC-004, section 5.4 NVR 001.08.3.a SAME-DOC-004 5.4 Software Engineering The DACU shall control the aging chamber piston upward movement by controlling a solenoid on the vacuum line, by sensing and controlling a mass flow controller on the vacuum line, and by sensing a pressure transducer monitoring the drive side pressure of the piston. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-2] See # 001.05.3.b TXX 001.09 SAME EIRD 1.i Multiple i) Flow linearity: for cold flow without sample present, flow stream lines (from the centerline to 3/4 of the distance to the wall) shall be linear (distance from wall shall not change more than 10% throughout the test sections). Can be validated by smoke tracers (1-g). Ground based testing will verify the flow linearity. This is not expected to change significantly in μg. The diameter variations at duct transitions shall be minimized. TXXX 001.09.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a test structure and instrumentation to validate flow stream lines The flow stream linearity will be characterized using video data provided by a camera located outside of the sample tube. A clear acrylic tube will be used to view the smoke tracers to show the streamlines in the Smoke Generation Duct.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p18,3,"NVR 001.09.2 Electrical Engineering Provide power, data and control support for test structure and instrumentation to validate flow stream lines The flow stream linearity will be characterized using video data provided by a camera located outside of the sample tube. A clear acrylic tube will be used to view the smoke tracers to show the streamlines in the Smoke Generation Duct. NVR 001.09.3 Software Engineering Provide software to support test structure and instrumentation to validate flow stream lines See SAME-DOC-004, section 8.0 NVR 001.09.3.a SAME-DOC-004 8.0 Software Engineering Software will be provided to support test structures and instrumentation to validate flow stream lines. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-2] Development Software NVR A-3",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p19,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 001.10 SAME EIRD 1.j Title j) Sample Location: Sample shall be located so its long dimension is oriented perpendicular to the flow and the sample shall be greater than 5 (10 preferred) times its small dimension from the duct entrance. Samples shall be centered on the duct axis +/- 2mm. NVR 001.10.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide: 01. Sample heater coil support with sample's long dimension perpendicular to the flow 02. Sample heater coil support 5-10 times the sample's small dimension away from the duct entrance 03. Sample heater coil support centered on the duct axis +/- 2mm 1, 02. SAME Drawing #____________ 03. Centerline requirement will be verified (inspection) on ground prior to packing for launch. IXXX 001.11 SAME EIRD 1.k Title k) Fan or flow generator: Either end of the duct is acceptable, duct outlet preferred. NVR 001.11.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide Mechanism for inducing flow through the smoke generation duct at the outlet end of the duct. Flow is induced by increasing the volume of the sample side of the Aging Chamber, drawing the smoke from the sample, through the Smoke Generation Duct, and into the Aging Chamber.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p19,1,"SAME Drawing #____________ I X 001.12 SAME EIRD 1.l Title l) Surface coating: Where feasible, objects in the camera field of view should be blackened and roughened to reduce stray reflections. Acceptance will be based on testing with flight-like unit. With the illumination on, it should be possible to discriminate punk smoke from the wall reflections. NVR 001.12.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide rough, black surface on the interior of the Smoke Generation Duct within field of view The duct will be aluminum, and the blackening of the visible area will be accomplished by roughened black anodizing or diffused black paint. I X 001.12.4 Diagnostics Verify that the punk smoke is visible in the Smoke Generation Duct Ground test will verify smoke visibility. T X X 001.13 SAME EIRD 1.m Title m) Particle loss by impaction, diffusion, thermophoresis, or electrostatic attraction shall be minimized and characterized by test and/or analysis in 1-g. (<5% desired) NVR 001.13.1 Mechanical Engineering 01. Design all smoke flow ducts such that: Use conductive surfaces (electrostatic) where possible Minimize interior surface area (fusion) Gradual bends/turns (impaction) Minimize thermal gradients (thermophoresis) Minimize obstructions (impaction) 02. Perform particle loss analysis 1. SAME Drawing #____________ 2. Analysis will be coordinated with science.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p19,2,"A X 002 SAME EIRD 2 Title Diagnostics Duct (Includes Aging Chamber Outlet Elbow, Sampling Duct Assembly, and Detector Duct Assembly) NVR 002.01 SAME EIRD 2.a Title a) Unswept volume minimized (< 5% desired) NVR 002.01.1 Mechanical Engineering Transitions must be gradual to minimize flow separations SAME Drawing #____________ Science will review the design to determine that unswept volume is minimized. IXXX 002.02 SAME EIRD 2.b Title b) Transit time minimized (<60 s desired) and unaffected by changes in the flow rate in the smoke generation duct. The transit time is a function of the number of diagnostics operating and the tube lengths and diameters. NVR 002.02.1 Mechanical Engineering Transit time shall be minimized through selection and routing of the tubing that delivers the sample to the diagnostics. A X A-4",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p20,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 002.03 SAME EIRD 2.c Title c) Smoke leaving the aging chamber shall be reliably sampled (< 10% error for each size range) by the diagnostics for the expected particle size ranges (0.02 to 1.0 µm, 1.0 to 2.5 µm, 2.5 to 6.0 µm) with total particle sampling error minimized and characterized by 1-g testing or analysis. NVR 002.03.1.a Mechanical Engineering Provide a ground test apparatus to test and characterize system Verification of this requirement will be testing or analysis performed by NIST. Inspection of their characterization will close the verification. IXXXXX 002.03.1.b Mechanical Engineering Provide an analysis to characterize the transit losses A X 002.04 SAME EIRD 2.d Multiple d) Temporal delay from the smoke source shall be well characterized. Required accuracy of temporal delay calibration: +/- 5 seconds in residence time for all duct flow rates. (Temporal delay includes transit time from the Smoke Aging Chamber to the diagnostics sampling point, transit time in the sampling tube and instrument response time) The temporal delay will be characterized using ground based testing to determine the time from the gate valve opening to the instrument response.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p20,1,"TXX 002.04.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide a test structure and instrumentation for characterizing temporal delays in ground based testing NVR 002.04.2 Electrical Engineering Provide power, data and control support for test structure and instrumentation to characterize temporal delays NVR 002.04.3 Software Engineering Provide software to support test structure and instrumentation to characterize temporal delays See SAME-DOC-004, section 8.0 NVR 002.04.3.a SAME-DOC-004 8.0 Software Engineering Software will be provided to support test structures and instrumentation to characterize temporal delays of the smoke. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-4] Development Software NVR 002.05 SAME EIRD 2.e Title e) Volume sufficient to house the inlets to the diagnostics NVR 002.05.1 Mechanical Engineering Provide diagnostic ducting that is large enough to provide inlets to all diagnostic equipment listed in requirements 12 through 16 SAME Drawing #____________ I X 002.06 SAME EIRD 2.f Title f) Particle loss by impaction, diffusion, thermophoresis or electrostatic attraction shall be minimized and characterized by test and/or analysis in 1-g. (< 5% desired). NVR 002.06.1 Mechanical Engineering 01. Design all smoke flow ducts such that: Use conductive surfaces (electrostatic) where possible Minimize interior surface area (fusion) Gradual bends/turns (impaction) Minimize thermal gradients (thermophoresis) Minimize obstructions (impaction) 02. Perform particle loss analysis 1. SAME Drawing #____________ 2.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p20,2,"Analysis will be coordinated with science. A X 002.07 SAME EIRD 2.g Title g) Dilution air can be added as long as the smoke concentration is maintained in the operating range of the diagnostics. If dilution is used, either the dilution level must be known +/- 10% or the concentration entering the Smoke Aging Chamber must be known. NVR 002.07.1 Mechanical Engineering The positive displacement system of the aging chamber will allow for concentration of smoke to be known. A X 002.08 SAME EIRD 2.h Title h) The concentration seen by each of the diagnostics must be the same or at a known dilution level (consistent with achieving required instrument accuracy). Current design should support this requirement. NVR A-5",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p21,0,"SAME-PLN-005 Rev F F.M. Flt. Full-up System Sub-System Flight Drawings Outside Source Documentation/ Procedures Requirement Number Requirement Source Discipline Requirement System Response & Notes Method Safety HFITSeries In MSG EU Documents VDS # Hardware System GSE Required 002.08.1 Mechanical Engineering Sample inlet locations for all diagnostic instruments will be on the same length of duct. Dilution will be provided to deliver smoke concentrations in the operating ranges of the diagnostics including: 01. Smoke and GN2 to dynamic diluter 02. Laminar flow element and voltage sensitive orifices to the smoke and GN2 inlets 03. Control and measurement instrumentation 04. Smoke and GN2 to static diluter Dynamic diluter features include: 01. Active dilution, constant draw from diagnostics duct while active with a dilution ratio variable from 100:1 to 10:1 02. Smoke volume accurately measured through a laminar flow element 03. Control loop provides self-adjusting flow to compensate for changes in the system Static diluter features include: 04. Static dilution ratio on the order of 4:1 that can be turned on or off T & I X X X 002.08.2 Electrical Engineering 01. Power for the Voltage Sensitive Orifice(s) (VSO) and the control/measurement instrumentation 02. Analog inputs/outputs for the VSO control and measurement instrumentation (including feedback from the Laminar Flow Element) 03.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document (VPRED)_January 2007.pdf,p21,1,"Discrete outputs for the VSO control and measurement instrumentation TX X X 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system. See SAME-DOC-004, sections 4.5.1 and 5.11 NVR 002.08.3 Software Engineering Software sequencing for the diagnostic sampling must be such that the samples are taken at approximately the same time, once the diagnostics duct is filled from the aging chamber. Software must also control the VSO and other control and measurement instrumentation in the dilution system.",,False
