PSI-102,Engineering Documents,"PSI-102_Engineering Documents_60089MFHP000-E SAME Fluids System Schematic, right side.pdf","data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_60089MFHP000-E SAME Fluids System Schematic, right side.pdf",p1,2,"### Balloon legend | PRESSURE | GAS | |---|---| | P - POSITIVE | N - NITROGEN | | V - VACUUM | M - MSG ENVELOPE | | B - BOTH (P OR V) | R - N AND/OR MSG ENV | | A - NEAR ATM | T - MSG ENV. AND/OR SMOKE | | | U - ANY | ### Dynamic Diagnostics Assembly (see 60089MDBA165 for detail) - AT05 inlet ""FROM MSG WORK VOLUME"" -> ISS SMOKE DETECTOR -> STS SMOKE DETECTOR -> AT06 filter (F) -> TO MSG RECIRCULATION FANS - AT13 SAMPLE OULET QD (as written) between the detectors ### Dynamic Diluter (see 60089MFHA180 for detail) - AT03 (SAMPLE QD2) -> AT07 -> LFE with AT08 PT across it -> AU01 STATIC MIXER -> AU02 SAMPLE QD1 - PN20 GN2 QD -> PN23 VSO ### P-Trak enclosure (see 60089MFHA500 for detail) - AU03 (SAMPLE QD) -> OPTICS CHAMBER -> AU05 filter -> VR07 orifice -> VR05 VACUUM QD; bypass AU06 filter -> VR08 orifice ### Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure (A) (see 60089MFHA600 for detail) - SAMPLE INLET 1 (AU07) -> AU08 filter -> OPTICS CHAMBER -> AU09 filter -> VR15 orifice -> VR16 VACUUM QD - SAMPLE INLET 2 (AT04) -> AT09 ION -> AT10 filter -> VM04 orifice ### Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure (B) (see 60089MFHA600 for detail) - SAMPLE INLET 1 (AU10) -> AU11 filter -> OPTICS CHAMBER -> AU12 filter -> VR17 orifice -> VR18 VACUUM QD - SAMPLE INLET 2 (AT17) -> AT18 ION -> AT19 filter -> VM13 orifice ### Thermal Precipitator Module (TPM) (see 60089MFHA300 for detail) - SAMPLE INLET 1 (AT14) and SAMPLE INLET 2 (AT15), each to 6 solenoid-valved sample lines (12 total) -> VM08 VACUUM QD",,True
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,"PSI-102_Engineering Documents_60089MFHP000-E SAME Fluids System Schematic, right side.pdf","data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_60089MFHP000-E SAME Fluids System Schematic, right side.pdf",p1,3,"### Legends (bottom edge) Instrument letter designations (partly cut off at left): first letter B burner, E emergency, F flow, H hand, L level, P pressure, S speed, T temperature, Z position, J power, U surge; succeeding letter A alarm, C control, D differential, H high, I indicate, L low, P permissive, R record, S shutdown, Y datalog. Instrument symbols: general; prgm logic controller; data collector facility; prgm process controller; fluids system labels.",,True
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,"PSI-102_Engineering Documents_Fluids Control Unit, Schematic, left side.pdf","data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_Fluids Control Unit, Schematic, left side.pdf",p1,0,"Description: Scanned left half of the SAME fluids system schematic (drawing 60089MFHP000, rev E per the matching right half): the Fluids Control Unit (nitrogen supply and vacuum manifolds), the aging chamber smoke piston, and the piping, actuator, control element and item designation symbol legends along the bottom. No title block on this half. Some lines in the vacuum section are drawn as bold dashed lines (see general note 05 on the right half: components designated with bold dashed lines have a maximum design pressure of 19.7 psia).",,True
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,"PSI-102_Engineering Documents_Fluids Control Unit, Schematic, left side.pdf","data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_Fluids Control Unit, Schematic, left side.pdf",p1,1,"### Fluids Control Unit (see 60089MFHA220 for detail) Nitrogen supply path (heavy line, 1/8"" OD SS): - MSG GN2 QD -> PN08 / PN01 quick disconnect (PN10, PN09 quick disconnect with shut-off and cap outside the unit) -> PN05 filter (F) -> PN04 ""RELIEF VALVE 1, 220 PSIG WITH MANUAL OVERRIDE"" -> PN02 PT -> PN03 solenoid valve -> PN06 pressure regulator, 15 PSIG - Downstream of PN06 (1/8"" ID TYGON): PN12 PT; PN13, PN14, PN16 relief valves, each 20 PSIG; solenoid valves PN07, PN24 (-> PN25 orifice), PN15, PN27 (-> PN28 orifice) - PN07 feeds solenoid valves BN08 and BN09 (bold dashed line from BN08/BN09 down to the vacuum section) - Mass flow controller BN19 between solenoid valves BN18 and BN17; BN20 solenoid valve to VENT; BN02 PT; BN16 solenoid valve - Lines leave through quick disconnects: BN03 VACUUM/GN2 QD1; BN23 VACUUM/GN2 QD2 Vacuum section (bold dashed lines): - VM06 solenoid valve -> VM05 orifice -> VM07 orifice -> AT16 filter (F) -> quick disconnect - VR12, VR11, VR06 solenoid valves to quick disconnects; VR04 PT - VM09 solenoid valve -> VM10 orifice -> AT20 PT -> AT11 filter (F) -> quick disconnect - VR02 ""RELIEF VALVE 2, 5 PSIG WITH MANUAL OVERRIDE"" - VR20 relief valve, 5 PSIG - 1/8"" ID TYGON -> VR01 ""MSG VACUUM QUICK DISCONNECT""; VR09 check valve; VR19 MSG VACUUM QD; VR13, VR14 quick disconnect; ""MSG SHUT-OFF VALVE"" - VR10 VACUUM QD1",,True
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,"PSI-102_Engineering Documents_Fluids Control Unit, Schematic, left side.pdf","data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_Fluids Control Unit, Schematic, left side.pdf",p1,2,"### Aging chamber (see 60089MFHA150 for detail) - SMOKE PISTON: SAMPLE SIDE (top) / DRIVE/VAC SIDE (bottom) - Sample side valves with actuators BN21 and BN22; line ""FROM MSG WORK VOLUME"" - BN25 VACUUM/GN2 QD1 on the drive/vac side; BN23 VACUUM/GN2 QD2 ### Symbol legends (bottom edge) Piping component symbols: butterfly valve; check valve; gate valve; relief valve; ball valve; voltage sensitive orifice; directional valves 2-way, 3-way, 4-way; pressure regulator; loader; cylinder regulator; turb flowmeter; orifice; venturi; rupture disk; quick disconnect; press gauge; filter; strainer; heat exch; pump; motor; quick disconnect with shut-off. Actuator symbols: cylinder; hand; pneu control; rotary motor; solenoid. Primary control element symbols: EP elec-pneu xdcr; EH elec-hydr xdcr; LLS liq level switch; LLT liq level xdcr; LS limit switch; PDS press diff switch; PDT press diff xdcr; PS pressure switch; PT pressure xdcr; TC thermocouple; TS temp switch. Item designations: locally mounted; control room mounted; altn control room mounted; ref drawing. Instrument letter designations table cut off at the right edge [illegible].",,True
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME_IgnitorWireCharacterizationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME_IgnitorWireCharacterizationReport.pdf,p1,0,"Smoke Experiment aka Characterization of Smoke Particulate for Spacecraft Fire Detection Engineering Report Characterization of Ignitor Wire Candidates Submitted by: ZIN Technologies, Inc Len Miller April 2002",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME_IgnitorWireCharacterizationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME_IgnitorWireCharacterizationReport.pdf,p2,0,"SMOKE Experiment Ignitor WIre Characterization Study April 2002 Purpose: The purpose of this report is to document the results of a study conducted in search of the ideal type of wire to be used for the hot wire ignitors for the Smoke Experiment. Objective: The objective of this study was to find a wire with a consistent, linear resistance versus temperature characteristic and with a “significant” slope. Scope: This study was conducted in the Phase A Laboratory (Bldg 77, Room 154A) on commercially available wire. The ignitor manufacturing technique and the pyrolization of samples is beyond the scope of this study. Procedure: Each type of wire was mounted on a test fixture. The test fixture was then placed into a ThermCraft laboratory oven. The oven was programmed to ramp up to a set point, hold the set point for fifteen minutes, and then ramp to the next set point until the final temperature of approximately 800 oC was attained. (See oven cycle graph in Appendix C.) Resistance readings were taken near the end of each “dwell” period. Several of the wire types were subjected to additional tests to examine the effects of the previous thermal cycle(s). The test fixture held approximately thirty-four inches (0.86 meters) of wire. A stainless steel sheathed type-k thermocouple (Omega Part No. KQSS-116U-24) was placed inside the oven in close proximity to the wire under test.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME_IgnitorWireCharacterizationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME_IgnitorWireCharacterizationReport.pdf,p2,1,Leads of high temperature furnace wire (#18 AWG) were brought out of the oven to enable the test conductor to measure the resistance of the wire under test. The resistance of the leads and the terminations was characterized by subjection the fixture and leads to a thermal cycle with a copper bus bar shorting the terminals of the fixture. The fixture resistance was subtracted from the total resistance reading for each test point to determine the net resistance of the wire under test. The results were recorded in an Excel Workbook. Several different data plots were prepared and studied.,,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME_IgnitorWireCharacterizationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME_IgnitorWireCharacterizationReport.pdf,p3,0,"SMOKE Experiment Ignitor WIre Characterization Study April 2002 Summary and Conclusions: This study examined the characteristics of eight types of wire: anodized mild steel, pure iron, Alomega (similar to Alumel), -Stainless steel, pure platinum, platinum-rhodium, tungster-5%rhenium, and tungsten-26%rhenium. Traditional heating wires, Nichrome and Kanthal were also included for comparison. Pure platinum has close-to-ideal characteristics. However the cost of the material may be prohibitive. Alomega and stainless steel ran a close second. Mild steel, iron and tungsten-composites oxidized in the oven and their characteristics changed significantly from one thermal cycle to the next. Nichrome and Kanthal have too flat a slope to meet the objectives. Further testing will be conducted to determine the optimum wire type and wire diameter. Representative test samples will by pyrolized using various ignitor configurations. The quality and quantity of the smoke will be visually evaluated. Test Results: The tests were conducted in the Phase A Lab from February 6, 2002 through March 20 2002, by Len Miller and Nathan Funk of Zin Technologies, Inc. The individual results for each test were tabulated and plotted. For those wires subjected to more than one thermal cycle, the comparative results were plotted. Finally, an overall plot of the combined results was prepared. The entire Excel Workbook, with tabulated test results and plots thereof, is included in Appendix A of this report.",,False
PSI-102,Engineering Documents,PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME_IgnitorWireCharacterizationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Engineering Documents/PSI-102_Engineering Documents_SAME_IgnitorWireCharacterizationReport.pdf,p3,1,Photographs of the laboratory apparatus are in Appendix B and a graph of the thermal cycle is in Appendix C.,,False
