PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p5,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 1 1.0 INTRODUCTION Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment (SAME) is an experiment payload planned for the Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) located on the International Space Station (ISS). The Crew Display (a.k.a. Human-Computer Interface or HCI) will operate on the MSG Laptop Computer MLC. It provides the crew with information about the current status of the experiment and an ability to issue commands to control the system. The crew interface for SAME is intended to be used only in off-nominal conditions. The nominal operation of the experiment will occur via ground commanding. 1.1 Purpose The purpose of this document is to describe the SAME Crew Display and to: 1) Provide details about the implementation of the SAME Crew Display requirements found in SAME-DOC- 004, Section 7.0 2) Satisfy the Preliminary and Candidate Display Deliveries as required for the Payload Display Review Team (PDRT) 1.2 Scope This document is limited to the operation of the SAME Crew Display. Issues concerning general operation of the experiment via the software operating on the DACU or ground systems are not discussed here.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p6,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 2 2.0 CREW INTERFACE 2.1 Purpose The purpose of the crew interface on SAME is to provide some basic information about the status of the experiment during off-nominal conditions, especially in case of loss of data telemetry and/or commanding from and to the ground. This information is minimal in nature. It represents the basic information that a crew member may be asked to report and commands they are asked to issue during off-nominal conditions. Section 3.0 provides flow diagrams showing the typical use of the crew interface. In summary, those uses are: • Commanding and Telemetry Check. Verification of SAME data transmittal and command reception. • Sample Positioning Check. Used to debug unexpected sample positioning behavior that could use a crew member’s eye. • Gate Valve Check. Used to debug unexpected gate valve behavior. • P-Trak Configure. Execute specified commands and verify specified results on the P-Trak to achieve reconfiguration and/or debugging. • Commercial Diagnostics Configure. Execute specified commands and verify specified results on the Commercial Diagnostics unit to achieve reconfiguration and/or debugging. • Attended Operations. Ability to power-up the SAME DACU, initialize the instrument, run a specified test, and shut down.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p7,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 3 2.2 Description The basic layout of the Crew Display is dictated by the requirements found in the Display and Graphics Commonality Standard (DGCS) (SSP 50313 Rev B). The standard describes the look-and-feel and deals with items such as colors, button type and placement, and major layout elements. Figure 1 below identifies the major layout elements. Figure 1- Display Components 2.2.1 Primary Button Panel These buttons represent the major functions and operations of the interface. Button Description Status The Status summary display is shown in the workspace. See 2.2.5.2 Operations The Test display is shown in the workspace. See 2.2.5.3 Commercial Diagnostics The Commercial Diagnostics display is shown in the workspace. See 2.2.5.4 P-Trak The P-Trak display is shown in the workspace. See 2.2.5.5 Log Displays the message log dialog panel. See 2.2.6 Help See 2.2.7 Exit Terminates the crew interface program. See 2.2.8 Table 1 - Primary Buttons 2.2.2 Menu Bar As required by SSP50313 the menu bar replicates the functionality of buttons and commands that appear in other parts of the interface. Workspac e Status Bar Payload Status Area Primary Button Panel Menu Bar",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p8,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 4 2.2.3 Payload Status Area This is an area reserved in the DGCS for payloads to present critical information that is shown at all times. SAME will use this area to display information about the experiment and testing status. The Run Mode field displays the current running status of the SAME experiment. Possible values are: STARTUP The DACU software is starting UNINITIALIZED The DACU software is waiting for the System Initialize command STANDBY The DACU software has been successfully initialized and is waiting for commands and is emitting housekeeping data. TEST RUNNING A test is currently in progress TEST PAUSED A test is paused SHUTDOWN The DACU software is in the process of shutting down. Table 2 - Run Mode Values The OK Status indicator replicates the physical LED labeled OK on the DACU housing. It represents the physical status of the experiment. When the indicator is lit the system is operational (e,g, powered, booted, ready to run); unlit indicates the system is not operational (e.g. not powered, in error). The Test Number field displays the current test number the DACU is running or prepared to run. The Elapsed Time field displays how long the current test has been running (HH:MM:SS). If no test is running the field will be blank The GPS field is required by SSP50313. It provides a time reference for payload operations. 2.2.4 Status Bar",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p8,1,"The latest information message received from the DACU will be displayed here. In addition, it is added to the general message log (see 2.2.6). The message is specific to SAME and is composed of four comma-separated parts. The first part is the GPS time the message was generated on the DACU. Next is the level of the message which is either INFO or ERROR. This is followed by the message text and optional detail text. The last two fields make up the Description field in the general message log. Timestamp Message Text Details Level Description",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p9,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 5 2.2.5 Workspace The workspace area is used for displaying information and controls for a given function. The contents will change based on the primary function selected. 2.2.5.1 Welcome Screen The welcome screen is the first thing displayed when the crew display is started. There is no real content display, but it serves the purpose of positively identifying the SAME experiment. Once the operator selects a function from the primary button panel, the workspace will change to the new display. There is no way of displaying the welcome screen again without exiting and restarting the program. Figure 2 - Welcome Screen",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p10,0,SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 6 2.2.5.2 Status Screen The summary status display is shown when the operator presses the Status button. It shows information about the state of SAME in addition to what is shown in the Payload Status Area (see 2.2.3). Figure 3 - Status Screen,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p10,1,"2.2.5.2.1 Sample Positioning Group The sample group consists of 3 fields. The Sample Number field is the number of the sample within the carousel being tested at that time. The Carousel Number field is the number of the carousel that is installed. A carousel holds six samples. The combination of the carousel and sample number uniquely identifies a particular test item. The Resistance field displays the measured resistance of the heater wire coil for the sample. This is used as a unique physical indicator of which sample is being tested. 2.2.5.2.2 SAME Power Group MSG provides SAME with 120VDC. SAME converts this into 5 separate voltages using DC/DC converters. The SAME Power group consists of fields indicating the state of the 5 voltages. For all voltages, the possible state include ON and OFF. In addtion the 12 VDC Heater, 12 VDC Misc, and 5 VDC can have a state of Enabled, which means that it was turned on, but has not yet been sensed as on. 2.2.5.2.3 Ethernet Switch Group The MLC Ethernet connection is routed through a switch inside SAME. The switch connects the MLC to either the ISS LAN Backbone or to the SAME DACU. The purpose of this is to allow the DACU to transfer data files to the MLC via FTP. The switch is nominally controlled by the DACU; however the crew can manually activate the switch if necessary. When the DACU is unpowered, the switch connects the MLC to the ISS LAN. The Ethernet Switch group reports the state of the switch. There are three possible values:",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p11,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 7 MLC to SAME The MLC network connection is connected to the SAME DACU (for the purpose of transferring files) MLC to LAN The MLC network connection is connected to the ISS LAN The above two states are achieved electronically by DACU commands Manual: MLC to SAME The manual switch has been used to force the MLC network connection to the SAME DACU. Table 3 - Ethernet Switch Position Values 2.2.5.2.4 Gate Valves Group The Gate Valves group displays the status of two valves; the Inlet and Outlet valves. These can either be OPEN or CLOSED. 2.2.5.2.5 Supply Group SAME uses the GN2 and vacuum resources supplied by MSG. SAME regulates the GN2 supply as needed. The Supply Group displays the status of these gas supplies and the vacuum state. The Main GN2 and Main Vacuum resources both have valves that are controlled by the DACU. The valve indicator fields show the state of these valves; OPEN or CLOSED. The pressure of the main GN2 supply, the vacuum, and the regulated GN2 are reported. 2.2.5.3 Operations The Operations display allows for sending commands that affect the operation of the SAME experiment. There are two groups of these commands. Figure 4 - Operations Screen 2.2.5.3.6 System Group These commands affect the entire SAME experiment.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p12,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 8 Button Name Description Check Communications Causes an innoculous command to be sent to the DACU to verify the ability of the interface to communicate with the DACU in the crew interface-to-DACU direction. If the message is successfully transmitted and received by the DACU, the DACU will send a reply message which will appear in the status bar. Communication in the DACU-to-crew interface direction is checked by verifying that the GPS is updating correctly. Initialize Causes a System Initialization command to be sent to the DACU causing it to initialize the hardware component and prepare for test operations. Successful execution of this command will cause the Run Mode to change from UNINITIALIZED to STANDBY. Shutdown… Causes a System Shutdown command to be sent to the DACU causing it to perform an orderly shutdown. The Run Mode will change to SHUTDOWN just prior to actual shutdown. This action must be confirmed using the following dialog box: Table 4 - SAME System Command Buttons",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p12,1,"2.2.5.3.7 Test Group These commands affect a SAME test. Button/Field Name Description Test Number This is a field where the user enters the desired test number to be used during execution of the Start… command. Start… Causes the DACU to start the test specified by the Test Number field. The response will be seen by a change of value in the Test Number field in the Payload Status Area. Also, the Run Mode field will change to TEST RUNNING. This action must be confirmed using the following dialog: Pause Causes the DACU to suspend the current test. The Run Mode will change to TEST PAUSED. Resume Causes the DACU to resume a paused test. The Run Mode field will change to TEST RUNNING. Abort… Causes the DACU to abort a test that has been started. This action must be confirmed using the following dialog box: Table 5 - SAME Test Commands",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p13,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 9 2.2.5.4 Commercial Diagnostics This display is shown when the operator clicks the Commercial Diagnostics button on the primary button panel. It provides a set of commands to control the Commercial Diagnostics apparatus. Figure 5 – Commerical Diagnostics Screen The Commercial Diagnostics unit contains a COTS instrument called a DustTrak. The DustTrak has a keypad and LCD as its primary user interface. For SAME, the keypad has been removed and replaced with an electronic interface that is controlled by the DACU. The LCD has been retained and is located on the Commercial Diagnostics package and serves as the mechanism to view the status of the unit. The picture at left shows the COTS packaging, not as repackaged for SAME. The interface display replicates the functions that would normally be found on the keypad. When a button is clicked on the display, a command is sent to the DACU which in turn sends a signal to the DustTrak unit. The unit will act on the signal and the response will be viewed on the LCD. There is no feedback to the crew interface itself. Button Name Description Power On Turns the unit on. Power Off Turns the unit off. Increment Sampling Mode Each click of this button causes the next in a series of 4 modes to be displayed on the LCD. The modes are Survey, Log1, Log2, and Log3.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p14,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 10 Button Name Description Start/Stop Sampling If the unit is sampling, clicking the button causes the unit to stop sampling. Otherwise, clicking the button will cause the unit to start sampling. View Statistics Each click of the button will cause the next in a series of 4 values to be displayed on the LCD. The values are the average, minimum, maximum readings, and the elapsed time of the most recently sampled data. Up Down These two buttons are used to changed values displayed on the LCD during calibration. Calibrate Causes the unit to enter its calibration mode. Clear Memory This will erase all data stored in the unit’s memory. This action must be confirmed using the following dialog box: View Log Interval View the current frequency/averaging period for recording data in LOG modes. Increment Log Interval Effectively presses and holds the View Log Interval key so that it increments to the next available Log Interval setting. View Time Constant View the current time-constant on the LCD. Increment Time Constant Effectively presses and holds the View Time Constant key so that it increments to the next available Time Constant setting. Table 6 - DustTrak Buttons",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p15,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 11 2.2.5.5 P-Trak This display is shown when the operator clicks the P-Trak button on the primary button panel. It provides a set of commands to control the P-Trak apparatus. Figure 6 – P-Trak Screen The P-Trak unit is a COTS item. It has a keypad and LCD as its primary user interface. For SAME, the keypad has been removed and replaced with an electronic interface that is controlled by the DACU. The LCD has been retained and is located on the SAME experiment package and serves as the mechanism to view the status of the unit. The picture at left shows the COTS packaging, not as repackaged for SAME. The interface display replicates the functions that would normally be found on the keypad. When a button is clicked on the display, a command is sent to the DACU which in turn sends a signal to the P-Trak unit. The unit will act on the signal and the response will be viewed on the LCD. There is no feedback to the crew interface itself. Button Name Description Power On Turns the unit on. Power Off Turns the unit off. Up, Down Left, Right These four buttons are used to changed values displayed on the LCD when such behavior is allowed. Enter Used with the arrow keys to navigate the LCD and change values when such behavior is allowed. View Setup Effectively presses the left and right buttons simultaneously causing the P-Trak to display setup information on the LCD. Table 7 – P-Trak Buttons",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p16,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 12 2.2.6 Log The message log lists all the messages that have been received or generated by the interface. Messages originate from two sources: the DACU and the interface itself. The latest DACU message also appears on the Status Bar (see 2.2.4). By default messages are sorted by GPS with the latest messages at the top. The window may be scrolled to see earlier messages. Sorting order and column can be changed by selecting the column heading. Sub-sorts are achieved by holding <CTRL> while selecting two or more headings. An arrow in the heading indicates whether sorting is by decreasing (down arrow) or increasing (up arrow) order. Messages appear in the list with three pieces of information. First is the GPS timestamp of the message. Second is a Level indicator. Messages are assigned one of the following levels: INFO, ERROR, or CONFIG. The final piece of information is the message description itself. Messages that originated from the DACU are always assigned an INFO or ERROR level. The DACU message is then summarized in a single text string with all the individual message fields identified. Messages originating from the interface can be CONFIG or ERROR level. Figure 7 - Log Window 2.2.7 Help Selecting Help opens the Help window containing information similar to this document. Figure 8 - Help Window",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p17,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 13 2.2.8 Exit In order to stop the crew interface program, select the Exit button on the Primary Button Panel. The following confirmation dialog panel will be displayed. Figure 9 - Exit Confirmation",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p18,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 14 3.0 FLOW DIAGRAMS Commanding and Telemetry Check Start SAME Crew Display Power On SAME no √ POIC Operations: Select Check Communications Wait 3 minutes Select Log Confirm: Select Yes no Operations: Select Shutdown Log: Select OK Wait 3 minutes Power Off SAME Select Exit Confirm: Select Yes √ POIC Confirm: Select Yes in out Crew operations, SAME crew display Crew operations, other Ground operations GPS Updating ? Check Communication Success message ?",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p19,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 15 Sample Positioning Check Start SAME Crew Display Report results to POIC Select Exit SAME is powered & initialized from ground no √ POIC Status: Verify that the sample number displayed matches the actual position and that sample resistance is good when carousel is engaged. Sample carousel is manipulated from the ground Confirm: Select Yes in out Crew operations, SAME crew display Crew operations, other Ground operations GPS Updating ?",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p20,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 16 Gate Valve Check Type Start SAME Crew Display SAME is powered & initialized from ground Position Commerical Diagnostics unit to view LCD. Priority no √ POIC Select Exit Gate valves are manipulated from the ground Status: Verify actual gate valve position(s) match those on crew display Report result to POIC Confirm: Select Yes in out Crew operations, SAME crew display Crew operations, other Ground operations GPS Updating ?",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p21,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 17 P-Trak Configure Start SAME Crew Display Report results to POIC Select Exit SAME is powered & initialized from ground P-Trak: select buttons as specified by execution notes. Observe LCD on actual P-Trak unit. no √ POIC Confirm: Select Yes in out Crew operations, SAME crew display Crew operations, other Ground operations GPS Updating ? Position P-Trak to view LCD.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p22,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 18 Commercial Diagnostics Configure Start SAME Crew Display Report LCD results to POIC Select Exit SAME is powered & initialized from ground Commercial Diagnostics: select buttons as specified by execution notes. Observe LCD on actual Commercial Diagnostics unit. Position Commerical Diagnostics unit to view LCD. no √ POIC Commercial Diagnostics: Select Unit A or B as directed Confirm: Select Yes in out Crew operations, SAME crew display Crew operations, other Ground operations GPS Updating ?",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p23,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 19 Attended Operations Power On SAME Start Crew Display no Operations: Select Start Operations: Select Initialize Operations: Enter Test Number √ POIC Wait 1 minute no √ POIC no √ POIC A Status: check some values (TBD) no √ POIC in Crew operations, SAME crew display Crew operations, other Ground operations GPS Updating ? Run Mode = STANDBY ? Run Mode = TEST RUNNING ? Good Values ? Confirm: Select Yes",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Crew Interface Description.pdf,p24,0,"SAME-DOC-008 Rev A 20 Attended Operations (continued) A Select Exit Wait for Run Mode = STANDBY (20-50 min) Operations: Select Resume no √ POIC Power OFF SAME Wait 2 min Operations: Select Shutdown Confirm: Select Yes out Crew operations, SAME crew display Crew operations, other Ground operations no √ POIC Wait 3 minutes no √ POIC Status: Sample # matches actual ? Run Mode = TEST RUNNING ? Run Mode = TEST PAUSED ? Check Every 10 min Confirm: Select Yes",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p1,0,"SAME-DOC-007 Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment (SAME) Operations Manual Prepared For: National Aeronautics and Space Administration John H. Glenn Research Center at Lewis Field Exploration Systems [address removed] Prepared By: ZIN Technologies, Incorporated [address removed] Under Contract NAS3-99155, 60089-DO-084 January, 2007",,False
