PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p4,2,................................................................... 8 6.1.2 Using SAME processor command line to transfer files........................................................ 8 6.2 Converting Housekeeping file to engineering units...................................................................... 8 7.0 MISCELLANEOUS PROCEDURES AND INFORMATION................................................... 9 7.1 Obtaining a directory listing ......................................................................................................... 9 7.2 Replacing the registry file............................................................................................................. 9 7.3 Piston Calibration.......................................................................................................................... 9 7.3.1 Inlet Velocity ........................................................................................................................ 9 7.3.2 Sample Heating Start Time ................................................................................................. 10 7.3.3 Secondary Aging Volume................................................................................................... 11 7.3.4 Exhaust Rate .......................................................................................................................,,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p4,3,11 7.4 Changing flight software during flight........................................................................................ 11 7.5 Description of HeaterCorrectOffset Utility................................................................................. 12 7.6 Transfer and Installation of Crew Interface application.............................................................. 13 7.7 Use of tar utility .......................................................................................................................... 13 8.0 KNOWN ISSUES.......................................................................................................................... 14,,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p5,0,SAME-DOC-007 v 9.0 ACRONYMNS AND ABBREVIATIONS .................................................................................. 15 10.0 APPENDIX A – COMMERCIAL DIAGNOSTICS OPERATION......................................... 16 11.0 APPENDIX B – P-TRAK OPERATION.................................................................................... 23,,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p6,0,"SAME-DOC-007 1 1.0 INTRODUCTION 1.1 Purpose This document will eventually hold most of the info rmation needed to operate the SAME experiment on ISS from the ground from the TSC. It will also descri be alternate methods of performing tasks when the experiment hardware is not on ISS, but is instead easily accessible. For now, however, this document is a place to collect miscellaneous operational information and methods that need to be documented and under configuration control. 1.2 Scope TBD. 1.3 Applicable Documents [1] SAME-DOC-005 Software Interface Control Document",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p7,0,"SAME-DOC-007 2 2.0 IN-FLIGHT FUNCTIONAL CHECKLIST This is a list of items that should be addressed after the hardware is installed in the MSG and before any samples are tested. Not currently in any order. - Perform piston calibration and alter registry values as necessary. See procedure later in this document. Run a test with heater setpoint below R0 and no use of the thermal precipitors in order to confirm correct calibration. - Figure out good values for STSPumpFailureLowLimit and STSPumpFailureHighLimit registry values. These depend somewhat on atmospheric pressure and should be adjusted for the ISS environment. Correctly set, these values help detect failure of the STS pump. - Log some data with the P-Trak and DustTrak and confirm that their settings are correct. For example: log interval, time constant, and date/time. - Record some video, downlink, and examine. - Experimentally determine the maximum setpoints that the mass flow controller will meet given ISS vacuum and GN2 sources. Determine if any desired inlet velocities or diagnostic combinations are affected. - The registry value, DisableChecksumCheck, can be set to 1 to avoid the startup delay associated with calculating the checksum each time. - Revisit the decision about whether the heater controller offset correction will be done automatically as part of the test sequence or manually using the HeaterCorrectOffset utility prior to the test sequence. Adjust the CorrectOffset registry value appropriately.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p8,0,SAME-DOC-007 3 3.0 STARTING THE SOFTWARE 3.1 Ground Computer On the ground computer you’ll need to first log on. Then start TReK with SAME configuration files. Also start SAMEDisplay. More TBD. 3.2 SAME Flight Software The software that controls the system starts automa tically upon power on. Within 30 seconds of turning on 120V power to the system there should be telemetr y data received at the SAMEDisplay application. The GMT will be updating and startup messages will appear in the log window. The Run Mode will change from Startup to Unitialized.,,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p9,0,"SAME-DOC-007 4 4.0 TEST CHECKLIST This is a list of important things to do or confirm pr ior to, during, and after the running of a sample test. Placement of a highly visible printout of this lis t on console is recommended. Steps depend on whether sample heater offset correction is being done automatically or manually. Check the CorrectOffset registry value to find out (0=manual, >0=automatic). - Once per carousel open the gate valves and turn on the diagnostics for TBD minutes. Downlink the data and verify correctness. The purpose is to collect baseline measurements of the MSG work volume required by the science team. 4.1 With Automatic Heater Offset Correction - Power on the system, wait for telemetry reception. - Send the Initialize command. - If the desired sample is not engaged, engage it using the Self Test command. - Turn on the 12VDC Heater Power. - Note the sample resistance and compare to ground measured resistance. If they differ significantly, TBD. Determine the setpoint. - Look at test parameters and change as necessary. Use manual positioning option, but carousel and sample number must be correct. - Start the test. - When the test pauses check the following: o A “sample type is confirmed” message appears in the log. o The offset correction returned a value less than 50uV in the log. o The sample resistance is still good. Within TBD % of before. o The desired diagnostics are powered and readings are nominal. o GN2 regulated pressure and vacuum pressure are good.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p9,1,"Range is TBD. - If the offset correction returned a value greater than 50uV, abort the test and restart. If the offset is still greater than 50uV, change the CorrectOffset registry value to 0 (manual) and use the manual heater offset correction checklist instead. - Resume the test. - Watch the log for details about when the sample heater will start. When it does, be ready to send an Abort command. - Watch the sample resistance and compare to the desired setpoint and MaxRt. The software will too, and will abort heating if the resistance/temperature gets too high. But watch anyway and send the Abort command in the highly unlikely event of too high resistance/temperature. - After sample aging, during exhaust, watch the output of the diagnostics for unexpected results. - After test is complete, power on any P-Trak or DustTrak that was used and retrieve the latest logged test ids. Turn back off. - As desired, force a transfer of the current housekeeping and log files to the MLC. Their transfer during power down has a slight chance of failing. Doing it here ensures success. - Perform any other desired OS or system maintenance, such as directory listings or self tests. - Disengage the sample. - Send the Shutdown command with Shutdown option and power off system, or proceed to next test.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p10,0,"SAME-DOC-007 5 4.2 With Manual Heater Offset Correction - Power on the system, wait for telemetry reception. - If the desired sample is engaged, DO NOT send the Initialize command. - If the desired sample is not engaged, send Initialize, engage sample using the Self Test command, and send Shutdown command with Restart option to bring system back to uninitialized state. - Send the OSCommand command with parameter ‘/root/HeaterCorrectOffset_g’. If this command returns 0 in the log, then proceed, otherwise repeat until it does. (Look at the value returned, not the details message which always says the same thing.) See section 7.5 for details about the HeaterCorrectOffset utility. - Send the Initialize command. - Turn on the 12VDC Heater Power. - Note the sample resistance and compare to ground measured resistance. If they differ significantly, TBD. Determine the setpoint. - Look at test parameters and change as necessary. Use manual positioning option, but carousel and sample number must be correct. - Start the test. - When the test pauses check the following: o A “sample type is confirmed” message appears in the log. o The sample resistance is still good. Within TBD % of before. o The desired diagnostics are powered and readings are nominal. o GN2 regulated pressure and vacuum pressure are good. Range is TBD. - Resume the test. - Watch the log for details about when the sample heater will start. When it does, be ready to send an Abort command.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p10,1,"- Watch the sample resistance and compare to the desired setpoint and MaxRt. The software will too, and will abort heating if the resistance/temperature gets too high. But watch anyway and send the Abort command in the highly unlikely event of too high resistance/temperature. - After sample aging, during exhaust, watch the output of the diagnostics for unexpected results. - After test is complete, power on any P-Trak or DustTrak that was used and retrieve the latest logged test ids. Turn back off. - As desired, force a transfer of the current housekeeping and log files to the MLC. Their transfer during power down has a slight chance of failing. Doing it here ensures success. - Engage the next desired sample using the SelfTest command. Engaging the next sample here shortens the time for starting the next test. - Perform any other desired OS or system maintenance, such as directory listings or self tests. - Send the Shutdown command with Shutdown option and power off system, or proceed to next test.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p11,0,"SAME-DOC-007 6 5.0 RUNNING A TEST 5.1 Initialization Select the Initialize button on the System tab, sending the Initialize command, and wait until the Run Mode changes from Unitialized to Ready. 5.2 Setting parameters From the Testing tab, select Change… to change to the set of test parameters you want to look at. Create a new test by selecting the CreateNew button. Select Set anywhere in the window to change the parameter for the selected test. The Test Setup group box is the way to choose which di agnostics to use. Select the Pri. or Sec. radio button to see the difference in setup between primary and secondary aging. Heater Control Setpoint should be set after knowing the unheated resistance of a sample, a.k.a. R0. Nominal setpoints are a multiple, Rt/R0, of R0 depending on sample type: Sample Type Setpoint Multiplier, Rt/R0 DBP TBD Kapton 1.380 Lampwick 1.245 Silicone 1.327 Teflon 1.440",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p11,1,"To find the R0 of a sample, engage the sample to th e inlet tube either by manual blocking or using the SelfTest command with EngageSample option (on Syst em tab). Then on the Smoke Generation tab turn on the 12 VDC Heater Enable (right click on the ‘ LED’ and select ‘Set On’). Sample Resistance will settle to a valid measurement within 4 seconds. 5.3 Registry File Concerns Assuming you’ve set the test parameters to a specif ic carousel number, sample number, heating setpoint and duration, you may think you are ready to go. Y ou may not be. There are a bunch of sample-related limit checks that may cause an error or warning prior to heating. There are also many timing constants that affect the system behavior. These can be cha nged by selecting Set Registry… on the System tab. See SAME-DOC-005, Software Interface Control Document for key names and descriptions. A system timing simulator, currently located in SAMEFluidMode l.xls, will be available on console to help see the effect of registry value changes. Testing with the engineering unit is also recommended. Alternately, registry values can be changed by editing the registry.txt file on the DACU. This file is located in the /config directory. Also in that dir ectory is registry.orig.txt which contains the original registry settings. Important: Changes to registry values will not take e ffect until you restart the software. Follow the steps for shutting down, below, but use the restart parameter option.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p12,0,"SAME-DOC-007 7 5.4 Running the Test From the Testing tab select Start… and enter the test number to run, if it hasn’t already been entered in the field, and select Send. After sample positioning and aging chamber setup has occurred the test will pause so you can examine the log for any warnings or errors. Then send the Resume button. The test should proceed to finish. You can abort anytime by sending the Abort command. An abort causes all valves to be turned off as well as all diagnostics. 5.5 Shutdown It is recommended that the system be gracefully shut down using the Shutdown button on the System tab, then cut power about 2 minutes later. Current housek eeping and log files are transferred to the laptop during the first minute. Use the second minute to ensu re that the system did not restart, looking for the restart of low rate telemetry packets and updating GMT. If the system restarts, select Shutdown again or send the OS command with parameter, ‘/bin/shutdown –b’. 5.6 Self-Tests The flight software has a few operations available through the SelfTest command. These may be useful for other than self-test purposes, for instance to engage a sample to the inlet tube to check R0. Parameters depend on the self test:",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p12,1,"Self Test Parameter Definition Disengage Carousel no parameters Increment Carousel no parameters, rotates carousel one position Engage Carousel no parameters Dynamic Dilutor Parameter1: setpoint in sccm Parameter2: duration in seconds ISS Detector no parameters Fill Chamber Parameter1>0: Fill rate is this parameter divided by 10 in LPM Parameter1=0: Fill rate is ChamberSelfTestFillRate registry value Parameter2 not used. Exhaust Chamber Parameter1>0: Exhaust rate is this parameter divided by 10 in LPM Parameter1=0: Fill rate is ChamberSelfTestExhaustRate registry value Parameter2>0: Percentage to exhaust. Parameter2=0: Exhaust entire chamber. Sample Heater Parameter1>0: Duration in seconds Parameter1=0: Get previous run’s data and save to file. Do not heat sample. (Useful for aborted heating sequences.) Parameter2: not used. Setpoint ratio, Rt/R0, is SampleHeatingSelfTestRatio registry value. STS Detector no parameters Watchdog Timer no parameters. System will reset in 2 minutes.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p13,0,"SAME-DOC-007 8 6.0 PROCESSING FILES 6.1 Transferring files from DACU to the MLC All files will be automatically transferred from th e DACU to C:\ftp on the MSG laptop. Housekeeping files (.hkp) are transferred on the hour and upon shut down (with either the shutdown or restart option). The Log file is also transferred upon shutdown. P- Trak, DustTrak, and heater files are transferred soon after they are created. P-Trak and DustTrak file s are not automatically created since their data is embedded in the housekeeping data. To explicitly retrie ve logged data select the Transfer Data button on the DustTrak and P-Trak tabs. This should be do ne no more frequently than once per minute because the filename is derived from the GMT, including the latest minute, but not the latest second. If you need the data immediately or a file doesn’t get downlinked for some reason you’ll have to do it manually using one of two methods described here: 6.1.1 Using SAMEDisplay and FTP Cmd command. You’ll need to identify what file you need and the dir ectory it is in. Files are stored in a directory named according to GMT year and day. For instance /data/2006_132/. The .log file will be named 2006_132_00.log in this example. Housekeeping files will be 2006_132_15.hkp, 2006_132_16.hkp, etc. according to the GMT. With the flight software and SAMEDisplay running (does not need to be Initialized), select the FTP Cmd from the System tab. Type:",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p13,1,"LCD /data/2006_132, for example, to change local directory (on the DACU) and send the command. Then select FTP Cmd again a nd type: PUT 2006_132_16.hkp, for example, and send the command. Watch your letter case – it matters. 6.1.2 Using SAME processor command line to transfer files For this to work you’ll need a keyboard and mon itor connected to the GSE port on the SAME DACU. Also, verify that the switch on the SAME DACU is sw itched to Override position. This is obviously a ground use only method. From the command line on the GSE monitor (if the so ftware is running you can get another console window by typing <CTRL><ALT>n, where n is 1 or 2), navigate using cd and ls to the directory with the file you want to transfer. Type: ftp [ip removed] <Enter>. Type: anonymous<Enter> for name and just <Enter> for password. Then at ftp> prompt t ype: bin<Enter>, then t ype: put 2006_ 132_16.hkp, for example. File should end up in C:\SAME Data on laptop. After done transferring file(s), type: bye<Enter> on the DACU. You can also use the two above methods to transfer files to the DACU using GET and get instead of puts. 6.2 Converting Housekeeping file to engineering units Use SAMEFileConverter. More instructions TBD.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p14,0,"SAME-DOC-007 9 7.0 MISCELLANEOUS PROCEDURES AND INFORMATION 7.1 Obtaining a directory listing An example: - OSCommand(‘ls –l /data/2006_237 > /data/dir.txt’). - FTPCommand(‘LCD /data’). - FTPCommand(‘PUT dir.txt’). - Have MSG cadre downlink the file dir.txt. 7.2 Replacing the registry file Normally you would change individual entries usi ng the SetRegistry command. This method could be used to set the registry to a known state, perhaps a f ile under source control, using an uplinked file. And this is the only way I could get the copied-over registry.txt file to ‘take’. If the system is shut down nicely through the shutdown command then it appears to revert to the old registry file. - FTPCommand(‘LCD /config’). - FTPCommand(‘GET registry.new.txt’) - OSCommand(‘cp /config/registry.txt /config/registry.old.txt’) - OSCommand(‘cp /config/registry.new.txt /config/registry.txt’) - Do a hard shutdown: power off - Power on and the new settings should take effect. 7.3 Piston Calibration Piston calibration, a.k.a. piston characterization, is a process of adjusting some registry values so that piston rates and time marks are correct for current conditions. This process should be performed whenever there is a change of hardware, especially the aging chamber and piston itself; a change of environment, such as the move to the ISS; a change of fluid source strength; or a change of hardware condition, perhaps after many tests have been run.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p14,1,"7.3.1 Inlet Velocity Actual inlet velocity is required to match desired inle t velocity to within +/- 0.5 cm/sec. Actual velocity is not a directly measured value. It is a function of piston bottom to top travel time, chamber volume, and inlet tube diameter. The equation is: t Vv *60*105.2= where, v = actual velocity (cm/sec) V = chamber volume (L) = 5.65 t = piston travel time (seconds) 2.105 = conversion beween LPM and cm/sec Increasing the registry value, FillLeakRateLPM, will in crease the actual velocity. To determine piston travel time, perform the following steps: - Run a test with the desired inlet velocity and no heating (setpoint set below R0 and/or heater duration set to zero and/or no sample engaged.) - Abort the test during or after the aging period. - Obtain the drive side pressure data during chamber fill and graph it.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p15,0,"SAME-DOC-007 10 - Pick the points where the piston begins moving, characterized by the start of increasing pressure, and where the piston stops moving, characterized by drastically decreasing pressure. - Subtract the start time from the stop time and calculate velocity. Adjust FillLeakRateLPM and repeat as necessary. 7.3.2 Sample Heating Start Time Ideally, the start of sample heating is calculated so that the end of sample heating occurs before the piston reaches the top of the chamber. Actually, even before that, so that most of the smoke in the inlet tube at the end of heating enters the chamber as well. And really, even before that, so that some clean air follows the ‘last’ of the smoke and more of the smoke enters the chamber. The amount of the latter clean air is determined by the BackEndCleanAirOffset registry value. Because there is no direct physical measurement of where the piston is in the middle of its stroke, the software must estimate when the stroke begins and how long the stroke will take. Then it can back off from there to determine sample heating stop and start times. The registry values, FillWaita.b, where a.b is 0.0 to 5.0 by 0.5, help compensate for timing inconsis tencies which are a function of fill rate in LPM. (These values were more drastically a function of f ill rate before the FillLeakRateLPM registry value was increased from 0.0. See section 7.3.1.).",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p15,1,"Piston chamber fill movement begins by evacuating the the drive side of the piston at a rate of 3.0 LPM until the drive side pressure reaches 13.2 psia. These values have been determined to be reliable, but can be changed if necessary by altering the registry values, FillStartRateLPM and FillStartPressurePSIA. Then the software waits for a period determined by th e desired fill rate, a function of inlet velocity, and the FillWait registry values. For a fill rate of 3.0 the software waits for the value of FillWait3.0. For rates in between, the wait time is interpolated. After wai ting this time the software marks the start of piston movement, logging a message for the user. Sample heating and other start and stop times are based on this mark. To determine the best FillWait registry values, - Perform the following steps for each rate, R= 0.5 to 5.0 by 0.5, and then estimate FillWait0.0 interpolating by hand on a graph of the other values versus rate. - Fill the chamber using the SelfTest command and the rate. - Note when the drive side pressure reaches 13.2 psia. The software issues a “x seconds before piston moving mark” message to indicate this point. The aim of this procedure is to get the ‘x’ right. - Note when the piston reaches the top of the chamber. Visual confirmation is best, but examination of the drive side pressure also works. The start of the drastic drop in pressure is the point at which the top is reached.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p15,2,"Do not rely on the “Aging chamber is filled” log message. This comes only after the FillTopPressurePSIA has been reached. - Subtract the two times to get the time between 13.2 psia and the top, tactual. - Registry value for this rate, a.b is, where, 60*. teLPMFillLeakRaba Vt chamber ideal −= and, chamberV = Chamber volume = 5.65 L FillLeakRateLPM = registry value by this name idealactual ttbFillWaita −=.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p16,0,"SAME-DOC-007 11 7.3.3 Secondary Aging Volume When a secondary aging period and volume are speci fied, the piston must stop a percentage of the distance between the top and bottom of the chamber. There is no direct physical measurement of the running distance, therefore it is estimated from th e exhaust rate over time. The registry value, ExhaustPercentageCorrectionFactor, is applied to the running percentage. To adjust this value, - Perform a SelfTest to exhaust the chamber to a specific percentage. For best results use the exhaust rate and secondary volume that will be used during the actual test. Or use 5 LPM and 50%. - Perform a SelfTest to exhaust the chamber the rest of the way. Use the same rate and 100%. - Calculate the time taken for each portion using log messages or pressure data and figure the percentage of total time taken by the second exhaust. Adjust the registry value and repeat until this percentage is within the desired accuracy of the secondary aging volume percentage. 7.3.4 Exhaust Rate It was verified that the system exhausts at a rate sufficient to achieve overpressure of the diagnostic duct for all combinations of diagnostic use except when usi ng the STS detector with both DustTraks. If this situation changes, the exhaust rate can be increased using the ExhaustOverpressureRate registry value. 7.4 Changing flight software during flight Description: Transfer new version of flight software from MLC to DACU.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p16,1,"Uses crew time, SAME Utility Cable, and HyperTerminal application on MLC to perform ftp transfer, backup old flight software, and replace it with the new. Reboots are necessary b ecause the software runs au tomatically on startup and cannot be replaced while running. Therefore, afte r making a backup, the software is removed so that it cannot autostart. Then the new version is copied to the autostart name. Upon reboot, the system finds the autostart name again, the new version of software, and operation proceeds normally. *Note that only the steps marked with an asterisk n eed to be done by the crew. The others can be accomplished using the ground display and the OSCommand and FTPCommand. - Uplink or transfer from media, SAME_g.new to MLC’s C:/ftp directory. - *Connect SAME Utility Cable between DACU and COM port x of MLC - *On MLC, start HyperTerminal (HT) and configure to Connect using: COMx with 19200 bps, 8 data bits, No parity, 1 stop bit, Xon/Xoff flow control. In Settings tab, Emulation is auto- detect. Or a configuration file can be uplinked and the crew will just double-click that. - Power on SAME. Wait for 30 seconds. - At HT, press <Enter> and verify that a prompt, “#”, appears. - At ‘#’ prompt, type ‘cd /root<ENTER>’. If you like, type ‘ls<ENTER>’ to show listing of the /root directory. - Type ‘ftp [ip removed]<ENTER>’. At prompt for Name enter ‘anonymous’. At prompt for Password just press <ENTER>.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p16,2,"Verify message saying ‘230 Anonymous user logged in’ and ‘ftp>’ prompt. - At ftp> prompt, type ‘bin<ENTER>’. - Type ‘get SAME_g.new<ENTER>’. Verify progress indication and return of ftp> prompt with no errors. - Type ‘bye<ENTER>’. ‘#’ prompt appears.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p17,0,"SAME-DOC-007 12 - At ‘#’ prompt, type ‘chmod 777 *<ENTER>’. - Type ‘cp SAME_g SAME_g.old1<ENTER>’. Wait for prompt, approx. 20 seconds. - Type ‘rm SAME_g<ENTER>’. - Type ‘shutdown<ENTER>’. Wait 30 seconds for DACU to reboot. - *At HT, press <Enter> and verify that a prompt, “#”, appears. - *At ‘#’ prompt, type ‘cd /root<ENTER>’. - *Type ‘cp SAME_g.new SAME_g<ENTER>’. Wait for prompt, approx. 20 seconds. - *Type ‘shutdown<ENTER>’. - *Exit HT. - Ground team will confirm new version is running (or check log messages on Crew Display for report of version number), and then shutdown SAME. - *Remove cable. 7.5 Description of HeaterCorrectOffset Utility Sample heater offset correction is a feature of the sample heater microcontroller that reduces measurement error by automatically removing off set voltages from the signal path. After offset correction is performed, all subsequent resistance me asurements will include the o ffset correction. Until the system is powered off, that is. At power up the offset correction is zero. Offset correction should be run for each sample while the sample is engaged with the inlet tube and sample heater contacts. Occasionally the offset correction circuitry produces a co rrection that is obviously too high. The result is that subsequent reports of resistance are lower than ac tual. This can be detected at the time of the test pause by looking at the sample resistance and by lo oking for the log message indicating the value of the offset correction.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p17,1,"It should be approximately 200uV or less. If the offset correction at the time of the test pause is too high, then the test must be aborted and restarted to give the offset correction circuitry a second chan ce. To avoid this time-consuming detour a utility separate from the flight software has been developed. It is HeaterCorrectOffset_g in the /root directory of the DACU. It can be run from the flight software by sending the OSCommand command with a parameter of ‘/root/HeaterCorrectOffset_g’. The HeaterCorrectOffset utility app lies 12VDC to the sample heater , waits a few seconds, and then requests an offset correction from the heater microcontro ller. If the returned off set is in range then the utility exits, otherwise it requests the offset correction ag ain for several times until it is in range. Either way, 12VDC is removed from the sample heater befo re the utility exists. As long as system power (not 12VDC) remains applied, the offset correction initiated by the utility will be used by the flight software. If the utility fails to correct the offset to within the desired range then it returns a -1 (which is reported in the flight software log as 65280), otherwise it returns a zero to indicate success. There is one caveat to running the HeaterCorrectOffset utility. The flight software must be either not running, which will not occur during flight, or must be in the uninitialized state.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p17,2,"Once the software is initialized the utility cannot gain serial access to the heater micr ocontroller. Thus the sequence gymnastics in section 4.0. Additionally, if the HeaterCorrectOffset utility is used, then offset correction prior to the test pause should be disabled. Otherwise an incorrect correction he re will negate the use of the utility. Setting the CorrectOffset registry value to zero will disable au tomatic offset correction during the test, allowing the value created by the HeaterCorrectOffset utility to be used.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p18,0,"SAME-DOC-007 13 For advanced users, the HeaterCorrectOffset uti lity is configurable via its own registry file, /config/HeaterCorrectOffsetRegistry.txt, on the DACU. Changes on the flight system can be made by uplinking a new file. Parameters are as follows: Key Name Description Default Value Units Data Type HCOMaxOffset Maximum allowa ble offset. If above this value the utility retries the correction. 200 microvolts float HCORetries Number of times to retry the correction before returning -1. 3 n/a short HCOSleep1Msec This key plus next key is time to wait between turning on the 12VDC and requesting an offset correction. 3000 milliseconds ushort HCOSleep2Msec Time to wait between retries. 2000 milliseconds ushort As stated before, the HeaterCorrectOffset utility report s success or failure back to the flight software. More details, including the correction factor and resi stance measurement immediately after for each retry can be found in a log file in /data/HeaterCorrectOffset on the DACU. Beware that this file will be named the same as the current day’s flight software l og, but it will not be automatically downlinked upon shutdown. 7.6 Transfer and Installation of Crew Interface application FTPCommand(‘LCD /crewinterface’) FTPCommand(‘PUT SAMECrewInterfaceX.YInstall.exe’), where X.Y is the version. The transfer takes about 1.5 minutes and the file should end up in c:\ftp on the MLC. An EDLFSerialPort Incorrect Checksum error may appear in the log. It can be ignored.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p18,1,"At this point the MSG cadre will use their commands to run the install file. The format is: SAMECrewInterfaceX.YInstall.exe /SP- /VERYSILENT ,where X.Y is the version number. No user interaction is required with these options. The crew interface and support files will be installed in c:\01 on the laptop. See SAME-DOC-008, Crew Interface Description; SAME-PRO-002, Software Installation Procedure; and SAME-DOC-011, Flight Software VDD, for more information. The crew interface and support files are uninstalled by the MSG using the following: c:\01\unins000.exe /SILENT 7.7 Use of tar utility The tar utility can be used to assemble or disassemble collections of files on the DACU, much like WinZip does on a PC. It may be useful to tar a collection of files into one file prior to downlink. The executable is located in the /root directory on the DACU and therefore should be able to execute from any local directory. It is not necessary to specify the path to tar. Use the OSCommand and parameters according to http://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/6.3.0/neutrino/utilities/t/tar.html.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p19,0,"SAME-DOC-007 14 8.0 KNOWN ISSUES - Aborting a test occasionally causes the software to restart. If this should happen you’ll need to reissue the Initialize command. - Try to avoid running a test sequence through the change of hour (i.e. 12:45-13:05). Transfer of the housekeeping file at the top of the hour takes a good bit of processor time and the processor is close to 100% capacity, possibly affecting diagnostic measurement retrieval. - It is recommended that the system be gracefully shut down using the Shutdown button on the System tab, then cut power about 2 minutes later. However, about 20% of the time the software will hang or restart before completely shutting down. If it restarts, issue the Shutdown command again or issue the OS command with parameter, ‘/bin/shutdown –b’. If it hangs (you may only know this if you have a monitor connected to the GSE port) a hard power off will have to suffice. The operating system will clean up any file ‘litter’ on the next power up. - Maximum inlet velocity with the FillLeakRateLPM registry value equal to 0.30 is 9.8 cm/sec. Above this the software does not correctly use the FillWait registry values to time chamber fill operations, including sample heating start time. 10.0 cm/sec can be achieved if FillLeakRateLPM is 0.25 LPM or less. But timing is affected in other ways and ground testing or flight testing without sample heating is recommended. - Whenever the P-Trak is on, the DACU requests a measurement from it once per second.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p19,1,"Occasionally the P-Trak does not respond with a measurement and the DACU substitutes a -999 value instead to mark this occurance. If the P-Trak is logging during this time, that missing data can be recovered by issuing the Transfer Data command to retrieve the logged data. It is recommended that this be done for each test, soon (within days) after the test is complete. - The P-Trak memory can hold approximately 60 20-minute tests. When all memory is used the unit does not respond with any serial measurements while logging. In other words, continuous - 999 readings is one indicator of full memory. It is suggested that memory be cleared after 50 tests to be safe. - Sample heater offset correction occasionally produces an incorrect correction factor. The result is that reported sample resistance is lower than actual. For more information see section 7.5. - Using ground support equipment there was insufficient fluid flow during chamber exhaust to maintain overpressure of the diagnostic duct, as required, when the STS detector and both DustTraks are in use. When the STS detector is used it is recommended that at most one DustTrak is used. - If the heater gives an “Abort on MaxRt” error, the system must be power cycled before proceeding. Otherwise, as will be apparent, the resistance measurement will be in error. - When specifying inlet velocities below 2 cm/sec, the FillWait registry values may not be accurate.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p19,2,"The registry value, BackEndCleanAirOffset, must be adjusted to ensure correct heater timing. For 1 cm/sec, a value of 170 seconds is good. But run a test with no heating to be sure. - During ground testing the connection between the DACU and the MSGSimulator occasionally drops out. Packet count numbers of the MSGSimulator no longer increment and change from green to blue. To reestablish the connection deactivate it and activate it again.",,False
PSI-102,Science Documents,PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Science Documents/PSI-102_Science Documents_SAME_Operations Manual_007.pdf,p20,0,SAME-DOC-007 15 9.0 ACRONYMNS AND ABBREVIATIONS CSV Comma Separated Variable DACU Data Acquisition Control Unit DIO Digital Input/Output ECW Experiment Control Word EDLF ESTEC Data Link Format ESTEC European Space Research & Technology Centre FTP File Transfer Protocol GRC Glenn Research Center H&S Health and Status I/O Input - Output ISS International Space Station LAN Local Area Network LRT Low Rate Telemetry MDM Multiplexer/De-Mutiplexer MLC Microgravity Science Glovebox Laptop Computer MLCS Microgravity Science Glovebox Laptop Computer Server MSFC Marshall Space Flight Center MSG Microgravity Science Glovebox POIC Payload Operations Integration Center PSRP Payload Safety Review Panel RC Rack Controller SAMS Space Acceleration Measurement System SC Sample Carousel SAME Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment SPLC Standard Payload Computer SRD Science Requirements Document STS Space Transportation System (eg. Space Shuttle) TCP/IP Transfer Control Protocol/Internet Protocol TP Thermal Precipitator Unit TReK Telescience Resource Kit TSC Telescience Support Center UIP Utility Interface Panel VCU Video Control Unit WV Work Volume,,False
