PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p7,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 1 1.0 INTRODUCTION 1.1 Purpose This document defines the specific requirements to be satisfied throughout the implementation of the software for the Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment (SAME) instrument. The information contained within this document provides the technical requirements to be implemented and enables the development team to assess whether or not the completed software complies with the requirements of the system. The data included in this document provides the criteria for acceptance of the integrated software configuration. 1.2 Scope The requirements specified herein apply only to SAME instrument software and custom MSG Laptop Computer (MLC) software. All ground based telemetry data processing and command uplink software requirements will be identified in future documents as required. 1.3 Applicable Documents [1] Exploration Investigation Requirements Document for SAME a.k.a Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment, Rev. 1.01, Dr. David Urban, NASA Glenn Research Center, January 3, 2005.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p7,1,"[2] SAME-PLN-003 Product Assurance Plan [3] SAME-PLN-005 Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document [4] SAME-PLN-006 Software Management and Assurance Plan [5] SAME-DOC-001 Concept Description Document [6] SAME-DOC-005 Software Interface Control Document [7] MSFC-RQMT-2888 Microgravity Science Glovebox Investigation Interface Requirements Document [8] MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001 MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document [9] MSFC-RQMT-3098 Software Requirements Specification (SRS) for Microgravity Science Glovebox Laptop Computer Server Software [10] MSFC-HDBK-3168 Software Users Guide For Microgravity Science Glovebox Laptop Computer Server Software [11] PHOTRON-SPEC Ethernet Command Specification for Ultima 512 [12] SSP 58700-ANX5 U.S. Payload Operations Data File Management Plan, Annex 5: Payload Display Review [13] SSP 58700-ANX6 U.S. Payload Operations Data File Management Plan, Annex 6: Payload Developers Review Guide [14] SSP 50313 Display and Graphics Commonality Standard",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p8,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 2 2.0 INSTRUMENT OVERVIEW The SAME instrument is designed to quantify the size and distribution of smoke particulate in low gravity so that this information is available for the future design of effective low gravity smoke detectors. The instrument will also be used to evaluate the performance of two existing U.S. spacecraft smoke detectors and one commercial smoke detector. Measurements will be made for the smoke from several materials found in spacecraft considering the effects of sample temperature, air flow, smoke residence time and aging. The primary targets are materials that produce liquid smoke particulate but solid smoke producers will also be considered.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p8,1,"Figure 1 SAME Instrument Assembly Inside MSG WV The instrument is installed and operated in the MSG Work Volume (WV) which provides a minimal level of containment (see Figure 1). A general description of the major subsystems follows below. See SAME-DOC-001, Concept Description Document, for more detail. • Sample Carousel Turning Module, Sample Carousel and Heater Wire Coil These components are used to select and heat a sample in order to generate smoke. • Camera A camera and light(s) are mounted to see the sample and production of smoke. • Aging Chamber The aging chamber has a piston and two gate valves for holding the smoke for a time before releasing it downstream. The piston is moved by pressure and vacuum provided by MSG and controlled by SAME. • Thermal Precipitator Module This assembly collects samples of smoke on TEM grids for post-flight inspection. It has access to smoke before and after the aging chamber. Aging Chamber Data Acquisition and Control Unit (DACU) Power Distribution and Control Unit Thermal Precipitator Module Sample Carousel Smoke Inlet Duct ISS Smoke Detector STS Smoke Detector Carousel Mounting Brackets Camera Controller P-Trak Enclosure Dynamic Dilutor Commercial Diagnostics Enclosure Fluid Control Unit",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p9,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 3 • Smoke Diagnostics After the aging chamber, the smoke is passed to several diagnostics: the TSI P-Trak™ particle concentration counter, the TSI DustTrak™, a residential-grade ionization detector, the ISS photoelectric smoke detector and the STS ionization smoke detector. • Fluids Control Unit Pressure and vacuum supplied by MSG is controlled for smoke aging chamber piston movement and for all other fluid flow to and from the diagnostic equipment. • System Diagnostics Besides the diagnostic instruments measuring smoke characteristics, there are pressure transducers, temperature gauges, and voltage and current sensors to monitor system performance. • External Interfaces External interfaces provided by MSG include power, video recording, temperature control, post- pyrolization venting, nitrogen and air supply/vacuum, an RS-422 interface for commanding and telemetry, ethernet for file transfer, and a laptop for crew access to the SAME experiment. A SAMS unit measures local µg and vibrations and forwards that data to the ground requiring no SAME or MSG processing. • Avionics Contained in the Data Acquisition Control Unit (DACU) box, the SAME avionics conduct automated experiments, controlling the other sub-systems to produce and analyze the smoke. Commands from the ground are processed. Experiment data is saved to files on a local storage medium and forwarded to MSG for subsequent downlink. Housekeeping data is downlinked every second and supplied to MSG.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p9,1,"Off- nominal conditions are reported to MSG and the ground. In addition, a means for the crew to access SAME data and control is provided.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p10,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 4 3.0 AVIONICS OVERVIEW SAME avionics are located in the DACU within the MSG Work Volume (WV). Cables will stretch to the SAME diagnostic hardware for control and feedback, to J311 of the WV for command and data transfer, and to J404 of the WV for ethernet connection to the MSG laptop computer (MLC) for file transfer and crew display operation. There is also a cable for the camera. 3.1 Data Interfaces To interface to the MSG, and subsequently to ground systems, the DACU CPU will communicate primarily to the MSG Rack Controller, also known as the SPLC (Standard Payload Computer) using an RS-422 interface (see Figure 2). SAME will use this interface to receive uplink commands and send health and status (H&S) data and low rate telemetry (LRT) data.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p10,1,"Figure 2 SAME to MSG Data Interfaces Payload commands are transmitted from the ground through the ISS Payload MDM and eventually to the SPLC via MIL-STD-1553. The SPLC abstracts this interface from the payload developer, relieving the burden of knowing the details about the 1553 bus and command validation. A packet containing SAME health and status is automatically transmitted in the reverse direction on the 1553 bus and is received on the ground. Also included in the payload health and status packet is the Experiment Control Word or ECW, a single dedicated memory word that represents the status of the payload. The ECW is monitored by various ISS subsystems and provides a quick alert if an off-nominal condition is indicated in it.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p11,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 5 File-based SAME science data such as experiment measurement data and instrument ancillary data will be transferred to the MLC using FTP utilizing a 10BaseT ethernet connection. Once these files are stored on the MLC, the MSG Operations Team at the Payload Operations Integration Center (POIC) will periodically initiate their downlink and forward them to the SAME Operations Team. Since SAME is not permitted to transmit data on the ISS LAN, the DACU will automatically switch the active network connection from the MLC to SAME and ISS using a custom designed ethernet switch when the FTP transfer is required. In its normal state, the MLC will be connected to ISS LAN. The camera looking at the SAME sample is hooked up via an NTSC cable to a feedthrough inside the WV which is then patched on the outside to a video recorder channel in the video drawer. 3.2 DACU Overview The DACU is the central point for SAME control. It is ultimately responsible for all experiment operations and interfacing with the environment external to SAME. It contains all of the logic necessary to autonomously operate experiments. A brief list of its major responsibilities is as follows: 1. Receive, validate and process commands from the MSG. 2. Periodically transmit SAME system status and select parameters to the MSG. 3. Transmit completed and requested data files to the MLC via FTP. 4. Manage and store all science and system files on the local data storage medium. 5.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p11,1,"Autonomously control the progress of preprogrammed tests. 6. Provide diagnostic information and manual control of all sensors and controls using the GSE port. 7. Control the MSG video recorder(s) capturing SAME images. 8. React to sensor(s) out of tolerance by notifying MSG and ground systems, and performing software inhibits or other actions as appropriate. 3.3 Crew Display Overview During long periods of LOS or failure of telemetry, it is beneficial to provide limited visibility into SAME operation. Payloads may utilize the MLC to present payload status to the crew and provide a mechanism to control the payload directly from it. Two options for providing the SAME Crew Display are possible. Option 1 is the preferred solution at this time because of a likely delay in an upgrade to provide a dedicated ethernet link between the MLC and an experiment. 3.3.1 Option 1 - Custom Win32 or Java Based Application All data transmitted to the SPLC via H&S and LRT is also transmitted to the MLC via the MSG 1553 bus, and is therefore available on the MLC. SAME may develop a custom Win32-based or Java-based application that displays a limited set of the SAME downlink data for the crew to see and provides commanding to the SAME instrument. 3.3.2 Option 2 - Embedded Web Pages A dedicated ethernet interface between the MLC and SAME may be leveraged to provide a web-based interface into the DACU using the MLC native web browser.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p11,2,The operating system running on the DACU is capable of running an embedded web-server such as Apache or NASA’s Tempest servers.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p12,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 6 4.0 SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS 4.1 System Status 4.1.1 Update Rate [SAME-SW-REQ-4-1-1-1-1] The DACU software shall update its internal status once per second. 4.1.2 LED Status Indicators The DACU will control two LEDs mounted on the outside of the DACU enclosure using discrete outputs. These LEDs are described in Table 1. Table 1 Status LEDs LED Name Description OK STATUS Indicates nominal operation of the entire SAME apparatus. TEST RUNNING Indicates that a test is currently running. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-1-2-1-1] The DACU shall illuminate the OK STATUS LED while the CPU is properly running. If the software detects an internal fault or error condition, the LED shall be extinguished. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-1-2-1-2] The DACU shall illuminate the TEST RUNNING LED while a test is running. Upon completion, the LED shall be extinguished. 4.2 Data Communication There are three two-way paths of data communications connected to the DACU. An RS422 interface is used for communications with the SPLC. An ethernet interface is used for communications with MLC. An RS232 interface is available while not on orbit for development, maintenance, and debugging activities. 4.2.1 SPLC Communications 4.2.1.1 Serial Port Configuration [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-1-1] A single bi-directional RS422 channel between the DACU and the SPLC shall be used to communicate with the MSG systems. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-1-2] RS422 serial port settings shall be 19200 baud, no parity, 8 data bits, and 1 stop bit.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p13,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 7 4.2.1.2 Data Format [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-2-1] All data that the DACU transmits to and accepts from the SPLC shall conform to the ESTEC Data Link Format as specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC- 0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document, section 3.5.1.1. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-2-2] All data that the DACU transmits to and accepts from the SPLC shall conform to the byte and bit order as specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document, section 1.4.3. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-2-3] All data that the DACU transmits to and accepts from the SPLC shall conform to the word alignment as specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document, section 2.3. 4.2.1.3 Health and Status Packet [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-3-1] Once per second, the DACU shall transmit to the SPLC a health and status packet containing information such as voltages, currents, and pressures necessary to verify safe and proper operation. Included in this information are the C&W flags which indicate nominal or off-nominal operation to the rack systems. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-3-2] The content of the health and status packet shall conform to the format specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-3-3] The Cycle Counter field in the health and status packet shall be incremented once per second up to the value 0xffff, then roll over to 0.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p13,1,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-3-4] The C&W flags fields in the health and status packet shall be asserted only upon the conditions listed in Section 6.2. 4.2.1.4 Low Rate Telemetry Packet [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-4-1] Once per second, the DACU shall transmit to the SPLC a low rate telemetry packet representing the current state of the instrument. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-4-2] The content of the low rate telemetry packet shall conform to the format specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document. 4.2.1.5 Video Command Packet [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-5-1] The DACU shall have the ability to control MSG video drawer functions by sending video command packets to the SPLC. The controllable video drawer functions are determined by the requirements of the video drawer command group, Section 4.4.5. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-5-2] The content of video drawer packets issued by the DACU shall conform to the format specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document. 4.2.1.6 Time of Day Packet [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-6-1] The DACU shall accept time of day packets from the SPLC when they adhere to the format specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p14,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 8 4.2.1.7 Experiment Command Packet [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-1-7-1] The DACU shall accept experiment command packets from the SPLC when they adhere to the format specified in MSG-ORIGIN-IC-0001, MSG RC ASW Interface Control Document. 4.2.2 MLC Communications Upon completion of a science run, all data files will be stored on DACU internal storage. A copy of these data files will automatically be forwarded to the MLC via FTP on a point-to-point cross-wired ethernet cable. The SAME Operations Team will submit a request to the MSG Team at MSFC to downlink the files on the MLC at the earliest possible time. The downlinked files will then be electronically forwarded to GRC. Because the connection is made directly to the MLC, no bandwidth restrictions are necessary to ensure ISS LAN resource availability. 4.2.2.1 FTP Usage [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-2-1-1] All data files stored on the DACU local storage shall be automatically forwarded to the MLC via FTP for backup and downlink. 4.2.2.2 Network Details [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-2-2-1] A dedicated standard 802.3 (10BaseT) ethernet connection from the MLC to the DACU shall be used to perform the FTP transfer. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-2-2-2] Communications with the FTP server shall be done utilizing a server IP address of [ip removed], port 21. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-2-2-3] The DACU will automatically switch the ethernet source from MLC→ISS to MLC→SAME when communication to the MLC is required.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p14,1,"When the transaction is completed, the original MLC→ISS connection will be restored. 4.2.2.3 SAME File Maintenance [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-2-3-1] The DACU shall provide the ability to remove SAME files from the FTP server as needed to ensure proper storage space utilization. 4.2.3 Remote Console Communications [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-3-1-1] The DACU shall provide a telnet console login session on an RS232 port. All administrative features and capabilities available via telnet shall be supported. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-2-3-1-2] The telnet console RS232 serial port settings shall be, 19200 baud, no parity, 8 data bits, and 1 stop bit.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p15,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 9 4.3 Data Storage Requirements The data storage requirements reflect two main needs. First, that a record of all status, measurements and events is preserved beyond the flight of the instrument. And second, that individual values of status, measurements or events can be easily located in stored data. 4.3.1 Storage Media [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-1-1-1] The DACU shall store all files on a media that can preserve all generated files for extraction after payload down-mass. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-1-1-2] The storage media shall have a capacity of at least 520 megabytes. 4.3.2 File Conventions 4.3.2.1 Directory Structure Because the execution of ISS instrument experiments are highly scheduled, the date and time that procedures and ground commanding is performed is usually reliably recorded by NASA cadre and experiment ground operation teams. As such, finding the data files for a previously run experiment or for a particular logged event is facilitated by a data storage structure based on the date. Therefore, [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-2-1-1] All files created shall be organized into a hierarchical directory structure based on the date that files were created. 4.3.2.2 File Names [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-2-2-1] All files created shall have unique file names. 4.3.3 Stored Data 4.3.3.1 Housekeeping Data [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-1-1] Once per second data relating to the status of the instrument shall be stored to a file.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p15,1,[SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-1-2] Each second of housekeeping data stored to a file shall be time-tagged. 4.3.3.2 Messages [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-2-1] Messages generated by the instrument shall be stored to a file. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-2-2] Each message stored to a file shall be time-tagged. 4.3.3.3 Experiment Results [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-3-1] Experiment results shall be stored to a file [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-3-2] Experiment results stored to a file shall be time-tagged. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-3-3-3-3] DELETED.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p16,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 10 4.4 Command Requirements Commands are the primary means of initiating SAME instrument operations. Commands to an instrument are typically formatted into an array of data and inserted into command packets and then transmitted from the ground operation station or from the crew display on the MSG MLC on-board the ISS. When command packets reach the DACU the command data within is extracted and interpreted and the appropriate software functions are executed. The command requirements below deal mainly with the functions required by the instrument and not with how those functions are implemented or with how the command data is formatted. See SAME-DOC-005, Software Interface Control Document, for the latter information. 4.4.1 General Command Requirements [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-1] DELETED [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-2] DELETED Table 2 DELETED DELETED",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p16,1,"[SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-3] At the start of execution of a command the DACU shall generate and downlink a message that includes the name of the command and its parameters. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-4] At the successful completion of a command the DACU shall generate and downlink a message announcing the successful completion. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-5] The DACU shall perform out-of-bound checking on all command parameters and reject commands with out-of-bound parameters. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-6] The bounds for command parameters shall be those specified in SAME- DOC-005, Software Interface Control Document. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-7] The DACU shall allow for suspension of bound checking on command parameters. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-1-1-8] The DACU shall indicate when a command is being executed with bound checking suspended. 4.4.2 System Command Group [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-2-1-1] The DACU shall accept a set of commands to perform system level operations. The system group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 3.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p17,0,SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 11 Table 3 System Command Group Command Name Operation Description NoOp Command generates the required received and complete messages but performs no operation. Useful for a quick test of the command interface. Initialize Initialize hardware components and prepares the system to run a test. Shutdown Perform an orderly system shutdown. Reset Reset the computer running the software. SelfTest Perform a system self-test and report the result. OSCommand Execute an operating system level command as if it were entered at the command prompt. FTPCommand Perform an FTP command SetDigitalOutput Turn on or off the specified digital output. SetAnalogOutput Set the output of the specified analog output channel. ClearEvents Clears all outstanding events IgnoreBounds Bounds checking is suspended for only the next command sent after this one. SetRegistryValue Manually sets a value in the file registry. 4.4.3 Test Command Group [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-3-1-1] The DACU shall accept a set of commands to perform automated tests. The test group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 4.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p17,1,"Table 4 Test Command Group Command Name Operation Description StartTest Start the specified test. If the specified test has not been defined or if it has any undefined or invalid parameters then an error shall be generated and the test shall be aborted. PauseTest Suspend the currently running test ResumeTest Resume a currently running test that has been suspended. AbortTest Stop the currently running test, otherwise it finishes on its own. 4.4.4 Test Configuration Command Group [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-4-1-1] The DACU shall accept a set of commands to configure test parameters. The test configuration group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 5. All operations are for a specified test.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p18,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 12 Table 5 Test Configuration Command Group Command Name Operation Description SetSampleNumber Sets the desired sample number for the test. SetCarouselNumber Set the carousel number (for reference only, not for control of which carousel is installed). SetInletVelocity Sets the target flow velocity for the inlet duct during sample heating. SetHeaterSetpoint Sets the resistance/temperature setpoint of the heater controller. SetHeaterDuration Sets the duration of the sample heating sequence. SetHeaterPIDCoefficients Sets the PID control coefficients for the heater sequence. SetMixingDuration Sets the time that the mixing fan is on. SetThermalPrecipitatorChannel Sets which channels the smoke will be sent to on the thermal precipitator unit for pre-aged and post-aged smoke. SetPrimaryAgingDuration Sets the duration that smoke is kept in the smoke aging chamber. SetSecondaryAgingDuration Sets an optional extended aging duration that applies to a portion of the total smoke. SetSecondaryAgingVolume Sets the portion of smoke left in the smoke aging chamber for the optional secondary aging duration. SetLFEFlowRate Sets the dilution rate for the P-Trak in terms of the laminar flow element (LFE) flow rate. This is the target amount of smoke aerosol reaching the P-Trak. Dilution GN2 makes up the remainder of the 700 sccm flowing to the P-Trak. SetTestSetup Sets a number of discretes indicating which diagnostics should be used and other test setup variables.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p18,1,DownlinkTestParameters Downlinks all parameters of a specified test. CreateNewTest Creates a new test with the specified number and configures it with default values. Error occurs if test already exists. 4.4.5 Video Drawer Command Group [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-5-1-1] The DACU shall accept a set of commands to perform MSG video drawer operations. The video drawer group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 6. Table 6 Video Drawer Command Group Command Name Operation Description Power Switch power on or off to the specified recorder. Record Start recording on the specified recorder or resumes from pause. Stop Stop recording on the specified recorder. Pause Pause recording on the specified recorder. 4.4.6 DustTrak Command Group [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-6-1-1] The DACU shall accept a set of commands to activate the keys on the DustTrak. The DustTrak group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 7.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p19,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 13 Table 7 DustTrak Command Group Command Name Operation Description PowerOn Powers on the DustTrak and performs some initialization. PowerOff Powers off the DustTrak. Up Activates the Up key on the DustTrak keypad. Down Activates the Down key on the DustTrak keypad. IncrementSamplingMode Activates the Sampling Mode key on the DustTrak keypad, incrementing the sampling mode. StartStopSampling Activates the Sample key on the DustTrak keypad. ViewStatistics Activates the Statistics key on the DustTrak keypad. ViewLoggingInterval Activates the Logging Interval key on the DustTrak keypad to view the current setting. IncrementLoggingInterval Activates the Logging Interval key on the DustTrak keypad to increment to next setting. ViewTimeConstant Activates the Time Constant key on the DustTrak keypad to view the current setting. IncrementTimeConstant Activates the Time Constant key on the DustTrak keypad to increment to next setting. Calibrate Activates the Calibrate key on the DustTrak, pressing it long enough to enter calibration mode. ClearMemory Activates the Clear Memory key on the DustTrak, pressing it long enough to clear the memory. TransferData Retrieves data of the specified test ID and places it in a DustTrak file. 4.4.7 P-Trak Command Group [SAME-SW-REQ-4-4-7-1-1] The DACU shall accept a set of commands to activate the keys on the P- Trak. The P-Trak group commands shall perform the operations listed in Table 8.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p19,1,Table 8 P-Trak Command Group Command Name Operation Description PowerOn Powers on the P-Trak and performs some initialization. PowerOff Powers off the P-Trak. Up Momentarily activate the Up key on the P-Trak keypad. Down Momentarily activate the Down key on the P-Trak keypad. Left Momentarily activate the Left key on the P-Trak keypad. Right Momentarily activate the Right key on the P-Trak keypad. Enter Momentarily activate the Enter key on the P-Trak keypad. Setup Activates the keypad combination which enters setup mode. TransferData Retrieves data of the specified test ID and places it in a P-Trak file.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p20,0,SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 14 4.5 Experiment Sequencing Requirements 4.5.1 Miscellaneous Sequencing Requirements [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-1-1-1] Residence time of smoke in the aging chamber shall be controllable from 0 to 100 (or greater) minutes per aging period. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-1-1-2] The DACU shall command the diagnostic instruments to sample the smoke once the diagnostic duct is filled from the aging chamber. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-1-1-3] The DACU shall control the timing of diagnostic measurements such that a steady state signal is achieved on all instruments. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-1-1-4] The DACU shall allow the option of multiple smoke samples from the aging chamber with separate aging times. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-1-1-5] The DACU shall purge the diagnostic duct between multiple smoke samples from the aging chamber. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-1-1-6] The DACU shall control the system to allow the P-Trak to sample air from the MSG work volume. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-1-1-7] The DACU shall control the system to allow the DustTrak to sample air from the MSG work volume. 4.5.2 Test Matrix Modifiable Parameters [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-2-1-1] DELETED. 4.5.3 Test Definition [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-3-1-1] DELETED. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-3-1-2] There shall be predefined tests with parameters as determined by the Principal Investigator. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-3-1-3] Additional tests shall be possible to define with parameters determined by uplink commands. [SAME-SW-REQ-4-5-3-1-4] It shall be possible to confirm all parameters for a specific test.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p21,0,SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 15 5.0 SUBSYSTEM REQUIREMENTS 5.1 Sample Carousel 5.1.1 Sample Selection [SAME-SW-REQ-5-1-1-1-1] The DACU shall control a motor to rotate the sample carousel to a specified sample number. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-1-1-1-2] The DACU shall use four proximity sensors to confirm correct positioning of the correct sample carousel. 5.1.2 Sample Positioning [SAME-SW-REQ-5-1-2-1-1] The DACU shall mate the selected sample tube with the smoke generation duct and the sample contacts with heater wire coil contacts by controlling a linear actuator. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-1-2-1-2] The DACU shall confirm correct mating of a sample tube and sample contacts by monitoring the resistance between the two heater wire coil contacts. 5.2 Sample Heater (Heater Wire Coil) [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-1] The DACU in combination with the heater microcontroller shall control the sample surface temperature and duration of the heating. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-2] The DACU in combination with the heater microcontroller shall achieve steady state temperature of the heater within 5 seconds. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-3] The DACU in combination with the heater microcontroller shall set the temperature of the heater wire coil to any temperature between 50 and 500 degrees Celcius. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-4] The DACU in combination with the heater microcontroller shall provide active temperature control of the heater.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p21,1,[SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-5] The DACU in combination with the heater microcontroller shall control the heater temperature and duration so as not to ignite the sample. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-6] The DACU shall measure and store the sample temperature at least once per second. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-2-1-1-7] The DACU shall measure and store the gas plume temperature at least once per second. 5.3 Camera and Lighting 5.3.1 Lighting [SAME-SW-REQ-5-3-1-1-1] The DACU shall control power to any combination of the camera lights. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-3-1-1-2] DELETED.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p22,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 16 5.3.2 Camera [SAME-SW-REQ-5-3-2-1-1] The DACU shall control the gain and gamma settings of the camera. 5.4 Aging Chamber [SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-1] The DACU shall control the aging chamber piston upward velocity to achieve 1 to 10 cm/s average flow velocity in the smoke generation duct. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-2] The DACU shall control the aging chamber piston upward movement by controlling a solenoid on the vacuum line, by sensing and controlling a mass flow controller on the vacuum line, and by sensing a pressure transducer monitoring the drive side pressure of the piston. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-3] The DACU shall control the aging chamber piston downward velocity in order to provide smoke at the rate required for the particular diagnostics in use. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-4] The DACU shall control the aging chamber piston downward movement by controlling a solenoid on the GN2 line, by sensing and controlling a mass flow controller on the GN2 line, and by sensing a pressure transducer monitoring the sample side pressure of the piston. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-4-1-1-5] The DACU shall control a mixing fan in the aging chamber to be on during a specified portion of the residence time. 5.5 Thermal Precipitator Module 5.5.1 Valve Control [SAME-SW-REQ-5-5-1-1-1] Using digital outputs the DACU shall control the valves of the thermal precipitator module so that at most only one valve is open at a time.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p22,1,5.5.2 Heater Control [SAME-SW-REQ-5-5-2-1-1] Using a digital output the DACU shall control power to one thermal precipitator module heater at a time. 5.6 DustTrak 5.6.1 Control [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-1-1-1] The DACU shall control the DustTrak via an RS-232 serial port. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-1-1-2] The DACU shall turn the DustTrak on and off by controlling the on/off keypad using custom electronics. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-1-1-3] The DACU shall control the DustTrak keypad using custom electronics. 5.6.2 Data Reception [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-2-1-1] The DACU shall receive data from the DustTrak via an RS-232 serial port. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-6-2-1-2] The DACU shall sample DustTrak data at a minimum rate of 0.2 Hz.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p23,0,SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 17 5.7 P-Trak 5.7.1 Control [SAME-SW-REQ-5-7-1-1-1] The DACU shall control the P-Trak via an RS-232 serial port. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-7-1-1-2] The DACU shall turn the P-Trak on and off by controlling its momentary on/off switch via custom electronics. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-7-1-1-3] The DACU shall control the P-Trak keypad using custom electronics. 5.7.2 Data Reception [SAME-SW-REQ-5-7-2-1-1] The DACU shall receive data from the P-Trak via an RS-232 serial port. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-7-2-1-2] The DACU shall sample P-Trak data at a minimum rate of 0.2 Hz. 5.8 Ionization Detector (Residential Smoke Detector) [SAME-SW-REQ-5-8-1-1-1] The DACU shall record measurements from the ionization detector at a minimum rate of 0.2 Hz. 5.9 ISS Detector The ISS detector is an Allied Signal/HoneywellTM engineering model. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-9-1-1-1] The DACU shall control the ISS detector. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-9-1-1-2] The DACU shall sample the output of the ISS detector at a sampling rate of 1 Hz or greater. 5.10 STS Detector The STS detector is a Brunswick DefenseTM engineering model. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-10-1-1-1] The DACU shall control the STS detector. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-10-1-1-2] The DACU shall sample the output of the STS detector at a sampling rate of 1 Hz or greater. 5.11 Dilution Air [SAME-SW-REQ-5-11-1-1-1] The DACU shall control the dilution of smoke using analog input(s) and output(s) and discrete output(s) to a voltage sensitive orifice and measurement instrumentation.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p24,0,SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 18 5.12 Miscellaneous Diagnostic Measurements [SAME-SW-REQ-5-12-1-1-1] The DACU shall record the air flow speed in the smoke generation duct with a range of at least 0 to 15 cm/s using calibration coefficients as necessary. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-12-1-1-2] The DACU shall record the MFC settings and the pressure transducer data at the rate of at least 0.1 Hz to be used in the calculation of the air flow speed. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-12-1-1-3] The DACU shall record the air flow speed in the smoke generation duct. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-12-1-1-4] The DACU shall measure and store the ambient air pressure of the work volume at a rate of at least 0.1 Hz. [SAME-SW-REQ-5-12-1-1-5] The DACU shall measure and store the ambient air temperature of the work volume at a rate of at least 0.1 Hz.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p25,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 19 6.0 OFF-NOMINAL AND CONTINGENCY REQUIREMENTS 6.1 Maintenance Mode Operation The software will contain provisions to allow experiment operations to continue in the event of the following: [SAME-SW-REQ-6-1-1-1-1] In the event of a loss of the ability to write to the data hard drive, the system shall continue to accept uplink commands and transmit status data to ensure safe operation. [SAME-SW-REQ-6-1-1-1-2] In the event of a loss of ethernet communication between the DACU and the MLC, operations shall continue. Data recorded on the flight avionics may be manually commanded for retransmission once communication is regained. 6.2 Caution and Warning Three discrete bits in the health and status packet are used to indicate to MSG the presence of a payload condition warranting a warning, caution, or advisory classification and reaction. See MSFC-RQMT-2888, MSG Investigation Interface Requirements Document, Section 3.11 for details of each level. The specific payload conditions that result in warning and caution levels are determined by the Payload Safety Review Panel (PSRP) during Phased Safety Reviews. Automatic safing will be performed if the warning or caution bit is set. The advisory bit can be used to reflect errors and off-nominal conditions seen only in the low rate telemetry data to the health and status data.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p25,1,"If, due to limited communication coverage or communication error, the health and status data is received while the low rate telemetry is not, then these off-nominal conditions will still be noted and time-tagged. 6.2.1 Warning There are no conditions that constitute a “Class 2 -Warning” condition and a subsequent set of the Warning bit in the Health and Status packet payload ECW. 6.2.2 Caution There are no conditions that constitute a “Class 3 - Caution” condition and a subsequent set of the Caution bit in the Health and Status packet payload ECW. 6.2.3 Advisory There are no conditions that constitute a “Class 4 - Advisory” condition and a subsequent set of the Advisory bit in the Health and Status packet payload ECW. 6.3 Automatic Safing [SAME-SW-REQ-6-3-1-1-1] In the event of a GN2 regulated pressure reading of over 21 psig, the DACU shall automatically shut off the main GN2 valve. [SAME-SW-REQ-6-3-1-1-2] In the event of a vacuum pressure reading of over 19 psia, the DACU shall automatically shut off the main vacuum valve.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p26,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 20 7.0 CREW DISPLAY REQUIREMENTS 7.1 Graphic Standards The development and approval process for the project crew display follows the guidelines in SSP 58700-ANX5, U.S. Payload Operations Data File Management Plan, Annex 5: Payload Display Review, and in SSP 58700- ANX6, U.S. Payload Operations Data File Management Plan, Annex 6: Payload Developers Review Guide. [SAME-SW-REQ-7-1-1-1-1] The crew display shall meet all applicable standards defined in SSP 50313, Display and Graphics Commonality Standard. 7.2 Interfaces 7.2.1 Host Computer [SAME-SW-REQ-7-2-1-1-1] The crew display shall be able to run on the MSG Laptop Computer with features and operating system as described in MSFC-RQMT-2888, Microgravity Science Glovebox Investigation Interface Requirements Document, Section 3.3.5. 7.2.2 MLC Server",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p26,1,"[SAME-SW-REQ-7-2-2-1-1] The crew display shall send commands from the MLC to the instrument using the MLC Server Socket Interface. [SAME-SW-REQ-7-2-2-1-2] The crew display on the MLC shall receive health and status data and low- rate telemetry from the instrument using the MLC Server Socket Interface. [SAME-SW-REQ-7-2-2-1-3] The crew display shall meet the MLC Server Socket Interface requirements documented in MSFC-RQMT-3098, Software Requirements Specification (SRS) for Microgravity Science Glovebox Laptop Computer Server Software, Section 4.6. 7.3 Features 7.3.1 Commanding [SAME-SW-REQ-7-3-1-1-1] The crew display shall include a subset of the full instrument command list. 7.3.2 Data Display [SAME-SW-REQ-7-3-2-1-1] The crew display shall display a subset of the full instrument health and status and/or low-rate telemetry data. 7.4 Enhanced Mode [SAME-SW-REQ-7-4-1-1-1] DELETED",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p27,0,SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 21 8.0 DEVELOPMENTAL SOFTWARE The software necessary for developmental purposes is subject to change; therefore the items in this section are not requirements but estimates of necessary software effort. Note the use of ‘will’ instead of ‘shall’. The software lead will maintain a true list of necessary software for development tasks. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-1] Software will be provided to support test structures and instrumentation to characterize flow profiles. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-2] Software will be provided to support test structures and instrumentation to validate flow stream lines. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-3] DELETED. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-4] Software will be provided to support test structures and instrumentation to characterize temporal delays of the smoke. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-5] Software will be provided to support test structures and instrumentation to characterize the sample heater rate. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-6] Software will be provided to test and characterize the accuracy of the heater temperature. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-7] Software will be provided to support a test rig for characterization of mass loss rate for the sample heater system. [SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-8] Software will be provided to support a ground test apparatus to test and characterize thermal precipitator sample line transit times.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p27,1,[SAME-SW-REQ-8-1-1-1-9] Software will be provided to support a ground test apparatus to test and characterize thermal precipitator sampling efficiency and particle loss.,,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p28,0,"SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 22 9.0 TRACEABILITY MATRIX A matrix identifying the ancestry of each requirement identified in this document and the method employed to verify them can be found in SAME-PLN-005, Verification Plan and Engineering Requirements Document. Software requirements that do not contain the word ‘shall’ do not require verification. See SAME-PLN-006, Software Management and Assurance Plan, for an overall description of verification and verification methods.",,False
PSI-102,Reference Data,PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-102/Reference Data/PSI-102_Reference Data_SAME_Software Req Document.pdf,p29,0,SAME-DOC-004 Rev D 23 10.0 ACRONYMNS AND ABBREVIATIONS DACU Data Acquisition Control Unit DIO Digital Input/Output ECW Experiment Control Word EDLF ESTEC Data Link Format ESTEC European Space Research & Technology Centre FTP File Transfer Protocol GN2 Nitrogen GRC Glenn Research Center H&S Health and Status I/O Input - Output ISS International Space Station LAN Local Area Network LRT Low Rate Telemetry MDM Multiplexer/De-Mutiplexer MLC Microgravity Science Glovebox Laptop Computer MLCS Microgravity Science Glovebox Laptop Computer Server MSFC Marshall Space Flight Center MSG Microgravity Science Glovebox POIC Payload Operations Integration Center PDRP Payload Display Review Panel PSRP Payload Safety Review Panel SAME Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment SAMS Space Acceleration Measurement System SC Sample Carousel SPLC Standard Payload Computer SRD Science Requirements Document STS Space Transportation System (eg. Space Shuttle) TCP/IP Transfer Control Protocol/Internet Protocol TEM Transmission Electron Microscope TSC Telescience Support Center UIP Utility Interface Panel VSO Voltage Sensitive Orifice WV Work Volumer,,False
