PSI-106,metadata,PSI-106_metadata_PSI-174_SLICE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-106/metadata/PSI-106_metadata_PSI-174_SLICE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,0,"ONTOLOGY SOURCE REFERENCE Term Source Name EFO PSI Term Source File http://data.bioontology.org/ontologies/EFO https://psi.nasa.gov/ Term Source Version 132 1.0 Term Source Description Experimental Factor Ontology User defined terms INVESTIGATION Investigation Identifier Investigation Title Investigation Description Investigation Submission Date Investigation Public Release Date INVESTIGATION PUBLICATIONS Investigation PubMed ID Investigation Publication DOI Investigation Publication Author List Investigation Publication Title Investigation Publication Status Investigation Publication Status Term Accession Number Investigation Publication Status Term Source REF INVESTIGATION CONTACTS Investigation Person Last Name Investigation Person First Name Investigation Person Mid Initials Investigation Person Email Investigation Person Phone Investigation Person Fax Investigation Person Address Investigation Person Affiliation Investigation Person Roles Investigation Person Roles Term Accession Number Investigation Person Roles Term Source REF STUDY Study Identifier PSI-106 Study Title Structure and Liftoff In Combustion Experiment Study Description Study Submission Date 18-Sep-2024 Study Public Release Date 18-Sep-2024 Study File Name s_PSI-106.txt Comment[Acknowledgments] Comment[Approach] The SLICE apparatus consisted of a fan-driven flow duct, exchangeable burner tubes, and integrated sensors to measure flow rates and temperature.",,False
PSI-106,metadata,PSI-106_metadata_PSI-174_SLICE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-106/metadata/PSI-106_metadata_PSI-174_SLICE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,1,"This setup used several fuels such as methane, ethylene, and nitrogen-diluted mixtures. Flames were then manually ignited and observed under controlled coflow air conditions. A high resolution camera captured still images while sensor data was overlaid on these images. Calibration tests for airflow velocity, soot measurement, and the fans were conducted to ensure the accuracy of the results. More than 4,000 high-resolution images were captured across 13 testing experiments. Data was then analyzed to correlate flame structure with stability with flow dynamics and fuel properties. Comment[DOI] 10.60555/pm12-rg61 Comment[Data Source Accession] Comment[Data Source Link] Comment[Experiment Hardware] Comment[Experiment Hardware Link] Comment[Flight Platform] International Space Station (ISS) Comment[Funding] Comment[Hypothesis] The hypothesis is that the structure and stability of laminar diffusion flames in microgravity are significantly influenced by burner diameter, coflow velocity, and fuel composition and are fundamentally different than flames observed on Earth. Comment[Identifiers] Comment[Investigation Acronym] SLICE Comment[Investigation End Date] Comment[Investigation Start Date] Comment[Managing NASA Center] Glenn Research Center (GRC) Comment[Mission End] Comment[Mission Link] Comment[Mission Name] Comment[Mission Start] Comment[Objective] ""Objective:",,False
PSI-106,metadata,PSI-106_metadata_PSI-174_SLICE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-106/metadata/PSI-106_metadata_PSI-174_SLICE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,2,"The objective of this investigation is to better understand the structure and stability of laminar diffusion flames in microgravity. Specific objectives of this investigation were: 1. To investigate the structure of laminar diffusion flames in microgravity to understand the stabilizing mechanisms at the flames base. 2. Determine the liftoff velocity limits for different fuels and burner diameters. 3. Evaluate the impact of fuel dilution with nitrogen on soot formation and flame stability. 4. Quantify the effects of microgravity on flame length and radiative heat loss compared to normal gravity conditions. 5. Validate numerical models for flame dynamics, soot production, and thermal radiation in non-premixed flames."" Comment[Project Identifier] Comment[Project Link] Comment[Project Title] Comment[Project Type] Microgravity Investigation Comment[Proposal Title] Comment[Research Area] Combustion Science Comment[Research Impacts] ""1. Improved numerical models for flame behavior lead to more efficient combustion technologies on Earth. 2. Understanding soot formation aids in designing combustion systems with minimum emissions. 3. Insights into flame stability and behavior to enhance fire safety. 4.",,False
PSI-106,metadata,PSI-106_metadata_PSI-174_SLICE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-106/metadata/PSI-106_metadata_PSI-174_SLICE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,3,"Findings will inform the development of cleaner, more efficient engines and industrial burners."" Comment[Space Program] National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) Comment[Study Grant Number] Comment[SubResearch Area] STUDY DESIGN DESCRIPTORS Study Design Type fuel dilution inlet velocity Diffusion Flame Fuels Study Design Type Term Accession Number https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ Study Design Type Term Source REF PSI PSI PSI PSI STUDY PUBLICATIONS Study PubMed ID Study Publication DOI 10.1016/j.proci.2014.05.138 10.1080/00102202.2022.2041621 10.1016/j.proci.2014.05.064 10.1016/j.combustflame.2016.02.012 Study Publication Author List Cao S., Ma B., Bennett B.A.V., Giassi D., Stocker D.P., Takahashi F., Long M.B., Smooke M.D. Dobbins Richard R., Tinjero Jesse, Squeo Joseph, Zhao Xinyu, Hall Robert J., Colket Meredith B., Long Marshall B., Smooke Mitchell D. S Cao, BAV Bennett, B Ma, D Giassi, DP Stocker, F Takahashi, MB Long, MD Smooke Ma Bin, Cao Su, Giassi Davide, Stocker Dennis P., Takahashi Fumiaki, Bennett Beth Anne V., Smooke Mitchell D., Long Marshall B. Giassi Davide, Cao Su, Bennett Beth Anne V., Stocker Dennis P., Takahashi Fumiaki, Smooke Mitchell D., Long Marshall B. Study Publication Title A computational and experimental study of coflow laminar methane/air diffusion flames:",,False
