PSI-107,Reports,PSI-107_Reports_SPICE_30-DayPostFlightReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reports/PSI-107_Reports_SPICE_30-DayPostFlightReport.pdf,p9,2,"Fire in Freefall, San Diego, CA, 2001, Ch. 3, pp. 83 – 182. [24] B.-H. Jeon, O. Fujita, Y. Nakamura, H. Ito, J. Thermal Science Technol. 2 (2007) 281 – 290. [25] K.-C. Lin, G. M. Faeth, AIAA J.37 (1999) 750 – 765. [26] K.–C. Lin, G.M. Faeth, P.B. Sunderland, D.L. Urban, Z.–G. Yuan, Combust. Flame 116 (1999) 415 – 431. [27] Z. Dai, G.M. Faeth, Proc. Combust. Inst. 28 (2000) 2085 – 2092. [28] D.B. Spalding, Combustion and Mass Transfer, Pergamon, New York, 1979, pp. 185 – 195. [29] S. Mahalingam, J. H. Ferziger, B. J. Cantwell, Combust. Flame 82 (1990) 231 – 234.",,False
PSI-107,Reports,PSI-107_Reports_SPICE_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reports/PSI-107_Reports_SPICE_FinalReport.pdf,p1,0,"i Smoke Point in Co-flow Experiment (SPICE) Laminar Smoke Point Properties of Non-buoyant Round-Jet Diffusion Flames by Dr. David L. Urban 1 NASA Glenn Research Center, [address removed] Professor Peter B. Sunderland 2 University of Maryland, [address removed] and Dr. Zeng-Guang Yuan 3 National Center for Space Exploration Research, [address removed] Final Report 2 December 2011 Need to show PI, Co-I and PS? ----------------------------------------- 1 Branch Chief, Combustion and Reacting Systems Branch, [address removed], MS 77-5. 2 Prof>>>>> 3 Staff Scientist, [address removed], MS 110-3.",,False
PSI-107,Reports,PSI-107_Reports_SPICE_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reports/PSI-107_Reports_SPICE_FinalReport.pdf,p2,0,"ii Smoke Point in Co-flow Experiment (SPICE) Abstract The laminar smoke point is the condition (and the luminous flame length) where a flame begins to emit soot. Smoke points indicate the sooting propensity of a fuel in diffusion flames. In normal gravity, laminar smoke points have been measured for many gaseous and condensed fuels. Laminar smoke points have been found to correlate with soot volume fractions and radiative loss fractions of turbulent diffusion flames. SPICE project measured laminar smoke points in non) buoyant laminar jet diffusion flames in co)flowing air aboard the International Space Station. Fifty)two smoke points were found for ethylene, propane, propylene, and propylene/nitrogen mixtures. Burner diameters were 0.41, 0.76, and 1.6 mm and co)flow velocities varied from 5.4 – 65 cm/s. These flames afford extensive control over residence time via variations in dilution, burner diameter, and co)flow velocity. Increasing co)flow velocity decreases luminous flame lengths, but moves smoke points to higher fuel flow rates and longer luminous flame lengths. Smaller burners have longer smoke points for co)flowing flames. These trends can be explained qualitatively by considering total residence time effects. The luminous flame lengths generally correlate with fuel mass flow rate divided by stoichiometric mixture fraction, although they increase slightly as air velocity decreases.",,False
PSI-107,Reports,PSI-107_Reports_SPICE_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reports/PSI-107_Reports_SPICE_FinalReport.pdf,p2,1,"The measured smoke point lengths were found to correlate with burner diameter to the –0.91 power times coflow velocity to the 0.41 power. The measurements allow estimates of pseudo threshold soot indices (TSI) for microgravity diffusion flames, indicating an unusually high sooting propensity for propane in microgravity. A model of residence times in coflowing flames is developed, and although residence time helps explain many of the observations, it does not correlate the measured smoke points. The measurements yielded estimates of sooting propensities of the present fuels in microgravity diffusion flames. Acknowledgments The authors acknowledge G.M. Faeth for conceiving this work, the SPICE engineering team, and the astronauts that conducted the tests (M. Barratt, E.M. Fincke, S.H. Magnus, and K. Wakata). The SPICE project was conducted through the ISS Exploration Research Project of the Exploration Technology Development Program. The support of this program is also acknowledged. Certain commercial entities, equipment, or materials may be identified in this document in order to describe an experimental procedure or concept adequately. Such identification is neither intended to imply recommendation or endorsement by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, nor intended to imply that the entities, materials, or equipment are necessarily the best available for the purpose.",,False
