PSI-107,Presentations,PSI-107_Presentations_Laminar Smoke Points in Coflow Measured Aboard ISS.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Presentations/PSI-107_Presentations_Laminar Smoke Points in Coflow Measured Aboard ISS.pdf,p1,0,"48th AIAA Aerospace Sciences Meeting Orlando, FL, January 6, 2010 Laminar Smoke Points in CoflowLaminar Smoke Points in Coflow Measured Aboard the International Space Measured Aboard the International Space StationStation K. T. Dotson1, P.B. Sunderland1, Z -G Yuan2 D L Urban2 Z. G. Yuan , D.L. Urban 1University of Maryland, Department of Fire Protection Engineering 2NASA Glenn Research Center Acknowledgments Gerard M. Faeth SC 1 SPICE Engineering Team ISS Crew: M. Barratt, E.M. Fincke, S.H. Magnus, K. Wakata Supported by NASA cooperative agreement NNX07AO91A",,False
PSI-107,Presentations,PSI-107_Presentations_Laminar Smoke Points in Coflow Measured Aboard ISS.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Presentations/PSI-107_Presentations_Laminar Smoke Points in Coflow Measured Aboard ISS.pdf,p2,0,"MotivationMotivation • Soot – Pollutant, carcinogen, contributes to glacial melting • Smoke point (SP) measures sooting propensity. – Luminous flame length before smoke emission. – Correlates w/ soot volume fraction and radiative loss fraction. • Prediction: Non-buoyant flames don’t have smoke points. – Residence time would not scale with flow rate. •Smoke points found aboard parabolic aircraft and space shuttle.pp p • It is believed that residence time influences smoking behavior. Use coflow to control residence time 2 ԟ Use coflow to control residence time.",,False
