PSI-107,Presentations,PSI-107_Presentations_Laminar Smoke Points in Coflow Measured Aboard ISS.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Presentations/PSI-107_Presentations_Laminar Smoke Points in Coflow Measured Aboard ISS.pdf,p23,0,"ConclusionsConclusions Over the presently tested range of fuel, ua, and d: • Smoke point increases with increasing coflow velocitySmoke point increases with increasing coflow velocity. • Smoke point decreases with increasing burner diameter. • Fuel sooting propensities agree with those of buoyant flames. – Propane < Ethylene < 50% Propylene < 75% Propylene < Propylene • Ethylene less sooty than propane for 0.76 mm burner. (appears as a different TSI for propane) • LSP scales with fuel mass flow rate. – Consistent with simplified theory. 23 – Second order effect: Longer LSP for low ua / uf . – Coflow theory neglects radial velocity, v.",,False
