PSI-107,Raw Data,PSI-107_Raw Data_SPICE Image Organization Guide.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Raw Data/PSI-107_Raw Data_SPICE Image Organization Guide.docx,all,0,"Spice Image Organization Guide The file “Spice Flight Test Data II.xls” is where the specifics of each data/image point and the associated image reference numbers are collected. The folder “Data Images xxx” holds the images for tests run on that day of the year (xxx is the number of the day of the year -- January 1, 2009 is 001, January 2, 2009 is 002, February 1, 2009 is 032, February 14, 2009 is 045, etc.). “Spice Flight Test data II.xls” contains this information in column A “GMT date”. The MM/DD/YY date is time stamped on the .png downlink frame grabs and in the contact sheet. The folder “Downlink frame grabs” has images pulled directly from the operations live feed. There are many collected including of the astronaut outside the test facility. The images capturing the flames at test conditions have been renamed in the format “xxx TP yy vlcsnap-zzzzzzz.png” where xxx is the GMT date (column A), yy is the test number (column H) and zzzzzzz is the “# in filename” in column AC of “Spice Flight Test data II.xls”. Some of these files are not collected here but may be found as jpegs. “Spice_Flight_Flamelength.xls” is a spreadsheet of the calibration of flame lengths from select data points and calibration values. This is only in folder “Data image 045”.",,False
PSI-107,Raw Data,PSI-107_Raw Data_SPICE Image Organization Guide.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Raw Data/PSI-107_Raw Data_SPICE Image Organization Guide.docx,all,1,"The folder “SPICE_GMT045_2009_16bit_Tiffs” contains color still images taken inside the test facility with file name numbers matching column AD, “NIKON DIMS Exchange File No.”, in “Spice Flight Test data II.xls”. These are only in “Data images 045”. For dates up to and including 055, the folder “SPICE_GMTxxx_2009_Contact Sheets” contains contact sheets of the image together with the name from column AD ,“NIKON DIMS Exchange File No.”, and the relevant imaging details; camera, exposure, f-stop, exposure mode, metering mode, focal length and AF mode. These are all collected together in “SPICE_GMTxxx_2009_NikonD100_PictureBook.ppt”. The collected picture books are available up through files for GMT 062. From GMT 136 through 174, this collection is a .pdf file called “SPICE_GMT2009_xxx_ContactSheet.pdf” where xxx again refers to the numbered date. The folder “SPICE_GMTxxx_2009_jpegs” contains the color still jpeg images identified by the names in column AD, “NIKON DIMS Exchange File No.”. Details of the flame condition (columns I, J, K, L, M, N, R, S, T and U) and imaging conditions (columns AF and AG) may be found for select trios of these images with matching flame conditions but different camera settings in the file “Spice Flight Test data II.xls”. If it is not included in the spreadsheet, check the contact sheets detailed above. The folder “SPICE_GMTxxx_2009_NEF” contains color still Nikon raw file images identified by the names in column AD, “NIKON DIMS Exchange File No.”.",,False
PSI-107,Raw Data,PSI-107_Raw Data_SPICE Image Organization Guide.docx,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Raw Data/PSI-107_Raw Data_SPICE Image Organization Guide.docx,all,2,Folder Data Image 052 contains “GMT052nikon_propy.xls” that is a comparison of the color saturation ratios for 75% propylene flames on different diameter nozzles.,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE-MEM-010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE-MEM-010.pdf,p1,0,"SPICE-MEM-010 Page 1 of 2 Memorandum SPICE-MEM-010 TO: Tibor Lorik, SPICE Project Lead, ZIN Technologies, Inc. FROM: Kevin Magee, Project Engineer, ZIN Technologies, Inc. SUBJECT: SPICE Flow Duct Velocity Measurements DATE: July 6, 2009 ______________________________________________________________ This Memo addresses the SPICE flow duct velocity measurements, which were performed by Kevin Magee on the flight hardware from June 16-24, 2008. A hot wire anemometer probe was used to measure the air velocity at various points within the flow duct. This probe was passed into the duct through a special multi-piece top window with probe ports at the desired locations. Unused ports were taped shut on the inside and outside to prevent leakage and minimize flow disturbances. The window itself was assembled and sealed with Kapton tape around its perimeter. The probe used was a TSI, Inc., Model 1261A miniature boundary layer probe (S/N# 012077) with a T1.5 (coated tungsten platinum) wire and a 56 ohm control resistor to give a 260oC operating temperature. Its calibration was performed in a free jet facility on April 28, 2006.",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE-MEM-010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE-MEM-010.pdf,p1,1,"Measurements were taken at two axial stations of 13 and 56 mm as measured by the permanent scale bonded to the floor of the duct. These locations are where the tip of the probe was located, not where the ports were located since the probe curves forward into the air flow. At each axial station, measurements were taken at three width locations: the duct centerline, 20 mm off the centerline towards the back wall (called the “inside” position), and 20 mm off the centerline towards the front window (called the “outside” position). At each width position, measurements were taken at three heights: 19, 36, and 57 mm off the floor of the duct. It should be noted that the 36 mm height corresponds to the height of the nozzle, which was installed for the tests. However, no flow out of the nozzle was done during the tests. All locations are shown in Figure 1. At each measured position, the anemometer probe’s velocity readings were recorded for FAN dial settings of 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, and 10. The FAN and AIR readings were also noted. Plots were then created to compare the probe velocity, AIR reading, and FAN reading for the different FAN dial settings. Velocity profile plots were also generated at the various axial stations in both width and height. These plots and the raw data can be found in the spreadsheets at the following location. (Z:) Zindata1\zinapp\Shares\MRDOC\60107_086_SPP\SPICE\Duct Flow Data",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE-MEM-010.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE-MEM-010.pdf,p2,0,SPICE-MEM-010 Page 2 of 2 Figure 1 - SPICE Flow Duct Measurement Locations,,False
PSI-107,Raw Data,PSI-107_Raw Data_SPICE_FuelFlow_EngineeringUnitControl.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Raw Data/PSI-107_Raw Data_SPICE_FuelFlow_EngineeringUnitControl.pdf,p1,0,1 8 9 15 The 15-­‐Pin D connector to MFC Original condi:on 15-­‐Pin D connector from control box 15-­‐Pin D connector to MFC 15-­‐Pin D connector from control box 15-­‐Pin D connector to MFC Inser:on Connec:on with inser:on D connector from control box D connector to MFC 1 2 5 6 7 8 11 12 15 Wire/Pin #: Red wires go to voltage meter Inser:on Connec:on with inser:on D connector from control box D connector to MFC Original condi:on Pictures Diagram 1. Only 9 pins are used. 2. Voltage of pin 2 and 8 are measured with pin 15 as ground. 3. The set point of MFC can be controlled with the control box or with an external voltage supplier by switching wire 8 (dashed end) to diﬀerent sources.,,False
PSI-107,Raw Data,PSI-107_Raw Data_SPICE_FuelFlow_EngineeringUnitControl.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Raw Data/PSI-107_Raw Data_SPICE_FuelFlow_EngineeringUnitControl.pdf,p2,0,"Electrical Connections Chapter Three: Overview 34 15-Pin Type “D” Connector Table 8 lists the pinout of the 15-pin Type “D” connector for a mass flow controller. 15-Pin Type “D” Connector Pinout Pin Assignment Pin Assignment 1MKS Test Point*9No Connection 2Flow Signal Output (0 to +5 VDC) 10 Optional Input* 3Valve Close* (TTL low) 11 Signal Common 4 Valve Open* (TTL low) 12 Signal Common 5 Power Supply Common 13 No Connection 6-15 VDC14No Connection 7 +15 VDC 15 Chassis Ground 8 Set Point Input* (0 to +5 VDC) * For an MFC only, No Connection for an MFM Table 8: 15-Pin Type “D” Connector Pinout Note 1. The “No Connection” pin assignment refers to a pin with no internal connection. 2. The 0 to 5 VDC flow signal output comes from pin 2 and is referenced to pin 12 (signal common). 3. Any appropriate 0 to 5 VDC input signal of less than 20K ohm source impedance referenced to pin 12 can be used to supply a set point signal to pin 8.",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_60054MAFH000-C SPICE Experiment Assembly.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_60054MAFH000-C SPICE Experiment Assembly.pdf,p1,0,"Description: Top-level assembly drawing (very long R3 sheet, content rotated 90°) of the Smoke Points in Co-flow Experiment (SPICE) hardware: housing with main/top windows, fan housing with fan and flow straightener, burner tubes, ignitor, gas supply (bottle, regulator, mass flow controller, filter, quick connect), soot screen, scale arm and captive brackets. Shows Section A-A, Section B-B / C-C, Section D-D and a view labelled ""FAN HOUSING ASSEMBLY REMOVED FOR CLARITY"", all with item balloons (H0xx = drawn parts, C nn = commercial parts). Followed by a drawing list and two commercial parts lists. Stamp ""AUTHORIZED BY ____ DATE ____ NASA DR. NO."" (blank).",,True
