PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p1,0,"4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 1 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml OBJECTIVE: Reset the SPICE Power/Video Unit firmware to return the SPICE display to normal (NOR) mode in support of SLICE (Structure and Liftoff in Combustion Experiment). To do this, the Power/Video Unit will be removed from MSG so the unit’s Video Titler Box can be opened to scrape off conformal coating from circuit board pins (five of ten) to make temporary jumper connections for advancing through the setup menu. TOOLS: 8.5"" Handrail, if needed 3"" Common Tip Screwdriver, if needed 4 mm Hex Head, 1/4"" Drive Driver Handle, 1/4"" Drive Vacuum Cleaner Fiberglass Stick P/N 182SO020 or Leatherman Tool, crew preference ISS Pin Kit P/N SJG33110644-301 Pin to Socket Test Jumper Lead, 24 in. P/N SEG33112714-307 (five) Static Wrist Tether Fine Point Sharpie D2Xs Camera MATERIALS: Kapton Tape 4X4 Ziplock Bag or equivalent 1. MSG/SLICE CONFIGURATION 1.1 Install 8.5"" Handrail onto MSG Facility left seat track at position G2 (if required). CAUTION Failure to remove the SAMS-II Seat Track Device/SE prior to Work Volume extension could result in MSG and/or SAMS hardware damage. LAB1S2 1.2 Remove the SAMS-II Seat Track Device/SE from the top of the MSG fwd seat track (upper left corner of MSG rack) by loosening thumbscrews (two). Tmpry stow with cable still attached. 1.3 While holding 8.5"" Handrail with left hand, use right hand to:",,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p1,1,"Push knob, slide Core Facility to fully extended position, ensure MSG Loading Ports are completely visible. Pull knob (secure locking mechanism). ✓Core Facility Position securely locked ✓Each PDC LED – On (green) 1.4 ✓All operator rings, watches removed",,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p2,0,"4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 2 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml 1.5 Remove: Left MSG Loading Port Left, right front MSG Blank Glove Ring Assembly (two) CMP 1.6 EXPERIMENT POWER OUTLETS ✓OUTLET1 +28V, +12V, +5V ON LED (three) – OFF 2. SPICE POWER/VIDEO UNIT REMOVAL WV 2.1 Install lens cap on SPICE Video Camera Assembly. 2.2 Install lens cap on Still Camera. 2.3 Still Camera disconnections: SPICE Feedthrough Cable P43 ←|→ SHUTTER J43 SPICE Power Cable P41 ←|→ J41 SPICE Still Camera Cable P42 ←|→ J42 2.4 Pull and hold plunger on Still Camera. Remove Still Camera from SPICE Still Camera Mount and temporarily stow. 2.5 Loosen fasteners (three) on the SPICE Still Camera Mount. Slide the Still Camera Mount 2” left before lifting to avoid damage to the SPICE Experiment Assembly. Temporarily stow SPICE Still Camera Mount. 2.6 MSG W301 Video Extension cable disconnections : P2 ←|→ VIDEO OUT J15 (SPICE Power/Video Unit) P2 ←|→ STILLOUT J17 (SPICE Power/Video Unit) 2.7 SPICE Video Cable P14 ←|→ VIDEO IN J14 (SPICE Power/Video Unit) 2.8 SPICE Still Camera Cable P16 ←|→ STILL IN J16 (SPICE Power/Video Unit) Temporarily stow SPICE Still Camera Cable. 2.9 SPICE Power Cable P1 ←|→ EXP. POWER J1 (SPICE Power/Video Unit) 2.10 SPICE Feedthrough Cable P12 ←|→ SERIAL IN J12 (SPICE Power/Video Unit) [3"" Common Tip Screwdriver, if necessary] 2.11 Loosen fasteners (two) and remove SPICE Power/Video Unit from WV rear wall. 2.12 SPICE Power/Video Unit P303 ←|→ EXP.",,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p2,1,OUTLET 1 J303 (WV rear wall) 2.13 Remove SPICE Power/Video Unit from MSG WV.,,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p3,0,"4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 3 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml NOTE The SPICE Power/Video Unit consists of a Video Titler Box (right) and a Power Conditioning Box (left) which are mounted on an aluminum adapter plate. 3. SPICE POWER/VIDEO UNIT CHECK AND CONFORMAL COATING REMOVAL 3.1 Setup ISS Vacuum near SPICE Power/Video Unit. 3.2 Don Static Wrist Tether WARNING The interior of the Video Titler Box was not intended to be accessed during on-orbit operation and may contain sharp edges. 3.3 Remove the front cover of the Video Titler Box (SPICE Video/Power Unit right side) and place non-captive fasteners (two) in 4X4 Ziplock Bag [4 mm Hex Head, Driver Handle, 1/4"" Drive]. Use ISS Vacuum to suction loose particles, if needed. 3.4 Take 3 to 5 digital images of the Video Titler Box Circuit Board, with focus on the firmware version label [D2Xs Camera].",,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p4,0,4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 4 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml Figure 1. Video Titler Circuit Board,,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p5,0,"4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 5 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml 3.5 ✓POIC with firmware version, copyright date, confirm button batteries present, and confirm conformal coating on 10-pin connectors. (Refer to Figure 1) If no conformal coating on 10-pin connectors, Go to step 3.7 Figure 2. Pin Location 3.6 Remove conformal coating along the entire length of Pins 1, 2, 5, 6 and 8 [Fiberglass Stick or Leatherman Tool, crew preference]. Refer to Figure 2. Use ISS Vacuum to suction loose particles. 3.7 Label both ends of a Pin to Socket Test Jumper Lead with the number 1 [Kapton Tape, Fine Point Sharpie]. Repeat with additional leads for number 2, 5, 6 and 8. Refer to Figure 2. 3.8 If socket exteriors not insulated, apply Kapton Tape to socket end of the Pin to Socket Test Jumper Lead. Repeat for all Pin to Socket Test Jumper Leads.",,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p6,0,"4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 6 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml 3.9 Connect one end of each Pin to Socket Test Jumper Lead (five) to its matching pin (1, 2, 5, 6 and 8). Keep the pins separated so they do not touch each other. 4. SPICE POWER/VIDEO UNIT INSTALLATION 4.1 SPICE Power/Video Unit P303 →|← EXP. OUTLET 1 J303 (WV rear wall) 4.2 Secure SPICE Power/Video Unit to WV rear wall: SPICE Power/Video Unit A1 to WV rear wall B1 SPICE Power/Video Unit A2 to WV rear wall D1 4.3 SPICE Feedthrough Cable P12 →|← SERIAL IN J12 (SPICE Power/Video Unit) [3"" Common Tip Screwdriver, if necessary] 4.4 SPICE Power Cable P1 →|← EXP. POWER J1 (SPICE Power/Video Unit) 4.5 SPICE Video Cable P14 →|← VIDEO IN J14 (SPICE Power/Video Unit) 4.6 MSG W301 Video Extension cable P2 →|← VIDEO OUT J15 (SPICE Power/Video Unit) MSG W301 Video Extension cable P2 →|← STILLOUT J17 (SPICE Power/Video Unit) WV 4.7 Remove lens cap on SPICE Video Camera Assembly, place on MSG Long Velcro Strip. SPICE Exp. Assy 4.8 SCALE lever → Push to hard stop Video Drawer 4.9 sw MAIN POWER → ON ✓MAIN POWER ON LED – On (green) sw MONITORS 1,2 → ON ✓MONITORS 1,2 ON LED (two) – On (green) sw CAMERAS 1,2 → ON ✓CAMERAS 1,2 ON LED (two) – On (green) CMP 4.10 EXPERIMENT POWER OUTLETS sw OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V → ON ✓OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V ON LED (three) – On (green)",,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p7,0,"4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 7 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml 5. JUMPER CONNECTIONS TO RETURN OVERLAY TO NOR MODE Figure 3. MODE=HEX NOTE Continuous contact will quickly advance through menu. If advance past desired selection, then simply cycle back to selection. 5.1 Make jumper connection for pin 6 and 2 (one simo momentary connection) to enter titler setup menu. ✓MODE = HEX (displayed on MSG Video Monitor 1, Refer to Figure 3) *********************************************************************** If MODE=NOR ✓SAVE THIS SETUP = YES Go to step 5.6 If SAVE THIS SETUP=NO make jumper connection for pin 8 and 2 twenty-two times to scroll through titler setup menu to select SAVE THIS SETUP . ✓SAVE THIS SETUP is flashing Go to step 5.5 ********************** ***********************************************************************",,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p8,0,"4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 8 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml 5.2 Make jumper connection for pin 8 and 2 eight times to scroll through titler setup menu to select MODE . ✓MODE is flashing Figure 4. MODE=NOR 5.3 Make jumper connection for pin 5 and 1 (one simo momentary connection). ✓MODE = NOR (Refer to Figure 4) If SAVE THIS SETUP = YES Go to step 5.6 5.4 Make jumper connection for pin 8 and 2 fourteen times to scroll through titler setup menu to select SAVE THIS SETUP . ✓SAVE THIS SETUP is flashing 5.5 Make jumper connection for pin 5 and 1 (one simo momentary connection) to select YES. ✓SAVE THIS SETUP = YES 5.6 Make jumper connections for pin 5 and 2 (one simo momentary connection) to exit the titler setup menu. SPICE-like display may be visible, but will likely be garbled. CMP 5.7 EXPERIMENT POWER OUTLETS sw OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V → Off",,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p9,0,"4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 9 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml ✓OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V ON LED (three) – Off (green) 5.8 Wait 30 seconds. 5.9 EXPERIMENT POWER OUTLETS sw OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V → On ✓OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V ON LED (three) – On (green) Figure 5. MSG Video Monitor displaying Flow Conditions NOTE SPICE does not drive the video overlay during the first 5 seconds, during which time the data overlay might be garbled. 5.10 ✓MSG Video Monitor 1 has good digital data overlay of flow conditions (Refer to Figure 5) 5.11 Repeat step 5.7through step 5.10two times to cycle power. CMP 5.12 EXPERIMENT POWER OUTLETS sw OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V → Off ✓OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V ON LED (three) – Off (green) 5.13 Remove Pin to Socket Test Jumper Lead (five) from SPICE Power/Video Unit. Trash Kapton Tape. 5.14 Install Video Titler Box front cover, secure with fasteners from 4X4 Ziplock Bag [4mm Hex Head, Driver Handle, 1/4"" Drive].",,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p10,0,"4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 10 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml 5.15 Doff Static Wrist Tether. 6. MSG/SLICE RECONFIGURATION WV 6.1 Install SPICE Still Camera Mount: SPICE Still Camera Mount B1 to MSG cold plate B1 SPICE Still Camera Mount B2 to MSG cold plate B2 SPICE Experiment Assembly C1 to SPICE Still Camera Mount 6.2 Route all cables behind SPICE Still Camera Mount. 6.3 Install Still Camera onto SPICE Still Camera Mount. ✓Spring Plunger fully engaged (See Figure 7) 6.4 SPICE Power Cable P41 →|← J41 (Still Camera, right side) SPICE Still Camera Cable P42 →|← J42 (Still Camera, right side) SPICE Still Camera Cable P16 →|← STILL IN J16 (SPICE Power/Video Unit) SPICE Feedthrough Cable P43 →|← SHUTTER J43 (Still Camera, left side) Tape SPICE Feedthrough Cable connection P43 to J43 [Kapton Tape]. Tether Cables out of the camera field of view as necessary using MSG Velcro Cable Ties. 6.5 Remove lens cap on Still Camera, place on MSG Long Velcro Strip. CMP 6.6 EXPERIMENT POWER OUTLETS sw OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V → On ✓OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V ON LED (three) – On (green) WV 6.7 Pull and hold plunger on Still Camera. Remove Still Camera from SPICE Still Camera Mount. 6.8 Rotate Still Camera power switch → ON",,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p11,0,"4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 11 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml Figure 6. Still Camera Rear View 6.9 Set NEF (RAW) Recording to Lossless Compression: pb MENU → Press (Figure 6) ✓SHOOTING MENU Screen is displayed *********************************************************************** If SHOOTING MENU not displayed, Select the SHOOTING MENU Screen (UP/DOWN Multi-Selector (MS) Button) Press the RIGHT MS Button to select. ********* *********************************************************************** Highlight NEF (RAW) RECORDING (UP/DOWN MS Button). pb RIGHT MS Button → Press (to select) pb UP/DOWN → Press (to select TYPE) pb RIGHT MS Button → Press (to select) pb UP/DOWN → Press (to select LOSSLESS COMPRESSED) pb OK → Press (to accept)",,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p12,0,"4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 12 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml Figure 7. METER Selection Switch 6.10 Rotate METER Selection Switch to middle position (Full MATRIX). See Figure 7. 6.11 Install Still Camera onto SPICE Still Camera Mount. ✓Spring Plunger fully engaged SPICE Control Box Assy 6.12 pb SHUTTER → Press firmly and HOLD for 7 seconds. 6.13 Perform {2.007 SLICE FLASH CARD EXCHANGE}, steps 2-4 (US PODF: MSG SLICE) CMP 6.14 EXPERIMENT POWER OUTLETS sw OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V → Off ✓OUTLET 1 +5V, +12V, +28V ON LED (three) – Off (green) 6.15 Install: Left MSG Loading Port CMP 6.16 ✓HEALTH & STATUS WV PORTL LED – On (green) ✓HEALTH & STATUS WV PORTR LED – On (green) 6.17 sw FUNCTIONS AIR CIRC. → On",,False
PSI-107,Reference Data,PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Reference Data/PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf,p13,0,"4.001 SPICE VIDEO OVERLAY RESET (mgolall000251/IMPACT) Page 13 of 13 pages 1Feb12 MGUEMSGSLCEC001.xml 6.18 While holding 8.5"" Handrail with left hand, use right hand to: Push knob, slide Core Facility to retracted position. Pull knob (secure locking mechanism). ✓Core Facility position securely locked LAB1S2 6.19 Install the SAMS-II Seat Track Device/SE on the top of the MSG fwd seat track (upper left corner of MSG rack) by tightening thumbscrews (two). 7. FINAL STOW 7.1 Copy D2Xs images to SSC for downlink and notify POIC 7.2 Stow: 3"" Common Tip Screwdriver 4 mm Hex Head, 1/4"" Drive Driver Handle, 1/4"" Drive 4X4 Ziplock Bag Fiberglass Stick, if used Leatherman Tool,if used ISS Pin Kit Pin to Socket Test Jumper Lead (five) ISS Vacuum Static Wrist Tether Fine Point Sharpie D2Xs Camera Kapton Tape Table 2. Procedure Hazard Control List Hazard Report Number Hazard Control Number Procedure Step(s) STD-SLICE-01 14/14b.1 Go to step 1.6, step 5.12 MSG-G1 4/4.1 Go to step 1.6 MSG-G4 2/2.2 Go to step 1.3, step 6.18",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p1,0,"SPICE-RPT-002 Smoke Point in Coflow Experiment (SPICE) Science Verification Report May, 2007 Prepared For: National Aeronautics and Space Administration John H. Glenn Research Center at Lewis Field Exploration Systems [address removed] Prepared By: ZIN Technologies, Incorporated [address removed] Under Contract NAS3-99155, 60107-DO-086",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p2,0,"SPICE-RPT-002 ii APPROVALS Prepared by: Date: Tibor Lorik ZIN Technologies, Inc. SPICE Project Lead Reviewed by: Date: Dr. David L. Urban NASA GRC Glovebox Investigator Accepted by: Date: Robert W. Hawersaat NASA GRC SPICE Project Manager Accepted by: Date: Professor Peter Sunderland University of Maryland Co-Investigator Accepted by: Date: Dr. Zeng-Guang Yuan National Center for Space Exploration Research Project Scientist",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p3,0,SPICE-RPT-002 iii REVISIONS Revision Letter Publication Date Change Description Approval -- 5/08 Baseline Release,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p4,0,SPICE-RPT-002 iv TABLE OF CONTENTS 1.0 INTRODUCTION ....................................................................................................................... 1 2.0 VERIFICATION CLOSURE RECORD ................................................................................... 1 3.0 CURRENT VERIFICATION STATUS .................................................................................... 1 APPENDIX A – SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEETS .................................. A-1,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p5,0,"SPICE-RPT-002 1 1.0 INTRODUCTION The purpose of this document is to identify the science requirements for the SPICE Experiment manifested to fly on the ISS. The science requirements are traced to the Science Requirements Document. 2.0 VERIFICATION CLOSURE RECORD Appendix A contains each Science Verification Sheet, which identifies the science requirement, verification method, and verification closure summary. The verification summary identifies the SPICE project document that addresses the verification closure. All SPICE project documents are under configuration control and available upon request. This document will be updated periodically to reflect the closure status of the verifications. 3.0 CURRENT VERIFICATION STATUS There are 18 verification requirements. All requirements have been closed. One verification, Radiometer accuracy is not compliant.",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p6,0,ole_aInlpILLN ]IM ]llaj`et ] o_eaj_a namqenaiajp ranebe_]pekj odaapo,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p7,0,SPICE-RPT-002 A-2 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION : 1 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT Round gas jet diffusion flame in a co-flowing oxidizer stream IMPLEMENTATION Configuration is that the gas flow nozzle is in the flow duct and the gas and air flow are in the same direction. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: INS Verify via design drawings that the experiment assembly is configured such that the gas flow is in the same direction as the air flow. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT Drawing 60054MAFH000 shows that the gas nozzle is inside the flow duct and the gas exit is in the same direction as the airflow. This has also been verified via functional testing.,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p8,0,SPICE-RPT-002 A-3 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION : 2 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT Experiment is to operate at ambient pressure. IMPLEMENTATION Experiment will operate in the MSG on the ISS VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: ANALYSIS Verify that the MSG facility maintains ambient pressure. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT MSFC-ICD-3270 is the Interface Control Document for SPICE in the MSG. The ICD states that the MSG will nominally operate at a negative pressure of 1.3 mBar relative to ISS cabin Pressure.,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p9,0,"SPICE-RPT-002 A-4 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 3 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT The ignition of the gases will be via Hot wire. IMPLEMENTATION The SPICE flight hardware will contain a Hot Wire Ignitor. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: INS Verify that the design drawings include a hot wire ignitor. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT Drawing 60054MAFH060, Ignitor Assembly includes an ignitor wire, item H063 with part number 60054MDFH063.",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p10,0,SPICE-RPT-002 A-5 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 4 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT The Fuel Flow shall be controlled from 0 to 500 SCCM N2 equivalent flow. IMPLEMENTATION The design will include a mass flow controller with the setpoint input controlled with a potentiometer. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: INS Verify that the design drawings include a mass flow controller with the correct flow range capability and that the setpoint potentiometer allows the MFC to be set in this range. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT The Flow Controller is from MKS and is Model 179A. The specification sheet states an accuracy of ±1% of Full Scale and a resolution of 0.1% of Full Scale. The mass flow controller was calibrated on 2/27/08 from 0 – 500 SCCM N2.,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p11,0,SPICE-RPT-002 A-6 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 5 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT The Oxidizer Velocity range is from 5 to 50 cm/s IMPLEMENTATION The Oxidizer Velocity and Co-Flow Velocity are the same measurement and they will be measured by an Air Velocity Transducer made by TSI with Model number 8475. The output is routed to an amplifier for scaling with the output monitored by the A/D Converter on the Tattletale board. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: INS Verify that the Velocity Probe is capable of supporting the required range and accuracy. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT The specification sheet for the Velocity Probe states that the velocity range is from 0.05 to 2.54 m/s (5 -254 cm/s) which is within the range of 5 – 50 cm/s required. The accuracy is stated to be ±3% of the reading ±1% of the selected range. This is within the required accuracy. Inspection of the data acquired and stored in the Experiment Assembly shows that 4 samples per second are acquired.,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p12,0,"SPICE-RPT-002 A-7 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 6 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT The burner tube nozzle diameters are manually selectable to be 0.4, 0.8, or 1.6 mm. IMPLEMENTATION The design will include the ability for the crew to replace the burner tubes on- orbit and that hardware includes the correct nozzle diameters. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: INS Verify that the design drawings include the burner tubes with the correct diameters. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT Drawing 60054MAFH201, Component Stowage Case Assembly includes the 0.4 mm Diameter Burner Tube, 60054MDFH034, the 0.8 mm Diameter Burner Tube, 60054MDFH033, and the 1.6 mm Diameter Burner Tube, 60054MDFH032. The design of the Burner Tube is such that the tube slides on to the burner tube holder located in the Experiment Assembly. This operation has been demonstrated.",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p13,0,SPICE-RPT-002 A-8 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 7 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT The Flame Length shall be from 5 to 100 mm. IMPLEMENTATION A still camera and video camera as well as the scale bar in the flow duct will be used to measure the flame length. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: DEMO Verify that the design includes the ability to image the flame. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT Testing has been performed that verifies images and video of the flame can be acquired.,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p14,0,"SPICE-RPT-002 A-9 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 8 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT The fuel mass flow shall be measured with an accuracy of 10% of the reading at a sampling rate of 4 Hz, and over a range of 25 – 500 SCCM. IMPLEMENTATION The mass flow controller provides an output signal of 0 – 5 VDC which is proportional to the mass flow rate. This signal will be monitored by the A/D Converter on the Tattletale board. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: TEST Verify that the MFC is capable of supporting the required range and accuracy. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT Inspection of the data acquired and stored in the Experiment Assembly shows that 4 samples per second are acquired. The MFC has been calibrated with a maximum error of 0.32 % of FS.",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p15,0,"SPICE-RPT-002 A-10 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 9 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT The Co-Flow Fan Voltage shall be measured with an accuracy of 10% of the reading, at a sampling rate of 4 Hz, and over a range of 0.5 – 12 volts. IMPLEMENTATION The Co-Flow Fan Voltage signal will be monitored via a voltage divider proportional to the voltage in the range of 0.5 to 12 VDC. This signal will be monitored by the A/D Converter on the Tattletale board. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: TEST Perform a functional test per SPICE-TST-006. The functional test will verify that when the Fan Speed potentiometer is set at its lowest and highest setting that the measurement is not out of range and that the change is as expected. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT The fan voltage minimum is 5.5 volts due to the safety requirement that the fan provide airflow at all times even when the Fan Flow Potentiometer is set to zero. The measurement displayed on the video monitor and in the data ranges from 55 to 139. This represents 5.5 volts – 13.9 volts going to the fan. Inspection of the data acquired and stored in the Experiment Assembly shows that 4 samples per second are acquired.",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p16,0,"SPICE-RPT-002 A-11 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 10 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT The Co-Flow Velocity shall be measured with an accuracy of 10% of the reading, at a sampling rate of 4 Hz, and over a range of 5 – 50 cm/s. IMPLEMENTATION The Oxidizer Velocity and Co-Flow Velocity are the same measurement and they will be measured by an Air Velocity Transducer made by TSI with Model number 8475. The output is routed to an amplifier for scaling with the output monitored by the A/D Converter on the Tattletale board. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: INS Verify that the Velocity Probe is capable of supporting the required range and accuracy. If time permits the probe will be calibrated. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT The specification sheet for the Velocity Probe states that the velocity range is from 0.05 to 2.54 m/s (5 -254 cm/s) which is within the range of 5 – 50 cm/s required. The fact that the fan is always on limits the lower range that the Velocity Probe reaches. The measurement displayed on the video monitor and in the data ranges from 15 to 53 CFM for minimum fan velocity to maximum fan velocity. The accuracy is stated to be ±3% of the reading ±1% of the selected range. This is within the required accuracy. Inspection of the data acquired and stored in the Experiment Assembly shows that 4 samples per second are acquired.",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p17,0,"SPICE-RPT-002 A-12 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 11 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT The Flame Radiation shall be measured with an accuracy of 10% of the reading, at a sampling rate of 4 Hz, and at a wavelength from visible to near infra-red. IMPLEMENTATION The Flame Radiation measurement will be made by a Thermopile Detector made by ORIEL with Model number 71768. The output is routed to an amplifier for scaling and amplification with the output monitored by the A/D Converter on the Tattletale board. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: INS Verify that the Radiometer is capable of measuring from visible to near infra- red. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED – NOT COMPLIANT The specification sheet for the Thermopile Detector states that the CAF2 coated version present in the SPICE system has a spectral range of 0.13 to 11 µm. This range encompasses the visible and near infrared as required. The Radiometer is highly susceptible to EMI. Accurate readings can only be done by comparing the value prior to flame initiation and during flame activity. Inspection of the data acquired and stored in the Experiment Assembly shows that 4 samples per second are acquired.",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p18,0,SPICE-RPT-002 A-13 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 12 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT A color video of the flame shall be recorded. IMPLEMENTATION The SPICE hardware consists of a Color CCTV Camera made by Panasonic with Model number WV-CP654. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: TEST Perform a functional test per SPICE-TST-006. The functional test will verify that the system records the region of the flame. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT Testing has shown that the flame will be in the field of view.,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p19,0,SPICE-RPT-002 A-14 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION : 13 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT The field of view of the video image shall include the last 10 mm of the Burner Tube to the end of the duct. IMPLEMENTATION This camera is mounted such that its field of view is of the interior of the Experiment Assembly. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: TEST Perform an interface and functional test in the MSG engineering unit or similar mechanical interface with the SPICE flight hardware. The functional test will verify that the system records the region of interest. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT Testing in the MSG Engineering Unit has shown that the region of interest will be in the field of view.,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p20,0,"SPICE-RPT-002 A-15 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION : 14 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT Acquire High Resolution Still images of the Flame. A maximum of 3 images are required per test run. IMPLEMENTATION The SPICE hardware consists of a Nikon D100 Digital SLR Camera. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: INS Perform an inspection of the camera specifications to verify that the camera is of sufficient resolution. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT The Nikon D100 camera is capable of storing the digital images on removable disk. The camera image is a 12-bit RGB CCD with 6 Mpixels. The camera is capable of 3000 x 200, 2240 x 1488, or 1504 x 1000 pixels image size. These specifications clearly shows that the camera is high resolution.",,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p21,0,SPICE-RPT-002 A-16 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 15 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT The High resolution color images shall be stored in raw (uncompressed) mode. IMPLEMENTATION Select a camera that can store files in raw uncompressed format. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: INS Verify that the NIKON D100 camera can store the images in raw pixel count format rather than being compressed into a format such as JPEG. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT The NIKON D100 camera manual states that the camera is capable of storing images in raw format.,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p22,0,SPICE-RPT-002 A-17 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 16 ERD PARA: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT Fuel flow is adjustable by the crew. IMPLEMENTATION A potentiometer that will control the set point flow rate of the Mass Flow Controller will be available to the crew external to the MSG. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: TEST Perform a functional test per SPICE-TST-006. The functional test will verify that when the Fuel Flow potentiometer is adjusted that the fuel flow rate can be adjusted. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT Testing has shown that the fuel flow potentiometer located on the Control Box outside the WV allows the crew to change the flow rate.,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p23,0,SPICE-RPT-002 A-18 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 17 ERD PARAGRAPH: VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT The video science data will be downlinked real-time for the initial runs and prior to performing the next series of runs after the initial runs. IMPLEMENTATION The Color Video signal is routed to the MSG facility Monitor and Video Recorder. The MSG facility is capable of downlinking this data as ISS bandwidth permits. The project will submit a resource request for this downlink data. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: INS Verify that the ICD includes the resource request. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT The ICD states: SPICE experiment video is required to be downlinked as soon as possible either during or immediately after each flame test. The digital photos from the SPICE still camera are required to be downlinked as soon as possible after selected flame tests for ground confirmation prior to proceeding with the following test.,,False
PSI-107,Engineering Documents,PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Engineering Documents/PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf,p24,0,"SPICE-RPT-002 A-19 SPICE SCIENCE REQUIREMENT VERIFICATION SHEET VERIFICATION: 18 ERD PARAGRAPH: 4.6.1.a.2 VERIFICATION LEAD(S): REQUIREMENT Available fuels are methane, propane, ethylene, mixture of 50% propylene and 50% nitrogen, mixture of 75% propylene and 25% nitrogen, and pure propylene. IMPLEMENTATION The project will assemble, test, and launch 12 gas bottles with two bottles of each type of gas or gas mixture. VERIFICATION VERIFICATION METHOD: INS Verify that the ICD includes the upmass resource. VERIFICATION STATUS: CLOSED - COMPLIANT The ICD includes Table I which shows 12 bottles in 2 stowage cases.",,False
PSI-107,Experimental table,PSI-107_Experimental table_Spice.csv,data/raw/files/PSI-107/Experimental table/PSI-107_Experimental table_Spice.csv,all,0,sheet PSI-107_Experimental table_Spice.csv: 470 non-empty rows header: GMT date | GMT ignition | GMT Flame Off | Fuel | Nozzle (mm) | Fuel Rate (sccm) | Fuel Rate (mg/s) | HC Fuel Rate (mg/s) | Air Rate (cm/s) row 1: 45 | 14:37:08 | 14:48:03 | Ethylene | 1.6002 | 103.8 | 1.971 | 1.971 | 36.39 row 2: 45 | 14:37:08 | 14:48:03 | Ethylene | 1.6002 | 103.8 | 1.971 | 1.971 | 36.39 row 3: 45 | 14:50:07 | 14:52:11 | Ethylene | 1.6002 | 51 | 0.968 | 0.968 | 40.15 row 4: 45 | 14:50:07 | 14:52:11 | Ethylene | 1.6002 | 51.6 | 0.98 | 0.98 | 40.15 row 5: 45 | 15:35:44 | 15:37:00 | Ethylene | 1.6002 | 138 | 2.62 | 2.62 | 57.08,,False
PSI-107,metadata,PSI-107_metadata_PSI-176_SPICE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-107/metadata/PSI-107_metadata_PSI-176_SPICE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,0,"ONTOLOGY SOURCE REFERENCE Term Source Name EFO PSI Term Source File http://data.bioontology.org/ontologies/EFO https://psi.nasa.gov/ Term Source Version 132 1.0 Term Source Description Experimental Factor Ontology User defined terms INVESTIGATION Investigation Identifier Investigation Title Investigation Description Investigation Submission Date Investigation Public Release Date INVESTIGATION PUBLICATIONS Investigation PubMed ID Investigation Publication DOI Investigation Publication Author List Investigation Publication Title Investigation Publication Status Investigation Publication Status Term Accession Number Investigation Publication Status Term Source REF INVESTIGATION CONTACTS Investigation Person Last Name Investigation Person First Name Investigation Person Mid Initials Investigation Person Email Investigation Person Phone Investigation Person Fax Investigation Person Address Investigation Person Affiliation Investigation Person Roles Investigation Person Roles Term Accession Number Investigation Person Roles Term Source REF STUDY Study Identifier PSI-107 Study Title Smoke Point In Co-flow Experiment Study Description Study Submission Date 18-Sep-2024 Study Public Release Date 18-Sep-2024 Study File Name s_PSI-107.txt Comment[Acknowledgments] Comment[Approach] The experimental setup in the microgravity science glovebox consisted of an apparatus with a flow duct with integrated nozzles, cameras, and fuel delivery systems.",,False
PSI-107,metadata,PSI-107_metadata_PSI-176_SPICE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-107/metadata/PSI-107_metadata_PSI-176_SPICE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,1,"Fuels tested included methane, ethylene, propane, propylene, and propylene mixtures. Each flame was ignited in a controlled co-flow air environment, with burner diameters of 0.41 mm, 0.76 mm, and 1.6 mm and co-flow velocities ranging from 5.4 cm/s to 65 cm/s. Fuel flow rates were adjusted to identify the smoke point, marked by the flame's transition to emitting soot. Data such as flame lengths were recorded using still and video cameras. Measurements included flame height, radiative heat losses, and co-flow air velocity distributions. In total 526 flames were studied. Comment[DOI] 10.60555/9kbh-e962 Comment[Data Source Accession] Comment[Data Source Link] Comment[Experiment Hardware] Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) Comment[Experiment Hardware Link] https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Human_and_Robotic_Exploration/Columbus/Microgravity_Science_Glovebox Comment[Flight Platform] International Space Station (ISS) Comment[Funding] Comment[Hypothesis] The research hypothesizes that by minimizing gravity-driven transport phenomena scientists can study the laminar smoke-point properties of non-buoyant jet diffusion flames. Flame properties are expected to differ significantly from those observed under normal gravity.",,False
PSI-107,metadata,PSI-107_metadata_PSI-176_SPICE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-107/metadata/PSI-107_metadata_PSI-176_SPICE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,2,"Comment[Identifiers] Comment[Investigation Acronym] SPICE Comment[Investigation End Date] Comment[Investigation Start Date] Comment[Managing NASA Center] Glenn Research Center (GRC) Comment[Mission End] Comment[Mission Link] Comment[Mission Name] Comment[Mission Start] Comment[Objective] ""The objective of SPICE is to better understand the complex process of combustion by observing flames in a microgravity environment aboard the International Space Station (ISS) and analogous ground experiments. Specific objectives were: (1) Identify the laminar smoke-point properties of non-buoyant jet diffusion flames under various co-flow conditions in microgravity. (2) Determine the effects of burner diameter and co-flow velocity on the luminous flame lengths and smoke points. (3) Quantify the sooting propensity of different fuels, such as ethylene, propane, and propylene mixtures. (4) Validate the correlation between luminous flame shape and residence time under microgravity conditions. (5) Investigate the impact of air and fuel flow rate variations on smoke point phenomena in space."" Comment[Project Identifier] Comment[Project Link] Comment[Project Title] Comment[Project Type] Microgravity Investigation Comment[Proposal Title] Comment[Research Area] Combustion Science Comment[Research Impacts] ""(1) Will improve our understanding of soot formation mechanisms and help design safer spacecraft environments.",,False
PSI-107,metadata,PSI-107_metadata_PSI-176_SPICE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-107/metadata/PSI-107_metadata_PSI-176_SPICE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,3,"(2) Will contribute to refining our computational models for predicting flame behaviors under non-buoyant conditions. (3) Will enhance our knowledge of soot generation (4) Could possibly provide insights to better fuel formulations and combustion processes."" Comment[Space Program] National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) Comment[Study Grant Number] Comment[SubResearch Area] Fire Safety STUDY DESIGN DESCRIPTORS Study Design Type Soot Laminar diffusion flames Emisssions butane-lighter flame Study Design Type Term Accession Number https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ Study Design Type Term Source REF PSI PSI PSI PSI STUDY PUBLICATIONS Study PubMed ID Study Publication DOI 10.2514/6.2010-1105 10.1007/978-1-4020-8682-3_22 10.2514/6.2006-941 10.1080/00102202.2022.2041621 10.1016/j.combustflame.2004.12.009 10.1016/S0010-2180(98)00100-X 10.1016/j.firesaf.2011.08.002 Study Publication Author List Dotson Keenan, Sunderland Peter, Yuan Zeng-Guang, Urban David Sunderland Peter B., Urban David L., Nayagam Vedha RA Spivey, LB Jeter, C Vonk Spivey Reggie, Luz Paul Dobbins Richard R., Tinjero Jesse, Squeo Joseph, Zhao Xinyu, Hall Robert J., Colket Meredith B., Long Marshall B., Smooke Mitchell D. Aalburg C., Diez F.J., Faeth G.M., Sunderland P.B., Urban D.L., Yuan Z.-G. LIN K, FAETH G, SUNDERLAND P, URBAN D, YUAN Z KT Dotson Dotson K.T., Sunderland P.B., Yuan Z.-G., Urban D.L.",,False
PSI-107,metadata,PSI-107_metadata_PSI-176_SPICE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-107/metadata/PSI-107_metadata_PSI-176_SPICE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,4,"Study Publication Title Laminar Smoke Points in Coflow Measured Aboard the International Space Station Scaling of Gas-Jet Flame Lengths in Elevated Gravity The Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG), a Resource for Gravity-Dependent Phenomena Research on the International Space Station (ISS) The Microgravity Science Glovebox as a Platform for Exploration Research on the International Space Station A Combined Experimental and Computational Study of Soot Formation in Normal and Microgravity Conditions Shapes of nonbuoyant round hydrocarbon-fueled laminar-jet diffusion flames in still air Shapes of nonbuoyant round luminous hydrocarbon/air laminar jet diffusion flames Smoke Points of Microgravity and Normal Gravity Coflow Diffusion Laminar smoke points of coflowing flames in microgravity Study Publication Status published published published published published published published published published Study Publication Status Term Accession Number http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0001796 Study Publication Status Term Source REF EFO EFO EFO EFO EFO EFO EFO EFO Comment[Study Publication Link] http://dx.doi.org/10.2514/6.2010-1105 https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4020-8682-3_22",,False
PSI-107,metadata,PSI-107_metadata_PSI-176_SPICE.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-107/metadata/PSI-107_metadata_PSI-176_SPICE.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,5,http://ntrs.nasa.gov/archive/nasa/casi.ntrs.nasa.gov/20070013892_2007012126.pdf http://dx.doi.org/10.2514/6.2006-941 http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/00102202.2022.2041621 http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2004.12.009 http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/s0010-2180(98)00100-x https://drum.lib.umd.edu/items/e1cf9d1d-2c54-475a-a201-a57d3874011b https://doi.org/10.1016/j.firesaf.2011.08.002 STUDY FACTORS Study Factor Name Study Factor Type Study Factor Type Term Accession Number Study Factor Type Term Source REF STUDY ASSAYS Study Assay File Name Study Assay Measurement Type Study Assay Measurement Type Term Accession Number Study Assay Measurement Type Term Source REF Study Assay Technology Type Study Assay Technology Type Term Accession Number Study Assay Technology Type Term Source REF Study Assay Technology Platform STUDY PROTOCOLS Study Protocol Name Study Protocol Type Study Protocol Type Term Accession Number Study Protocol Type Term Source REF Study Protocol Description Study Protocol URI Study Protocol Version Study Protocol Parameters Name Study Protocol Parameters Name Term Accession Number Study Protocol Parameters Name Term Source REF Study Protocol Components Name Study Protocol Components Type Study Protocol Components Type Term Accession Number Study Protocol Components Type Term Source REF STUDY CONTACTS Study Person Last Name Sunderland Urban Study Person First Name Peter David Study Person Mid Initials Study Person Email pbs@umd.edu david.urban@nasa.gov Study Person,,False
