PSI-115,metadata,PSI-115_metadata_PSI-185_Utilization of the S.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-115/metadata/PSI-115_metadata_PSI-185_Utilization of the S.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,0,ONTOLOGY SOURCE REFERENCE Term Source Name EFO PSI Term Source File http://data.bioontology.org/ontologies/EFO https://psi.nasa.gov/ Term Source Version 132 1.0 Term Source Description Experimental Factor Ontology User defined terms INVESTIGATION Investigation Identifier Investigation Title Investigation Description Investigation Submission Date Investigation Public Release Date INVESTIGATION PUBLICATIONS Investigation PubMed ID Investigation Publication DOI Investigation Publication Author List Investigation Publication Title Investigation Publication Status Investigation Publication Status Term Accession Number Investigation Publication Status Term Source REF INVESTIGATION CONTACTS Investigation Person Last Name Investigation Person First Name Investigation Person Mid Initials Investigation Person Email Investigation Person Phone Investigation Person Fax Investigation Person Address Investigation Person Affiliation Investigation Person Roles Investigation Person Roles Term Accession Number Investigation Person Roles Term Source REF STUDY Study Identifier PSI-115 Study Title Utilization of the Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment Data for Advanced Modeling and Simulation of Smoke Generation in Micro-Gravity Study Description Study Submission Date 18-Sep-2024 Study Public Release Date 18-Sep-2024 Study File Name s_PSI-115.txt Comment[Acknowledgments] Comment[Approach] The formation and growth of dibutyl-phthalate (DBP) particles in Earth gravity and microgravity environments,,False
PSI-115,metadata,PSI-115_metadata_PSI-185_Utilization of the S.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-115/metadata/PSI-115_metadata_PSI-185_Utilization of the S.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,1,"was simulated. Direct numerical simulation via a Navier-Stokes nodal approach was utlilized. The smoke originates from pyrolysis vapor expelled from a heated sample in a geometry similar to that considered in the SAME experiments. This study considered two cross-flow velocities of sufficiently low speed that buoyancy cannot be neglected in the presence of gravity. Simulations are presented to assess the dependence of particle size distributions on thermophoresis, particle settling, and flow geometry. The final report for this investigation introduces the simulation and modeling methodology, followed by a description of the flow geometry and the parameter space under consideration.The differences in the wake geometry attributable to buoyancy for the lower of two flow speeds was examined. Instantaneous scalar fields were used to describe particle accumulation and growth in the buoyant and non-buoyant wakes that form downstream of the hot vapor source. Comment[DOI] 10.60555/q0v5-1v28 Comment[Data Source Accession] Comment[Data Source Link] Comment[Experiment Hardware] Comment[Experiment Hardware Link] Comment[Flight Platform] Not Applicable Comment[Funding] NNX17AF08A Comment[Hypothesis] Without buoyancy, mixing and heat transfer are reduced, the time-temperature history of the combustion vapors (or particle precursors) and particle trajectories are significantly altered, changing the composition of the smoke.",,False
PSI-115,metadata,PSI-115_metadata_PSI-185_Utilization of the S.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-115/metadata/PSI-115_metadata_PSI-185_Utilization of the S.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,2,"The chemical composition of the burning material also affects the characteristics of the smoke generated. Comment[Identifiers] Comment[Investigation Acronym] Comment[Investigation End Date] 12-31-2019 Comment[Investigation Start Date] 01-30-2017 Comment[Managing NASA Center] Glenn Research Center (GRC) Comment[Mission End] Comment[Mission Link] Comment[Mission Name] Comment[Mission Start] Comment[Objective] The objective of this investigation was to use numerical simulation to explore the influence of gravity on pyrolysis smoke growth from a fundamental perspective. The intention of this effort is to quantify the differences between microgravity and Earth gravity smoke, and to identify the fluid dynamic mechanisms that cause the differences. Comment[Project Identifier] Comment[Project Link] Comment[Project Title] Comment[Project Type] Ground Investigation Comment[Proposal Title] Comment[Research Area] Combustion Science Comment[Research Impacts] Models and tools that characterize particles generated as smoke can be used to ascertain how temperature, gravity, and material properties affect the smoke produced. This would directly lead to better smoke detection devices and prevent fires that can lead to loss of materiel and loss of life.",,False
PSI-115,metadata,PSI-115_metadata_PSI-185_Utilization of the S.zip,data/raw/files/PSI-115/metadata/PSI-115_metadata_PSI-185_Utilization of the S.zip,member i_Investigation.txt,3,"Comment[Space Program] National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA), PSI Grant Comment[Study Grant Number] Comment[SubResearch Area] Smoke Aerosol, Combustion Simulation STUDY DESIGN DESCRIPTORS Study Design Type SAME Data Aerosol Smoke Generation Nanoparticles Navier-Stokes nodal approach Ground-based research Numerical Simulation Dibutyl pthalate Particle settling thermophoresis Study Design Type Term Accession Number https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ https://psi.nasa.gov/ Study Design Type Term Source REF PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI PSI STUDY PUBLICATIONS Study PubMed ID Study Publication DOI Study Publication Author List Study Publication Title Study Publication Status Study Publication Status Term Accession Number Study Publication Status Term Source REF STUDY FACTORS Study Factor Name Study Factor Type Study Factor Type Term Accession Number Study Factor Type Term Source REF STUDY ASSAYS Study Assay File Name Study Assay Measurement Type Study Assay Measurement Type Term Accession Number Study Assay Measurement Type Term Source REF Study Assay Technology Type Study Assay Technology Type Term Accession Number Study Assay Technology Type Term Source REF Study Assay Technology Platform STUDY PROTOCOLS Study Protocol Name Study Protocol Type Study Protocol Type Term Accession Number Study Protocol Type Term Source REF",,False
