PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p1,0,"Final August 15, 2020 UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY Sean C. Garrick NASA Grant NNX17AF08A FinalReport",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p2,0,"Executive Summary 3 Introduction 4 Formulation 5 Fluid dynamics Particle ﬁelds Nucleation Coagulation Condensation and evaporation DBP vapor balance Results 9 Flow conﬁguration Physical conditions Numerical speciﬁcations Physical Assumptions Fluid ﬂow, temperature, and mass fraction Particle nucleation, number density, and size Effect of particle settling Effect of thermophoresis Effect of heated vapor source orientation Effect of ﬂow speed Smoke visualization Summary 15 UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 2 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p3,0,"1 Executive Summary This work examines how microgravity conditions on spacecraft change the development of smoke aerosol relative to smoke on Earth. We perform simulations of particle formation and growth with the goal of capturing smoke dynamics similar to those realized in experiments performed aboard the International Space Station. The simulations provide detailed information about ﬂuid/thermal/particle interactions that cannot be acquired experimentally on orbit. A surrogate material, dibutyl pthalate, is expelled from a hot surface in a rectangular chamber while ambient air is blown through the domain, convecting the developing smoke downstream. The study considers two ﬂow rates, two geometric con- ﬁgurations, and the isolated effects of gravitational particle settling and thermophoresis. In Earth gravity, buoyancy produces asymmetries in all ﬁelds of interest and ampliﬁes vapor-to-particle conversion near room temperature surfaces at the upper boundary of the simulation domain. Gravitational settling re- moves large particles from the aerosol, limiting the maximum particle size to ∼ 40 µm and the mode particle size to ∼ 5 µm. Without buoyancy or settling, the microgravity smoke plume is spatially symmet- ric and contains very large particles (> 40 µm). A bimodal particle size distribution results in microgravity when prominent recirculation occurs behind the hot vapor source.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p3,1,"The distribution is comprised of large particles (> 20 µm) associated with long residency time, vapor rich, high temperature recirculation, and smaller particles (< 10 µm) that originate farther downstream of the hot vapor source. A distinct large particle mode does not appear in microgravity ﬂows that do not have prominent high temperature re- circulation, although the aerosol still contains large particles. Without recirculation, the mode particle diameter in microgravity is ∼ 10 µm, or approximately twice that of terrestrial smoke. Results show that future experiments should carefully consider and quantify surface interactions and the presence of hot, recirculating vapor, in order to control geometry-dominated features of the particle size distribution. A manuscript based on this work will be submitted to the journal Aerosol Science & Technology. The ﬁnal document, when accepted, will be provided to NASA. UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 3 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p4,0,"2 Introduction Gravity affects the evolution of aerosols by introducing buoyancy and particle settling. Buoyant convec- tion modiﬁes the time-temperature history of the aerosol, as well as the spatial distribution of particles, condensing vapor, and thermal energy. Gravity also causes the settling of large particles out of the aerosol, provided the chemistry and vapor concentrations allow for the generation of large particles with appreciable settling velocities. These factors combine to increase particle residency times in high temperature, vapor rich regions in microgravity systems relative to terrestrial or Earth gravity systems (Urban et al., 1998, 2005). As a result, smoke particles produced in microgravity tend to be larger than those produced on Earth (Ku et al., 1995, Urban et al., 1998). Measurements suggest microgravity smoke particles to be 2 − 8 times larger than terrestrial smoke particles (Urban et al., 2005). Moreover, large particles (not only smoke particles) persist in microgravity, with mass concentrations of particles with diameter > 100 µm measured at ten-times that of a nominal terrestrial environment (Liu et al., 1991). Total particulate mass concentrations are also more than ﬁve times greater in microgravity (Liu et al., 1991).",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p4,1,"Understanding gravitational effects is of practical interest because it assists in the design of mission-critical smoke detection systems for use in microgravity environments, including the Interna- tional Space Station (Mulholland et al., 2015); systems developed and calibrated for terrestrial use may not perform correctly in microgravity because of inappropriate particle size sensitivities (Urban et al., 2005). The present work uses numerical simulation to explore the inﬂuence of gravity on pyrolysis smoke growth from a fundamental perspective. We simulate the formation and growth of dibutyl-phthalate (DBP) particles in Earth gravity and microgravity environments. We utilize direct numerical simulation via a Navier-Stokes nodal approach. The smoke originates from pyrolysis vapor expelled from a heated sample in a geometry similar to that considered in the SAME experiments (Mulholland et al., 2015). The intention of this effort is to quantify the differences between microgravity and Earth gravity smoke, and to identify the ﬂuid dynamic mechanisms that cause the differences. This study considers two cross- ﬂow velocities of sufﬁciently low speed that buoyancy cannot be neglected in the presence of gravity. Simulations are presented to assess the dependence of particle size distributions on thermophoresis, particle settling, and ﬂow geometry. The paper is organized as follows:",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p4,2,"we introduce the simulation and modeling methodology, followed by a description of the ﬂow geometry and the parameter space under consideration; we then examine differences in the wake geometry attributable to buoyancy for the lower of two ﬂow speeds; next, we present and discuss instantaneous scalar ﬁelds as a means to describe particle accumulation and growth in the buoyant and non-buoyant wakes that form downstream of the hot vapor source; after developing an understanding of how the particle ﬁelds interact with the ﬂuid dynamics for the low speed cases, we consider the effects of thermophoresis, particle settling, ﬂow geometry, and the higher ﬂow speed; we conclude with a discussion of the results, summarize ﬁndings relevant to future experimentation, and outline directions for future work. UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 4 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p5,0,"3 Formulation 3.1 FLUID DYNAMICS The gaseous systems are described by the compressible mass, momentum, and energy equations, considered here in dimensionless form. Conservation of mass is given by ∂ρ⋆ ∂t⋆ + ∂ρ⋆u⋆ i ∂x⋆ i = 0, (1) where ρ⋆ is the density and u⋆ i is the velocity (superscript asterisks denote dimensionless variables and subscripts denote vector components). Conservation of momentum is given by ∂ρ⋆u⋆ i ∂t⋆ + ∂ρ⋆u⋆ ju⋆ i ∂x⋆ j = −∂P⋆ ∂x⋆ i + 1 Re ∂τ⋆ ij ∂x⋆ j + 1 Fr2 (ρ⋆ − 1), (2) where P⋆ is the pressure, Re is the Reynolds number, τ⋆ ij is the viscous stress tensor, and Fr is the Froude number. The viscous stress tensor is deﬁned by τ⋆ ij = µ⋆ ( ∂u⋆ j ∂x⋆ i + ∂u⋆ i ∂x⋆ j ) − 2µ⋆ 3 ∂u⋆ k ∂x⋆ k δij, (3) where µ⋆ is the dynamic viscosity and δij is the Kronecker delta. The ﬂuid is deﬁned as an ideal gas with the corresponding equation of state, P⋆ = ρ⋆ αR⋆ αT⋆, (4) where T⋆ is the temperature and R⋆ α is the gas constant of species α (summation is implied by repeated subscript indicies). Conservation of total energy is given by ∂ρ⋆E⋆ ∂t⋆ + ∂ρ⋆u⋆ jH⋆ ∂x⋆ j = µ⋆ (γ− 1)M 2RePr ∂q⋆ j ∂x⋆ j + 1 Re ∂u⋆ iτ⋆ ij ∂x⋆ j + ∂ ∂x⋆ j ( ρ⋆h⋆ α ReSc ∂Yα ∂x⋆ j ) , (5) where E⋆ is the speciﬁc total energy, H⋆ is the speciﬁc total enthalpy, γ is the ratio of speciﬁc heats, M is the Mach number, Pr is the Prandtl number, q⋆ j is the heat ﬂux, Sc is the Schmidt number, Yα is the mass fraction of species α, and h⋆ α is the speciﬁc enthalpy of species α.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p5,1,"The heat ﬂux is computed from Fourier’s law, q⋆ j = −k⋆∂T⋆ ∂x⋆ j , (6) where k⋆ is the thermal conductivity and T⋆ is the temperature. The speciﬁc enthalpy is computed according to h⋆ = Yα ( CpαT + hf α ) /ho, (7) where hf α is the enthalpy of formation of species α, ho is a reference enthalpy, and Cpα is the speciﬁc heat at constant pressure. The speciﬁc total energy is computed from E⋆ = (1 2uiui + Yα ( CvαT + ef α )) /ho, (8) where ef α is the energy of formation, and Cvα is the speciﬁc heat at constant volume. The speciﬁc total enthalpy H⋆ is analogously deﬁned by H⋆ = (1 2uiui + Yα ( CpαT + hf α )) /ho, (9) where Cpα is the speciﬁc heat at constant pressure. Species transport is given by ∂ρ⋆Yα ∂t⋆ + ∂ρ⋆Yαu⋆ j ∂x⋆ j = ∂ ∂x⋆ j ( ρ⋆ ReSc ∂Yα ∂x⋆ j ) + ˙ω, (10) where ˙ω accounts for the conversion of species α vapor to the particle phase via nucleation and con- densation. UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 5 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p6,0,"3.2 PARTICLE FIELDS Particles are transported in space by convection, diffusion, thermophoresis, and gravitational settling. Their size is affected by nucleation, coagulation, condensation, and evaporation. This work utilizes a nodal approximation to the general dynamic equation (Prakash et al., 2003), where the particle size distribution is discretized by a set of nodes k, each associated with a particle diameter dk. The spatio- temporal transport of each particle size node k is considered with respect to its mass fraction Yk = ρk/ρ, ∂ρ⋆Yk ∂t⋆ + ∂ρ⋆Yk ( u⋆ j + utf⋆ j,k + uss⋆ j,k ) ∂x⋆ j = ∂ ∂x⋆ j ( ρ⋆ ReSck ∂Yk ∂x⋆ j ) + ˙ωk, (11) where utf⋆ j,k is the j component of the thermophoretic velocity of particle node k, uss⋆ j,k is the j component of the Stokes settling velocity of particle node k, Sck is the particle Schmidt number, and ˙ωk is a source term that accounts for transport in size space (this term will be deﬁned in Section 2.6). The particle diffusivity is deﬁned by Dk = KbTCk 3πµdk , (12) where Kb is Boltzmann’s constant, Ck is the Cunningham correction factor, and dk is the diameter of particles in node k. The Cunningham correction factor is taken as Ck = 1 + Knk (1.142 + 0.558exp (−0.999/Knk)) (13) where Knk is the Knudsen number of particles in node k Knk = 2λ/dk.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p6,1,"(14) The mean free path in air λ is computed from λ = λo (Patm P )(T T1 )2 (T1 + Ts T + Ts ) (15) where Patm is the atmospheric pressure and the model parameters are Ts = 110.4K, T1 = 296.15K, and λo = 67.3nm (Otto et al., 1999). The Talbot equation deﬁnes the thermophoretic velocity, utf⋆ j,k = −ktf µ ρUoT ∂T ∂xj , (16) where ktf = 2.294Ck (k/kp + 2.2Knk) (1 + 3.493Knk) (1 + 2k/kp + 4.4Knk), (17) and kp is the thermal conductivity of the particulate phase (Pyyk¨onen and Jokiniemi, 2000, Talbot et al., 1980). The dimensionless Stokes settling velocity, uss⋆ j,k , is deﬁned by uss⋆ j,k = (ρc −ρ) gjd2 k 18µUo , (18) where gj is the gravitational acceleration in the j direction. The source term ˙ωk controls transport in size space due to nucleation, coagulation, condensation, and evaporation, ˙ωk = (ωN k + ωc k + ωs k) × Lomk Uoρo , (19) where mk is the mass of a particle in node k, ωN is the number density change due to nucleation, ωc is the number density change due to coagulation, and ωs is the number density change due to the combined effects of condensation and evaporation. UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 6 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p7,0,"3.3 NUCLEATION We consider the classical nucleation expression modiﬁed by a self-consistent correction proposed by Girshick and Chiu (1990). The nucleation rate is scaled by a material-speciﬁc enhancement factor, cnuc, resulting in a nucleation rate J deﬁned by J = cnuc (ρY )2 ρcmm √ 2σ πmm 1 S exp ( 3 √ 36πv2mσ kBT − πσd2 c 3kBT ) , (20) where dc is the critical diameter of nucleating particles given by dc = 4σvm kBT ln(S). (21) The nucleation rate depends on the mass mm and volume vm of a vapor molecule, the surface tension σ, and the saturation ratio S. Particles nucleating with size dc do not correspond exactly to any of the discretized size nodes. Following Prakash et al. (2003), the nucleated particles are placed in the node k with a diameter dk that is minimally larger than dc. This procedure results in the nucleation source term for node k, ωN k = d3 c d3 k J. (22) The nucleation source term for all other nodes is zero. 3.4 COAGULATION The rate of change of particles in size class k due to coagulation is deﬁned by ωk c = 1 2 Ns∑ i=2 Ns∑ j=2 χijkβijQiQj −Qk Ns∑ i=2 βikQi (23) where Qi is the number density of node i, βij is the collision frequency function corresponding to nodes i and j, and χijk is the mass splitting function deﬁned by (Prakash et al., 2003) χijk =    vk+1 −(vi+vj ) vk+1 −vk if vk ≤ vi + vj < vk+1 (vi+vj )−vk−1 vk−vk−1 if vk−1 ≤ vi + vj < vk 0 else.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p7,1,"(24) The Dahneke coefﬁcients are used to extend the near-continuum frequency function βco to all relevant values of Kn (Otto et al., 1999). The collision coefﬁcients are given by βij = βco 1 + KnD 1 + 2KnD + 2Kn2 D , (25) where βco is the near-continuum collision frequency function, βco = 2kBT 3µ ( v1/3 i + v1/3 j )( Ci v1/3 i + Cj v1/3 j ) . (26) The enhancement Knudesen number KnD is deﬁned as KnD = 4Dij cijdij , (27) where Dij = Di + Dj and dij = di + dj andcij = √ c2 i +c2 j. The mean molecular speedc is given by ck = √ 8kBT/πmk. UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 7 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p8,0,"3.5 CONDENSATION AND EVAPORATION The saturation monomer number density with respect to particles of size dk is computed according to Qs 1,k = Ps kbT exp ( 4σMW RTρcdk ) , (28) where Ps is the saturation pressure and MW is the molecular weight of the condensing species. Using this saturation concentration, the rate of change of particles in size class k due to condensation and evaporation is given by (Prakash et al., 2003) ωk s =    vm vk−vk−1 β1,k−1 ( Q1 −Qs 1,k−1 ) Qk−1 if Q1 >Qs 1,k−1 − vm vk+1 −vk β1,k+1 ( Q1 −Qs 1,k+1 ) Qk+1 if Q1 <Qs 1,k+1 − vm vk+1 −vk β1,k ( Q1 −Qs 1,k ) Qk if Q1 >Qs 1,k vm vk−vk−1 β1,k ( Q1 −Qs 1,k ) Qk if Q1 <Qs 1,k , (29) where Q1 is the number density of monomers and Qk is the number density of particles of size class k. 3.6 DBP VAPOR BALANCE Nucleation, condensation, and evaporation are interactions between the vapor and particle phases. The source term in the vapor transport equation, Eqn. (10), is given by ˙ω = − ( d3 c d3m J + N∑ k=2 vk vm ωk s ) × Lomm Uoρo (30) where the ﬁrst term on the right-hand-side is the sink due to nucleation and the second term on the right-hand-side is the source associated with condensation and evaporation (Prakash et al., 2003). UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 8 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p9,0,"4 Results 4.1 FLOW CONFIGURATION We simulate the formation and growth of DBP particles in air ﬂowing over a heated sample that expels DBP vapor. The simulation mimics the smoldering or pre-conﬂagration stage of a ﬁre studied in experi- ments performed aboard the ISS (Mulholland et al., 2015). A schematic of the computational domain is shown in Fig. 1. Room temperature air enters a long chamber and ﬂows over a heated rectangular sam- ple. Vapor released from the sample nucleates into particles that grow and are subsequently collected downstream for analysis. The ﬂow is laminar and steady, allowing the two-dimensional representation to present physical characteristics largely representative of the SAME experiments (Mulholland et al., 2015). DBP is used as the surrogate material because its properties are known and its use in modeling is well-established (unlike the materials used in the SAME experiments). 4.2 PHYSICAL CONDITIONS Constant ﬂuid properties for air and DBP are provided in Table 1, and variable DBP properties are provided in Table 2. The enhancement factor required for the nucleation expression, Eqn. (20), is deﬁned for DBP as cnuc = 3.2 × 10−4 (Nguyen et al., 1987, Pyyk¨onen and Jokiniemi, 2000). The thickness of the heated DBP source serves as the reference length scale, Lo = 0.0044 m (illustrated in Fig. 1), which is representative of the length scale in the SAME experiments (Mulholland et al., 2015).",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p9,1,"The air enters from the left with velocity Uo and temperature To = 300 K. We consider two inlet velocities: Uo = 0.08 m/s, the velocity considered in the SAME experiments, and a slightly faster ﬂow of Uo = 0.10 m/s. The upper and lower boundaries are impermeable, no-slip, iso-thermal surfaces (Twall = To). The heated rectangle is also no-slip, has a ﬁxed temperature of Ts = 473.2 K, and releases DBP vapor at a constant mass ﬂux of q′′ = 8.4 × 10−5 kg/m2 · s. The mass ﬂux has been selected to be representative of the mass loss data presented by Mulholland et al. (2015). Zero-derivative conditions are imposed for all species and particle ﬁelds at the inlet and outlet. The gravitational acceleration is considered at terrestrial (go = 9.81 m/s2) and microgravity (go = 0 m/s2) conditions. The inlet velocities and the gravitational accelerations considered represent four cases with varying Reynolds and Froude numbers. A summary of the simulations performed at the two ﬂow speeds and gravitational levels is given in Table 3. The ﬂags “EG” and “MG” identify the “Earth gravity” and “micro- gravity” conditions, while 1 and 2 identify the Uo = 0.08 m/s and Uo = 0.1 m/s conditions, respectively. Case 1MG is therefore deﬁned to have an inlet velocity of Uo = 0.08 m/s and go = 0 m/s2, corre- sponding to a Reynolds number Re = 22.3 and a Froude number Fr = ∞. In addition to the four base cases listed in Table 3, we examine the aerosol development sensitivity to:",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p9,2,"(1) particle settling, (2) ther- mophoresis, and (3) the ﬂow geometry. For this purpose, a series of additional cases, deﬁned in Table 3, are constructed by modifying the setup of Case 1MG and/or Case 1EG. Case 1EG-NS (where NS stands for “no settling”) is identical to Case 1EG, but the settling mechanism has been neglected. Case 1MG-NT (where NT stands for “no thermophoresis”) is identical to Case 1MG, but the thermophoresis transport mechanism has been neglected. Cases 1MG-R and 1EG-R (where R stands for “rotated”) are identical to Cases 1MG and 1EG, except the heated vapor source has been rotated 90◦. In all cases, data is collected and presented at a time of t = 7 seconds. 4.3 NUMERICAL SPECIFICATIONS The domain is non-uniformly discretized by 960 grid points in the x direction and 240 grid point in the y direction. The governing equations are solved on this computational mesh with a second-order MacCormack scheme. The particle size space is discretized by 45 particle nodes, with the volume of each successive node increased by a factor of 2.25, i.e. vk = 2.25 ×vk−1. The volume of the ﬁrst node is selected to be the volume of a DBP molecule, v1 = vm = 4.44 × 10−28 m3. Boundary conditions are UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 9 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p10,0,"implemented by considering the characteristic relations for the Euler equations accounting for gravity (Poinsot and Lele, 1992). The computational tool used for this work has been well-validated and used for prior studies of aerosols (Garrick, 2011, 2015, Garrick and Khakpour, 2004, Garrick et al., 2001, Khakpour and Garrick, 2003, Modem and Garrick, 2003). 4.4 PHYSICAL ASSUMPTIONS All assumptions utilized in this work are listed below. • From the perspective of thermal conductivity and viscosity, the condensing vapor phase is con- sidered sufﬁciently dilute such that µ and k are computed for air as a function of temperature and density (Kadoya et al., 1985). Both the air and the condensing vapor are considered calorically perfect gases with constant properties deﬁned in Table 1. • The Mach number M is only important for ﬂows with M & 0.3. Although the physics are insen- sitive to variation in Mach number when the Mach number is small, the ratio of sound speed to convective speed imposes a large computational burden. The computational challenge is often remedied with a Boussinesq approximation, but this approach is not valid in the presence of large density variations. Additionally, an elliptical solve for pressure is computationally intractable for the number of iterations required to resolve the particle time scales.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p10,1,"We instead use a scaled dynamic pressure as the reference pressure Po = ρo (c1Uo)2, such that P⋆ = ραRαT Po , where c1 is speciﬁed to reduce the effective speed of sound to 8 m/s. The ﬂow ﬁeld was found to be independent of c1 for the values used. • All particles are assumed to be spherical. • Condensed phase DBP properties are deﬁned by their continuum values. • There is no momentum exchange between the particles and the ﬂuid (the air/DBP vapor mixture); the particle velocity equals the ﬂuid velocity, augmented by the thermophoretic velocity and the stokes settling velocity. The stokes settling velocity is assumed to apply for all particles in the domain (including the large particles). • The ﬂuid and particles are in thermal equilibrium and have the same temperature. • Particle diffusion coefﬁcients decrease rapidly with diameter. This poses a numerical challenge due to the lack of a physical mechanism for diffusing numerical errors. For this reason, the mini- mum diffusion coefﬁcient considered in the particle mass fraction transport equations corresponds to the ﬁfth particle size node – any node greater than the ﬁfth node is assigned the particle diffu- sion coefﬁcient of the ﬁfth node. • The mass ﬂux of DPB from the heated rectangle is spatially uniform and all surfaces have spatially uniform temperature. • Particles that impact a solid surface “stick” with 100% efﬁciency, and are removed from the ﬂow.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p10,2,"• The aerosol does not achieve a rigorous steady-state because particle evolution continues (slowly) in regions of the ﬂow with recirculation, particularly behind the hot vapor source. The recirculation is characterized by high temperatures, long particle residency times, and the continuous intro- duction of additional vapor. The hydrodynamic and thermal ﬁelds, conversely, become steady after only a few seconds. Although a rigorous steady-state does not exist, all aerosol features develop and effectively stabilize shortly after the hydrodynamic ﬁelds. We therefore present all results at time t = 7 seconds, which is considered to be the effective steady-state. At his time, all ﬁelds are either steady, or changing at a rate that does not affect the conclusions drawn from the simulations. UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 10 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p11,0,"4.5 FLUID FLOW, TEMPERATURE, AND MASS FRACTION The velocity ﬁeld controls the convection of thermal energy, condensible species vapor, and particles. Vorticity ω, deﬁned as the curl of the velocity vector ω = ∇× u, (31) is positive in regions of the ﬂow where ﬂuid rotates counterclockwise, and is negative in regions where the ﬂuid rotates clockwise. The sign and magnitude of vorticity therefore identiﬁes the location, orienta- tion, and intensity of vortices and boundary layers. Figure 2 shows the steady dimensionless vorticity. Panel (a) shows Case 1MG and panel (b) shows Case 1EG. The grey rectangles represent the heated vapor source. Red ﬂuid rotates counterclockwise (positive vorticity) and blue ﬂuid rotates clockwise (negative vorticity). Immediately upon entering the channel, the ﬂuid at the walls is brought to rest by the no slip condition, signiﬁed by the large vorticity gradients in the upper and lower left corners of panels (a) and (b). As the ﬂuid travels downstream from left to right, boundary layers develop symmetrically on the upper and lower walls due to viscous diffusion. Further downstream, the heated rectangle reduces the cross-sectional ﬂow area, and the pressure ﬁeld accelerates the ﬂuid through ﬂow paths above and below the heated vapor source.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p11,1,"The microgravity case in panel (a) remains symmetric near the heated vapor source, whereas the Earth gravity case in panel (b) develops an asymmetry with the majority of ﬂuid passing below the heated vapor source (indicated by larger vorticity magnitudes below than above). Comparatively low vorticity values on the downstream side of the rectangles indicate the presence of low speed, recirculating wakes. The microgravity wake in panel (a) is symmetric, whereas the Earth gravity wake in panel (b) is asymmetric. As the ﬂuid continues to ﬂow downstream, the boundary layers thicken in both cases and the ﬂuid exits the domain to the right. Variations in density due to heat transfer from the rectangle have inﬂuenced the vorticity ﬁelds via baroclinic torque and buoyancy. Because there is no gravity in Case 1MG in panel (a), density variations due to heating do not disturb the symmetry of the ﬂow ﬁeld. The asymmetry apparent in panel (b) is due to buoyancy effects. Temperature ﬁelds with overlaid velocity vectors are shown in Fig. 3 at time t = 7 seconds. Panel (a) shows Case 1MG and panel (b) shows Case 1EG. The downstream portion of the domain has been expanded for clarity. Velocity vectors in panel (a) show a symmetric wake proﬁle with two counter- rotating vortices surrounded by dividing streamlines. Panel (b) shows an asymmetric buoyant wake, also with two counter rotating vortices:",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p11,2,"one near the lower right corner of the heated vapor source, and another near the upper right corner of the heated vapor source. Both panels show hot ﬂuid in white, warm ﬂuid in red, and room temperature ﬂuid in black. Thermal energy diffuses from the heated vapor source to the adjacent ﬂuid. Hot ﬂuid convects energy downstream and cools while diffusing energy to cooler ﬂuid and the isothermal upper and lower walls. The highest temperatures appear near the heated surface and in the wake. In both cases, the gas in the center of the channel exits the domain at greater than 350 Kelvin. The microgravity case maintains a symmetric temperature ﬁeld because there is no gravity, and therefore no asymmetric coupling between the vorticity and temperature via buoyancy. In panel (b), buoyant convection resulting from gravitational forces caries the low density, hot ﬂuid towards the upper surface. As the hot ﬂuid encounters the isothermal upper surface, a thin thermal boundary layer develops. Hot ﬂuid cools rapidly when in close proximity to the upper surface due to the thin thermal boundary layer. DBP vapor mass fraction is shown in Fig. 4 at time t = 7 seconds. Panel (a) shows Case 1MG and panel (b) shows Case 1EG. Consistent with the vorticity and temperature, the DBP vapor mass fraction is symmetric in Case 1MG and asymmetric in Case 1EG.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p11,3,"The highest mass fractions of DBP (approx- imately 0.05) appear in white on the immediate downstream side of the heated vapor source in both panels (a) and (b). The recirculating wakes in both cases contain a small concentration of DBP vapor, but are encompassed by streaks of DBP vapor issuing from the upper and lower surfaces of the heated vapor source. In Case 1MG, the DBP vapor streaks interact via diffusion at the downstream side of the wake and eventually merge. In Case 1EG, merging velocity characteristics, indicated by the merging velocity vectors at the downstream side of the wake, rapidly combine the upper and lower vapor streaks. In both Case 1MG and Case 1EG, the majority of the vapor is consumed before the domain outlet. UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 11 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p12,0,"4.6 PARTICLE NUCLEATION, NUMBER DENSITY, AND SIZE The particle nucleation rate is a function of vapor mass fraction and temperature (and pressure). Figure 5 shows the DBP nucleation rate at time t = 7 seconds for Case 1MG in panel (a) and Case 1EG in panel (b). Panel (a) shows that nucleation occurs exclusively downstream of the heated vapor source in Case 1MG, and most rapidly at a rate of ∼ 1012 [ m3 · s ]−1 near the periphery of the recirculating wake. Nucleation does not occur at a signiﬁcant rate anywhere in the wake. The nucleation rate decreases quickly after approximately ten dimensionless length units downstream of the heated vapor source. Panel (b) shows two primary zones of nucleation in Case 1EG: downstream of the upper wake vortex and in the region downstream and above the upper wake vortex, near the upper surface. Nucleation occurs at a higher rate, ∼ 1014 [ m3 · s ]−1 , in Case 1EG than in Case 1MG. Particle number densities at time t = 7 seconds are shown in Fig. 6 for Cases 1EG and 1MG in panels (a) and (b), respectively. Panel (a) shows a particle number density of ∼ 1010 m−3 at the center of each counter-rotating wake vortex. Thermophoretic forces prevent particles from contacting the downstream surface of the heated vapor source (this will be demonstrated in Section 4.8), resulting in a void of particles between the heated vapor source and the particle-dense wake vortices. Particles are also less numerous on the downstream side of the recirculating wake:",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p12,1,"particles do not nucleate in this region, dividing streamlines prevent particles from being convected into this region, and low diffusion rates limit particles from rapidly diffusing into this region. Even further downstream, the particle number density remains symmetric, with the largest particle number densities of ∼ 1011 m−3 appearing slightly separated from the symmetry line on top and bottom. No particles appear near the upper and lower surfaces. Panel (b) shows the particle number densities for Case 1EG. More particles accumulate in the upper wake vortex than in the lower wake vortex. The Case 1EG wake contains fewer particles than the Case 1MG wake. In Case 1EG, a high particle concentration of nearly ∼ 1012 m−3 appears over a large area near the upper surface due to the high nucleation rate in the region. Signiﬁcantly more particles appear in this near-wall region than anywhere in the Case 1MG particle ﬁeld. Unlike Case 1MG, Case 1EG contains particles throughout the entire vertical span of the ﬂow chamber: particles that appear in lower concentrations near the bottom wall have arrived there via the gravitational settling mechanism. Particles that settle are sufﬁciently large to be removed from the aerosol via gravity. This can be demonstrated by considering the spatial distribution of particle size. The diameter of average mass dm is deﬁned by dm = ( 6 π ∑Ns i=2 viQi ∑Ns i=2 Qi )1/3 .",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p12,2,"(32) Figure 7 shows the diameter of average mass at time t = 7 seconds for Case 1MG in panel (a) and Case 1EG in panel (b). The diameter of average mass is typically 10 − 20 µm in both cases. The diameter of average mass is notably smaller in regions where nucleation rates or particle number densities are large (near the upper surface in Case 1EG, for example). The Case 1EG wake contains only isolated regions of large particles, including in the upper and lower vorticies, while the entirety of the Case 1MG wake contains large particles. A signiﬁcation region downstream and below the heated vapor source contains very large particles in Case 1EG: particles sufﬁciently large to rapidly settle out of the ﬂow dominate this region. Downstream of the Case 1MG wake, the diameter of average mass depends signiﬁcantly on cross-stream position, but not on stream-wise position. The largest particles appear on the symmetry line, corresponding to a region of low particle number density. The particle size distribution (PSD) provides a quantitative measure of particle number and size. We construct the size distribution by numerically “collecting” a volume of the aerosol and counting the number of particles in each node k contained in the sample. The sample is collected by computing the ﬂux of particles through a vertical plane located at x′, where x′ is the dimensionless distance from the downstream surface of the heated element, indicated in Fig. 1.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p12,3,"The collected number density ¯Qk [ m−3] of particles belonging to size k is therefore ¯Qk = 1 h ∫ h/2 −h/2 u (y) Qk (y) dy, (33) where h = 6.82Lo is the height of the domain and u is the x-component of the velocity vector. The collected number density ¯Qk is therefore the number of particles collected per unit time and per unit volume at the measurement plane x′. UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 12 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p13,0,"Figure 8 shows the particle number densities for Cases 1MG and 1EG at downstream measurement plane x′ = 26 at time t = 7 seconds. Case 1MG is shown in red and Case 1EG is shown in black. The Case 1MG distribution contains particle diameters ranging from hundreds of nanometers to tens of micrometers, with a small percentage of particles approaching one hundred micrometers. The most frequent size is approximately 5 µm. The Case 1MG distribution contains two local maximums: one at 5 µm and another at 30 µm. The Case 1EG distribution contains only one local maximum at 5 µm. Case 1EG contains a larger number density than Case 1MG for all particle sizes < 20 µm, and Case 1EG contains no particles > 40 µm – these large particle settle out of the system. Case 1EG contains orders of magnitude more particles < 5 µm than Case 1MG. 4.7 EFFECT OF PARTICLE SETTLING In order to isolate the effects of particle settling, we modify Case 1EG by neglecting the settling mecha- nism (by setting uss⋆ j,k = 0). This case is referred to as Case 1EG–NS, where NS stands for “no settling”. Figure 9 compares size distributions for Case 1MG, Case 1EG, and Case 1EG–NS at measurement plane x′ = 26 at time t = 7 seconds. The Case 1EG–NS distribution is considerably wider than the other cases. It contains particles ranging in size from nanometers to 100 µm. Case 1EG–NS also contains two local maxima in the particle size distribution, much like Case 1MG and unlike Case 1EG.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p13,1,"Eliminating the settling mechanism has expanded the size distribution to include both smaller and larger particles, and has also introduced a new local maximum in the size distribution near 35 µm. 4.8 EFFECT OF THERMOPHORESIS Figure 10 shows particle number densities at time t = 7 seconds for Case 1MG in panel (a) and Case 1MG-NT in panel (b) (where NT stands for “no thermophoresis”). When thermophoresis is active, as in panel (a), particles do not appear adjacent to the surface of the heated vapor source; the temperature gradient is large normal to the surface, and thermophoresis forces the particles out of this region. When thermophoresis is inactive, particles are able to remain near the hot surface. In this region, the temperature is sufﬁciently high to cause evaporation. Small particles are able to diffuse out of the wake, resulting in lower particle number densities inside the Case 1MG-NT wake than the Case 1MG wake. Downstream of the wake, larger particle number densities are present in Case 1MG-NT relative to Case 1MG, following from the decreased number of particles trapped in the Case 1MG-NT wake vorticies. Figure 11 shows particle size distributions for Cases 1MG and 1MG-NT at time t = 7 seconds. Consis- tent with Fig. 10, the peak number density in Case 1MG-NT is greater than Case 1MG by approximately a factor of ﬁve – the most frequent particle size is ∼ 5 µm for both cases. A local maximum appears in both distributions around dp = 30 µm.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p13,2,"This local maximum contains fewer particles in Case 1MG-NT than in Case 1MG, potentially because this local maximum is comprised of particles that have inter- acted with the wake region. In Case 1MG-NT, some of these particles have undergone evaporation and shifted to smaller particle sizes. 4.9 EFFECT OF HEATED VAPOR SOURCE ORIENTATION Figure 12 shows the particle number densities at time t = 7 seconds for Case 1MG-R in panel (a) and Case 1EG-R in panel (b) (where R stands for “Rotated”). Cases 1MG-R and 1EG-R are identical to Cases 1MG and 1EG, except the heated vapor source has been rotated 90◦. The wake regions of Cases 1MG-R and 1EG-R are considerably smaller than Cases 1MG and 1EG, and neither Case 1MG-R nor Case 1EG-R contain particles in their wakes. The results from Case 1MG-R differ from Case 1MG in that they do not prominently display two high particle number density streaks around the symmetry line, but instead contain large particle number densities everywhere near the symmetry plane. Case 1EG-R presents large particle number densities near the cool upper surface, similar to Case 1EG. Unlike Case 1EG, Case 1EG-R does not contain particles inside its comparatively small downstream wake region, but instead contains particles in a large recirculation region that appears above the heated vapor source.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p13,3,The number density of particles in the recirculation region directly above the heated vapor source is lower than the particle number density downstream of the recirculation region. Settling UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 13 SEAN C. GARRICK,,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p14,0,"particles are not apparent, as in Case 1EG, which may be attributable to the lack of a wake region. Figure 13 shows particle size distributions for Cases 1MG-R and 1EG-R at time t = 7 seconds. Both distributions present a single local maximum in particle size – the local maximum prominent at dp = 30 µm for Case 1MG is not present in Case 1MG-R. Consistent with Case 1EG, Case 1EG-R contains fewer particles larger than approximately dp = 10 µm, and no particles larger than dp = 40 µm. The most frequent particle size for Case 1EG-R is the same as Case 1EG (dp = 5 µm). The most frequent particle diameter for Case 1MG-R is dp = 10 µm – approximately two times larger than Cases 1MG, 1EG, and 1EG-R. 4.10 EFFECT OF FLOW SPEED The Case 1 simulations were performed with an 8 cm/s inlet ﬂow velocity. Case 2MG and Case 2EG consider a 10 cm/s inlet ﬂow velocity. Particle number densities and velocity vectors for Case 2MG and Case 2EG are shown in Fig. 14 at time t = 7 seconds. Panel (a) shows Case 2MG and panel (b) shows Case 2EG. Case 2MG presents many features similar to Case 1MG, including symmetry about the center line and high particle number densities in the wake vortices. Case 2MG has an elongated wake associated with a higher ﬂow speed. Case 2EG differs from Case 1EG in that particles are not prominent near the upper surface until much farther downstream – this is attributable to the decrease in Froude number, and corresponding decrease in upward motion due to buoyancy.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p14,1,"Although the particle ﬁeld remains asymmetric, the increased ﬂow velocity has decreased the inﬂuence of buoyancy, reducing interaction with the upper surface. The presence of particles in the lower portion of the domain, as in Case 1EG, is attributable to the gravitational settling dynamic. Particle size distributions for Case 2MG and Case 2EG computed at x′ = 26 and time t = 7 seconds are shown in Fig. 15. The most common particle size is ∼ 5 µm for both cases. Case 2EG, unlike Case 1EG, contains particles on the nanometer scale – nucleation begins near the upper surface further downstream, and particles resulting from nucleation remain in the ﬂow near the measurement plane x′ = 26. 4.11 SMOKE VISUALIZATION The simulated smoke is visualized by computing the particle surface area densityS(x) according to S(x) = Ns∑ k=2 πd2 kQk(x). (34) The particle surface area density S(x) is shown in Fig. 16 at time t = 7 seconds. Panel (a) shows Case 1MG, panel (b) shows Case 1EG, panel (c) shows Case 2MG, and panel (d) shows Case 2EG. Darker regions correspond to large smoke concentrations and white areas contain no smoke. High concentrations of smoke are apparent in the wake vortices of all cases. The microgravity cases in panels (a) and (c) contain larger and darker wake regions than the Earth gravity cases in panels (b) and (d). Panel (b), corresponding to Case 1EG, presents high smoke concentrations near the upper wall.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p14,2,"The visible smoke in Case 1EG also occupies a larger downstream area than the other cases, resulting from the high nucleation rates near the upper surface. When gravitational forces dominate, wall effects can be signiﬁcant, as demonstrated by case Case 1EG in panel (b). In Earth gravity, the smoke is more prominent downstream than in the wake region. Visual inspection also suggests the Earth gravity smoke to be more sensitive to small perturbations in ﬂow speed than the microgravity smoke (in the conﬁguration tested). UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 14 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p15,0,"5 Summary We have simulated smoke formation and growth in microgravity and Earth gravity to examine how buoyancy and particle settling affect thermal/ﬂuid/particle interactions and the resultant smoke particle size distributions. The simulated geometry emulates the SAME experiments performed aboard the International Space Station (Mulholland et al., 2015). Dibutyl pthalate (DBP) vapor is released from a heated rectangular sample, which serves as a surrogate for the smoldering material used in the SAME experiments. Large particles (> 30 µm) are present in the wake region downstream of the heated vapor source, and large particle number densities (∼ 1010 m−3) occur inside the wake vortices. The wake contains large particle number densities because dividing streamlines prevent the particles from being convected away, and the particles continue to grow as condensation consumes all available DBP vapor. The particles simultaneously grow via coagulation. Downstream of the wake, two parallel particle streams contain high number densities of both large and small particles. No particles are present far downstream with diameter < 400 nm because nucleation only occurs for a short distance downstream of the heated va- por source. The particle size distributions contain local maximums near dp = 5 µm and dp = 35 µm for both ﬂow speeds. The faster ﬂow conditions present a slightly larger wake but this does not manifest as a meaningful difference in particle size distribution.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p15,1,"Thermophoresis prevents particles from contact- ing the heated surface, which has been demonstrated by turning off the thermophoresis mechanism. Without thermophoresis, particles contact the heated vapor source and undergo evaporation. With re- spect to the particle size distribution, this has the effect of decreasing the number of large particles and increasing the number of small particles – this supports an assertion that the wake is responsible for producing the largest particles in the system. When gravity is present, all ﬁelds become asymmetric due to buoyant convection. Upward convection in the faster ﬂow brings DBP vapor into close proximity with the cool upper surface, resulting in locally high nucleation rates near the upper wake vortex and the upper surface. Large particle number densi- ties (∼ 1011 m−3) are present along the upper surface, and slightly lower number densities are present in the upper wake vortex. Comparably few particles are present in the lower wake vortex, where the diameter of average mass is maximized. Downstream of the wake vortices, gravitational settling elimi- nates all particles with diameter > 35 µm. Unlike the microgravity case, the 8 cm/s Earth gravity case is not bimodal, with a single local maximum at dp = 5 µm. Without particle settling, a bimodal distribution results with two local maximums at particle diameters comparable to the microgravity case.",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p15,2,"Rotating the heated vapor source 90◦ dramatically reduces the wake volume, and no particles appear in the small wake. Rotating the heated vapor source does not affect the Earth gravity case as signiﬁcantly as the microgravity case. Increasing the ﬂow speed from decreases the prominence of buoyancy, delays inter- action with the upper surface until further downstream, widens the particle size distribution to include particles on the nanometer scale, and introduces a second local maximum to the PSD near dp = 30 µm. Microgravity smoke presented in this study contains larger particles than terrestrial smoke. When the microgravity PSD is bimodal, the larger mode contains particles with dp > 35 µm, while the terrestrial PSD contains effectively zero particles in this size range. Alternatively, the microgravity PSD can be uni- modal with a mode diameter of dp = 10 µm – roughly twice that of the terrestrial case. Results exhibit three primary mechanisms that differentiate the microgravity and Earth gravity smoke: (1) different re- circulation geometries and volumes modify the time-temperature-concentration history of the aerosol in regions of high temperature and high vapor mass fraction; (2) particle settling eliminates large particles from Earth gravity smoke, which reduces the maximum particle size and modiﬁes aerosol development by changing coagulation and condensation rates of the remaining particles;",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p15,3,"(3) buoyant convection can transport condensible species toward relatively cool surfaces, increasing vapor-to-particle conversion. Each of these factors tend to increase the size of particles in microgravity relative to Earth gravity. How- ever, each of these factors is also highly material- and geometry-dependent: (1) the wake dynamic becomes unimportant if nucleation occurs far downstream of the wake, or if there is a time dependent ﬂow without a stable recirculation region; (2) particle settling is irrelevant if the condensing species forms particles that are too small to settle (the DBP considered in this work forms large particles); (3) if smoke is produced in an open environment, there may not be cool surfaces nearby to increase nucleation rates. In these situations, microgravity and Earth gravity smoke are hypothesized to be more similar. In UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 15 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
PSI-115,Reports,PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Reports/PSI-115_Reports_NNX17AF08A_FinalReport.pdf,p16,0,"contrast, a quiescent, enclosed system is a relevant case where Earth gravity and microgravity smoke are hypothesized to differ signiﬁcantly, and is a consideration of future work. UTILIZATION OF THE SAME DATA FOR ADVANCED MODELING AND SIMULATION OF SMOKE GENERATION IN MICROGRAVITY 16 SEAN C. GARRICK",,False
