PSI-115,Science Documents,PSI-115_Science Documents_DataOrganizationDocument.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Science Documents/PSI-115_Science Documents_DataOrganizationDocument.pdf,p1,0,"PSI Data Organization Document Utilization of the Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment Data for Advanced Modeling and Simulation of Smoke Generation in Micro-Gravity To gain an initial understanding of this experiment, refer to the Final Report, located on “Pub and Docs” tab of this Investigation Record. The simulation data is organized in PSI in “Experiment Data” on the “Data” tab, in two high level categories: • Earth Gravity Figure Data • Microgravity Figure Data The simulation data is further categorized into specific cases: • Case 1 with inlet velocity: Uo = 0:08 m/s • Case 2 with inlet velocity: Uo = 0:1 m/s Additional test parameters and conditions are located in Table 3 of the Final Report: Where EG = Earth Gravity; MG = Microgravity; Uo = Inlet Velocity; go = Gravitational Acceleration; Re = Reynolds number; Fr = Froude number. In addition to the four base cases listed in Table 3 of the Final Report, and as shown above, the data is further categorized as the researcher examines the aerosol development sensitivity to: • Particle settling • Thermophoresis • The flow geometry For this purpose, a series of additional cases, defined in Table 3 of the Final Report, and as shown above, are constructed by modifying the setup of Case 1MG and/or Case 1EG.",,False
PSI-115,Science Documents,PSI-115_Science Documents_DataOrganizationDocument.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-115/Science Documents/PSI-115_Science Documents_DataOrganizationDocument.pdf,p1,1,"• Case1-Earth Gravity-NS: (where NS stands for “no settling”) is identical to Case1-Earth Gravity except the settling mechanism has been neglected. • Case1-Earth Gravity-R: (where R stands for “rotated”) is identical to Case1-Earth Gravity except the heated vapor source has been rotated 90o. • Case1-Microgravity-NT: (where NT stands for “no thermophoresis”) is identical to Case1- Microgrativy except the thermophoresis transport mechanism has been neglected. • Cases1Microgravity-R: (where R stands for “rotated”) is identical to Case1-Microgrativy except the heated vapor source has been rotated 90o. In all cases, data is collected and presented at a time of t = 7 seconds.",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p1,0,"Cool Flames in Space Tanvir Farouk* Department of Mechanical Engineering, University of South Carolina *ONR Fellow , Naval Research Laboratory NASA Physical Sciences Informatics (PSI) Seminar March 26, 2026",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p2,0,"Acknowledgements Collaborators: Yiguang Ju (Princeton), Tom Avedesian (Cornell), Fred Dryer (Princeton), Forman Williams (UCSD), Sang Hee Won (USC), Daniel Dietrich (NASA), Michael Hicks (NASA), Vedha Nayagam (NASA) Students: Rajib Mahamud (Idaho State University), Fahd Alam (GE Vernova), Sheikh Ahmed (Honeywell), Ali Charchi (Mitsubishi), Sudipta Saha (Pratt and Whitney), Ejaz Ahmed (Virginia Tech Graduate School)",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p3,0,"Introduction Cool flame: A flame having a typical temperature of ~ 650 K, reaction is not vigorous and releases little heat, light, or carbon dioxide (source: Wikipedia) Sir Humphrey Davy 1817 Low temperature flame Hot platinum wire into a mixture of air and diethyl ether vapor Formation of pale phosphorescent light in the dark Harry Julius Emeléus First to record their emission spectra 1929 coined the term Cool /Cold flame Gasoline Engine",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p4,0,"Introduction Traditional view of cool flames prior to 2012-2014 • Cool flames associated with premixed combustion leading to ignition of hot fuel/air mixtures – commonly encountered in car-engine knock Gasoline Engine Diesel Engine HCCI Engine Curran et al. Combustion & Flame (1998) Temperature Ignition delay of n-heptane Pre-1900 1920 1930 1940 1950 1960 1970 1980 1990 2000 First observation: Davy (1817), Perkin (1882) First flow tube experiments Pease C3H8 cool flame studies in a closed vessel Extensive mapping of p-T ignition diagrams (Townsend et al.) RH + O2 product analysis and R + O2 = RO2 mechanisms proposed Thermokinetic models Zero dimensional numerical simulation and reduced/detailed kinetic model development Reaction – diffusion models Poincore theorems and Liapounov stability analysis Lotka-Volterra isothermal oscillations Sal’nikov non-isothermal theory Gray and Yang unified chain-thermal theory Zeldovich chemical reactor theory Chemical reactor stability and non-linear dynamics (Aris, Uppal et al.)Stirred closed vessel and CSTR studies Technical development and applications Cool flames in space (µg) History of cool flame research – Professor Griffiths (2004) Cool flames → Ignition",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p5,0,First observation of multiphase cool flames – “Anomalous Combustion” High Temperature Combustion “Cool Flame”????,,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p6,0,"Evolution of droplet diameter and burning rate (n-heptane, do = 4.00 mm, air, atmospheric pressure) Classical “Cool Flames” (premixed systems) ―“Cool Flame” to Hot Ignition. Cool Flames – Model Predictions",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p7,0,"Driving Mechanism Heat loss Heat generation Evolution of heat generation and losses (n-heptane, do = 4.00 mm, air, atmospheric pressure) • Radiative heat loss pushes the system to transition to the second stage, cool flame burn. • A dynamic balance between heat generation and heat loss maintains the long-duration cool flame burn.",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p8,0,"Sensitivity of Kinetic Parameters Sensitivity coefficients for burning rate and extinction diameter predictions. In general increase in QOOHO2 → QOOH + O2 rate decreased the burning rate and increased the extinction diameter. Vice versa QOOHO2→ Ketohydroperoxide + OH (Q = CnH2n) Uncertainty in droplet diameter and burning rate predictions, (n- heptane, do = 4.00 mm, air, atmospheric pressure)",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p9,0,"Cool Flames → Revised Kinetics In general increase in QOOHO2 → QOOH + O2 rate decreased the burning rate and increased the extinction diameter. Vice versa QOOHO2→ Ketohydroperoxide + OH (Q = CnH2n) Uncertainty in droplet diameter and burning rate predictions, (n- heptane, do = 4.00 mm, air, atmospheric pressure) Predicted droplet diameter and burning rate evolution with revised chemical kinetics model.",,False
