PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p24,0,"Temperature Ketohydroperoxides 80 x Ddrop Blue: Oscillatory cool flame Red: Stable cool flame  Flame expands radially  gradual decrease in flame T  Contracts (t < 0.1 s)  Stable cool flame for brief period of time (~0.12 s @ ~710 K) Do = 0.5 mm XO2 = 10% XO3 = 5% XN2 = Balance P = 1 atm, T = 298 K Cool Flame under Near Limit Conditions",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p25,0,Terrestrial Cool Flame Experiments,,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p26,0,"Counterflow Diffusion Flame Cool diffusion flame, 1 g (Won et al.)  Excellent configuration for studying flame’s strength under the effect of stretch.  Useful platform for validating reaction kinetics and transport models.  Idealized condition, it can be treated as a 1D problem:  Leads to the derivation of an analytical solution  Makes numerical modeling tractable",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p27,0, The numerical effort to study counterflow flame configuration has been predominantly centered upon OPPDIF module of CHEMKIN chemical kinetic package  This module implements a similarity transformation approach that reduces the flow field to a one-dimensional/ plug flow problem with the following assumptions:  Radial component of velocity is linear in radius  Dependent variables become functions of the axial direction only What are the physical conditions that can be represented by this assumption?  High Reynolds number flow configuration: High strain rate (a)  Thin flame sheet: Hot flame (high activation energy) Limitations of the conventional numerical approach  Low Re: Low strain rate (a ~100 s-1)  Finite Flame Thickness: low activation energy) Cool flames,,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p28,0,"Effect of buoyancy a) Temperature distribution and streamlines, and b) heat release rate, for DME diffusion cool flame with and without buoyancy for identical conditions (XDME= 0.587, UF = 62.3 cm/s, UO = 50.9 cm/s, a ~ 90 s-1, P = 1 atm)  Buoyancy lifts the flame upward.  Without buoyancy, 1D and 2D models predict the flame at or near the stagnation plane due to momentum- balanced BC.  The peak flame temperature in 1D is slightly lower compared to the 2D prediction, but the peak HRR shows the opposite trend.  Temperature gradient is higher in 1D calculation. More diffusive heat loss.",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p29,0,"Flow field analyses c d Computed centerline distribution of a) axial velocity, b) axial velocity gradient Computed radial distribution of c) axial, and d) radial velocity at the fuel-side nozzle exit  The extended entrance channels allow the velocity at the nozzle exits to be perturbed by the flame.  2D model captures the perturbation in a self-consistent manner.  2D model without buoyancy retains the momentum-balanced condition and forms the stagnation plane at the axis midpoint, exhibiting identical perturbation on either side  Axisymmetric dip in 2D simulation, observed in experiments of Sarnecki et al.",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p30,0,"( ) 2 hotg T TDRi V β ∞−=  Richardson number 2 GrRi Re = 1 1 11 p RT R T T p pTβ ∂∀ ∂= = = =∀ ∂ ∀∂ ∀  ( ) 3 2 hotg T TDGr V β ∞−= Flow characteristics based in Richardson number Ri < 0.1 Natural convection is negligible 0.1< Ri <10 Both natural and forced convection are important Ri > 10 Forced convection is negligible Effect of global strain rate XCH2O contours of DME diffusion cool flame at different global strain rates (XDME = 0.587, P = 1 atm)  Flame is pushed away from the nozzle as the convective effect becomes dominant with the increase of global strain rate.  The flame location ceases to move further when the buoyancy effect becomes negligible (i.e., low Ri).",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_PSI_Users_Group_Cool_Flames_in_Space_03302026.pdf,p31,0,Summary • Insights obtained from modeling on cool flame characteristics and dynamics • Models were developed for a number of scenarios • Models were validated against the FLEX data and then utilized to explore other scenarios • Extensive amount of experimental data available for interpretation and new insights,,False
