PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p2,0,"2 T. I. Farouka and F. L. Dryera,b a Department of Mechanical Engineering University of South Carolina, [address removed], USA b Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Princeton University, [address removed], USA 39th International Symposium on Combustion Vancouver, Canada July 24th – 29th, 2022 Extinction characteristics of isolated n-alkane fuel droplets during low temperature cool flame droplet burning in air",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p3,0,"3 “Cool” Flame – NASA FLEX Cool Program V. Nayagam et al. Combustion and Flame (2012), 159, 3583 – 3588. T. Farouk and F. L. Dryer Combustion and Flame (2014), 161, 565 – 581 V. Nayagam et al. Combustion and Flame (2015), 162, 2140 – 2147 T. Farouk et al. Proc. Comb. Inst (2017), 36, 2523 – 2530 A. Cuoci et al. Proc. Comb. Inst (2017), 36, 2531 – 2539 “Cool Flame” First Stage Second Stage Hot Flame n-octane n-decane n-heptane n-dodecane 2 stage hot-cool flame 2 stage hot-cool flame 2 stage hot-cool flame Single stage cool flame 2 stage hot-cool flame 3 stage hot-warm-cool flame",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p4,0,"4 Evolution of droplet diameter and FSR (n-decane, do = 4.00 mm, air, atmospheric pressure) V. Nayagam et al. Combustion and Flame (2015), 162, 2140 – 2147 T. Farouk et al. Proc. Comb. Inst (2017), 36, 2523 – 2530 Evolution of burning rate and peak temperature (n-decane, do = 4.00 mm, air, atmospheric pressure) Transition from hot to cool flame burning. Radiative extinction • Initiation energy plays a critical role in driving the hot flame condition. • Radiative loss is critical for transition from hot to cool flame burning. n-Decane: Two Stage Burning",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p5,0,"5 Evolution of droplet diameter and FSR (n-decane, do = 4.00 mm, air, atmospheric pressure) • Low ignition energy • Combustion behavior affected by fuel flash point relative to ambient and initial fuel liquid temperature Evolution of burning rate and peak temperature (n-decane, do = 4.00 mm, air, atmospheric pressure) • Sufficiently constraining initiation energy can lead to single stage sustained cool flame burning. n-Decane: Single Stage Burning T. Farouk et al. Proc. Comb. Inst (2017), 36, 2523 – 2530",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p6,0,"6 n-Dodecane: Three Stage Burning T. Farouk et al. Proc. Comb. Inst (2019), 37, 3353 – 3361 Evolution of droplet diameter, radiant heat and FSR (n-dodecane, do = 4.40 mm, 2.75 atm, 21% O2, 29% He, balance N2) • Qualitative agreement between predictions and observations • No optimization of model input, physical, or kinetic parameters to obtain these comparisons",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p7,0,"7 Gas Phase: • Mass conservation • Species equation • Energy conservation  Multi-component molecular diffusion  Detailed chemical kinetics - high, intermediate and low temperature  Statistical narrowband radiation model  C7 – C20 kinetic model, 583 species, 2609 reactions* mv T r ∂λ ∂ qR mpsdr dt l T r ∂λ ∂ , , li li YD r ∂ ∂ Droplet Surface: • Surface regression – Adaptive Grid • Evaporation of fuel • Condensation/Dissolution of products • Radiative heat exchange Fiber: • One dimensional temperature distribution • Energy exchange with flame and ambient fibq ambientq lossq Transient Droplet Combustion Model Liquid Phase: • Mass conservation • Species equation • Energy conservation  Activity coefficient – UNIFAC Formulation Soot: • Discrete sectional method • Definition of classes of molecules (BINS) describing large PAH and soot particles • Reactions describing nucleation, coagulation, oxidation of BINs *M. Sarathy et al. Combust. Flame (2011), 158, 2338 – 2357",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p8,0,"8 n-heptane n-decane Shorter Chain Length Longer Chain Property n-C7H16 n-C10H22 n-C12H26 Molecular weight, Mw (g/mol) 100.20 142.29 170.34 Flash point, Tf (K) 269.1 319.1 344.0 Boiling point, Tb (K) 372.0 447.4 487.0 Density, ρL (kg/m3) 679.5 730.2 749.5 Specific Heat, cP (kJ/kmol K) 224.64 314.56 376.15 Heat of vaporization, ∆HV (kJ/mol) 36.41 52.09 61.0 Heat of combustion, ∆Hc (kJ/mol) 4849.2 6779.2 7901.4 Longer straight chain HC more prevalent low temperature kinetics n-dodecane Properties: n-heptane, n-decane, n-dodecane",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p9,0,"9 Experiment dext HF = 3.41 mm dext CF = 1.13 mm Kavg HF = 0.46 mm2s-1 Kavg CF = 0.38 mm2s-1 n-Alkane Burning Characteristics: Model Validation (1 atm, Air) Hot Flame Extinction Cool Flame Extinction Hot Flame Extinction Cool Flame Extinction Hot Flame Extinction Cool Flame Extinction Model dext HF = 3.53 mm dext CF = 0.81 mm Kavg HF = 0.44 mm2s-1 Kavg CF = 0.39 mm2s-1 Experiment dext HF = 3.32 mm dext CF = 0.59 mm Kavg HF = 0.41 mm2s-1 Kavg CF = 0.39 mm2s-1 Model dext HF = 3.42 mm dext CF = 0.30 mm Kavg HF = 0.37 mm2s-1 Kavg CF = 0.42 mm2s-1 Experiment dext HF = 4.16 mm dext CF = 0.53 mm Kavg HF = 0.38 mm2s-1 Kavg CF = 0.52 mm2s-1 Model dext HF = 4.34 mm dext CF = 0.24 mm Kavg HF = 0.32 mm2s-1 Kavg CF = 0.52 mm2s-1",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p10,0,"10 • During transition to second stage burn, the FSR and the Tmax for all three fuels are almost identical. n-Alkane Burning Characteristics: 1 atm, Air Evolution of droplet diameter, FSR, Kb and Tmax for n-alkane droplets (do = 4.00 mm, 1 atm, air) FSR Critical flame diameter, results in excessive radiative loss – transition to second stage • Kb,CF C12 > Kb,CF C10 > Kb,CF C7 • Tmax,CF C12 ~ 775 K > Tmax,CF C10 ~ 737 K > Tmax,CF C7 ~ 720 K • de,CF C7 > de,CF C10 > de,CF C12",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p11,0,11 Extinction Diameter Comparison of measured and predicted hot and cool flame extinction diameter for different initial droplet diameter for the three n-alkane fuels (atmospheric air) • High temperature combustion is strongly governed by the small species kinetics – all three fuels share nearly identical values of HF extinction diameter for the same do • The LTC differs with carbon number as each involve reactions associated with the carbon number of the fuel and the large intermediates produced. The overall rates of isomerization of radical-O2 adducts controlling rate of degenerate chain branching rate increase from C7 to C12 • The CF extinction is dictated by the increase in decomposition of •O2QnOOH for all three fuels.,,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p12,0,"12 Extinction Characteristics: Pressure Dependence Evolution of FSR, Kb and Tmax for n-dodecane droplets for different pressure (do = 4.00 mm, air) • For pressure ≤ 1.0 atm, two stage combustion – hot and cool flame extinction. • At 1.25 atm, multi-cycling of hot-cool-hot-cool flame transition. • At 3.0 atm, the system transitions to a stable “Warm” flame. • Cool flame temperature increases with pressure and results in a hot flame reignition. • “Warm” flame is dictated by NTC kinetics and competition for OH between hydrocarbon and oxygenated hydrocarbon fragments.",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p13,0,"13 Multicycle Behavior Evolution of Kb and Tmax for n-dodecane droplet combustion at 1.25 atm (do = 7.00 mm, air) • Four “Hot-Cool” flame transition. • At every transition, the cool flame temperature decreases significantly, the burn time increases. • The hot flame behavior remains unaltered.",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p14,0,"14 Multicycle Behavior Evolution of Kb and Tmax for n-dodecane droplet combustion at 1.25 atm (do = 7.00 mm, air) • Four “Hot-Cool” flame transition. • At every transition, the cool flame temperature decreases significantly, the burn time increases. • The hot flame behavior remains unaltered. Spatiotemporal contour of a) gas phase temperature and mass fraction of b) H2O, c) CO2, d) CO, e) H2O2 and f) CH2O for n-dodecane droplet undergoing multiple hot-cool flame transitions (do = 7.00 mm, 1.25 atm, air).",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p15,0,"15 Extinction Diameter: Pressure Dependence Comparison of predicted and measured de,HF and de,CF as a function of operating pressure for of n-dodecane droplets burning in air. (do = 4.00 mm). • Both hot and cool flame extinction diameter has a non-linear dependence on pressure. • de,CF decreases as pressure is increased, and is absent at 3 atm as the droplet burns to completion following a “Warm” flame combustion • The de,CF is most sensitive to: •O2QnOOH → •OQn`OOH + OH •O2QnOOH → QnOOH + O2",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p16,0,"16 • Two-stage hot-cool flame burning mode transitions are demonstrated to exist for the three fuels, and the maximum flame temperature at which transition to the second stage occurred was universally found to be ~ 1200K. • Hot flame characteristics of all three fuels indicate that predicted extinction phenomena are all very similar. • Cool flame extinction properties are significantly affected by the n-alkane molecular weight, and the chain length of the n-alkane has significant effect on the cool flame reactivity - larger n- alkane exhibited more robust cool flame (i.e., higher temperature) and hence smaller extinction diameters. • Universally, de,CF for each fuel varies linearly with initial droplet diameter, but unlike de,HF these relationships do not collapse to a single function. • n-Dodecane droplet combustion reveal a strong dependence of observations on ambient pressure. Single, two-stage hot-cool flame combustion transitions to a multi-cycle combustion behavior at ambient pressures above one atmosphere, and eventually into a hot - “Warm” flame transition. Summary",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p17,0,"17 • This work was supported by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration through grants NNX17AF97A (TIF). • Thanks to Drs. Daniel Dietrich, Vedha Nayagam and Forman Williams for interest and insightful discussions throughout the course of this investigation. Acknowledgements",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Extinction_Characteristics_TFarouk.pdf,p18,0,"18 Backup Evolution of radiant energy (n-heptane, do = 3.89 mm, air, 1 atm) Evolution of radiant energy (n-decane, do = 3.88 mm, air, 1 atm) Similar radiant energy during extinction for n-heptane and n-decane.",,False
