PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,p7,0," Flame expands radially  gradual decrease in flame T  Contracts (t < 0.1 s)  Stable cool flame for brief period of time (~0.12 s @ ~710 K)  How does the oscillation start?  Role of LT chemistry?  Why does the oscillation grow? Oscillatory Cool Flame: Primary Observations  Initiation energy results in Tpeak, gas exceeding ~1150 K locally 7 Temperature Ketohydroperoxides 80 x Ddrop",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,p8,0,"Inception of oscillation  O3 kinetics (dissociation/recombination) elevates local O2 mole fraction  Initial local XO2 : 0.10  0.16 (at flame location)  Gradient in O2 mole fraction compels O2 to diffuse radially outward direction  causing local depletion Flame temperature increases due to higher O2 availability Flame contracts due to consumption of O2 Oscillations repeats Flame moves radially out Pulsing starts at XO2 < ~0.135 As time progresses, local O2 depletes 8",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,p9,0,"Low-temperature oxidation and autoignition chemistry of large hydrocarbons. Combustion (5th Edition), Chapter 3, Page 104 Role of Low Temperature Chemistry on the Oscillation Consumption of C7H14OOH depends on the extend of reactivity from chain propagation/termination reactions and degenerate chain branching reactions Chain propagation / termination reaction class correlates with temperature Chain branching reaction class undergoes three distinct stages 9 RH Ṙ Ṙ OO Q OOH OOQ OOH HOOQ - H OOH HOOQ - H O + Ȯ H ȮQ - H O + 2 Ȯ H +X - XH Alkyl radical + O 2 first O 2 addition Alkylperoxy radical Internal H abstraction Hydroperoxy alkyl radical + O 2 second O 2 addition internal H abstraction Ketohydroperoxide Degenerate chain branching Alkene + H Ȯ 2 ȮH+ cyclic ethers, aldehydes or ketones ROOH + O 2 RO + OH Chain branching Alkene + H Ȯ 2 ȮH+ cyclic ethers, aldehydes or ketones OOQ OOH HOOQ - H OOH HOOQ - H O + Ȯ H ȮQ - H O + 2 Ȯ H + O 2",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,p10,0,"Favorable Tmax for O2C7H14OOH production @ 1 atm hovers ~700 K 𝐝𝐝𝐝𝐝 𝐝𝐝𝐝𝐝 𝐎𝐎𝟐𝟐𝐂𝐂𝟕𝟕𝐇𝐇𝐇𝐇𝐇𝐇𝐎𝐎𝐎𝐎𝐇𝐇 Tgas Drop in O2C7H14OOH mole fraction, and shutting down HR contribution from O2C7H14OOH C7H14O3 + OH Initial high temperature decompose C7H14O3 via endothermic reaction (stage # 1) C7H14O3 R’CHO + R’(CO) R’’ +OH C7H14OOH C7H14O + OH O2C7H14OOH C7H14OOH + O2 Exothermicity from other classes are not enough to maintain same Tgas w/o additional contribution Steep fall in peak gas temperature (stage # 1)  drop in C7H14 mole fraction Role of LT Chemistry: Stage # 1 Stage # 1 10",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,p11,0,"Drop in Tgas beyond a threshold ( point A, ~712 K)  changes C7H14O3 profile slope  Reactivates O2C7H14OOH C7H14O3 + OH (exothermic) Role of LT Chemistry: Stage # 2 & 3 Gas phase temperature starts to increase (change slope)  Reflection of C7H14 Simultaneous exothermic contributions from degenerate branching and chain propagation  sharp increase in Tgas Tgas > Tthreshold  degenerate branching contribution starts to diminish  abrupt change in C7H14O3 Balance between two classes of reactions continue for brief period of time  Temperature increases to ~750 K Insufficient heat release from the chain branching/termination route compels the gas temperature to fall @ t~0.638 s Stage # 2 Stage # 3 11",,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,p12,0,Dynamic Competition of LTC & Fuel Leakage Radial Growth of Oscillation: Fuel Leakage 12,,False
PSI-117,Presentations,PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-117/Presentations/PSI-117_Presentations_Oscillatory_Cool Flame_TFarouk.pdf,p13,0," Dynamics of near-limit cool flame burning of isolated submillimeter (Do = 0.5 mm) n-C7H16 droplet is numerically studied.  Reduced ambient O2 mole fraction (10%) is considered to facilitate near- limit condition.  Under selective O3 seeding (5%), the droplet briefly attempts to initiate stable cool flame before entering continuous oscillatory cool flame burn.  Local depletion of O2 (<13.5%) near the droplet initiates the pulsing behavior with initial small fluctuation in flame temperature.  The oscillation is found to increase in magnitude with time, and at each successive cycle.  Dynamic interaction of degenerate chain branching and chain propagation / termination reaction classes of QOOH (within NTC region) is responsible for the observed oscillation.  Continuous fuel leakage through the flame location assists the oscillation to grow in radial direction with time. Summary 13",,False
