PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p1,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 1 Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G) ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022*) Prepared by: Dennis P. Stocker, NASA Glenn Research Center dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov, 216-433-2166 Prof. Peter B. Sunderland, University of Maryland Prof. Richard L. Axelbaum, Washington University in St. Louis Prof. Forman A. Williams, University of California, San Diego Cool flame appearance after hot flame quenching in a composite time sequence of intensified images. *Data analysis and the publication of results is still underway, so it is expected that this report will be updated in the future with at least a revision of the list of papers and presentations.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p2,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 2 Table of Contents Page 1. Overview 3 2. Science Overview 4 A. Background 4 B. Rationale for Microgravity 5 C. Science Objectives 6 3. Hardware Overview 6 A. General Description 6 B. Hardware Shortcomings 15 4. Preliminary Results 17 A. Changed Test Plans 17 B. Overview of Experiment Test Operations 18 C. Summary of Preliminary Results 23 D. Surprises and New Discoveries 23 E. Accomplishment of Approved Objectives 23 F. Additional Objectives Achieved 24 G. Impacts, Benefits, and Products 24 H. Patents 24 5. Appendices 25 A. Abbreviations and Acronyms 25 B. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increment 64 28 C. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increment 65 35 D. Operational Timeline 48 E. Astronauts who Conducted ISS Operations for CFI-G 50 F. ISS Operations per the Payload Weekly Science Summary 51 G. Crew Activity per Space Station Science Highlights 68 H. Crew Activity per the ISS Daily Summary Report 72 I. Crew Activity per the NASA Space Station Blog 79 J. Crew Activity in Publicly Available NASA Photos 80 K. ACME Operations per @ISS_Research on Twitter 81 L. ACME Operations per Astronauts on Twitter 85 M. Summary of the ISS data collection 86 N. Publications, Presentations, Awards, and Outreach 87 1. Publications and Presentations with ISS Results 87 2. Publications and Presentations (comprehensive list) 87 3. Awards 88 4. Outreach 88",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p3,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 3 1. Overview The Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G) experiment is one of six independent experiments that make up the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project for which the tests were conducted within the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on the International Space Station (ISS). The experiment’s Principal Investigator (PI) is Prof. Peter B. Sunderland of the Department of Fire Protection Engineering at the University of Maryland. The two Co-Investigators (Co-Is) are Profs. Richard L. Axelbaum and Forman A. Williams who are respectively from the Washington University of St. Louis and the University of California, San Diego (UCSD). Meanwhile, Dennis P. Stocker of the NASA Glenn Research Center is the Project Scientist (PS) for ACME including CFI-G and the rest of the ACME investigations. In ACME’s preparatory drop testing, etc., Dr. Fumiaki Takahashi of the Case Western Reserve University (CWRU) served as the Deputy Project Scientist. For ACME’s spaceflight operations, the NASA science team included Drs. Uday G. Hegde and Vedha Nayagam who are also with CWRU.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p3,1,"The ISS operations for CFI-G were conducted from March to Oct. 2021 in two sequential rounds of testing. The two rounds occurred during Increments 64-65 and 65-66 and respectively featured normal and inverse flames. The inverse flame tests were conducted with the chamber filled with a mixture of nitrogen and gaseous fuel while an oxygen/nitrogen mixture flowed from the burner. As a result, the inverse flames were effectively inside out, e.g., where the soot formed outside rather than inside of the flames. Although it was in the second round, the final day of CFI-G testing was carried out in the normal flame configuration, with gaseous fuel (with or without nitrogen) flowing into an oxygen/nitrogen atmosphere. This was an added test day carried out under conditions in which low-temperature combustion occurred during the first round of testing. CFI-G was a late addition to ACME, where the experiment was jointly selected for spaceflight by the National Science Foundation (NSF) and the Center for the Advancement of Science in Space (CASIS) rather than NASA. The selection was notably announced after the core ACME hardware had launched to the ISS and just weeks before ACME’s on-orbit operations began. It is effectively an extension of ACME’s Flame Design experiment for which the PI and Co-I are respectively Axelbaum and Sunderland. And it is likewise a follow-on to the Cool Flames Investigation (CFI), led by Williams, in which liquid droplets were burned rather than gaseous fuel.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p3,2,"Although CFI-G wasn’t selected for spaceflight by NASA, its ISS operations and the development of its unique hardware, software, and procedure were carried out by the same teams of personnel as the other ACME experiments, primarily from ZIN Technologies, Inc.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p4,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 4 2. Science Overview A. Background This background is the Project Summary from the experiment’s 2017 proposal in response to a solicitation from the NSF/CASIS Collaboration on Combustion and Thermal Transport Processes Research on the International Space Station to Benefit Life on Earth. Overview: Cool diffusion flames were discovered in 2012 aboard the ISS. This spawned a rapidly growing research field, but only a small number of measurements at limited conditions are available. Consequently, cool flame kinetics mechanisms cannot be adequately tested or advanced. This could be impeding the development of cleaner and more efficient internal combustion engines. It is proposed here to observe cool diffusion flames supported by porous spherical burners fed with propane, n-butane, and n-pentane. Normal and inverse flames will be considered for a broad range of dilution conditions and pressures. The ISS is the ideal platform for observing cool diffusion flames because they typically require 20 s to establish and they could burn indefinitely. Tests will be performed using the ACME insert in the CIR. Minimal hardware development will be required because the spherical porous burners and gas delivery system will be those used by the Flame Design flight experiment, while the diagnostics will be those used by the CFI flight experiment.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p4,1,"The objectives are to observe diverse cool diffusion flames aboard the ISS, to simulate the tests with an advanced CFD model, and to identify or develop a chemical kinetics mechanism that accurately models these conditions. Intellectual Merit: Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences. The proposed experiments will take advantage of many state-of-the-art diagnostics that are already available on the ISS. The flame system to be studied will allow steady spherical cool diffusion flames to be observed for the first time. An advanced CFD model will simulate the flames and will aid the testing and development of cool flame kinetics models. Observing cool flames burning as steady spherical diffusion flames will yield valuable insight into cool flame chemical kinetics. The measurements proposed cannot be performed in normal gravity or in ground-based microgravity facilities. Broader Impacts: The research will be disseminated widely to the combustion research community and to industry. It should advance the state-of-the-art in cool flame kinetics mechanisms, which in turn could lead to cleaner, more efficient internal combustion engines.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p4,2,This project will have several educational impacts. Graduate students will conduct much of the,,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p5,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 5 research. The research results will be integrated into three combustion courses taught by the PI. The PI will team with two organizations at UMD - the Women in Engineering Program and the Center for Minorities in Science and Engineering - to foster interest in science and engineering among female and minority secondary school students. A day-long flame laboratory will be developed for secondary school students that will foster their interest in combustion and scientific inquiry. B. Rationale for Microgravity This is the ‘Justification for Testing Aboard the ISS’ section (3.1) of the experiment’s 2017 proposal in response to a solicitation from the NSF/CASIS Collaboration on Combustion and Thermal Transport Processes Research on the International Space Station to Benefit Life on Earth. The included equation is formatted differently in this copy. Previous ground-based microgravity experiments have demonstrated the scientific value of spherical, burner-supported diffusion flames. These one-dimensional flames offer a simple, fundamental configuration to improve the understanding of diffusion flame behavior and to explore such effects as transport, kinetics, soot formation, and radiation, which can be more pronounced under microgravity conditions (Atreya et al. 1992; Law et al. 2001; Lecoustre et al. 2012).",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p5,1,"Spherically-symmetric flames are not generally practical in normal gravity. This can be explained by considering the Richardson number, which is the ratio of the buoyancy force to the inertial force, Ri = g (ΔT L) / (T u2) , (1) where g is the acceleration of gravity, T is temperature, L is the characteristic length scale, and u is the characteristic velocity. Taking as representative values L = 50 mm, u = 5 cm/s, ΔT = 2000 K, and T = 300 K, Ri = 1300 in normal gravity. Ri can be reduced with a judicious choice of fuels and inert; Law et al. (2001) have shown that nearly spherical flames can be created in normal gravity using low pressure hydrogen inverse flames. However for typical spherical diffusion flames the only way to obtain Ri << 1 is to reduce g by four orders of magnitude. This requires a microgravity environment. Available microgravity drop tower facilities do not offer sufficient time to observe quasi-steady spherical diffusion flames, let alone their radiative extinction followed by cool diffusion flames. Experiments performed in the 5 s drop facility confirm that typical spherical diffusion flames continue growing during 5 s (see Fig. 2). Observations of cool flames in the FLEX, FLEX-2, and CFI droplet experiments involved or will involve burn times of up to 60 s.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p5,2,"Analytical work supports these observations of slow flame development. Radial velocities in spherical diffusion flames scale with 1 / r2, where r is radius. Thus, at only a few burner diameters distance from the sphere, diffusion is the dominant transport mechanism. The characteristic diffusion time is r2/D, where D is mass diffusivity. For r = 25 mm and D = 20 mm2/s, this diffusion time is 30 s. Decreasing the flame size can reduce this time, but will not permit the observation of radiative extinction followed by cool diffusion flames. Our analytical and numerical calculations indicate that approximately 30 s of jitter-free (less than 10-5 g) microgravity is needed to approach quasi-steady state for these flames. The ISS is the only microgravity platform that can provide this environment.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p6,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 6 C. Science Objectives The objectives text presented here is the ‘Hypotheses and Objectives’ section (1.3) of the experiment’s 2017 proposal in response to a solicitation from the NSF/CASIS Collaboration on Combustion and Thermal Transport Processes Research on the International Space Station to Benefit Life on Earth. The hypotheses of this research are that testing aboard the ISS can demonstrate the following for the first time, contributing to an improved understanding of cool flame kinetics. 1. Steady-state cool diffusion flames on spherical porous burners. 2. Cool diffusion flames burning propane, n-butane, and n-pentane. 3. Cool diffusion flames for a wide range of Zst, Tad, and pressures. 4. Inverse cool diffusion flames. 5. Cool diffusion flames for which a decrease in fluent flow rate will cause extinction, whereas an increase will cause a transition to a hot diffusion flame, with oscillation possible between hot and cool flames.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p6,1,"The research objectives of the proposed work are as follows. 1. Plan and support flight testing on the ISS. 2. Determine the conditions for which cool diffusion flames occur for propane, n-butane, and n-pentane; normal and inverse flames; and a broad range of Zst, Tad, and fluent flow rate. 3. Support the flight tests with advanced CFD simulations. 4. Identify or develop a chemical kinetics mechanism that yields good agreement between the CFD predictions and the ISS measurements. 5. Disseminate the results as widely as possible. In contrast to objective 2 (and hypothesis 3), it was not feasible with the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) and ACME chamber insert to conduct tests with n-pentane as proposed (because of its low vapor pressure) and testing was instead conducted with ethane as an alternate. 3. Hardware Overview A. General Description This section is copied from section 2.A of the April 2021 version of ‘ACME Engineering Implementation’ which should be consulted for additional details. References to other sections within the boxed text of this section refer to the ACME Engineering Implementation document.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p6,2,"This section provides a general description of the ACME engineering from the ‘3.0 Experimental’ section of the following 2014 conference paper: Dennis P. Stocker, Fumiaki Takahashi, J. Mark Hickman, and Andrew C. Suttles, “Gaseous Non-Premixed Flame Research Planned for the International Space Station,” Spring Technical Meeting of the Central States Section of the Combustion Institute, Tulsa, OK, 16– 18 March 2014.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p7,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 7 IMPORTANT: The 2104 paper from which this section was extracted proceeded completion of the spaceflight hardware which was mostly launched in 2017. As a result, it does not accurately describe the final engineering in some regards. Therefore updates, clarifications, additional details, shortcomings, references, etc. have been included in boxed notes like this following the original description. As a minor note, the sizes of figures were adjusted in this report, but none were cropped. As previously discussed, the ACME experiments will be conducted within the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) using a chamber insert, depicted in Figure 2, which was designed to accommodate gas delivery to a variety of exchangeable burners. Besides the chamber insert, other ACME-unique hardware includes a high-definition color camera and an avionics package, which is for data acquisition and control. Figure 2: Design of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) chamber insert that will be used in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on ISS.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p7,1,"ACME’s chamber insert, which is ~0.4 m in diameter, provides common mounting for a variety of burners on the chamber axis (but only one at a time) and includes a retracting resistive igniter. As depicted in Figure 3, there will initially be gas-jet burners (with inner diameters ranging from 0.4 to 3.2 mm), a small coflow burner (where the two concentric tubes have inner diameters of 2.1 and 25 mm), porous spherical burners (with diameters ranging from 6.35 to 12.7 mm), and two flat porous burners for the BRE experiment (with inner diameters of 25 and 50 mm). Most burners will be equipped with a surface thermocouple, and each BRE burner will have two heat flux transducers. The internal pressure of the porous burners will be measured to allow monitoring of the pressure differential across the outlet surface. The igniter will have a crew-exchangeable tip and arm (to accommodate the various burners) and its stepper-motor drive will allow for precise positioning.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p8,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 8 Figure 3: ACME burner types, including from left to right, gas-jet burners, a co-flow burner, porous spherical burners, and flat porous burners for the BRE experiment. Ultimately, the spherical burners were limited to a single diameter of 6.35 mm, and the fabricated gas-jet burners were limited to two sizes with nominal inner diameters of 1.3 and 2.1 mm. However, only the larger gas-jet burner was used during the spaceflight tests. The burners are addressed in detail in section 3.C. Unfortunately, the spherical burners have not always yielded spherically symmetric flames, presumably as a result of (1) greater burner flow through the joint between the porous sphere and supply tube, and (2) heat loss to the support tube (encompassing the supply tube) causing local flame quenching. Two types of igniter tips have been used during ACME testing. Most tests have been conducted using a loop igniter tip like those which had been successfully used in the droplet combustion experiments which preceded ACME research in the CIR. However, some tests were conducted with a coil igniter tip, where that included the CFI-G investigation and the second-round testing for both the Burning Rate Emulator (BRE) and Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s- Flame) experiments, with the exception of the latter’s first day testing. For more information about the igniter tips, see section 3.D.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p8,1,"The chamber insert includes three mass flow controllers (visible on the left of Fig. 2) for the delivery of fuel, oxidizer or inert (e.g., helium or carbon dioxide), and nitrogen to the burner. The maximum fuel flow is 2 slpm (on a nitrogen basis), whereas much higher flows, e.g., 10 slpm (on a nitrogen basis), are possible for the oxidizer and nitrogen lines. The crew can exchange the mass flow controllers to provide appropriate ranges that maximize the flow accuracy. The gas delivery system enables on-orbit dilution of the fuel or oxidizer (but not both simultaneously), coflow, inverse flames (e.g., oxidizer delivery to the center of a coflow burner), and premixed flames, although the latter are not planned for any of the current ACME experiments. Gases can also be delivered premixed to the space station where that is necessary in some circumstances, such as the blending of different fuel gases. CIR’s standard bottle sizes are 1, 2.25, and 3.8 liters but ACME will sometimes use smaller bottles as a hazard control to limit the deficient reactant (e.g., fuel) that can be released into the combustion chamber. Thus far, ACME tests were not conducted with carbon dioxide as a diluent. Furthermore, the fuel diluted with helium was premixed prior to launch rather than on orbit. Ultimately, oxidizer flows of up to 20 slpm (on a nitrogen basis) were possible, while the nitrogen flow was limited to a",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p9,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 9 maximum of 5 slpm. ACME has not used gas bottles smaller than 1 liter as indicated above; the bottle fill pressure was instead limited as an additional hazard control. ACME’s primary flame diagnostics are a suite of imaging systems including five video cameras and two illumination systems, of which three cameras are part of the CIR facility and are already in use on orbit. All but one of the cameras is mounted outside of the chamber. Collectively, the imaging systems will enable color, CH*, and OH* imaging; pyrometry; measurement of the soot volume fraction; and operational support. However, it is unlikely that all functions would be exercised at any one time as will become apparent in the subsequent discussion. Color imaging is provided by two cameras: a high-definition digital camera mounted outside of the chamber and a standard analog camera mounted on the chamber insert (with a turning mirror). Both cameras are part of the ACME hardware that is now in development. The analog camera signal will be downlinked in near real time to support the experiment operations. Light- emitting diodes within the chamber can be used to illuminate the igniter and burner to both support the operations and provide reference images. The 12-bit digital camera records at up to 30 full frames (1360x1024) per second, and is equipped with a motorized lens with independent control of the zoom, iris, and focus.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p9,1,"The digital camera will be equipped with a filter assembly with four options, where selection of a clear “filter” allows for color imaging. Color imaging can also be carried out with a blue-green BG7 Schott glass filter, which balances the color planes to avoid saturation in the red while maintaining sensitivity in the blue and green. The natural flame color can be approximately reconstructed, but the primary purpose of the filter is pyrometry and measurement of the soot volume fraction, again via the techniques of [23-24]. Alternately, CH* imaging can be conducted with this camera because the filter assembly includes a 430-nm bandpass filter with a 10-nm full width at half-maximum transmission (FWHM). A 450-nm bandpass filter (again with a 10-nm FWHM) can be used with the CH* filter to effectively subtract interfering broadband soot emission. However, the filter assembly rotates slowly so such subtraction effectively requires a separate test with each filter and of course reproducible flame conditions. The video from the analog operations (i.e., ops) camera, also referred to as the internal camera, is not archived in space. The video is instead recorded on the ground in the operations center. Its gain was sometimes adjusted for improved imaging of dim flames, e.g., approaching extinction.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p9,2,"The sensitivity of the color digital camera, often referenced as the ACME DataCam (i.e., Data Camera), to the dim microgravity flames was such that it was often used with long exposure times and thus low framing rates, for example on the order of 1 frame per second. The clear “filter” is of fused silica. Unfortunately, the thickness of the blue-green BG7 Schott glass filter and the added antireflective (AR) coating caused too great a reduction in the red, so it does not balance the intensity of the three color planes as intended and thereby can limit the accuracy of temperature determination. Meanwhile, rather than the 450-nm bandpass filter described above, the high definition color camera was equipped with a bandpass filter for imaging formaldehyde emissions. For more information, see the Data Camera Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-010 Rev. A). Imaging of the dim hydrogen flames in the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment was particularly challenging with these, if not all, cameras.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p10,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 10 Pyrometry can alternately be accomplished with CIR’s monochrome High-Bit Depth Multispectral (HiBMs) camera, equipped with a Liquid-Crystal Tunable Filter (LCTF), which allows for imaging at multiple spectra, but requires a quasi-steady flame for measurement at each desired band. This camera has a telecentric optical system and is recorded at 512x512 and 30 frames per second (fps). Although it has a 1024x1024 pixel array, binning has been necessary because of signal loss through the LCTF. Both soot pyrometry and Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) are possible, where ACME is equipped with a crew-replaceable array of 5 silicon carbide (SiC) fibers. The fibers are orthogonal to the flame axis and the array can be translated along the flame axis to the desired position downstream of the burner, e.g., based on the downlink of the analog video. During set up, the crew can manually orient the array so that it is orthogonal to either ACME’s color digital camera or the HiBMs camera with the LCTF. An alternate fiber array including a platinum wire will be used for inflight characterization, which in combination with preflight calibration will enable determination of the soot volume fraction [24].",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p10,1,"In contrast to the preceding paragraph, ACME did not use a HiBMs camera for pyrometry, but instead relied on use of ACME’s color DataCam for such temperature measurements. For an example of how this can be done, see the techniques described in the following papers: P.B. Kuhn, B. Ma, B.C. Connelly, M.D. Smooke, and M.B. Long, “Soot and Thin-filament Pyrometry Using a Color Digital Camera,” Proceedings of the Combustion Institute, 33 (2011) 743-750. B. Ma, M. B. Long, “Absolute light calibration using S-type thermocouples,” Proceedings of the Combustion Institute, 34 (2) (2013) 3531-3539. Thus far, TFP tests have been conducted with 14-micron fibers spaced apart by 5 mm for a total span of 20 mm (in the case of 5 intact ceramic fibers), neglecting optional translation of the array. Tests have been conducted using arrays with and without the platinum wire in the furthest downstream position. Tests have also been conducted with some of the fibers broken and subsequently removed, leaving only three fibers in the final testing for the BRE and Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame (CLD Flame) experiments and for all of the CFI-G testing.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p10,2,"CIR has a second HiBMs camera, without a filter, that will be used in combination with CIR’s illumination package for alternate measurements of the soot volume fraction. This will be accomplished via an extinction technique previously employed in microgravity [3,6,26], where the camera and the collimated light source (with its two fiber-coupled laser diodes) are positioned at opposite windows. The images are recorded at 1024x1024 and 30 fps. Thus far, the primary purpose of this combined system has been the measurement of the droplet diameter for determination of the droplet burning rate. A HiBMs camera without the Liquid Crystal Tunable Filter (LCTF) has been used throughout much, but not all, of ACME’s operations. In the first round of the CLD Flame experiment, it was used without a filter for flame imaging, but this proved to be largely unsuccessful because the camera is very insensitive to blue. Afterward, it was equipped with a red filter corresponding to the CIR illumination package for the rest of the ACME testing. Unfortunately, that filter is sufficiently broad that significant incandescence can pass through the filter limiting its utility for determinations of soot volume fraction via light extinction, e.g., through the technique described in the following papers:",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p11,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 11 D.L. Urban, Z.G. Yuan, P.B. Sunderland, K.C., Lin, Z. Dai, and G.M. Faeth, “Smoke-Point Properties of Nonbuoyant Round Laminar Jet Diffusion Flames,” Proceedings of the Twenty-Eighth Symposium (International) on Combustion 28 (2000) 1965-1972. F.J. Diez, C. Aalburg, P.B. Sunderland, D.L. Urban, Z.-G. Yuan, G.M. Faeth, “Soot properties of laminar jet diffusion flames in microgravity,” Combustion and Flame, 156 (2009) 1514-1524. P.S. Greenberg and J.C. Ku, “Soot Volume Fraction Imaging,” Applied Optics, 36 (22) (1997) 5514- 5522. Nonetheless, soot volume fraction can also be determined from the color DataCam images. During ACME operations, the HiBMs camera is typically archived at 15 fps because of data limits for transfer and downlink operations. And because operations are constrained by the data limits, image data from this camera are sometimes deleted without downlinking to enable additional testing. And in some testing, HiBMs images aren’t even acquired given their lesser value. Unfortunately, the HiBMs backlight illumination is significantly obstructed by the bracket for the electrode mesh further limiting its utility for the E-FIELD Flames experiment. This was a design trade-off necessitated primarily by ACME’s large number of cameras and other optical instruments.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p11,1,"CIR’s Low-Light-Level Ultraviolet (LLL-UV) package is a monochrome camera with an intensifier (for 280-700 nm) and a 310-nm bandpass filter (with a 10-nm FWHM) for OH* imaging. Again as a result of the signal loss through the filter, binning is employed such that the OH* images are recorded at 512x512 and 30 fps. During ACME operations, the LLL-UV camera has only been used for the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment. While not discussed in the preceding section, another intensified camera was used throughout ACME testing, although its image data was sometimes deleted or not even acquired to allow the performance of additional tests. This was the Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) camera which was generally configured with its CH* filter, where an exception is some s-Flame testing with particularly dim flames. Due to design trade-offs, its Field of View (FOV) was partially obstructed by the arms of the Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) fiber array when it was moved inwards to the flame. More information about this camera can be found in its characterization report, CIR-RPT- 4600.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p11,2,"The ACME chamber insert will also be equipped with non-imaging optical diagnostics including both thermopile radiometers and Photomultiplier Tubes (PMTs). There is a crew-exchangeable linear array of radiometers of which there are two versions supporting the initial set of experiments. The array has a fixed position which is parallel to the chamber axis and centered (along the chamber length) relative to the chamber windows and thus most of the other instrumentation. One array is equipped with a single wide-view radiometer with two narrow- view radiometers that are downstream and two more that are upstream, where all views are orthogonal to the chamber axis, as depicted in Figure 4. The narrow-view radiometers are intended to support studies of gas-jet flames, where ACME can accommodate shorter burners, making the “lower” detectors useful. The other array, also seen in Fig. 4, provides for three wide- view measurements of the flame, where one view is masked to nominally block radiant emission",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p12,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 12 from the burner. It was specifically designed for the BRE experiment but will be a resource available to other ACME experiments. Given that the radiometer array can be exchanged by the crew, alternate arrays could be developed. Figure 4: Crew-exchangeable radiometer arrays on the ACME chamber insert: (top) standard (ACME) array of 5 radiometers, and (bottom) array with three radiometers, designed for the BRE experiment, where the lowest detector is masked to avoid view of the burner. A third radiometer array was used in the CFI-G experiment, where it is important to understand that only a single radiometer array can be used at a time. Unlike ACME’s other radiometer arrays, each sensor in the CFI-G array has two fixed gains, i.e., a high gain and low gain. It is described in the Cool Flames Radiometer Characterization Report (CIR-RPT-4600). The narrow-view radiometers in the standard array are only fully sensitive in small spots, but they are each sensitive in a much greater Field of View (FOV) than represented in the preceding figure.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p13,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 13 They do not have lenses; their FOV is a result of an aperture where most of their FOV is within the penumbra which is only visible to a portion of the sensor. Figures representing the measured FOVs of the as-built standard and BRE radiometer arrays are found in section 3.F of this document. Unfortunately, the radiometers in the BRE array typically saturated in the first round of BRE testing, so the standard array was used in its second round. However, those radiometers similarly saturated in many of round 2 tests. In this regard, each of ACME’s BRE and standard radiometers have a fixed gain. For more information about the standard radiometer array used for most ACME testing, see the Radiometer Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-011). Meanwhile, you can learn about the array used in the first round of BRE testing from the BRE Radiometer Characterization Report (ACME- RPT-012). An additional wide-view radiometer, that is fixed and not crew exchangeable, is positioned downstream of the burner and flame at 30o from the chamber axis. This detector is primarily intended to support the spherical flame experiments and will have a quicker response than the more sensitive detectors that will used in the linear arrays. Like the radiometers on the two linear arrays, it will have a spectral range of nominally 0.2-11 micron and its field of view will be established by an appropriately selected aperture.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p13,1,"The FOV of the off-set radiometer is also shown in section 3.F. Learn more about this sensor from the Radiometer Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-011). To detect the quenching of dim flames, the chamber insert will be equipped with a set of three PMTs. Each PMT will have a wide-angle view, a sight line that is orthogonal to the chamber axis, and will be centered (along the chamber length) relative to the chamber windows so that it is aligned with most of the other instrumentation. The PMTs’ spectral range will be 230-700 nm, i.e., visible and near ultraviolet, but one will have a 310-nm bandpass filter and another will have a 430-nm bandpass filter (where both are with a 10-nm FWHM). The pair of detectors will enable measurement of OH* and CH* emission, respectively. The third PMT will be equipped with a neutral density filter to prevent saturation. Like many other components, the PMT array can be removed from the chamber insert by the crew. Unlike the radiometers, each PMT had a readily adjustable gain. However, the PMT signals sometimes saturated when the gains were set too high. See the PMT Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-013) to learn more about the three Photomultiplier Tube (PMTs).",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p13,2,"The chamber insert is also equipped with six far-field thermocouples to measure the spread of the thermal field in the spherical flame experiments. They are of limited utility for other experiments because they are positioned upstream of the burner tip. Therefore, they will generally not be installed by the crew to reduce the chance of accidental damage. There will be two versions of a four-thermocouple rake to allow a choice of near and far measurements. Other arrays could be designed to support future experiments, but they cannot extend downstream of the burner tip without interfering with other (e.g., optical) measurements.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p14,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 14 The two lone thermocouples, which are both at nominally 200 mm from the center of the spherical burner, have been installed throughout ACME testing. The tip of one was seen to be slightly bent early in testing, as discussed and depicted in section 3.G of this report. Only the short (or far) version of the radiometer rake has been used thus far, where factors include the need for crew time to switch between rakes and the heat loss that could result from thermocouples in the long (close) rake. The short (far) rake’s thermocouple tips should nominally be at 45, 60, 100, and 100 mm from the spherical burner center. For thermocouples at the same distance from the burner center, the angle between the rays from the burner center to the two thermocouples should be 90 degrees. The bead size of the thermocouples was not measured, but it is undoubtedly at least twice the size of the 0.2 mm diameter wire. The two thermocouples closest to the burner outlets on each four-thermocouple rake are type B, while the others are type K. Except for the final test day, CFIG’s second-round testing was carried out where the fine tip of the thermocouple closest to the flame was bent at roughly 90 degrees. In the following section (3.B), his can be seen in Figure 5 where, as a size reference, the spherical burner is 6.35 mm in diameter.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p14,1,"Experiment-unique hardware is also mounted on the chamber insert. For example, to accommodate the E-FIELD Flames experiment, the insert is equipped with a high-voltage supply and a crew-removable electrode mesh which can be set between +10 and -10 kV. The circular mesh is orthogonal to the chamber axis and is positioned (during set up) at either ~35 or ~50 mm downstream of the insulated burner. The ion current between the burner and electrode mesh will be determined via measurement of the voltage drop across a known resistor and application of Ohm’s Law. Additional images of the chamber insert can be seen in subsequent sections of this report. The hardware specific to E-FIELD Flames is discussed further in section 2.B. Additional supporting measurements will be made, where the chamber pressure is a simple example. The ACME experiments will use the CIR’s gas chromatograph for pre- and post-test analysis of the chamber atmosphere, e.g., for oxygen, nitrogen, carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide, and fuel gases. Acceleration measurements will be provided by the Space Acceleration Measurement System (SAMS) [27], which is the primary instrument for such measurements on ISS and previously served that role for the space shuttle.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p14,2,"The CIR’s Gas Chromatograph Instrumentation Package (GCIP) was not used for the CLD Flame or E-FIELD Flames investigations and thus far was only sometimes used during BRE, Flame Design, and s-Flame testing. Its use requires significant time and as such effectively reduces the number of tests that can be conducted. However, estimates of the chamber contents were computed assuming that the fuel in a normal flame test (for example) was either fully or not at all oxidized, e.g., for unsustained ignition in the latter case. The CIR facility was equipped with a 3-axis SAMS sensor head and the acceleration measurements, along with relevant documentation, are accessible via the home page for the Principal Investigator Microgravity Services (PIMS), https://gipoc.grc.nasa.gov/wp/pims/home/. To be clear, the",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p15,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 15 acceleration measurements are not archived with the ACME experiment data in the Physical Sciences Informatics (PSI) website, www.nasa.gov/PSI. All ACME experiments will be constant volume (i.e., rather than constant pressure) studies, where the chamber’s free volume will be about 90 liters. Burner flows will generally be constrained to limit the pressure rise, and tests will typically be 0.5-2 minutes in duration. The chamber’s free volume is approximately 83 liters depending on the installed hardware. The ACME experiments will be configured, but not conducted, by the ISS crew. Instead, the experiments will be commanded from the ground, specifically from the Telescience Support Center (TSC) at the NASA Glenn Research Center in Cleveland, Ohio. They can be conducted in a nominally automated mode using pre-programmed scripts. However, it will also be possible to effectively operate the experiments in a “manual” mode, where changes can be made in response to the analog video downlink and the numerical data available in the telemetry stream. For example, a flow rate could be adjusted to approach a stability or soot limit. Step and ramp changes can be made in the flow rate(s) or electric field potential. The TFP fiber array can be translated uniformly through a soot-free flame to get a continuum measurement of the temperature field. In summary, ACME’s experiment control will allow for broad flexibility.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p15,1,"The TSC is now also referred to as the Glenn ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC). Thus far, the ACME tests have always been conducted in a nominally automated mode. B. Hardware Shortcomings CFI-G’s two primary challenges were in initiating cool flames and discerning their presence. Based on the findings of the earlier studies with droplet combustion, non-premixed cool flames can be directly ignited by limiting the ignition energy or can occur after the self-extinguishment of (normal) hot flames. Tests intended to directly ignite cool flames sometimes unintentionally resulted in hot flames. Meanwhile, evidence of cool flames was first discovered in review of the data a few days after the testing. During that testing, it wasn’t known that the cool flames were present after hot flame extinction and the tests were terminated, which unknowingly and sadly shortened the duration of the cool flame combustion. In later testing with the long/near thermocouple, two of the tests exhibited repeated hot flame quenching and reignition, which resulting from intervening low-temperature combustion. It appears that the closest thermocouple, approximately 1 cm from the burner surface, was a factor in the reignition. The exposed type-B thermocouple includes platinum, a catalyst for the oxidation of hydrocarbons. The catalysis contributed to the rapid reignition limiting the duration of the low- temperature combustion in these tests.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p15,2,"A challenge for cool flame detection was concern about potential damage to the CFI intensified camera by exposing it to bright light, especially from the energized igniter, while the intensifier gain was set high. There were also issues with the Image Processing and Storage Units (IPSUs) which affected CFI-G operations. On 30 Aug. 2021 (GMT 242), the primary IPSU, used to record the imaging from both the ACME DataCam and CFI intensified camera (IPSU-G, also known as IPSU5), caused issues and",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p16,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 16 eventually stopped working. Only the first two tests on that day were successful, where two tests aborted due to an IPSU error, and three tests failed to ignite. In closing checkout, it was determined that the hot-wire igniter tip had also failed. With the IPSU-G failure, the test day was nearly a total loss. Except for a small number of sample images downloaded during the midst of testing, the primary image data from even the successful tests was lost. Furthermore, there was no more propane available for use in inverse-flame testing, so the test day could not be repeated. However, astronaut Mark Vande Hei replaced the failed igniter tip on Sept. 8 and the failed hard drive in the IPSU-G on Sept. 10, and CFI-G testing successfully resumed on Sept. 13, just 2 weeks after the double failure.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p16,1,"IPSU1, which is used with the High Bit-depth Multi-spectral (HiBMs) camera and the opposed CIR illumination package, ceased functioning on 29 March 2021 (GMT 088). Tests 21088M1-M3 aborted due to an error with IPSU1 communication, where test M4 was successful when the test was run without use of the HiBMs camera. The troublesome IPSU1 was replaced by astronaut Megan McArthur on May 18 (GMT 138), but it was not available during the intervening test days. However, this wasn’t particularly a loss to CFI-G because that camera, with its red filter, is only sensitive to the red backlight from the CIR illumination package and visible thermal radiation, e.g., from glowing soot particles. Furthermore, the illumination could make it more difficult to discern cool flames, so the HiBMs camera, illumination package, and IPSU1 were rarely used for CFI-G. The principal exception was during the second round of testing. With the inverse-flame tests, the soot is generated outside of the flames, so this photography was used to track potential soot deposition on the chamber windows, etc. However, the end-of-day imaging did not appear to change over time, suggesting that the soot was not an issue for the chamber windows. However, the Photomultiplier Tube (PMT) modules and radiometers were closer to the flame than the windows and might had been adversely affected by soot.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p16,2,"When at a small fraction (e.g., less than 10%) of the maximum flow rate, Mass Flow Controller (MFC) performance has been seen to be poor during CFI-G testing, i.e., where actual flow rate differed from that desired. Meanwhile, at high flow rates of fuel and nitrogen, the burner does not produce spherically symmetric flames. While nominally spherical at moderate flow rates, the flame’s center becomes increasing offset from the burner center. More specifically, the flame is shifted toward the supply tube indicating that the sintered attachment of the porous sphere to the supply tube is more ‘open’ than the pores of the spherical burner. In other words, the flow from the burner is not spherically symmetric. At sufficiently high flow rates, the flame becomes obviously non-spherical, where it can appear to be ellipsoidal or even parabolic in shape. In the latter case, the flame locally quenches near the supply tube.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p16,3,"During Burning Rate Emulator (BRE) testing on 23 Dec. 2020, it was discovered that the lowest of the 14-micron silicon carbide fibers on the Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) fiber array had broken. Recovery plans were developed and enacted on 18 Jan. 2021 to remove the broken fragments from the harp-like array. Unfortunately, the next fiber was broken during the delicate operations where the fibers are only spaced 5 mm apart. Although ACME does have alternate arrays, it also had months of remaining testing, so the decision was made to continue testing with only 3 fibers (instead of 5) for the final BRE testing and the subsequent CLD Flame and CFI-G tests. This was a minor loss, where the fiber array was only occasionally used in CFI-G testing.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p17,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 17 Except for the final test day, CFIG’s second-round testing was carried out where the fine tip of the thermocouple closest to the flame was bent at roughly 90 degrees. This can be seen in Figure 5 where, as a size reference, the spherical burner is 6.35 mm in diameter. Figure 5: Image of the spherical burner, the coiled hot-wire igniter (just to the left), and two thermocouples (in the lower right) which are part of the short/far thermocouple rake. In addition to sections 3.A-B, shortcomings are discussed in Appendices B and C of this report and in the ACME Engineering Implementation document. The latter document includes discussion of deviations and waivers (in section 4.B), etc. 4. Preliminary Results A. Changed Test Plans The most significant change was the substitution of ethane for n-pentane in the test matrix after it was determined through ground testing that the use of the latter was not feasible because of the n-pentane’s low vapor pressure.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p17,1,"An additional normal-flames test day was added after the conclusion of the inverse-flame testing. That change was made because it wasn’t until after completion of the first round of testing, through analysis of the final day’s results, that evidence of cool flames was discovered in the CFI- G results. The added test day, conducted on 18 Oct. 2021 (GMT 291) was carried out with the long (near) thermocouple rake with the goal of measuring the cool flame temperature. Unfortunately, an unwanted outcome was hot-flame reignition (from the cool flames) caused by catalysis from the platinum in the exposed type-B thermocouple closest to the burner. While temperature measurements were made, the cool flames on that additional day of testing were regrettably short-lived. While this was arguably a hardware shortcoming, the use of fine type B thermocouples had been recommended in the ACME science requirements for the closest thermocouples on the long (near) rake.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p18,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 18 Other changes to the test plans were minor in comparison. B. Overview of Experiment Test Operations While not yet prepared, additional details will be made available in the ACME/CFI-G Experimental Procedure document. The narrative description of the experimental procedure is an excerpt from the ‘Methods / Experimental’ section of the following conference paper. Y.-C. Chien, J. Tinajero, D. Stocker, U. Hegde, and D. Dunn-Rankin, “Electric Field Effects on Flames in Microgravity on the International Space Station,” 2018 Spring Technical Meeting of the Central States Section of The Combustion Institute, Minneapolis, MN, 20-22 May 2018. While the paper was written for the Electric-Field Effects on Laminar Diffusion Flames (E-FIELD Flames) experiment, much of the discussion applies to ACME in general, including the CFI-G investigation. The figure referenced in this excerpt is not included herein. Clarifications and additional information, not in the conference paper, are included in boxed notes like this.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p18,1,"The CIR experiments are configured, but not conducted, by the ISS crew. Thus far, ACME’s activity has included the majority of the crew members, regardless of their nationalities. Most often the astronauts and cosmonauts exchange gas bottles, mass flow controllers, and igniter tips. In the latter case, the igniter tip exchange can occur after failure or proactively before failure when the crew already has changes to make changes in the interior of the insert. While these activities may seem mundane, the crew play a critical role in setting up the hardware for each experiment. As an extreme example, there were crew activities on 8 separate days in Sept.-Oct. 2017 to configure the CIR and ACME hardware for the first ACME experiment, namely the Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame (CLD Flame) experiment. In contrast, only two crew days were required to reconfigure the hardware for the first E-FIELD Flames tests because of the initial use of the same coflow burner. Planning is critical because crew activity is scheduled three weeks in advance, for which detailed procedures must be prepared and uplinked to the ISS. As a correction, including two maintenance activities, there were 11 crew days in Sept.-Oct. 2017 to prepare for the initial CLD Flame testing on the space station.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p18,2,"All CIR tests are remotely commanded from the ground from NASA’s Glenn ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC) in Cleveland, Ohio. Typically, four to six people staff four consoles during the testing. The CIR hardware is controlled from the Rack Officer (RO) and Data Management Officer (DMO) consoles, and the ACME hardware is controlled from the Payload Developer (PD) console. Two shifts of personnel are scheduled to staff these three consoles on test days because they typically last 14 hours with the many pre- and post-testing activities. Given that the tests are remotely commanded, the operations are carried out in the day and evening in Cleveland, e.g., from 7 AM to 9 PM. A day’s tests, i.e., with flames, are normally conducted in a period of 10 hours of less. Tests are normally conducted on three weekdays per two weeks. On intervening weekdays, the image data is downlinked and the crew often swap one or both gas bottles. Transferring and downlinking data from a day’s tests also requires a 2-shift day for the RO and DMO consoles, where it is always necessary to verify that data was downlinked without errors and that data is not missing. Those two positions are also staffed to guide and oversee the crew",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p19,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 19 activity. As a result, the CIR consoles are normally actively staffed on four weekdays per week. A team of four carry out the detailed planning for the CIR and ACME console staff, and the daily operational plans typically exceed 50 pages on days including testing. The science console allows for review of the analog video and non-imaging telemetry (at 1 Hz) in near real time but doesn’t allow for commanding. In the case of E-FIELD Flames, arrangements were made so that the analog video is streamed to a secure computer at UC Irvine to allow the research team to participate in test operations and to recommend adjustments to the day’s planned parameters. That team does not see the telemetry during the testing, but is in communication via phone and e-mail with their NASA research partners at the GRC ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC). While there are many preparatory steps, the tests are ultimately conducted using an uploaded software script and parameter files. In advance of the test day, the UC Irvine science team plans the nearly 60 parameters in the voltage sweep script and specifies other parameters that are configured outside of the script, such as camera settings. It is also possible to effectively conduct tests in a ‘manual’ mode, where parameters are changed in response to the analog video downlink or telemetry. For example, the electric field potential can be adjusted to approach a stability or soot limit.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p19,1,"Provisions were made to enable the investigators from all ACME experiments to remotely view the analog video while they communicated verbally with NASA science personnel in the operations center. All ACME flame tests, including those for CFI-G, were conducted in a nominally automated manner using software scripts and extensive parameter files.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p19,2,"Typically, ten E-FIELD Flames tests are conducted per test day. Despite the short test durations, the low rate results from several unavoidable factors. After a test is conducted, the 125-Hz non- imaging data is downlinked for immediate review by the science team. A small sample of images are often downlinked to ensure that the camera exposures are appropriate, where the times for those images are typically selected based on non-imaging data to provide extreme examples. The review helps to verify parameters or plan for adjustments in subsequent tests, e.g., to select the voltage range after determining where the ion current saturates for the given flame conditions. After a test is conducted, a fan circulates the chamber atmosphere after which the chamber is vented (to space) back to atmospheric pressure, if needed. Midway through a day’s E-FIELD Flames tests, the atmosphere is circulated for 15 minutes through a filter cartridge filled with molecular sieves to capture any soot and adsorb at least a portion of the combustion products, e.g., carbon dioxide, water vapor, and incomplete combustion products. The tests are only conducted when both Ku and S band communication is available through NASA’s Tracking and Data Relay Satellite (TDRS) System. Depending on the various orbits, there are periodic communication outages with a loss of signal (LOS) that can be a short handover (from one satellite to another) or can last as long as 30 minutes.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p19,3,"Typically, on the order of 12 tests were conducted per CFI-G Flame test day. In contrast to E-FIELD Flames, recirculation during CFI-G test operations was only conducted prior to venting: (1) at the end of a test day and/or (2) between atmospheres, where the latter was limited to days with normal-flame testing.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p20,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 20 A variety of constraints limit the number of tests that can be performed, both overall and on a daily basis. The next combustion experiments in the CIR will feature solid fuels and focus on flame spread and material flammability. That follow-on plan limits the duration of ACME operations, where each ACME experiment is allotted four months for testing. Although it requires additional crew time, these allotments are split into two rounds so that the investigators have a break from testing for careful review of the early results so that they can maximize the scientific outcome of their second round. This paper reports on early results from the first round of E-FIELD Flames.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p20,1,"While nitrogen is available from the space station, the combustion testing is limited by the available gas bottles that were and are launched to support the tests. For E-FIELD Flames and most of ACME’s experiments, the test matrices used to plan those gas resources were established in 2010. At that time, NASA limited the scope of the ACME tests matrices with the intent of focused experiments with limited objectives rather than comprehensive experiments. As such, the general target was 50 tests per experiment. For most ACME experiments, the limiting gas constraint is the oxygen/nitrogen bottles used to fill the chamber. This resource constraint is exacerbated by the CIR because it is equipped with a pair of redundant safety timers to limit the fuel that can flow into the combustion chamber (where the timers can be set for the oxidizer flow in the case of inverse flame testing). The timers can only be reset by venting the combustion chamber. Therefore, the timer limit combined with other constraints effectively corresponds to the day’s maximum flow time for the fuel (or more generally, the limiting reactant). For E-FIELD Flames, the safety timers are set to the maximum value of 999 seconds, which corresponds to the 10 tests typically completed per day. The maximum limit of 999 seconds for the valve safety timers was used for all ACME testing.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p20,2,"Another constraint is the available time in a day, where the console personnel and ISS resources, such as the video downlink, are planned for a limited number of hours. When necessary for the research, NASA does have continuous 24-hour/day operations for some non-CIR experiments. But as discussed, the ACME tests are typically two minutes or less in duration, so the testing is almost always limited to the planned durations. Data limits are yet another constraint, where the on-orbit hardware can store a limited amount of data. More importantly, 127,000 MB is the most that can be reasonably managed in a two- shift transfer and downlink day. Therefore, tests days are limited to this amount of data, where image data from select cameras is often archived at reduced rates to mitigate this challenge. Nonetheless, the limit sometimes forces decisions to abandon recorded images. This detailed description of operations and hardware provides the context under which the ACME experiments are conducted. … Table 1 and 2 are summary logs of the flames ignited for CFI-G during the two consecutive rounds of testing, including a limited overview of the test conditions. They were copied from this report’s Appendix C. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increment 65 which comprehensively reflects all CFI-G testing on the ISS.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p21,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 21 Table 1. Flames ignited for the CFI-G experiment in the first round of testing, during Increments 64-65, with normal flames (where fuel from the burner flows into oxidizer) Test Day1 Year Month Date Atmosphere2 Fuel bottle Hot flames ignited3 Increment 64 21088 2021 3 29 ~40% O2, 13.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia ~18% O2, 14.7 psia 100% C2H6 14 21102 4 12 ~40% O2, 14.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia 50% n-C4H10 with N2 9 21106 16 ~40% O2, 7.35 psia ~21% O2, 7.35 psia 10 Increment 65 21112 2021 4 22 ~40% O2, 13.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia 100% C3H8 13 21125 5 5 ~40% O2, 14.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia 30% n-C4H10 with N2 6 21130 10 ~21% O2, 29.4 psia ~21% O2, 44.1 psia 30% C3H8 with N2 15 21137 17 ~40% O2, 14.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia 12 21153 6 2 ~40% O2, 7.35 psia ~40% O2, 29.4 psia g30% C2H6 with N2 14 21162 11 ~40% O2, 14.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia 12 21174 23 ~40% O2, 7.35 psia ~40% O2, 29.4 psia 30% n-C4H10 with N2 16 1Test Day includes the year as the first two digits followed by the 3-digit day of the year. 2Atmosphere, diluted by nitrogen in all cases, is only nominal where the pressure increases and O2% drops with vitiation. 3Flames ignited is the number of sustained or momentary hot flames ignited on that day (neglecting the day change on ISS).",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p22,0,ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 22,,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p22,1,"Table 2. Flames ignited for the CFI-G experiment in the second round of testing, during Increments 65-66, with inverse flames (where oxidizer from the burner flowed into a gaseous fuel/nitrogen mixture) plus an added day of normal-flame testing Test Day1 Year Month Date Atmosphere2 Fluent reactant bottle Hot flames ignited3 Increment 65 21196 2021 7 15 ~30% C2H6, 14.7 psia ~30% C2H6, 7.35 psia 85% O2 with N2 13 21202 21 ~6% C2H6, 29.4 psia ~12% C2H6, 29.4 psia ~12% C2H6, 14.7 psia 11 21209 28 ~24% C2H6, 7.35 psia 11 21216 8 4 ~10% C2H6, 44.1 psia ~10% C2H6, 14.7 psia 12 21222 10 ~8% C3H8, 44.1 psia 9 21228 16 ~9% C3H8, 29.4 psia ~9% C3H8, 14.7 psia 17 21235 23 ~21% C3H8, 14.7 psia ~21% C3H8, 7.35 psia 9 21242 30 ~43% C3H8, 7.35 psia 2 21256 9 13 ~9% n-C4H10, 29.4 psia ~7% n-C4H10, 29.4 psia 17 21265 22 ~6% n-C4H10, 44.1 psia 15 21270 27 ~17% n-C4H10, 14.7 psia ~14% n-C4H10, 14.7 psia 10 21277 10 4 ~36% n-C4H10, 7.35 psia ~25% n-C4H10, 7.35 psia ~18% n-C4H10, 7.35 psia ~14% n-C4H10, 7.35 psia 11 Increment 66 21291 2021 10 18 ~40% O2, 29.4 psia ~40% O2, 14.7 psia 30% n-C4H10 with N2 22 1Test Day includes the year as the first two digits followed by the 3-digit day of the year. 2Atmosphere, diluted by nitrogen in all cases, is only nominal where the pressure increases and O2% drops with vitiation. 3Flames ignited is the number of sustained or momentary hot flames ignited (or reignited) on that day (neglecting the day change on ISS).",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p23,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 23 C. Summary of Preliminary Results Abstract for Spherical Gas-Fueled Cool Diffusion Flames: An improved understanding of cool diffusion flames could lead to improved engines. CDFs are investigated here using a spherical porous burner with gaseous fuels in the microgravity environment of the International Space Station. Normal and inverse flames burning ethane, propane, and n-butane were explored with various fuel and oxygen concentrations, pressures, and flow rates. The diagnostics included an intensified video camera, radiometers, and thermocouples. Spherical CDFs burning gases were observed for the first time. However, these CDFs were not readily produced and were only obtained for normal n-butane flames at 2 bar with an ambient oxygen mole fraction of 0.39. The hot flames that spawned the CDFs were 2.6 times as large. An analytical model is presented that combines previous models for steady droplet burning and the partial-burning regime for CDFs. The results identify the importance of burner temperature on the behavior of these CDFs. They also indicate that the observed CDFs reside in rich regions near a mixture fraction of 0.53.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p23,1,"Abstract for Cool Diffusion Flames in a Stably Stratified Stagnation Flow: Cool diffusion flames have been a growing research topic since their discovery in 2012. Until now their study has been hindered by the high cost of the experimental systems used to observe them. A method is presented here for observing CDFs inexpensively using a pool of liquid n-heptane and parallel plates heated so as to produce a stably stratified stagnation flow. The flames were imaged with a color camera and measurements included gas phase temperatures, fuel evaporation rates, and formaldehyde emissions. These are the first observations of CDFs burning near the surface of fuel pools. The measured peak temperatures were between 705 – 760 K, which corresponds to an increase in gas temperature of 70 K. Autoignition first occurred at 550 K. Cantera autoignition simulations for homogeneous mixtures with the CRECK mechanism predicted a cool flame temperature of 885 K, which is significantly higher than observed.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p23,2,"Publications: • M. Kim, K.A. Waddell, P.B. Sunderland, V. Nayagam, D.P. Stocker, D.L. Dietrich, Y. Ju, F.A. Williams, P. H. Irace, R.L. Axelbaum, Spherical Gas-Fueled Cool Diffusion Flames, Proceedings of the Combustion Institute (2022) in press. • M. Kim, Spherical Gas-Fueled Cool Diffusion Flames, MS Thesis, University of Maryland, 60 pp., May, 2022. • K.A. Waddell, H. Lee, V. Nayagam, R.L. Axelbaum, P.B. Sunderland, Cool Diffusion Flames in a Stably Stratified Stagnation Flow, Combustion and Flame, submitted. • K.A. Waddell, Cool Diffusion Flames in Stagnation Flow, Ph.D. Dissertation, University of Maryland, in preparation. D. Surprises and New Discoveries • Spherical cool diffusion flames burning gases were observed for the first time. • Cool diffusion flames burning near the surface of fuel pools were observed for the first time. E. Accomplishment of Approved Objectives All the approved objectives were accomplished, neglecting substitution of ethane for n- pentane in the test matrix after it was determined through ground testing that the use of the",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p24,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 24 latter was not feasible because of the n-pentane’s low vapor pressure. F. Additional Objectives Achieved Cool diffusion flames burning near the surface of fuel pools in normal gravity were observed for the first time. G. Impacts, Benefits, and Products Combustion provides 86% of the world’s energy and an even higher fraction of the energy used for transportation. The internal combustion engines used for transportation generally have thermal efficiencies of 38% or lower because of thermal losses. These efficiencies may increase to 60% if future engines can take advantage of ultra-lean combustion. However, this progress is being impeded by the limited understanding of the chemistry of cool flames. Research into cool diffusion flames (whose peak temperatures are 500 – 1000 K) has grown exponentially since they were discovered in 2012. The measurements performed for this project will help to test and advance cool flame kinetics mechanisms. This should facilitate the development of more efficient internal combustion engines. H. Patents No patents have been filed from the CFI-G research.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p25,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 25 Appendix A. Abbreviations and Acronyms ##### ACME days are identified by year (2 digits) and the day of the year (3 digits, 001-366) #####x# ACME tests are identified by day (5 digits;",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p25,1,"see above), a letter, and a 1-digit number 0-g microgravity 1-g normal gravity (on the surface of the Earth) ACME Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (CIR project) ADP Acceptance Data Package ASGSR American Society for Gravitational and Space Research AVP avionics package BRE Burning Rate Emulator (an ACME experiment) C2H4 ethylene C3H8 propane CH2O formaldehyde CASIS Center for the Advancement of Science in Space CCD Charge-Coupled Device (such as a camera sensor) CFI Cool Flames Investigation (an MDCA experiment preceding ACME in the CIR) CFI-G Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (an ACME experiment) CH4 methane CIA Chamber Insert Assembly (inserted into the CIR combustion chamber) CIR Combustion Integrated Rack (ISS facility in which ACME experiments are conducted) CLD Flame Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame (an ACME experiments) CM Combustion Module (space shuttle precursor to the CIR) cm centimeter CO2 carn dioxide Co-I Co-Investigator CRS Commercial Resupply Service (a designation of ISS cargo missions) CSA Canadian Space Agency CSV comma-separated values CWRU Case Western Reserve University Da Damköhler number DataCam Data Camera (ACME’s primary camera which was characterized for pyrometry, etc.) E-FIELD Flames Electric-Field Effects on Laminar Diffusion Flames (an ACME experiment) EMI electromagnetic interference EMS Experiment Mounting Structure ERD Engineering Requirements Document ESA European Space Agency Fiji Fiji Is Just ImageJ (a version of ImageJ",,False
