PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p26,1,"used for the DataCam) HV high voltage HVPS High-Voltage Power Supply ICDERS International Colloquium on the Dynamics of Explosions and Reactive Systems ID inner diameter (e.g., of burner tube) ImageJ Image Processing and Analysis in Java (open-source image processing software) IPSU Image Processing and Storage Unit (CIR device for recording images on ISS) IPSU-G IPSU for GigE cameras (including the DataCam and CFI intensified camera) ISRD Integrated Science Requirements Document (for a set of experiments) ISS International Space Station JAXA Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency kPa kilopascal kV kilovolt LLL-UV Low Light Level Ultraviolet camera ((developed for the CIR facility) LSP Laminar Soot Processes (a shuttle study conducted in the Combustion Module) MDCA Multi-user Droplet Combustion Apparatus (a CIR chamber insert) MFC Mass Flow Controller Mist Water-Mist Fire Suppression Experiment (a Combustion Module experiment) MIUL Materials Identification/Usage List mm millimeter MSG Microgravity Science Glovebox (ISS facility in which SLICE was conducted) N2 nitrogen NSF National Science Foundation NTSC National Television System Committee O2 oxygen OMA2 Optical Multi-channel Analyzer 2 (open-source software for image processing) ops operations PARIS Passive Rack Isolation System (with which the CIR is equipped, but not used for ACME) PI Principal Investigator PIMS Principal Investigator Microgravity Services PMT Photomultiplier Tube (where ACME is equipped with three PMT",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p26,2,"modules) PS Project Scientist PSI Physical Science Informatics System psia pounds per square inch PT pressure transducer QD quick disconnect qty quantity RSA Russian Space Agency, i.e., Roscosmos s second SAMS Space Acceleration Measurement System SDP Safety Data Package s-Flame Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (an ACME experiment) SiC Silicon Carbide (material of the TFP fibers) SLICE Structure & Liftoff In Combustion Experiment (CLD Flame precursor) slpm standard liters per minute (a measure of the mass or molar flow rate of a gas) SOFBALL Structure Of Flame Balls At Low Lewis-number (a Combustion Module study)",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p27,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 27 SoFIE Solid Fuel Ignition and Extinction (project to follow ACME in the CIR facility) SORD Science Operations Requirements Document (an ACME supplement to the ISRD) SRD Science Requirements Document TDRS Tracking and Data Relay Satellite (System) TFP Thin Filament Pyrometry (a method for gas-phase temperature measurements) TIFF or TIF Tagged Image File Format (for the CFI intensified, HiBMs, and LLL-UV cameras) TM thermistor TS Transport Stream (a video format) TSC Telescience Support Center (also known as the GIPOC) UV ultraviolet Ze Zeldovich number zero-g zero gravity, i.e., microgravity ZIN ZIN Technologies, Inc. (ACME engineering and operations provider) µA microamp While this list of abbreviations and acronyms is long, it is not comprehensive. Contact ACME Project Scientist Dennis Stocker (216-433-2166, dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov) for additional clarification.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p28,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 28 Appendix B. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increment 64 21 June 2021 30-day Operational Accomplishments Report … for the International Space Station (ISS) Increment 64 [incorrectly identified as 63] ACME Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments CFI-G Cool Flames Investigation with Gases The CFI-G investigation is one of several independent experiments that are part of the ACME project, which uses a single modular set of hardware to carry out studies of flames burning gaseous fuels in the ISS’ Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). Unlike the other ACME studies, CFI-G was selected for spaceflight by the Center for the Advancement of Science in Space (CASIS) and the National Science Foundation (NSF). CFI-G testing began late in Increment 64 and should be completed in Increment 65. Prepared by the PI team and Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov), the ACME Project Scientist, who is the primary contact for s-Flame and ACME information. Principal Investigator (PI): Prof. Peter B. Sunderland, University of Maryland, College Park, MD Co-Investigator (Co-I): Prof. Richard L. Axelbaum, Washington University in St. Louis, MO Co-Investigator (Co-I): Prof. Forman A. Williams, U. of California San Diego, San Diego, CA NASA Project Scientist (PS): Dennis P. Stocker, NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, OH PS team member: Dr. Vedha Nayagam, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, OH",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p28,1,"I. Introduction Tests for the Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G) experiment are conducted in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on the International Space Station, as part of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project. As explained in the research proposal: Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet experiments aboard the International Space Station (ISS) in 2012, and this initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. Droplet experiments necessarily involve unsteady burning rates, so cool diffusion flames have never been observed as steady spherical flames. A natural progression toward understanding cool diffusion flames is to observe quasi-steady spherical flames on porous burners, and this is the topic of this proposal. The flames to be studied are rich in the physics and chemistry of combustion and thermal transport processes.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p29,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 29 Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences. The flame system to be studied will allow steady spherical cool diffusion flames to be observed for the first time. The tests, performed with gaseous fuel and a porous spherical burner, cannot be performed in normal gravity or in ground-based microgravity facilities.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p29,1,"CFI-G had the following experimental hypotheses and experimental objective when it was proposed in 2017: Hypotheses The hypotheses of this research are that testing aboard the ISS can demonstrate the following for the first time, contributing to an improved understanding of cool flame kinetics. 1. Steady-state cool diffusion flames on spherical porous burners. 2. Cool diffusion flames burning propane, n-butane, and n-pentane. 3. Cool diffusion flames for a wide range of Zst, Tad, and pressures. 4. Inverse cool diffusion flames. 5. Cool diffusion flames for which a decrease in fluent flow rate will cause extinction, whereas an increase will cause a transition to a hot diffusion flame, with oscillation possible between hot and cool flames. Experimental Objective A. Determine the conditions for which cool diffusion flames occur for propane, n-butane, and n-pentane; normal and inverse flames; and a broad range of Zst, Tad, and fluent flow rate. where Zst and Tad are respectively the stochiometric mixture fraction and adiabatic flame temperature. However, it was later determined that the CIR facility could not support testing with pentane because of the fuel’s low vapor pressure so the test matrix was revised to instead include testing with ethane in addition to the propane and n-butane.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p29,2,"The additional research objectives are as follows, where CFD stands for Computational Fluid Dynamics. B. Support the flight tests with advanced CFD simulations. C. Identify or develop a chemical kinetics mechanism that yields good agreement between the CFD predictions and the ISS measurements. D. Disseminate the results as widely as possible. The experiment is carried out using the same spherical burner configuration as ACME’s Flame Design and s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames) experiments. The instrumentation is the same as that used for Flame Design except that an alternate radiometer array was developed for CFI-G. Furthermore, the CFI-G tests were conducted using the",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p30,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 30 formaldehyde filter on the CFI intensified camera rather than the typically used CH* filter. In this regard, the CFI camera was developed for the Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) which preceded ACME and studied non-premixed cool flames in the combustion of liquid droplets.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p30,1,"Microgravity studies of flames allow for a range of simplifications and provide various advantages over normal-gravity studies. For example, the natural flicker of flames, which is a buoyancy- driven (i.e., gravity-driven) instability, is eliminated yielding quasi-steady flames in microgravity. Characteristic length scales are increased in microgravity flames facilitating analysis of the flame structure. Momentum-dominated flames, which are important for most practical combustion systems, can be studied at low velocities to simplify analysis. Microgravity flames tend to have a much stronger sensitivity to their atmosphere and exhibit a much broader range of characteristics than normal-gravity flames because of the near absence of buoyant entrainment. The long residence times in microgravity flames can lead to strong soot production, but many microgravity flames are soot free. The absence of buoyancy makes microgravity flames advantageous for studies of limit and stability behavior where chemical kinetics are important. For example, chemi-ion species, electronically-excited chemiluminescent species, are at very low concentration in typical flames, making the reduced buoyancy environment valuable for evaluating the minor species chemistry. With these advantages, microgravity testing can lead to new understanding of the complex nature of combustion – regardless of the gravitational environment of the combustion application.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p30,2,"II. Methods/Research Operations A. Discussion of Method/Protocol The CFI-G investigation and other ACME experiments are configured, but not conducted, by the ISS crew. The crew’s role is to set up the hardware, gas bottles, etc. for each set of tests. The most common changes include the gas bottles on the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) and the mass flow controllers on the modular ACME chamber insert. All testing is commanded from the ground, specifically from the Glenn ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC). The science portion of the tests were all conducted in a nominally automated mode using pre-programmed scripts. No CFI-G tests were conducted in a ‘manual’ mode, where changes are made to a control parameter in response to the analog video downlink and the numerical data available in the telemetry stream. Thus far, that has been the case for all ACME tests, with the mundane exception of ‘manually’ halting the test after the flame has extinguished or perhaps if a temperature has reached a critical value. Testing is not conducted when thrusters fire or spacecraft dock or undock from ISS to ensure an acceptable acceleration environment. Data is downlinked between each set of tests, i.e., day of testing, to allow for preliminary analysis and requisite planning for subsequent testing.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p31,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 31 III. Results A. Baselined Activities Completed – Pre-flight, In-flight, Post-Flight A total of 33 hot flames were ignited for the CFI-G experiment during Increment 64, where data is still be reviewed to assess the presence of cool flames. This was just the beginning of testing, which is expected to continue through most if not all of Increment 65. The CFI-G experiment does not have living subjects like a life science experiment, but the tests are independently conducted with various gaseous fuels, including ethane, propane, and n-butane. These are simple hydrocarbon gases for which the hot flame chemistry is reasonably well understood, where there is more to be learned about cool flame chemistry. No samples (e.g., of soot) were collected for CFI-G; all of the ISS results were electronically transmitted to the ground. That is furthermore the case for all currently planned ACME experiments. A summary log of the flames ignited for CFI-G during Increment 64, including a limited overview of the test conditions, is found in Table 1.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p31,1,"Table 1. Flames ignited for the CFI-G experiment during Increment 64 Test Day1 Year Month Date Atmosphere2 Fuel bottle Hot flames ignited3 First round of tests, with normal flames (where fuel from the burner flows into oxidizer) 21088 2021 3 29 ~40% O2, 13.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia ~18% O2, 14.7 psia 100% C2H6 14 21102 4 12 ~40% O2, 14.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia 50% n-C4H10 with N2 9 21106 16 ~40% O2, 7.35 psia ~21% O2, 7.35 psia 10 1Test Day includes the year as the first two digits followed by the 3-digit day of the year. 2Atmosphere, diluted by nitrogen in all cases, is only nominal where the pressure increases and O2% drops with vitiation. 3Flames ignited is the number of sustained or momentary hot flames ignited on that day (neglecting the day change on ISS). Pre-flight and post-flight activities are generally not addressed in this report. B. List of Preflight, In-flight, Postflight Anomalies 1. Mission anomalies The Image Processing and Storage Unit (IPSU1) used with the High Bit-depth Multi- spectral (HiBMs) camera and the opposed CIR illumination package ceased functioning on the first day, March 29 (GMT 088). Tests 21088M1-M3 all aborted due",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p32,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 32 to an error with IPSU1 communication, where test M4 was successful when the test was run without use of the HiBMs camera. The troublesome IPSU1 was replaced by astronaut Megan McArthur on May 18 (GMT 138), but it was not available during the intervening test days. However, this wasn’t particularly a loss to CFI-G because the camera, with its red filter, is only sensitive to soot. Furthermore, the illumination could make it more difficult to discern cool flames. However, the camera and illumination package will be used during the second round CFI-G testing with inverse flames to track possible soot deposition on the chamber windows. 2. Any other difficulties in obtaining scientific data CFI-G’s two primary challenges are in initiating cool flames and discerning their presence. Based on the findings of the earlier studies with droplet combustion, non- premixed cool flames can be directly by limiting the ignition energy or can occur after the self-extinguishment of (normal) hot flames. Tests intended to directly ignite cool flames sometimes unintentionally resulted in hot flames. A challenge for cool flame detection is concern about potential damage to the CFI intensified camera by exposing it to bright light, especially from the energized igniter, while the intensifier gain is set high.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p32,1,"When at a small fraction (e.g., less than 10%) of the maximum flow rate, Mass Flow Controller (MFC) performance has been seen to be poor during CFI-G testing, i.e., where actual flow rate differed from that desired. Meanwhile, at high flow rates of fuel and nitrogen, the burner does not produce spherically symmetric flames. While nominally spherical at moderate flow rates, the flame’s center becomes increasing offset from the burner center. More specifically, the flame is shifted toward the supply tube indicating that the sintered attachment of the porous sphere to the supply tube is more ‘open’ than the pores of the spherical burner. In other words, the flow from the burner is not spherically symmetric. At sufficiently high flow rates, the flame becomes obviously non-spherical, where it can appear to be ellipsoidal or even parabolic in shape. In the latter case, the flame locally quenches near the supply tube.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p32,2,"During Burning Rate Emulator (BRE) testing on 23 Dec. 2020, it was discovered that the lowest of the 14-micron silicon carbide fibers on the Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) fiber array had broken. Recovery plans were developed and enacted on 18 Jan. 2021 to remove the broken fragments from the harp-like array. Unfortunately, the next fiber was broken during the delicate operations where the fibers are only spaced 5 mm apart. Although ACME does have alternate arrays, it also has months of remaining testing, so the decision was made to continue testing with only 3 fibers (instead of 5) for the final BRE testing, the subsequent CLD Flame tests, and at least",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p33,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 33 the start of CFI-G testing. This is viewed as a minor loss, where for example the fiber array is only used in some CFI-G tests. C. Completeness/Quality of Data Only the first 3 of 22 CFI-G test days were conducted during Increment 64, so only a small fraction of the test matrix has been completed. In this regard, CFI-G testing is expected to continue through most of Increment 65. The cameras are sensitive to radiation where pixels gradually become ‘hot’ (i.e., dead) over time. While these bright spots are addressed in image post-processing, they are a degradation of the image quality. It is possible that the tests are subtly affected by the acceleration environment, but there is not obvious indication of this; and it has thus far not been evaluated with available measurements from the Space Acceleration Measurement System (SAMS). Recorded image data is sometimes deleted without downlinking. This is done because of operational constraints which limit the amount of data that can be managed per test day. Given the constraint, the decision is sometimes made to sacrifice some image data to allow additional tests to be conducted. This is intentionally done in order to maximize the scientific return of each day of testing within the project constraints.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p33,1,"D. Status of Data Analysis [provided by the U. of Maryland investigator team and lightly edited by Stocker] Data sets of spherical diffusion flames as a function of the fuel mass flow rate, atmospheric O2 concentration, fuel dilution, and pressure were obtained. The raw test measurements made from devices - such as thermocouples, photomultiplier tubes (PMTs), gas flow meters, and radiometers - were saved in a csv file format per each test. The data rate of the non-imaging measurements was 125 per sec. The raw data was processed to transform it into scientific measurements. The transformed data were visualized as plots for further analysis. In addition, test images recorded in tiff and hobj formats respectively for the CFI intensified camera and ACME DataCam have been analyzed to find flame diameters. Of these the intensified camera is of most value and all of its images to date have been converted to video files and reviewed. Particular attention has been given to the PMT-2 measurements due to its sensitivity to wavelengths of 230 – 700 nm for CH2O* to investigate the presence of cool flames. PMT-2 measurements have been analyzed to determine flame durations as well.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p34,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 34 Burner temperatures will be analyzed to find their correlation to the cool flame diameters. E. Preliminary Research Findings [provided by the U. Maryland investigator team] The first three days of testing have yielded dozens of flames, some lasting several minutes. Based on the output from the photomultipliers, radiometers, and intensified camera images we do not believe any self-sustained cool flames were created. While it is possible that momentary cool flames were created, these are difficult to detect without increasing the gain on the intensified camera before the hot flames extinguish, which is planned. The video record will furnish excellent measurements of flame diameter versus time for a broad range of fuels and conditions. These will be compared with a simple analytical model and detailed CFD simulations using Sphdif. Thin-filament pyrometry has been performed and the results will soon be analyzed. F. Communication Imagery 1. Still Imagery Photos are available online at www.flickr.com/photos/space-flames. Although the Facebook page at www.facebook.com/space.flames is meant to provide updates on ACME and related combustion research, it is good source for recent photos, e.g., of flames, that may not be available on the Flickr site. Additional photographs may be requested from the Project Scientist, Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov).",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p34,1,"2. Video ‘The Power of Studying Combustion on the ISS,’ which was developed with input from the ACME Project Scientist, is available at https://science.nasa.gov/science- news/news-articles/the-power-of-studying-combustion-on-the-iss. Video(s) describing the ACME project can be found at https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/acme/. Videos related to CFI-G and other microgravity combustion research can also be seen on Facebook at www.facebook.com/space.flames. Contact Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov) for access to those videos or for additional videos. IV. Additional Comments from science team None.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p35,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 35 Appendix C. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increment 65 14 Dec. 2021 30-day Operational Accomplishments Report … for the International Space Station (ISS) Increment 65 ACME Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments CFI-G Cool Flames Investigation with Gases The CFI-G investigation is one of several independent experiments that are part of the ACME project, which uses a single modular set of hardware to carry out studies of flames burning gaseous fuels in the ISS’ Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). Unlike the other ACME studies, CFI-G was selected for spaceflight by the Center for the Advancement of Science in Space (CASIS) and the National Science Foundation (NSF). CFI-G testing began late in Increment 64 and was completed at the very beginning of Increment 66. That final testing is reflected in this report as well as the early testing conducted in Increment 64. Prepared by the PI team and Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov), the ACME Project Scientist, who is the primary contact for CFI-G and ACME information.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p35,1,"Principal Investigator (PI): Prof. Peter B. Sunderland, University of Maryland, College Park, MD Co-Investigator (Co-I): Prof. Richard L. Axelbaum, Washington University in St. Louis, MO Co-Investigator (Co-I): Prof. Forman A. Williams, U. of California San Diego, San Diego, CA NASA Project Scientist (PS): Dennis P. Stocker, NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, OH PS team member: Dr. Vedha Nayagam, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, OH V. Introduction Tests for the Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G) experiment are conducted in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on the International Space Station, as part of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project. As explained in the research proposal: Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet experiments aboard the International Space Station (ISS) in 2012, and this initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. Droplet experiments necessarily involve unsteady burning rates, so cool diffusion flames have never been observed as steady spherical flames. A natural progression toward understanding cool diffusion flames is to observe quasi-steady spherical flames on porous burners, and this is the topic of this proposal. The flames to be studied are rich in the physics and chemistry of combustion and thermal transport processes.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p36,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 36 Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences. The flame system to be studied will allow steady spherical cool diffusion flames to be observed for the first time. The tests, performed with gaseous fuel and a porous spherical burner, cannot be performed in normal gravity or in ground-based microgravity facilities.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p36,1,"CFI-G had the following experimental hypotheses and experimental objective when it was proposed in 2017: Hypotheses The hypotheses of this research are that testing aboard the ISS can demonstrate the following for the first time, contributing to an improved understanding of cool flame kinetics. 1. Steady-state cool diffusion flames on spherical porous burners. 2. Cool diffusion flames burning propane, n-butane, and n-pentane. 3. Cool diffusion flames for a wide range of Zst, Tad, and pressures. 4. Inverse cool diffusion flames. 5. Cool diffusion flames for which a decrease in fluent flow rate will cause extinction, whereas an increase will cause a transition to a hot diffusion flame, with oscillation possible between hot and cool flames. Experimental Objective A. Determine the conditions for which cool diffusion flames occur for propane, n-butane, and n-pentane; normal and inverse flames; and a broad range of Zst, Tad, and fluent flow rate. where Zst and Tad are respectively the stochiometric mixture fraction and adiabatic flame temperature. However, it was later determined that the CIR facility could not support testing with pentane because of the fuel’s low vapor pressure so the test matrix was revised to instead include testing with ethane in addition to the propane and n-butane.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p36,2,"The additional research objectives are as follows, where CFD stands for Computational Fluid Dynamics. B. Support the flight tests with advanced CFD simulations. C. Identify or develop a chemical kinetics mechanism that yields good agreement between the CFD predictions and the ISS measurements. D. Disseminate the results as widely as possible. The experiment is carried out using the same spherical burner configuration as ACME’s Flame Design and s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames) experiments. The instrumentation is the same as that used for Flame Design except that an alternate radiometer array was developed for CFI-G. Furthermore, the CFI-G tests were conducted using the",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p37,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 37 formaldehyde filter on the CFI intensified camera rather than the typically used CH* filter. In this regard, the CFI camera was developed for the Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) which preceded ACME and studied non-premixed cool flames in the combustion of liquid droplets.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p37,1,"Microgravity studies of flames allow for a range of simplifications and provide various advantages over normal-gravity studies. For example, the natural flicker of flames, which is a buoyancy- driven (i.e., gravity-driven) instability, is eliminated yielding quasi-steady flames in microgravity. Characteristic length scales are increased in microgravity flames facilitating analysis of the flame structure. Momentum-dominated flames, which are important for most practical combustion systems, can be studied at low velocities to simplify analysis. Microgravity flames tend to have a much stronger sensitivity to their atmosphere and exhibit a much broader range of characteristics than normal-gravity flames because of the near absence of buoyant entrainment. The long residence times in microgravity flames can lead to strong soot production, but many microgravity flames are soot free. The absence of buoyancy makes microgravity flames advantageous for studies of limit and stability behavior where chemical kinetics are important. For example, chemi-ion species, electronically-excited chemiluminescent species, are at very low concentration in typical flames, making the reduced buoyancy environment valuable for evaluating the minor species chemistry. With these advantages, microgravity testing can lead to new understanding of the complex nature of combustion – regardless of the gravitational environment of the combustion application.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p37,2,"VI. Methods/Research Operations B. Discussion of Method/Protocol The CFI-G investigation and other ACME experiments are configured, but not conducted, by the ISS crew. The crew’s role is to set up the hardware, gas bottles, etc. for each set of tests. The most common changes include the gas bottles on the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) and the mass flow controllers on the modular ACME chamber insert. All testing is commanded from the ground, specifically from the Glenn ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC). The science portion of the tests were all conducted in a nominally automated mode using pre-programmed scripts. No CFI-G tests were conducted in a ‘manual’ mode, where changes are made to a control parameter in response to the analog video downlink and the numerical data available in the telemetry stream. Thus far, that has been the case for all ACME tests, with the mundane exception of ‘manually’ halting the test after the flame has extinguished or perhaps if a temperature has reached a critical value. Testing is not conducted when thrusters fire or spacecraft dock or undock from ISS to ensure an acceptable acceleration environment. Data is downlinked between each set of tests, i.e., day of testing, to allow for preliminary analysis and requisite planning for subsequent testing.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p38,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 38 VII. Results G. Baselined Activities Completed – Pre-flight, In-flight, Post-Flight A total of 280 hot flames were ignited (or in some cases naturally reignited) for the CFI-G experiment during Increments 64-66, where 33, 225, and 22 flames were ignited in the three increments, respectively. Testing began near the end of Increment 64 and the final test day was conducted at the start of Increment 66. Cool flame evidence was seen in some tests and 15 cool flames, listed and discussed later, have been identified thus far. The CFI-G experiment does not have living subjects like a life science experiment. The tests were instead conducted burning various gaseous fuels, including ethane (C2H6), propane (C3H8), and n-butane (n-C4H10). These are simple hydrocarbon gases for which the hot flame chemistry is reasonably well understood, but where there is much remaining to be learned about the cool flame chemistry. No samples (e.g., of soot) were collected for CFI-G; all of the investigation’s data was electronically transmitted to the ground. That is furthermore the case for all current ACME experiments.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p38,1,"Summary logs of the flames ignited for CFI-G for the two rounds of testing are found in Tables 1 and 2. The first round of testing featured normal flames in which the fuel flowed from the burner into an oxygen/nitrogen atmosphere. In contrast, the second round was largely conducted with inverse-flame tests in which an oxygen/nitrogen mixture flowed from the burner into a mixture of nitrogen and gaseous fuel. The round two exception was the final test day (at the start of Increment 66) which was again conducted in the normal-flames mode.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p39,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 39 Table 1. Flames ignited for the CFI-G experiment in the first round of testing, during Increments 64-65, with normal flames (where fuel from the burner flows into oxidizer) Test Day1 Year Month Date Atmosphere2 Fuel bottle Hot flames ignited3 Increment 64 21088 2021 3 29 ~40% O2, 13.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia ~18% O2, 14.7 psia 100% C2H6 14 21102 4 12 ~40% O2, 14.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia 50% n-C4H10 with N2 9 21106 16 ~40% O2, 7.35 psia ~21% O2, 7.35 psia 10 Increment 65 21112 2021 4 22 ~40% O2, 13.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia 100% C3H8 13 21125 5 5 ~40% O2, 14.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia 30% n-C4H10 with N2 6 21130 10 ~21% O2, 29.4 psia ~21% O2, 44.1 psia 30% C3H8 with N2 15 21137 17 ~40% O2, 14.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia 12 21153 6 2 ~40% O2, 7.35 psia ~40% O2, 29.4 psia g30% C2H6 with N2 14 21162 11 ~40% O2, 14.7 psia ~21% O2, 14.7 psia 12 21174 23 ~40% O2, 7.35 psia ~40% O2, 29.4 psia 30% n-C4H10 with N2 16 1Test Day includes the year as the first two digits followed by the 3-digit day of the year. 2Atmosphere, diluted by nitrogen in all cases, is only nominal where the pressure increases and O2% drops with vitiation. 3Flames ignited is the number of sustained or momentary hot flames ignited on that day (neglecting the day change on ISS).",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p40,0,ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 40,,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p40,1,"Table 2. Flames ignited for the CFI-G experiment in the second round of testing, during Increments 65-66, with inverse flames (where oxidizer from the burner flowed into a gaseous fuel/nitrogen mixture) plus an added day of normal-flame testing Test Day1 Year Month Date Atmosphere2 Fluent reactant bottle Hot flames ignited3 Increment 65 21196 2021 7 15 ~30% C2H6, 14.7 psia ~30% C2H6, 7.35 psia 85% O2 with N2 13 21202 21 ~6% C2H6, 29.4 psia ~12% C2H6, 29.4 psia ~12% C2H6, 14.7 psia 11 21209 28 ~24% C2H6, 7.35 psia 11 21216 8 4 ~10% C2H6, 44.1 psia ~10% C2H6, 14.7 psia 12 21222 10 ~8% C3H8, 44.1 psia 9 21228 16 ~9% C3H8, 29.4 psia ~9% C3H8, 14.7 psia 17 21235 23 ~21% C3H8, 14.7 psia ~21% C3H8, 7.35 psia 9 21242 30 ~43% C3H8, 7.35 psia 2 21256 9 13 ~9% n-C4H10, 29.4 psia ~7% n-C4H10, 29.4 psia 17 21265 22 ~6% n-C4H10, 44.1 psia 15 21270 27 ~17% n-C4H10, 14.7 psia ~14% n-C4H10, 14.7 psia 10 21277 10 4 ~36% n-C4H10, 7.35 psia ~25% n-C4H10, 7.35 psia ~18% n-C4H10, 7.35 psia ~14% n-C4H10, 7.35 psia 11 Increment 66 21291 2021 10 18 ~40% O2, 29.4 psia ~40% O2, 14.7 psia 30% n-C4H10 with N2 22 1Test Day includes the year as the first two digits followed by the 3-digit day of the year. 2Atmosphere, diluted by nitrogen in all cases, is only nominal where the pressure increases and O2% drops with vitiation. 3Flames ignited is the number of sustained or momentary hot flames ignited (or reignited) on that day (neglecting the day change on ISS).",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p40,2,Pre-flight and post-flight activities are generally not addressed in this report.,,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p41,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 41 H. List of Preflight, In-flight, Postflight Anomalies 3. Mission anomalies On Aug. 30 (GMT 242), the primary Image Processing and Storage Unit, used to record the imaging from both the ACME DataCam and CFI intensified camera (IPSU-G, also known as IPSU5), caused issues and eventually stopped working. Only the first two tests on that day were successful, where two tests aborted due to an IPSU error, and three tests failed to ignite. In closing checkout, it was determined that the hot-wire igniter tip had also failed. With the IPSU-G failure, the test day was nearly a total loss. Except for a small number of sample images downloaded during the midst of testing, the primary image data from even the successful tests was lost. Furthermore, there was no more propane available for use in inverse-flame testing, so the test day could not be repeated. However, astronaut Mark Vande Hei replaced the failed igniter tip on Sept. 8 and the failed hard drive in the IPSU-G on Sept. 10, and CFI-G testing successfully resumed on Sept. 13, just 2 weeks after the double failure.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p41,1,"Looking back to Increment 64, the Image Processing and Storage Unit (IPSU1) used with the High Bit-depth Multi-spectral (HiBMs) camera and the opposed CIR illumination package ceased functioning on March 29 (GMT 088). Tests 21088M1-M3 aborted due to an error with IPSU1 communication, where test M4 was successful when the test was run without use of the HiBMs camera. The troublesome IPSU1 was replaced by astronaut Megan McArthur on May 18 (GMT 138), but it was not available during the intervening test days. However, this wasn’t particularly a loss to CFI-G because that camera, with its red filter, is only sensitive to the red backlight from the CIR illumination package and visible thermal radiation, e.g., from glowing soot particles. Furthermore, the illumination could make it more difficult to discern cool flames, so the HiBMs camera, illumination package, and IPSU1 were rarely used for CFI-G. The principal exception was during the second round of testing. With the inverse-flame tests, the soot is generated outside of the flames, so this photography was used to track potential soot deposition on the chamber windows, etc. However, the end-of-day imaging did not appear to change over time, suggesting that the soot was not an issue for the chamber windows. However, the Photomultiplier Tube (PMT) modules and radiometers were closer to the flame than the windows and might had been adversely affected by soot.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p41,2,"4. Any other difficulties in obtaining scientific data CFI-G’s two primary challenges are in initiating cool flames and discerning their presence. Based on the findings of the earlier studies with droplet combustion, non- premixed cool flames can be directly ignited by limiting the ignition energy or can occur after the self-extinguishment of (normal) hot flames. Tests intended to directly ignite cool flames sometimes unintentionally resulted in hot flames. Meanwhile, evidence of cool flames was first discovered in review of the data a few days after the testing. During that testing, it wasn’t known that the cool flames were present after",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p42,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 42 hot flame extinction and the tests were terminated, which unknowingly and sadly shortened the duration of the cool flame combustion. In later testing with the long/near thermocouple, two of the tests exhibited repeated hot flame quenching and reignition, which resulting from intervening low- temperature combustion. It is likely that the closest thermocouple, approximately 1 cm from the burner surface, was a factor in the reignition. The exposed type-B thermocouple includes platinum, a catalyst for the oxidation of hydrocarbons. The catalysis contributed to the rapid reignition limiting the duration of the low- temperature combustion in these tests. A challenge for cool flame detection was concern about potential damage to the CFI intensified camera by exposing it to bright light, especially from the energized igniter, while the intensifier gain is set high. When at a small fraction (e.g., less than 10%) of the maximum flow rate, Mass Flow Controller (MFC) performance has been seen to be poor during CFI-G testing, i.e., where actual flow rate differed from that desired.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p42,1,"Meanwhile, at high flow rates of fuel and nitrogen, the burner does not produce spherically symmetric flames. While nominally spherical at moderate flow rates, the flame’s center becomes increasing offset from the burner center. More specifically, the flame is shifted toward the supply tube indicating that the sintered attachment of the porous sphere to the supply tube is more ‘open’ than the pores of the spherical burner. In other words, the flow from the burner is not spherically symmetric. At sufficiently high flow rates, the flame becomes obviously non-spherical, where it can appear to be ellipsoidal or even parabolic in shape. In the latter case, the flame locally quenches near the supply tube. During Burning Rate Emulator (BRE) testing on 23 Dec. 2020, it was discovered that the lowest of the 14-micron silicon carbide fibers on the Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) fiber array had broken. Recovery plans were developed and enacted on 18 Jan. 2021 to remove the broken fragments from the harp-like array. Unfortunately, the next fiber was broken during the delicate operations where the fibers are only spaced 5 mm apart. Although ACME does have alternate arrays, it also had months of remaining testing, so the decision was made to continue testing with only 3 fibers (instead of 5) for the final BRE testing and the subsequent CLD Flame and CFI-G tests. This was a minor loss, where the fiber array was only occasionally used in CFI-G testing.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p43,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 43 I. Completeness/Quality of Data The experiment’s proposal to CASIS and NSF stated: “It is assumed here that approximately 50 ISS flames can be conducted. These will include approximately 12 chamber fills following evacuation.” Far more testing was carried out for this experiment, where that included the ignition of 280 hot flames in 23 days of ISS testing, some of which included multiple chamber fills. Although some of the flames were short lived, the testing far exceeded the quantity that had been proposed. The cameras are sensitive to radiation where pixels gradually become ‘hot’ (i.e., dead) over time. While these bright spots are addressed in image post-processing, they are a degradation of the image quality. It is possible that the tests are subtly affected by the acceleration environment, but there is not obvious indication of this; and it has thus far not been evaluated with available measurements from the Space Acceleration Measurement System (SAMS). Recorded image data is sometimes deleted without downlinking. This is done because of operational constraints which limit the amount of data that can be managed per test day. Given the constraint, the decision is sometimes made to sacrifice some image data to allow additional tests to be conducted. This is intentionally done to maximize the scientific return of each day of testing within the project constraints.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p43,1,"J. Status of Data Analysis [provided by the U. of Maryland investigator team and lightly edited by Stocker] Data sets of spherical diffusion flames as a function of the fuel mass flow rate (for normal flames), the oxidizer mass flow rate (for inverse flames), O2 concentration, fuel dilution, and pressure were obtained for all the normal and inverse flame test days. The raw test measurements made from devices - such as thermocouples, photomultiplier tubes (PMTs), gas flow meters, and radiometers - were saved in a csv file format per each test. The data rate of the non-imaging measurements was 125 per sec. The raw data was processed to transform it into scientific measurements. The transformed data were visualized as plots for further analysis. In addition, test images recorded in tiff and hobj formats respectively for the CFI intensified camera and ACME DataCam have been analyzed to investigate the presence of cool flames and to find flame diameters. Of these the intensified camera is of most value, and all of its images to date have been converted to video files and reviewed. Since the intensified camera images were taken under the maximum possible gain applied, the images were averaged and filtered to decrease the overall noise level, and the intensity of the images was increased by a factor of 50 to facilitate the search of intensity peaks.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p44,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 44 Particular attention has been given to the PMT-2 measurements due to its sensitivity to wavelengths of 230 – 700 nm for CH2O* to investigate the presence of cool flames. PMT- 2 measurements have been analyzed to determine flame durations as well. Because cool diffusion flames were identified through the readings of radiometers after the radiative extinction of hot flames, the radiometer data was carefully analyzed. Burner temperatures were analyzed to find their correlation to the ignition and diameter of cool flames. K. Preliminary Research Findings [provided by the U. of Maryland investigator team and lightly edited by Stocker] A total of 280 hot diffusion flames were observed. Among them, 189 flames experienced radiative extinction, and the rest were extinguished when their flow was terminated, either because the preset flow time was out or the burner overheated. Of those that extinguished radiatively, 15 subsequently reignited as cool diffusion flames. These cool flames were observed only with the following conditions: a) concentrations of 30 vol% n- butane and 40 vol% O2, b) normal flames, c) a pressure of 2.03 bar, and d) mass flow rates between 0.75 – 1.5 mg/s.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p44,1,"Cool diffusion flames were initially identified by the readings of radiometers after the radiative extinction of hot flames. Unlike the heat flux shown in Fig. 1(a), Fig. 1(b) shows that the radiant heat flux did not drop to zero after the radiative extinction of a hot flame. Instead, it decreased to a certain level and stayed at that level until the fuel stopped flowing. Figure 1(c) shows multiple hot-cool flame transitions. The first cool flame, the first drop of the radiant heat flux, followed the radiative extinction of the first hot flame. In a few seconds, the heat flux abruptly increased due to reignition of a hot flame. This cycle of radiative extinction, transition to a cool flame, and reignition of a hot flame continued until the flow was terminated. The observed cool flames are summarized in Table 3.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p44,2,"Cool flames were also identified by processed images taken from the intensified camera. When cool flames appeared after the radiative extinction of the hot flames, no flame luminosity was evident for about 1 s, after which cool flames appeared. For test points 21174C3, C4, C6, and F1, the cool flames lasted for approximately 14 s and disappeared as soon as the fuel flow stopped. For the tests of multiple hot-cool flame transitions, 21291A1 and 21291C1, the first cool flame lasted longer than the following cool flames. The intensified camera images of hot and cool flames were used to find flame radii as well. The radii of hot flames were measured at the time just before the flame oscillation. Representative images of a hot flame and a cool flame are presented in Fig. 2. It is shown that the hot flame is brighter and larger than the cool flame. While a flame sheet is clearly indicated in the hot flame image, the intensity of a cool flame gradually decreases from the peak along radial direction, which implies that the reaction zone of the cool flame is",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p45,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 45 broader than that of the hot flame. In addition, it is noted that the hot flame is more spherical and less attached to the burner tube than the cool flame. Fig. 1. Radiant heat flux and fuel flow rate versus time for (a) a hot flame that extinguishes, (b) a hot flame that transitions to a cool diffusion flame, and (c) multiple hot-cool flame transitions. These flames involve 30 vol% n-butane flowing at 1.5 mg/s into (a) 37.9, (b) 39.5, and (c) 39.9 vol% O2 at 300 K. The pressure for (a) is 0.5 bar whereas that for (b) and (c) is 2.0 bar.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p46,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 46 Table 3. Summary of the cool flame tests test pt.1 XO2 Xf p (bar) 𝑚̇ 2 (mg/s) flame duration (sec) rhf (mm) rcf (mm) rhf/rcf 21174C3 0.395 0.300 2.03 1.50 13.9 17.6 7.6 2.3 21174C4 0.394 0.300 2.03 1.00 13.8 14.6 5.3 2.8 21174C6 0.385 0.300 2.03 0.75 14.3 12.4 5.1 2.4 21174D13 0.392 0.300 2.03 1.00 - 13.9 - - 21174F1 0.390 0.300 2.03 1.50 13.8 17.4 7.5 2.3 21291A1-1 0.399 0.300 2.03 1.50 10.9 17.2 7.1 2.4 21291A1-2 0.399 0.300 2.03 1.50 3.4 17.3 6.5 2.7 21291A1-3 0.399 0.300 2.03 1.50 3.8 17.6 6.3 2.8 21291A1-4 0.399 0.300 2.03 1.50 4 17.6 6.2 2.8 21291A1-5 0.399 0.300 2.03 1.50 3.7 17.7 6.2 2.9 21291C1-1 0.394 0.300 2.03 1.00 6.2 14.4 5.1 2.8 21291C1-2 0.394 0.300 2.03 1.00 5.8 14.9 5.5 2.7 21291C1-3 0.394 0.300 2.03 1.00 5.5 14.7 5.7 2.6 21291C1-4 0.394 0.300 2.03 1.00 5.4 14.9 5.7 2.6 21291C1-5 0.394 0.300 2.03 1.00 5.3 14.4 5.5 2.6 1 Subnumbers after a dash represent the sequential order of reignited hot flames and following cool flames during a single test. 2 Hydrocarbon mass flow rate. 3 The cool flame radius could not be evaluated due to low camera gain. L. Communication Imagery 1. Still Imagery Photos are available online at www.flickr.com/photos/space-flames. Although the Facebook page at www.facebook.com/space.flames is meant to provide updates on ACME and related combustion research, it is good source for recent photos, e.g., of",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p46,1,Fig. 2. Images of (a) a hot flame and (b) a cool flame at test point 21174C3. The dashed circle represents the gas-fed porous sphere burner. These images were converted from the grayscale camera output to the expected blue colors shown here.,,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p47,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 47 flames, that may not be available on the Flickr site. Additional photographs may be requested from the Project Scientist, Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov). 2. Video ‘The Power of Studying Combustion on the ISS,’ which was developed with input from the ACME Project Scientist, is available at https://science.nasa.gov/science- news/news-articles/the-power-of-studying-combustion-on-the-iss. Video(s) describing the ACME project can be found at https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/acme/. Videos related to CFI-G and other microgravity combustion research can also be seen on Facebook at www.facebook.com/space.flames. Contact Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov) for access to those videos or for additional videos. VIII. Additional Comments from science team None.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p48,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 48 Appendix D. Operational Timeline This summary timeline of CFI-G operations was prepared by Dennis Stocker, where the red text indicates the key days when flames were ignited. The dates were based on plans, where it is possible that the dates for some simple tasks, e.g., hardware gather or stowage, may have shifted slightly. The timeline also includes the launch and landing of spacecraft crews who supported the CFI-G operations. Date GMT Activity CFI-G (first round, normal flames) 2020 Oct. 14 288 Launch of Kud-Sverchkov, Rubins, and Ryzhikov Nov. 15 320 Launch of Glover, Hopkins, Noguchi, and Walker 2021 March 9-10 068-069 ACME chamber insert reconfigured for CFI-G by Rubins March 11 070 Hardware stow by Rubins; Mass Flow Controller (MFC) testing & tuning March 16 075 Bottle swaps, etc. by Hopkins March 29 088 CFI-G tests using 100% C2H6 April 9 099 Bottle swap by Hopkins;",,False
