PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p49,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 49 CFI-G (second round, inverse flames except for the final test day) 2021 July 2 183 ACME chamber insert reconfigured for inverse-flame tests by Vande Hei July 7 188 MFC testing & tuning July 9 190 MFC testing & tuning July 12 193 Bottle swaps by McArthur July 15-16 196-197 CFI-G tests using C2H6 (with ops planned past midnight EDT) July 19 200 Bottle swap by McArthur July 21-22 202-203 CFI-G tests using C2H6 (with ops planned past midnight EDT) July 26 207 Bottle swap by McArthur July 28-29 209-210 CFI-G tests using C2H6 (with ops planned past midnight EDT) Aug. 2 214 Bottle swaps by Vande Hei Aug. 3 215 Bottles opened by Kimbrough Aug. 4-5 216-217 CFI-G tests using C2H6 (with ops planned past midnight EDT) Aug. 10-11 222-223 Bottle swaps by Vande Hei; CFI-G tests using C3H8 (with ops past midnight) Aug. 16 228 Bottle swap by Vande Hei; CFI-G tests using C3H8 Aug. 20 232 Bottle swap by Kimbrough Aug. 23 235 Bottle swap by Kimbrough; CFI-G tests using C3H8 Aug. 30-31 242-243 Bottle swap by Vande Hei; CFI-G tests using C3H8 (with ops past midnight) Sept. 8 251 Igniter tip replacement by Vande Hei Sept. 10 253 IPSU-G hard drive replacement by Vande Hei; no-flame check-out Sept. 13-14 256-257 Bottle swap by McArthur; CFI-G tests using n-C4H10 (ops past midnight) Sept. 20 263 Bottle swap by McArthur Sept. 22 265 Bottle swap by McArthur; CFI-G tests using n-C4H10 Sept. 27-28 270-271 Bottle swap by Kimbrough;",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p49,1,"CFI-G tests using n-C4H10 (ops past midnight) Oct. 4-5 277-278 Bottle swap by Vande Hei; CFI-G tests using n-C4H10 (ops past midnight) Oct. 5 278 Launch of Shkaplerov Oct. 12 285 ACME chamber insert reconfigured for normal-flame tests by McArthur Oct. 13 286 Bottle swaps by Kimbrough Oct. 14 287 Bottle swap by McArthur Oct. 17 290 Landing of Novitsky Oct. 18 291 CFI-G normal-flame tests using 30% C3H8 (with ops planned until midnight) Nov. 8 312 Landing of Hoshide, Kimbrough, McArthur, and Pesquet 2022 March 30 089 Landing of Dubrov, Shkaplerov, and Vande Hei",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p50,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 50 Appendix E. Astronauts who Conducted ISS Operations for CFI-G This list was prepared by Dennis Stocker from his log of ACME operations. The CFI-G experiment was supported by five NASA astronauts, including three men and two women. They are listed by the number of days in which they conducted supporting on-orbit activities and alphabetically. Crew members from partner agencies were not involved (at least directly) in the research. Crew member Year Day(s) of year # days Vande Hei, Mark (NASA) 2021 126, 127, 165, 183, 214, 222, 228, 242, 251, 253, 277 11 McArthur, K. Megan (NASA) 2021 138, 145, 193, 200, 207, 256, 263, 265, 285, 287 10 Kimbrough, Shane (NASA) 2021 118, 119, 159, 215, 232, 235, 270, 286 8 Hopkins, Mike (NASA) 2021 075, 099, 109 3 Rubins, Kate (NASA) 2021 068-069, 070 3 The early CFI-G tasks were carried out by Kate Rubins and Mike Hopkins, but most of the on-orbit work was undertaken by Mark Vande Hei, Megan McArthur, and Shane Kimbrough. Additional information about the crew activity can be seen in Appendix D and some of the subsequent appendices.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p51,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 51 Appendix F. ISS Operations per the Payload Weekly Science Summary 2021 (for all of CFI-G) First round (normal flames) Increment 64 Week 21: March 5-11 On GMT 062, the final test points were performed for ACME CLD Flame (Coflow Laminar Diffusion). The Principal Investigator, Marshall Long from Yale University, considers the experiment to have been very successful. On this day, 11 flames were ignited, which contributed to the over 300 that were ignited for all of CLD Flame. Early results show that the dilution limits are extended in microgravity compared to normal gravity. Steady microgravity flames are also more stable and have lower lift-off heights due to the lack of buoyancy. We have gathered a superior set of experimental data to be used for improving our ability to computationally model highly diluted flames. This capability could be implemented in design tools resulting in more efficient and less polluting combustion here on Earth. On GMT 068 & 069, Kate Rubins configured the ACME Chamber Insert to support the next ACME experiment, CFI-G Part 1 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). CFI-G will study flames with unusually low temperatures known as cool flames. These flames were discovered in 2012 with the first subrack payload to use CIR, but this investigation only used liquid fuels. ACME’s hardware will allow CFI-G to study gaseous fuels to gain valuable insight into the combustion processes in engines.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p51,1,"The time-lapse CLD Flame image associated with this submission is not included in this report. Week 22: March 12-18 On GMT 075, Mike Hopkins lubricated the CIR GC Argon QDs and replaced the gas bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4. These tasks prepared the CIR for supporting the next ACME experiment, CFI-G Part 1 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). CFI-G will study flames with unusually low temperatures known as cool flames. These flames were discovered in 2012 with the first subrack payload to use CIR, but this investigation only used liquid fuels. ACME’s hardware will allow CFI-G to study gaseous fuels to gain valuable insight into the combustion processes in engines. Week 24: March 26 – April 1 On GMT 088, the first test points were performed for ACME CFI-G Part 1 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) with 14 ethane flames, sometimes diluted with nitrogen, ignited in various oxygen/nitrogen atmospheres. CFI-G studies flames with unusually low temperatures known as cool flames, which were discovered in 2012 with droplet combustion research being conducted in CIR at the time. That experiment burned liquid fuel droplets, which shrink as they burn. Instead, CFI-G will burn gaseous fuel ejected from a porous spherical burner to investigate cool flames under quasi-steady conditions. Cool flame chemical kinetics are important to ignition and the results could lead to cleaner burning and more efficient internal combustion engines.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p52,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 52 Figure 1. Preliminary False-Colored Image of Hot Ethane Flame for CFI-G This false-colored image shows a spherical flame, which is only possible in microgravity without the buoyant flows created by Earth’s gravity. Spherical flames greatly simplify experimental analysis by reducing the flame structure to a single dimension, which allows computational models to focus on chemical kinetics and transport phenomena. The hot flame in Figure 1 has a hole at the bottom because of heat loss to the fuel tube, which supplies the ethane to the porous sphere. The three bright lines passing through the flame are 14-micron ceramic fibers, which were translated into the flame to determine temperature measurements from the color of the glowing fibers. Residual soot clusters, like the fibers, are glowing because of their high temperature. These soot clusters were “pushed” to the bottom half of the flame by the insertion of the fibers.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p52,1,"Week 26: April 9-15 On GMT 102, nine nitrogen-diluted butane flames were ignited for ACME CFI-G (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). Non-premixed (i.e., fuel and oxidizer are not mixed prior to reacting) cool flames were discovered in 2012 with CIR droplet combustion research using liquid fuels. CFI-G’s goal is to study non- premixed cool flames using gaseous fuels, which have not been observed before and so far have not been observed in CFI-G testing. It is still possible that they may be detected in a more thorough review of the data. Whether cool flames are present or not, we are still acquiring useful results, such as data about soot processes. CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG In this test point video, a bright, sooty spherical flame is ignited with a retracting hot-wire igniter. Due to microgravity, the soot remains in the flame longer and forms clusters. Many clusters escape the flame while trapped soot eventually fades as it cools. On its right side, the flame starts to open due to heat loss to the burner’s fuel supply tube. With increasing heat loss, the flame nearly extinguishes, which creates a larger hole. This large hole in the flame shell allows for the direct mixing of the fuel and oxygen, which causes the flame to",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p53,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 53 momentarily reestablish. After a few oscillations, the hot flame extinguishes. The results from this and the other tests will be carefully reviewed to look for evidence of cool flame behavior. Increment 65 Week 2: April 16-22 On GMT 106, additional tests were performed with nitrogen-diluted butane for ACME CFI-G (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). While last week’s tests with this fuel were performed at a chamber pressure of 1 atm, this week’s tests were performed at half that pressure. Ten hot flames were ignited, and the data is being analyzed to determine if a cool flame occurred after the hot flame extinguished. In 13 other tests, data is being analyzed to determine if a cool flame was ignited directly with a low-energy ignition. Meanwhile, the data collected from the hot flames contribute to an earlier ACME experiment, Flame Design, which only tested with ethylene fuel. On GMT 109, Mike Hopkins replaced the gas bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4 to support continuation of CFI-G test points.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p53,1,"CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG This clip from a test point video shows a 50% butane flame ignited in a chamber atmosphere with elevated oxygen at 0.5 atm. The flame is bright and sooty, where the soot is formed when the fuel molecules within the flame combine in the absence of oxygen, which is outside the flame. The flame is like a bubble that separates the two reactants. The flame shell, like the film of the bubble, is a thin sheet where the fuel and oxygen come together and react, producing heat, light, carbon dioxide, and water vapor. Due to microgravity, the soot remains in the flame longer and forms clusters. Some of these soot clusters escape the flame—being pushed by the outward flow of the fuel from the burner. Others are trapped in the flame as a temperature gradient drives them away from the flame shell and toward the cooler burner. These opposing forces cause some soot clusters to just “dance” in place. After the video clip ends, the soot continues to fade as the flame temperature drops, where hardly any soot remained when the flame extinguished.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p53,2,"Week 3: April 23-29 On GMT 112, ACME CFI-G (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) test points were performed using propane fuel. Flames with unusually low temperatures, known as cool flames, were discovered in 2012 with CIR droplet combustion research using liquid fuels. CFI-G’s goal is to study cool flames using gaseous fuels to gain valuable insight into the combustion processes in engines. On GMT 118 and 119, Shane Kimbrough replaced an ACME controller and the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support continuation of CFI-G test points. Week 4: April 30 – May 6 On GMT 125, ACME CFI-G (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) test points were performed using nitrogen-diluted butane fuel at both elevated and normal oxygen concentrations. The data is being analyzed to determine if the elusive and hard-to-detect cool flames were burning after the hot flames extinguished or if they ignited directly. Regardless, the testing is providing valuable results that will add to our understanding of flammability limits and soot production, clustering, and oxidation.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p54,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 54 CFI-G studies flames with unusually low temperatures known as cool flames, which were discovered in 2012 with droplet combustion research being conducted in CIR at the time. That experiment burned liquid fuel droplets, which shrink as they burn. Instead, CFI-G will burn gaseous fuel ejected from a porous spherical burner to investigate cool flames under quasi-steady conditions. Cool flame chemistry is important to ignition, and the results could lead to cleaner burning and more efficient internal combustion engines. Figure 1. CFI-G Hot Flame of Nitrogen-Diluted Butane Figure 1 shows a hot flame of nitrogen-diluted butane in an atmosphere of elevated oxygen. The porous spherical burner of sintered stainless-steel sits on top of the tube that supplies the gaseous fuel. The blue flame shell is spherical, which is only possible in microgravity without the buoyant flows created by Earth’s gravity. Spherical flames greatly simplify experimental analysis by reducing the flame structure to a single dimension, which allows computational models to focus on the chemistry and transport phenomena rather than the prediction of the flame shape.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p54,1,"The yellow particles are clusters of soot. Their color is the result of thermal radiation and is indicative of their temperature. Soot is formed when the fuel molecules within the flame combine in the absence of oxygen, which is outside the flame. The flame is like a bubble that separates the two reactants. The flame shell, like the film of the bubble, is a thin sheet where the fuel and oxygen come together and react, producing heat, light, carbon dioxide, and water vapor. Due to microgravity, the soot remains in the flame longer and forms clusters. Some of these soot clusters escape the flame because they are pushed by the outward flow of the fuel from the burner. Others are trapped in the flame as a temperature gradient drives them away from the flame shell and toward the cooler burner. Week 5: May 7-13 On GMT 130, ACME CFI-G (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) test points were performed using nitrogen-diluted propane fuel at chamber pressures of both 2 and 3 atm. Fifteen hot flames were ignited.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p55,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 55 Figure 1. CFI-G Hot Flame of Nitrogen-Diluted Propane Figure 1 shows images of a CFI-G hot flame from two different cameras. The left image is from a color camera. The right image, which has been false-colored, is from an intensified camera filtered for formaldehyde (CH2O) emissions, which are associated with cool flame chemistry. When cool flames were first discovered in 1809, Sir Humphrey Davy observed a “pale phosphorescent light.” Residual soot created during ignition escapes from opposite sides of the flame. In the right image, soot is visible as light spots, such as at the top of the porous sphere. Week 6: May 14-20 On GMT 137, ACME CFI-G (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) test points were performed using nitrogen-diluted propane fuel in elevated oxygen concentrations (about 40%). These tests were performed at a chamber pressure of 1 atm, while last week’s tests with this fuel were performed at 2 and 3 atm. Twelve hot flames were ignited. Figure 1. CFI-G Hot Flame of Nitrogen-Diluted Propane",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p56,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 56 Figure 1 shows a hot flame of nitrogen-diluted propane in an atmosphere of elevated oxygen. At ignition, this flame was bright yellow from the soot, but the soot diminished over time due to radiative heat loss. In the absence of buoyancy, the soot remained in the flame longer and formed the large clusters seen in Figure 1. By the time the flame extinguished, the soot had largely faded away. The porous spherical burner of sintered stainless-steel sits on top of the tube that supplies the gaseous fuel. The opening in the bottom of the flame is due to heat loss to the tube. Week 7: May 21-27 On GMT 138, Megan McArthur replaced a failing FCF IPSU (Image Processing and Storage Unit) on the back of the CIR Optics Bench. Later in the week, we powered up the new IPSU and its associated camera package. The checkout was successful! On GMT 145, Megan replaced the gas bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4 to support continuation of CFI-G test points. Week 8: May 28 – June 3 On GMT 153, 14 nitrogen-diluted ethane flames were ignited for ACME CFI-G (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) in atmospheres ranging between 30-40% oxygen with pressures of both 0.5 and 2 atm. Figure 1. False-colored Image of a CFI-G Flame with Residual Soot Clusters",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p56,1,"Figure 1 shows a hot flame of nitrogen-diluted ethane in an atmosphere with elevated oxygen and pressure. Initially, the flame was bright yellow with soot, and over time some of the soot escaped from the flame. This preliminary false-colored image is from later in the test, where residual soot clusters were still visible within the flame when it self-extinguished due to excessive heat loss. Week 9: June 4-10 On GMT 159, Shane Kimbrough replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #2 to support continuation of CFI- G test points.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p57,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 57 Week 10: June 11-17 On GMT 162, 12 nitrogen-diluted ethane flames were ignited for ACME CFI-G (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) at ambient pressures. Some tests were performed in atmospheres with 21% oxygen, while some tests were performed in higher oxygen concentrations of 35-40%. During two tests, the 14-micron ceramic fibers were translated into the flame to optically measure the flame temperature through a technique known as Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP). On GMT 165, Mark Vande Hei replaced the gas bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4 to support continuation of CFI-G test points. Figure 1. Flame Temperature Measurement of CFI-G Hot Flame Figure 1 shows two images of the same nitrogen-diluted ethane flame. The right-hand image has been enhanced to show the faint spherical flame. The color of the fibers’ glow in the left-hand image is indicative of the temperature, where yellow is hotter than orange, which in turn is hotter than red. Using a camera characterized for quantitative measurements, we can determine a radial temperature distribution across the flame by analyzing the color variation along the length of a fiber.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p57,1,"The top fiber is outside of the flame. Its glow can be used to determine the temperature near the flame, where it is obviously cooler despite its proximity. The fibers that pass through the flame are much brighter except for their centers, which are dimmer and cooler. The flame shell separates the fuel and the oxygen, where the heat, light, carbon dioxide, and water vapor are created at its surface, so the flame is hottest at its shell. Week 11: June 18-24 On GMT 174, we ignited 16 nitrogen-diluted butane flames in the final tests for the first round of ACME CFI-G (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). Cool flames remained elusive, where there has been no clear evidence of these low-temperature phenomena during this round of testing. In the coming weeks and months, we will review the data more closely to assess their potential presence. Regardless, we gathered a wealth of valuable data that will improve our fundamental understanding of soot process and flame dynamics. Since CFI-G tested with different fuels, these data expand upon the results gained from the other ACME experiments.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p57,2,"Next week, the ACME Chamber Insert will be configured to support the second and final round of CFI-G testing. This testing will explore the potential existence of cool flames in an inverse configuration. The flow direction will be reversed, where a mixture of nitrogen and oxygen will flow from the burner into the Combustion Chamber, which will be filled with a mixture of nitrogen and gaseous fuel. As a result, the flames will be inside out, where, as an example, the soot will be formed outside of the flame.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p58,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 58 CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG In this test point video, soot that forms at ignition escapes from the flame, especially opposite of the burner’s supply tube (toward the left). The escaping soot clusters exacerbate the heat loss experienced by the flame and cause it to locally quench, creating a hole in the flame. This hole allows for direct mixing of the fuel and oxygen, which are normally separated by the flame, thereby enhancing flammability. As a result, the flame reforms, closing the hole, but it opens again due to heat loss. This repeated opening and closing of the flame creates a visually dramatic flame that swirls around the porous spherical burner. Second round (inverse flames except for the final test day) Week 12: June 25 – July 1 Recent data analysis shows evidence of cool flames! Data from several of the ACME CFI-G (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) tests on GMT 174 indicate the presence of cool flames. These tests were performed at a chamber pressure of 2 atm with nitrogen- diluted butane fuel. Cool flames give off little heat or light and were too faint to be visible real time during testing. But their presence was revealed by thermal radiation and burner temperature measurements after the hot flames quenched.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p58,1,"Non-premixed (i.e., fuel and oxidizer are not mixed prior to reacting) cool flames were discovered in 2012 with CIR droplet combustion research using liquid fuels. Now, we’ve achieved non-premixed cool flames using gaseous fuels! Cool flame chemistry is important to ignition and the results could lead to vehicles that experience less wear on engine parts, get better gas mileage, and create less pollution. Figure 1. Time Sequence from CFI-G Hot Flame to Cool Flame",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p59,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 59 The images in Figure 1 are from an intensified camera filtered to look for the very faint emissions of a cool flame (associated with the excited formaldehyde molecule, CH2O*). The sequence shows the quenching of the hot flame due to radiative heat loss, followed by a comparatively dark period lasting roughly a second, after which the cool flame becomes evident. As expected, the cool flame is much smaller than the hot flame. The dark center in each cool flame image is the porous spherical burner and its fuel supply tube can be seen extending to the right. Week 13: July 2-8 On GMT 183, Mark Vande Hei configured the ACME Chamber Insert to support CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). CFI-G studies flames with unusually low temperatures known as cool flames. Part 2 testing will explore the potential existence of cool flames in an inverse configuration. The flow direction will be reversed, where a mixture of nitrogen and oxygen will flow from the burner into the Combustion Chamber, which will be filled with a mixture of nitrogen and gaseous fuel. As a result, the flames will be inside out, where, as an example, the soot will be formed outside of the flame. Cool flame chemistry is important to ignition and the results could lead to vehicles that experience less wear on engine parts, get better gas mileage, and create less pollution.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p59,1,"Week 14: July 9-15 On GMT 193, Megan McArthur replaced the gas bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4 to support the start of CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) test points. Week 15: July 16-22 On GMT 196, the first test points were performed for ACME CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases), where inverse flames were ignited in space for the first time. We ignited seven sustained inverse flames with diluted ethane fuel at both elevated and reduced pressures. With inverse flames, the diluted oxygen flows from the burner into a chamber filled with diluted gaseous fuel. As a result, inverse flames are inside out, where, as an example, the soot is formed outside rather than inside the flame. On GMT 200, Megan McArthur replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support continuation of CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) test points. Figure 1. CFI-G Inverse Flame of Nitrogen-Diluted Ethane",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p60,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 60 Figure 1 shows a bright blue, spherical inverse flame. The porous spherical burner of sintered stainless- steel sits on top of the tube that typically supplies gaseous fuel for normal flames. Instead, for inverse flames it supplies oxygen, which may be diluted with nitrogen. Week 16: July 23-29 On GMT 202, we ignited 11 inverse flames for CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). For these tests, an oxygen/nitrogen mixture flowed from the burner into the Combustion Chamber filled with an ethane/nitrogen mixture. We observed that flames in 12% ethane burned longer than those in 6% ethane. Tests were performed at a chamber pressure of both 1 and 2 atm, and some tests used the array of 14-micron ceramic fibers to make flame temperature measurements. On GMT 207, Megan McArthur replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support continuation of CFI-G Part 2 test points. Figure 1. Composite Image of CFI-G Inverse Flames at Increasing Oxidizer Flow Rates Figure 1 compares the flames from three tests with different flow rates of oxidizer from the burner. The flame size is dependent on the limiting reactant, which for inverse flames is the oxygen.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p60,1,"The porous spherical burner and its supply tube are visible within each spherical flame. Heat loss to the supply tube causes the flame to locally quench and creates an opening. This opening leads to direct mixing of the reactants, which would otherwise be separated by the flame shell. Mixing enhances the combustion and brightens the flame at the opening. Week 17: July 30 – Aug. 5 On GMT 209 and 216, we ignited 23 inverse flames in a Combustion Chamber filled with ethane and nitrogen for CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). These two test days ignited flames in different fuel concentrations and pressures while an oxygen/nitrogen mixture flowed from the porous spherical burner. We gained valuable information about the conditions that influence soot formation. On GMT 214, Mark Vande Hei replaced the gas bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4. On GMT 215, Shane Kimbrough opened the valves on these two bottles. These tasks were performed to support continuation of CFI-G Part 2 test points. CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG This test point video of a CFI-G inverse flame is at 3x normal speed. The flame is initially blue but overtime an orange band appears just outside the flame. This orange band is made up of soot particles, which are black but glow because of their high temperature—just like metal in a forge.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p61,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 61 The soot is formed when the fuel, at a high temperature, reacts with itself in the absence of oxygen. The temperature is highest at the flame’s surface, and for inverse flames, the fuel is outside the flame. Therefore, in this test, the soot forms just outside the flame. The glowing soot doesn’t fully encompass the flame because of heat loss to the burner’s supply tube and because the flow from the burner is not quite uniform. However, in this instance, these shortcomings reveal the sensitivity of soot formation to temperature, residence time, and reactant mixture. Week 18: Aug. 6-12 On GMT 222, Mark Vande Hei replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). We then ignited seven inverse flames in a Combustion Chamber filled with propane and nitrogen. These sustained flames flowed oxygen/nitrogen mixtures over a broad range of concentrations from the spherical burner into the fuel-filled chamber. The testing today was limited to conserve the oxidizer for upcoming tests with a higher propane concentration but lower chamber pressure. Figure 1. Comparison inverse flame tests with the same oxygen flow, but a greater nitrogen flow on the right.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p61,1,"This image compares two CFI-G inverse flames that had the same oxygen flow rate, but where the flame on the right had a greater nitrogen flow rate, making the oxygen more dilute and the total flow rate greater. The chamber had been filled to 3 atm with propane and nitrogen. The flames, photographed with an intensified camera, are similar in size. Flame size is determined by access to sufficient fuel to consume the oxygen, which was at the same flow rate in both cases. Week 19: Aug. 13-19 On GMT 228, Mark Vande Hei replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). We then ignited sixteen sustained inverse flames in a Combustion Chamber filled with propane and nitrogen at two different pressures. The test parameters included",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p62,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 62 varying the concentration ratios of the oxygen/nitrogen mixture flowing from the burner and the flow rates. Figure 1. Composite Image of CFI-G Inverse Flames at Increasing Flow Rates This composite image compares inverse flames with multiples of oxygen/nitrogen flow rates from the porous spherical burner faintly seen within the flames. The oxygen flows outward until it reaches sufficient fuel to be consumed. As such, the tests with the greater oxidizer flow rates result in larger flames. Radiative heat loss scales with flame size, and the larger flames are dimmer than the smaller flames. The oxygen/nitrogen ratio, propane concentration, and Combustion Chamber pressure were consistent in this trio of tests. These flames are locally quenched by heat loss to the burner’s supply tube leaving an opening in the flame. The opening, which allows reactant mixing, often oscillates in size while growing until the flame fully extinguishes. In some tests the extinction occurs abruptly, but in others the oscillations can last for tens of seconds. Meanwhile, some flames are quasi-steady despite the opening. This is one example of how these spherical flames are not as simple as had earlier been imagined.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p62,1,"Week 20: Aug. 20-26 Shane Kimbrough replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #2 on GMT 232 and the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 on GMT 235 to support CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). We then ignited eight sustained inverse flames in a Combustion Chamber filled with a 20% propane mixture at two different pressures. These tests also varied the flow rate and concentration ratio of the oxygen/nitrogen mixture flowing from the burner. CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG This clip from the beginning of a test point video shows an inverse flame that is initially sooty, but the soot quickly fades away as the flame temperature drops. With inverse flames, the fuel is outside the flame, so that is where the soot forms. Soot is created when the fuel in the chamber, heated to a high temperature by the flame, combines in the absence of oxygen, which is inside the flame. The flame cools due to both radiative heat loss and conductive heat loss to the burner, especially to the tube supplying the oxygen/nitrogen mixture. The soot lasts the longest away from the supply tube where the flame is hottest. To visibly glow due to their temperature, materials must",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p63,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 63 be about 800 K. Therefore, if it’s not hot enough for soot to glow, then it’s also not hot enough for the fuel to combine and then form the soot. Week 21: Aug. 27 – Sept. 2 On GMT 242, Megan McArthur replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). We then ignited only two inverse flames before a hard drive failure in the FCF Image Processor, which processes and stores our image data. Then, during the end-of-the-day check, we discovered that the igniter tip had burned out as well. This day was our fourth day of testing with inverse propane flames, and we have yet to see evidence of cool flames with this fuel. After the hardware is replaced, we will start inverse-flame testing with butane— the fuel with which cool flames were discovered in earlier normal-flame testing. CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG In this test point video, the bright soot shell that surrounds the propane flame hides the spherical burner. The high temperature of the flame is revealed when it extinguishes, and you can see the burner glowing because of its high temperature. The test point automatically stops when the burner reaches 650 °C. Materials start glowing because of their temperature at about 525 °C (the Draper point).",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p63,1,"Week 22: Sept. 3-9 On GMT 251, Mark Vande Hei replaced the burnt-out igniter tip for CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). Following replacement of the FCF Image Processor failed hard drive later this week, we’ll return to performing CFI-G test points. Week 23: Sept. 10-16 On GMT 253 on the back of the CIR Optics Bench, Mark Vande Hei replaced the failed hard drive in the FCF Image Processor. Afterwards, the CIR was powered up and a checkout of the new hard drive and igniter tip was successful. On GMT 256, Megan McArthur replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). We then ignited 17 inverse flames in a Combustion Chamber filled with nitrogen and butane at two different concentrations. Tests were performed at a chamber pressure of about 2 atm, and we varied the oxygen concentration and oxidizer flow rate from the burner into the fuel-filled chamber. A couple tests also used the array of 14-micron ceramic fibers to make flame temperature measurements.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p64,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 64 Figure 1. CFI-G Inverse Flames at Increasing Oxidizer Flow Rate and Butane Concentrations This composite image shows the effects of both the oxidizer flow rate from the burner and the butane concentration on the inverse flames. The oxygen concentration was held constant in these four tests. From bottom to top, the oxidizer flow rate was doubled, which greatly impacted the flame size. From left to right, the ~20% increase in the butane concentration had a more subtle effect. At the higher fuel concentration, the flames are slightly smaller and much brighter. Week 24: Sept. 17-23 Megan McArthur replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #2 on GMT 263 and the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 on GMT 265 to support CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). We then ignited 15 inverse flames in a Combustion Chamber filled with butane and nitrogen while an oxygen/nitrogen mixture flowed from the porous spherical burner. Tests were performed at a chamber pressure of 3 atm, and we varied the oxidizer flow rate and oxygen/nitrogen ratio. Figure 1. Effects of Oxidizer Flow Rate from the Burner on CFI-G Inverse Flames",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p65,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 65 This composite image shows the effects of the oxidizer flow rate on inverse flames. In these tests, the flame size depends on the amount of fuel in the atmosphere required to consume the oxygen, so a larger flow rate results in a larger flame. However, a flame’s radiative heat loss increases with its size and the larger flame is significantly dimmer. Measurements of thermal radiation from the flame on the right were several times greater than from the flame on the left. Week 25: Sept. 24-30 On GMT 270, Megan McArthur replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). We then ignited ten inverse flames. For these tests, we varied the concentration of the butane fuel in the Combustion Chamber, the flow rate of the oxygen/nitrogen mixture from the burner, and the oxygen concentration. Figure 1. Effects of Butane Concentration on a CFI-G Inverse Flame",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p65,1,"Figure 1 is a graph of the thermal radiation emitted by an inverse flame, and it compares two tests with the same oxidizer mixture ratio and flow rate but a different butane concentration. The dim flame in the 14.0% butane experienced large oscillations (shown in blue on the graph) due to heat loss to the burner’s supply tube. These oscillations grew in strength until the flame self-extinguished. In contrast, the flame in 17.5% butane was much brighter. It eventually experienced similar oscillations (shown in red on the graph), but they were weaker and didn’t cause the flame to extinguish during the test. Week 26: Oct. 1-7 On GMT 277, Mark Vande Hei replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support CFI-G Part 2 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). We then ignited eleven inverse flames. The tests were carried out at a pressure of 0.5 atm with various butane concentrations ranging from 14 to 36% (by volume), where the",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p66,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 66 fuel was diluted with nitrogen. The other test parameters included changing the flow rate and ratio of the oxygen/nitrogen mixture issuing from the burner. This marks the completion of CFI-G Part 2! Figure 1. Example CFI-G Inverse Butane Flame This example image can be readily identified as an inverse flame because the soot is outside, rather than inside, of the flame. The soot is formed when the fuel combines with itself at high temperatures in the absence of oxygen. In this regard, the inverse flame is like a bubble separating the outer fuel from the inner oxygen, where the two reactants are consumed producing light, heat, and chemical products (e.g., carbon dioxide and water vapor). Flames of hydrocarbon fuels are naturally blue, while the high- temperature soot glows red, orange, yellow, or even white depending on its temperature. At ignition, this flame was all bright and sooty, but the system is not fully symmetric. There is some asymmetry to the burner flow, and there is heat loss to the tube supplying the gas to the porous spherical burner. As a result, the soot faded from the lower hemisphere, which in this image appears to be soot free. Meanwhile, the upper hemisphere remained enveloped in a layer of glowing soot. Tests like this have yielded valuable data for improving our understanding of how to control soot generation.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p66,1,"Week 27: Oct. 8-14 On GMT 285, Megan McArthur configured the ACME Chamber Insert to support an extra day of testing for CFI-G Part 1 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases). Part 1 studies normal flames as opposed to inverse flames like Part 2. For normal flames, the gaseous fuel flows from the burner into a Combustion Chamber filled with diluted oxygen. The reverse is true for inverse flames, making them inside out. The purpose of this extra day is to recreate the test conditions in which we achieved cool flames in earlier testing. The goal of CFI-G is to study these low temperature flames to gain valuable insight into the combustion processes in engines. Also, to support this extra day of testing, Shane Kimbrough set the CIR Valve Timers and replaced the gas bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4 on GMT 286 and Megan McArthur replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 on GMT 287.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p67,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 67 Increment 66 Week 1: Oct. 15-21 On GMT 291, we performed the last 12 tests for ACME CFI-G (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) during an extra day of testing with normal flames. The purpose of this extra day was to recreate the test conditions in which we achieved cool flames in earlier testing, and we successfully achieved cool flames! In two tests, the butane hot flames extinguished due to radiative heat loss, but then seconds later they reignited. This extinction and reignition occurred several times during each of these tests. The butane reignited because a cool flame occurred between the hot flames. Cool flame chemistry is important for ignition in internal combustion engines and an improved understanding would aid the development of vehicles that experience less wear on engine parts, get better gas mileage, and create less pollution. CAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG This video clip is from the end of a test in which the hot butane flame repeatedly extinguishes and reignites. While the low-temperature cool flames between the hot flames aren’t visible in this video, they cause the closest thermocouple to warm enough to visibly glow prior to reignition.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p67,1,"Reignition causes the temperature to abruptly increase, but then it drops while the hot flame burns and weakens. When the hot flame extinguishes and transitions to a cool flame, the temperature starts increasing again until another temperature spike caused by the next reignition. In another test, we saw evidence of a cool flame where the temperature temporarily rose after the hot flame extinguished but not enough to reignite a hot flame.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p68,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 68 Appendix G. Crew Activity per Space Station Science Highlights The subsequent text and images are from the weekly highlights prepared by the Lead Increment Scientist and made available via e-mail and online via www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/station/research/. With the major exception of the Space Station Science Highlights, links were generally not included in the excerpts herein because they typically go to secondary sources of information for the ACME project, for which the primary web site is https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/acme/. Clarifications have been added in red. 2021 (for all of CFI-G) First round (normal flames) Week of March 8, 2021 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • ACME is a set of six independent studies of gaseous flames. ACME’s goals are to advance fuel efficiency and reduce pollutant production in practical combustion on Earth, and to improve spacecraft fire prevention.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p68,1,"Week of April 26, 2021 Observing flames The Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) include six independent studies of flames that seek to improve fuel efficiency and reduce pollution from combustion on Earth and help prevent fires in spacecraft. ACME investigations are conducted in the space station’s Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR), which includes a combustion chamber, gas bottles, and five cameras for observing flames. The research aimed at spacecraft fire prevention examines different materials, including how to put them out when burning, conditions needed for a material to continue burning, and the effectiveness of existing methods to screen and select materials for spacecraft. The crew replaced controllers for ACME during the week. Week of June 7, 2021 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • ACME includes six independent studies to improve fuel efficiency and reduce pollution from combustion on Earth and help prevent fires in spacecraft. The spacecraft fire prevention studies examine different materials, conditions needed for them to burn, how to extinguish them, and the effectiveness of methods to screen and select materials for spacecraft. Note that only one of the six ACME experiments was focused on spacecraft fire safety and that was Burning Rate Emulator (BRE), not CFI-G.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p69,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 69 Second round (inverse flames except for the final test day) Week of July 12, 2021 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • ACME is a set of six independent studies of gaseous flames intended to advance fuel efficiency and reduce pollutant production on Earth and improve spacecraft fire prevention. Currently, Cool Flames Investigation with Gases observes the chemical reactions of cool diffusion flames. Week of July 19, 2021 This Space to Ground video is accessible via the following sites, where the CFI-G discussion appears halfway through the 3 minute clip. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4q8yN1bSsr8 https://images.nasa.gov/details-jsc2021m000030_Space%20to%20Ground_380_210723 Week of July 26, 2021 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • ACME includes six independent studies of flames that seek to improve fuel efficiency and reduce pollution from combustion on Earth and help prevent fires in spacecraft. Week of August 2, 2021 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • Cool Flames Investigation with Gases, part of the ACME series of studies, observes chemical reactions of cool flames, which burn at lower temperatures. Nearly impossible to create in Earth’s gravity, cool flames are easily created in microgravity and studying them may improve understanding of combustion and fires on Earth.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p70,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 70 Week of August 9, 2021 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • Cool Flames Investigation with Gases, part of the ACME series of studies, observes chemical reactions of cool flames, which burn at lower temperatures. Nearly impossible to create in Earth’s gravity, cool flames are easily created in microgravity and studying them may improve understanding of combustion and fires on Earth. Week of August 16, 2021 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • Cool Flames Investigation with Gases, part of the ACME series of studies, observes chemical reactions of cool flames, which burn at lower temperatures. Nearly impossible to create in Earth’s gravity, cool flames are easily created in microgravity and studying them may improve understanding of combustion and fires on Earth. Week of August 23, 2021 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • Cool Flames Investigation with Gases, part of the ACME series of studies, observes chemical reactions of cool flames, which burn at lower temperatures. Cool flames were first discovered aboard the space station in 2012 burning liquid fuel; more recently, the investigation created cool flames burning gaseous fuels. Studying these flames may improve understanding of combustion and fires on Earth.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p70,1,"Week of September 6, 2021 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • Cool Flames Investigation with Gases, part of the ACME series of studies, observes chemical reactions of cool flames, which burn at lower temperatures. Nearly impossible to create in Earth’s gravity, cool flames are easily created in microgravity and studying them may improve understanding of combustion and fires on Earth. Week of September 20, 2021 Other investigations involving the crew: • Cool Flames Investigation with Gases, part of the ACME series of studies, observes chemical reactions of cool flames, which burn at lower temperatures. Nearly impossible to create in Earth’s gravity, cool flames are easily created in microgravity and studying them may improve understanding of combustion and fires on Earth. Week of September 27, 2021 Other investigations involving the crew: • Cool Flames Investigation with Gases, part of the ACME series of studies, observes chemical reactions of cool flames, which burn at lower temperatures. Nearly impossible to create in Earth’s gravity, cool flames are easily created in microgravity, and studying them may improve understanding of combustion and fires on Earth.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p71,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 71 Week of October 4, 2021 Other investigations on which the crew performed work: • Cool Flames Investigation with Gases, part of the ACME series of studies, observes chemical reactions of cool flames, which burn at lower temperatures. Nearly impossible to create in Earth’s gravity, cool flames are easily created in microgravity, and studying them may improve understanding of combustion and fires on Earth. Week of October 11, 2021 Coolest flames in space The Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project is a set of six independent studies of gaseous flames conducted in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). The researchers’ goals are to gain information that can lead to improved fuel efficiency, reduced pollutant production in practical combustion on Earth, and improved spacecraft fire prevention. One of the ACME studies currently being conducted is known as the Cool Flames Investigation with Gases. A cool flame is one that burns at about 600 degrees Celsius. A candle is about two times hotter, burning at around 1,400 degrees Celsius. The results of this investigation could lead to cleaner, more efficient internal combustion engines. This past week, a crew member was tasked with replacing a butane bottle in preparation for the experiment.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p71,1,"Week of October 18, 2021 Other investigations involving the crew: • Cool Flames Investigation with Gases, part of the ACME series of studies, observes chemical reactions of cool flames, which burn at lower temperatures. Nearly impossible to create in Earth’s gravity, cool flames are easily created in microgravity and studying them may improve understanding of combustion and fires on Earth.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p72,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 72 Appendix H. Crew Activity per the ISS Daily Summary Report This appendix was compiled from https://blogs.nasa.gov/stationreport. Headings were added identifying the investigation’s two rounds of testing, but CFI-G operations listed under the headings “All activities are complete unless otherwise noted” or “Completed Task List Activities” were not included in this report. Some days of crew activity were only listed in those ways and as a result this section does not comprehensively document crew activity in support of the CFI-G experiment. Minor additions were made in red to correct the names and abbreviations of ACME investigations. 2021 (for all of CFI-G) First round (normal flames)",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p72,1,"2021 March 9: GMT 068 Combustion Integrated Rack/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (CIR/ACME): The crew performed the first part of the work needed to reconfigure the ACME chamber insert from Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame (CLD Flame) support to Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G) support, the next ACME experiment to be performed. Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet combustion experiments aboard the ISS in 2012, and this initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. A cool flame is one that burns at about 600 degrees Celsius. A typical candle is about two-and- a-half times hotter, burning at around 1,400 degrees Celsius. Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p72,2,"2021 March 10: GMT 069 Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR)/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME): The crew performed the second part of the work needed to reconfigure the ACME chamber insert from Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame (CLD Flame) support to Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G) support, which is the next ACME experiment to be performed. Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet combustion experiments aboard the ISS in 2012, and this initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. A cool flame is one that burns at about 600 degrees Celsius. A typical candle is about two-and-a-half times hotter, burning at around 1,400 degrees Celsius. Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p72,3,"2021 March 16: GMT 075 Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) / Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME): The crew performed the steps necessary to lubricate the quick disconnect (QD) for the gas chromatograph argon gas bottle. They also replaced the N2/O2 gas bottle at manifold 2 and installed an ethane fuel bottle at the manifold 4 location. This was performed in support of the Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G) experiment. Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet combustion experiments aboard the ISS in 2012, and this initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. A cool flame is one that burns at about 600 degrees Celsius. A typical candle is about three-and-a-half times hotter, burning at around 1,400 degrees Celsius. Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p73,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 73 chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences. 2021 April 9: GMT 099 CIR/ACME/CFI-G (Combustion Integrated Rack/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments/Cool Flames Investigation with Gases): As a continuation of the on-going CFI-G experiment, the crew removed the 100% ethane fuel bottle and installed a 50% butane, 50% nitrogen fuel bottle. Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet combustion experiments aboard the ISS in 2012, and this initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. A cool flame is one that burns at about 600 degrees Celsius. A typical candle is about three-and-a-half times hotter, burning at around 1,400 degrees Celsius. Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences. 2021 April 19: GMT 109 Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) – Fluids and Combustion Facility: The crew replaced the Manifold 2 and 4 gas bottles for upcoming CIR operations. The CIR includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion investigations in microgravity.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p73,1,2021 April 28: GMT 118 ACME (Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments): Crew replaced an ACME controller. The crew opened the rack doors and the combustion chamber of the ACME and then replaced the Fuel Controller. The Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project is a set of six independent studies of gaseous flames to be conducted in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). ACME’s primary and secondary goals are (1) improved fuel efficiency and reduced pollutant production in practical combustion on Earth and (2) spacecraft fire prevention through innovative research focused on materials flammability.,,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p73,2,"2021 May 7: GMT 127 CIR/ACME (Combustion Integrated Rack/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments): The crew replaced the 40% O2/60% N2 manifold bottle with a new bottle of the same composition. They also replaced the 30% butane/70% N2 bottle with a new bottle of the same composition. This was performed in support of the CFI-G (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) experiment. Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet combustion experiments aboard the ISS in 2012, and this initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. A cool flame is one that burns at about 600 degrees Celsius. A typical candle is about three-and-a-half times hotter, burning at around 1,400 degrees Celsius. Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences. 2021 May 18: GMT 138 CIR IPSU Replacement: Today, a crewmember replaced the failed FCF IPSU in the CIR (Combustion Integrated Rack), which included moving the FCF IPSU at position UML2 to replace the FCF IPSU at position",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p74,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 74 UML5. The Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion investigations in microgravity. 2021 May 25: GMT 145 ACME (Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments): A crewmember replaced CIR Manifold #4 with a new C4H10 (Butane) and C2H6 (Ethane) bottles and replaced O2 bottles in Manifold #2. The Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project is a set of six independent studies of gaseous flames to be conducted in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). ACME’s primary and secondary goals are (1) improved fuel efficiency and reduced pollutant production in practical combustion on Earth, and (2) spacecraft fire prevention through innovative research focused on materials flammability.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p74,1,"2021 June 8: GMT 159 CIR/ACME (Combustion Integrated Rack/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments): The crew replaced a 40% O2, 60% N2manifold bottle with another bottle of the same composition. This was performed in support of the CFI-G part 1 (Cool Flames Investigation with Gases) experiment. Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet combustion experiments aboard the ISS in 2012, and this initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. A cool flame is one that burns at about 600 degrees Celsius. A typical candle is about three-and-a-half times hotter, burning at around 1,400 degrees Celsius. Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p74,2,"2021 June 14: GMT 165 Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G): The crew replaced manifold #2 and #4 bottles with bottles of the same composition. This was performed in support of part 1 of the CFI-G experiment. Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet combustion experiments aboard the ISS in 2012, and this initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. A cool flame burns at about 600 degrees Celsius. A typical candle is about three-and-a-half times hotter, burning at around 1,400 degrees Celsius. Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences. Second round (inverse flames except for the final test day)",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p74,3,"2021 July 2: GMT 183 Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) / Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME): The crew performed the steps necessary to access the ACME combustion insert and reconfigured from Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G) part 1 to CFI-G part 2. This involved replacing the igniter tip, replacing three ACME flow controllers, etc. Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet combustion experiments aboard the ISS in 2012. This initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. A cool flame is one that burns at about 600 degrees Celsius. A typical candle is about two times hotter, burning at around 1,400 degrees Celsius. Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p75,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 75 on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences. 2021 July 12: GMT 193 Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) – Fluids and Combustion Facility: The crew removed the O2 Bottle from the CIR Manifold #2 rack and replaced it with a high percentage oxygen bottle containing 85% O2 and 15% N2. The crew also replaced the fuel bottle in the CIR Manifold #4 rack with a bottle containing 100% C2H6 (Ethane). The CIR includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion investigations in microgravity. 2021 July 19: GMT 200 Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) – Fluids and Combustion Facility: The crew exchanged the manifold #4 bottle with a bottle containing 100% C2H6. The CIR includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel, and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion investigations in microgravity.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p75,1,"2021 July 26: GMT 207 Combustion Integrated Rack/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (CIR/ACME): The crew removed a used 100% ethane (CH3-CH3) fuel bottle with a new bottle of the same composition. This will allow the continuation of the Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G) part 2 test points. Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet combustion experiments aboard the ISS in 2012, and this initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. A cool flame is one that burns at about 600 degrees Celsius. A typical candle is about two times hotter, burning at around 1,400 degrees Celsius. Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p75,2,"2021 Aug. 2: GMT 214 Combustion Integrated Rack/Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (CIR/ACME): The crew exchanged a used 85% O2/ 15% N2 bottle with a new one of the same composition; however, during the installation, a loose pressure relief valve body was found. The ground is discussing steps to re-tighten the valve body and proceed with operations. This will allow the continuation of the Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G) part 2 test points. Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet combustion experiments aboard the ISS in 2012, and this initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. A cool flame is one that burns at about 600 degrees Celsius. A typical candle is about two times hotter, burning at around 1,400 degrees Celsius. Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences. 2021 Aug. 10: GMT 222 Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) – Fluids and Combustion Facility (FCF): The crew replaced the Manifold 4 fuel bottle with 1.00 liters of 100% C3H8 (Propane). The CIR includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion investigations in microgravity.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p76,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 76 2021 Aug. 16: GMT 228 Cool Flames Investigation with Gases: The crew replaced the Manifold 4 fuel bottle with 1.00 liters of 100% C3H8 (Propane). Cool diffusion flames were discovered during droplet combustion experiments aboard the ISS in 2012, and this initiated a rapidly growing field of combustion research. A cool flame is one that burns at about 600 degrees Celsius. A typical candle is about three-and-a-half times hotter, burning at around 1,400 degrees Celsius. Most internal combustion engines are designed using computer models that neglect cool flame chemistry, but ignition and flame propagation in engines depend on cool flame chemistry. Cool flame chemistry also has a significant impact on fuel octane and cetane numbers, whose understanding has large economic consequences.",,False
