PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p85,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 85 Appendix L. ACME Operations per Astronauts on Twitter This tweet is from an ISS crew member who conducted operations in support of the CFI-G experiment. There may be others which are not included here. Megan K. McArthur (@Astro_Megan) on Twitter, https://twitter.com/astro_megan/ 2021 May 24: GMT 144",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p86,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 86 Appendix M. Summary of the ISS data collection The CFI-G data is all digital and includes both image and non-image data, where this is true for the other ACME experiments. To be clear, there was no collection of soot or other physical samples that were subsequently transported to the ground. The CFI-G image data includes images from the following cameras: 1. ACME DataCam, used with a clear fused-silica ‘filter’ and various zoom and focus settings (but those settings were consistent within each individual test) 2. CFI intensified camera, used always (or at least nearly so) with a formaldehyde filter for CFI-G 3. High-Bitdepth Multispectral (HiBMs) camera, equipped with a red filter corresponding to the opposing CIR illumination package, which was sometimes left off The raw DataCam images are in a Halcon Iconic Object (hobj) format, while the images from the CFI, and HiBMs cameras are provided in a Tagged Image File (tif) format. In contrast, the analog video was recorded on the ground in a Transport Stream (ts) format. The key non-imaging data from a test is a comma-delimited file that is described in detail in the Software Interface Design Description (ACME-DOC-010 Rev. D). The raw data is currently archived at NASA and work is underway to make it available publicly through NASA’s Physical Science Informatics System (PSI).",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p87,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 87 Appendix N. Publications, presentations, awards, and outreach Papers, etc. listed in italics are general to ACME rather than specific to the CFI-G experiment. N.1 Publications and Presentations with ISS Results Refereed Journal Articles 1. M. Kim, K.A. Waddell, P.B. Sunderland, V. Nayagam, D.P. Stocker, D.L. Dietrich, Y. Ju, F.A. Williams, P. H. Irace, R.L. Axelbaum, Spherical Gas-Fueled Cool Diffusion Flames, Proceedings of the Combustion Institute (2022) in press. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.proci.2022.07.015",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p87,1,"Conference Proceedings, Abstracts, Posters, Etc. 1. Dennis P. Stocker, Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments on the International Space Station, Work-in-Progress Poster, 12th U.S. National Combustion Meeting, virtual, 24- 26 May 2021. 2. Dennis P. Stocker, Recent Research on Flames of Gaseous Fuel Aboard the International Space Station, Conference of the Japanese Society of Microgravity Application (JASMAC-33), virtual, 13-15 Oct. 2021, 11 pp. 3. Peter B. Sunderland, Transformative Combustion Research in the ACME Suite of ISS Experiments, 37th Annual Meeting of the American Society for Gravitational and Space Research, Baltimore MD, 3-6 Nov. 2021. 4. Dennis P. Stocker, Laminar Non-Premixed Flames of Gaseous Fuel Aboard the International Space Station, Spring Technical Meeting of The Central States Section of the Combustion Institute, Detroit, Mi, 15-17 May 2022, 10 pp. 5. M. Kim, K.A. Waddell, P.B. Sunderland, V. Nayagam, D.P. Stocker, D.L. Dietrich, Y. Ju, F.A. Williams., P. H. Irace, R.L. Axelbaum, The First Observations of Spherical Gas-Fueled Cool Diffusion Flames, Spring Technical Meeting of the Eastern States Section of The Combustion Institute, Orlando, 6 pp., 2022. Theses and Dissertations 1. M. Kim, Spherical Gas-Fueled Cool Diffusion Flames, MS Thesis, University of Maryland, 60 pp., May 2022. N.2 Publications and Presentations (comprehensive list) Papers, etc. in this section which include ISS results are enclosed in borders like this.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p87,2,"Refereed Journal Articles 1. M. Kim, K.A. Waddell, P.B. Sunderland, V. Nayagam, D.P. Stocker, D.L. Dietrich, Y. Ju, F.A. Williams, P. H. Irace, R.L. Axelbaum, Spherical Gas-Fueled Cool Diffusion Flames, Proceedings of the Combustion Institute (2022) in press. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.proci.2022.07.015 2. K.A. Waddell, H. Lee, V. Nayagam, R.L. Axelbaum, P.B. Sunderland, Cool Diffusion Flames in a Stably Stratified Stagnation Flow, Combustion and Flame, submitted.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p88,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 88 Conference Proceedings, Abstracts, Posters, Etc. 1. Dennis P. Stocker, Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments on the International Space Station, Work-in-Progress Poster, 12th U.S. National Combustion Meeting, virtual, 24- 26 May 2021. 2. Dennis P. Stocker, Recent Research on Flames of Gaseous Fuel Aboard the International Space Station, Conference of the Japanese Society of Microgravity Application (JASMAC-33), virtual, 13-15 Oct. 2021, 11 pp. 3. Peter B. Sunderland, Transformative Combustion Research in the ACME Suite of ISS Experiments, 37th Annual Meeting of the American Society for Gravitational and Space Research, Baltimore MD, 3-6 Nov. 2021. 4. Dennis P. Stocker, Laminar Non-Premixed Flames of Gaseous Fuel Aboard the International Space Station, Spring Technical Meeting of The Central States Section of the Combustion Institute, Detroit, Mi, 15-17 May 2022, 10 pp. Theses and Dissertations 1. M. Kim, Spherical Gas-Fueled Cool Diffusion Flames, MS Thesis, University of Maryland, 60 pp., May 2022. 2. K.A. Waddell, Cool Diffusion Flames in Stagnation Flow, Ph.D. Dissertation, University of Maryland, in preparation. N.3 Awards In 2021, CFI-G Principal Investigator Peter Sunderland was inducted as a Fellow of the Combustion Institute for his “significant contributions to understanding laminar flame dynamics and soot formation and oxidation.”",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p88,1,"While the awards proceed the CFI-G experiment, CFI-G Co-Investigator Forman Williams was recognized by the Combustion Institute with the Silver Combustion Medal (1978) and the Bernard Lewis Gold Medal (in 1990). He furthermore was induced in the inaugural 2018 class of Fellows of the Combustion Institute for his “pioneering investigations in energy and combustion including nitric oxide emissions, spray and turbulent combustion, chemical kinetics and fire research.” N.4 Outreach NASA Project Scientist Dennis Stocker created the Space Flames Facebook page (www.facebook.com/space.flames) and Flickr site (https://www.flickr.com/photos/space-flames), where some entries are specific to CFI-G. He continues to administer these sites, where most content thus far has been about the ACME project of which CFI-G is a part. As an example of educational outreach, a simple board game related to the ACME’s spherical flames experiments can be downloaded from https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/acme/downloads/. ACME fact sheets, papers, posters, and presentations providing a project overview and status can also be downloaded from that page. Printed copies of fact sheets have been distributed at meetings of The Combustion Institute and the American Society for Gravitational and Space Research (ASGSR).",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p88,2,"The University of Maryland and Washington University in St. Louis promoted awareness of the CFI-G investigation online, as can be seen via these links: • ‘Cool Flame’ Discovered Aboard International Space Station • ‘Cool flames’ ignited in space",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p89,0,"ACME/CFI-G Final Report (2022) 21 November 2022 89 • Fire Protection Experts Available to the Media • Fires in Microgravity • FPE Professor Peter Sunderland Designated PI on Upcoming NIST and NSF Projects • FPE Prof Peter Sunderland discusses BRE experiments, currently aboard the ISS, with media. • Sunderland Gives Invited Talk at ASGSR 2021 Symposium • Unlocking the Mysteries of Fire ... in Space • Unusual ""cool flames"" discovered aboard International Space Station CASIS and NSF, which selected the experiment for spaceflight, promoted awareness of the research as can be seen through these links: • Gravity’s grip on heat and fire to be studied in space • Science in Space: NSF & ISS Briefing • The National Science Foundation and the ISS National Lab Announce Three Selected Projects from Combustion and Thermal Transport Funding Opportunity Onboard the International Space Station • Unusual ""cool flames"" discovered aboard International Space Station Tweets and Facebook posts from the investigators’ organizations and NSF are found on subsequent pages, where there could easily be more. Meanwhile, NASA promoted awareness of ISS combustion research with the video ‘The Power of Studying Combustion on the ISS’ which was posted on March 13, 2018. Awareness of ISS combustion research, including the ACME project, was promoted on Oct. 15, 2018 by The Combustion Institute in their online news article, “International Space Station (ISS) Combustion Experiments.”",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p89,1,"A photo collage and 1-paragraph article on ACME by Michael Greshko titled “Cosmic Combustion” was published on page 22 of the Oct. 2022 issue of the National Geographic Magazine, for which there is an online version. While general to ACME, the article addresses CFI-G with a statement about the flames: “In microgravity they can swell into ethereal … orbs that can burn at a surprisingly cool 900 degrees Fahrenheit (a gas stove burner’s high setting is about 3100oF).” Despite its brevity, Sunderland and Stocker were both interviewed for the article.",,False
PSI-159,Reports,PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Reports/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf,p89,2,"Stocker is involved in a broad range of NASA educational outreach to students and teachers, Scouts of both genders, and the general public. His primary outreach focus is the fundamentals of microgravity and human spaceflight, for example through microgravity design challenges which he helps create and administer. But he periodically addresses other topics, e.g., where he sometimes speaks on ‘NASA and the Science of Superman’ – for example at the Superman Celebration in Metropolis, IL. In this regard, his NASA outreach is carried out locally, regionally, nationally, and online. For example, he regularly presents on microgravity at Space Center Houston’s Space Exploration Educators Conference (SEEC). He has exhibited the last five National Scout Jamborees, five national Boy Scout conferences, the last three national Girl Scout conventions, and the 2019 World Scout Jamboree (which was held in the U.S.). Stocker has been a key lead for the agency in NASA’s participation in these Scouting events, where as an example the latter outreach is visually described at www.nasa.gov/feature/stem/world-scout-jamboree.html.",,False
