PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p1,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 1 Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) ACME Engineering Implementation Prepared by: Dennis P. Stocker, ACME Project Scientist NASA Glenn Research Center dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov, 216-433-2166 Canadian astronaut David Saint-Jacques working to replace the igniter tip on the ACME chamber insert, which is partially withdrawn from the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR), on 12 May 2019. [ISS059e060847] Given that this document provides a limited overview of the ACME hardware and operations, readers searching for additional information are welcome to contact Dennis Stocker with questions. Identification of commercial products is for clarification and does not indicate endorsement by NASA or the federal government.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p2,0,ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 2 Table of Contents Page 1. Introduction 3 2. Engineering Overview 5 A. General Description 5 B. Overview for E-FIELD Flames 13 3. Engineering Details (selected) 19 A. Chamber Insert Layout 20 B. Gases and Gas Delivery 22 C. Burners 25 1. Gas-Jet Burners 25 2. Coflow Burner 27 3. Spherical Burners 43 4. BRE Burners 45 D. Igniter Tips 54 E. Optical Layout 55 F. Radiometer Fields of View 61 G. Thermocouples 63 4. Engineering Documents 64 A. General Documents 64 B. Deviations and Waivers 73 5. Drawings 79 A. Top Level Assembly 79 B. Common Electrical 80 C. Quick Disconnects (QDs) and Hoses 81 D. Experiment Mounting Structure (EMS) 81 E. Gas Supply 82 F. Burners 83 G. Igniter 85 H. Analog Camera System 86 I. Diagnostic System 86 J. Measurement System 87 K. E-Field System 88 L. Cooling System 89 M. ACME Data Camera Package 90 N. ACME Avionics Package 92 O. Ground Support Equipment 94 P. Shipping Containers 96 Q. Trainer 96 6. Photographs 98 7. Internet Links 100 8. Nomenclature 101 Figure 1. NASA astronaut Jessica Meir working on 24 Oct. 2019 to remove and replace a failed hard drive in one of the CIR’s Image Processing and Storage Units (IPSUs). [Cropped detail from ISS061e013306],,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p3,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 3 1. Introduction The purpose of this report is to provide a description of the experimental hardware and operations of used in the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project that is sufficient for informed interpretation of the experimental results. In contrast, it is not the intent of this document to review the project management, engineering development, design process, etc. although such aspects may be briefly addressed. The ACME suite of experiments are being carried out on the International Space Station (ISS) in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). ACME features investigations with gaseous fuels, such as methane and ethylene. In addition to the fuel state, the current ACME experiments use laminar, non-premixed (i.e., diffusion) flames for their studies. To simplify analysis, these flames are furthermore either axisymmetric or spherically symmetric, depending upon the experiment. Currently, ACME includes six independent experiments of which the first five were selected for testing in spaceflight by NASA while the sixth was selected by the Center for the Advancement of Science in Space (CASIS) and the National Science Foundation (NSF).",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p3,1,"The CIR facility was launched in 2008 and with the Multi-user Droplet Combustion Apparatus (MDCA) was used for a series of liquid-fueled experiments from 2009 to 2017. The facility was reconfigured and set up for ACME testing by the ISS crew in September and October 2017, where over 25 astronauts from Canada, Germany, Italy, Japan, Russia and the United States have thus far supported the project’s research. As of this writing, over one thousand ACME flames have been ignited on the ISS, where that began in Nov. 2017. It is expected that the planned ACME testing will be completed in 2021, when the CIR operations should subsequently shift to the Solid Fuel Ignition and Extinction (SoFIE) project. The discussion of the CIR hardware herein is limited, but the research facility is described in various documents, such as those listed below. These are listed in reverse chronological order, where the oldest predate the facility’s completion and have become outdated in some regards. References 1, 2 and 4 also discuss ACME, but were all prepared prior to the completion of ACME’s spaceflight hardware. 1. Suleyman A. Gokoglu, Daniel L. Dietrich, Dennis P. Stocker, Paul V. Ferkul, Sandy L. Olson, Michael C. Hicks, Amelia Rai (editor), Neesha Hosein (editor), and Cory Duke (designer), “Combustion Science: A Researcher’s Guide to International Space Station,” NASA Publication NP-2015-10-034- JSC (2015). 2. J.M. Hickman, D.L. Dietrich, M.C. Hicks, V. Nayagam, V., and D. Stocker, “FLEX:",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p3,2,"A Decisive Step Forward in NASA's Combustion Research Program,” 1st Annual International Space Station Research and Development Conference, Denver, Colorado 26-28 June (2012). 3. “Unlocking Mysteries in Microgravity: NASA Glenn Provides the Keys with the Fluids and Combustion Facility,” NASA fact sheet FS-2011-06-035-GRC, PS-00538-0611 (2011). 4. T.F. O'Malley, W.A. Sheredy, and D.P. Stocker, “Combustion Research on the International Space Station,” 59th International Astronautical Congress, Vol. 2, pp. 756-766 (2008). 5. Terence F. O’Malley, Karen J. Weiland, Craig A. Myhre, Frank G. Gati, Myron E. Hill, and John H. Snead, “The Fluids and Combustion Facility and First Payloads: The Multi-User Droplet Combustion Apparatus and the Light Microscopy Module: Enabling Research on the ISS,” 57th International Astronautical Congress, IAC-06-A2.1.07, 2-6 Oct. 2006, Valencia, Spain (2006). 6. T. O’Malley and C. Myrhe, “The Fluids and Combustion Facility Combustion Integrated Rack and the Multi-user Droplet Combustion Apparatus: Microgravity Combustion Science Using Modular",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p4,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 4 Multi-User Hardware,” 53rd International Astronautical Congress, The World Space Congress 2002, IAC-02-J.5.05, 10-19 Oct. 2002, Houston, Texas (2002). 7. Terence F. O'Malley and Karen J. Weiland, “The FCF Combustion Integrated Rack: Microgravity Combustion Science Onboard the International Space Station,” NASA Technical Memorandum TM--2002-210981, AIAA-2001-4927, September 2002. 8. Robert L. Zurawski and Nora G. Bozzolo, “ISS Fluids and Combustion Facility: Combustion Integrated Rack: Payload Accommodations - Principal Investigator’s Guide,” NASA report, September 2000.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p4,1,"The CIR facility was preceded by the Combustion Module (CM) which flew on the space shuttle Columbia. In 1997, the combustion research facility was within Spacelab on the STS-83 and STS-94 (Microgravity Science Laboratory) missions. It was subsequently reconfigured and flew in 2003 within SPACEHAB, again in the shuttle’s payload bay, in the STS-107 mission. The Combustion Module investigations included Laminar Soot Processes (LSP), Structure Of Flame Balls At Low Lewis-number (SOFBALL), and the Water-Mist Fire Suppression Experiment (Mist). Although all three experiments burned gaseous fuel, each had its own chamber insert, whereas ACME features a single modular chamber insert to accomplish multiple investigations. A significant difference between the CIR and its shuttle precursor is that the ISS tests are remotely commanded from the ground without crew involvement, which wasn’t the case with the Combustion Module research. But in all cases the crew are required to configure the necessary hardware.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p4,2,"While extensive, this report is far from exhaustive but there are other reports which provide additional detail useful for interpreting ACME’s experimental results. Some key examples include the following: Camera characterization CFI Intensified Camera Package Characterization Report DRAFT REPORT Data Camera Characterization Report ACME-RPT-010 Rev. A Optical instrument characterization BRE Radiometer Characterization Report ACME-RPT-012 Cool Flames Radiometer Characterization Report CIR-RPT-4600 PMT Characterization Report ACME-RPT-013 Radiometer Characterization Report ACME-RPT-011 Requirement verification ERD Verification Report ACME-VER-002 Rev. B Inspection Verification Report ACME-VER-001 Rev. A Software and non-imaging data Software Interface Design Document ACME-DOC-010 Rev. D Abbreviations are addressed at the end of this report, but ERD stands for Engineering Requirements Document and many of the engineering requirements were directly derived from ACME’s science requirements. Meanwhile, key hardware dimensions, for example of the burners, and other details are confirmed in the Inspection Verification Report. Also note that the Burning Rate Emulator (BRE) and Cool Flames radiometer reports are specific to certain ACME experiments, whereas the other reports are general to all of ACME. ACME Project Scientist Dennis Stocker (216-433-2166, dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov) may be contacted with questions about the ACME project.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p5,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 5 2. Engineering Overview A. General Description This section provides a general description of the ACME engineering from the ‘3.0 Experimental’ section of the following 2014 conference paper: Dennis P. Stocker, Fumiaki Takahashi, J. Mark Hickman, and Andrew C. Suttles, “Gaseous Non-Premixed Flame Research Planned for the International Space Station,” Spring Technical Meeting of the Central States Section of the Combustion Institute, Tulsa, OK, 16– 18 March 2014. IMPORTANT: The 2104 paper from which this section was extracted proceeded completion of the spaceflight hardware which was mostly launched in 2017. As a result, it does not accurately describe the final engineering in some regards. Therefore updates, clarifications, additional details, shortcomings, references, etc. have been included in boxed notes like this following the original description. As a minor note, the sizes of figures were adjusted in this report, but none were cropped. As previously discussed, the ACME experiments will be conducted within the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) using a chamber insert, depicted in Figure 2, which was designed to accommodate gas delivery to a variety of exchangeable burners. Besides the chamber insert, other ACME-unique hardware includes a high-definition color camera and an avionics package, which is for data acquisition and control.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p5,1,Figure 2: Design of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) chamber insert that will be used in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on ISS.,,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p6,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 6 ACME’s chamber insert, which is ~0.4 m in diameter, provides common mounting for a variety of burners on the chamber axis (but only one at a time) and includes a retracting resistive igniter. As depicted in Figure 3, there will initially be gas-jet burners (with inner diameters ranging from 0.4 to 3.2 mm), a small coflow burner (where the two concentric tubes have inner diameters of 2.1 and 25 mm), porous spherical burners (with diameters ranging from 6.35 to 12.7 mm), and two flat porous burners for the BRE experiment (with inner diameters of 25 and 50 mm). Most burners will be equipped with a surface thermocouple, and each BRE burner will have two heat flux transducers. The internal pressure of the porous burners will be measured to allow monitoring of the pressure differential across the outlet surface. The igniter will have a crew-exchangeable tip and arm (to accommodate the various burners) and its stepper-motor drive will allow for precise positioning. Figure 3: ACME burner types, including from left to right, gas-jet burners, a co-flow burner, porous spherical burners, and flat porous burners for the BRE experiment.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p6,1,"Ultimately, the spherical burners were limited to a single diameter of 6.35 mm, and the fabricated gas-jet burners were limited to two sizes with nominal inner diameters of 1.3 and 2.1 mm. However, only the larger gas-jet burner was used during the spaceflight tests. The burners are addressed in detail in section 3.C. Unfortunately, the spherical burners have not always yielded spherically symmetric flames, presumably as a result of (1) greater burner flow through the joint between the porous sphere and supply tube, and (2) heat loss to the support tube (encompassing the supply tube) causing local flame quenching. Two types of igniter tips have been used during ACME testing. Most tests have been conducted using a loop igniter tip like those which had been successfully used in the droplet combustion experiments which preceded ACME research in the CIR. However, some tests were conducted with a coil igniter tip, where that included the second round testing for both the Burning Rate Emulator (BRE) and Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiments, with the exception of the latter’s first day testing. The CFI-G investigation will also use the coil igniter tip. For more information about the igniter tips, see section 3.D.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p6,2,"The chamber insert includes three mass flow controllers (visible on the left of Fig. 2) for the delivery of fuel, oxidizer or inert (e.g., helium or carbon dioxide), and nitrogen to the burner. The maximum fuel flow is 2 slpm (on a nitrogen basis), whereas much higher flows, e.g., 10 slpm (on a nitrogen basis), are possible for the oxidizer and nitrogen lines. The crew can exchange the mass flow controllers to provide appropriate ranges that maximize the flow accuracy. The gas delivery system enables on-orbit dilution of the fuel or oxidizer (but not both simultaneously), coflow,",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p7,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 7 inverse flames (e.g., oxidizer delivery to the center of a coflow burner), and premixed flames, although the latter are not planned for any of the current ACME experiments. Gases can also be delivered premixed to the space station where that is necessary in some circumstances, such as the blending of different fuel gases. CIR’s standard bottle sizes are 1, 2.25, and 3.8 liters but ACME will sometimes use smaller bottles as a hazard control to limit the deficient reactant (e.g., fuel) that can be released into the combustion chamber. Thus far, ACME tests were not conducted with carbon dioxide as a diluent. Furthermore, the fuel diluted with helium was premixed prior to launch rather than on orbit. Ultimately, oxidizer flows of up to 20 slpm (on a nitrogen basis) were possible, while the nitrogen flow was limited to a maximum of 5 slpm. ACME has not used gas bottles smaller than 1 liter as indicated above; the bottle fill pressure was instead limited as an additional hazard control.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p7,1,"ACME’s primary flame diagnostics are a suite of imaging systems including five video cameras and two illumination systems, of which three cameras are part of the CIR facility and are already in use on orbit. All but one of the cameras is mounted outside of the chamber. Collectively, the imaging systems will enable color, CH*, and OH* imaging; pyrometry; measurement of the soot volume fraction; and operational support. However, it is unlikely that all functions would be exercised at any one time as will become apparent in the subsequent discussion. Color imaging is provided by two cameras: a high-definition digital camera mounted outside of the chamber and a standard analog camera mounted on the chamber insert (with a turning mirror). Both cameras are part of the ACME hardware that is now in development. The analog camera signal will be downlinked in near real time to support the experiment operations. Light- emitting diodes within the chamber can be used to illuminate the igniter and burner to both support the operations and provide reference images. The 12-bit digital camera records at up to 30 full frames (1360x1024) per second, and is equipped with a motorized lens with independent control of the zoom, iris, and focus. The digital camera will be equipped with a filter assembly with four options, where selection of a clear “filter” allows for color imaging.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p7,2,"Color imaging can also be carried out with a blue-green BG7 Schott glass filter, which balances the color planes to avoid saturation in the red while maintaining sensitivity in the blue and green. The natural flame color can be approximately reconstructed, but the primary purpose of the filter is pyrometry and measurement of the soot volume fraction, again via the techniques of [23-24]. Alternately, CH* imaging can be conducted with this camera because the filter assembly includes a 430-nm bandpass filter with a 10-nm full width at half-maximum transmission (FWHM). A 450-nm bandpass filter (again with a 10-nm FWHM) can be used with the CH* filter to effectively subtract interfering broadband soot emission. However, the filter assembly rotates slowly so such subtraction effectively requires a separate test with each filter and of course reproducible flame conditions. The video from the analog operations (i.e., ops) camera, also referred to as the internal camera, is not archived in space. The video is instead recorded on the ground in the operations center. Its gain was sometimes adjusted for improved imaging of dim flames, e.g., approaching extinction. The sensitivity of the color digital camera, often referenced as the ACME DataCam (i.e., Data Camera), to the dim microgravity flames was such that it was often used with long exposure times and thus low framing rates, for example on the order of 1 frame per second. The clear “filter” is",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p8,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 8 of fused silica. Unfortunately, the thickness of the blue-green BG7 Schott glass filter and the added antireflective (AR) coating caused too great a reduction in the red, so it does not balance the intensity of the three color planes as intended and thereby can limit the accuracy of temperature determination. Meanwhile, rather than the 450-nm bandpass filter described above, the high definition color camera was equipped with a bandpass filter for imaging formaldehyde emissions. For more information, see the Data Camera Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-010 Rev. A). Imaging of the dim hydrogen flames in the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment was particularly challenging with these, if not all, cameras.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p8,1,"Pyrometry can alternately be accomplished with CIR’s monochrome High-Bit Depth Multispectral (HiBMs) camera, equipped with a Liquid-Crystal Tunable Filter (LCTF), which allows for imaging at multiple spectra, but requires a quasi-steady flame for measurement at each desired band. This camera has a telecentric optical system and is recorded at 512x512 and 30 frames per second (fps). Although it has a 1024x1024 pixel array, binning has been necessary because of signal loss through the LCTF. Both soot pyrometry and Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) are possible, where ACME is equipped with a crew-replaceable array of 5 silicon carbide (SiC) fibers. The fibers are orthogonal to the flame axis and the array can be translated along the flame axis to the desired position downstream of the burner, e.g., based on the downlink of the analog video. During set up, the crew can manually orient the array so that it is orthogonal to either ACME’s color digital camera or the HiBMs camera with the LCTF. An alternate fiber array including a platinum wire will be used for inflight characterization, which in combination with preflight calibration will enable determination of the soot volume fraction [24].",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p8,2,"In contrast to the preceding paragraph, ACME did not use a HiBMs camera for pyrometry, but instead relied on use of ACME’s color DataCam for such temperature measurements. For an example of how this can be done, see the techniques described in the following papers: P.B. Kuhn, B. Ma, B.C. Connelly, M.D. Smooke, and M.B. Long, “Soot and Thin-filament Pyrometry Using a Color Digital Camera,” Proceedings of the Combustion Institute, 33 (2011) 743-750. B. Ma, M. B. Long, “Absolute light calibration using S-type thermocouples,” Proceedings of the Combustion Institute, 34 (2) (2013) 3531-3539. Thus far, TFP tests have been conducted with 14-micron fibers spaced apart by 5 mm for a total span of 20 mm (in the case of 5 intact ceramic fibers), neglecting optional translation of the array. Tests have been conducted using arrays with and without the platinum wire in the furthest downstream position. Tests have also been conducted with some of the fibers broken and subsequently removed, leaving only three fibers in the final testing for the BRE and Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame (CLD Flame) experiments and at least the initial testing for the Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G).",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p8,3,"CIR has a second HiBMs camera, without a filter, that will be used in combination with CIR’s illumination package for alternate measurements of the soot volume fraction. This will be accomplished via an extinction technique previously employed in microgravity [3,6,26], where the camera and the collimated light source (with its two fiber-coupled laser diodes) are positioned at opposite windows. The images are recorded at 1024x1024 and 30 fps. Thus far, the primary purpose of this combined system has been the measurement of the droplet diameter for determination of the droplet burning rate.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p9,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 9 A HiBMs camera without the Liquid Crystal Tunable Filter (LCTF) has been used throughout much, but not all, of ACME’s operations. In the first round of the CLD Flame experiment, it was used without a filter for flame imaging, but this proved to be largely unsuccessful because the camera is very insensitive to blue. Afterward, it was equipped with a red filter corresponding to the CIR illumination package for the rest of the ACME testing. Unfortunately, that filter is sufficiently broad that significant incandescence can pass through the filter limiting its utility for determinations of soot volume fraction via light extinction, e.g., through the technique described in the following papers: D.L. Urban, Z.G. Yuan, P.B. Sunderland, K.C., Lin, Z. Dai, and G.M. Faeth, “Smoke-Point Properties of Nonbuoyant Round Laminar Jet Diffusion Flames,” Proceedings of the Twenty-Eighth Symposium (International) on Combustion 28 (2000) 1965-1972. F.J. Diez, C. Aalburg, P.B. Sunderland, D.L. Urban, Z.-G. Yuan, G.M. Faeth, “Soot properties of laminar jet diffusion flames in microgravity,” Combustion and Flame, 156 (2009) 1514-1524. P.S. Greenberg and J.C. Ku, “Soot Volume Fraction Imaging,” Applied Optics, 36 (22) (1997) 5514- 5522. Nonetheless, soot volume fraction can also be determined from the color DataCam images.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p9,1,"During ACME operations, the HiBMs camera is typically archived at 15 fps because of data limits for transfer and downlink operations. And because operations are constrained by the data limits, image data from this camera are sometimes deleted without downlinking to enable additional testing. And in some testing, HiBMs images aren’t even acquired given their lesser value. Unfortunately, the HiBMs backlight illumination is significantly obstructed by the bracket for the electrode mesh further limiting its utility for the E-FIELD Flames experiment. This was a design trade-off necessitated primarily by ACME’s large number of cameras and other optical instruments. CIR’s Low-Light-Level Ultraviolet (LLL-UV) package is a monochrome camera with an intensifier (for 280-700 nm) and a 310-nm bandpass filter (with a 10-nm FWHM) for OH* imaging. Again as a result of the signal loss through the filter, binning is employed such that the OH* images are recorded at 512x512 and 30 fps. During ACME operations, the LLL-UV camera has only been used for the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-Flame) experiment.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p9,2,"While not discussed in the preceding section, another intensified camera was used throughout ACME testing, although its image data was sometimes deleted or not even acquired to allow the performance of additional tests. This was the Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) camera which was generally configured with its CH* filter, where an exception is some s-Flame testing with particularly dim flames. Due to design trade-offs, its Field of View (FOV) was partially obstructed by the arms of the Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) fiber array when it was moved inwards to the flame. More information about this camera can be found in its characterization report, CIR-RPT- 4600. The ACME chamber insert will also be equipped with non-imaging optical diagnostics including both thermopile radiometers and Photomultiplier Tubes (PMTs). There is a crew-exchangeable",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p10,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 10 linear array of radiometers of which there are two versions supporting the initial set of experiments. The array has a fixed position which is parallel to the chamber axis and centered (along the chamber length) relative to the chamber windows and thus most of the other instrumentation. One array is equipped with a single wide-view radiometer with two narrow- view radiometers that are downstream and two more that are upstream, where all views are orthogonal to the chamber axis, as depicted in Figure 4. The narrow-view radiometers are intended to support studies of gas-jet flames, where ACME can accommodate shorter burners, making the “lower” detectors useful. The other array, also seen in Fig. 4, provides for three wide- view measurements of the flame, where one view is masked to nominally block radiant emission from the burner. It was specifically designed for the BRE experiment but will be a resource available to other ACME experiments. Given that the radiometer array can be exchanged by the crew, alternate arrays could be developed. Figure 4: Crew-exchangeable radiometer arrays on the ACME chamber insert: (top) standard (ACME) array of 5 radiometers, and (bottom) array with three radiometers, designed for the BRE experiment, where the lowest detector is masked to avoid view of the burner.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p11,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 11 A third radiometer array was developed for the CFI-G experiment, where it is important to understand that only a single radiometer array can be used at a time. The narrow-view radiometers in the standard array are only fully sensitive in small spots, but they are each sensitive in a much greater Field of View (FOV) than represented in the preceding figure. They do not have lenses; their FOV is a result of an aperture where most of their FOV is within the penumbra which is only visible to a portion of the sensor. Figures representing the measured FOVs of the as-built standard and BRE radiometer arrays are found in section 3.F of this document. Unfortunately, the radiometers in the BRE array typically saturated in the first round of BRE testing, so the standard array was used in its second round. However, those radiometers similarly saturated in many of round 2 tests. In this regard, each of ACME’s BRE and standard radiometers have a fixed gain. Meanwhile, each sensor in the radiometer array developed for the CFI-G investigation has two fixed gains, i.e., a high gain and low gain. For more information about the standard radiometer array used for most ACME testing, see the Radiometer Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-011). Meanwhile, you can learn about the other radiometer arrays from the BRE Radiometer Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-012) and the Cool Flames Radiometer Characterization Report (CIR-RPT-4600).",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p11,1,"An additional wide-view radiometer, that is fixed and not crew exchangeable, is positioned downstream of the burner and flame at 30o from the chamber axis. This detector is primarily intended to support the spherical flame experiments and will have a quicker response than the more sensitive detectors that will used in the linear arrays. Like the radiometers on the two linear arrays, it will have a spectral range of nominally 0.2-11 micron and its field of view will be established by an appropriately selected aperture. The FOV of the off-set radiometer is also shown in section 3.F. Learn more about this sensor from the Radiometer Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-011). To detect the quenching of dim flames, the chamber insert will be equipped with a set of three PMTs. Each PMT will have a wide-angle view, a sight line that is orthogonal to the chamber axis, and will be centered (along the chamber length) relative to the chamber windows so that it is aligned with most of the other instrumentation. The PMTs’ spectral range will be 230-700 nm, i.e., visible and near ultraviolet, but one will have a 310-nm bandpass filter and another will have a 430-nm bandpass filter (where both are with a 10-nm FWHM). The pair of detectors will enable measurement of OH* and CH* emission, respectively. The third PMT will be equipped with a neutral density filter to prevent saturation. Like many other components, the PMT array can be removed from the chamber insert by the crew.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p11,2,"Unlike the radiometers, each PMT had a readily adjustable gain. However, the PMT signals sometimes saturated when the gains were set too high. See the PMT Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-013) to learn more about the three Photomultiplier Tube (PMTs). The chamber insert is also equipped with six far-field thermocouples to measure the spread of the thermal field in the spherical flame experiments. They are of limited utility for other experiments because they are positioned upstream of the burner tip. Therefore, they will",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p12,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 12 generally not be installed by the crew to reduce the chance of accidental damage. There will be two versions of a four-thermocouple rake to allow a choice of near and far measurements. Other arrays could be designed to support future experiments, but they cannot extend downstream of the burner tip without interfering with other (e.g., optical) measurements. The two lone thermocouples, which are both at nominally 200 mm from the center of the spherical burner, have been installed throughout ACME testing. The tip of one was seen to be slightly bent early in testing, as discussed and depicted in section 3.G of this report. Only the short (or far) version of the radiometer rake has been used thus far, where factors include the need for crew time to switch between rakes and the heat loss that could result from thermocouples in the long (close) rake. The short (far) rake’s thermocouple tips should nominally be at 45, 60, 100, and 100 mm from the spherical burner center. For thermocouples at the same distance from the burner center, the angle between the rays from the burner center to the two thermocouples should be 90 degrees. The bead size of the thermocouples was not measured, but it is undoubtedly at least twice the size of the 0.2 mm diameter wire. The two thermocouples closest to the burner outlets on each four-thermocouple rake are type B, while the others are type K.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p12,1,"Experiment-unique hardware is also mounted on the chamber insert. For example, to accommodate the E-FIELD Flames experiment, the insert is equipped with a high-voltage supply and a crew-removable electrode mesh which can be set between +10 and -10 kV. The circular mesh is orthogonal to the chamber axis and is positioned (during set up) at either ~35 or ~50 mm downstream of the insulated burner. The ion current between the burner and electrode mesh will be determined via measurement of the voltage drop across a known resistor and application of Ohm’s Law. Additional images of the chamber insert can be seen in subsequent sections of this report. The hardware specific to E-FIELD Flames is discussed further in section 2.B. Additional supporting measurements will be made, where the chamber pressure is a simple example. The ACME experiments will use the CIR’s gas chromatograph for pre- and post-test analysis of the chamber atmosphere, e.g., for oxygen, nitrogen, carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide, and fuel gases. Acceleration measurements will be provided by the Space Acceleration Measurement System (SAMS) [27], which is the primary instrument for such measurements on ISS and previously served that role for the space shuttle.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p12,2,"The CIR’s Gas Chromatograph Instrumentation Package (GCIP) was not used for the CLD Flame or E-FIELD Flames investigations and thus far was only sometimes used during BRE, Flame Design, and s-Flame testing. Its use requires significant time and as such effectively reduces the number of tests that can be conducted. However, estimates of the chamber contents were computed assuming that the fuel in a normal flame test (for example) was either fully or not at all oxidized, e.g., for unsustained ignition in the latter case. The CIR facility was equipped with a 3-axis SAMS sensor head and the acceleration measurements, along with relevant documentation, are accessible via the home page for the Principal Investigator",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p13,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 13 Microgravity Services (PIMS), https://gipoc.grc.nasa.gov/wp/pims/home/. To be clear, the acceleration measurements are not archived with the ACME experiment data in the Physical Sciences Informatics (PSI) website, www.nasa.gov/PSI. All ACME experiments will be constant volume (i.e., rather than constant pressure) studies, where the chamber’s free volume will be about 90 liters. Burner flows will generally be constrained to limit the pressure rise, and tests will typically be 0.5-2 minutes in duration. The chamber’s free volume is approximately 83 liters depending on the installed hardware.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p13,1,"The ACME experiments will be configured, but not conducted, by the ISS crew. Instead, the experiments will be commanded from the ground, specifically from the Telescience Support Center (TSC) at the NASA Glenn Research Center in Cleveland, Ohio. They can be conducted in a nominally automated mode using pre-programmed scripts. However, it will also be possible to effectively operate the experiments in a “manual” mode, where changes can be made in response to the analog video downlink and the numerical data available in the telemetry stream. For example, a flow rate could be adjusted to approach a stability or soot limit. Step and ramp changes can be made in the flow rate(s) or electric field potential. The TFP fiber array can be translated uniformly through a soot-free flame to get a continuum measurement of the temperature field. In summary, ACME’s experiment control will allow for broad flexibility. The TSC is now also referred to as the Glenn ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC). Thus far, the ACME tests have always been conducted in a nominally automated mode.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p13,2,"B. Overview for E-FIELD Flames While focused on the Electric-Field Effects on Laminar Diffusion Flames (E-FIELD Flames) experiment, this section provides a good general description relevant to all ACME experiments. This text is from the ‘Methods / Experimental’ section of the following conference paper: Y.-C. Chien, J. Tinajero, D. Stocker, U. Hegde, and D. Dunn-Rankin, “Electric Field Effects on Flames in Microgravity on the International Space Station,” 2018 Spring Technical Meeting of the Central States Section of The Combustion Institute, Minneapolis, MN, 20-22 May 2018. See the paper itself for the references, etc. as they not included in this report. As a minor note, the sizes of some figures were adjusted in this report, but none were cropped. The figures have not been renumbered herein but are effectively 5-8 for this report (rather than 1-4). In contrast to the preceding section, this paper was written after the beginning of E-FIELD Flames (and CLD Flame) operations on the ISS. However, additional details are again included within this section in boxed notes like this.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p13,3,"The E-FIELD Flames experiment is being conducted in 2018 within the ISS’ Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) [15], which was launched in 2008 and used from 2009 to 2017 for a series of droplet combustion experiments [16]. The CIR facility is similar in size to a vending machine as can be seen in Figure 1. It includes a 100-liter combustion chamber capable of operation at pressures of up to 300 kPa. The chamber features a ring of 8 sapphire windows and is equipped with a set of reconfigurable imaging systems. The CIR was designed with gas delivery, venting, thermal control, electrical power, data handling, image processing and storage, and vibration isolation to enable combustion research with gaseous, liquid, and solid fuels.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p14,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 14 Figure 1: European Space Agency (ESA) astronaut Paolo Nespoli installing the chamber insert for the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). This photograph [ISS053e098207] was taken on the International Space Station (ISS) on 12 Oct. 2017. E-FIELD Flames is one of the six unique experiments featuring non-premixed laminar flames of gaseous fuels which currently make up the ACME project [17]. A modular set of hardware, including the critical chamber insert depicted in Figures 2 and 3, was developed to carry out the ACME investigations in the CIR facility. The 0.4-m diameter ACME chamber insert provides common mounting for a variety of burners on the chamber axis (but only one at a time) and a retracting resistively-heated igniter. The igniter has a crew-exchangeable tip and arm (to accommodate the various burners) and stepper-motor drive for radial positioning. The chamber insert includes three mass flow controllers (visible on the far left of Figures 2-3 behind an orange hose and black cables) for delivering fuel, oxidizer (or an inert), and nitrogen to the burner or chamber. The crew can exchange the mass flow controllers to provide appropriate accuracy for the desired flow ranges. ACME’s gas delivery system enables on-orbit dilution of the gaseous fuel or oxidizer with nitrogen (available from the space station), but not both simultaneously.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p14,1,"However, bottles of premixed fuel and oxidizer are also used, where the CIR’s standard bottle sizes are 1.00, 2.25, and 3.80 liters. Fuels are stored in 1-liter bottles, while the 21/79 oxygen/nitrogen mixture (on a volume basis) used by E-FIELD Flames comes in 3.80-liter bottles. The ACME experiments are constant volume studies, where the chamber’s free volume, with the insert installed, is about 90 liters. Burner flows are generally constrained to limit the pressure rise and vitiation of the atmosphere, where typical ACME tests are only 0.5-2 minutes in duration.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p15,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 15 Figure 2: NASA astronaut Joe Acaba reviewing procedures to align the hot-wire igniter on the ACME chamber insert to his left, in a photograph [ISS054e014660] taken by NASA astronaut Mark Vande Hei on 5 Jan. 2018. Figure 3: Solid model representation of the ACME chamber insert.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p16,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 16 The E-FIELD Flames experiment is being conducted with two different burners: a simple gas-jet burner and a coflow burner, which can be seen in Figure 4(a). The results discussed in this paper are only from the first of the experiment’s tests conducted with the coflow burner. The stainless steel fuel tube in the burner has inner and outer diameter of 2.2 and 2.4 mm, respectively. The coflow burner’s steel outer tube has an inner diameter of 25 mm, where the annulus outlet is a 6.4-mm thick Hastelloy X honeycomb with 0.05-mm foil thickness and 0.81-mm cell size. The coflow burner’s inner tube protrudes 3.1 mm beyond the surface of the honeycomb outlet. While the inner tube is being used exclusively for fuel flow in E-FIELD Flames, valves in the ACME chamber insert allow the fuel, oxidizer, and nitrogen to be directed to either tube, making this burner capable of inverse flame testing.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p16,1,"Figure 4: (a) Detail of the ACME chamber insert featuring the coflow burner (in the image center) used by the E-FIELD Flames experiment. This is from a photograph [ISS053e096074] taken by ESA astronaut Paolo Nespoli on 12 Oct. 2017 after he installed the burner on the chamber insert. (b) Detail of the ACME chamber insert where the copper electrode mesh, used by the E-FIELD Flames experiment, can be seen above the coflow burner. This is from a photograph [ISS055e001252] taken by NASA astronaut Scott Tingle on 8 March 2018 following his installation of the electrode mesh.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p16,2,"In this study, a high-voltage electric field is generated between the burner and a copper electrode mesh located above (i.e., downstream of) the burner outlet, as can be seen in Figure 4(b). The circular mesh is energized at up to 10,000 volts with either polarity, where the burner serves as the electrical return and the flow of flame-produced ions completes the circuit. The ion current between the burner and electrode mesh is measured from the voltage drop across a known resistor and application of Ohm’s Law. With and without the copper ring, the electrode mesh is 8.9 and 8.4 cm in diameter, respectively. The square copper weave has 7.1 wires/cm, a wire diameter of 0.43 mm, and a 48% open area. The mesh permits gas to pass through while also acting as an electrical element. The electrode mesh is mounted 37 mm above the coflow burner tip and is centered on and orthogonal to the chamber axis. The ACME chamber insert can also",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p17,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 17 accommodate an alternate electrode position of 52 mm above the coflow burner tip, but the closer position is being used to produce the highest field strengths. The slow response time of the high-voltage power supplies (recommended in the ACME Integrated Science Requirements Document, i.e., ISRD) did not meet the requirements necessary to achieve the requested time response performance, and as such inhibited determination of the flame’s response time to rapid changes in the electric field strength. The negative and positive high-voltage power supplies were to have been coupled, as described for example in Appendix C of the ISRD. However, switching between the two high-voltage power supplies led to excessive Electromagnetic Interference (EMI) in ground testing so the power supplies were completely decoupled, where only one was installed at a time. As such, testing was limited to a single polarity at a time in contrast to the experimental plans documented in the experiment’s Science Requirements Document (SRD) and the ACME ISRD.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p17,1,"ACME’s non-imaging measurements are recorded at 125 Hz. The measured test conditions include the chamber pressure and temperature, the three gas flow rates, and the electrical potential of the electrode mesh. Acceleration measurements are provided by the Space Acceleration Measurement System (SAMS) [18], which is the primary instrument for such measurements on ISS and previously served that role for the space shuttle.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p17,2,"The flame behavior is observed optically with a variety of instruments. For most ACME experiments, including E-FIELD Flames, the radiant emission is measured with 6 thermopile detectors with a spectral range of nominally 0.2-11 microns. Five radiometers are in a crew- exchangeable linear array that is mounted parallel to the chamber axis. The other detector is downstream and off axis from the burner. Chemiluminescent emission is measured with a set of three Photomultiplier Tube (PMT) modules. Their spectral range is 230-700 nm, i.e. in the visible and near ultraviolet, but two are equipped with narrow bandpass filters for measurement of OH* and CH* emissions, respectively. The third PMT is equipped with a neutral density filter to prevent saturation, although the gain values for all three PMTs are independently and readily adjustable. Like many of the components, the array of PMT modules can be removed from the chamber insert by the crew. In addition to their scientific value, PMT measurements are used operationally to detect ignition and automatically deactivate and retract the igniter. The radiometer or PMT measurements can similarly be used to detect extinction and automatically end the gas flow and test.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p17,3,"Imaging measurements of the flames complement the optical sensors described above. Color imaging is provided by an analog color camera within the chamber and a high-definition (1360 x 1024), 12-bit color camera mounted outside the chamber on CIR’s optical bench. The latter is equipped with a motorized lens with independent control of the zoom, iris, and focus. It has a filter barrel with a fused silica window (for color imaging), a blue-green BG7 Schott glass filter, and bandpass filters for imaging CH* and HCHO (formaldehyde) chemiluminescence. The digital color camera was characterized prior to launch for color-ratio pyrometry and measurement of the soot volume fraction, via the techniques of [19, 20]. The camera makes soot pyrometry possible as well as Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP), where the chamber insert is equipped with a translating array of silicon-carbide (SiC) fibers. A 14-micron fiber array can be seen at the top of Figure 4(b). However, the fibers interfere with the electric field preventing the use of Thin Filament Pyrometry in the E-FIELD Flames experiment.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p18,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 18 Two intensified monochrome cameras mounted outside the combustion chamber are available for ACME use, although only one is being used by E-FIELD Flames. It is a high-definition (1390x1040), 12-bit camera equipped with a filter barrel similar to that used by the digital color camera discussed in the preceding paragraph, although it doesn’t have a blue-green filter. The CH* filter has been used in the intensified photography of the E-FIELD Flames tests. While unused by E-FIELD Flames, the CIR is also equipped with a high-definition (1024x1024) camera with an intensifier extending into the ultraviolet which can be equipped with a bandpass filter and corresponding optics for OH* imaging. Finally, a high-definition (1024x1024), 12-bit monochrome camera is mounted outside the chamber opposite a collimated light source with two fiber-coupled laser diodes emitting red light of 650-655 nm. This camera has a bandpass filter corresponding to the illumination source, allowing for determination of soot volume fraction through an extinction technique previously employed in microgravity research [21-23]. Unfortunately, because of trade-offs demanded by limited chamber volume and large number of instruments and components, the collimated light is obstructed substantially by the electrode mesh mount, limiting the utility of this camera for the E-FIELD Flames experiment.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p19,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 19 3. Engineering Details (selected) This section provides additional details about some of the key engineering elements, including the chamber insert (pp., 20-22), gases and gas delivery (pp. 22-25), burners (pp. 25-50), igniter tips (pp. 50- 51), optical layout (pp. 51-56), radiometers (pp. 57-58), and thermocouples (pp. 59-60). Most of the engineering subsystems are depicted in Figure 9, which is color-coded for ACME and the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) facility in which the ACME research is being conducted. Thus far, ACME has not used the HiBMs-2 camera as shown in the chart, but the project instead has taken advantage of the Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) intensified camera, a CIR asset developed for the CFI experiment. While not shown in the figure, ACME has also sometimes used the CIR’s Gas Chromatograph Instrumentation Package (GCIP) for the BRE, Flame Design, and s-Flame experiments. This is limited to pre- or post-test measurements of the chamber atmosphere for oxygen, nitrogen, carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide, and fuel gases. Figure 9. ACME subsystems as identified in ACME’s Critical Design Review (CDR) held in Nov. 2013.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p19,1,"Several of the subsystems identified in the figure and preceding paragraph are addressed in specific reports: ACME-RPT-010 Rev. A Data Camera Characterization Report ACME-RPT-011 Radiometer Characterization Report ACME-RPT-012 BRE Radiometer Characterization Report ACME-RPT-013 PMT Characterization Report CIR-RPT-4600 Cool Flames Radiometer Characterization Report DRAFT REPORT CFI Intensified Camera Package Characterization Report Meanwhile, a key document regarding the software and data files is: ACME-DOC-010 Rev. D ACME Software Interface Design Document",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p20,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 20 A. Chamber Insert Layout This section provides the layout of the ACME chamber insert from different perspectives. While the layout is accurate in these solid model representations, some details are not. Figure 10. Solid model representation of the chamber insert from two external perspectives.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p21,0,ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 21 Figure 11. Solid model representation of the chamber insert’s interior as seen looking toward the burner and the end plate on which it is mounted.,,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p22,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 22 Figure 12. Solid model representation of the chamber insert’s interior as seen looking away from the burner toward the opposite end plate. Note that the electrode mesh was only installed for the E-FIELD Flames experiment, where it obstructed use of the Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) fiber array. Furthermore, its mounting bracket interfered with illumination for possible soot volume fraction measurements via light extinction. B. Gas Delivery The tests are conducted using nitrogen available from the ISS and fuel, oxidizer, and inert gas bottles launched from Earth specifically for use with the CIR facility. This approach includes both the burner flow and the chamber atmosphere, where combustion tests were not conducted with the ISS cabin atmosphere, even including check-out or exploratory tests. The pure and premixed fuel gases launched for flame tests are listed in Table 1. Planned tests for four of the six ACME experiments are limited to the use of methane and ethylene of varying dilutions. The two exceptions are the Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames (s-",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p23,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 23 Flame) experiment and Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G). The s-Flame investigation included tests with hydrogen and hydrogen/methane mixtures while CFI-G features tests with ethane, propane, and n-butane. Table 1. Fuel gases launched to support the ACME experiments, where mixture fractions on a volume basis. Note that calibration gases for the CIR’s Gas Chromatograph Instrumentation Package (GCIP) are not included in this list. Flammable gas(es) Fuels H2 (hydrogen) s-Flame only 100% H2 45% H2, 55% He (helium) H2 and CH4 (methane) s-Flame only 66.7% H2, 33.3% CH4 55.6% H2, 44.4% CH4 44.4% H2, 55.6% CH4 30% H2, 15% CH4, 55% He 25% H2, 20% CH4, 55% He 20% H2, 25% CH4, 55% He CH4 100% CH4 45% CH4, 55% N2 (nitrogen) 40% CH4, 60% N2 30% CH4, 70% N2 45% CH4, 55% He C2H6 (ethane) CFI-G only 100% C2H6 30% C2H6, 70% N2 C2H4 (ethylene) 100% C2H4 50% C2H4, 50% N2 30% C2H4, 70% N2 30% C2H4, 70% He 25% C2H4, 75% He 20% C2H4, 80% He C3H8 (propane) CFI-G only 100% C3H8 30% C3H8, 70% N2 C4H10 (n-butane) CFI-G only 100% C4H10 50% C4H10, 50% N2 30% C4H10, 70% N2",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p23,1,"The launched oxidizer bottles have included 21%, 30%, 40%, and 85% oxygen (by volume) with a balance of nitrogen and 30% oxygen with a balance of helium. It is important to understand that ACME testing is limited to 40% oxygen in the combustion chamber’s atmosphere, where the 85% oxygen is for inverse flame testing, in which the oxidizer flows from the burner into the chamber filled with a fuel/inert mixture. Inverse flame tests are planned for both the CFI-G and Flame Design experiments, and no others, but have yet to be conducted as of this writing. Meanwhile, the only inert launched specifically to support ACME testing has been 100% helium where compressed nitrogen is available as a space station resource. Again, this discussion neglects gases for the GCIP, e.g., its carrier and calibration gases. It is important to understand that the CIR and ACME hardware only allows for controlled nitrogen dilution of one gas at a time. For most experiments, this isn’t a limitation since the chamber is",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p24,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 24 filled with its atmosphere before gas flows from the burner. But in tests using the coflow burner, either the inner or annular gas flows can be diluted, but not both at once in a controlled manner. This can be discerned from the schematic of the gas delivery found in Figure 13. As a result, one of the gases is premixed on the ground. For the CLD Flame and E-FIELD Flames investigation with the coflow burner, tests were conducted using premixed 21% O2 (79% N2) bottles while the ISS nitrogen was used to dilute the fuel. Figure 13. Schematic of gas delivery in ACME testing, where the FOMA is the CIR’s Fuel and Oxidizer Management Assembly. In ACME testing, the gas flow is controlled using the crew-exchangeable Mass Flow Controllers (MFCs) on the chamber insert, where there is one each used at a time for the fuel, oxidizer, and ACME Flow System Model Interface Resource Ring (IRR) ISS N2 from GIS Diluent Manifold 1 Oxygen Manifold 2 Manifold #3 Gas Bottle Line Not Used Static Mixer Fuel Manifold 4 SV A1 to Chamber SV A4 MFC A1 MFC A2 MFC A3 SV A3 to Chamber SV A2 to Chamber Bypass Line To Outer Coflow To Inner Coflow To Gas-Jet Burner To Spherical Burner PT A1 PT A2 SV A5 SV A6 SV A7 SV A8 SV A9 ACME FLOW System CIR FOMA QD13 QD A2 QD A3 QD A1 QD A4 QD A7 QD12 QD11 QD A5 QD A6 QD A8 QD A9",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p25,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 25 nitrogen. These MFCs are available with various flow ranges as seen in Table 2, where it is advantageous in terms of flow control and uncertainty to use MFCs commensurate with the needed gas flow rates. However, the advantages of swapping MFCs must be balanced with need for crew time to swap MFCs so tests are not always conducted under optimal conditions. For example, control at small fractions of the total flow range is unlikely to be good. Note that the CIR facility is equipped with MFCs for large gas flow rates, but these were simply forced to be fully open when gas flow was desired. More specifically, the CIR MFCs are for 0-30 slpm (i.e., standard liters per minute) on a nitrogen basis, with the important exception of the fuel MFC which is 0-2 slpm on a nitrogen basis. Otherwise they are not used to control the gas flow rates in ACME tests. Table 2. Mass Flow Controllers (MFCs) available for use on the ACME chamber insert MFC Gas type # of MFCS Maximum flow rates (on a nitrogen basis) A1 Oxidizer (or inert) 2 0.5, 20 slpm A2 Nitrogen 9 0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 2, 5 slpm A3 Fuel 6 0.05, 0.2, 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2 slpm C. Burners As discussed in the Hardware Overview, there are four types of burners, where two types were fabricated in different sizes and multiple spaceflight copies were made and used of the spherical burner. Meanwhile there is only a single spaceflight copy of the coflow burner.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p25,1,"Table 3. Burner types and sizes used for the ACME experiments Experiment Burners used (or to be used) BRE CFI-G CLD Flame E-FIELD Flames Flame Design s-Flame BRE burners, 25-mm (rounds 1 and 2) and 50-mm (round 2 only) Spherical burner(s) Coflow burner Coflow burner (round 1), 2.1-mm gas-jet burner (round 2) Spherical burners Spherical burner Additional details can be learned from the burner drawings listed in section 6.F.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p25,2,"1. Gas-Jet Burners The fabricated gas-jet burners were limited to two sizes with nominal inner diameters of 1.3 and 2.1 mm, where two spaceflight copies of each were fabricated because of concerns that they might be damaged. However, only a single copy of the larger diameter burner has thus far been used during the spaceflight tests, where that use was limited to the second round of the E-FIELD Flames testing. This gas-jet burner was used to allow comparison with tests carried out with the coflow burner in E-FIELD Flames’ first round. The same commercial stainless-steel tubing used for the coflow burner’s inner tube was also used for the 2.1-mm gas-jet burner. However, it is important to note that the nominal inner diameter (ID) in both cases was 2.16 mm, i.e., approximately 2.2 mm rather than 2.1 mm. While potentially confusing, this met the tolerance requirement for the 2.1- mm gas-jet burner. Meanwhile, the nominal outer diameter (OD) was 2.41 mm, again for the 2.1-mm gas-jet burner and the inner tube of the coflow burner. It is also important to understand that the gas-jet burner is slightly taller than the coflow burner, where the",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p26,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 26 electrode mesh was nominally 35 and 37 mm downstream of the gas-jet and coflow burners, respectively (based on the outlet of the inner tube in the case of the coflow burner). The gas-jet burner features an extremely fine, sheathed, type-K thermocouple mounted on the tube exterior with its tip 5 mm upstream of the burner outlet. In contrast, the coflow burner was not equipped with the thermocouple because of concerns that it might result in flow and thus flame non-uniformity. Figure 14. A gas-jet burner prior to launch. The white cable on the left was for grounding, while the yellow connector is for the burner’s type-K thermocouple.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p27,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 27 2. Coflow Burner The coflow burner is effectively a 2.1-mm gas-jet burner within a large tube, where gas could also flow from the annulus between the two tubes. The stainless-steel fuel tube in the burner has inner and outer diameter of 2.16 and 2.41 mm, respectively. The coflow burner’s steel outer tube has an inner diameter of 25 mm, where the annulus outlet is a 6.4-mm thick Hastelloy X honeycomb with 0.05-mm foil thickness and 0.81-mm cell size. The coflow burner’s inner tube protrudes 3.1 mm beyond the surface of the honeycomb outlet. Figure 15. The coflow burner aboard the space station, where the inner tube extends downstream of the annular outlet featuring a fine honeycomb. The white cable with the black connector on the right is for grounding, while the yellow connector on the left was used to secure the cable for the burner thermocouple, even though the coflow burner isn’t equipped with a thermocouple. [Cropped detail from ISS064e028847] The coflow burner has thus far only been used for the CLD Flame and E-FIELD Flames experiments, where in both cases the inner tube was used exclusively for fuel flow. However, ACME’s gas delivery manifold allows the fuel, oxidizer, and nitrogen to be directed to either tube, making the burner capable of inverse flame testing, as can be seen in section 3.B.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p27,1,"CLD Flame tests were always conducted with an oxidizer coflow, but some E-FIELD Flames tests with the coflow burner were conducted with no coflow. That enabled a three-way comparison with the tests conducted with a coflow and those with the gas-jet burner (which of course were without a coflow). As previously mentioned, the coflow burner is slightly shorter than the gas-jet burner, where the electrode mesh used for the E-FIELD Flames experiment was nominally 35 and 37 mm downstream of the gas-jet and coflow burners, respectively (based on the outlet of the inner tube in the case of the coflow burner). Given the two situations, the electrode mesh can appropriately be described as positioned nominally 36 mm downstream of the",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p28,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 28 two burners. Unlike the other burners, the coflow burner was not equipped with a thermocouple because of concerns that it could adversely result in flow and thus flame asymmetry. The final tests from E-FIELD Flames’ first round on 17 May 2018 featured sooty 100% ethylene (C2H4) flames and a positively-charged electrode mesh which ‘pushed’ the flames toward the burner leading to significant soot deposition on the coflow burner. Figure 16 reveals that soot accumulated on the burner’s inner tube to the extent that its shape was effectively altered and would impede the surrounding coflow. Meanwhile, the honeycomb coflow outlet was blackened in the central region near the inner tube, as seen in Figure 16, where that soot could have obstructed and reduced the coflow in the most important region. Figure 16. The coflow burner outlet imaged with the backlit HiBMs camera on 25 May 2018. The inner tube, from which the fuel exits in normal flame testing, has been coated with soot. Figure 17. The coflow burner outlet (as seen in the display of a handheld digital camera) from a high-definition video downlink from the ISS on 19 June 2018 prior to the burner cleaning. The central portion of the burner’s outlet, including the honeycomb is blackened by soot. A procedure to clean the coflow burner was developed on the ground and implemented",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p29,0,"ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 29 by astronaut Ricky Arnold on 19 June 2018. During the procedure, for which example images can be seen in Figure 18, Arnold used a toothbrush, piece of insulated wire, flashlight, vacuum sweeper, and mask (for personal protection). After the cleaning, the CLD Flame Principal Investigator Marshall Long subsequently declared that “we are satisfied that the burner will be able to provide flow conditions similar to those that have been measured in our lab before the burner was placed in orbit.” Figure 18. Images from the coflow burner cleaning carried out by astronaut Ricky Arnold on 19 June 2018. In the left image [ISS056e021501], a piece of insulated wire is used to ensure that the inner tube’s interior is unobstructed by soot. In the right image [iss056e021495], a flashlight is used to verify that the honeycomb is not blocked by soot.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p29,1,"The spaceflight burner’s flow profile was characterized by the CLD Flame team at Yale University prior to its launch, where measurements were made along the four different lines shown in Figure 19 where the velocities of the inner and annular flows were intended to be matched at 10, 20, 30, 40, and 50 cm/s. Hot-wire anemometry measurements were made for each of those velocity pairs at 0.5, 5, and 10 mm downstream of the burner’s inner tube, where those results are respectively presented in Figures 20-24, 25-29, and 30-34. The black dashed lines in the subsequent plots of velocity profiles represent the inner and outer tubes of the burner. Select measurements were also made for 11 mismatched velocity pairs at 0.5 mm downstream of the inner tube, where those are presented in Figures 35-45 (of which the first five are with no inner flow). Additional measurements may be found in the Dec. 2016 document titled ACME CLD burner hot wire anemometry measurements. Figure 19. Lines along on which the velocity profiles were measured with the spaceflight coflow burner prior to launch.",,False
PSI-159,Engineering Documents,PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,data/raw/files/PSI-159/Engineering Documents/PSI-159_Engineering Documents_ACME Engineering Implementation Rpt..pdf,p30,0,ACME Engineering Implementation 19 May 2022 30 Figure 20. Velocity profiles 0.5 mm downstream of the coflow burner’s inner tube with inner and annular velocities intended to be matched at 10 cm/s. Figure 21. Velocity profiles 0.5 mm downstream of the coflow burner’s inner tube with inner and annular velocities intended to be matched at 20 cm/s.,,False
